                        *That Which Hath Wings*\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER I*\par
\par
                      *PRESENTS TWO YOUNG PEOPLE*\par
\par
\par
In January, 1914, Francis Athelstan Sherbrand, Viscount Norwater, only\par
son of that fine old warrior, General the Right Honourable Roger\par
Sherbrand, V.C., K.C.B., first Earl of Mitchelborough, married Margot\par
Mountjohn, otherwise known as "Kittums," and found that she was\par
wonderfully innocent--for a girl who knew so much.\par
\par
It was a genuine love-match, Franky being a comparatively poor\par
Guardsman, with only two thousand a year in addition to his pay as a\par
Second Lieutenant in the Royal Bearskins Plain, and Margot a mere\par
Cinderella in comparison with heiresses of the American canned-provision\par
and cereal kind.\par
\par
It had seemed to Franky, standing with patent-leathered feet at the\par
Rubicon dividing bachelorhood from Benedictism, that all his wooing had\par
been done at Margot's Club.  True, he had actually proposed to Margot at\par
the Royal Naval and Military Tournament of the previous June, and\par
Margot, hysterical with sheer ecstasy, as the horses gravely played at\par
push-ball, had pinched his arm and gasped out:\par
\par
"_Yes_, but don't take my mind off the game just now; these dear beasts\par
are so _heavenly_! ..."\par
\par
And theatres, film-picture-shows and variety halls, race-meetings,\par
receptions, balls and kettledrums, polo and croquet-clubs, had fostered\par
the courtship of Franky and Margot; but all their love-making had been\par
carried out to the accompanying hum of conversation and the tinkle of\par
crystal and silver-plate in the dining-room of the "Ladies' Social,"\par
where Margot had her favourite table in the glass-screened corner by the\par
fire-place; or in the circular smoking-room with the Persian divan and\par
green-glass dome, that Margot had given the Club on her nineteenth\par
birthday; or in the boudoir belonging to the suite she had decorated for\par
herself on the condition that no other member got the rooms if Margot\par
wanted them, which Margot nearly always did....\par
\par
There was a big, rambling, ancient red-brick Hall, stone-faced in the\par
Early Jacobean manner, standing with its rare old gardens and\par
glass-houses, lawns and shrubberies, about it, within sight and sound of\par
the Channel, amidst pine and beech-woods carpeted with bilberry-bushes,\par
heathery moors, and coverts neck-high in July with the _Osmunda regalis_\par
fern.  The Hall belonged to Margot, though you never found her there\par
except for a week or two in September and three days at Christmas-tide.\par
The first fortnight with the birds was well enough, but those three days\par
at Christmas marked the limit.  Of human endurance Margot meant,\par
possibly.  She never vouchsafed to explain.\par
\par
She also possessed a house in town, but just as her deceased father's\par
spinster sister lived at the Hall in Devonshire, so did her dead\par
mother's brother Derek, with his collection of European moths and\par
butterflies and other _Lepidoptera_, inhabit the fine old mansion in\par
Hanover Square. Devonshire at Christmas marked the limit of dulness, but\par
Hanover Square all the London season through beat the band for sheer\par
ghastly boredom....  Not that there were any flies on little old\par
London....  Paris and Ostend were ripping places, and you could put in a\par
clinking good time at Monte Carlo....  Margot had tried New York and\par
liked it, except for the place itself, which made you think of\par
illustrations to weird Dunsany legends in which towering temples climb\par
up unendingly upon each other into black star-speckled skies.  But the\par
Club and London, with Unlimited Bridge and Tango, constituted Margot's\par
idea of earthly happiness.  She never had dreamed of marrying\par
anybody--until Franky had arrived on the scene.\par
\par
Perhaps you can see Franky, with the wholesome tan of the Autumn\par
Manoeuvres yet upon him.  Twenty-seven, well-made and muscular, if with\par
somewhat sloping shoulders and legs of the type that look better in\par
Bedford cords and puttees, or leathers and hunting-tops, than in tweed\par
knickers and woollen stockings, or Court knee-breeches and silks.\par
Observe his well-shaped feet and slight strong hands with pointed\par
fingers, like those of his ancestors, painted by Vandyke; his brown\par
eyes--distinctly good if not glowing with the fire of intellect, his\par
forehead too steep and narrow; his moustache of the regulation\par
tooth-brush kind, adorning the upper-lip that will not shut down firmly\par
over his white, rather prominent, front teeth.  Cap the small rounded\par
skull of him with bright brown hair, brushed and anointed to astonishing\par
sleekness, dress him in the full uniform of a Second Lieutenant in the\par
Bearskins Plain, and you have Franky on his wedding-day.\par
\par
Photographs of the happy couple published in the _Daily Wire_, the\par
_Weekly Silhouette_, the _Lady's Dictatorial_, and the _Photographic\par
Smile_, hardly do the bridegroom justice.  In that without the busby his\par
features are fixed in a painful grin, while in the other there are no\par
features at all.  But Margot--Margot in a hobble-skirt of satin and\par
chiffon, with a tulle turban-veil, starred with orange-flowers in pearls\par
and diamonds, and a long serpent-tail train of silver brocade, hung from\par
her shoulders by ropes of pearls, was "almost too swee," to quote\par
Margot's Club friends.  Search had been made, amongst the said friends,\par
many of whom were married, for a pair of five-year-old pages to carry\par
the bride's train; but there being, for some reason, a dearth of babies\par
among Margot's wedded intimates, the idea had to be given up.\par
\par
The wedding was quite the prettiest function of the season. The eight\par
bridesmaids walked in moss-green _crepe de Chine_ veiled with\par
silver-spotted chiffon.  On their heads were skull-caps of silver\par
tissue, each having a thirty-inch-high aigrette supported by a thin\par
_bandeau_ of gold, set with crystals and olivines, the gift of the\par
bride....  Their stockings were of white lace openwork, the left knee of\par
each being clasped by the bridegroom's souvenir, a garter of gold,\par
crystal, and olivines.  Silver slippers with four-inch heels completed\par
the ravishing effect.\par
\par
_O Perfect Love!_ was sung before the Bishop's Address, and the ceremony\par
concluded with _The Voice that Breathed_ and Stainer's _Sevenfold Amen_.\par
The bridal-party passed down the nave to the strains of the Wedding\par
Chorus from _Lohengrin_.  And there was a reception at the Werkeley\par
Square house of one of the dearest of Margot's innumerable dearest\par
friends, and the happy pair left in their beautiful brand-new\par
Winston-Beeston touring car _en route_ for the old red-brick Hall in\par
Devonshire.  Decidedly the honeymoon might have been termed ideal--and\par
four subsequent months of married life proved tolerably cloudless--until\par
Fate sent a stinging hailstorm to strip the roses from the bridal bower.\par
\par
An unexpected, appalling, inevitable discovery was made in Paris in the\par
_Grande Semaine_, at the end of the loveliest of June seasons.  It\par
utterly ruined--for two people--the Day of the Grand Prix, that marks\par
the climax of the Big Week, when the Parisian coaching-world tools its\par
four-in-hands to Longchamps Racecourse, and the smartest, richest, and\par
gayest people, mustered from every capital of Europe, parade under the\par
chestnut-trees that shade the sunny paddock, to display or criticise the\par
creations of the greatest _couturiers_.\par
\par
Margot had put on an astonishing gown for the occasion.... You will\par
recall that the summer dress designs of 1914 _were_ astonishing; the\par
autumn modes promised to be even more so, according to Babin, Touchet,\par
and the Brothers Paillot.  Skirts--already as short and as narrow as\par
possible--were to be even narrower; the Alpha and Omega of perfection\par
would be represented by the Amphora Silhouette.  And Margot, revolving\par
before her cheval-glass in a sheath of jonquil-coloured silk _lisse_,\par
embroidered with blue-and-green beetle-wings, found--to her horror and\par
consternation----\par
\par
Shall one phrase it that Dame Nature, intent upon her essential,\par
unfashionable business of reproduction, was at variance with Madame\par
Fashion _re_ the Amphora Silhouette? The slender shape was not yet\par
spoilt, but long before the autumn came, no art would mask the wealthy\par
curves of its maternity.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER II*\par
\par
                        *DAME NATURE INTERVENES*\par
\par
\par
"I can't bear it!--I won't bear it!" Margot reiterated. With her tumbled\par
hair, swollen eyes, pink uptilted nose, and the little mouth and chin\par
that quivered with each sobbing breath intaken, she looked absurdly\par
babyish for her twenty years, as she vowed that wild horses shouldn't\par
drag her to Longchamps, and railed against the injustice of Fate.\par
\par
"None of my married friends have had such rotten luck!" she asserted.\par
She stamped upon the velvety carpet and flashed at Franky a glance of\par
imperious appeal.  "Not Tota Stannus, or Cynthia Charterhouse, or Joan\par
Delabrand, or anybody!  Then, why me?  That's what I want to know? After\par
all the mascots I've worn and carried about with me....  Gojo and\par
Jollikins and the jade tree-frog, and the rest! ... Every single one\par
given me by a different woman who'd been married for years and never had\par
a baby! This very day I'll smash the whole lot!"\par
\par
"By the Great Brass Hat! ..."\par
\par
Franky exploded before he could stop himself, and laughed until the\par
tears coursed down.  So "Gojo," the black velvet kitten, and\par
"Jollikins," the fat, leering, naked thing that sat and squinted over\par
its pot-belly at its own huge, shapeless feet, and all the array of\par
gadgets and netsukis crowding Margot's toilette-table and _secretaire_,\par
down to "Pat-Pat," the bog-oak pig, and "Ti-Ti," the jade tree-frog,\par
were so many insurances against the Menace of Maternity.  By Jove! women\par
were regular children.... And Margot ... Nothing but a baby, this poor\par
little Margot--going, in spite of Jollikins and Gojo, to have a baby of\par
her own.\par
\par
"What is one to believe?  Whom is one to trust in? ..."\par
\par
"'Trust in.' ... My best child, you don't mean that you believed those\par
women when they told you that such twopenny gadgets could work charms\par
of--that or any other kind?"\par
\par
"Indeed, indeed they do!  Tota Stannus was _perfectly serious_ when she\par
came to my boudoir one night at the Club, about a week before our--the\par
wedding....  She said--I can hear her now; '_Well, old child, you're to\par
be married on Wednesday, and of course you know the ropes well enough\par
not to want any tips from me....  Still----'_"\par
\par
"That wasn't overwhelmingly flattering," Franky commented, "from a\par
married woman twice your age.  What else did she say?"\par
\par
"She said I must be aware," went on Margot, "that a woman who wanted to\par
keep her friends and her figure, simply couldn't afford to have kids."\par
\par
"And you----"\par
\par
Franky no longer battled with the grin that would have infuriated\par
Margot.  Something had wiped it from his face.\par
\par
"I said she was frightfully kind, but that I was quite\par
well-posted--everything was O.K., and she needn't alarm herself....  And\par
she said, 'Oh! if you've arranged things with Franky, jolly sensible of\par
him!  Too often a man who is open and liberal-minded before marriage\par
develops gerontocracie afterwards, don't you know? ...'  And I told her\par
that you were the very reverse of narrow-minded--and she kissed me and\par
wished me happiness, and went away.  And the maid knocked later on to\par
say Mrs. Stannus sent her apologies for having forgotten to leave her\par
little gift.  And the little gift was, Jollikins.  And my special pals\par
joined in to stand me a farewell dinner, and they drowned my enamel Club\par
badge in a bowl of Maraschino punch, and fished it up and gave me this\par
diamond and enamel one, mounted as a tie-brooch, instead.  And every\par
married woman brought me a mascot....  I had Gojo from Joan Delabrand,\par
and Ti-Ti from Cynthia Charterhouse, and the jade tree-frog from Patrine\par
Saxham, and the carved African bean from Rhona Helvellyn, and----"\par
\par
Franky objected:\par
\par
"Neither Patrine Saxham nor Rhona Helvellyn happen to be married women!"\par
\par
"Perhaps not; but Patrine is an Advanced Thinker, and Rhona Helvellyn is\par
a Militant Suffragist."\par
\par
Franky commented:\par
\par
"As for Suffragists, that Club of yours is stiff with 'em. Gassing about\par
their Cause....  I loathe the noisy crowd!"\par
\par
"Then you loathe me!  I share their convictions!" Margot proclaimed.  "I\par
hold the faith that Woman's Day will dawn with the passing of the Bill\par
that gives us the Vote...."\par
\par
"My best child, you wouldn't know what to do with the Vote if you had\par
it."\par
\par
Margot retorted:\par
\par
"I cannot expect my husband to treat me as a reasonable being while the\par
State classes his wife with infants and imbeciles."\par
\par
It will be seen that a very pretty squabble was on the point of\par
developing.  Fortunately, at this juncture a valet of the chambers\par
knocked at the door to say that a waiter from the restaurant begged to\par
know whether Milord and Miladi would take lunch _a la carte_, or prefer\par
something special in their own apartments?\par
\par
"Tell him no!" wailed Miladi, to the unconcealed consternation of\par
Milord, who had a healthy appetite.\par
\par
"Must keep up your pecker--never say die!"  Franky, stimulated by the\par
pangs of hunger, developed an unsuspected talent for diplomacy.  "Look\par
here!  We must talk over things quietly and calmly.  I'll order a taxi,\par
and we'll chuff to that jolly little restaurant in the Bois de\par
Boulogne--where you can grub in the open air under a rose-pergola--and\par
order something special and odd----"\par
\par
Since Eve's day, this lure has never failed to catch a woman.  Margot\par
began to dry her eyes.  Then she asked Franky to ring.\par
\par
"Three times, please....  That's for Pauline; I want another\par
handkerchief."\par
\par
"Have two or three while you're about it," advised Franky, obeying,\par
returning, and perching on the arm of the settee.  "And bathe your eyes\par
a bit, have a swab-over of the pinky cream-stuff, and a dab of powder."\par
He brushed some pale mealy traces from his right-arm sleeve and\par
coat-lapel, ending, "And put on your swankiest hat and come along to\par
Nadier's."\par
\par
"Could we get anything to eat at Nadier's that we couldn't get here--or\par
in London, at the Tarlton or the Rocroy? ..."\par
\par
"Stacks of things!  For instance--_Canard a la presse_.... They squeeze\par
the juice out of the duck, you twig, with a silver kind of squozzer, and\par
cook it on a chafing-dish under your nose.  Look here! ..."  Franky, now\par
desperate, produced his watch.  "All the cushiest little tables will be\par
taken if you don't look sharp."\par
\par
"Not on the day of the Grand Prix!"\par
\par
Franky retorted, spurred to maddest invention by the pangs of hunger:\par
\par
"My best child, there are about a hundred thousand wealthy Americans in\par
Paris who don't care a red cent about racing, while with most of 'em--to\par
eat _canard a la presse_ at Nadier's in the Bois de Boulogne in the June\par
season--is a--kind of religious rite!"\par
\par
So Margot disappeared to dab her eyes and apply the prescribed touches\par
of perfumed cream and powder, and duly reappeared, crowned with the most\par
marvellous hat that ever promenaded the _ateliers_ of the Maison Blin on\par
the head of a milliner's _mannequin_.\par
\par
You are to imagine the tiny thing and her Franky seated--not in one of\par
the smart automobiles that wait for hire outside Spitz's, but in a\par
little red taxi, borne along with the broad double stream of traffic of\par
every description that ceaselessly roared east and west under the now\par
withering red-and-white blossoms of the chestnut-trees of the Avenue of\par
the Champs Elysees, inhaling the stimulating breezes--flavoured with hot\par
dust and petrol, Seine stink, sewer-gas, coffee, patchouli, fruit, Regie\par
tobacco and roses--of Paris in the end of June.\par
\par
\par
All the world and his wife might be at Longchamps, but here were people\par
enough and to spare.  Luxurious people in costly automobiles or\par
carriages drawn by shiny high-steppers.  People in little public taxis,\par
men and women on motor-bicycles and the human-power kind.  People of all\par
stamps and classes, clustered like bees outside the big, smelly,\par
top-heavy auto-buses, soon to vanish from the Paris avenues and\par
boulevards, with the red and yellow and green-flagged taxis, to play\par
their part in the transport and nourishment of the Army of France.\par
People of all ranks and classes on foot, though as of old the\par
_midinette_ with her big cardboard bandbax, the military cadet, or the\par
student of Art or Medicine, the seminarist and the shaggy-haired and\par
bearded man with the deadly complexion, the slouch hat, the aged\par
_paletot_ and the soiled and ragged crimson necktie that distinguish the\par
milder breed of Anarchist, made up the crowd upon the sidewalks,\par
liberally peppered with the sight-seeing stranger of British, American,\par
or Teuton nationality--the brilliantly-complexioned, gaily-plumaged,\par
loudly-perfumed lady of the pavements; the gendarme and the National\par
Guard, and--with Marie or Jeannette proudly hanging on his\par
elbow--Rosalie in her black-leather scabbard dangling by his side, his\par
crimson _kepi_ tilted rakishly--the blue-coated, red-trousered French\par
infantryman, the _poilu_ whom we have learned to love.\par
\par
The Bois was not seething with fashionable life as it would be towards\par
the sunset hour.  The dandy Clubmen, the smart ladies, had gone to\par
Longchamps with the four-in-hands. Polo was going on near the Pont de\par
Suresnes, the band of a regiment of Cuirassiers was playing in the\par
Jardin d'Acclimatation, and Hungarian zithers and violins discoursed\par
sweet music on a little gilded platform at the axial point of Nadier's\par
open-air restaurant--which is shaped like a half-wheel, with pergolas of\par
shower-roses and Crimson Ramblers radiating from the gilded band-stand\par
to the outer circle of little white tables at which one can lunch or\par
dine in fine weather under a light screen of leaves and blossoms,\par
beneath which the green canvas awnings can be drawn when it comes on to\par
rain.\par
\par
The tables were crowded with French people taking late _dejeuner_, and\par
English, Germans, and German-Americans having lunch.  The gravelled\par
courtyard before the terrace was packed with showy automobiles.\par
\par
If _canard a la presse_ did not grace the meal supplied to Franky and\par
Margot on Nadier's terrace, the _potage printaniere_ and _ecrevisses_\par
and a _blanquette d'agneau_ were exquisitely cooked and served.\par
Asparagus and a salad of endive followed, and by the time they had\par
emptied a bottle of Chateau Yquem and the _omelette soufflee_ had given\par
place to _Peches Melba_, Margot had smiled several times and laughed\par
once.\par
\par
She was so dainty and sweet, so brilliant a little human humming-bird,\par
that the laughing, chattering, feasting crowd of smartly or\par
extravagantly dressed people gathered about the other trellis-screened\par
tables under Nadier's rose-pergola sent many a curious or admiring\par
glance her way.  And Franky was very proud of his young wife, and theirs\par
had been undeniably a love-match; yet in spite of the good dishes and\par
the excellent Chateau Yquem, little shivers of chilly premonition\par
rippled over him from time to time.  He had got to speak out--definitely\par
decline, in the interests of Posterity, to permit interference on the\par
part of Margot's Club circle in his private domestic affairs....  How to\par
do it effectively yet inoffensively was a problem that strained his\par
brain-capacity.  Yet--again in the interests of Posterity--Franky had\par
never previously interested himself in Posterity--the thing had to be\par
done.  He refused Roquefort, buttered a tiny biscuit absently, put it\par
down undecidedly, and as the waiter whisked his plate away--conjured\par
crystal bowls of tepid rose-water and other essentials from space, and\par
vanished in search of dessert--he spoke, assuming for the first time in\par
his five months' experience of connubial life the toga of marital\par
authority.\par
\par
"I think, do you know, Kittums"--Kittums was Margot's pet name--"that it\par
will be best to face the music!"\par
\par
"_Connu!_" Margot shrugged a little, widely opening her splendid brown\par
eyes, "But what music?"\par
\par
"The"--Franky took the plunge--"the cradle-music, if you will have it!"\par
\par
Margot's gasp of dismay, and the indignant fire of a stare that was\par
quenched in brine, awakened Franky to the fact of his having failed in\par
tactics.  The return of the waiter with a pyramid of superb strawberries\par
and a musk-melon on cracked ice alone stemmed the outburst of the\par
pent-up flood of reproach.  Entrenched behind the melon, Franky waited.\par
The waiter again effaced himself, and Margot said from behind another\par
handkerchief:\par
\par
"Oh, how _could_ you! ... I never _dreamed_ that I should _live_ to hear\par
you speak to me in that way."\par
\par
Over the melon, whose rough green quartered rind had delicate white\par
raised traceries all over it, suggesting outline maps of countries in\par
Fairyland, Franky curiously regarded his wife.  He said:\par
\par
"Why are you and all your friends so funky of--what's only a natural\par
phe--what do you call it? ... What do men and women marry for, if it\par
isn't to have--children? ... Perhaps you'll answer me?"\par
\par
"What do people marry for?"  Margot regarded him indignantly over the\par
neglected pyramid of luscious, tempting strawberries, "To--to be happy\par
together--to have a clinking time!"  Her voice shook.  "And this is to\par
be a gorgeous season.  Balls--balls! right on from now to the end of\par
July--then from the autumn all through winter. Period Costume Balls,\par
reviving the modes, music, and manners of Ancient\par
Civilisations--Carthagenian, Assyrian, Babylonian, Gothic--got up and\par
arranged by the Committees of the Cercle Moderne, here in Paris, and in\par
London by the New Style Club....  Tony Guisseguignol and Paul Peigault\par
and their set are busy designing the dresses and decorations--nothing\par
like them will ever have been seen!  And--Peigault says--Tango and the\par
Maxixe are to be chucked to the little cabbages.  A new dance is coming\par
from Sao Paulo that will simply wipe them out....  And now--just when I\par
was looking forward--when everything was to have been so splendid----"\par
\par
The shaking voice choked upon a note of anguish. Franky had picked up\par
the melon, quite unconsciously, and was balancing it.  At this juncture\par
he gripped the green globe with both hands, and said, summoning all his\par
courage to meet the agonised appeal of Margot's tear-drenched eyes:\par
\par
"Look here.  This is--strict Bridge....  Do you loathe 'em--the\par
kiddies--so horribly that the idea of having any is hateful to you?  Or\par
is it--not only the--the veto it puts on larking and kickabout and--the\par
temporary disfigurement--you're afraid of--but the--the--the inevitable\par
pain?"  He glanced round cautiously and looked back again at his wife,\par
saying in a low voice: "Nobody's listening....  Tell me frankly...."  He\par
waited an instant, and then said in an urgent whisper.  "Answer me! ...\par
For God's sake, tell the frozen truth, Margot!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER III*\par
\par
                        *FAIR ROSAMOND'S CHOICE*\par
\par
\par
The terrace under Nadier's roses--dotted with little tables covered with\par
napery, silver, crystal, and china, surrounded with laughing, chattering\par
feasters--the terrace was no longer a scene out of a comedy of the\par
lighter side of Parisian life.... Tragedy, pale and awe-inspiring in her\par
ink-black mantle and purple chiton, had stepped across the gravel in her\par
gold-buckled leather buskins, to offer to the girlish bride--a piece of\par
human porcelain, prinked in the height of the fashion, and lovely--with\par
her wild-rose cheeks and little uptilted nose, her floss-silk hair and\par
wide, dark, lustrous deer-eyes--Fair Rosamond's choice, the dagger or\par
the bowl....\par
\par
"Yes--yes....  It is the ugliness of the thing! ..."  The little mouth\par
was pulled awry as though it had sipped of verjuice.  The tiny hands\par
knotted themselves convulsively, and the colour fled in terror from her\par
face.  "The grotesque ugliness....  And the"--the last two words came as\par
though a pang had wrung them from the pale lips--"the pain--the awful\par
pain!  And besides--my mother died when I was born!"  Margot's voice was\par
a fluttering, appealing whisper; her great eyes were dilated and wild\par
with terror. "Perhaps that is why I am so deadly afraid"--she caught her\par
breath--"but there are heaps, heaps, _heaps_ of married women who\par
fear--_that_--equally!  And they arrange to escape it--I don't know how!\par
... For I knew--nothing--when I married you! ..."  She lifted her great\par
eyes to Franky's, and he realised that it had been so, actually.  "I've\par
been ashamed ever to confess that I was--ignorant about these things!\par
... I've talked a language--amongst other women--that I didn't\par
understand! ..."\par
\par
There are moments when even the shallow-brained become clairvoyant.\par
Franky's love for her made him see clear.  He looked back down the vista\par
of Margot's twenty years of existence, and saw her the motherless\par
daughter of a self-absorbed, cultivated, Art-loving valetudinarian, who\par
habitually spent the chillier part of each year in ranging from French\par
to Italian health-resorts, occupying the spring with Art in\par
Paris--returning to London for June and July, generally spending August\par
and September in Devonshire--to take flight Southwards before the\par
migrating swallows, at the first chill breath of October frosts.\par
\par
Margot had been educated at home, down in Devonshire, by a series of\par
certificated female tutors.  The spinster aunt, the younger sister of\par
her father, extended to her niece for a liberal remuneration a nominal\par
protection and an indifferent care....  And Mr. Mountjohn had died when\par
the girl was sixteen, leaving her unconditionally heiress to his\par
considerable fortune, and the aunt had let Margot have her head in every\par
imaginable way.  She had allowed her to take up her residence at the\par
"Ladies' Social" Club three years subsequently, on the sole condition\par
that a responsible chaperon accompanied Margot to Society functions.\par
Hence, Mrs. Ponsonby Rewes, the irreproachable widow of a late King's\par
Messenger, was evoked from Kensington Tower Mansions upon these\par
occasions--by telephone--to vanish when no longer wanted, in the\par
discreetest and most obliging way.\par
\par
"Poor little Margot! .... Poor little woman!..."  Franky could see how\par
it all had happened by the wild light of the great deer-eyes, so like\par
those in the portrait of the girl's dead mother--half Irish, half Greek\par
by birth.\par
\par
While Franky reflected, the tables had been emptying. People were\par
hurrying away to hear the band of the Jardin d'Acclimatation or to\par
fulfil other engagements of a seasonable kind.  Some remained to smoke\par
and gossip over liqueurs and coffee.  The light blue wreaths of cigar\par
and cigarette smoke curled up towards the awning overhead. Franky\par
mechanically produced his own case and lighted up. And Margot,\par
stretching a slender arm across the table, was saying:\par
\par
"Give me one!--I've forgotten mine! ..."\par
\par
"Ought you? ... Is it wise? ..."  Franky was on the point of asking, but\par
his good Angel must have clapped a hand before his mouth.  He silently\par
gave Margot a thick, masculine Sobranie and supplied a light; and as\par
their young faces neared and the red spark glowed, and the first\par
smoke-wreath rose between the approximating tubes of delicate\par
tobacco-filled paper, his wife whispered as their eyes met:\par
\par
"You're hurt!  But now you know--you're sorry for me, aren't you?"  It\par
was a dragging, plaintive undertone, not at all like Margot's voice.\par
\par
"Frightfully!  All the more because"--Franky drew so hard at his\par
cigarette that it burned one-sidedly--"I can't help being\par
thundering--glad!"\par
\par
"I--see! ..."\par
\par
She breathed out the words with a thin stream of fragrant Turkish vapour\par
crawling over her scarlet under-lip, it seemed to Franky, like a pale\par
blue worm.  And he bit through his Sobranie and threw it on his\par
dessert-plate, saying desperately:\par
\par
"Not yet.  Will you listen quietly to what I've got to say?"\par
\par
She nodded.  Franky launched himself upon the tide of revelation.\par
Nearly everybody who had been eating when he had come into Nadier's with\par
Margot had got up and gone away.  And the Cuirassiers band was playing\par
the love-music from _Samson et Dalila_ on the terrace of the Jardin\par
d'Acclimatation, as melodiously as only a French military band can play.\par
\par
"It's got to do with the Peerage.  Only a Second Afghan War-Earldom\par
dating from 1879--tacked on to the Viscounty they gave my\par
great-grandfather after Badajos--but worth having in its way, or the Dad\par
wouldn't have accepted it.  And, naturally enough--I want a boy to take\par
the Viscounty when I succeed my father, and have the Earldom when I've\par
absquatulated, just as the kiddy'll want one when his own time comes."\par
\par
Margot was burning a strawberry-leaf on her plate with her\par
cigarette-end.  She asked, impressing another little yellow scorched\par
circle on the surface of rough green:\par
\par
"Would it matter so very much if there wasn't any boy?"\par
\par
Franky jumped and turned red to the white, unsunned circle left by the\par
field-cap on the summit of his high forehead.\par
\par
"It would matter--lots!  For my Uncle Sherbrand, a younger brother of my\par
father's, would come in for the Viscounty when I succeeded the dear old\par
Dad.  And my Uncle Sherbrand is a blackguard!  Got cashiered in 1900,\par
when he was an Artillery officer in a gun-testing billet at Wanwich.\par
Kicked out of the Army--in War-time, mind you!--for not backing up his\par
C.O.  And the brute has got a son, too, an apprentice in an engine-shop,\par
if he isn't actually a chauffeur.  Probably the young fellow's\par
respectable, and of course it ain't the pup's fault he's got such a\par
sire.  But my Dad would turn in his grave at the idea of being succeeded\par
by the brother who disgraced him--and as for _his_ grandfather--the\par
jolly old cock 'ud bally well get up and dance, I should say....  So,\par
you see, I can't--sympathise with you as you want me to do in this,\par
darling!  I want you to buck up and be cheerful, and face the music like\par
a brick....  As for what you've told me--about your mother----"  In\par
spite of himself, Franky gulped, and little shiny beads of sweat stood\par
upon his cheeks and temples. "That sort of thing doesn't run in\par
families, like rheumatism"--he was getting idiotic--"or Roman noses!  Be\par
plucky--and everything will turn out all right.  Can't possibly go wrong\par
if we call in Saxham ... Saxham of 000, Harley Street--man my sister\par
Trix simply swears by.  Brought her boy Ronald into the world thirteen\par
years ago, and successfully operated on him for appendicitis only the\par
other day! ..."\par
\par
Margot looked at Franky attentively and bent her head slightly.  Had she\par
understood?  She must have.... Had she tacitly agreed?  Of course....\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER IV*\par
\par
                       *RAYMOND OF THE S. AE. F.*\par
\par
\par
The Masculine Will had conquered.  You had only to be firm with\par
women--bless their hearts! and they caved directly....  Couldn't hold\par
out....  Not built that way....  Franky's sternly-clamped upper-lip\par
relaxed. He beamed as he proposed a noonday stroll in the Bois.  In the\par
direction of the bigger Lake, by one of the narrower avenues, or if\par
Margot preferred a look-in at the Polo Club, another avenue,\par
intersecting the Allee de Longchamps and skirting the enclosure of the\par
Gun Club, would take them there in a jiffy, _via_ Bagatelle.\par
\par
Margot assented to the latter proposition, and, with a little flutter of\par
the lips Franky accepted as a smile, reached for her egret stole, a\par
filmy feathery thing she had removed on entering Nadier's, and drew on\par
her long mousquetaire gloves and pulled down her veil of sunset\par
_chiffon_, half shaded red, merging into jonquil yellow matching the\par
shade of her marvellous gown.  And Franky paid the bill in plump English\par
sovereigns (invariably exchanged as good for louis of twenty francs by\par
the suave and smiling waiter) and tipped the said waiter extravagantly,\par
and took his hat from the second waiter (who invariably starts up by the\par
side of the first when you are going) and tipped him, and got his stick\par
from the third waiter (who came forward with this, and the _en tout cas_\par
of Madame--a lovely thing in the latest dome-shape, of black net over\par
jonquil colour, with a flounce, and an ivory stick, upon the top of\par
which sat a green monkey in olivines, eating a ruby fruit), and lighted\par
another cigarette, and returned the elaborate bow of the manager with a\par
nod of the cheerful patronising order as he followed Margot through the\par
Rambler-wreathed archway leading by a flight of shallow steps from\par
Nadier's terrace to the wide carriage-sweep that links the broad Allee\par
de Longchamps with the narrower Route de Madrid.  And the towering plume\par
of her astonishing hat brought down a shower of red rose-petals as she\par
passed out before him--and Franky, with some of these on his\par
top-hat-brim and others nestling in the front of his waistcoat, was\par
irresistibly reminded of their wedding-day.\par
\par
Unconsciously, Franky and Margot quitted the broader, more frequented\par
avenue, crowded with people in carriages, people in automobiles, people\par
on motor-bicycles and bicyclettes, and followed narrower pathways,\par
stretching between green lawns adorned with shrubberies and clumps of\par
stately forest trees, and chiefly patronised by sweethearting couples,\par
nursemaids in charge of children, children in domineering but\par
affectionate charge of white-haired ladies, while venerable gentlemen\par
dozed on rustic benches over the columns of _Figaro_ or _Paris Midi_.\par
\par
When even these figures became rare, it was borne in upon Franky that he\par
and Margot were not upon a path that led to the Grounds of the Polo\par
Club.  Reluctantly, he admitted himself lost.\par
\par
"Does it matter? ..."  Margot's voice was weary. "If you're absolutely\par
set on it, we could ask one of those men in cocked hats and waxed\par
moustaches and red-and-yellow shoulder-cords to give us the straight\par
tip.  But I don't feel the least bit keen about the Polo Club any more\par
than the Lakes.  These alleys are quiet, and the grass is nice and\par
green.  I vote we go on."\par
\par
"Madame cannot pass this way.  It is not open for strangers."\par
\par
A Republican Guard, a good-looking _sous-officier_, had spoken,\par
comprehending the tone rather than the English words.\par
\par
"Why not?"  Margot's eyes suddenly brightened.  She eagerly sniffed the\par
air of the forbidden avenue.  The corporal, indicating with his\par
white-gloved hand other Republican Guards posted at equal distances down\par
the prohibited alley, and at its intersection with another some two\par
hundred yards distant, brought his eyes back to Margot to answer:\par
\par
"Madame, for the reason that certain military operations are taking\par
place here to-day."\par
\par
"But my husband is an English officer--" Margot was beginning, when\par
Franky, reddening to his hat-brim, exhorted her to be quiet, and the\par
Republican Guard, civilly saluting, stepped upon the grass and moved\par
away.\par
\par
"All the same, you are an English officer," Margot persisted, "and what\par
use is the Entente if that doesn't count?"\par
\par
"Best child, don't be a giddy goose!" Franky implored her.  "You don't\par
suppose the Authorities care a bad tomato for an English Loot--what\par
they'd cotton to would have to be a British Brass Hat of the very\par
biggest kind. Look there!--more to your left, little battums!"  He\par
indicated yet other Republican cocked hats strung at equal distances\par
down the length of a neighbouring alley, precisely outlining the farther\par
border of the sandwich-shaped halfacre of greensward by which their\par
particular avenue ran. "And there!"  His professional eye had noted a\par
big, grey-painted military motor-lorry, numbered, and lettered "S. Ae.\par
F."  Behind the driver's seat towered the slender T-shaped steel mast of\par
a Field wireless, whose spidery aerials, pegged to the turf, were in\par
charge of men in _kepis_ and blue overalls, while a non-commissioned\par
officer, wearing the telephone head-band of the operator, leaned on the\par
elbow-rest of the tripod supporting the apparatus, his finger on the\par
buzzer-key.  Near him his clerk squatted, pencil and pad in readiness,\par
while at a respectful distance from two oblong patches of white in the\par
middle of the green plat of turf, several active upright figures in dark\par
uniforms stood conversing, or walking to and fro.\par
\par
"_Officiers Aviateurs_, telegraphists and mechanics of the French\par
_Service Aeronautique_"--you are listening to Franky--"tremendously\par
well-organised compared with our little footling Flying Corps, tinkered\par
fourteen months ago out of the old Air Battalion of the R. E.  These\par
chaps are Engineers--goin' by the dark red double stripes on their\par
overalls and their dark blue _kepis_.  Some of their machines'll be out\par
for practice.  Despatch-droppin' or bombs.  Here's a man with brass on\par
his hat, coming our way....  Takes me for a German soger-orficer I\par
shouldn't wonder!--lots of 'em get their clothes cut in Bond Street.\par
But though you can hide Allemand legs in English trousers"--Franky was\par
recovering his customary cheeriness--"and some of 'em do it uncommon\par
cleverly--you can't deodorise an accent that hails from Berlin."\par
\par
The officer approaching--a youthful, upright figure walking quickly,\par
with the short, springy steps of a man much in the saddle--proved to be\par
grey-haired and grey-moustached. The double-winged badge of his Service\par
was embroidered in gold upon the right sleeve of his tunic, and upon the\par
collar, a single wing in this case, ending in a star.  He carried\par
binoculars suspended from his neck by a rolled-leather thong, and a\par
revolver in a black-leather case was attached to the belt about his\par
middle.  There was thick white dust upon the legs and uppers of his high\par
polished black boots, which the grass had scoured from the toes and\par
soles.  His bright blue-grey eyes ran over Franky as the slight\par
soldierly salute was exchanged.  He said, speaking in excellent English:\par
\par
"If Monsieur, the English officer, will obligingly mention his name,\par
rank, and regiment, it might be possible to allow him to continue his\par
promenade with Madame, the invention we are testing being the patent of\par
his countryman, and already familiar to the Authorities at the British\par
War Office."\par
\par
Thus coerced, Franky produced his card, Margot dimpled into smiles, the\par
polite officer saluted again, introduced himself as Raymond,\par
Capitaine-Commandant pilot of the --th _escadrille_, wheeled and walked\par
away.  But he returned to say, this time directly addressing Margot:\par
\par
"Should Madame la Vicomtesse desire to witness the test of her\par
countryman's--apparatus, there can be no objection to her doing so.  But\par
that Madame should keep clear of the vicinity of the"--he pointed to the\par
two oblong strips of white canvas adorning the middle of the expanse of\par
green,--"the signal, intended for the guidance of the aviator, is of\par
absolute necessity, Madame must understand!"\par
\par
"There won't be any...?" Margot was beginning, nervously.\par
\par
"_Mais non, Madame_.  _Pas d'explosion_," the officer assured her, and\par
stiffened to attention facing eastwards, and scanning the sky with eyes\par
that blinked in the dazzling glare of early noon.  For the droning whirr\par
of a plane just then reached them, drowning the sign of the hot south\par
breeze that rustled in the tops of the acacias and oaks, ilexes and\par
poplars, that rose about the arena of open ground....\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER V*\par
\par
                           *THE BIRD OF WAR*\par
\par
\par
"The _avion_ comes from Drancy."  The speaker looked back at Margot as\par
he focussed his binoculars.  "It is not one of our Army machines, but a\par
British monoplane built by your countryman and fitted with the invention\par
whose usefulness we are here to test."  He continued: "Should the\par
_officier-pilote_ in charge of the--apparatus--and who for the time\par
being represents an enemy--succeed in poising"--he hesitated a bare\par
instant--"for a stipulated number of moments over the target--those two\par
lengths of white canvas approximating on the grass represent the\par
target--he scores a bull's-eye."\par
\par
He blinked a little, and before Franky's mental vision rose the\par
aggregation of Government buildings near the _Carrefour des Cascades_,\par
marked "_Magazins et depots_" on Baedeker's maps.\par
\par
"He scores a bull's eye," resumed the speaker.  "He has already paid one\par
visit of the requisite duration to an address near the Porte\par
d'Aubervilliers."  Franky had a mental vision of the array of big,\par
bloated gasometers pertaining to the Strasbourg Railway Yards.  "He has\par
made a similar call at a point indicated between the station of the\par
Batignolles and the station of the Avenue de Clichy"--the well-preserved\par
teeth of the officer showed under the grey moustache as he smiled, and\par
Franky had another vision of the huge _Gare aux Marchandises_ tucked in\par
the angle between the Railway of the Geinture and the Western Railway\par
lines, as the speaker went on suavely "and the target succeeding this\par
will be the last.  It is situated on the Champ de Manoeuvres at Issy.\par
The wireless-telegraph operator of my _escadrille_ informs me that two\par
bull's eyes have already been registered--which for your countryman's\par
invention presages well."\par
\par
Franky, with British plumpness, queried:\par
\par
"And the invention?  Some new bomb-dropping device--planned to get rid\par
of the way the engine always puts on 'em?  If the English\par
inventor-fellow has done that, his goods are worth buying, I should\par
say!"\par
\par
Raymond, _Capitaine-Commandant_, answered as the droning song from the\par
sky grew louder:\par
\par
"Of certainty, Monsieur, if his invention prove worth buying, my\par
Government will undoubtedly purchase what has already been unavailingly\par
offered to yours.  It is our custom to examine and test, closely and\par
exhaustively, new things that are offered.  But what would you?  We seek\par
the best for France."\par
\par
"He isn't flying his aeroplane himself, is he?  Or working his own\par
invention, whatever it may be?"\par
\par
"But no, Madame!  One of our* Officiers-Aviateurs* is acting as pilot, a\par
skilled mechanic of our Service occupies the observer's place.  Despite\par
the Entente Cordiale--the happy relations prevailing between my country\par
and England--it would hardly be _convenable_ or discreet to permit even\par
an Englishman"--the tone of graceful, subtle irony cannot be conveyed by\par
pen or type--"_even_ an Englishman to fly over Paris, or any other\par
fortified city of France.  But see!  In the sky to the north-east--above\par
that silvery puff of vapour--arrives now the _avion_ built and\par
christened by your countryman."\par
\par
Margot asked, narrowing her beautiful eyes as she searched out the\par
darkish speck upon the hot blue background:\par
\par
"The plane, you mean.  What does he call it?"\par
\par
Raymond answered without removing his eyes from his binoculars:\par
\par
"Madame, he calls it 'The Bird of War.'"\par
\par
The tuff-tuff of a motor-cycle sounded faintly in the distance, as the\par
resonant vibrating noise of the aeroplane came more triumphantly out of\par
the hot blue sky.  Save for a scintillating white reflection to the\par
north that might have been the crystal dome of the great big Palm House\par
in the Jardin d'Acclimatation, and that unavoidable, useful ugliness,\par
the gilded lantern of the Tour Eiffel, thrusting up into the middle\par
distance over the delicately-rounded masses of new foliage upon the\par
right-hand looking east, the glory and shame and magnificence and\par
squalor of the Queen City of Cities might have lain a hundred leagues\par
away, so ringed-in by delicate austere brown of serried tree-trunks,\par
rising above rich clumps of blossoming lilac, syringa, yellow azalea,\par
and pink, mauve, and snowy rhododendron, was the spacious green arena\par
wherein Franky and Margot were destined to play their part.\par
\par
Now, followed by the wide-winged shadow that the sun of high noon threw\par
almost directly beneath her, darkening drifting cloud, and open city\par
spaces, passing over breasting tree-tops and wide stretches of municipal\par
greensward, the Bird of War drew nearer and more near....  And glancing\par
up as the portentous flying shadow suddenly blotted out the sunlight,\par
Franky realised that the two-seater monoplane was hovering, and buzzing\par
as she hovered, like a Brobdingnagian combination of kite-hawk,\par
dragon-fly, and bumblebee.\par
\par
He pulled out a pair of vest-pocket field-glasses and scanned her as she\par
hung there, gleaming in the sunlight, at a height of perhaps five\par
hundred feet above the white cloths on the grass.  He could make out the\par
Union Jack on her underwings, the huge black raking capitals of her name\par
BIRD OF WAR painted on the side of the tapering canvas-covered fuselage,\par
the diamond-shaped tail swaying between the pendant flaps of the huge\par
triangular elevators, clearly as though these features had been filmed\par
upon the screen. In a curious misty circle, spinning under the fuselage,\par
he suspected lay the secret of her kite-like poise and hover, and behind\par
his immaculate waistcoat he was sensible of a thrill.\par
\par
If the English inventor had not solved the baffling Problem of\par
Stability, he had come uncommonly near it, by the Great Brass Hat!  And\par
the dud-heads at Whitehall had shown the door to him and his invention.\par
"Good Christmas!--how like 'em!" reflected Franky, lowering the glasses\par
to chuckle, and looking round for Margot.\par
\par
There she was, some twenty yards distant, planted right in the middle of\par
the avenue, lost to the wide in rapt contemplation of the hovering\par
aeroplane.\par
\par
"Kitts!" he called, but she did not hear, or disdained to pay attention.\par
He tried to call again, but his mouth dried up and his feet seemed\par
rooted to the ground.  For, swinging round the turf-banked corner of the\par
avenue at its junction with another, charging at a terrific pace down\par
upon the little brilliant creature, came a whity-brown figure on a\par
motorcycle, the frantic honking of its horn and the racket of its\par
engine's open throttle mingling deafeningly with the tractor's roar.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER VI*\par
\par
                              *SHERBRAND*\par
\par
\par
The frantic honking of the pneumatic horn was lost in the crashing\par
collision of earth and metal.  Franky, pallid and damp with\par
apprehension, reassured himself by a rapid glance that Margot was safe\par
and sound.  The aeroplane had ceased buzzing and hovering, headed\par
southwards, and floated on, trailing her shadow, leaving the traces of\par
her passage in a smear of brown earth indicating a vicious slash made by\par
the right-side foot-rest of a motor-cycle in the greensward, conserved\par
and sacred to the French Republic--the upset machine to which the\par
foot-rest appertained, and an angry young man in dusty overalls, sitting\par
in the middle of the raked-up avenue.\par
\par
"You've had a spill! ..." Franky heard himself saying.\par
\par
"Yes....  I have had a spill--thanks to that young lady!"\par
\par
The dusty young man's tone was frankly savage; he regarded the brilliant\par
little figure in the distance with a scowl of resentment as he gathered\par
himself up from the gravel, and dabbed at a jagged, oozing cut on his\par
prominent chin with a handkerchief of Isabella hue.  "The brake-handle\par
did that," he curtly explained, more for his own benefit than apologetic\par
Franky's.  But he looked full in the flushed and dewy countenance of\par
Margot's lord as he added:\par
\par
"If I'd killed her, a French jury would have found that she deserved\par
it!--running like a corncrake across the avenue when I was scorching up\par
at top speed! ..."\par
\par
"I know," Franky stammered.  "I--I see how it all happened.  You had to\par
steer slap into the bank--to save my--my wife's life.  How can I\par
apologise? ... You see, she was crazy about the aeroplane....  She'd\par
been warned to keep well out of the way--you know what women are! ..."\par
\par
"Oh, as to that! ..."  The dusty young man, moving with a perceptible\par
limp, went to the prone motor-cycle, stood it up on its bent stand with\par
one twist of his big-boned wrist, and began to examine into its\par
injuries.  "Not much wrong," he said to himself, and straightened his\par
back, and in the act of throwing a leg over the saddle, felt Franky's\par
restraining grip upon his arm.\par
\par
"You don't go until my wife has thanked you!"  Franky's upper-lip was\par
Rhadamanthine.  "Margot!" he called, in a tone of authority such as he\par
had never previously heard from his own mouth; "Come here at once,\par
please!  I want to speak to you!"\par
\par
The fluttering little figure waved a hand to him.  The gay little voice\par
called back:\par
\par
"Yes....  Oh!--but look at them! ... Can they be going?  Why, I believe\par
they are! ..."\par
\par
The canvas strips had been rolled up by a mechanician of the Service\par
Aeronautique, and stowed away behind the big grey telegraph-car, in the\par
recesses of which the telescopic steel mast and aerials of the wireless\par
had been snugly tucked away.  The mechanics in _kepis_ and overalls had\par
stowed themselves away inside the _camion_; the wireless operator, a\par
kepi having replaced his headband, was acting as chauffeur. And,\par
occupying the front seat beside a junior officer, who piloted a second,\par
smaller car, Raymond, _Capitaine-Commandant pilot_ of the --th\par
escadrille of France's Service Aeronautique gave the signal for\par
departure with an upward wave of his hand.  Then, with some sharp,\par
staccato trills of a whistle and the double honk of a pneumatic horn,\par
the car of the commandant turned and sped down the avenue, followed by\par
the tractor-waggon; and both were lost to view.\par
\par
"But--they're gone! ... And--and the aeroplane...." Margot gasped out\par
the words in amazed discomfiture, sending her eyes after a dwindling\par
shape beating down the sky to the southward, and straining her ears to\par
catch the last of the tractor's whirring song.\par
\par
"Nearly at Issy, I should calculate--travelling at eighty miles an hour.\par
Impossible now to catch up with her in time to see her do the last\par
stunt.  Can choose my own pace for going, anyhow," said the\par
motor-cyclist ruefully.  "Nothing left to do but take the Bird over and\par
fly her back to the Drancy hangar."\par
\par
He tried to laugh, but his wrung face gave the lie to the plucky\par
pretence of indifference.  He went on, still doggedly mopping away at\par
his bleeding chin:\par
\par
"I was lucky in getting a hearing on this side of the Channel.  The\par
bigwigs at Whitehall simply referred me to the Superintendent of the\par
Royal Aircraft Factory at Frayborough, and as I'd tried him twice\par
already, I knew what _he'd_ got to say.  The Commander of the Central\par
School of Military Aviation was a brick--I'll say that for him.  He sent\par
a French flying officer to look me up at Hendon, who got me in touch\par
with the Inventions Bureau of their _Service Aeronautique_....  Well!\par
the big test's over by this time.  I shall know my fate in a week or\par
two--or possibly in a year?"\par
\par
"Oh!  You don't mean----"\par
\par
The horrified cry broke from Margot.  Franky yelled:\par
\par
"By the Great Brass Hat! ... You're the inventor! The whole thing was\par
your show!"\par
\par
"Yes, I'm the inventor," the tanned young man in the dusty overalls\par
answered rather contemptuously: "What did you take me for? ... A French\par
medical student having a joy-ride, or a _commis voyageur_?"\par
\par
"Can't say.  Never thought! ... Fact is--my wife had frightened me\par
horribly.  When your machine bore down on her--posted right in the\par
middle of the gravel--I was scared stiff--give you my honour!--you might\par
have sunk a brace of Dreadnoughts in the palms of my hands!"\par
\par
Franky made this absurd statement with so sincere an air, and clinched\par
it so effectually by displaying a lovely silk-cambric handkerchief in a\par
state of soppy limpness, that the abrased inventor nearly laughed.\par
\par
But his thick, silvery, fair eyebrows settled into a straight line\par
across his tanned forehead.  He said with a directness that seemed to\par
belong to his lean, keen, hatchet-faced type:\par
\par
"Once more, I am glad that no harm has happened to the lady.  The delay\par
caused by the--mishap can hardly have prejudiced my success.  For all I\par
know, the test of my hoverer may have favourably impressed the judges.\par
If it has done otherwise I have no right to blame man, dog, or devil,\par
for a failure that may be my own."\par
\par
He lifted his goggled cap to Margot with a good air, pulled it down, and\par
was in the act of lowering the visor, when Margot's voice arrested the\par
big-boned hand.  That voice Franky knew could be wonderfully coaxing.\par
It pleaded now, soft as the sigh of a Mediterranean breeze:\par
\par
"Whether the test is successful or isn't, will you promise that we shall\par
hear from you? ..."\par
\par
"Good egg!" joined in Franky.  "Do let us know! ... We're stopping at\par
the Spitz, Place Vendome."  He warmed and grew expansive in the light of\par
Margot's smile of approval.  "Drop in on us there," he urged, "as soon\par
as you've found out.  Come and dine with us in any case.... No!--we're\par
engaged to-night, but come and lunch at two sharp to-morrow, and tell us\par
all about your hoverer over a bottle of Bubbly.  Suite 10, Second Floor.\par
Name of Norwater.  Stick this away to remind you," he ended, tendering\par
his card.\par
\par
"You're awfully good.  But at the same time I hardly----"\par
\par
The voice broke off.  A glance at the proffered pasteboard had dyed the\par
inventor flaming scarlet from the collar of his dusty gabardine to the\par
edges of his goggled cap.  He dropped the card quietly upon the gravel,\par
and said, looking Franky straight between the eyes:\par
\par
"Even if I were able to accept I'd have to decline your invitation.  My\par
name's Sherbrand--I'm your Uncle Alan's son."  He settled himself in the\par
saddle and finished before he pulled up the starting-lever.\par
"Understand--I'd no idea who you were until I saw the name on your card.\par
It has been a queer encounter--I can't say a pleasant one.  Let me end\par
it by saying 'Good-day!' ..."\par
\par
Franky's new-found cousin touched the goggled cap and pulled up the\par
starting-lever.  With the customary bang and snort, the motor-bicycle\par
leaped away.  Margot had uttered a little gasp at the moment of\par
revelation.  Now she turned great eyes of dismay on Franky, and withdrew\par
them quickly. For Franky's eyes had become circular and poppy, his mouth\par
tried to shape itself into a whistle, but his expression was merely\par
vacuous.  He continued to explode with "Great Snipe!" at intervals, as\par
he and Margot made their way back to more populous avenues, chartered a\par
fortuitously passing taxi, and were driven back via the Porte Dauphine\par
to Spitz's gorgeous caravanserai in the Place Vendome, when Margot\par
vanished into her own bower, sending her French maid to intimate to\par
Milord that Miladi would take tea alone in that apartment, and did not\par
intend to dine.\par
\par
\par
Thus Franky, relieved from duty, presently found himself, in company\par
with a cigar, strolling bachelor-fashion through the streets of Paris.\par
No very clear recollection stayed with him of how he spent the\par
afternoon.  At one time he found himself with his features glued against\par
the plate-glass window of a celebrated establishment dedicated to the\par
culture and restoration of feminine beauty, contemplating divers gilt\par
wigs on stands--porcelain pots of marvellous unguents, warranted to\par
eliminate wrinkles; sachets of mystic herbs to be immersed in baths;\par
creams guaranteed to impart to the most exhausted skin the velvety\par
freshness of infancy.\par
\par
Later he strayed into a sunny, green-turfed public garden, full of white\par
statues, sparkling fountains, and municipal seats whereon Burgundian,\par
Dalmatian, and Alsatian wet-nurses dandled or rocked or nourished their\par
infant charges, and bonnes or governesses presided over the gambols of\par
older babies, who played with belled Pierrots, or toy automobiles, or\par
inflated balls of gorgeous hues.\par
\par
There is nothing profoundly moving in the sight of a stout, beribboned\par
wet-nurse suckling her employer's infant.  But into the company of these\par
important hirelings came quite unconsciously a young working-woman in a\par
shabby brown merino skirt and a blouse of white Swiss.  Her shining\par
black hair was uncovered to the sunshine.  On one arm she carried a\par
bouncing baby, on the other a basket containing cabbages and onions, and\par
a flask of cheap red wine, which receptacle its owner, having taken the\par
other end of the seat Franky occupied, set down between herself and the\par
young man.  She was a healthy, plump young woman with too pronounced a\par
moustache for beauty.  But when, having methodically turned the baby\par
upside down to rearrange some detail of its scanty dress, she reversed\par
it and bared her breast to the eager mouth, a strange thrill went\par
through Franky.  A dimness came before his vision, and it was as though\par
those dimpled hands plucked at his heart.  He suffered a sudden\par
revulsion strange in a young man so modern, up-to-date, and beautifully\par
tailored.  He knew that he longed for a son most desperately.  And the\par
devil of it was--Margot did not.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER VII*\par
\par
                           *THE CONSOLATRIX*\par
\par
\par
Thus, Franky got up and moved away, driven by the stinging cloud of\par
thoughts that pursued and battened on him, and presently found himself\par
following a stream of people up a flight of marble steps, and under an\par
imposing portico that ended in a turnstile and a National Collection of\par
Paintings and Sculptures.\par
\par
Wandering through a maze of long skylighted galleries where the\par
master-works of Modern Art are conserved and cherished, he was to\par
encounter the thought that haunted him in a myriad of images, wrought by\par
the chisel, the brush, the burin, and the graving-tool in marble or\par
bronze, upon canvas or panel, in ivory, or silver, or enamel, or gold.\par
\par
A sculptured Hagar mourning by the side of her dying Ishmael caught his\par
eye as he entered the first gallery. Farther on, Eve after the Fall\par
lifted the infant Cain to receive the kiss of Adam, homing to his shack\par
of green branches at the end of the labouring day.  And a shag-thighed,\par
curly-horned Pan romped with a litter of sturdy bear-cubs, and\par
medallions and panels of childhood were everywhere.\par
\par
It was the same in the galleries devoted to painting.  A Breton\par
christening-party, depicted with the roughness that hides consummate\par
mastery of technique, trudged along a snowy coast-road towards a little\par
chapel near the seashore. The young mother in her winged starched cap\par
and bodice of black velvet, yet pale from the ordeal of anguish, walked\par
between her smiling gossips, carrying her new-born infant,\par
chrysalis-like in its linen swaddlings, to be made into a good Christian\par
by M. le Cure.  And seated on a broken throne of red granite beneath the\par
towering propylaeum of a ruined Egyptian temple, whose colonnades of\par
lotus columns, and walls painted with processions of hierophants\par
offering incense to bird or beast-headed deities, and bewigged dancers\par
and musicians ministering to the pleasures of long-eyed kings, receded\par
down long perspectives into distance, a Woman, young and slender and\par
draped in a long blue cloak over a white robe, gazed downwards at a\par
naked Child sleeping upon her knees.  And about the downy temples of the\par
Child shone a slender ring of mystic brightness, and another, more\par
faint, haloed the chastely beautiful head of the Mother bending above.\par
\par
Another canvas, austere and gorgeous, with the marvellous blues and\par
emeralds and rich deep crimsons of old Byzantine ornament in relief\par
against a background of dull tawny gold, showed the same maternal\par
figure, far older and in darker draperies, seated upon a chair of\par
wrought ivory upon a dais, looking outward and upward with deep eyes of\par
unfathomable tenderness and sorrow, and pale hands lifted in\par
supplication to that Heaven whither Her Son ascended after His Victory\par
over Death.  Across the knees of the Consolatrix Afflictorum a mourning\par
mother lay prone and tearless.  And at the feet of the Virgin,\par
outstretched amidst the scattered petals of some fallen roses, you saw\par
the nude, beautiful body of a male child of some three years old.\par
\par
\par
But little of the inner meaning of Bouguereau's great picture filtered\par
through Franky's honest brown eyes to the mind that lay somewhere behind\par
them.  But he realised that for the grieving woman who had borne a son\par
and lost him there was no more joy in the world.\par
\par
The Child of that Woman upon whose knees she leaned her breaking heart\par
had lived to attain to the perfect ripeness of glorious Manhood.  But\par
then....  Franky followed the lines of the dark, downward-drifting veil\par
up to the rapt Mother-face with the sorrowful, close-folded mouth and\par
the deep, fathomless eyes, and remembered what had happened to Her Son.\par
\par
\par
"Beg pardon!" he found himself muttering between his teeth.  His hand\par
went up, and he had bared his sleek brown head before he knew.  This\par
wasn't a Roman Catholic Church, anyway ... there was no obligation even\par
to appear respectful; France had long ago kicked over the traces of\par
Religion--all French people were Freethinkers in these days.  Telling\par
himself this, Franky did not replace the shiny topper.  One rapid glance\par
to right and left had shown him that the gallery was nearly empty; the\par
few visitors it contained were too far distant to have observed the\par
action.  Except, possibly, one person, a lean, short, elderly man in\par
shabby black, who stood some paces behind, a little to the left of\par
Franky, holding a shovel-brimmed round-crowned beaver with both hands\par
against his sunken chest as he gazed with bright, absorbed eyes at the\par
wonderful rapt face of the Consoler; his lips moving rapidly as he\par
whispered to himself, not breaking off or twitching a muscle because\par
Franky had glanced round:\par
\par
Franky glanced round again, and this time encountered the oddly young\par
eyes of his neighbour, looking from a brown, deeply wrinkled visage\par
framed in thickly growing, straight black hair, heavily streaked with\par
white.\par
\par
"Monsieur is a lover of Art?"\par
\par
Undoubtedly a Frenchman, he addressed Franky in cultured English, with a\par
tone and manner excellently graced. The vivid clearness of his\par
amber-coloured eyes, set in the now smiling mask of walnut-brown\par
wrinkles, was attractive. And Franky answered, unconsciously warming to\par
the look and smile:\par
\par
"Must say I hardly know.  Things that clever, intellectual people go\par
into raptures over, bore me simply stiff.  Other things--things they\par
howl down--go straight to the spot, you see.  And all I can say when I'm\par
hauled over the coals for liking rubbish is, that the rubbish is good\par
enough for this child."\par
\par
"I comprehend.  Monsieur has the courage of his convictions. It is a\par
quality rare in these days.  And--this painting particularly appeals to\par
Monsieur?  May one be pardoned for asking why?"\par
\par
The voice was suave, but it somehow compelled an answer. Franky, with an\par
indistinct remembrance of _viva voce_ examinations awakening in him,\par
cleared his throat and fell back a pace or two....  Well set up and\par
well-bred, well-groomed and well-dressed, his figure, beside that other\par
in the priestly soutane of rusty alpaca, short enough to reveal coarse\par
ribbed stockings of black yarn, and cracked prunella shoes with worn\par
steel buckles, made a contrast sufficiently quaint to provoke a stare of\par
curiosity, had any observer passed just then.  But standing together on\par
the beeswaxed floor at the upper end of the long, bright, skylighted\par
gallery, the Guardsman and his temporary acquaintance were as private as\par
it is possible to be in a public place.\par
\par
Thus, at the cost of a heightened complexion and an occasional stammer,\par
Franky explained himself.  The painting appealed to him because it\par
recalled a Bible story--made familiar to Franky by reason of having\par
swotted it at School for Sunday Ques. with other fellows of the Fifth in\par
Greyshott's time.  Also, on the wind-up Sunday of his, Franky's, Last\par
Term, having passed for the Army with the dev--hem!--of a lot of\par
trouble--a beastly epidemic of diphtheria and scarlet fever having\par
broken out among the children of the Windsor poor, the Head had preached\par
from the text in Big Chapel.  And the text went something like this:\par
\par
"_A Voice in Rama was heard, of lamentation and mourning: Rachel\par
bewailing her children: and would not be comforted because they are\par
not._"\par
\par
The haggard, beautiful, tearless Rachel of the picture hadn't bucked at\par
the disfigurement and the pain and the danger of child-bearing.  She had\par
welcomed them for the sake of the kid....  It was a thundering pity he\par
hadn't lived--in Franky's opinion; "woman jolly well deserved to have\par
been let keep that clinking fine boy to rear."\par
\par
"I comprehend."  The clear eyes flashed into Franky's, the withered\par
brown mask was alight with sympathetic intelligence.  "To Monsieur, an\par
English officer and a member of the Protestant Church of England, that\par
woman who leans her bursting heart upon the knees of the Mother of\par
Consolation is Rachel."  He quoted:\par
\par
"'_Vox in Rama audita est, ploratus el ululatus: Rachel plorans filios\par
suos: et noluit consolari, quia non sunt._'"\par
\par
"That's it!" Franky nodded, admitting candidly: "Though I always was a\par
duffer at Latin, and we weren't taught at School to pronounce it--quite\par
in that way."\par
\par
Said the clear-eyed old man, whose dark wrinkled throat displayed no\par
edge of linen above the plain circular collar of the soutane, only a\par
significant border of purple from which two widish lappets of the same\par
colour depended beneath the peaked and mobile chin, and who might have\par
been a prelate of sorts, had it not been understood of simple Franky\par
that the State had abolished the Catholic religion and banished all\par
priests, monks, and nuns from France.\par
\par
"The Italianate Latin puzzles you....  It is--slightly different to the\par
Latin they taught you at Eton?  _Hein_? When I lived in England--not so\par
long ago--I counted several brave Eton fellows among my acquaintances.\par
And their mental attitude with regard to the language of Virgil, Horace,\par
and Tacitus was precisely that of Monsieur."\par
\par
He chuckled, and his oddly young eyes twinkled quite gaily as he pulled\par
out a battered little silver snuff-box and helped himself, wrinkling his\par
thin hooked nose with evident enjoyment.  As he dusted the pungent brown\par
grains from his lappets with a coarse blue-checked cotton handkerchief,\par
an amethyst ring on the wrinkled hand flashed pink and violet in the\par
light.\par
\par
"To Monsieur who is doubtless familiar with the Scriptures in Tyndall's\par
translation, I might suggest that the Latin of the Ancient Romans should\par
be pronounced in the Roman style!  But Monsieur will pardon this tone of\par
the pedagogue. I will not 'bore you stiff' with a classical\par
disquisition. Permit me to thank you for your amiable compliance with\par
the request of an old man, and to wish you good-day."\par
\par
He combined apology, farewell, and dismissal in a courtly little bow,\par
and as though undoubting that the other would pass on, plunged again\par
into the picture.  But Franky lingered to say, awkwardly:\par
\par
"Perhaps ... If you don't mind...."\par
\par
"_Hein_? ..."\par
\par
The keen eyes reverted to his embarrassed face instantly.\par
\par
"What if I do not mind? ... There is something you desire to ask me?"\par
\par
"Well, yes!" Franky admitted.  "Don't quite pipe why, but I rather\par
cotton to hearing your version....  Of the meaning of that picture, you\par
know! ..."\par
\par
"Yes--yes!  I understand! ..."  The vivid eyes flashed piercingly into\par
Franky's, and leaped back to the great glorious canvas within the\par
stately frame.  "To you who were once a boy at Eton that woman who has\par
no more tears to shed is Rachel of Rama....  To me, once Seminarist of\par
the Institut Catholique, as to others of my holy faith and sacred\par
calling--she is France--our beloved France, who leans upon the knees and\par
against the bosom of the Catholic Church in her bereavement--mourning\par
with anguish unutterable her children who are dead....  Dead to Faith,\par
dead to the Spiritual Life--members separated from the Body of Christ by\par
their own choice as by the act of Government.  Lost!--unless the ray of\par
Divine Grace find and touch them in their self-made darkness, and they\par
repent, and turn themselves to Christ again!"\par
\par
Franky said, with wholly lovable banality:\par
\par
"Rather sweepin', but natural conclusion, from a religious point o'\par
view.  Still, when a whole nation gets up like one man and bally well\par
chucks a Religion, there must be something jolly off-colour and\par
thundering rotten about that Religion, don't you know?"\par
\par
"A whole nation!"\par
\par
The bright eyes held Franky's sternly.  He lifted his right arm, and the\par
withered hand still shut upon the battered snuff-box shot up two fingers\par
in vigorous protest.  "Pardon, Monsieur--you are very seriously\par
mistaken.  France was never more Catholic at heart than now.  How\par
strange!--when but twenty-one miles of salt water divide Calais from\par
Dover--when the Entente Cordiale has established between your country\par
and mine nominally close and intimate relations; that so complete an\par
ignorance as to the French Nation, its Government, its mode of thought,\par
its moral, religious, and social conditions, should be found prevailing\par
in Great Britain to-day!"\par
\par
"My dear sir, you're off the bull--completely off!" protested\par
Franky--Franky whose second sister was married to a Frenchman, Franky\par
who knew Paris as well as the inside of his week-end suit-case, by Jove!\par
\par
A deprecating shrug and a supple outstretched hand cut short the\par
speaker.\par
\par
"Pardon, Monsieur l'Anglais--I know what you would say to me!  There is\par
much force in the argument....  It is _tres sensee_--and there is truth\par
in it, and yet it is false--to be guilty of a paradox.  The aristocracy\par
of Great Britain, like her plutocracy, set high value upon much that\par
comes from France.  British gold is poured into my country in return for\par
the newest and most fanciful modes in costume, millinery, and jewellery.\par
And not only do your beautiful women adorn themselves with the\par
inventions of our bold and original genius for ornament, but for your\par
_menus_, your pleasures, the novels and plays that paint in intoxicating\par
colours the joys of unchaste love and illicit passion, for the sensuous\par
poetry that is garlanded with the flame-hued flowers of Evil, you are\par
ready to praise and pay us lavishly, as though no nobler growth than\par
this rank luxuriance sprang from the intellectual soil of France.  Our\par
vices--alas!--with the appalling diseases that spring from them, and the\par
combinations of drugs that alleviate these--all find with you a ready\par
market.  And you attend our race-meetings at Longchamps and Auteuil,\par
where English jockeys ride French and Irish horses--and you believe,\par
you!--that you know the social life of France.  No!--but you are\par
ignorant--profoundly ignorant!  May GOD be thanked that you misjudge us\par
thus cruelly.  For if my country were no better than Great Britain and\par
other foreign nations believe her to be, it were time indeed for a rain\par
of fire from Heaven!"\par
\par
Hardly raising his voice above a clear whisper, the emotion and\par
vehemence with which he spoke, and the swift and fiery gesticulations\par
with which he illustrated utterance, made the sweat start out in beads\par
upon his wrinkled forehead and cheeks.  He wiped these off with the blue\par
checked handkerchief, saying:\par
\par
"Pardon!  I grow warm when I speak of these things. I recognise that if\par
in the judgment of other nations France is a courtesan drunk with\par
lechery, or at the best _un esprit follet_, she has brought this\par
judgment upon herself.  Flippancy, the desire to _faire de l'esprit_\par
under any circumstances--the bold and brilliant gaiety that is her\par
exclusive and most beautiful characteristic--these have caused her to be\par
misunderstood.  But whatever else she be, she is not Pagan nor Agnostic.\par
To believe that is to wrong her cruelly, Monsieur!"\par
\par
Franky, by now hopelessly at sea, endured the hailstorm of swift,\par
vehement sentences with an expression of amiable vacuity, his stiffly\par
pendent hands plainly yearning for the refuge of his trousers pockets,\par
his mind rocking on the waves of the stranger's passionate eloquence\par
like a toy yacht adrift on the bosom of the Atlantic.  And the resonant\par
Gallic voice went on:\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER VIII*\par
\par
                             *MONSEIGNEUR*\par
\par
\par
"The masters of France to-day are hostile to Christianity. They are\par
Freemasons (Freemasonry in England is not Freemasonry as it is\par
understood here); they are Freethinkers, Socialists, Internationalists,\par
and Hedonists, the avowed enemies of the Catholic Faith.  Hence,\par
churches, seminaries, and schools have been closed by Government,\par
communities of religious men and women have been uprooted and exiled.\par
Priests have been banished, ecclesiastical and private property has been\par
appropriated and confiscated, churches have been desecrated, the symbols\par
of Christianity and religion everywhere torn down.  In France upon Good\par
Friday the standard of the Republic waves proudly, while the flag of\par
every other Christian nation hangs at half-mast high.  And yet--the\par
great mass of the French people are--Catholic and nothing but Catholic!\par
The light may be hidden, but the fire of devotion still burns in\par
millions of faithful hearts gathered about the Church's altars, beating\par
beside the hearths of innumerable homes in France.  Blood--torrents of\par
blood--would not quench that sacred fire.  When the Day of Expiation\par
comes, as it will come, most surely, the Catholicism of France will\par
prove her salvation yet!"\par
\par
With the final sentence, the hand that had been lifted in gesture\par
dropped to the side of the speaker.  The flashing glance took in Franky\par
from the top of his sleek bewildered head to the tips of his beautiful\par
patent-leathers.  He said with a smile of irresistible amusement:\par
\par
"Monsieur, I fear I have fatigued you.  Let me thank you for your\par
admirable patience.  _Au revoir_, or if you prefer it--_Adieu_!"\par
\par
Another of the quick little bows, and he had covered himself and passed\par
on rapidly.  Franky reflected, staring after the short black figure in\par
the caped soutane with the worn purple sash and shabby beaver\par
shovel-hat, as it receded from his view.\par
\par
"Fruity old wordster, 'pon my natural!  Toppin' fine talker!  Wonder who\par
he is?  Head of a Public School, swottin' an address for the beginning\par
of the Midsummer Half term--a Professor of Divinity gettin' up a\par
lecture--the Archbishop of Paris rehearsin' a sermon.  Whichever they\par
call him, why don't he pitch his language at a man of his own size?"\par
\par
And he went back to the Spitz through the boulevards that were surging\par
with the afternoon life of Paris, and heard from Pauline that Miladi had\par
retired to bed.  She had already dispatched a billet of excuses to Sir\par
Brayham, with whom Miladi and Milord were engaged to dine downstairs\par
that evening, explaining that a headache prevented her from accompanying\par
Milord.  He--Milord--must be sure to make no noise in changing for\par
dinner, as Miladi, after a crisis of the nerves of the most alarming,\par
was now sleeping like an angel, having taken a _potion calmante_ of\par
orange-flower syrup with water, not the veronal so heartily detested of\par
Milord....\par
\par
\par
"Sleepin' like an angel, is she? ... Good egg!--though I thought angels\par
never went to bed--flew about singing all the giddy time.  Righto,\par
though!  I won't disturb her ladyship....  When she wakes, give her my\par
love...."\par
\par
And Franky entered his dressing-room on cautious tiptoe, lighted a\par
cigarette, rang the bell for his valet, and began to reflect.\par
\par
It was to have been a dinner of eight people--Brayham the host, with\par
Lady Wathe, skinny little vitriol-tongued woman!--a man unknown who was\par
to have sat next Margot; Commander Courtley--ripping good fellow old\par
Courtley! no better sailor walked the quarter-deck of a First-Class\par
Cruiser--damn shame those Admiralty bigwigs denied such a fellow\par
post-rank; and Lady Beauvayse, formerly Miss Sadie Sculpin of New\par
York--pretty American with pots of boodle, married to that ghastly\par
little bounder who'd stepped into the shoes a better man would be\par
wearing if his elder brother (handsome fellow who married an actress,\par
Lessie Lavigne of the Jollity--good old Jollity!) hadn't got pipped in\par
that scrum with the Boers in 1900-1901.\par
\par
Lessie, Lady Beauvayse, the widder called herself on the posters and\par
programmes.  Come down to second-rate parts in Music Hall Revue--gettin'\par
elderly and stout.  Must see red when she happened to spy the present\par
Lord Beauvayse's pretty peeress in the stalls or boxes....  Wonder why\par
the P.P. made such a pal of Patrine Saxham?  Niece of Saxham of Harley\par
Street--handsome as paint, proud as the devil, and an Advanced\par
Thinker--according to Margot. Remembering the gift of the jade\par
tree-frog, Franky involuntarily wrinkled his nose.\par
\par
With Lady Beau and the Saxham girl, there would be a party of seven,\par
counting the man unknown....  Might go on afterwards to the Folies\par
Bergere or the Theatre Marigny--or perhaps the Jardin de Paris.  Why\par
hadn't Jobling answered his master's bell?  Why had he deputised a\par
waiter to enquire whether his lordship wished his valet?  Did he think\par
waiters were paid to do his, Jobling's, work for him?  Or did he,\par
Jobling, suppose he was kept for show?\par
\par
The strenuous stage-whisper in which Franky addressed the recalcitrant\par
Jobling penetrated the door-panels of the adjoining bower, as such\par
whispers usually do.  But Margot was really sleeping--the orange-flower\par
water had had a few drops of chloral mingled with it.  Milord had never\par
prohibited chloral, as Pauline had pointed out.  But unsuspicious\par
Franky, unrigging (as he termed the process), while the tardy Jobling\par
prepared his master's bath and laid out his master's "glad rags," plumed\par
himself upon having made a notable advance in the science of\par
wife-government.  Even the blameless potion of orange-flower testified\par
to his masculine strength of will.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER IX*\par
\par
                        *SIR THOMAS ENTERTAINS*\par
\par
\par
You are invited to follow Franky, and sit with him at his friend Tom\par
Brayham's circular board, decorated with great silver bowls of\par
marvellous Rayon d'Or roses, that seemed to exhale the harvested\par
sunshine of summer from their fiery golden hearts.\par
\par
You remember the famous dining-room of the big Paris caravanserai, with\par
its archways supported by slender pillars of creamy pink Carrara marble,\par
wreathed with inlaid fillets of green malachite and lapis lazuli, and\par
its electric illuminants concealed behind an oxidised silver frieze.\par
And possibly you need no introduction to the deity--plain and\par
middle-aged--in whose honour Brayham--the Hon. Sir Thomas Brayham, an\par
ex-Justice of the King's Bench Division--in the remote mid-Victorian era\par
a famous Q.C.--made oblation of luscious meats and special wines.  The\par
clever, sharp-tongued, penniless niece of a famous Minister for Foreign\par
Affairs, she had made a love-match at twenty with Lord Watho Wathe, a\par
handsome and equally impecunious subaltern in a famous Highland\par
regiment, who was killed upon Active Service twenty years later, while\par
travelling upon a special mission to the Front Headquarters during the\par
South African War of 1900.\par
\par
Two years later his widow conferred her hand upon Mr. Reuben Munts, of\par
Kimberley and South Carfordshire, a diamond-mining magnate who had made\par
his colossal pile before the War.  She had never borne her second\par
husband's name, and when he died, leaving her sole mistress of his\par
millions, Lady Wathe resumed her place in Society, thenceforwards to\par
sparkle as never before.\par
\par
"The '_Chronique Scandaleuse_' in a diamond setting" some phrase-maker\par
clever as herself had aptly termed her. Without her riches, stripped of\par
her wonderful diamonds, Society might have found her to be merely a\par
little chattering woman, avid of the reputation of a humorist and\par
_raconteuse_, unflagging in her relish for stories, not seldom of the\par
broadest, related at her own expense or at the cost of other people, and\par
over-liberally garnished with nods and becks, darting glances, and\par
wreathed smiles.\par
\par
Upon this night of the Grand Prix--won, you will remember, by Baron M.\par
de Rothschild's "Sardanapole"--the little lady's jests fizzled and\par
coruscated like Japanese fireworks. Her gibes buzzed and stung like\par
wasps about a lawn-set tea-table, when new-made jam and fragrant honey\par
tempt the yellow-and-black marauders to the board.  And yet from the\par
soup to the _entremets_, Franky listened in dour and smileless silence,\par
unable to conjure up a grin at the sharpest of the Goblin's witticisms,\par
or swell the guffaw that invariably followed the naughtiest of her\par
_double-entendres_.\par
\par
"Off colour, what? ..." his crony Courtley queried in a sympathetic\par
undertone, catching a glimpse of Franky's cheerless countenance behind\par
the bare, convulsed back and snowy heaving shoulders of Lady Beauvayse,\par
who occupied the intervening chair.\par
\par
"Putridly off colour....  Walked in the Bois, and got a touch of the\par
sun, I fancy!" Franky whispered back too loudly, drawing upon himself\par
the Goblin's _equivoque_:\par
\par
"The sun or the daughter, did you say, Lord Norwater? Dear me!" the\par
Goblin shrilled; "you're actually blushing! You've revived a long-lost\par
Early Victorian art."\par
\par
"Was blushing really an art with the ladies of that dim and distant\par
era?" asked the friendly Brayham, not in the least comprehending\par
Franky's discomfiture, yet desirous of diverting the Goblin's glittering\par
scrutiny from her victim's scarlet face.\par
\par
"It was the art that concealed Heart--or assumed it!" Lady Wathe\par
retorted, with a peal of elfish laughter, turning her tight-skinned,\par
large-eyed, wide-mouthed ugliness upon the speaker, and nodding her\par
little round head until the huge and perfectly matched diamonds of the\par
triple-rayed tiara that crowned her scanty henna-dyed tresses flashed\par
blinding sparks of violet and red and emerald splendour in the\par
mellow-toned radiance of the electric lights.\par
\par
The Goblin had meant nothing, Franky assured himself, as the angry blood\par
stopped humming in his ears, and his complexion regained its normal\par
shade.  The bad pun that had bowled him over had possibly been uttered\par
without malicious intent....  Yet Lady Wathe rented a gorgeous suite\par
upon the floor below the Norwater apartments, and one of her three\par
lady's-maids might have been pumping Pauline....  What was she saying?\par
... Why was everybody cackling? ...\par
\par
The Goblin was launched upon a characteristic story.  Its\par
_denouement_--worked up with skill and related with point--evoked peal\par
upon peal of laughter from the guests at Brayham's table, with the sole\par
exception of Franky, whom the anecdote found sulky and left glum.  He\par
said to himself that if Lady Beauvayse, _nee_ Miss Sadie J. Sculpin of\par
New York, sole child and heiress of a Yankee who had made millions out\par
of Chewing Gum, chose to forget her position as the wife of a British\par
Peer, and mother of his children, by Jove! and scream at such nastiness,\par
it was her look-out.  If the big red-blond man who sat on Franky's right\par
shook with amusement, as he recapitulated the chief points of the story\par
for the benefit of the girl who sat next him, it was his affair. But\par
that the Saxham, an unmarried girl, who oughtn't to see the bearings of\par
such a tale, should openly revel in its saltness, made Franky feel\par
sick--on this particular night.\par
\par
He realised that he detested the Saxham girl, one of Margot's chosen\par
Club intimates, more fervently than even Tota Stannus or Joan Delabrand;\par
more thoroughly than Rhona Helvellyn; only little less heartily than he\par
hated Cynthia Charterhouse.  Big, bold, galumphing, provocative--in\par
fact, so much IT that you couldn't overlook her--he found her more\par
unpleasantly attractive than usual, in a bodice that was no more than a\par
fold of shimmering orange stuff above the waist--tossing the _panache_\par
of ospreys that startlingly crowned her, offering up her _persistant_\par
illusion perfumes for the delectation of the appreciative male.\par
\par
Only look at her, ready to climb into her neighbour's pocket.  Leaning\par
her round white elbows on the guipure table-cloth, half-shutting those\par
long greeny-brown Egyptian eyes of her, wreathing her long thick white\par
neck to send a daring challenge into the face of the laughing man.  A\par
big man, bright red-haired, blue-eyed, and broad-chested, showing every\par
shining tooth in his handsome grinning head....\par
\par
"She's _screaming_, isn't she, dear Lady Beau?"  Thus the Saxham to her\par
employer, friend, and ally, across the silver bowls of Rayon d'Or roses,\par
her naked shoulder brushing the coat-sleeve of her neighbour, the big\par
rufous man. And Lady Beau gushed back:\par
\par
"In marvellous form to-night....  Don't you think so, Count?  Do agree\par
with us!" and the big man agreed, with the accent of the German\par
Fatherland:\par
\par
"She is _kolossal_....  _Wunderlich_! ..."\par
\par
"Who's the German next me--big beggar Lady Beau and Miss Saxham are\par
gushing over?" Franky presently telegraphed to Courtley behind the\par
charming American's accommodating back.  And Courtley signalled in\par
reply:\par
\par
"Von Herrnung.  German Count of sorts--Engineer and Flieger officer.\par
Son of an Imperial Councillor, and cousin to Princess Willy of Kiekower\par
Oestern--really rather an interestin' beast in his way.  Made a one-stop\par
flight to Paris from Hanover in April, with an Albatros biplane.\par
Previously won an event in the Prinz Heinrich Circuit Competition."  He\par
added: "We can't decently blink their progress in military aviation.\par
It's one o' them there fax which the brass-hats at the War Office\par
pretend to regard as all my eye.  Yet they know the Fatherland--or if\par
they don't they oughter!  Good-lookin' chap this. Not over thirty, I\par
should guess him.  Always dodging in and out of the German Embassy.  The\par
Goblin frightful nuts on him....  Goin' to steer him through the next\par
London Season--suppose he's lookin' out for a moneyed wife!"\par
\par
"Hope he gets her!" Franky mentally commented.  But he looked with new\par
interest at his big blond German neighbour, mentally calculating that\par
with all that bone, brawn, and muscle, von Herrnung couldn't tip the\par
scale at less than sixteen stone.\par
\par
Small-boned himself and of stature not above the medium, Franky\par
appreciated height and size in other men.  And von Herrnung was\par
undeniably a son of Anak.  The noiseless, demure waiters who paused\par
beside his chair to refill his glass or offer him dishes were dwarfed by\par
his seated presence to the proportions of little boys.\par
\par
Once, when there was a momentary bustle at the principal entrance to the\par
now crowded restaurant, and a party of men, ceremoniously ushered by M.\par
Spitz in person, passed up the central gangway between the rows of\par
glittering tables, shielded by glass-panelled screens framed in oxidised\par
silver, and crowded now with gossiping, laughing, gobbling patrons--men\par
and women of varied nationalities, representing the elite of the\par
fashionable world, von Herrnung rose and remained imperturbably standing\par
at the salute, his eyes set and fixed, his head turned rigidly towards\par
the personage, semi-bald, stout, with a prominent under jaw and a hard\par
official stare rendered glassier by a frameless square monocle, and\par
showing beneath the open front of a loose military mantle a star upon\par
the left side of his evening dress-coat, and the glitter of an Order\par
suspended from a yellow riband about his thick bull-neck.\par
\par
"The German Ambassador, Baron von Giesnau," Lady Wathe returned to a\par
question from Lady Beauvayse, as the portly official figure creaked by,\par
leaving a whiff of choice cigars and a taint of _parfum tres\par
persistant_, lifting three fingers of a white-gloved hand in\par
acknowledgment of his countryman's salute, and von Herrnung unstiffened\par
and dropped back into his chair.  "No! ... I'm not sure where the\par
Emperor is...."  She added, with one of her laughs and a shrug of her\par
thin vivacious shoulders: "Ask Count von Herrnung--he's sure to know!"\par
\par
"_Gnaedige Graefin_," von Herrnung returned when interrogated, "I am not\par
able to answer your question."  He shrugged his broad shoulders and\par
showed his white teeth. "_Unser Kaiser_ is--who shall say where?  At the\par
Hof ... possibly at Homburg....  Stop! ... Now I remember! _Seine\par
Majestaet_ is at Kiel...."  He continued, arranging with a big white\par
hand displaying a preposterously long thumb-nail a corner of his\par
glittering, tightly rolled moustache: "At Kiel ... _ach_, yes! he has\par
been there since the 25th of June.  Entertaining the British and\par
American Ambassadors, visiting the Commander-in-Chief of your British\par
Squadron, superintending the armament of one of our own new\par
battle-cruisers,--seeing put into her those great big Krupp guns that\par
are to sink your super-Dreadnoughts by-and-by!"\par
\par
The deliberately-uttered words of the last sentence dropped into a\par
little pool of chilly silence.  He had spoken with perfect gravity, and\par
the Englishmen who heard him stared before they grinned.  Then the women\par
shrieked in ecstasies of amusement--the Goblin's laugh overtopping all.\par
\par
"For he hates us! ... You can't think how he hates us! ..." she crowed,\par
writhing her lean little throat, clasped by seven rows of shimmering\par
stones, wagging her Kobold's head, crowned by its diadem of\par
multi-coloured fire.  "Tell us how you hate us, Tido! ... Do--pray do!"\par
\par
"I hate you, _ach_ yes! ... All German officers are like\par
that--particularly the officers of our Field Flying Service," gravely\par
corroborated von Herrnung.  "We have many pleasant acquaintanceships\par
with men and women of British nationality, but your race--the\par
Anglo-Saxon branch of the great Teutonic oak-tree, it is natural that we\par
should hate! For that Germany must expand upon the west and north-west\par
as well as south and east, or suffocate, is certain.  She must wield the\par
trident of Sea Power; she must transform the map of Europe.  She must\par
exploit and disseminate German trade and German Kultur; therefore, as\par
the British, more than any other nation, stands in the way of German\par
development, we look forward to the Day when we shall exterminate you\par
and take our right position as masters of the world!"\par
\par
The women screamed anew at this.  The men were now laughing in good\par
earnest.  Franky found it impossible to restrain the convulsions that\par
shook him in his chair. Purple-faced Brayham tried to speak, but broke\par
down wheezing and spluttering.  The Goblin shrilled:\par
\par
"Tell them, Tido....  Please tell them! ... Do--ha! ha! tell them how\par
you're spoiling for a scrimmage with us!  Show them your thumb-nail,\par
pray do!"\par
\par
Thus adjured, the big German solemnly extended his left hand for general\par
inspection.  The pointed, carefully-manicured thumb-nail was at least\par
two inches long.  Its owner said with perfect gravity:\par
\par
"This is the badge of a Society of England-haters, chiefly Prussian\par
military officers, young men of noble birth, bound by an oath of blood.\par
This mark we carry to distinguish us. It is a sign of our dedication, to\par
remind us of the purpose for which we are set apart."  He added: "Count\par
Zeppelin himself set the fashion of the uncut thumb-nail.  It will be\par
cut when the Day comes, and it has been dipped in blood!"\par
\par
"In blood--how beastly!" said the Saxham girl, curling the corners of\par
her wide red mouth contemptuously.  "What a horrid crowd your noble\par
young Prussian officers must be! And when is the dipping to come off?"\par
Her voice was deep and resonant as a masculine baritone, and of so\par
carrying a quality that Franky started as though the words had been\par
spoken at his ear.\par
\par
"_Gnaedige Fraeulein_," von Herrnung answered, "I have already told you.\par
When the Day comes for which we are preparing.  When the great German\par
nation shall abandon Christianity--cast off the rusty fetters of\par
Morality and Virtue--call on the Ancient God of Battles--and beat out\par
the iron sceptre of World Power with sword-blows upon the anvil of War."\par
\par
"When we're all to be exterminated, he means!" Lady Wathe gasped behind\par
her filmy handkerchief.  "Tido, you're too absolutely screaming!  Do say\par
why your noble young Prussians keep us waiting? ..."  And von Herrnung\par
answered composedly:\par
\par
"Because we are not yet ready.  We shall not be perfectly ready before\par
the spring of 1916."\par
\par
His hard, bright glance encountered Franky's, and he lifted his full\par
glass of champagne and drank to him, smiling pleasantly.\par
\par
Of course the German was rotting, reflected Franky.  If he wasn't, the\par
combined insolence and brutality of such a menace, uttered at the table\par
of one of the Britons in whose gore von Herrnung and his comrades\par
yearned to dip their preposterous two-inch thumb-nails, took the bun, by\par
the Great Brass Hat!  He was perfectly cool, as his muscular white\par
hands--for the dinner had arrived at the dessert stage--manipulated the\par
silver knife that peeled a blood-red nectarine.  What a splendid ring, a\par
black-and-white pearl, large as a starling's egg, and set in platinum,\par
the fellow sported on the little finger of that clawed left hand.  What\par
was he asking, in the suave voice with the guttural Teutonic accent?\par
\par
"You were in the Bois, I believe, Lord Norwater, early in the midday.\par
Did you see any _avions_ of the _Service Aeronautique_?  Did the\par
invention they were testing come up to expectations? .... Did the\par
English aerial stabiliser answer well? ..."\par
\par
Franky knew, as he encountered the compelling stare of the hard blue\par
eyes, that he objected to their owner.  He returned, in a tone more\par
huffy and less dignified than he would have liked it to be:\par
\par
"Can't say....  I was merely walking in the Bois with a lady.  Wasn't on\par
the ground as--an investigator of the professional sort."\par
\par
"_So!_"  Von Herrnung's face was set in a smile of easy amiability.  The\par
shot might have missed the bull for anything that was betrayed there.\par
"And the name of the inventor?  It has escaped my memory.  Possibly you\par
could tell me, eh?"\par
\par
"Certainly," said Franky, planting one with pleasure. "He happens to be\par
a cousin of mine.  Would you like me to write down his address?"\par
\par
"_Gewiss_--thanks so very much.  But I will not trouble you!"\par
\par
Nobody had heard the verbal encounter.  Lady Wathe was holding the table\par
with another anecdote punctuated with staccato peals of laughter,\par
tinkling like the brazen bells of a beaten tambourine.  Mademoiselle\par
Nou-Nou, a Paris celebrity, belonging to the most ancient if not the\par
most venerable of professions, had promenaded under the chestnuts at\par
Longchamps that morning, attired, as to the upper portion of her body,\par
in a sheath of spotted black gauze veiling, unlined--save with her own\par
charms.  And a witty Paris journalist had said that "the costume was\par
designed to represent Eve, not before nor after, but behind the fall";\par
and Paillette, who was there, working up her "Modes" letter for _Le\par
Style_, had answered----\par
\par
Everybody at table was leaning forward and listening, as the Goblin\par
quoted the _riposte_ of Paillette.\par
\par
Von Herrnung, showing his big white teeth in a smile, chose another\par
nectarine from the piled-up dish before him, seeming to admire the\par
contrast between his own muscular white fingers and the glowing fruit\par
they held.  But Franky saw that he was angry as he neatly peeled the\par
fruit, split the odorous yellow flesh, tore the stone out crimson and\par
dripping like a little human heart, and swallowed both halves of the\par
fruit in rapid succession, dabbing his mouth with the fine serviette\par
held up before him in both hands.  Then, with an air of arrogant\par
self-confidence peculiar to him, he said loudly, addressing the whole\par
company:\par
\par
"Madame Paillette certainly deserves the Croix d'Honneur for so\par
excellent a _bon-mot_.  As for Mademoiselle Nou-Nou, I do not myself\par
admire her, but my brother Ludwig, when he was alive, paid intermittent\par
tribute to her charms."  He added: "He was killed in the charge by a\par
fall with his horse in the Autumn Manoeuvres of last year, while the\par
Emperor was being entertained by command at a shooting-party upon a\par
forest property of my father's that is about fifty kilometres from\par
Berlin."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER X*\par
\par
                              *A SUPERMAN*\par
\par
\par
"Do tell what the Kaiser said when he heard of the accident!" came in\par
the voice of Lady Beauvayse, pitched now in a high, nasal tone that was\par
a danger-signal to those who knew her, like the mischievous twinkle in\par
her beautiful eyes. "I guess he must have been real upset!"\par
\par
"_Ja, ja, gewiss_," returned von Herrnung, slightly shrugging his broad,\par
square shoulders.  "Of course the Emperor was greatly grieved for my\par
father's loss.  But naturally the programme had to be carried out.\par
There is another day's Imperial shooting; the business is\par
concluded--very satisfactorily--and _Seine Majestaet_ takes leave.....\par
But of course he sent to my mother a sympathetic message, which greatly\par
consoled her.  And his Chief Equerry, Baron von Wildenberg, represented\par
him at my brother's funeral.  And shortly afterwards he graciously\par
conferred upon my father the Second Class of the Order _Pour le\par
Merite_."\par
\par
"How nice!  But what for?" demanded the downright American, with\par
astonishment so genuine that Brayham strangled with suppressed chuckles,\par
and the bearded mouth of Commander Courtley assumed the curve of a sly\par
smile.\par
\par
"What for?" exclaimed von Herrnung.  He stiffened his big body\par
arrogantly, reddening with evident annoyance, and thickly through his\par
carefully-accentuated English the Teutonic consonants and gutturals\par
began to crop.  "_Gnaedige Graefin_, because that so coveted decoration\par
is the reward of special service rendered to the Emperor.  And my father\par
in his-personal-sorrow-conquering that it upon the amusements of\par
Imperial Majesty-might-not-intrude--had the noblest devotion and courage\par
exhibited--in the opinion of the All-Highest."\par
\par
"My land!" exclaimed Lady Beauvayse, stimulated by the undisguised\par
enjoyment of Brayham, Courtley, and Franky, "if that don't take the team\par
and waggon, with the yella dog underneath it, an' the hoss-fly sittin'\par
on the near-wheel mule's left ear!"  She added: "No wonder your Kaiser\par
thinks himself the hub of this little old universe--being nourished from\par
infancy on flapdoodle of that kind."  She added, dropping the saw-edged\par
artificial accent, and reverting to the agreeable, drawling tones\par
familiar to her friends: "But, last fall, when King George and Queen\par
Mary were allowing to spend the day with us at Foltlebarre Abbey, and\par
see the Gobelins tapestries after Teniers that were restored by our\par
great American dye-specialist, Charlotte B. Pendrill of New York--and I\par
had a dud head with neuralgitis, and couldn't have bobbed a curtsey\par
without screaming like peacocks before a wet spell--Lord Beauvayse just\par
sent a respectful note of excuse over by fast car to the place in our\par
county where their Majesties were spending a week-end, and got a kind,\par
cosy little line by return, making an appointment for a more convenient\par
day."\par
\par
"_Es mag wohl sein_," said von Herrnung stiffly, repeating an apparently\par
favourite phrase.  "It may be so--in Great Britain.  But in Germany the\par
trivial happenings of ordinary existence are not permitted to interfere\par
with the Imperial plans."\par
\par
"Mustn't spoil Great Caesar's shoot by letting a natural sorrow dim your\par
eye, in case you're unexpectedly informed of a family bereavement," said\par
Brayham to Lady Beauvayse.  "So now you know what to expect in case the\par
Kaiser should take it into his head to pop in on you at Foltlebarre\par
somewhere about July."\par
\par
"I surmise I'd expect a visitor of mine, whether he's the Kaiser, the\par
King, or the President," retorted Lady Beauvayse, "to be a gentleman!"\par
Her beautiful eyes blazed with genuine ire as she gave back von\par
Herrnung's dominating stare.  She continued, reverting more purposefully\par
than ever to the exaggerated New York accent, mingling cutting Yankee\par
humour with bitter irony in the sentences that twanged, one after\par
another, off her sharp American tongue: "And I guess, Count von\par
Herrnung--though between your father and Amos J. Sculpin of Madison\par
Avenue, New York, and Sculpin Towers, Schenectady, there's considerable\par
of a social gulf--if your Emperor had been a house-guest of my parpa's,\par
and my elder brother"--she lifted an exquisite shoulder significantly\par
ceilingwards--"had happened to get the hoist--parpa'd just have said:\par
'Your Imperial Majesty, I am unexpectedly one boy short, and far from\par
feeling hunkey.  My cars are waiting at my door to convey you right-away\par
to your hotel.  Look in on us after the interment, when Mrs. Sculpin has\par
had time to get accustomed to her mourning. And as my _chef_ had orders\par
to serve a special dinner in honour of your Majesty, I shall be\par
gratified by your taking the hull menoo along--outside instead of in!'"\par
\par
The Goblin cackled.  Ecstatic Brayham shrieked:\par
\par
"Magnificent, by Gad!  He ought to know your father!"  Franky and\par
Courtley yielded unrestrainedly to mirth, as did the Saxham girl.  While\par
her teeth, dazzling as those of a Newfoundland pup, gleamed in her wide\par
red mouth, and her long eyes glittered between their narrowed eyelids,\par
von Herrnung gave her a quick sidelong glance of anger.  She caught the\par
look, and suddenly ceased to laugh, as the young Newfoundland might have\par
stopped barking.  She said below her breath:\par
\par
"Vexed? ... Why, you're really! ... And Lady Beau wasn't joking about\par
your brother....  She wouldn't dream of such a thing! .... She's\par
tremendously kind and sympathetic.  Was he--your brother--nice? ..."\par
\par
"Most women thought so."\par
\par
"Would I have thought so?  What was he like?" the girl persisted.\par
\par
Von Herrnung turned in his chair so as to face her, answering:\par
\par
"You see him now, with one difference.  He was as black as I am red."\par
\par
The blue eyes of the man and the long agate-coloured eyes of the young\par
woman encountered.  She said slowly in her warm, deep voice, less like a\par
feminine contralto than the masculine baritone:\par
\par
"I like--red men--best!"\par
\par
"So!  Then it was lucky that, instead of me, my brother Ludwig died!"\par
said von Herrnung, so loudly that Lady Wathe's quick ear caught the\par
final words.  She shrilled out her laugh:\par
\par
"But you're a wretch, Tido!"  She shrugged her thin vivacious shoulders\par
under their glittering burden.  "A heartless wretch!"\par
\par
"Of course I was regretting my brother, yes!" said von Herrnung.  "But I\par
do not pretend that his death did not improve what you English would\par
call my worldly prospects. That is the cant of\par
Christianity--particularly the sentimental Christianity of England.  One\par
world is not enough for your greed of possession.  You must eat your\par
cake here and hereafter.  But for the robust super-humanity of Germany,\par
this world is both Hell and Heaven.  It is Hell for the man who is\par
stupid, weakly, poor, and conscience-ridden.  It is Heaven for the man\par
who has knowledge, power, health, wealth, the craft to keep his riches,\par
and the capacity to enjoy to the fullest the pleasures they can procure\par
him, with the courage to free himself from the bonds of what Christians\par
and Agnostics term Morality, and live precisely as Nature prompts.  So\par
when my brother fell in the charge," continued von Herrnung, with\par
perfect seriousness, "he opened for me the gates of Heaven.  Since then\par
I am a god!"\par
\par
"A mortal god," called out the chuckling Brayham; "for you've got to\par
die, you know, when your number's up."\par
\par
"When the time comes, of course I shall die," acquiesced von Herrnung,\par
"in the vulgar sense of the word.  But not so those who come after.  Our\par
bacteriologists will have discovered the microbe of old age and its\par
antitoxin, and then we shall die no more."\par
\par
"Dashed if I know the difference between the vulgar way of dying and the\par
other style!" Brayham snorted apoplectically, feeling in his\par
waistcoat-pocket for the box of digestive tabloids that showed in a\par
bulge.  "Dashed unpleasant certainty--however you look at it!  And a man\par
who weighs eighteen stone at fifty has _got_ to look at it, every time\par
his tailor lets out his waistcoats, and his valet asks him to order more\par
collars because the last lot have shrunk in the wash."\par
\par
"Ah, yes, to die is a hellish bore!" agreed von Herrnung, contemplating\par
his obese and purple host with a cruel smile. "But I and my friends have\par
no Hell, and we have done away with the myth of Heaven.  To dissolve and\par
be reabsorbed into the elements--that is the only after-life that is\par
possible for a Superman."\par
\par
"You'd hardly call it Life, would you?" came unwillingly from Franky.\par
For von Herrnung's eyes seemed to challenge his own.\par
\par
"'_Imperial Caesar, dead and turned to clay_,' what?" quoted Courtley,\par
to whom von Herrnung transferred his smiling regard.\par
\par
"I venture to hope that my clay may serve a more patriotic purpose than\par
stopping a draught-hole," said the German, carefully fingering the tight\par
roll of glittering red hair upon his upper-lip.  "It may be baked into a\par
sparking-plug for the aero-motor of one of our Zeppelin dirigibles--the\par
mysterious Z. X., for instance, in whose trial trip from Stettin across\par
the Baltic to Upsala in Sweden you were so keenly interested some months\par
ago.  Or some of my body's chemical constituents may pass into the young\par
tree beneath which my ashes will be deposited.  If beech or spruce, then\par
I may furnish ribs or struts for an Aviatik or a Taube.  But the best\par
way of continuing to exist after one is dead is to leave plenty of\par
vigorous sons behind one.  To perpetuate the race"--he continued\par
speaking to Lord Norwater, who had flushed and moved restlessly--"that\par
is the high and noble obligation Duty imposes upon the German Superman."\par
\par
"You'll have to hurry up your matrimonial arrangements, Tido,"\par
interposed the Goblin, with her cackle, "if your family is to tot up to\par
a respectable number before the year 1916."\par
\par
"You mean that I may get killed in our great War of Extermination?  That\par
is possible," agreed von Herrnung. "Our Flying Service is not a\par
profession conducive to long life.  Many of our keenest officers remain\par
unmarried for that reason.  The Emperor would prefer each of us to\par
marry, or at least adopt a son.  For myself, I would like to steal one\par
of your splendid British boys and rear him up as a true German----"\par
\par
Something sharp and keen and burning stabbed through Franky's brain to\par
his vitals.  It would have been a relief to have insulted von Herrnung.\par
He set his teeth, fighting with the desire, as the guttural voice went\par
on:\par
\par
"I would teach him to hate you...."  The speaker sucked in his breath as\par
though he relished the idea exceedingly.  "You cannot think how he would\par
hate you!--my German-British Superman."\par
\par
"By-the-by, the literary genius of Dreadnought type who invented the\par
Superman," began Courtley, who had been peaceably nibbling salted\par
pistachios, "can't pronounce his name for ginger-nuts, but it sounds\par
something like a sneeze----"\par
\par
Von Herrnung said stiffly:\par
\par
"You doubtless speak of our great Nietzsche, whose triumphant thought\par
has crushed all other mental systems."\par
\par
"Quite so.  Must be the chap!" said Courtley.  "That is, if he died a\par
lunatic....  But possibly I'm mixing him up with some other philosopher\par
of the crushing kind?"\par
\par
"No, no.  It is true," corroborated von Herrnung.  "The brain of\par
Nietzsche gave way under the terrific strain of incessant creation.  How\par
should it be otherwise?"  He became ponderous, even solemn, when he\par
descanted upon the literary idol of Modern Germany.  "How should it\par
indeed be otherwise?" he demanded.  "And was it not the fitting crown of\par
such a career--the appropriate end to such a life-work?--to evolve the\par
Superman--and die!"\par
\par
"Quite so, quite so!" Courtley agreed.  He smoothed his well-trimmed\par
beard with his broad hand, and his eyes assumed a meditative expression.\par
"Rather tantalising--always hearing about Germany's Supermen and never\par
seeing any.  What sort of chaps are they?  I'm really keen to know."\par
\par
"You have not to go far," returned von Herrnung.  His fine florid\par
complexion had suffered a deteriorating change. Savage anger boiled in\par
his blood.  He had thrown the iron gauntlet of German military\par
preparedness in the faces of these cool, well-bred, smiling English, and\par
brandished the iron thunderbolt of German intellectual supremacy--and\par
with this result--that they took his deadly earnestness as jest.\par
"_Kreutzdonnerwetter!_ these English officers....  The pig-dogs! the\par
sheep's heads! ..."  He swallowed down the abusive epithets he would\par
have liked to pitch at them, and stiffened his huge frame arrogantly as\par
he stared in Courtley's simple face:\par
\par
"_Aber_--you have not far to go, to visualise the type conceived by\par
Nietzsche.  I and my comrades--_we_ are Supermen!"\par
\par
"Thanks for explaining, frightfully!" said Courtley with artless\par
gratitude, as Brayham purpled apoplectically and even the Goblin\par
tittered behind her fan.  "Shall know what to ticket you now, you know.\par
Thanks very much!"\par
\par
"You have read Nietzsche?" the sailor's victim queried.\par
\par
Said Courtley, with his best air of frank simplicity:\par
\par
"His works were recommended to me by my doctor, when I had a bad attack\par
of insomnia, about a year ago. Ordered a volume of 'Thus Spake Zara\par
Somebody.'  Half a chapter did the business.  No insomnia since then.\par
Sleep like a mite in a Gorgonzola, the instant my head touches the\par
pillow--never read another word.  But heaps of friends in the Fleet'll\par
be wanting to borrow the book presently, depend on it.  For we'll all be\par
too scared of Germany to sleep--in the year 1916."\par
\par
Laughter broke forth.  Lady Wathe gasped, dabbing her tearful eyes with\par
a lace-bordered handkerchief:\par
\par
"Oh, Tido! will you dead-in-earnest Germans never learn what pulling a\par
leg means?"\par
\par
"_Ach ja_!  I should have understood!"  He had stared, frowned, and\par
reddened savagely.  Now, with a palpable effort, his equanimity was\par
regained.  He turned with a smiling remark to Patrine Saxham, as Lady\par
Beauvayse breathed in Courtley's ear:\par
\par
"You perfect pet!  How I love you for that!"\par
\par
"Man simply suffering for a set-down.  Good egg, you!" murmured Franky\par
in the other ear of the Commander.\par
\par
"Felt sorry for him.  Had to do something--common humanity!" rejoined\par
Courtley, eating more and more pistachios.  "Seems as over-crammed with\par
their _Kultur_ as a pet garden-titmouse with coco-nut.  Vain too, but\par
that's the fault of the women.  Lord! how they gush at those big,\par
good-looking blighters.  See the Saxham!--ready to climb into his\par
waistcoat-pocket and stop there.  Would, too, if she wasn't built on\par
Dreadnought lines herself."\par
\par
She was laughing into von Herrnung's smiling visage as he offered her a\par
light from his cigar.  For with the arrival of coffee and liqueurs, the\par
fragrance of choice Havana and Turkish had begun to mingle with the tang\par
of Mocha, the heady bouquet of choice wines, and the odours of fruit and\par
flowers.  The screens of frosted glass were rearranged,--the ladies had\par
produced their cigarette-cases,--of gold with the monogram of the Goblin\par
set in diamonds; of platinum adorned with turquoises and pearls wrought\par
into the Beauvayse initial and coronet; and of humbler tortoiseshell,\par
bearing in fanciful golden letters the name "Patrine"----\par
\par
"Patrine..."\par
\par
"The Saxham girl" had taken the tortoiseshell cigarette-case from the\par
front of her low-cut, sleeveless bodice.  Von Herrnung had leaned\par
towards her, boldly exploring with his eyes the bosom where the trinket\par
had been hiding, and read the golden letters.  He smiled as he met her\par
puzzled eyes, saying:\par
\par
"'Patrine' is your name....  Now I know it I will not forget it!  Tell\par
me!"--he spoke in lowered tones--"why do you carry your cigarette-case\par
just in that place?"\par
\par
She laughed, half-shutting her long eyes and slightly lifting her big\par
white shoulders.  "Simply for convenience--when I'm in evening kit.\par
Dressmakers don't allow us poor women pockets in these days."\par
\par
"It may be so!"  As von Herrnung spoke with a calculated roughness that\par
he had found useful in dealing with many women, he took the\par
cigarette-case from her, momentarily covering her hand with his own.  As\par
his curving fingers touched her palm, he felt the soft warm flesh wince\par
at the contact.  Her black brows drew together, her sleepy agate eyes\par
shot him a hostile sidewise glance.\par
\par
"I have not offended?" he whispered in some anxiety. And she answered in\par
a louder tone, under cover of the talk, and laughter of the others:\par
\par
"No! ... Only--I hate to be touched, that's all."\par
\par
He smiled under the crisp tight roll of his red moustache, and his\par
large, well-cut nostrils dilated and quivered.\par
\par
"One day you will not hate it.  I will wait for that day. But--about\par
your cigarette-case--you do not now tell me the truth! ... The real\par
reason is more subtle.  You carry that thing there--under your\par
corsage--to make live men envious of an object that cannot feel!"\par
\par
"Really! ... What a lot you must know about women!"\par
\par
The words were mocking, but the voice that uttered them was big, warm,\par
and velvety.  Far above the ordinary stature of womanhood--you remember\par
that Franky regarded her as a great galumphing creature--her head would\par
yet have been much below the level of von Herrnung's, but for the height\par
of the extraordinary diadem or turban that crowned her masses of dull\par
cloudy-black hair. Folds of vivid emerald-green satin rose above a wide\par
band of theatrical gilt tinsel, set with blazing stage rubies, and above\par
the centre of the wearer's low, wide brow a fan-shaped panache of\par
clipped white ospreys sprang, boldly challenging the eye.  Thrown with\par
royal prodigality upon the back of the chair she occupied was an\par
opera-mantle of cotton-backed emerald-green velvet lavishly furred with\par
ermine and sables that were palpably false as the garish gold and jewels\par
of the diadem that crowned her, yet became her big, bold, rather brazen\par
beauty as well as though the Siberian weasel and the Arctic marten had\par
been trapped and slain to deck and adorn her, instead of the white\par
rabbit of ordinary commerce and the domestic pussy-cat.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER XI*\par
\par
                            *PATRINE SAXHAM*\par
\par
\par
Who was the girl--the woman rather--who diffused around her so powerful\par
a magnetic aura, whom prodigal Nature had dowered with such opulence of\par
bodily splendour, that cheap, tawdry clothes and ornaments borrowed from\par
her a magnificence that conjured up visions of the Salammbo of Flaubert,\par
gleaming moon-like through her gold and purple tissues--of Anatole\par
France's Queen of Sheba treading the lapis-lazuli and sardonyx pavements\par
of King Solomon's palace in her jewelled sandals of gilded serpent-skin,\par
darting fiery provocations from under the shadow of her painted lashes\par
towards the Wise One rising from his cushions of purple byssus, between\par
the golden lions of his ivory throne?\par
\par
What a voice the creature had! thought von Herrnung. Soft and velvety\par
like that dead-white skin of hers. The tortoiseshell case he held in his\par
big palm still glowed with the rich vital warmth of her.  His blood\par
tingled and raced in his veins; his hard, brilliant stare grew\par
languorous, and his mouth relaxed into sensuousness.  He said almost\par
stupidly, so keen was his enjoyment:\par
\par
"You English ladies smoke a great deal, I think."\par
\par
"Why should we leave all the pleasant vices to the men?"\par
\par
She asked the queer question, not defiantly, but bluntly. Her strange\par
eyes laughed a little, as she saw Franky wince. "Lord Norwater hates me.\par
Well, that's about the limit!" she told herself.  "And I helped on his\par
love-affair for little Margot's sake!"  "I beg your pardon, Lord\par
Norwater!  You were saying something? ..."\par
\par
"You're an Advanced Thinker, aren't you, Miss Saxham?  At least, my wife\par
tells me so," Franky began. "Well, I'm not!  But I've got my doubts as\par
to whether vice is pleasant, for one thing--and for another, whether the\par
general run of women in these days aren't quite as vicious as the men?"\par
\par
"He wants to be nasty....  Poor boy, what have I done to him?" passed\par
through the brain topped by the bizarre diadem.  But before its wearer\par
could reply, von Herrnung interposed:\par
\par
"Naturally they are vicious--if they desire to please men.  A dash of\par
vice--that is the last touch to perfect an exquisite woman.  It is the\par
chilli in the _mayonnaise_, the garlic and citron in the _ragout_, the\par
perfume of the carnation, the patch of rouge that lends brilliance to\par
the eye, the bite in the kiss! ..."\par
\par
"The bite in the ... Great Snipe! what an expression!" thought Franky,\par
whose attack of propriety had reached the acute stage.  Patrine Saxham\par
repeated slowly, and with brows that frowned a little:\par
\par
"'_The bite in the kiss_'...."\par
\par
"You pretend not to understand..." said the guttural voice of von\par
Herrnung, speaking so that his wine- and cigar-scented breath stirred\par
the heavy hair that hid her small white ear.  "But you are wiser than\par
you would have me believe.  Are you not?  Tell me!--am I not right?"\par
\par
He bent closer, and she broke a web that seemed in the last few moments\par
to have been spun about her, invisible, delicate, strong, making captive\par
the body and the mind. Her odd agate-coloured eyes laughed into his\par
jeeringly.  Her wide red mouth curved and split like a ripe pomegranate,\par
showing the sharp white teeth that, backed by a vigorous appetite and\par
seconded by a splendid digestion, had done justice to every course of\par
Brayham's choice menu.\par
\par
Men always waxed sentimental or enterprising towards the close of a\par
rattling good dinner.  Patrine didn't care, not a merry little hang!\par
They might say and look what they liked, as long as they kept their\par
hands off.  At a touch, the quick revulsion came.\par
\par
"You are amused....  I understand...."  Von Herrnung spoke between his\par
teeth, in a tone of stifled anger. "Always to rot; it is your English\par
fashion....  When you encourage a man to make love to you, you are\par
rotting. When you say sweet things to him--possibly you are rotting\par
too?"\par
\par
She turned her face away from him, striving to control her irresistible\par
laughter.  In vain; it took her as a sudden gale takes a pennant at the\par
masthead--seized and shook her--as von Herrnung could have shaken her\par
had they been alone.  He turned savagely from her; she heard him speak\par
to Brayham, who responded with what-whattings, his fleshy hand to his\par
deafest ear.  Von Herrnung repeated his utterance. Brayham goggled in\par
astonishment.  Courtley murmured to Franky:\par
\par
"Hear what the blighter's saying....  No keeping him down, is there? ...\par
Buoyant as one of his own Zeppelins!"\par
\par
They looked and listened.  Brayham's thick bull-neck was shortening as\par
his shoulders climbed to his mottled ears. They caught a sound between a\par
snort and a bellow.  Then Lady Wathe's diamonds flashed all the colours\par
of the rainbow as she turned vivaciously to her friend....  Count Tido\par
wanted to propose a toast, the custom in dear, sentimental Germany....\par
Why shouldn't he?  Rather amusing.  She begged him to go on.  Said von\par
Herrnung:\par
\par
"To-night the laugh goes much against me.  I have been most frightfully\par
rotted.  Now, in my country it is the custom when a guest has been made\par
game of that those who have laughed at him must drink a toast with\par
him--to show there is no ill-will."\par
\par
"Never heard of such a custom--and I've lived in Germany a good deal."\par
\par
This from Brayham.  The German persisted:\par
\par
"Still, it is a custom, and it may be you will gratify me?"  He went on,\par
now addressing the company generally: "Here at the Spitz they have a\par
Tokayer that is very old and very excellent.  If I might order some?  It\par
would be amusing if you would all join me in drinking to The Day! ..."\par
\par
The speaker, without waiting consent, beckoned to one of the attendants.\par
Brayham, his cockatoo-crest of stiff grey hair erect, stared, as at a\par
new and surprising type of the human kind.\par
\par
But the words Brayham might have uttered were taken out of his mouth.  A\par
swift glance had passed between the English Naval officer and the rather\par
stupid, titled young Guardsman occupying the seat left of von Herrnung.\par
And while the Commander coolly intimated to the advancing waiter by a\par
sign that his services were not needed, Lord Norwater, lobster-red and\par
rather flurried, turned to von Herrnung and said, not loudly, yet\par
clearly enough to be heard by every guest at the table:\par
\par
"Stop!  Sorry to swipe in, Count, but you'd better not order that wine,\par
I think!"\par
\par
"You think not?" asked von Herrnung, with coolest insolence.\par
\par
"I--don't think so.  I'm dead-sure!" said Franky, getting redder.  "We\par
Britons laugh at brag and bluffing, and the gassy patriotism shown by\par
some foreigners we're apt to call bad form.  We abuse our Institutions\par
and rag our Governments--we've done that since the year One--far as I\par
can make out.  And when other people do it we generally sit tight and\par
smile.  We've no use for heroics.  But when the pinch comes--it ain't so\par
much that we're loyal.  We're Loyalty.  We're IT!"\par
\par
With all his boggling he was so much in earnest, and with all his\par
earnestness so absurdly, quaintly slangy, that the listeners, men and\par
women of British race, whose blood warmed to something in his face and\par
utterance, were forced to struggle to restrain their mirth.  Some\par
inkling of this increased the speaker's confusion.  He cast a drowning\par
glance at his bulwark Courtley, and Courtley's eye signalled back to\par
his, "Good egg! ... Drive on, old son!"\par
\par
"You're a foreigner here, of course ..."  Franky pursued before the\par
German could interrupt him.  He appeared oblivious to his own analogous\par
case.  Perhaps for the moment the Hotel Spitz in the Place Vendome,\par
Paris, and its gorgeous namesake in the London West End, were confused\par
in his not too intellectual mind.  He went on: "We're ready to make\par
allowances--too rottenly ready sometimes.... But I read off the\par
iddy-umpties to Full Stop, a minute back....  Count von Herrnung, when\par
you ask English ladies and Englishmen--two of 'em in the Service--to\par
drink that toast with you--you must know you're putting your foot in\par
your hat!"\par
\par
"Especially," said Courtley, as Franky collapsed, dewy all over and\par
wondering where his breath had gone to--"especially as--a friend of mine\par
happens to have heard that toast proposed rather recently during a Staff\par
banquet at a military headquarters in Germany.  And the words,\par
are--not--quite exactly flavoured to suit the British taste."\par
\par
"'_To the Day of Supremacy.  On the Land and on the Sea, under the Sea\par
and in the Air, Germany Victorious for ever and ever!_'" said von\par
Herrnung, who had got upon his legs, and loomed gigantic over the\par
lace-covered, flower-decked table, now in the after-dinner stage of\par
untidiness, with its silver-gilt and crystal dishes of choice fruit and\par
glittering bonbons disarranged and ravaged, its plates littered, its\par
half-emptied wine-goblets pushed aside to make room for fragrant,\par
steaming coffee-cups in filigree holders, and tiny jewel-hued glasses of\par
Maraschino Cusenier, and Pere Kermann.  There was a rustle, and a\par
general scraping-back of chairs.  Courtley had also risen, and Lord\par
Norwater.  A susurration of excitement had passed through the long,\par
lofty, brilliant dining-room.  People were getting up from the\par
tables--the pink-and-yellow sheets of _Paris Soir_, the late edition of\par
the _Daily Mail_, and another of the _Liberte_, were fluttering from\par
hand to hand....  And the shrill voice of Lady Wathe was heard.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XII*\par
\par
                      *THE GATHERING OF THE STORM*\par
\par
\par
"Sit down, Tido!" said Lady Wathe.  "What is the matter with everybody?\par
What are they talking about?  Tell a waiter to get us a paper!  What do\par
you say, Sir Thomas? Of course!  Stupid of me to forget.  To-day was to\par
be the official summing-up of the evidence in the Perdroux Murder Case.\par
A French Jury won't guillotine a woman--you said they wouldn't, Sir\par
Thomas, from the beginning.  But of course the verdict's 'Guilty' for\par
Madame! ..."\par
\par
Brayham, with a King's Bench cough, admitted that he had few misgivings\par
as to the ultimate upshot.  Upon the waiter's return without a\par
newspaper, affirming a copy not to be procurable, judicial inquiries\par
elicited from the man that the general _furore_ for news was less due to\par
popular interest in the famous _cause celebre_ than to popular thirst\par
for details with reference to the Assassinations at Serajevo. Which\par
brought from Lady Wathe the shrill query:\par
\par
"Sarajevo--where's Sarajevo?  Ask him about the Verdict--I simply must\par
know!"\par
\par
The Verdict had been "Not Guilty," according to the waiter....  The\par
Goblin screamed:\par
\par
"But she is!--she is!  Good heavens, my dear Sir Thomas! Isn't it murder\par
to riddle an editor to death in his own office, before his subordinates,\par
with bullets from a revolver you've hidden in your muff?"\par
\par
Brayham summoned up his best King's Bench manner to answer:\par
\par
"If he dies--and a jury don't happen to decide that you're innocent--the\par
evidence is against you, my dear ma'am!"\par
\par
Lady Wathe's vivacious gestures provoked astounding coruscations from\par
her panoply of jewels.  She had been certain from the first that there\par
would be no capital sentence.  But "Not Guilty." ... Surely it should\par
have been Mazas for life.  Or New Caledonia--didn't they send\par
murderesses to New Caledonia?\par
\par
Brayham, with a tone and manner even more deeply tinged with the King's\par
Bench, begged leave to correct--arah!--his very dear friend's impression\par
that the blameless and much-tried lady, now\par
probably--aha--arah!--supping in the company of her husband and her\par
advocate in her own luxurious dining-room, might, without libel, be\par
called a murderess.  Like--aha!--many other highly-strung women, Madame\par
Perdroux had had recourse to the revolver as the _ultima ratio_.  But\par
the Verdict pronounced by the President of the Paris Court of Assize\par
that afternoon had--arah!--purged----\par
\par
"Bother the Verdict!" snapped the Goblin.\par
\par
Brayham, incensed at this irreverence, replied with acrimony.  The pair\par
wrangled as Paris had wrangled since March 16th, while the great,\par
crowded restaurant buzzed with the name of an obscure town in Eastern\par
Europe--"_Sarajevo, Sarajevo_"--tossed and bandied from mouth to mouth.\par
\par
\par
We have learned to our bitter cost the appalling significance of this\par
crime of Sarajevo, which had dwarfed in the estimation of the\par
keen-witted Parisians the most sensational _cause celebre_ ever tried\par
before a French Criminal Court.\par
\par
The Perdroux trial and its probable result had split Paris into hostile\par
factions.  The Press had attacked or defended, lauded or vilified the\par
chief personages of the drama with tireless energy for weeks.  The\par
Verdict of "Not Guilty" would have caused fierce rioting upon the\par
boulevards this sultry night of July.  Blood would have been spilt\par
between the partisans of Madame Perdroux and her opponents, but for this\par
unexpected bolt from the blue.\par
\par
Berlin had had the story of the assassinations with its breakfast-rolls\par
and hot creamed coffee.  Now, in the blue-white glare of the great\par
electric arc-lamps of the Paris boulevards, men and women leaned over\par
one another's shoulders to get a whiff of the big black letters on the\par
displayed contents-bills; at every kiosk and bookstall the\par
newspaper-vendors were sold out; much-thumbed copies of the papers were\par
bought by knowing speculators, to be sold and bought and sold again.\par
\par
The Kaiser at Kiel was racing his own clipper when the operator of the\par
Imperial private wireless read a story from the notes of the singing\par
spark that smote him pale and sick. When his anointed master heard the\par
gory news, his chief regret seems to have concerned the untimely decease\par
of the partner of his "life-work."  "It will have," he said with\par
bitterness, "to be begun all over again!"\par
\par
\par
One wonders, in the blood-red light of four years of dreadful carnage,\par
seeing Hell and its dark Powers still unchained, and raging on this\par
War-torn earth of ours--what would have been the nature of the edifice\par
reared by these two Imperial craftsmen, had the younger not been removed\par
by a violent and sudden death?\par
\par
Did the prospect of unlocking--with one touch on an electric button and\par
the scrawl of a wet pen--the brazen gates of Death and Terror ever\par
strike cold to the heart of the rufous Hapsburg Archduke?  Madness, we\par
know, is in the blood of his evil-fated House.  But, when the shots from\par
a Bosnian High School student's revolver pierced Franz Ferdinand's brain\par
and body, was he sane enough to realise that the crime of the Anarchist\par
had saved his own name from foul, indelible, and hideous infamy?  We\par
shall know when the trumpet of the Archangel sounds the Last Reveille,\par
and the grave gives up its dead, and the Sea spews forth its victims,\par
and the secrets of that deeper abyss, the human heart, are revealed in\par
the sheer, awful Light that streams from the Throne of God.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XIII*\par
\par
                             *THE SUPERMAN*\par
\par
\par
People had for some time been rising, passing out through the oxidised\par
silver-framed glass doors of Spitz's big brilliant dining-room; beyond\par
these the vestibule was now full to the walls, so that its palms and\par
tree-ferns rocked amidst the billows of a heaving human sea.  Many\par
guests lingered in conversation, standing in groups near the vacated\par
tables.  The glitter and blaze of jewels, adorning bizarre coiffures,\par
bare and powdered throats, bosoms, arms, and backs,--the dazzling\par
display of brilliantly-hued toilettes, made an _ensemble_ marvellously\par
gay.  And now, returning as they had arrived, but unattended by M.\par
Spitz, came the party of notables from the German Embassy, talking\par
together in loud, harsh, Teutonic accents.  Von Herrnung, erect,\par
stiffening to the salute as previously, remained in the rigid attitude\par
until the Ambassador had passed.  But this time the official finger\par
beckoned.  He turned, pushed back his chair, and in a stride, joined the\par
squat, elderly figure. The yellow-white, heavily-featured face with its\par
stiff brush of white hair above the square brain-box turned to him, the\par
deeply-pouched, shrewd grey eyes looked past _him_ to the table he had\par
left.  The coarse mouth under the white moustache with the brushed-up\par
points, uttered a few emphatic words.  Then, with a slight nod, the\par
representative of the All Highest at Berlin passed on.  The swing-doors\par
opened and shut behind him and his following.  And von Herrnung rejoined\par
his party, saying with a queer, excited breathlessness:\par
\par
"The ladies will pardon....  His Excellency had something to say!"\par
\par
The ladies were rising, looking for their theatre-wraps. He deftly\par
lifted the barbaric garment of green velvet and sable-edged ermines from\par
the back of Miss Saxham's chair, and, opening it, held it to receive her\par
tall, luxuriant person, mentally commenting:\par
\par
"With such hips, such a bosom, and such shoulders, the jade must be\par
twenty-eight or nine."  And remembering how boldly she had said to him\par
that she liked red men, he thought: "Amusement here....  Nothing needed\par
but time and opportunity--which this Bosnian affair reduces to a\par
minimum."  "_Gnaediges Fraulein_ will you not put on your _mantel_?"\par
\par
She told him that she was too hot.  He insisted, with all the Teuton's\par
dread of chill:\par
\par
"But it will be cooler in the vestibule, and cooler still when we are\par
driving.  Do we not go on to a theatre?  I think Lady Wathe has told me\par
so?"\par
\par
She shrugged her splendid shoulders.\par
\par
"Nothing so proper.  The _Jardin des Milles Plaisirs_, on the Champs\par
Elysees.  We're all dead nuts on seeing the new dance from Sao Paulo.\par
The thing that has exploded Tango and Maxixe, you know.  Look!--the\par
others are moving.  Don't let's lose them!  No!  I won't take your arm.\par
Please carry my wrap with your coat."\par
\par
"I will put my coat on.  Then I shall better carry your _mantel_."\par
\par
An attendant deftly hung von Herrnung's thin black, sleeveless garment\par
over his broad shoulders, and gave him his white silk wrap and soft\par
crush felt.  He slipped a coin into the man's palm, its small value\par
being instantly reflected in the features of the receiver, and moved\par
towards the swing-doors with Patrine.  She said, as a slight block\par
momentarily arrested their progress:\par
\par
"What are they all jabbering about?  Who has been assassinated?  What\par
has happened at this place with the crack jaw name? ..."\par
\par
"Sarajevo..." came in von Herrnung's guttural accent.\par
\par
"Sarajevo....  Not that I know where it is," said the deep warm voice,\par
that was more like a young man's baritone than a young woman's\par
contralto.  And von Herrnung answered, with a renewal of that tingling\par
thrill:\par
\par
"Sarajevo is the capital of Bosnia in Eastern Europe. When Austria\par
annexed Bosnia and Herzegovina in 1909, she made her seat of Government\par
at Sarajevo.  The Slavs grumbled.  They wished for union with\par
Servia--that little nation of pig-breeders! ... They themselves--the\par
Bosnians--are stupid peasants, _duemmer Teufels!--Schafskopfs_! They\par
cultivate their land with the wooden ploughs that were used at the date\par
of the Trojan War....  But this does not interest you at all, I think?"\par
\par
"How do you know it doesn't interest me?"\par
\par
"Because dress and jewellery and amusement are the chief things in your\par
life, _gnaediges Fraeulein_.  You are not even interested in _der\par
Politik_, or in the higher _Kultur_.  The social progress of your own\par
country is nothing to you.  You are too----"\par
\par
"Too frightfully stupid....  Thanks!"\par
\par
"I did not say too stupid," von Herrnung contradicted. "But if you were\par
stupid, you are too hellishly handsome for that to matter in the least."\par
\par
To be called hellishly handsome pleased her.  Her eyes gave him a\par
flashing side-glance.  As a surge in the crowd pressed her curving hip\par
against his tall, muscular body, she took his offered arm with a rough,\par
brusque grace.  They were near the swing-doors when she spoke:\par
\par
"Tell me about the Sarajevo business....  Who is the official swell the\par
Trojan ploughmen have hoisted--as Lady Beau would say?"\par
\par
"I will tell you.  It has happened only this morning----"\par
\par
She felt the man's powerful muscles thrill and become rigid with\par
suppressed excitement under the hand that rested on his arm.\par
\par
"Two personages of the highest rank have been horribly assassinated.\par
The Archduke Franz Ferdinand, _Kronprinz_ of the Imperial House of\par
Austria, and his wife; you have heard of the _Graefin_ Sophie Chotek,\par
created Duchess of Hohenberg?  Virtually she was\par
_Erzherzogin_--Archduchess--but the wife of the Archduke by a _mariage\par
de la main gauche_. A morganatic marriage--such unions have been heard\par
of in your virtuous England."\par
\par
They had passed the swing-doors now, and mingled, with the crush in the\par
vestibule.  Patrine said, signalling with a pair of long black suede\par
gloves and a vanity-bag of gilded metal chain-mail:\par
\par
"There's Lady Beau.  Behind the second column right of the entrance.\par
And here's Captain Courtley coming to hurry us up!"\par
\par
Courtley, smiling and unruffled as ever, dodged under the huge roseate\par
elbow of an immense lady in Oriental kincob tissues.  He gave his\par
message, turned and dived back again. The rich, womanly baritone of Miss\par
Saxham said, addressing von Herrnung:\par
\par
"Lady Wathe and Sir Thomas Brayham have gone on in Lady Wathe's\par
auto-brougham.  Lord Norwater has done a bunk.  Pretended he had an\par
appointment; he's been frightfully fed up with all of us this evening.\par
Lady Beauvayse says her chauffeur is on the string all right, but about\par
a million cars are ahead of him.  Why did your Austrian Archduke and his\par
wife go to that place in Bosnia if it wasn't healthy for Royalties?\par
Fancy!--they went to their deaths this Sunday morning!  Why does one\par
always forget it's Sunday in Paris?"\par
\par
"That English Sunday of yours," exclaimed von Herrnung, "is very good to\par
forget, I think!"\par
\par
She gave her deep, soft laugh.  He went on rapidly:\par
\par
"Of the Archduke and the Duchess I tell you, since you have asked me....\par
They inspected the troops--regiments of the Austrian garrison.  Then\par
they drove in their automobile along the Appel Quay, towards the\par
Sarajevo Town Hall.  They are passing beneath the shade of an avenue of\par
tamarind and oak trees when a bomb is thrown at them by a man hidden\par
among the branches....  The Archduke is very prompt--he wards off the\par
bomb with his arm.  He is not then hurt, nor is the Duchess.  But his\par
_Adjutant_--in the car behind them--is wounded in the neck. When they\par
arrive at the Town Hall the Mayor commences the address of welcome.  To\par
him Franz Ferdinand says angrily: '_Halt den Mund!_ ... Shut up, you\par
silly fellow! What the big devil is the use of your speeches?  I came to\par
Sarajevo on a visit, and I get bombs thrown at me....  It is too damned\par
rotten for anything! ..."\par
\par
"Yes, yes! ... Go on!"  She bit her lips, fighting a nervous impulse to\par
laugh.\par
\par
"So the Imperial cortege drove away, and a student threw at the Archduke\par
another bomb.  It did not explode, so he shot him with an automatic\par
revolver, an American Browning. The Duchess tried to cover him with her\par
body, and the assassin shot her also.  The Archduke begged her to live\par
for their children, but both victims died as they were being taken to\par
the Governor's house....  They have arrested the assassins, he who tried\par
to kill, and the fellow who succeeded....  They are both young, and men\par
of Serb race.  They are rebels all--they hate their Austrian rulers.\par
Sarajevo is swarming with fellows of the same breed...."\par
\par
"What will the Austrian Government do to them, now they've caught them?"\par
\par
"To the regicides," von Herrnung returned harshly, "Austria will\par
do--nothing that very much matters.  It is not an important thing to\par
destroy two trapped rats.  But I think there will be an ultimatum from\par
Vienna to the Servian Government; and if the terms of that are not\par
complied with, then the Emperor of Austria may give the signal for his\par
monitors upon the Donau to open fire upon the capital of Belgrade."\par
\par
Patrine asked negligently, as a new surge of the crowd thrust her tall,\par
lithe figure away from her companion's, forcing her to tighten her hold\par
upon his arm:\par
\par
"'Monitors?' ... I used to think monitors were big schoolboys and\par
schoolgirls.  Senior pupils told off to keep order.  I was one myself\par
once....  Chosen because I was bigger, and noisier, and naughtier than\par
any other girl in my class...."\par
\par
"Ha, ha, ha! ... Praechtig! ... That is capital!"  She could feel the\par
laughter shaking his big ribs.  "That is just what they are--those\par
monitors of the Donau.  Each is a big girl who keeps order _von anderen\par
Sorte_.  But they have turned-up noses, not Egyptian and beautiful like\par
yours!"\par
\par
He added, with the calculated roughness that had previously pleased her:\par
\par
"You shall now put on your _mantel_.  For the car, I see, is open."  He\par
shrugged his broad square shoulders closer into his overcoat and pulled\par
up the collar about his throat, saying ill-temperedly: "Always does one\par
find it with the English.  It is _laecherlich_--that passion for the\par
air."\par
\par
"Lovely, did you say? ..."\par
\par
Ignorant or careless that he had said "ridiculous," Patrine suffered him\par
to wrap her mock ermines about her, seeing above the frieze of waiting\par
figures that filled in the lower part of the picture framed by the\par
portico, the emerald-green bird-of-Paradise plume of Lady Beauvayse\par
whisk into the big white Rolls-Royce, past the neat black-haired head of\par
Courtley, and the peaked cap and pale Cockney profile of Morris, the\par
chauffeur.  She threw back a jest as she passed out:\par
\par
"I'm glad you think it lovely.  It's one of the nicest things about\par
us--that we're keen on soap and water and can't do without lots of fresh\par
air."\par
\par
She was in the car before his outstretched hand could touch her.  He\par
followed, letting Courtley precede him because he wished to sit\par
opposite, and the great Rolls-Royce purred out of the jam beneath the\par
illuminated glass archway, and in a moment was out of the Place Vendome\par
and moving with the stream of vehicles down the Avenue of the Champs\par
Elysees.  In the mingling of moonlight and electric light the tawdry\par
paste jewels of Patrine's preposterous diadem rivalled the costly\par
splendours of the jewelled fillets adorning Lady Beauvayse's coiffure,\par
her _panache_ of white osprey flared above her broad, dark brows as\par
insolently as though they crowned a Nitocris or a Cleopatra. But--and\par
here was a titillating discovery--the strange face with its broad brows,\par
wide, generously-curving cheeks, and little rounded chin, did not belong\par
to a woman of thirty, or even twenty-five.  She was much younger than\par
the German, who plumed himself upon his _flair_ for the accurate dating\par
of women, had at first credited.  It would be amusing--he told himself\par
again--hellishly amusing, to cultivate this curious hybrid, half hoyden,\par
half _femme-du-monde_.\par
\par
Sarajevo--still Sarajevo.  You caught echoes of the crime of that\par
morning in the tongues of twenty nationalities upon the Paris boulevards\par
that night.  People in automobiles and open carriages, people in the\par
little red and blue flagged taxis, people crowding the auto-buses and\par
Cook's big open brakes, the army of people on foot, endlessly streaming\par
east and west along the great splendid thoroughfares, tossed the name of\par
the Bosnian capital backwards and forwards, as though it had been a\par
blood-stained ball.\par
\par
A gay masculine voice called from a knot of chatterers standing near the\par
wide illuminated archway of electric stars and crowns and flowers under\par
which streamed a variegated crowd of pleasure-seekers as the big\par
Rolls-Royce deposited its load:\par
\par
"_Nom d'un chien_!  What a pack of assassins these Serbians! ... And\par
yet--what if the whole show were got up by Rataplan at Berlin? ... His\par
bosom friend, you say--the big Franz Ferdinand?  _Zut!_ what of that?\par
... Sometimes one finds inconvenient the continued existence of even a\par
bosom friend."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XIV*\par
\par
                         *A PARIS DANCE-GARDEN*\par
\par
\par
By "Rataplan" was meant the Kaiser, Patrine comprehended, as her\par
companion glanced over his shoulder at the candid speaker, muttering\par
something that sounded like a German oath.  But Lady Beauvayse was\par
twittering through a filmy screen of verd-blue chiffon, now discreetly\par
enveloping her lovely Romney head:\par
\par
"We're going to hunt up Lady Wathe and Sir Thomas. Take care of Miss\par
Saxham, Count von Herrnung, in case we get separated in the crush....\par
Don't forget our programme, Pat.  A whiff of Cafe Concert ... Colette\par
Colin is billed to sing some of her old songs and the very newest of the\par
new ones....  Then we're coming to the Pavilion de la Danse to see the\par
Sao Paulo sensation....  La Rivadavia and Herculano, and all the rest of\par
the crowd....  Meet you there....  So long!  Mind, you're not to get\par
lost!"\par
\par
"In London you often hear La Colette," said von Herrnung, as he paid the\par
lean-jawed functionary in the gold-laced light-blue uniform--the usual\par
notice of free entry having vanished from the entrance--and passed with\par
his companion into the gravelled promenade of the open-air concert-hall.\par
"But to-night you will hear no songs of old France, no Chansons\par
Pompadour nor Chansons Crinoline. She comes to this place from her own\par
theatre to oblige an old comrade.  There is Nou-Nou in that box with\par
some smart women and the Turk who wears our Prussian Order of the Red\par
Eagle with the Star and Crescent of the Medjidie.  He is Youssouf Pasha,\par
the Sultan's Envoy-Extraordinary. Nou-Nou has brought him to hear La\par
Colette.  Shall we not sit here?"\par
\par
"Who is Nou-Nou?" Patrine asked, as she settled her tall, luxuriant\par
person on one of the little green-painted iron chairs.\par
\par
"Who is Nou-Nou?" her companion echoed.  "You saw her to-day at\par
Longchamps in her black confection. Everybody was looking....  She is\par
wonderfully _chic_--Nou-Nou! May I be permitted to light a _zigarre_?\par
..."\par
\par
"Do! ... But--why is she so much the rage?  She isn't even pretty, your\par
Mademoiselle Nou-Nou."  Patrine said it with her bright gaze fastened on\par
the famous Impropriety who had paraded under the chestnuts of Longchamps\par
in the sheath of black gauze unlined, save with her own notorious\par
attractions--both irresistible and fatal, judging by their recorded\par
effects upon excitable Parisian _viveurs_ and _gommeux_.  She saw a\par
triangular and oddly-crumpled face, rouged high upon the cheek-bones in\par
circular patches, a pair of almost extinguished eyes, indicated by\par
streaks of blue pencil, and caught a sentence screamed at the stout Turk\par
in a voice like a hoarse cockatoo's.  Boldly erect upon the skull\par
adorned by a scanty thatch of lemon-yellow balanced a black feather,\par
long and attenuated as the wearer. Nou-Nou's stick-like, fleshless arms,\par
the cadaverous and meagre torso unblushingly revealed by the transparent\par
casing of her upper person, might have enthralled a keen student of\par
anatomy.  But of feminine charms, in the accepted sense of the word, she\par
possessed not one, it seemed to Patrine.\par
\par
"Do not look at her too hard, or she may send round and invite you to\par
supper," warned the laughing voice of von Herrnung speaking close to her\par
ear.  "She has all the vices--the good Nou-Nou!"\par
\par
"Including the vice of indiscriminate hospitality," Patrine laughed; but\par
a little uncontrollable shudder rippled over her as she withdrew her\par
eyes from the painted, crumpled visage, leering with half-extinguished\par
eyes from under the canary-coloured wig.\par
\par
"That is so.  Tell me--you and Lady Beauvayse seem great friends--quite\par
inseparable...."  He leaned nearer, his bold eyes closely scrutinising\par
her face.  "How comes it that she leaves you alone in a Paris\par
dance-garden: with me, whom you have met to-night--for the first time?"\par
\par
"She knows I can take jolly good care of myself, wherever and with\par
whomsoever I may happen to be!"  Her black brows frowned; it was evident\par
she resented his criticism. "And--what are you getting at?  What's the\par
matter with poor old Paris?  You know--perhaps it sounds odd!--but I've\par
never been in Paris before....  And I love it! Down to the ground--it\par
suits me!  It's so gay and brightly-coloured and pagan.  The public\par
buildings and parks are dreams; the shops--too entrancing for anything!\par
And this place, with its jabber and music and stagy illuminations,\par
trellises where real roses mix up with artificial ones--ornamental beds\par
of geraniums and calceolarias and thingumbobs bordered with smelly\par
little oil lamps, gilt band-stands, concerts, and lovely trees in\par
blossom....  Is it so luridly awful?  To me, it's rather sweet!  Of\par
course the dancing--everybody knows the dancing is pretty well the\par
limit.  But one has seen such a lot of Tango in London--the bloom will\par
be pretty well rubbed off!"\par
\par
"Yet some lingers.  You have still something to learn from Herculano and\par
La Rivadavia! ..."\par
\par
"Do I strike you as such a perfect daisy of inexperience?"  Something in\par
his tone stung, for the full white cheeks coloured faintly.  "You didn't\par
talk to me at dinner as though I were one!"\par
\par
"How could I help that?" he asked, with the roughness that had\par
previously intrigued her.  "Am I to blame that you look like Phryne or\par
Aspasia when you are only Mademoiselle de Maupin--before she set out\par
upon her travels? For you have only got as far as Paris with your friend\par
Lady Beauvayse.  Why does she bring you?  I am curious to know."\par
\par
"Because I am her paid secretary and amanuensis."  Patrine brought the\par
words out with a rush; it was clear that she thought the candour a\par
necessity, but hated it.  "She can't get on without one, and her\par
husband, Lord Beauvayse--awful little bounder!--won't stand her having a\par
man.  Don't great ladies have secretaries in Germany?  Can't you see me\par
doing Lady Beau's correspondence in my fearful fist--enclosing cheques\par
to people who solicit donations for charities with a committee and Hon.\par
Treasurer--tearing up the begging letters full of howlers in the\par
spelling-line--smelling of bad tobacco and beer or gin?  Then I have to\par
keep her posted in her engagements, go to shows, and functions, and\par
kettledrums with her when she hasn't a pal handy--that's where my share\par
of the fun comes in.  Just as I'm visiting Paris, as I dare say I shall\par
visit other centres of lively iniquity--in the character of the\par
sheep-dog that doesn't bow-wow at the wrong man!"\par
\par
"You should bow-wow at me."  His teeth were hidden, but his eyes were\par
crinkled up with soundless laughter. "For I am a very wrong--a very\par
wicked man!"\par
\par
"How sad!"  Her brows were still frowning, but her wide red mouth was\par
beginning to curl up at the corners. "Couldn't you reform?  Is it too\par
late?"\par
\par
"I hope so!" he answered her.  "For if I were good I should possess no\par
attraction for a woman of your type.  And to charm you I would give my\par
soul--if I had a soul!"\par
\par
"Great Scott!  You're candid....  Modest too.... And complimentary!"\par
\par
"I am candid, because I cannot help myself."\par
\par
Three comedians had come upon the stage.  She told him not to talk to\par
her.  She wanted to see the turn; she liked music-hall stuff.  He\par
obeyed, mentally congratulating himself on having ascertained her social\par
status, something better than a typist, hardly on the same level with\par
his sister Gusta's _dame de compagnie_.\par
\par
While his bold eyes read the book of her provoking beauty, the\par
performance on the stage, backed by the deep green palmate foliage and\par
white or ruddy candelabra-like blossom-sprays of the chestnuts, framed\par
by a broad band of electric lamp-flowers, was culminating to its final\par
gag.  A preposterously fat man attired in the historic low-crowned hat,\par
Union Jack waistcoat, brass-buttoned blue tail-coat, leathers and\par
hunting-tops of the traditional John Bull, another comedian in the\par
legendary costume of M. Jacques Prud'homme, and a truculent-looking\par
personage whose Teutonic French accent, spiked silver helmet with the\par
Prussian eagle, First Imperial Guards cuirass and tunic, breeches and\par
spurred jack-boots, in combination with a well-known moustache with\par
upright ends, a huge Iron Cross, and a great many other property\par
decorations, left no doubt as to the political bent of the scrap of\par
pantomime.\par
\par
It was an ordinary bit of comic knockabout, to which the tragic\par
circumstances of the day lent a peculiar tang.  One has seen it before,\par
played by the three comedians, in the green-baize aprons, brown duffel\par
knee-breeches, and shirt-sleeves sported by the waiters of low-class\par
Paris or Munich brasseries.\par
\par
In the centre of the stage, instead of a bright-hooped beer-barrel on a\par
wooden cellar-stand, was a revolving globe representing the World.  And\par
each of the three comedians, being armed with a tumbler, a spile-awl,\par
and a spigot-tap, proceeded, with appropriate patter, gesture, and\par
grimaces, to insert his spigot, draw, and drink.  John Bull turned the\par
globe to the United Kingdom, and tapped the big black patch in East\par
Middlesex.  Creamy-headed London porter filled his glass.  He held it\par
up, nodded a "Here's to you!" and toped off.  M. Prud'homme punctured\par
France in the rich vine-growing district of Epernay.  Champagne crowned\par
the goblet, and he drank in dumb show to Gallia, the land of love,\par
laughter, and wine.  It was then the turn of the Teuton.  He bored, and\par
Brandenburg yielded a tall bock of foaming blonde lager.  He sucked it\par
down with guttural _Achs_ of delight.\par
\par
But this was not all.  John Bull exploited the East Indies. A stream of\par
rubies and emeralds filled his glass.  He bored deep in the Union of\par
South Africa--diamonds and gold-dust heaped the vessel.  Fired by his\par
success, M. Prud'homme inserted his spigot into wealthy Bordeaux,\par
whipped it out, applied his lips, and drank deep.  He corked the oozing\par
spot and tapped Algerian Africa.  Coffee rewarded him, fragrant and\par
richly black.  He next exploited Pondicherry, Chandernagore on the\par
Hooghly, French Equatorial Africa, and New Caledonia.  Nothing came.  He\par
tried Cochin China, and drew off a glass of yellow tea at boiling-point.\par
Encouraged to drink the strange beverage by the appreciative pantomime\par
of his British neighbour, he swallowed it, with results of a Rabelaisian\par
nature, at which everybody laughed heartily, including Patrine.\par
\par
It was now the turn of the Teuton.  He drew German beer from Togoland,\par
Cameroon; German South-West and South-East Africa yielded an indifferent\par
brand of the beverage.  German New Guinea in the Pacific, the Solomon,\par
Caroline, and other islands, with Asian Kiao-Chao, merely wetted the\par
bottom of the glass with a pale fluid, German beer by courtesy.\par
"_Sapperlot_!  _Der Teufel_! _Kreuzdonnerwetter_!"  He tasted, spat,\par
stamped, and sputtered forth strange expletives, M. Prud'homme's terror\par
at these unearthly utterances being provocative of more humour of the\par
Rabelaisian kind.  Then he decided to try again, excited to envy by the\par
spectacle of the stout Briton drawing gold from Australia, gold from\par
Canada, gold from New Zealand and the West Indies, and gold from Ceylon,\par
gold from the Crown Colonies in China, gold from the Gold Coast, gold\par
from Rhodesia and Nigeria, gold from everywhere; filling the capacious\par
pockets of his blue brass-buttoned coat, of his tight breeches, of his\par
nankeen waistcoat, until he bulked enormously, a Bull of Gargantuan\par
size.\par
\par
Such wealth roused respect in Prud'homme, who esteems the yellow metal.\par
He embraced the Briton, heartily congratulating him.  This roused the\par
Teuton's ire.  He seized the spigot and once more plunged it into\par
Germany, Prussia, Bavaria, Saxony--each of the States yielding beer,\par
beer, BEER.  He went on, tapping, filling, and guzzling.... Twelve full\par
tumblers and he had begun to swell most horribly.  Fifteen--and his\par
rotundity equalled that of John Bull.  But one State remained untapped.\par
He swilled down the twenty-fourth bock, drawn out of Lubeck--plunged the\par
spigot into the Reichsland--once Alsace-Lorraine----\par
\par
And the big glass crimsoned with a sudden spurt of blood.\par
\par
It was over in an instant, the comedians had skipped nimbly from the\par
scene, the globe had developed a pair of very thin human legs and\par
followed them off at a proscenium-wing, before many of those who had\par
witnessed, clearly understood.  Only the men and women of Gallic race\par
among the cosmopolitan, polyglot audience answered with a deep, inward\par
thrill to the ruby gush that told how the blood of France still ran red\par
in the throbbing arteries of the beloved, reft, alienated province, in\par
spite of her forty-five years of separation, in defiance of the loathed\par
laws, customs, language, service, all the gyves rivetted on her by the\par
Teuton, her conqueror.  Now round after round of applause signified\par
their comprehension.  But the comedians did not answer the call.\par
\par
Von Herrnung, who had worn the same contemptuous smile for every phase\par
of the clumsy by-play, relaxed his stiff features.  A stout tenor from\par
the Opera appeared and sang a Spring song by Tchaikovsky, following it\par
with the exquisite Serenade of Rimsky Korsakov, "Sleep, Sad Friend."\par
\par
The tenor was recalled.  Colette Colin succeeded him. She sang "_Notre\par
Petite Compagnon_" and "_La Buveuse d'Absinthe_" to the accompaniment of\par
a pale, lean, red-nosed man with a profile grotesque as ever adorned a\par
comic poster; who touched the piano-keys as though they were made of\par
butter; and had a way of sucking in his steep upper-lip and cocking his\par
eye at the star as he waited on her famous efforts, that made Patrine\par
shake with suppressed laughter on her green iron chair.\par
\par
The last ironic line of Rollinat's ballad, marvellously uttered rather\par
than sung, died out upon a stillness.  A storm of approval broke.  Men\par
and women stood up applauding in their places, and the singer came back,\par
to sigh out the bitter-sweet lyric of Jammes, "_Le Parle de Dieu_."\par
Then, while her name still tossed on the surges of human emotion,\par
backwards and forwards under the electrics, Colette Colin, the pet of\par
Paris, the eclipser of the famous Theresa, was gone.  Something of the\par
yearning anguish of Jammes, who sees Religion as a dusty collection of\par
ancient myths and folk-tales; to whom Faith is mere superstition, but\par
who would give his all to be able to pray once more as in childhood, had\par
given the girl lumps in the throat as she listened to Colette Colin.\par
Though, unlike the sad, Agnostic poet, Patrine had no tender,\par
sentimental memories in connection with a mother's knee.\par
\par
Not from Mildred Saxham had she learned her first childish prayer, but\par
from a procession of nurses; beginning with "Now I Lay Me Down" and\par
"Gentle Jesus," instilled by Hannah, a Church of England woman,\par
continuing with the Lord's Prayer, insisted on by Susan, a Presbyterian;\par
culminating in the "Our Father" "learned the childer" by Norah the Irish\par
Catholic, a petition which--minus the final line--was just the same as\par
the Lord's Prayer.  Also the Creed in English, and a surreptitious "Hail\par
Mary" which brought about the sudden exit of Norah from the domestic\par
scene.\par
\par
For teaching Patrine and Irma about God and Heaven and all that, was\par
sufficiently interfering, said Mrs. Saxham, but when it came to Popery,\par
_rank Popery_, it was time the woman went.  So Norah ceased to be, from\par
the point of view of the little Saxhams--and He who had risen above the\par
horizon of childish intelligence, a Being vaguely realised as\par
all-powerful and awful, great and beneficent, stern and tender, sank and\par
vanished at the same time.\par
\par
But the Idea of Him remained to be merged in the personality of the\par
child Patrine's dada.  Dada, so handsome and jolly, and nearly always\par
kind to his rough little romping Pat.  The boy, Patrine's senior by\par
sixteen months, had died in infancy.  Captain Saxham was always gloomy\par
on the deceased David's birthday.  Mildred reserved a nervous headache\par
of the worst for the anniversary, the kind that is accompanied by temper\par
and tears.\par
\par
She was indifferent to Patrine, who resembled the Saxhams.  But she was\par
devoted to Irma, her own image bodily and mentally.  Thus nothing\par
interfered with Patrine's adoration of her father.  The handsome,\par
genial, ex-officer of cavalry was his daughter's god, until Mildred tore\par
away the veil of Deity, broke the shrine and cast down the idol, one day\par
when Patrine was fourteen years old.\par
\par
The girl learned that Captain Saxham's noisy fun and alternating fits of\par
rage were due to over-indulgence in brandy-and-soda.  That he gambled\par
away Mildred's income over cards and Turf speculations, as he had wasted\par
the sum of money for which his Commission had been sold. That he was\par
"not even faithful"--that he spent week-ends "at hotels with fast\par
women"; that he was not worthy the sacrifice Mildred had made for him.\par
\par
Had she not for his sake jilted his younger brother, Owen! Even on the\par
verge of their marriage; the presents received; the house taken and\par
furnished; the trousseau ready, everything perfect to the last pin in\par
the wedding veil.  Nobody could resist David when he chose to woo, but\par
why, why had Mildred yielded?  So fierce a sense of shame awakened in\par
the daughter as she listened, that it seemed to her as though her face\par
and body scorched in the embrace of an actual, material flame.\par
\par
"How could he? ... How could you? ... Betray Uncle Owen....  One of you\par
was as low-down as the other, to play a beastly, sneaking game like\par
that!"\par
\par
"You insult your mother and father.  Leave the room!" commanded Mildred.\par
And Patrine left it, vigorously slamming the door.\par
\par
Captain Saxham, who had sold out of the Army when Patrine and Irma were\par
respectively seven and six years old, never knew what he had lost in the\par
esteem of his elder daughter.  She loved him still, but he had ceased to\par
be her god. They lived at Croybourn and occupied three sittings at one\par
of its several Anglican Churches.  The Vicar, a strenuous man, whipped\par
in Patrine and Irma for Confirmation classes. They studied the\par
Thirty-Nine Articles, the Athanasian Creed, and dipped once more into\par
the Protestant Church Catechism, first instilled at the certified High\par
School for the Daughters of Gentlemen--an establishment they attended as\par
day-pupils, and were to leave, without passing the Oxford Secondary, in\par
the following year when Captain Saxham died.\par
\par
For David, that cheerful, easy-going Hedonist, dropped off the perch\par
quite suddenly, in the smoking-room of his London Club.  In life he had\par
been of the easy-going type of Christian, who avoids open scandal, and\par
hopes to die at peace with the clergyman.\par
\par
An attack of cerebral effusion had anticipated the clergyman.  Mildred\par
and Irma wept bitterly, Patrine sat dry-eyed.  Even in the face of the\par
new tombstone at Woking Cemetery, testifying to the many virtues of\par
David, as soldier, husband, and father, her stiff eyelids remained\par
unmoistened by a tear.  At the base of the scrolled Cymric Cross ran a\par
text in leaded letters:\par
\par
               BLESSED ARE THE DEAD WHO DIE IN THE LORD.\par
\par
\par
The undertaker had recommended the text to the widow because it\par
contained the right number of letters required to fit in at the bottom.\par
But did it fit in, Patrine had sometimes wondered, quite so\par
appropriately, at the close of her father's life?\par
\par
She treasured his portrait, taken at the age of thirty, the tinted\par
presentment of a handsome, stupid young officer, resplendent in the gold\par
and blue and scarlet of a crack Dragoon regiment.  It had fallen to her\par
keeping when her mother had re-married.  But she cherished no illusions\par
regarding the original.  How often, since her own eyes had been opened\par
to the fact of their existence, had she not screened David's vices from\par
strangers' eyes.\par
\par
She had made him her ideal, and Mildred had revealed him to her as\par
vicious, unprincipled.  She could not forgive her mother for telling her\par
those horrors, she, Mildred--seemed to forget whenever she was pleased.\par
But Patrine had never forgotten.  She would wake at night even now with\par
the dry sobs shaking her....  To have been able to believe in that dead\par
father as noble, chivalrous, good, would have been so sweet; she had\par
shed big surreptitious tears in sympathy with the anguish of Jammes, who\par
would have so loved to believe in the existence of Almighty God, and the\par
dear little Jesus, the Blessed Virgin, and the holy Angels, because\par
Faith is so restful, _si paisible_....\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER XV*\par
\par
                         *THE BITE IN THE KISS*\par
\par
\par
But von Herrnung was saying, as they moved with a straggling procession\par
of similar pleasure-seekers, over smooth sanded pathways between beds of\par
geranium and verbena and lobelia, ivy-leaved geranium and gaily coloured\par
foliage-plants, bordered with little twinkling lamps:\par
\par
"Shall I tell you what I have just heard as those people passed us?  The\par
tall man with the white moustache, and the chic little woman in the\par
Spanish _mantilla_.  She told her friend that we make a handsome couple.\par
Perhaps that makes you a little angry? ... Shall I make you still more\par
angry?  Well then, listen? ... If we were really a couple you would not\par
have that so-black hair...."\par
\par
"Why not?"  He had roused her curiosity.  She put away the little damp,\par
laced handkerchief.  "Would your cruel usage of me have turned it\par
white?"\par
\par
"Not that, but you would have added the one touch that makes perfection.\par
You are too sombre--too much like a night in October with all that\par
cloudy blackness....  You would have bleached and dyed your hair--not\par
yellow, nor yet orange--nor even flame....  The colour of beech-leaves\par
in winter, as one sees them burning against a snow-bank. And--all the\par
women would be crazy with jealousy--and all the men would be dying at\par
your feet!  For you would be Isis then--you would be the Sphinx-woman of\par
whom La Forgue wrote and Colette has sung to us.  You would be\par
hellishly, divinely beautiful!"\par
\par
"Hellish again."  She gave her low, deep laugh, prolonging it a trifle\par
stagily.  "What do you bet me I don't--do what you said?"\par
\par
"Bleach and dye...?"\par
\par
"That's it."  She nodded.  "To the colour of--what was it?\par
'Beech-leaves in winter.' ..."\par
\par
"Against a snow-bank."  He added: "The snow is your wonderful skin.  And\par
I will bet you four hundred and twenty marks--that is twenty pounds\par
English.  Is it agreed? ... Do you not say--Done? ..."\par
\par
"Twenty pounds...."  She shrugged her big white shoulders.  "My dear\par
man, I haven't got twenty pounds in this blessed old world!"\par
\par
He hesitated; finally said with reluctance:\par
\par
"I will lend you twenty pounds--it will cost you twenty pounds to have\par
your hair done here in Paris....  But you will be _sehr schoen_--the\par
money will be well spent.  No? ..."--for she had shaken her head,\par
frowning.  "It is offered--why will you not accept?"\par
\par
"Because I won't....  There are some things I draw the line at.\par
Borrowing money's one of them."\par
\par
"Then I will bet you my magpie pearl--you may have seen it"--he\par
displayed the ornamented little finger--"against that not-very-good\par
diamond you wear on your left hand."\par
\par
She burst out laughing and repeated through her laughter: "'Not very\par
good.'  I call that insulting....  When it cost me fifteen francs in the\par
Palais Royal.  Well, done with you!"\par
\par
"It is done!  But you have not done with me."  Von Herrnung's tone had a\par
new note of triumph.  He urged: "You go back to London--when? ... The\par
day after to-morrow....  _Gut!_ ... I have myself to visit London upon\par
business--I shall see Isis with her beautiful new hair.  One thing more.\par
An address where I may call and see it.  Be quick!  We turn down here!\par
..."\par
\par
Patrine protested, peering with narrowed eyes through the dusk-blue\par
twinkling semi-darkness.  "But no! ... That big marquee-thing at the end\par
of this avenue--with all the festoons of lights and the ring of\par
promenade about it--surely that's the _Pavilion de la Danse_?"\par
\par
"_Halt den Mund!_"  His hand closed peremptorily on her arm: he hurried\par
her down the trellised vine walk that invited on the left of them, as\par
light measured footsteps padded on the gravel, and a man ran past\par
calling, as it seemed, to somebody ahead:\par
\par
"Miss Saxham ahoy! ... Lady Beauvayse----"\par
\par
"He's calling me.  It's Captain Courtley...." Patrine persisted.\par
\par
"Let him call!  Are you not with me?"  Von Herrnung's tone was\par
masterful.  "You shall go to him when you have given me that London\par
address!"\par
\par
She was amused and yet annoyed by his persistency.\par
\par
"Oh, all right!  'The Ladies' Social Club, Short Street, Piccadilly,\par
West.'  That's where I'm generally to be found when I'm in town."\par
\par
"_Sehr gut_!  Tell me once again, then I shall not forget, no!"\par
\par
"Write it on your cuff!"\par
\par
"It is written in a safer place," he told her.  "We Prussian officers\par
are trained to remember without writing things down.  A face, an\par
address, a conversation, the outlines of a country.  Though for\par
_reconnaissance_ there is nothing like _die Photographie_."  He added:\par
"When we meet in London I shall be able to tell you everything you wore\par
to-night."\par
\par
"Really! ... How flattering! ... You've made a mental inventory?"\par
\par
They were retracing their steps to the avenue recently quitted.  He\par
walked with noiseless strides behind the tall, supple figure as it moved\par
between the trellised vines and roses, gowned with its flaunting diadem,\par
robed in the insincere splendours of the opera-mantle already described.\par
\par
"As you say.  I shall be able to tell you that the back of your _mantel_\par
was cut in a V-shape nearly reaching to your waist-line.  Shall I tell\par
you why?"\par
\par
"If you're keen to...."  She felt a scorching breath between her\par
shoulders and quickened her pace, making for the avenue.  But he moved\par
with her, his voice came thickly: "Because your back is so superbly\par
beautiful you cannot bear to hide it from men!"\par
\par
"_Ah-h!_"\par
\par
She whirled about, glaring like an angry leopardess, her strong white\par
arm upraised to strike.  Face, throat, and bosom glowed with painful\par
crimson.  Between her violated, insulted shoulders, his furious kiss\par
still burned and stung.\par
\par
"How dare you touch me!" she gasped.  But he had shot past her even as\par
she turned.  He was running towards the avenue, calling gaily:\par
\par
"Were you looking for us, Lady Beauvayse?  Here we are!"\par
\par
"Cad, cad!" she stammered.  "Insufferable! beastly!"  Then, because a\par
scene was quite out of the question, she went forward with head held\par
high, and resentment heaving her broad bosom, to meet Lady Beauvayse.\par
\par
"Pat!  You needle in a haystack," cried her friend, "where did you get\par
to?"\par
\par
"Nowhere.  We missed you at the Cafe Concert," Patrine began.\par
\par
"And then," von Herrnung explained, "we happened to take the wrong turn.\par
But we have not gone far before we are recalled."\par
\par
"--To the path of probity," suggested Lady Beauvayse, adding: "And in\par
this instance the path of probity leads to the _Pavilion de Chahut_."\par
She explained to Patrine: "_Chahut_ is the modern version of the\par
_can-can_--famous in the days of the Second Empire; when the great\par
cocodettes of the Court of the Tuileries--rivals of Cora Pearl and\par
Skittles and other naughty persons--did high-kicking under the rose\par
here, and they called the place Mabille."\par
\par
\par
It was not easy to get near the Pavilion, so dense and variegated a\par
crowd had congregated before its illuminated entrance.  But the entrance\par
fee was doubled.  Gold must be paid to see the famous Sao Paulo dance.\par
Thus many would-be pleasure-seekers of the less affluent kind turned\par
back disappointed from the row of gilt turnstiles under the blazing\par
archway, compelled to content themselves with the outer promenade.\par
\par
Breasting the human eddy caused by these, Patrine and her party passed\par
the barrier, climbed a flight of shallow gilt marble stairs carpeted\par
with pink plush and decorated with roses and tree-ferns and reached the\par
elevated promenade. Set within the circumference of the outer one, it\par
commanded a complete view of the circular ball-room, to whose level\par
descended from it at intervals yet other flights of broad gilt stairs,\par
similarly carpeted and flower-decked for the convenience of those who\par
wished to join the dancers, or return from the ball-room to the level of\par
the promenade.\par
\par
The revels were in full swing.  Standing upon the brink, looking down as\par
into a cockpit, you saw Patrine, superb in her false diadem and mock\par
ermines, leaning her bare white hand upon a velvet-covered rail.  At\par
first she could only make out a giddying whirl of arms and heads and\par
shoulders. Presently, the picture began to clear.\par
\par
To the wail, clang and clash of strange, discordant, exotic music,\par
rendered by an orchestra of coloured performers, two wide circles of\par
dancers rhythmically spun.  The floors they danced on were set at\par
different levels, and rotated automatically,--each floor revolving in a\par
different direction. Coloured lights, flung at intervals from reflectors\par
in the ceiling, conveyed to Patrine the impression of staring down upon\par
the whirling planes of a huge gyroscopic top.\par
\par
Only the central space of shining parquet was void within the double\par
circle of gyrating dancers.  A crash from the orchestra and three\par
couples, oddly costumed, leaped suddenly out upon the floor.  Patrine\par
could not make out where they had come from.  They appeared, and there\par
was a slight commotion.  A hedge of applauding spectators, four or five\par
deep, formed about the central, stationary patch of parquet.  The music\par
changed, the six Brazilians began the famous dance.\par
\par
They were not beautiful to look at it seemed to Patrine, the men,\par
familiarly styled by voices in the crowd as Lauro, Pedro, and Herculano,\par
being undersized, sleek-headed, lithe and sallow, attired in faultlessly\par
fitting evening dress-coats, white vests, black satin knee-breeches,\par
black silks, and buckled pumps.  They wore shallow collars of curious\par
cut, lawn-frilled shirts and wide black neckties.  Their female\par
companions were swarthy as Indians, even through their paint, and plain\par
of feature.  But their superb hair and eyes, the rounded grace of hip\par
and waist and limb, the slenderness of throat and wrist and ankle,\par
testified, like their tiny feet and high-arched insteps, to a strain of\par
Spanish blood.\par
\par
"La Rivadavia, Alexandrina, and Silvana," the eager spectators named\par
them.  They wore transparent sheaths, and brief, oddly _bouffante_\par
overskirts, like flounced muslin lamp-shades with a boldly suggestive\par
forward tilt.  They began the dance with some familiar Tango figures.\par
The poses, the approaches, the hesitations, were well known to Patrine.\par
\par
"Nothing very new....  But--the music made by those buck niggers!\par
'_Bizzarramente_' isn't the word for it. One expects to see gombos\par
covered with serpent-skin, trumpets of elephant-tusk, skull-rattles, and\par
all the paraphernalia of Obeah in the orchestra, instead of those huge,\par
superb brass wind-instruments, cymbals as big as table-tops and ten-foot\par
silver trumpets, like poor de Souza's....  Raised in the States, but\par
wasn't he a Brazilian by birth?"  It was the voice of Lady Beauvayse,\par
and von Herrnung's answered from behind Patrine:\par
\par
"It may be so.  But the _Blechinstrumente_ and the\par
_Blasinstrumente_--for the biggest of those they have to go to Germany.\par
Nowhere else can they be made as there.... Bravo! ... _Bis--bis!_"\par
\par
He applauded....  Everybody was applauding.  The gyroscopic whirl of\par
dancers had become stationary.  All now were eager spectators.  And the\par
three couples from Sao Paulo had reached the culminating point of a\par
uniquely curious and exotic figure.  Savage and violent, sinuous and\par
creeping; suggestive of the nocturnal gambols of enamoured jaguars, in\par
the deep primeval forests of Brazil.\par
\par
"Horrid!  One expects them to lash tails and roar.... I've got what Mrs.\par
Wiggs of the Cabbage Patch called 'cold clams walking up my backbone.'"\par
Lady Beauvayse shuddered and made a pretty grimace.  "All the same I\par
think I'll go down and look at them a little closer.  Ah-h! ... Good\par
grapes!  Why, he simply picked her up by the scruff of the neck with his\par
teeth and shook her....  I've just _got_ to see that done over again!"\par
\par
She was gone, with a whisk of the emerald bird of paradise and a waft of\par
_parfum tres persistant_.  Captain Courtley vanished in her wake.\par
Patrine made no motion to follow them.\par
\par
The tense excitement, the pungent exhalations rising from the crowded\par
ball-room were affecting her brain.  She felt giddy, and the steady\par
pressure of the crowd behind her was thrusting her to the very verge of\par
the promenade.  She yielded automatically, unconscious of danger near.\par
\par
You are to see her there, poised on the verge of the rose-carpeted\par
precipice, her hand gripping the velvet-covered railing, her wide\par
nostrils distended, her broad bosom heaving as she inhaled the sultry,\par
vitiated atmosphere, heavy with a myriad perfumes, tainted by a thousand\par
breaths.  Her stare, lifeless as the enamelled, glittering regard of\par
some Princess-mummy of Old Egypt, was fixed upon the artists, of whom\par
two couples had retired, as though in despair of competition with the\par
chief favourites, leaving La Rivadavia and her comrade Herculano in\par
possession of the floor.\par
\par
And the passions expressed by the rhythmical, sinuous movements of these\par
dancers grew moment by moment less human, and more bestial.  Art of the\par
most consummate was displayed and degraded.  Beauty and Truth shone\par
pre-eminent in the hideous display.  Now the woman sank towards the\par
ground, with supple limbs outstretched and her wild head thrown back in\par
fierce surrender.  Her white fangs gleamed, her dumb mouth seemed to\par
roar.  And as her conqueror crept stealthily towards her, the play of\par
his great muscles could be seen beneath his civilised attire, as though\par
his supple body had been clothed with the tawny-golden, black-dappled\par
hide of the Brazilian jaguar.\par
\par
As Herculano crouched and sprang, La Rivadavia's muscles visibly\par
tightened.  She bounded high, turned in the act....  Their gleaming\par
fangs clashed in mid-air.  And from the massed spectators came a hiss of\par
excitement, "_Th-h-h!_ ..." like the hissing of a thousand snakes.\par
\par
"Great Scott!" Patrine heard herself saying.  "_Great--Scott!_"\par
\par
She no longer heard von Herrnung harshly breathing behind her....  He\par
had moved to the leftward.  His tall, broad-shouldered figure now stood\par
against the railing some dozen feet away.  His well-cut face, seen in\par
profile, was purplish-red to the crisp, scarlet waves topping his high\par
square forehead.  The big white hands that held the glasses glued to his\par
eyes, jerked, and as he pressed against the railing Patrine knew that he\par
was shuddering.  Now he looked at her, and his ravaged face was\par
terrifying to the girl.\par
\par
"Will you not..." he began, thickly.\par
\par
She quivered, cast a look about; saw the ugly emotion under which he\par
laboured reflected in every face within her range of vision, as round\par
after round of plaudits rose to the roof of the pavilion, escaping\par
through the wide-open spaces between its gilded, rose-twined pillars\par
into the night.  The rafters vibrated with demands for a repetition of\par
the popular sensation.  The dancers accepted the encore.\par
\par
If von Herrnung beckoned now, asking Patrine to go down with him amongst\par
the acrid exhalations of that cockpit of variegated lights, thronged\par
with excited men and strangely-bedizened women, rent by devastating\par
emotions, drunk with strange excitements, would Patrine say Yes or No?\par
...\par
\par
_Ouf!_ but it was hot.  How thick the air was with those illusion\par
perfumes.  And from whence was that cool breeze blowing that suddenly\par
freshened the heavy air? ...\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XVI*\par
\par
                           *THE WIND OF JOY*\par
\par
\par
Patrine drew back from the edge of the promenade.  A stout, swarthy\par
Frenchman, a Southerner evidently, whose full brown face streamed with\par
little rills of perspiration, stepped nimbly into her vacated place.\par
His female companion instantly took his.  The same movement was\par
repeated--the packed bodies seemed to melt before her.  In a few more\par
steps she had merged from the crowd, upon the outer edge of the elevated\par
promenade.\par
\par
There was another velvet railing there, and steps leading down to the\par
promenade upon the ground-level.  Against the background of starlit sky\par
and illuminated gardens stood the tall figure of a man.  He was\par
broad-shouldered and lightly built, the poise and balance of his figure\par
admirable.  But for the gleam of his living eyes in his tanned face, and\par
the movements of his head as he turned it from side to side, evidently\par
seeking somebody, he might have been a statue of Mercury cast in\par
light-hued bronze.\par
\par
For he wore loose, waist-high leggings strapped at the ankles, and a\par
belted gabardine of thin light brown material, while a cap with an\par
upturned brim and ear-flaps dangled from his sunburnt hand.  And a\par
uniformed official, all lacquered moustaches and gold-laced blue cloth,\par
stood gesticulating a few paces from him, keen on defending from so\par
unceremonious an intruder the integrity of the Upper Promenade.\par
\par
"Monsieur cannot possibly descend into the ball-room ... the costume of\par
Monsieur is not appropriate.  It offends against good taste.  It\par
outrages the proprieties.... It is _peu convenable_ even that Monsieur\par
should be here."\par
\par
Patrine heard the protest, saw it driven home by swift expressive Gallic\par
gestures, caught a gleam of mirth in the eyes of the oddly-garbed\par
intruder, and the quirk of a smile at the corners of his mouth.  No\par
doubt the suggestion of the proprieties in connection with the\par
traditions of Mabille had evoked it.  She liked his face; it was lean\par
and hard and rather hatchety, with a brave outlook of clear light eyes\par
under the marked eyebrows, thick and straight and silvery-fair against\par
his sunburnt skin.  To her woman's eyes, Fatigue was stamped upon it and\par
anxiety, and a kind of rueful impatience, as he apologised for the\par
necessity of the intrusion in fragmentary but excellently accentuated\par
French. He came in search of a friend, who was here and must be found;\par
it was imperative...\par
\par
"There is to-morrow!--there is always to-morrow!" the official stated\par
with a wave.\par
\par
"That's just the point....  To-morrow! ..."  The stranger's forehead was\par
ploughed with lines of anxiety. He spoke in English now--the well-bred,\par
modern, clipped English of the public school and the University.  "No!\par
you don't understand"--for the official had vigorously disclaimed all\par
knowledge of the strange, barbarous tongue in which the other addressed\par
him.  "And I don't believe I'd ever make you.  If I could only hammer\par
into you what sort of a hat I'm in!"\par
\par
He knitted his brows; pulled himself together for a crowning effort.\par
Patrine spoke, not as a stranger yielding to a sudden, helpful impulse,\par
but quite simply, with a little, joyful catching of her breath:\par
\par
"Could I explain for you, do you suppose?"\par
\par
"A--thanks!  You're awfully good!"\par
\par
He turned to her eagerly, if with a certain embarrassment.\par
\par
"If you would....  There is a man here I have to get word to.  And--what\par
French I have is simply technical.... You hardly find it in modern\par
dictionaries--the argot of the engine-shop and the Flying School."\par
\par
"Now I understand...."  She smiled in his perplexed face, drinking in\par
deep breaths of the fresh fragrant air that blew about them as they\par
stood together behind the thick wall of bodies that hid the cockpit from\par
their view.  A deep dimple von Herrnung had never seen showed low down\par
in one of her pale cheeks.  Their whiteness was slightly tinged with\par
delicate pale rose.  And her eyes had lost their brilliant enamelled\par
hardness.  They shone like dusky stars as she went on: "Now I know why I\par
thought of wide green spaces and a breeze blowing to me over gorse and\par
heather as I looked at you.  Sub-conscious memories of Hendon and\par
Brooklands and Upavon.  For you're a Flying Man!"\par
\par
"Just that!"  His ruefulness was banished.  "And now you know how I come\par
to be in Paris with the clothes I stand up in and not another rag....\par
Two of us flew the Channel yesterday morning....  If the weather holds\par
decent, we should be on the wing again by four A.M.  And my mechanic's\par
given me the slip.  To say he's taken French leave would be appropriate\par
under the circumstances.  Left a line--the cool--beggar!--to say I'd\par
find him here."\par
\par
"Too bad!" she said, as fresh furrows dug themselves into the tanned\par
forehead.  "Not fair to leave you in the cart like that.  No wonder you\par
followed--hot upon his track."\par
\par
"Combed the whole place--everywhere they'll let me in. But my aviator's\par
kit's against me.  I've seen some rummy get-ups.  But they draw the line\par
at Carberry's overalls."\par
\par
One hand rested easily on his hip, in the other hand he swung the eared\par
cap with goggles.  A pedestal in the moonlight would have suited him.\par
It occurred to her to ask:\par
\par
"What was he like--your runaway mechanic?"\par
\par
"I hardly ... Oh! ... Little black-avised Welshman--barely tips the\par
scale at eight stone.  Has to be a light-weight, because I weigh all of\par
eleven.  And with the hovering-gear--but that can't interest you."\par
\par
"Indeed it does.  What of the hovering-gear?"\par
\par
His face darkened and hardened.  He said:\par
\par
"It's an invention of mine.  And after no end trying--our own people at\par
Whitehall simply wouldn't have anything to do with me--the chiefs of the\par
French Service Aeronautique consented to give it a test."\par
\par
"Sporting of them, wasn't it?"\par
\par
He agreed:\par
\par
"No end sporting.  So I bucked the tiger over the Channel with Davis--to\par
find that an officer and mechanic of the S. A. were told off to try the\par
hoverer over the selected area.  For us to engineer the thing ourselves\par
wasn't '_l'etiquette militaire_.'  That's the French for Government\par
red-tape."\par
\par
"Bother etiquette!  I'm beginning to sympathise with Davis!"\par
\par
His vexation broke up in laughter.\par
\par
"That's what _she_ did.  She sympathised with Davis and carried him off\par
here."\par
\par
Patrine said, a light breaking in on her:\par
\par
"Why, of course, there would be a girl....  He'd hardly come to a place\par
like this alone, would he?"\par
\par
Some query in his look made her add hastily:\par
\par
"What was she like?"\par
\par
"Like....  The girl who's carried off Davis? ..."  He reflected a\par
moment.  "Pretty and plump and fluffy, with a pair of goo-goo eyes!\par
She's daughter or niece or something"--he boggled the explanation\par
rather--"to the German chap who hired us the hangar at Drancy--if you\par
can give that name to a ramshackle shed in a waste building-lot!  And\par
Davis--thundering good man, but once on a spree..."  He whistled\par
dismally.  "If I could only get my claws on him! ..."\par
\par
Here the uniformed official returned to the charge:\par
\par
"Monsieur has found his friend--Monsieur has explained the situation.\par
To enter the _Salon de Danse_ with Madame is not permissible--in the\par
costume Monsieur displays. No doubt Madame will understand!"\par
\par
Patrine said, with a slight catch in her breath, as though some drops of\par
chilly pleasant perfume had been suddenly sprayed on her:\par
\par
"He supposes ... he thinks ... that I'm ... your friend!"\par
\par
"I'll explain."  He reddened, turning to the official, saying in the\par
French of the British schoolboy, laborious, devoid of colloquialisms:\par
\par
"_Monsieur, vous n'avez pas compris.  Madame elle--elle n'etes qu'une\par
etrangere.  Pour mon ami, je ne lui vois.  Si vous permettre d'entrer,\par
peut-etre----_"\par
\par
"_Rototo!  Voyez, man blousier, j'connais bien la sorte! Sufficit!\par
Assez!  Ca m' fait suer, comprends?_"  The gold-braided arm described a\par
magnificent sweep, the large white kid-covered hand indicated remote\par
distance--"_Sortez!_ ..."\par
\par
The Briton, thus invited to retire, looked at Patrine.\par
\par
"I can't quite follow, but it's plain he's telling me to hook it.  The\par
rest is--pretty--strong?"\par
\par
She nodded, biting her lip.\par
\par
"Frightfully rude.  Not that I know much Paris slang. But a friend of\par
mine--"  She broke off to listen, as from under the functionary's waxed\par
moustache rattled another sentence:\par
\par
"_A l'instant, ou j'appelle l' sergent d'ville!_"\par
\par
"He's talking about sending for the police now!"  She added hastily:\par
"Don't let him do that!  Offer him a tip!"\par
\par
The magic word must have been comprehended of the braided functionary.\par
He ceased to fulminate.  He waited, his avid eye upon the pair.  The\par
lean hatchety face of the aviator had flamed at Patrine's suggestion.\par
He said:\par
\par
"Don't you think I'd have tipped him in the beginning--if I'd had the\par
wherewithal?  But expenses have been frightful!--the waste lot with the\par
shed I've stalled the machine in costs as much as a suite of rooms at a\par
decent middle-class hotel would.  Had to fork rent in advance too.\par
Proprietor's a German as well as a jerry-builder, and when I've paid his\par
goo-goo girl for our coffee and rolls to-morrow morning"--the speaker\par
exhibited a disc of shiny metal bearing the classical capped and\par
oak-wreathed head of the Republic, value exactly\par
twopence-halfpenny--"I'll have just one of these blessed tin things\par
left."\par
\par
"How rotten!"  In the gilt metal vanity-bag, Patrine's inseparable\par
adjunct, lurked, in the company of a mirror, powder-puff, and note-book,\par
a tiny white silk purse.  In the purse nestled two plump British half\par
sovereigns, the moiety of Patrine's salary for the previous week.\par
"Would you jump down my throat if I asked you to let me finance you?"\par
she pleaded, an eager hand in the depths of the receptacle.  "Why not?"\par
\par
"Because I'm a decent man!"  If he had been previously crimson he was\par
now scarlet as a boiled lobster.  "Thanks all the same, though!  I can't\par
wait here, even to catch Davis....  I must bike back to Drancy, where\par
I've left the Bird--the machine--in the German's shed...  Not a soul to\par
keep an eye on her! ... My heart's in my mouth when I think of what\par
might hap--"  He bit off the end of the sentence and went on: "But if\par
you'd be so awfully kind as to take charge of this, in case you ...\par
There's a message written on it...."  He offered her a soiled, bent\par
card.\par
\par
"I understand.  If I should chance to come across your Davis....  A\par
little man ... looking like a Welshman.... But you haven't told me\par
whether he's dark or fair!"\par
\par
"Black as a crow," he told her.  "Not dressed like me!"  His well-cut\par
mouth began to twist upwards at the corners.\par
\par
"Quite a swell, in a silk-faced frock-coat, white vest and striped\par
accompaniments.  A silk hat, too, rather curly brimmed, but still, a\par
topper.  I suppose a friend of the lady's rented Davis the kit."\par
\par
"Of the lady's? ..."  She remembered.  "Yes, yes! Of course! ... The\par
German's appendage....  Why! ... Look! ... Those two people who have\par
just passed the turn-stile at the other end of the Promenade....  If\par
there's anything in description, here comes Davis with the goo-goo\par
girl!"\par
\par
"By--gum!  You've nailed me the pair of them."  As the aviator's long\par
strides bore him down in the direction of the little sallow,\par
black-avised mechanic in the capacious silk-faced frock-coat, and his\par
high-bosomed, florid, flaxen-haired enchantress, and before the\par
moustached guardian of the Promenade could renew his indignant protest,\par
Patrine had dropped the little sovereign-purse in his deep, rapacious\par
hand.  And at that instant the music ended with a crashing succession of\par
barbaric chords.  The Sao Paulo dance was done.\par
\par
"_Merci millefois, Madame!_ ..."\par
\par
Patrine turned from the hireling's thanks to see the high head and\par
powerful square shoulders of von Herrnung forging towards her, towering\par
above the polyglot, variegated crowd.  He hailed her with:\par
\par
"So you met a friend?  Is that why I found myself deserted?"\par
\par
She answered coldly:\par
\par
"I did not desert you--and I did not meet a friend."\par
\par
His face, still suffused with a purplish flush, pouched and baggy about\par
the eyes, told of the maelstrom of unhealthy excitement the dance of the\par
jaguars in the jungle had set whirling in his brain.  She guessed that\par
he had taken advantage of their separation to descend into the\par
ball-room, and that as one of the spectators in the front rank he had\par
revelled in the final thrill.  He persisted:\par
\par
"_Was_?  But what means it?  I have lost you.... I think you must have\par
gone down into the ball-room after your friend....  I follow and you are\par
not there.  I come back to find you....  Who was that dirty bounder I\par
saw you talking to?"\par
\par
"He wasn't a dirty bounder!"  His rudeness enraged her.  "He was a nice,\par
clean, first-class, top-hole, plucky English boy!"\par
\par
He sneered:\par
\par
"'_Boy_' ... Men of forty are boys, in the mouths of you English ladies.\par
You borrow the term from women of the street-walking class."\par
\par
"Then I'll call him a man.  The best kind of man going! English--from\par
the top of his nice head to the very tips of his toes."\par
\par
"How can you tell if he was not a friend of yours?  What do you know of\par
him?"  He fixed his eyes compellingly on hers.\par
\par
She answered:\par
\par
"Nothing but that he flew the Channel yesterday--with Davis--to test his\par
invention--and he has got to be on the wing for home at four."\par
\par
"So!  He has told you all this, and you do not know his name, even?\par
Perhaps it is on that card you hold in your hand?"\par
\par
She started, and the card fluttered from her twitching fingers to the\par
carpet.\par
\par
"Allow me...."  Von Herrnung stooped as though to retrieve the bit of\par
pasteboard.  "Curious!  It has gone! ... It is not there!" he said.\par
\par
"I think you have your foot on it."  Her eyeballs ached, she felt weary,\par
and flat, and stale.  "Please lift up your foot and let me see if it is\par
there," she urged, and grown suddenly obtuse, he lifted up the wrong\par
foot.  She was trying to explain that he had done so when they were\par
rejoined by Courtley and Lady Beauvayse.\par
\par
"Say, did you see she wore a head-band with a rubber mouth-hold at the\par
back of her neck?  And waist-fixings under her frillies so's Herculano\par
could swing her around his head.  My land! that man has jaw-power to\par
whip Teddy Roosevelt, and she's got vim enough for a nest of\par
rattlesnakes....  Used up, Pat? ... If you aren't, you look it!"  The\par
speaker yawned prettily: "I'm about ready to be taken back to by-by,\par
though it's only two o'clock."\par
\par
Von Herrnung escorted the wearer of the green bird of paradise as they\par
went through dark alleys and illuminated avenues back to the archway\par
with the blazing crowns and stars.  Courtley accepted the offer of a\par
lift back to the hotel.  The German declined, saying that he preferred\par
to walk, as the car was closed.\par
\par
"Pardon! ..."  His voice had arrested Morris on the point of starting\par
the Rolls-Royce.  His handsome face had appeared in the frame of the\par
car-window.  "Excuses! but this belongs to Miss Saxham!"  His cuff shone\par
white in the semi-darkness, the great magpie pearl on his little finger\par
gleamed maliciously as he dropped the missing card upon Patrine's lap,\par
and drew back, uncovered and smiling, as the car moved away.  Later on,\par
when she was safe in her room, she looked at the card, and read upon it\par
in plain black lettering:\par
\par
    +-----------------------------------------------+\par
    |                                               |\par
    |  ALAN SHERBRAND,                              |\par
    |                                               |\par
    |  PILOT-INSTRUCTOR AND BUILDER OF AEROPLANES,  |\par
    |  FANSHAW'S SCHOOL OF FLYING.                  |\par
    |                                               |\par
    |  THE AERODROME,                               |\par
    |  COLLINGWOOD AVENUE,                          |\par
    |  HENDON, N. W.                                |\par
    |                                               |\par
    +-----------------------------------------------+\par
\par
\par
Something was scrawled in violet pencil on the upper blank space.  Being\par
a girl with notions about squareness, Patrine would not at first read,\par
remembering that it was his private message to Davis, whom Chance had\par
brought within his master's reach.  But later still, or earlier, when,\par
after a brief interval of silence, the traffic of Paris began to roll\par
over the asphalt, principle yielded to impulse.  She switched on the\par
electric light above her pillow and read:\par
\par
\par
"_This Sarajevo business spells War.  Must get back at once to Hendon.\par
I trust to your Honour not to fail me.  You know what this means to_\par
\par
"_A. S._"\par
\par
\par
So the young Mercury in gabardine and overalls was a professional, a\par
teacher; a pilot who helped men to qualify for the certificate given by\par
the Royal Aero Club without breaking too many bones.  She had seen the\par
big painted sign in the Collingwood Avenue, Hendon, that advertised\par
Fanshaw's Flying School.\par
\par
"_I trust to your Honour_," he had written to his mechanic. The word\par
would have seemed big, and awful, and imposing, spelt like that, with a\par
capital "H," if the writer had been a gentleman.\par
\par
Disillusioned, she tore the card into little pieces and sank into a\par
heavy sleep before the broad yellow sunshine of Monday outlined the pink\par
velvet brocade curtains unhygienically drawn before the open windows.\par
And she dreamed, not of the magic wind that had blown upon her that\par
night, nor of the Mercury-like figure in the suit of Carberrys, but of\par
the supple bodies that had bounded and whirled, and of the gleaming\par
panther-fangs that had clashed in mid-air.  Then the dominant figure\par
became that of von Herrnung.  Again the red mouth under the tight-rolled\par
red moustache alternately flattered, insulted, and cajoled. Again she\par
felt that violation of her virgin flesh, its moist, hot touch upon her\par
naked shoulder.  Its kiss bit and stung.\par
\par
She awakened late from those poisoned dreams to a riotous blaze of\par
colour and a breath of musky fragrance.  On the coffee-stand beside her\par
bed lay a great sheaf of long-stalked roses; deep orange-hearted, with\par
outer petals of ruddy flame.  She plunged her face deep into the\par
flowers. The corner of a large square envelope thrust from amongst them.\par
She caught it between her teeth and pulled it out.\par
\par
It was from von Herrnung, written on paper bearing the device of the\par
Societe Aeronautique Internationale in the Faubourg St. Honore.  It was\par
brief enough.\par
\par
\par
"_That I offended yesterday, Isis will pardon.  The address I promised\par
is--'Atelier Wiber, 000, Rue de la Paix.'  The good Wiber demands no fee\par
for making Beauty yet more beautiful.  All has been arranged._\par
\par
"_Devotedly,_\par
       "_T. v. H._"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XVII*\par
\par
                       *INTRODUCES AN OLD FRIEND*\par
\par
\par
Saxham, M.D., F.R.C.S., M.V.O., Consulting Surgeon to St. Stephen's and\par
the Hospital of St. Stanislaus and St. Teresa, sat busily writing at the\par
big leather-topped table in the consulting-room, that, with the\par
well-stocked library adjoining, occupied the rearward ground-floor of\par
the Harley Street corner house.\par
\par
The hands of the table-clock pointed to eleven A.M.  Since nine the\par
doctor had sat at the receipt of patients, the crowd in the waiting-room\par
had melted down to half a dozen souls. Fourteen years had gone by since\par
Saxham, late Temporary Captain, R.A.M.C., attached Headquarters Staff,\par
H.I.M. Forces, Gueldersdorp, had taken over the lease and bought his\par
practice from the fashionable physician who had been ruined by the war\par
slump in South African mining-stocks.\par
\par
The broken speculator's successor had struck pay-reef from the outset.\par
Society had taken Saxham up and could not afford to drop him again.  He\par
was harsh and unconciliatory in manner--a perfect bear, according to\par
Society--but quite too frightfully clever; and as yet no speedier rival\par
had outrun him in the race.\par
\par
Now as the July sunshine, its fierceness tempered by the short curtains\par
of pale yellow silk that screened the wide-open windows, came streaming\par
in over the fragrant heads of a row of pot-grown rose-trees, ranged on\par
the white-enamelled window-seat, it shone upon a man to whom both Time\par
and Fortune had been kind.  The admirable structure of bone, clothed\par
with tough muscle and firm white flesh, had not suffered the degrading\par
changes inseparable from obesity. Nor had the man waxed lean and grisly\par
in proportion as his banking account grew fat.  His scholar's stoop\par
bowed the great shoulders even more, disguising the excessive\par
development of the throat and deltoid muscles.  The square, pale face,\par
with the short aquiline nose and jutting under-lip, was close-shaven as\par
of old.  The thickly growing black hair was streaked with silver-grey\par
and tufted with white upon the temples.  His loosely fitting clothes of\par
fine silky black cloth were not the newest cut, neither were they\par
old-fashioned. They were suited perfectly to the man.\par
\par
While Saxham minutely copied his prescription, the patient who sat\par
facing the window in the chair on the doctor's left hand had not ceased\par
from the enumeration of a lengthy catalogue of symptoms, peculiar to the\par
middle-aged, self-indulgent, and tightly-laced.  At the close of a\par
thrilling description of after-dinner palpitations, she became aware\par
that her hearer's attention had strayed.  Following up his glance she\par
ran him to earth in one of three tinted photographs that stood in a\par
triptych frame upon his writing-table, and glowed with an indignation\par
that tinged with violet a plump face coated with the latest\par
complexion-cream.\par
\par
"How very charming your wife is--still!"\par
\par
The speaker, her recent character of patient now merged in that of\par
visitor, plucked down her veil of violet gauze with a gesture that\par
betrayed her wrath.  But her voice was carefully honeyed to match her\par
smile--as she continued:\par
\par
"You have been married quite an age, haven't you?"\par
\par
The anniversary of her own second honeymoon was due next week.  She went\par
on answering her own query:\par
\par
"Nearly fourteen years, I think?"\par
\par
Saxham answered, not glancing at the silver table-almanac but at the\par
threefold photograph frame:\par
\par
"To be precise, just fourteen years and six weeks.  We were married on\par
the 6th of June, 1900."\par
\par
"You have a good memory--for some things!"\par
\par
The undisguised resentment in her tone pulled Saxham's head round.  He\par
surveyed her with genuine surprise.  She bit her lips and tossed her\par
head, waggling her tall feather, jingling her strings of turquoise and\par
amber, coral and onyx, kunzite and olivine, big blocks of which\par
semi-precious stones were being worn just then, strung on the thinnest\par
of gold chains.  Each movement evoked a whiff of perfume from the scanty\par
folds of her bizarre attire.  Her frankly double chin quivered, and her\par
redundant bosom, already liberally displayed through its transparent\par
covering of embroidered chiffon, threatened to burst its confining bands\par
of baby-ribbon, as the Doctor said:\par
\par
"Is it not natural that I should have a particularly clear recollection\par
of the greatest day of all my life--save one?"\par
\par
"You're quite too killing, Owen!"\par
\par
She laughed tunelessly, clanking her precious pebbles.\par
\par
"Of course, we all know you're fearfully swanky about your wife's\par
beauty.  I saw her yesterday at Lord's--sitting under the awning on the\par
sunny side, with the Duchess of Broads and Lady Castleclare.  Your boy\par
was with them, jumping out of his skin over Naumann's bowling for\par
Oxford. Really marvellous!  Your poor dear Cambridge hadn't a chance!\par
Tremendously like you he grows--I mean Bawne. Really, your very image!"\par
\par
"I should prefer," said Saxham, stiffly, "that my son resembled his\par
mother."\par
\par
"Ha, ha, ha!  How quite too romantic!"  She threw back her head, its\par
henna-dyed hair plastered closely about it and fastened with buckles of\par
jade, set with knobs of turquoise.  A kind of stove-pipe of enamel green\par
velvet crowning her, was trimmed with a band of miniature silk roses in\par
addition to the towering violet plume.  The plume, carefully dishevelled\par
so as to convey the impression of a recent wetting, threatened the\par
electric globe-lamp springing from a standard near.  Her crossed legs\par
liberally revealed her stockings of white silk openwork, patterned with\par
extra-sized dragon-flies in black chenille, and her laugh rattled about\par
Saxham's vexed ears like Harlequin's painted bladder, full of little\par
pebbles or dried peas.  "In love with your wife--and after fourteen\par
years and six weeks!"  Her fleshy shoulders shook, and her opulent bosom\par
heaved stormily.  She passed a little filmy perfumed handkerchief under\par
her violet gauze veil and delicately dabbed the corners of her eyes.\par
"You remind me of my poor David.  I was always the _one woman on earth_,\par
in his opinion.  To the last, he was jealous of the slightest reference\par
to you!"\par
\par
"To _me_?  Why should he have been?"\par
\par
Mildred--for this was Saxham's faithless bride-elect of more than twenty\par
years previously--swallowed her wrath with an effort, and went on with\par
the mulish obstinacy of her type:\par
\par
"Perhaps it was absurd.  But men in love are unreasonable creatures, and\par
David was perfectly mad where I was concerned.  He worshipped me to the\par
point of idolatry! He never could _quite_ believe that I did not regret\par
my--my choice--that my heart did not sometimes escape from his keeping\par
in dreams, and become yours again, Owen!  He never _really_ cared for\par
Patrine, because she has a look of you....  Absurd, considering that she\par
was born two years after you disappeared into South Africa....  Though\par
of course I could not truthfully say that I did not--think of you a\par
great deal!"\par
\par
It seemed to the silent man who heard, that Mildred offended against\par
decency.  His soul loathed her.  She went on:\par
\par
"Her brother--my darling boy who died--was the very image of David!"\par
Her tone was even womanly and tender in speaking of the dead boy.  "But\par
Patrine--a year younger--Patrine is really wonderfully like you, with\par
her commanding figure and almost Egyptian profile, those long eyes under\par
straight eyebrows--and all those masses of dead-black hair!"  As Saxham\par
writhed under the category she gave out her irritating laugh again.\par
"Ah!--I forgot! When Patrine was in Paris with Lady Beauvayse for the\par
Big Week--Lady Beau took her to the Atelier Wiber--the famous\par
hairdresser's establishment at 000, Rue de la Paix--where they\par
specialise in _Chevelures des Teintes Moderne_--all the newest effects\par
displayed by stylish mannequins--and really the change is\par
astonishing--her sister Irma and I hardly knew Patrine when she came to\par
see us at Kensington--looking superb, with hair--one might almost call\par
it terra-cotta coloured--showing up her creamy-white skin."\par
\par
"Do you tell me that Patrine has bleached her splendid hair and stained\par
it with one of those vile dyes that are based on aniline--or Egyptian\par
henna at the best?"\par
\par
Mildred retorted acidly:\par
\par
"It was a very expensive process....  Five hundred francs--but I\par
understand that Lady Beauvayse was so good as to insist on paying\par
Wiber's charges herself."\par
\par
Saxham answered brusquely:\par
\par
"I would have given ten times the money to know my niece's hair\par
unspoiled.  Whoever paid, the process will prove an expensive one to\par
Patrine when she finds herself excruciated by headaches, or when the\par
colour changes--as it will by-and-by!"\par
\par
Mildred shrugged:\par
\par
"She can have it re-dipped, surely?  Or let it return to its original\par
black!"\par
\par
"There are many chemical arguments against human hair so altered\par
returning to its original colour," came from Saxham grimly.  "As these\par
women who have made coiffures of orange, pink, crimson, blue and green,\par
fashionable, had previously found to their cost.  Do you not realise\par
that from mishaps of this kind resulted the chromatically tinted heads\par
one sees at public functions?  Bizarre and strange in the electric\par
lights, hideous in the sun."\par
\par
"Ha, ha, ha!"  Mildred's laugh rattled about the Doctor's ears like a\par
shower of walnuts.  "I shall certainly bring Patrine to call upon you,\par
if her hair happens to turn peacock-green or pinky-crimson.  I would not\par
miss seeing your face for all the world!  But seriously, my dear Owen,\par
when a girl is as handsome as my girl and has no _dot_ to back her, she\par
must make herself attractive and desirable to eligible men."\par
\par
"By trying to make herself look like a Parisian _cocotte_, she renders\par
herself neither attractive nor desirable--to the kind of man whom I\par
should like to see married to my niece. The cleanly kind of man, with\par
wholesome tastes, a sound constitution, and an upright character."\par
\par
"My dear Owen, you might be composing an advertisement for a butler or a\par
_chauffeur_!"\par
\par
Mildred ostentatiously controlled a yawn as the Doctor continued:\par
\par
"As to a provision for Patrine on her marriage, you know that I shall\par
gladly give it.  Of course, upon condition----"\par
\par
"Yes, yes, I know what your condition would be!"  Mildred's finger-tips,\par
adorned with nails elaborately veneered and dyed, drummed a maddening\par
little tattoo on the table-ledge.  "That she marries the 'right kind of\par
man, with wholesome tastes,' and all the rest of it.  The question\par
is--would Patrine be able to endure him?  She is--let us say--more than\par
a little difficult to get on with--and essentially an independent,\par
up-to-date girl."\par
\par
"If Patrine would have subdued her ideas about independence and given up\par
this idea of taking a place as salaried companion, I would have welcomed\par
her, and so would my wife!"\par
\par
"Patrine is--as you are very well aware--something very different to a\par
mere companion.  She is reader and secretary to Lady Beauvayse.  Her\par
Club subscription is paid, she moves there amongst gentlewomen, and is\par
treated at Berkeley Square exactly like a favoured guest.  You should\par
see the presents Lady Beauvayse absolutely showers upon her--and she\par
gets all her expenses and a hundred a year."\par
\par
Saxham was silent.  Patrine might have had all this and much more, if\par
she would have accepted the home he offered. Not only because she was\par
his niece, but the girl was dear to him.  His wife loved her, and in her\par
strange, wild way Patrine returned some measure of Lynette's tenderness.\par
\par
"She is worth loving," Lynette had told her husband. "She has a\par
generous, brave, independent nature and a deep heart.  She is not easily\par
won because she is so well worth winning.  Ah! if the Mother were only\par
with us, how well she would understand and help Patrine!"\par
\par
But Mildred had risen to depart.  Saxham rose too, not without alacrity,\par
and taking her offered hand, pressed it and let it fall to her side.\par
\par
"Well, good-bye.  My kind regards to Captain Dyneham."  He referred to\par
the second legal possessor of Mildred's once coveted charms.  "When can\par
I dine with you at Kensington, do you ask?  I fear I have very few\par
opportunities for sociality.  Some day! ... Tell Patrine to come and see\par
me.  Half-past one o'clock to-morrow. Lunch after my scolding--and a\par
chat with Lynette."\par
\par
"You are extremely kind to Patrine."  Mildred's tone was sweetly\par
venomous.  "But I fear just at present she has little time to spare.\par
Men in love are so exacting.  Dear me, what a feather-brained creature I\par
am! ... Haven't I told you about Count von Herrnung?"\par
\par
"You have told me nothing," said Saxham, "and you know it.  Who and what\par
is the man?"\par
\par
Mildred said with a great air of dignity:\par
\par
"He is a distinguished officer of the Prussian Flying Service, the son\par
and heir of a high official in the German Foreign Office.  He holds the\par
rank of Count by courtesy. I assure you I never met a more agreeable\par
young man."\par
\par
"Even were he all that you say, and more, and even while I regard the\par
German Army as a marvel of organisation and efficiency--I should not,\par
knowing the type of man that is the product of their military system,\par
desire my niece to marry a German officer."\par
\par
Mildred mocked:\par
\par
"'Marry'--who said anything about marriage? ... When they have not known\par
each other for a month.  Not"--her tone became sentimental--"that I am a\par
disbeliever in love at first sight.  No one could doubt that Patrine is\par
attracted, and he--the Count"--she dropped her eyelids--"is simply too\par
fearfully gone for words.  Absolutely dead-nuts!"\par
\par
"'Gone.' ... 'Dead-nuts.' ..."\par
\par
"I give you my word.  Entangled hopelessly.  'What a captive to lead in\par
chains,' I said to Patrine--he is quite six feet in height or over, and\par
has the most perfect features; simply magnificent eyes, the most\par
fascinating manner, and the build of a Greek athlete.  He is staying at\par
the 'Tarlton,' and I must say Lady Beauvayse is extremely sympathetic.\par
For since they came back from Paris together the Count has been taking\par
Patrine about everywhere.  She can hardly have had a glimpse of my gay\par
girl....  Dinners, theatres, the opera, and heaven knows what else, they\par
have crowded into the week!"  The smiling speaker shrugged her ample\par
shoulders.  "To say nothing of cabaret suppers and dances. He even\par
promises to take her to the famous 'Upas Club.'  Wonderful, by all\par
accounts.  They say the French Regency came nowhere near it.  Dancing in\par
the Hall of the Hundred Pillars, a simply wonderful three A.M. supper,\par
and champagne of the most expensive brands, served up in gold-mounted\par
crystal jugs."\par
\par
"Can it be possible? ..." broke from Saxham.  "Are you mad, that you\par
countenance this German in taking Patrine to such an infamous place?"\par
\par
"'Infamous!'  Really, Owen, your notions are too old-fashioned for\par
anything."  Her laughter broke out, and her chains and bangles jingled\par
an accompaniment.  "Do," she urged, "come out of your shell.  Dine with\par
us on Thursday. We have a box for the 'Ministers' Theatre.  We'll go on,\par
you and I, George and Irma, from there to the cabaret supper at the\par
'Rocroy.'  We can't afford the 'Upas,' the subscription is too fearfully\par
prohibitive.  But the entertainment at the 'Rocroy' is really\par
_chic_--the dancing is as good--everyone says--as they have it at\par
Maxim's.  Do come!  Of course, you can trust us not to blab to your\par
wife! Mercy! how severe you look!"  Her tone changed, became wheedling,\par
her made-up eyes languished tenderly.  "Odd! how we poor, silly women\par
prefer the men who bully us. Come!  One chance more.  Dine Thursday and\par
see 'Squiffed' at the 'Ministers'--try a whiff of Paris at the 'Rocroy'\par
after midnight, 'twill buck you up like nothing else--take my word!\par
Won't you?"\par
\par
"I will not!"\par
\par
"Why not?"\par
\par
"I have told you why not.  Because these places are centres of\par
corruption, schools for the inculcation and practice of vice in every\par
form.  Men and women, young or old, those who take part in or witness\par
one of these loathsome dances, hot and reeking from the brothels and\par
voodoo-houses of Cuba and the Argentine are equally degraded.  I had\par
rather see my niece Patrine dead and in her coffin than know her capable\par
of appreciating such abominable exhibitions, pernicious in their\par
effects, as I, and others of my profession have grave reason to\par
know!--ruinous in their results to body, mind, and soul!"\par
\par
"Intolerable!"\par
\par
Her plump, middle-aged face was leaden grey beneath her violet veil as\par
she screamed at him:\par
\par
"You have insulted me!  Horribly--abominably! ... How dare you tell me\par
that I frequent infamous places, and encourage my daughter to visit\par
schools of vice!  And it is not for Irma you are so rottenly scrupulous,\par
but for Patrine, your wife's favourite!  Who will do as she pleases, and\par
marry whom she prefers without 'by your leave' or with mine!  She is a\par
mule for self-will and obstinacy--another point of resemblance to\par
yourself! ..."\par
\par
He had recovered his stern self-possession.  His face was granite as he\par
said:\par
\par
"I have not insulted you, but if you will set no example to your\par
daughters in avoiding these evils, it is my duty to expostulate."\par
\par
She reared like an angry cobra, then spat her jet of scalding venom.\par
\par
"I take leave to think my present example quite harmless to Irma and\par
Patrine.  Now yours--of a few years ago--was certainly calculated to\par
damage the bodily and worldly prospects of your son."  She added, as\par
Saxham silently put out his hand to touch the bell: "No! please don't\par
ring.  I know my way out.  Good-morning....  Pray remember me to Bawne\par
and your wife!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XVIII*\par
\par
                             *SAXHAM PAYS*\par
\par
\par
Thus, having shot her bolt, Mildred departed.  The Dop Doctor standing\par
in the open doorway, watched the gaily-accoutred, middle-aged figure in\par
the peg-top skirt and bouffante tunic of green taffeta patterned with a\par
violet grape-vine, moving down the white-panelled corridor.\par
\par
Saxham watched her out of sight before he shut the door and went back to\par
his chair.  There he sat thinking.... No one would disturb the Doctor\par
until he touched his electric bell.\par
\par
Ah! if the truth were told, not all of us find solace in the thought\par
that in the niches of Heaven are safely stored our ancient idols.  To\par
Owen Saxham it was gall and verjuice to remember that for love of this\par
woman, weak, vain, silly, spiteful, he, the man of intellect and\par
knowledge, had gone down, quick, to the very verge of Hell.\par
\par
Mildred was just eighteen when he had wooed and won her.  She had been\par
slight and willowy and pale, with round, surprised brown eyes, an\par
indeterminate nose, and a little mouth of the rosebud kind.  Her neck\par
had been long and swanlike, her waist long and slim, her hands and feet\par
long and narrow.  He had desired her with all the indiscriminating\par
passion of early manhood.  He had planned to pass his life by her side.\par
He had hoped that she might bear him children--he had wrought in a\par
frenzy of intellectual and physical endeavour to take rank in his chosen\par
profession, that Success might make life sweeter for Mildred--his wife.\par
\par
She had seemed to love him, and he had been happy in that seeming.  Then\par
the shadow of a tragic error had fallen blackly across his path.  From\par
the omission to copy in his memorandum-book a prescription made up by\par
himself in a sudden emergency had sprung the branding suspicion that\par
culminated in the Old Bailey Criminal Case of the Crown _v._ Saxham.\par
His acquittal restored to him freedom of movement. He left the Court\par
without a stain on his professional reputation, but socially and\par
financially a ruined man.\par
\par
Friends and patients fell away from Saxham--acquaintances dropped him.\par
Mildred--his Mildred--was one of the rats that scurried from the sinking\par
ship.  She had thrown him over and married David, his brother.  Her\par
betrayal had been the wreath of nightshade crowning Saxham's cup of woe.\par
Those vertical lines graven on his broad white forehead, those others\par
that descended from the outer angles of the deep-cut nostrils to the\par
corners of that stern mouth of his, and yet those others at the angles\par
of the lower jaw, were chiefly Mildred's handiwork.  They told of past\par
excess, a desperate effort to drown Memory and hasten longed-for death\par
on the part of a man who had quarrelled with his God.\par
\par
The demons of pride and self-will, defiance and scorn had been cast out.\par
An ordeal such as few men are called upon to endure had purified,\par
cleansed, and regenerated the drunkard.  Friendship had taken the\par
desperate man by the hand, plucked his feet from the morass, led him\par
into the light and set his feet once more on firm ground.  His\par
profession was his again to follow.  Love, real love, had come to him\par
and folded her rose-white wings beside his hearth.\par
\par
Years of pure domestic happiness, of successful work, had passed, and\par
now--the July sunshine had no warmth in it, though it streamed in\par
through the open window over the tops of the pot-roses.  The Dop\par
Doctor's head was bowed upon his hands, his great shoulders shook as\par
though he strove with a mortal rigour, the wood of the table where his\par
elbows leaned, the boards beneath the thick carpet on which his feet\par
rested, creaked as the long shudders convulsed him at intervals.\par
\par
It had seemed to Saxham--in whom the seed of Faith had germinated and\par
put forth leaves in one great night of storm following upon years of\par
arid dryness--that Almighty God must have forgiven those five worse than\par
wasted years.\par
\par
Fool! he now cried in his heart.  The Divine Mercy is boundless as the\par
ocean of air in which our planet swims, and for the cleansing of our\par
spotted souls the Blood of the Redeemer flowed on Calvary.  But He who\par
said in His wrath that the sins of the fathers should be visited on the\par
children, does not break, even for those repentant prodigals whom He has\par
taken to His Heart again--the immutable laws of Nature.  Nature, of all\par
forces most conservative, wastes nothing, loses nothing, pardons\par
nothing, avenges everything.\par
\par
The shouted curse, like the whispered blessing, is carried on the\par
invisible wings of Air forever.  Thus, the deformed limb, the devouring\par
cancer, the loathsome ulcer, and the degrading vice, are perpetuated and\par
reproduced as diligently and faithfully as the beautiful feature, the\par
noble quality, the wit that charms, the genius that dominates.  Nay,\par
since Nature turns out some millions of fools to one Dante or\par
Shakespeare or Moliere or Cervantes, it would appear that she prefers\par
the fools.\par
\par
So it is.  Divine Grace has reached and saved the sinner. The ugly vice,\par
the base appetite, have been eradicated by prayer and mortification, by\par
years of self-control and watchfulness.  Free will, moral and physical\par
force, self-command and self-respect are yours again.  And with sobs of\par
gratitude the erstwhile slave of Hell gives thanks to Heaven.\par
\par
Saved.  Cured.  Great words and true in Saxham's case as in many others.\par
But though they are saved and cured they cannot ever forget.  Their eyes\par
have a characteristic look of alert, suspicious watchfulness.  For\par
wheresoever they move about the world, in the drawing-rooms of what is\par
called Society, in the business circles of the City, in the barracks or\par
the mining-camp, on the ship's heaving deck or the floor of the Pullman\par
carriage; amidst the sands of the Desert or the golden-rod of the\par
prairie, or the red sand and dry karroo scrub of the lone veld, they\par
will hear, when they least expect it, the thin, shrill hiss of the Asp\par
that once bit them to the bone.  Or supposing that they have forgotten\par
in reality--so cleverly has the world pretended to!--with what a pang of\par
mortal anguish Memory awakens.  When you recognise the devil that once\par
entered and possessed you, looking out of the eyes of your child.\par
\par
\par
When Saxham lifted up his ashen face and looked at the portrait in the\par
third leaf of the triptych frame and met the clear, candid gaze of his\par
son's blue eyes, you know what he was seeking, and praying not to find.\par
\par
To have given Lynette a drunkard for her son would be the most terrible\par
penalty that could be exacted by merciless Nature for those five sodden,\par
wasted years.\par
\par
Ah! to have had a clean, unspotted life to share with Bawne's fair\par
mother.  That his priceless pearl of womanhood should gleam upon a\par
drunkard's hand--his spotless Convent lily have opened to fullest bloom\par
in a drunkard's holding, had been from the outset of their married life,\par
verjuice in Saxham's cup by day, and a thorn in his pillow by night.\par
\par
But never before had it occurred to the man of science, the great\par
surgeon, the learned biologist, that relentless Nature might be saving\par
up for him, Saxham, a special rod in saltest brine.\par
\par
Bawne....  He sat in silence with set teeth, asking himself the bitter\par
question:\par
\par
"How could I have forgotten--Bawne?"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XIX*\par
\par
                                *BAWNE*\par
\par
\par
As so often happens, the thought of the beloved heralded his well-known\par
thump upon the door-panel.  When had the Dop Doctor ever cried, "Come\par
in!" with such a leaden sinking of the heart?\par
\par
The boy who came in was alert, upright, slim, and strong for his twelve\par
years.  You saw him attired in the dress with which we are all\par
familiar--the loose shirt of khaki-brown, with its knotted silk\par
neckerchief of dark blue, the lanyards ending in clasp-knife and\par
whistle, the roomy shorts upheld by a brown leather pouch-belt\par
supporting a serviceable axe, the dark blue stockings turned over at the\par
knee, fitting close to the slim muscular legs, the light strong shoes,\par
the brown smasher hat with the chin-strap, completed the picture of a\par
Scout of whom no patrol need be ashamed.  He carried his light staff at\par
the trail, and entering, brought it to an upright position, and saluted\par
smartly.  The salute formally acknowledged, he came straight to the\par
table and stood at his father's elbow, waiting, as Saxham feigned to\par
blot a written line.  Outwardly composed, the drumming of the man's\par
heart deafened him, and a mist before his eyes blurred the page they\par
were bent upon.  Fatherhood gripped him by the throat as in the first\par
moment of his son's separate existence.  A thing we prize is never so\par
poignantly precious as when we contemplate the possibility of its ruin\par
or loss.\par
\par
"Father, you aren't generally pleased when I come bothering you in\par
consulting hours, but this time it is really serious business, no kid,\par
and Honour bright!"\par
\par
Saxham answered with equal gravity:\par
\par
"If you have a reasonable excuse for coming, I have said that you may\par
come."\par
\par
The boy was like him.  You saw it as he stood waiting. The vivid\par
gentian-blue eyes were Saxham's, as were the thick throat and prominent\par
under-jaw and the square facial outline.  But the plume of hair that\par
swept over the broad forehead was red-brown like Lynette's.  The\par
delicate, irregular profile and a sensitive sweetness about the lips\par
were gifts from his mother.  The directness of his look, and the tinge\par
of brusqueness in his speech were unconsciously modelled on the\par
father's, as he said, sacrificing sufficient of manly independence to\par
come within the curve of the Doctor's strong arm:\par
\par
"First, I wanted to show you my new badge."\par
\par
Saxham's left hand squeezed the arm most distant from him, where a\par
familiar device was displayed upon the sleeve, midway, between the\par
shoulder and elbow, below the six-inch length of colours distinctive of\par
this Scout's Patrol.\par
\par
"Turn round and show it, then!"\par
\par
"Father, you're larking.  That's my General Scout Badge.  I've had it\par
ever since I passed my Second Class tests.  Before then, you know, when\par
I was a Tenderfoot, I'd only the top-part--the _fleur-de-lis_ without\par
the motto, and you wear that in your left pocket button-hole.  But this\par
is something special, don't you see?"\par
\par
Saxham eyed the row of little enamelled circles on the sleeve next him\par
with respectful gravity.  The boy went on, trying to control the gleeful\par
tremor in his voice:\par
\par
"I've got the Ambulance Badge!--look at the Geneva Cross!--and the\par
Signaller's Badge--this is it--with the crossed flags--and the\par
Interpreter's Badge--the one with the two hands holding.  But this is\par
the very latest.  Our Scoutmaster gave it to me after parade to-day.\par
It's the Airman's Badge--"  He caught his breath, the secret was coming\par
in a moment....  He went on: "To get it you must have made a model\par
aeroplane.  Not a flying-stick, any kid of nine can make one--but a\par
model that will really fly.  That's my special reason for coming.\par
Mother was out--and--and next to her I wanted to tell you!"\par
\par
"And next after me?"\par
\par
The boy considered a moment before he looked up to answer:\par
\par
"Cousin Pat, because she can keep a secret so tightly."\par
\par
Saxham patted the sturdy square shoulders.\par
\par
"You are fond of Cousin Patrine, aren't you?"\par
\par
"Rather!"\par
\par
"Just tell me why?"\par
\par
"Because"--the young brows were puckered--"because she's so big and\par
so--beautiful.  And she'd just die for you and Mother....  She comes in\par
my prayers next after you two."\par
\par
"And--the Chief Scout?"\par
\par
"Father, wouldn't it be--a bit cheeky to go and pray for a man like\par
that?"\par
\par
A spark of laughter wakened in Saxham's sombre eyes.\par
\par
"Not quite respectful, you think?  Is that it?  Why so, when you're\par
taught to pray for the Holy Father, Mother Church, and the King and\par
Queen?"\par
\par
The boy's puckered brows smoothed.  The question was settled.\par
\par
"Of course.  I forgot.  Then the Chief Scout must come in after Cousin\par
Patrine.  Because a gentleman must always give place to a lady.  That's\par
what Mother says."\par
\par
"Suppose Cousin Patrine never came to see you any more, what would you\par
do then?"\par
\par
Bawne straightened the sturdy body and proclaimed:\par
\par
"I would go and find her and bring her back!"\par
\par
"Suppose she did not want to come?"\par
\par
Bawne said instantly:\par
\par
"I would tell her Mother was wanting her.  For Mother would be, you\par
know.  And Cousin Pat wouldn't keep her waiting.  Not much, sir, she\par
wouldn't!"\par
\par
"She cares so?"\par
\par
"Doesn't she!  Why, have you forgotten when I was a little shaver and\par
Mother was so ill?"\par
\par
Saxham, with a certain tightening of the muscles of the throat, recalled\par
the wan, red-eyed spectre that had haunted the landing outside the\par
guarded bedroom where Lynette lay, white and strengthless, while her\par
husband fought for her with Death.\par
\par
"Well, well.  Go on loving Patrine and praying for her! Now tell me of\par
your model."\par
\par
The boy said, controlling his exultation:\par
\par
"It has to be left at our District Headquarters until to-morrow.  You\par
see--it's rather a special affair.  It's not a flying stick, like the\par
things I used to make when I was a shaver, nor a glider--you see men in\par
spectacles flying those every day to please the kids on Hampstead Heath\par
and in Kensington Gardens, but a model of a Bristol monoplane with a\par
span of thirty inches, and a main-plane-area of a hundred and fifty"--he\par
caught his breath and with difficulty kept his eager words from tumbling\par
over one another as he reached the thrilling climax--"and I built up her\par
fuselage with cardboard and sticking-plaster out of the First Aid case\par
you gave me to carry in my belt-pouch, and cut the propeller out of a\par
tin toy engine I've had ever since I was a kid--and made the planes of\par
big sheets of stiff foolscap strengthened with thin strips of glued\par
wood, and her spars, sir!--the upright ones are quills, and her stays\par
and struts I made of copper wire and she's weighted with lead ribbon\par
like what you wrap about the gut when you're bottom-fishing for tench or\par
barbel--and her motor-power is eighteen inches of square elastic\par
twisted--and father"--he broke into a war-dance of ecstasy\par
unrestrained--"when Roddy Wrynche and me went on a secret expedition to\par
Primrose Hill to test her--she flew, sir!  First go-off--by George!"\par
\par
"Really flew? ... You are certain?"\par
\par
"Upon my life, sir, and that's my Honour.  Scout's Honour and life are\par
the same thing.  That's what the Oath rubs into us."  He squared his\par
shoulders and lowered his voice as a boy speaking of high matters that\par
must be dealt with reverently.  "I think it's--ripping.  I can say it.\par
Would you like me to?"\par
\par
Saxham nodded without speaking, because of that choking something\par
sticking in his throat.  That something Lear called "the mother."  And,\par
dammed away behind his eyes, were scalding tears that only men may shed.\par
As the young voice said:\par
\par
"On my Honour I promise that I will do my best to be loyal to God and\par
the King.\par
\par
"On my Honour I promise that I will do my best to Help other people at\par
all times.\par
\par
"On my Honour I promise that I will do my best to obey the Scout Law....\par
You see"--the boyish arm was on Saxham's shoulder now, the ruddy-fair\par
cheek pressed against the pale, close-shaven face--"you see, Father,\par
when a Scout says 'On my Honour' it's just as if he swore on the\par
Crucifix!"\par
\par
Saxham said, crushing down the fierce emotion that had almost mastered\par
him:\par
\par
"It is--just the same!  For the man who breaks a promise will never keep\par
an oath....  I have a friend of whom I have told you....  I think he\par
would like to hear about your model aeroplane....  May I tell him, or\par
would you prefer to tell him yourself?"\par
\par
Bawne's fair face glowed.  He gasped in ecstasy:\par
\par
"_Father_....  You mean Mr. Sherbrand--your Flying Man who's in the\par
Hospital?"\par
\par
"My Flying Man--but he is well again and back at work at Hendon.  There\par
was not much the matter with him; a slight obstruction in one of the\par
nasal passages that prevented him from breathing with his mouth shut as\par
he should.  Now he has asked me--this afternoon if I am at leisure--to\par
bring my little son to the aerodrome and see him make a flight."\par
\par
"And go up in his aeroplane with him?  Father, say Yes! Do, please do!"\par
\par
As the little figure bobbed up and down beside him in joyous excitement,\par
Saxham answered, not without an inward tug:\par
\par
"If your mother says 'Yes' I shall not say No!  Now off with you, my\par
son!"\par
\par
The boy saluted and went.  Even his bright obedience wrung his father's\par
heart.  The man looked haggard and old. He hid his careworn face in his\par
hands for a minute.  His lips were still moving when he looked up and\par
made the Sign so well known to many of us upon his forehead and breast.\par
Prayer, that most powerful of all therapeutic agents, so often\par
prescribed by Saxham for his patients, was his own tonic and sedative in\par
moments of bodily exhaustion and mental overstrain.\par
\par
He had prayed, he, the sceptic, on that unforgettable night at\par
Gueldersdorp, when he wrestled with his possessing fiend....  Lynette\par
had taught him the habit of prayer. And even as she, a friendless,\par
neglected waif, had learned to look up and see the shining Faces of our\par
Divine Redeemer and His Virgin Mother through the features of a pure and\par
tender woman; so her husband, looking in the eyes of Lynette, had found\par
the gift of Faith lost years before.\par
\par
"Oh! ... Prayer!" you say--"Faith!" ... and I see you shrug and sneer a\par
little, you who are intellectual and highly educated, and have ceased to\par
believe in what you term the Hebraic myth or the Christian legend--since\par
you learned to point out the weak places in the First Book of Genesis,\par
and sneer at the discrepancies between the statements of the Gospel\par
narrators--though you will hear such testimonies sworn to in good faith,\par
wherever witnesses are examined in a Court of Law.\par
\par
But no! you tell me, you are not an Agnostic.  You credit the existence\par
of Almighty God, but prayer is the parson's affair.  Well, because a man\par
wears a straight black coat, will you abandon to him so inestimable a\par
privilege?  Is it not a marvellous thing that you or I should lift up\par
our earth-made, earth-begrimed hands, and that He who set this tiny\par
planet to spin out its aeons of cycles amidst the innumerable millions\par
of systems wheeling through His Universe should stoop to hear the words\par
we utter?  Feeble cries, drowned by the orchestras of the winds, and the\par
chorus of the Spheres revolving in their orbits, or silent utterances\par
imperceptible to any Ear save His alone.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER XX*\par
\par
                        *THE MODERN HIPPOCRATES*\par
\par
\par
Patients rapidly succeeded one another in the chair that faced the\par
window.  There were confirmed invalids who were really healthy men and\par
women, and certain others who came in smilingly to talk about the\par
weather and the newest Russian Opera, who bore upon their faces the\par
unmistakable stamp of mortal disease.  The wife or the husband, the\par
father or the mother had worried for nothing....  Would the Doctor\par
prescribe a little tonic to buck them, or the surgeon alleviate a little\par
trouble of the local kind?  Really nothing--but--Death's knock at the\par
door.  And there were cases--open or unacknowledged--of the liquor-habit\par
and the drug-mania.  To these, instead of dropping out bromide of\par
potassium and throwing in the chloral hydrates with strychnine and the\par
chloride of the metal that is crushed and assayed out of the quartz reef\par
near Johannesburg, or pick-axed out of the frozen ground of the\par
Klondyke, Saxham dealt out that savage tonic Truth, in ladlesful.\par
\par
The secret dipsomaniac or druggard could not deceive this man's keen\par
scrutiny, or escape his unerring diagnosis. When, beaten, they admitted\par
the fact, Saxham said to them as to the others:\par
\par
"You say you cannot conquer the craving.  I myself once thought so.\par
Your moral power can be restored, even as was mine.  In your case the\par
habit is barely as ingrained as in the case I quote to you.  I drank\par
alcohol to excess for a period of five years."\par
\par
Some of the sufferers--elderly women and mild-mannered old\par
gentlemen--were horrified.  Others thought such candour brutal--but\par
attractively so.  Yet others responded to the sympathy masked by the\par
stern, impassive face, and the blunt, brusque manner.\par
\par
"At any rate the man's no humbug!" such and such an one would stutter.\par
"And seems to have any amount of Will.  Think I shall put myself in his\par
hands for a bit."  Adding with a rueful twinkle: "He knows how the dog\par
bites, if anyone does!"\par
\par
He did, and those hands of his were strong, prompt and unfaltering.\par
Since the grip of human sympathy had fastened on the Dop Doctor of\par
Gueldersdorp, and drawn him up out of the depths into sunlight and free\par
air, and set his feet once more on the firm ground, how many of his\par
fellow-sufferers had Saxham not hauled reeking and squelching out of the\par
abysmal sludge, whose secrets shall only be revealed upon the Last Day.\par
\par
Yet Saxham realised that the grand majority of these twentieth-century\par
men and women really wanted little more of the physician and surgeon\par
than the thirteenth-century patient desired of the apothecary or the\par
leech.  A patient hearing given to their category of evils--a little\par
hocus-pocus, and a nostrum or so.\par
\par
We scoff, thought Saxham, at the ignorance of those men of the Dark\par
Ages, yet in this enlightened era the eye of newt and toe of frog, the\par
salted earthworms, and the _Pulvis Bezoardicus Magistralis_ or _Pulvis\par
Sanctus_, dissolved in the liquor of herbs gathered under a propitious\par
conjunction of their ruling planets with the Moon--have but given place\par
to extract of the dried thyroid gland of the sheep, the ovaries of the\par
guinea-pig, the spinal cord and brain of rabbits and mice and other\par
small mammalia, with--instead of broth of vipers, liquor distilled from\par
the parotid secretion of the tropical toad; identical with the reptile\par
administered in boluses to Pagan patients by the Greek Hippocrates.\par
With other remedies hideously akin to the hell-brews that whipped the\par
sated desires of Tiberius and Nero.... Such as the pastelloids\par
frequently prescribed by bland-mannered, frock-coated, twentieth-century\par
physicians--professing Christians who pay West-End pew-rents, and\par
deplore the abnormal drop in the birth-rate--for the spurring of the\par
sense of debilitated Hedonists.\par
\par
Thus, summed Saxham, we have rediscovered Organotherapy. We have\par
harnessed the bacillus to Hygeia's silver chariot.  In Surgery the Short\par
Circuit is the latest word. It is wonderful to know how well one can get\par
on, at a pinch, without organs hitherto deemed indispensable to\par
existence. Radiology reveals to us the inner mysteries of the human\par
machine, alive and palpitating.  The splintered bone, the bullet or the\par
shell-splinter embedded in the muscle or the osseous structure, can be\par
detected and photographed by the teleradiographic apparatus.  The\par
electro-magnet automatically carried out the removal of such fragments,\par
provided only that they are of steel.  Ah yes!  We are very clever in\par
this twentieth century, reflected the Dop Doctor. Modern Science has\par
even weighed the Soul.\par
\par
Could Dee and Lilly have bettered that?  Debate--consider.... This\par
quenchless spark of Being, kindled in Saxham's breast and in yours and\par
mine by the Supreme Will of the Divine Creator--this Ego for whose\par
eternal salvation Christ died upon the bitter Cross, dips the scale at\par
precisely one-sixteenth of an ounce avoirdupois.  The expiring man,\par
weighed a moment previously to dissolution, and again immediately\par
afterwards, was found to have lost so much and no more.\par
\par
The dying world is in the scales to-day, thought Saxham, bitterly and\par
sorrowfully.  Religious Faith being the soul of the world, one wonders,\par
when the last thin hymn shall have died upon the fierce irrespirable\par
air; when the last human sigh shall have exhaled from Earth, how much in\par
ponderability shall be lacking to the acorn-shaped lump of whirling\par
matter.  Will the result proportionate with the moribund's sixteenth of\par
an ounce?\par
\par
It seemed to Saxham, that without a moral and social upheaval upon a\par
vaster scale than historian ever recorded or visionary ever dreamed; a\par
cataclysmic cleansing, a purging as by fire; the regeneration of the\par
human race, the reconstitution of the human mind, the renaissance of the\par
Divine Ideal, could never be brought about.  Unconsciously he sought for\par
the decadent world some such ordeal as he himself had passed through.\par
You looked at him and saw the scars of suffering.  The soil of his\par
nature had been rent by volcanic convulsions and seared by the upburst\par
of fierce abysmal fires, before the green herb clothed the sides of the\par
frowning steeps, the jagged peaks were wreathed with gentle clouds; the\par
pure springs gathered and ran; the valleys became fruitful and the\par
plains carpeted themselves with flowers.\par
\par
A miracle had been wrought for Saxham the Man, and he saw the need of\par
one for the World, and said in his heart that, though holy men might\par
pray, it would not, could not, ever be vouchsafed.  And all the while\par
the miracle was ripening, the Day was coming, the Great Awakening was at\par
hand.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXI*\par
\par
                           *MARGOT LOOKS IN*\par
\par
\par
It drew on to the luncheon hour.  The last patient a very young, very\par
little, very pretty married woman, was summoned by the neat maid from\par
the waiting-room, in a remote corner of which a husband of military type\par
and ordinarily cheerful countenance, remained, maintaining with obvious\par
effort a fictitious interest in the pages of a remote issue of _Punch_.\par
\par
The dainty little lady bore a name well known to Saxham. The fact that a\par
title was attached to it did not interest him, nor had it shortened her\par
term of waiting by a second of the clock.  But her youth smote him with\par
a sense of pity as she took the chair upon his left hand facing the\par
window, and without overmuch embarrassment made clear her case.\par
\par
She was going to have a baby.  Franky, her husband, earnestly desired\par
the kiddie for family reasons, yet its advent was unwelcome to him, in\par
that it must inevitably involve physical pain and mental anxiety for the\par
little lady, Franky's wife.\par
\par
The little lady had been frightfully downed by the prospect. She rather\par
cottoned to kiddies, she explained, than otherwise.  It was the bother\par
of having them that didn't appeal.  It put everything in the cart as\par
regarded the Autumn Season.  Besides--there were family reasons on her\par
side, why the prospect should not be too rosy.  She stated the reasons,\par
and Saxham's listening face grew grave.  He realised the danger of a\par
Preconceived Idea.\par
\par
He said nothing.  Margot went on talking.  Her beautiful deer-eyes were\par
alternately wistful and coaxing.  They entreated sympathy.  They begged\par
for gentleness.  They grew brilliant with enthusiasm as she explained\par
that after a lot of chinning, she and Franky had hit upon a perfectly\par
ripping plan.\par
\par
A friend, recently encountered in Paris, had thrown a ray of hope upon\par
the doubtful prospect.  No doubt Dr. Saxham was in sympathy with the\par
pioneers of the New Crusade against Unnecessary Pain....  Of course, Dr.\par
Saxham knew all about the wonderful experiments of German\par
gynaewhatdoyoucall'ems.  The right term was frightfully crack-jaw.\par
Perhaps Dr. Saxham knew what was meant?\par
\par
Saxham reassured the little lady.\par
\par
"You refer of course to the experiments of Professors von Wolfenbuchel\par
of Vienna, and Krauss of the Berlin _Fraueenklinik_, resulting in the\par
method of treatment now known throughout the Continent as 'Purple\par
Dreams.'  Wolfenbuchel and Krauss have published a pamphlet on the\par
subject. Perhaps you have read the pamphlet?"\par
\par
"Yes--I've read it.  A wonderful book that has been translated into\par
every language.  A German officer, friend of a friend I met in Paris,\par
told her about it.  His sister had tried the treatment, and found it A1.\par
So I bought a French translation of the book in Paris, and an English\par
one at a shop in the Haymarket.  It's bound in rose-coloured vellum\par
stamped with a rising sun in gold.  'Weep No More, Mothers!' it's\par
called.  Isn't that a charming title?  And the subject is: 'Pangless\par
Childbirth, Produced through Purple Dreams.'"\par
\par
In a sweet, coaxing voice that trembled a little, she began to tell the\par
Doctor about the wonderful results obtained by hypodermics of Krauss and\par
Wolfenbuechel's marvellous combination of drugs.  And Saxham hearkened\par
with stern patience, while the table-clock ticked and the luncheon hour\par
drew near, and Franky chewed the cud of suspense in the Doctor's\par
waiting-room.\par
\par
Thousands of peasant women, and others of the lower middle-class in\par
Germany had become mothers under the Purple Dreams treatment.  Maternity\par
Hospitals in Paris, Brussels, and New York had adopted the method after\par
controversy and hesitation.  It had triumphed over every doubt.  An\par
American woman whose brother's wife had had a "Purple Dreams" baby at\par
the Berlin Institute had told the little narrator only yesterday how\par
quite too wonderful was the discovery of the enlightened Krauss and the\par
gifted Wolfenbuchel.  Everything was made easy.  When your ordeal drew\par
near you simply went to the place, and signed your name in a book, and\par
put yourself in the hands of skilled persons.  You felt no pain--not a\par
twinge.  Only the prick and throb of the hypodermic needle-syringe, and\par
most people were used to the _pique_ nowadays--administering the first\par
subcutaneous injections of the wonderful new drug.... Under its mild\par
sedative influence you dozed off to sleep presently.  And when you woke\par
up--there was the baby--beautifully dressed, and lying on a lace pillow\par
in the arms of a smartly dressed, fresh-cheeked nurse.\par
\par
This had been the experience of the sister of the German officer, as of\par
the wife of the brother of the American lady. The same thing happening\par
to thousands everywhere.  The philanthropic Wolfenbuchel and the\par
benevolent Krauss had made of the stony Via Dolorosa by which Womanhood\par
attains maternity--a path of soft green turf bordered with fragrant\par
lilies and bestrewn with the perfumed petals of the rose.\par
\par
She ended.  Saxham had kept his keen blue eyes steadily upon her during\par
the eloquent recital.  Not a hair of his black brows had twitched, not a\par
muscle of his pale face had moved--betraying his urgent inclination to\par
smile.  His fine hand, lying upon the blotter near the small black\par
case-book, might have been carved out of ancient Spanish ivory, or\par
yellow-white lava.  Now he said:\par
\par
"There is nothing new nor marvellous about the 'Dreams' method.  It\par
is--persistent narcosis obtained from the subcutaneous injections of\par
morphine with the hydrobromide of hyoscine, another alkaloid obtained\par
from henbane.  I have visited not only the Institute at Berlin, but the\par
Rottburg Fraueenklinik--and an establishment of the same type in Paris,\par
and another in Brussels.  It is a fact that when a patient awakens from\par
the anaesthesia there is no recollection of anything that has taken\par
place subsequently to the injection of the drug."\par
\par
"There has been no pain.  Absolutely--none whatever!"  She spoke with a\par
little, joyful catch in her breath.\par
\par
"Pardon me," said Saxham.  "You labour under a delusion which the\par
rose-coloured pamphlet was not written to dispel.  There must have been\par
pain--if there has been childbirth.  Perhaps there has been overwhelming\par
pain. Pain manifested by outcries and convulsions--violent\par
struggles--subdued by the attendants and nurses--for the friends and\par
relatives of the patient are rigidly excluded--the patient enters and\par
leaves the Home alone.  Two or three days may have vanished in that\par
vacuum which has been created in her memory.  Days in which she has been\par
lying--it may be--strapped to the bed in the private ward of the nursing\par
home--her purple, congested face and staring eyes concealed by a mask of\par
wetted linen--her agonies only witnessed by paid attendants whose\par
interests are best served by denial or concealment--supposing anything\par
to have gone wrong?"\par
\par
The relentless surgeon's hand had torn away the painted curtain.  Margot\par
contemplated the grim truth in silence for a moment.  Then she found\par
words:\par
\par
"But nothing ever _does_ go wrong.  The pink pamphlet says so.  My\par
American friend's sister-in-law says so.... Thousands of women have had\par
children under scopolo--what's its name?  And none of them felt\par
pain--not the slightest.  And in every case--in _every_ case--there was\par
the baby when they woke up!"\par
\par
The sweet bird-voice quivered.  She had entered the room so full of hope\par
and enthusiasm, and this man with the piercing eyes and the brusque,\par
direct manner was putting things before her in a way that dashed and\par
damped.  Hear him now:\par
\par
"Yes, there is generally a baby--when it is necessary there should be\par
one.  Though the patients who are treated in the free wards of German\par
and Austrian _Kliniks_ may not always be scrupulous upon this point.\par
Still, if the treatment can be carried out without undue peril for the\par
mother--and I do not allow this for a single moment--have you not\par
considered the risk for the child?"\par
\par
Margot had pulled off one long glove.  Now she murmured, setting the tip\par
of a little bare, jewelled finger near the corner of a distracting\par
little mouth:\par
\par
"You consider that it's handicapping the start for--the kiddie?"\par
\par
The avalanche fell; shocking and freezing and stunning her.\par
\par
"Ask yourself, Lady Norwater, and do not forget to ask your husband:\par
Will a healthy or a degenerate type of man or woman be eventually reared\par
from an infant in whom the springs of Life have been deliberately\par
poisoned with henbane and morphia--before its entrance into the world?"\par
\par
She gasped:\par
\par
"Then it's all U.P.?"  She was slangy even in her tragic misery.  She\par
sought in her gold vanity-bag and produced the envelope that held the\par
cheque, but Saxham waved it away.\par
\par
"Pray put that back....  Neither from rich nor poor do I accept unearned\par
money.  You have not really consulted me.  You have asked my opinion\par
upon a course of treatment. And I have given it, for what it is worth.\par
You will go home, and tell your husband that I have talked tosh, and\par
consult another physician."\par
\par
"No, I won't!" She said it bravely.  "I want you to prescribe!"\par
\par
"If I prescribe," Saxham told her, "you shall certainly fee me.  But you\par
do not need treatment."  His eyes smiled though his mouth did not relax\par
its grimness, as he added: "You strike me as being in excellent health."\par
\par
She owned to feeling "top-hole," first-class, and simply awfully beany!\par
Though, and her dimple faded as she owned it, the thought of what must\par
happen in November took "the gilt off the gingerbread."\par
\par
"Do not think of what is going to happen in November," Saxham advised\par
her.  "Or teach yourself to think of it in the right way."  The sense of\par
her childishness and inexperience went home to the sensitive quick\par
beneath the man's hard exterior, as she said to him with an\par
unconsciously appealing accent:\par
\par
"But how am I to find out what is the right way?"\par
\par
He had gained upon her confidence.  The admission proved it.  With\par
infinite tact he began to win yet another woman to drain out her chalice\par
of Motherhood, untinctured with the druggist's nepenthe,--to gain for\par
the race yet another babe unmarred before its birth.  For this end no\par
labour was too great for Saxham.  A crank you may call him, but that\par
cranks of this type are the leaven of the world, you know.\par
\par
It is typical of the human butterfly Saxham dealt with, that his clothes\par
pleased Margot.  She liked their characteristic mingling of elegance\par
with simplicity.  Some fashionable doctors got themselves up like\par
elderly bloods, others affected garments dating from the year One.\par
There was neither perfume upon Saxham's handkerchief nor flue upon his\par
coat-sleeve.  His shirt of soft white cashmere, his slightly starched\par
linen cuffs and narrow double collar were fastened with plain buttons of\par
mother o' pearl, the black silk necktie was blameless of pin or ring.\par
The handsome gold chronometer he carried because it had been presented\par
to him by the Staff and patients of St. Teresa and St. Stanislaus. The\par
chain attached to it--rather worn and shabby now--was of woven red-brown\par
hair.\par
\par
The hair of his wife.  A creamy-pale Niphetos rose stood where her hands\par
had placed it near his writing-pad, in a tall, slender beaker of\par
green-and-gold Venetian glass.  His eyes drank at the beauty of the\par
lovely scarce-unfolded blossom. Perhaps the resemblance of the fair\par
flower to the beloved giver softened the lines of the stern square face\par
into the smile that Margot liked, as he found her eyes again, saying:\par
\par
"Perhaps I could better answer your question by telling you how another\par
patient bore herself in--circumstances akin to yours.  Will it tire you?\par
I promise not to be unduly prolix.  And to listen commits you to no\par
course of action. Now, shall I go on?"\par
\par
"I'd love you to go on!"\par
\par
Always in extremes, the little wayward creature.  She flushed and\par
sparkled at the Doctor as he took from its place on his writing-table a\par
triptych photograph-frame in gold-mounted mother-o'-pearl, folded the\par
leaves so as to reveal but one of the portraits, and held under Margot's\par
eyes the delicately-tinted photograph of a girl of twenty.  The portrait\par
had been taken the year following Saxham's return from South Africa with\par
his young wife.\par
\par
"How beautiful!" Margot exclaimed.\par
\par
"Beautiful, as you say, but does she look happy?"\par
\par
Margot wrinkled her dainty eyebrows, puzzling out the question.  Did she\par
look happy, the girl of the portrait, whose face and figure might have\par
served one of the old Greek masters as model for an Artemis to be carved\par
upon a gem? Well, perhaps not quite happy, now one came to look again.\par
\par
The black-lashed eyes of golden hazel were full of wistful sadness,\par
there was a faintly indicated fold between the fine arched eyebrows,\par
much darker than the rippling red-brown hair, whose luxuriance seemed to\par
weigh down the little Greek head.  The closely-folded, deeply-cut lips\par
spoke dumbly of sorrow, the nymph-like bosom seemed rising on a breath\par
of weariness.  Something was lacking to complete her beauty.  So much\par
was plain even to Margot.  But not until the Doctor showed by the side\par
of the first, the second portrait, did she realise what that Something\par
was.\par
\par
In the first portrait both face and figure were shown in profile.  In\par
the second, bearing a date of two years later, the beautiful, sensitive\par
face of the young woman was turned towards you.  Still rather grave than\par
smiling, she held in her arms a sturdy baby boy of some twelve months,\par
upon whose downy head her chin lightly rested.  The clasp of her slender\par
arms about her child, the poise of her still nymph-like figure,\par
expressed fulness of life, buoyant energy, and happiness in fullest\par
measure.  What was previously lacking was now made clear.\par
\par
"Lovely, quite lovely!" trilled the sweet little voice. "And what an\par
exquie kiddy!"\par
\par
"Then you do not dislike children?" Saxham asked, as his visitor's\par
husband had done not long ago.\par
\par
"On the contrary," the little lady assured him, "I rather cotton to\par
them.  But"--she shrugged her little shoulders prettily and quoted\par
boldly from another woman--"but the fag of having them doesn't--appeal!"\par
\par
The Doctor replaced the threefold frame and turned his regard back upon\par
his visitor.\par
\par
"These photographs speak for themselves ..." he said quietly.  "She--the\par
mother of the boy you see, was, when she first knew that she was to be a\par
mother, fragile and delicate in body, and in mind highly-strung and\par
sensitive.  As a child she had known neglect and unkind usage.  Twice\par
she had sustained an overwhelming shock, physical and mental; she had\par
rallied, passed through a crisis and regained lost ground.  But the\par
possibility of a relapse was not to be blinked at.  It was a lion in the\par
path!"\par
\par
The slight form of the listener was convulsed by a shudder.  The pretty\par
face lost its wild-rose tint.  The lion in the path ... Margot saw him\par
crouching, his tawny eyes aflame, his great jaws slavering, his tail\par
lashing the dust, his great muscles tightening for the fearful spring.\par
And Saxham went on:\par
\par
"She maintained from the first a sweet, sane mental standpoint.  She\par
tamed her lion by sheer force of will.  Her courage was her own: she did\par
not owe it to the physician and surgeon.  But he advised as he knew\par
best, and she followed his advice implicitly, as to wholesome diet and\par
regular exercise, thus keeping her body in health.  She surrounded\par
herself with objects that were beautiful in form and colour. She made a\par
point of hearing great music and of re-reading the works of great poets,\par
essayists, and novelists.  She wished her child to owe much to pre-natal\par
influences.  For that these----"\par
\par
The speaker faltered for a moment, before he resumed the thread of his\par
discourse.\par
\par
"--That these form character for good or evil no physiologist can deny.\par
Therefore while she did not flee from, she avoided the sight of\par
deformity or ugliness, as she shunned active infection, or tainted air.\par
It was desirable that her child should be healthy, strong, and\par
beautiful.  But the love of loveliness, though one of the dominants of\par
her character, scales lowest of the triad.  Human love, the love of\par
mother, husband, and friend rank above it, and first of all stands the\par
love of God."\par
\par
"How awfully good she must be!"\par
\par
"She took the child, first and last, as a gift from God to her.  If she\par
lived or died, and she longed inexpressibly to live--Death, like Life,\par
would be the fulfilment of the Divine Will.  Fortified by the Sacraments\par
of her Church she lay down upon her bed of pain as though it were an\par
altar.  She suffered intensely----"\par
\par
His voice broke.\par
\par
"She suffered inexpressibly.  Not until the actual crisis did I have\par
recourse to chloroform.  When I was about to use it she said to me:\par
'_Not yet! ... I will wear it a little longer...  this mother's crown of\par
thorns._' To-day the crown is one of roses.  Does not this appeal to\par
you?"\par
\par
The Doctor's supple hand displayed the third portrait in the triptych,\par
and Margot saw the same assured joy, rounded with a richer and more deep\par
content.  The exquisite face was fuller, the outlines of the form\par
displayed the ripeness of early maturity, the slender palm was now a\par
stately tree. The girl of twenty was merged in the woman of thirty, rich\par
in all feminine graces, beautiful exceedingly, with the beauty that is\par
not only of line and proportion, form and colour, but shines from\par
within, irradiating the perishable living clay with the immortal\par
radiance of the soul.  Her boy stood at her side, a manly square-headed\par
young British twelve-year-old, wearing a simple, distinctive dress;\par
familiar to us all.\par
\par
"Y-yes.  But I'm afraid you have forgotten: I told you at the beginning,\par
or I meant to....  My--my own mother died when I was born!"\par
\par
"And that sad fact increases your natural fear and repugnance.\par
Naturally.  It will strike you as a curious point of resemblance between\par
your case and that of the--patient whose portrait I have shown you, when\par
I tell you that her mother did not survive the birth of a later child.\par
May I tell you further that the possibility of some inherited weakness\par
does not render you more promising--regarded as a subject for the\par
treatment of Wolfenbuchel and Krauss."\par
\par
Margot was beginning to hate this stern-faced man who set forth things\par
so clearly.  He had bored her almost to weeping.  Why on earth had she\par
come?  The fact that Franky's sister Trix's boy Ronald had been helped\par
into the world by Saxham thirteen years ago and recently operated on for\par
the removal of the appendix, was no reason that Franky's wife should\par
regard him as infallible.  She glanced at her tiny jewelled wrist-watch.\par
Ten whole minutes had gone.  She rose.\par
\par
"You have been so kind, and I have been so much interested.  But I must\par
go now!" she said, like a weary child pleading to be let out of school.\par
"Franky--my husband--will be waiting.  I have promised to lunch with him\par
at the Club."\par
\par
"If he is here, perhaps Lord Norwater would like to speak to me," Saxham\par
suggested.\par
\par
Margot lied badly.  She reddened as she answered:\par
\par
"Oh, what a pity that he did not come!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXII*\par
\par
                           *MARGOT IS SQUARE*\par
\par
\par
She was in what she would have termed "a blue funk" for fear that Saxham\par
would accompany her to the threshold. But he merely bowed her out of the\par
consulting-room and smartly shut his door.  Then she tripped to the\par
waiting-room and beckoned forth Franky with an air of buoyant,\par
fictitious cheerfulness.  Her eyes were radiant, her little face was\par
dressed in artful smiles....\par
\par
"Did I seem long?  Were you getting the hump?" she asked of Franky, who\par
rose and hurried to meet her, dropping _Punches_ all over the place.\par
His smooth hair was almost rumpled and his brown eyes begged like a\par
retriever's. He asked in the kind of whisper that travels miles:\par
\par
"Yes--no!  Did you pull off the interview?  What does the Doctor----"\par
\par
"S-sh!"  She glanced anxiously towards the one remaining patient.  "Tell\par
you when we get out.  Impossible here!"\par
\par
He urged: "But is it all right?"\par
\par
"As right as rain!"\par
\par
"Good egg!"  She had got him out of the room and as far as the hall\par
door.  "Stop! ... Wait!  Oughtn't I to go and thank----"\par
\par
"No--no!"  The door was open, the neat little landau-limousine that had\par
brought them was waiting by the kerb-stone.  Before Franky knew it,\par
Margot had plucked him down the steps, pulled him into the car, and\par
given the chauffeur the signal.  They were in Hanover Square before he\par
recovered his breath.\par
\par
"Oh come, I say, Kittums!  That sort of Sandow business can't be good\par
for you.  Why you're in such a thundering hurry to get me away, I'd\par
rather like to know?"\par
\par
Her heart shook her, but she lied again bravely.\par
\par
"Didn't you want to hear what the Doctor told me about the 'Purple\par
Dreams' treatment?"\par
\par
"More than anything in the world.  That drug with the freak name! ...\par
Can it do any harm--to you and----"\par
\par
"Not a scrap!"\par
\par
She planted a flying kiss between his ear and his collar. He greatly\par
appreciated the attention, though it tickled him horribly.\par
\par
"Dr. Saxham said it was a frightfully clever, practicable method.\par
Absolutely harmless, and the patient doesn't suffer--not that much!"\par
She measured off an infinitesimal bit of finger-nail and showed him, and\par
went on as he caught the little hand and gratefully mumbled it: "You\par
don't know a thing that happens.  You simply go to bye-bye. And--there's\par
always the baby when you wake up!"\par
\par
"A first-class baby?"  His harping maddened her.  "A healthy little\par
buffer to send to Eton and represent us in the Regiment, and inherit the\par
title presently when his poor old Pater pops?  Just look me in the face\par
like the little sport you are, Margot, and tell me that you're playing\par
square with me.  For this--for this is the game of Life!"\par
\par
He had both her hands.  He made her look at him.  She met his eager\par
stare with limpid eyes.  And all the while that sentence of Saxham's\par
about the pre-natal poisoning of the springs of existence, drummed,\par
drummed at the back of her brain.  "_What a little beast I am!_" she\par
mentally commented, hearing her own voice answering:\par
\par
"I've told you No, and that I am playing square with you!"  She grasped\par
the fact that Franky had suffered, by the grunt of relief with which he\par
loosed her hands.  "And so it's settled I go to Berlin about the middle\par
of--September, say?"\par
\par
"Wow-wow!  It's us for the gay life!  Just when the beastly hole's as\par
dusty as the Sahara and as hot as hell!"\par
\par
"You won't be in the beastly hole, and perhaps I needn't go before the\par
beginning of October.  You can go down to Brakehills and slay away at\par
the pheasants, and run over when I cable, to bring me back----"\par
\par
"With my boy!  Our boy, Kittums!"\par
\par
His simple, kind face was quivering.  He put out a strong brown hand and\par
laid it on hers, and she gave the hand a little affectionate nip:\par
\par
"Hullo!"  Perhaps he talked on to cover up the momentary lapse into\par
sentiment.  "Pipe old St. George's, where we did the deed!  Hardly seems\par
close on six months since we got spliced, does it?  And there's the\par
Bijou Cottage...."  Franky thus irreverently designated the large, drab,\par
stucco-faced, eminently respectable if mousey mansion on the Square's\par
east side, where Margot's bachelor Uncle Derek lived with his collection\par
of moths and beetles. "Shall we stop and give the old gentleman a\par
cheero?  Is he at all likely to be in?"\par
\par
His hand was on the silk-netted rubber bulb of the chauffeur's whistle,\par
when Margot caught it back.\par
\par
"No, don't stop!  Of course he's in.  He never goes out, unless it is to\par
a meeting of the Entomological Society, or the Museum of Natural\par
History, or some other place equally stuffy and scientific.  Besides,\par
Uncle Derek is a vegetarian--and there wouldn't be anything but tomato\par
soup, and pea-flour cutlets, and Lepidoptera for lunch!"\par
\par
"Poor little woman, was she peckish, then?  All lity, we'll chuff along\par
and fill up tanks at the Club.  Bally odd bill of fare, pea-flour\par
cutlets and Lepidop--what's-their-names? But we'll get things nearly as\par
rummy served up to us in Berlin.  Pork chops with sweet gooseberry\par
sauce, and pink sausages with lilac cabbage and dumplings.  Why do you\par
look so scared?"\par
\par
She forced a laugh.\par
\par
"Not scared, but you said '_we_' ..."\par
\par
"You don't suppose I could go shooting when you were--facing what you've\par
got to face?" he asked her, and added, in a tone and with a look that\par
she had once before encountered from him: "When you go to Berlin in\par
October, Kittums, I go with you; take that as straight from\par
Headquarters, old child!  Unless--something happens to prevent our going\par
there at all!"\par
\par
He added, answering the mute question in her eyes:\par
\par
"Something that's been on the cards since the Anglo-French Agreement of\par
1904.  It cropped up again in 1905, when the German Kaiser's feelings\par
were so upset by John Bull's carryings-on with the pretty lady in the\par
tricolour petticoat and Cap of Liberty, that he called on the Sultan of\par
Morocco at Tangier to ask his Sublimity to interfere. And again in 1908\par
we were up against it ... when Austria annexed Bosnia and Herzegovina,\par
and Russia took the needle, and William ordered out his best suit of\par
shining armour in readiness for a scrap....  If there's anything in the\par
Triple Entente, the fat was nearly in the fire then.... And again in\par
1911, over the French occupation of Morocco, when the German gunboat\par
_Panther_ and the German cruiser _Berlin_ were sent to the closed Fort\par
of Agadir near the mouth of the smelly River Sus.  That piffed out after\par
a good deal of what they call 'acute tension between the Powers.'  To\par
the Services acute tension means the stoppin' of leave.  And I'd fixed\par
things up for spendin' the July fortnight before Henley with some jolly\par
people at Baden-Baden, and if the trip had come off, the chances are I'd\par
have come back engaged to another girl!"\par
\par
"Are you sorry?"\par
\par
"Do I look sorry?" was the quick _riposte_.  He went on: "France and\par
Germany went in for 'precautionary measures' that time.  Precautionary\par
measures mean concentration of troops on both frontiers, and General\par
Manoeuvres on the biggest scale.  Dress-rehearsal for a general\par
mobilisation, you tumble?  While our Home Fleet quietly concentrated on\par
our north-east coast.  And just when the lid seemed on the point of\par
being taken off, Billiam the Bumptious climbed down, and withdrew from\par
Agadir.  The squabble was patched up.  France got a free hand in Morocco\par
in return for the open door and 100,000 square miles of the Congo Basin.\par
French and German troops left off mugging at one another across the\par
frontiers.  Whitehall Wireless, Nordeich Station, and the Eiffel Tower\par
emitted radios reversin' the weather-signals from 10 to 0, which means a\par
dead calm. And the British Fleet gave up all hope and went home to bed.\par
\par
"But--and don't you swipe in, Kittums, for I'm gettin' to the thrillin'\par
part--the bigwigs who manage Foreign Affairs weren't taken in so easily.\par
They knew the bad blood had got to break out somewhere, and it did.\par
Italy and Turkey went to war in November, 1911, and the Balkan Rumpus\par
broke out ten months later.  Turkey didn't win, though her Army has had\par
German instructors ever since von Moltke licked it into shape in 1835,\par
and Germany'd naturally expected her to finish as top-dog.  So the\par
concessions Germany wanted from Turkey were lost.  I rather think the\par
Prussian Eagle had its eye on Adrianople on the Black Sea coast, and the\par
Gallipoli Peninsula, for the furtherin' of her views on the Near\par
East--and Austria had a fancy for the Sanjak of Novibazar--and wanted\par
Salonika as a base for operations on the Mediterranean.  Anyhow, both of\par
'em were wiped on the jaw.  And William the All Too Knowing, as Courtley\par
calls him--Courtley's going in strong for Nietzsche just now--says his\par
works are a slogging attack on Teutonism!--William has got to the end of\par
his patience. The shining armour's been hanging up all these years,\par
getting too tight for an Emperor inclined to run to tummy. The shining\par
sword was getting rusty in its regulation sheath. And then in the nick\par
of time--happens the Affair of Sarajevo.  The news came through that\par
Sunday in Paris.  I remember how Spitz's Restaurant boiled over, and the\par
people were shouting 'Sarajevo' on the boulevards.  By George!  I forgot\par
you were in bed and asleep while we were dining."\par
\par
Margot, between waking and sleeping, had got some inkling of the tragedy\par
of that night.  She asked, as Franky took off his hat and proceeded to\par
mop his non-intellectual forehead:\par
\par
"And is Sarajevo likely to stop me from going to Berlin?"\par
\par
Franky left off mopping and said, looking at her squarely:\par
\par
"If Austria's Note to Serbia is--what the Kaiser would like it to\par
be--you may take it we're on the giddy verge of a General All-Round\par
Scrap."\par
\par
"You mean--a war?"\par
\par
"I mean _the_ War that'll dwarf all others by comparison. The War of\par
Nations, that the prophet wrote of in Revelations.  Armageddon....  The\par
Last Battle.  The Big Bust Up that comes before the end."\par
\par
"Darling old boy, what rot!"\par
\par
"Rot if you like.  You wait and see what happens. D'you pipe me tipping\par
you the gag Asquithian?"  He grinned at the idea.\par
\par
"Franky, you've set me asking myself something."\par
\par
"Why you've married an idiot? ... Is that it?"  He turned upon her a\par
rueful face from which the grin had been wiped away.\par
\par
Margot said, as the car turned smoothly into Short Street and stopped\par
before the Club portico:\par
\par
"No, but--How is it you know--all the things you know, when I've always\par
known you knew nothing about anything?"\par
\par
He shook his head.\par
\par
"Give it up! ... No, I don't!  The answer is--I'm one of those\par
fellows--and the Services are simply stiff with 'em, who are absolute\par
asses till it's necessary for 'em to be something else."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXIII*\par
\par
                            *A MODERN CLUB*\par
\par
\par
Perhaps in those prehistoric days before the War, you knew the big,\par
cool, ground-floor dining-room of the "Ladies' Social" Club.  They\par
lunched excellently at Margot's pet table in the corner near the\par
conservatory, between whose rows of well-tended pot-plants you pass to\par
the smoke-room, celebrated for its Persian divan, and\par
green-and-rose-coloured glass dome.\par
\par
Soon the Club would be abandoned to sweeps, painters, charwomen, and\par
window-cleaners.  Just now everything was in full swing.  As the little\par
tables became vacant, the drawing-rooms and lounges filled up.  The\par
smoke-room was a crush of well got-up men and extravagantly-caparisoned\par
women, chattering nineteen to the dozen under a thick blue canopy of\par
Turkish, Egyptian, and Virginian. The tang of Kuemmel and Benedictine\par
and Creme de Menthe came to you with the fragrance of the Club's\par
especial coffee and the reek of innumerable illusion perfumes.\par
\par
People were having a cigarette and a gossip before going on to Lord's to\par
see the tennis-singles between Oxford and Cambridge; or the\par
Inter-Regimental Polo Finals at Hurlingham. Others had just motored back\par
from witnessing the rowing-matches at Henley, between Eton and Darley,\par
and the Eton second Eight and Montbeau College, and were recuperating\par
before dropping in for a whiff of the new comedy at the Ambassador's, or\par
the latest revue at the Fleur de Lis.  To be followed by Tango Tea at\par
the Rocroy, or Unlimited Bridge at the house of an accommodating friend.\par
\par
Perhaps you can recall them--those men and women of the best and bluest\par
blood in Britain, strenuously spending their days in doing nothing as\par
expensively as ever it could be done.  Light, frivolous, shallow,\par
dry-hearted; restlessly seeking new things on which to waste their\par
barren energies, they seemed, and bore out their seeming in all\par
thoroughness; the degenerate sons and daughters of a once great and\par
splendid race.\par
\par
Save Vanity and the Pride of Life there seemed but little in Eve or\par
Adam.  Not overmuch grey brain-matter appeared to be contained within\par
their small neat skulls. Though in comparison with the modern Eve,\par
slangy, loud, extravagantly attired in every tint of the Teutonic\par
dye-chemist's chromatic register, topped with feathers that missed the\par
ceiling by a bare half-foot, Adam in his neutral greys, and buffs and\par
browns, and umbers, struck you as a being of mild demeanour and uncostly\par
apparel, until looking closer, you found him out.\par
\par
His nice hair was gummed about his head as sleekly as a golliwog's.  He\par
sported stays, for the preservation of his silhouette.  His gossamer\par
cambric exhaled perfumes like a Georgian dandy's.  Fashionable\par
complexion-creams lent his tanned and well-shaved cheek a tempting\par
peachiness. His socks were all too lovely for description by this feeble\par
pen of mine.  The uppers of his boots were of every imaginable material\par
and substance, ranging from silk brocade, green lizard, and ivory-white\par
shark skin, to sandy-pink armadillo-belly, or the tender grey of the\par
African gazelle.\par
\par
The results of the Olympic Games of 1912 must have made dour reading for\par
the fathers of these youthful Britons, remembering their own triumphs in\par
the early eighties.  A bitter pill for those stark old men, their\par
grandfathers, makers of 'Varsity records in '61 and '67, whose faith in\par
the superiority of British lungs and muscles had been bequeathed them by\par
their own sires.  Yet their juniors took it calmly.  They carried the\par
stigma of inferiority with cheerful indifference.  Even while holding it\par
the thing best worth living for--they placidly submitted to be\par
outclassed in sport.\par
\par
And both the man and the woman of this era were possessed by strange\par
crazes and pleased with vivid contrasts. The musical jig-saw puzzles of\par
Lertes, Hein, and de Blonc vied in their favour with the weird Oriental\par
Operas of the Russian Rimsky-Korsakov and the delicate rhapsodies of\par
Delius, and the sylvan nymphs and fauns of Russian Ballet shared their\par
plaudits with Senora Panchita and Herr Maxi Zuchs, the celebrated\par
exponents of the Tango.\par
\par
Ah, yes, it was an extraordinary era.  Slips from that old, old Tree\par
that bore the Forbidden Fruit had been successfully grafted upon so many\par
old-world stocks in British orchards, that you caught a tang of its\par
exotic flavour in almost everything.  Play ran high.  Luxury ran riot.\par
Period Balls and Upas Club Cabaret Suppers were IT--absolutely IT.\par
Morality was at lowest ebb--Religion a forgotten formulary.  And as the\par
Christian virtues cheapened, so the prices of dress, jewellery,\par
motor-cars, and other indispensables of modern existence climbed to\par
still more amazing altitudes.  The marvel was, because nobody seemed to\par
have any money--where the money came from to pay for these things?  What\par
we are yet to pay for the wholesale levelling of moral barriers, and the\par
abolition of old-world modesty and good taste, that distinguished the\par
years of ill-fame 1913 and 1914, only Heaven knows.\par
\par
Even more comprehensively pervasive than the illusion perfumes extracted\par
from coal-tar by German chemists, and supplied us by German\par
manufacturers; even more striking than the dazzling, vivid aniline dyes,\par
procured from the same source, even more potent than the vast array of\par
by-product drugs which represent as it were the scum of the insulated\par
vats wherein the Teuton chemist macerates and mingles his high\par
explosives--was the strange, mysteriously pervasive flavour, the\par
seductively-suggestive tang of evil in the social atmosphere.  You\par
caught the look of secret, intimate, half-cynical knowledge in the faces\par
not only of the merest youths, but of the youngest, freshest, prettiest\par
girls.  Subjects held unmentionable a few years ago were openly\par
discussed in English drawing-rooms.  Curious lore in strange things old\par
and new was much sought after at this period, when Cubism and Futurism\par
governed design, not only in dress and stage scenery, but in Painting,\par
Sculpture, and Architecture; and dances known in the voodoo-houses of\par
East Africa and the West Indies, and the hells of Central America and\par
the Argentine were seen in the ball-rooms as in the brothels, of Paris\par
and London, Petrograd and Brussels, Vienna, New York, and Berlin.\par
\par
Novelty was so much the rage, that if the Arch-Enemy of Mankind had\par
appeared among the exclusive patrons of a fashionable night-club in any\par
one of these cities, a hearty welcome would have been extended to him,\par
and his ripe experience would have been laid under contribution with a\par
view to imparting to the latest Cabaret entertainment some exotic\par
novelty from Hell.\par
\par
\par
Franky with obtrusive care selected a comfortable corner of the Persian\par
divan for Margot, and while she signed for coffee and Kuemmel,\par
established himself at her side.\par
\par
They were isolated, it seemed to Kittums.  Friends nodded and smiled\par
cordially, but did not attempt to join them.  Was it because Franky's\par
too-possessive manner had told secrets? ... She shivered and glanced at\par
her lord.  He said, as the light-footed button-boys scoured about with\par
coffee and liqueur-trays, while the electric fans purred, the blue\par
smoke-canopy thickened under the green and rose glass dome, and the\par
clamour of many feminine voices, in combination with the gaudy feathers\par
of the clamourers, suggested the South American macaw-house at the Zoo:\par
\par
"My eye! you're pretty thick in here.  Might be a fog in mid-Channel."\par
He mounted a square monocle recently purchased in Paris and the pride of\par
his bosom, threw back his head and stared up into the famous green and\par
rose dome. "Swagger affair.  How much did it tot up to?"\par
\par
"Seventeen hundred, clear, with the carpet and the divan."\par
\par
"Pretty stiff!"  His doleful whistle set Margot's teeth on edge.  She\par
added:\par
\par
"And rattling cheap at the price!  And--if it wasn't, I was spending my\par
own money....  There was nobody--then--to interfere!"\par
\par
He conceded:\par
\par
"Of course I don't suggest that you were done in the eye. Probably you\par
got the value of your dibs.  But you'll have something better to spend\par
cash on presently.  Me, too! We must both draw in our horns now,\par
Kittums.  For the sake of--you know who! ... Hullo!  Is anything wrong?"\par
\par
She had winced, but she gritted her little teeth, and fought back the\par
rising hysteria.  She could have shrieked, or thrown the little\par
coffee-pot at his head.  He went on, recognising friends through the\par
smoke-haze:\par
\par
"There's Lady Beau with that German aviator-chap we met in Paris.  Big\par
red-headed brute.  You remember him?  And--who's the girl?  But for her\par
hair, I'd say it was Miss Saxham.  By the Great Brass Hat, it is!  With\par
a wig, or dyed...."\par
\par
"Dyed.  It was done in Paris--done most beautifully."  Margot's eyes had\par
lighted up with interest.  "I must have forgotten to tell you.  I've\par
known it three or four days. Don't you like it?"\par
\par
"Like it?"  Franky had reached for his little glass and gulped the\par
contents hurriedly.  "My stars, I never saw such a transformation.\par
Order another Kuemmel, please, to give me a buck-up."\par
\par
"Take mine.  I simply loathe the sticky stuff."  She added, as Franky\par
obliged: "_I_ think that Pat looks ripping."\par
\par
"All too ripping.  That's where the trouble comes in."  He went on:\par
"When her hair was black, you knew where it was you'd seen her.  Makin'\par
one in an endless procession of women--all with long eyes and big busts\par
and curving hips, walkin'--like pussy-cats along a roof-ridge, on the\par
walls of those old Egyptian temples we did together--that November when\par
I got such spoons on you--going up with the Gillinghams from Cairo to\par
Philae--a flat-bottomed Nile tug towin' the whole crowd in a string of\par
dahabeahs.  You remember those ochre-coloured Nile sunrises?  When a\par
dust-storm had been blowin' over the Desert, and the River was all\par
wrinkly white, like curdled milk."\par
\par
"How killingly poetic!"\par
\par
"Am I poetic?  Good egg!  Never thought I'd live to be called that."\par
\par
"Live and learn!"  Margot's laugh was a hard little silvery tinkle.  She\par
too was remembering the sunrises and sunsets of Egypt, and the long days\par
under the green canvas awnings.  How beautiful she had thought the brown\par
eyes that seemed only vacuous now.  She, Margot, would be ugly very soon\par
now, she told herself.  Already her small face showed lines and hollows.\par
Soon beauty-loving men and women would turn their eyes away....  Her\par
cheval-glass would tell her why, and shop-windows when she passed them\par
would reveal her shapelessness.  She would only possess interest for\par
three people.  For the doctor, as a patient.  For the certificated\par
nurse, as a Case.  For her husband, as the potential mother of the boy\par
he longed for. And--what price Margot?\par
\par
"Should you like me to take you to see some polo, or wouldn't a\par
chuff-chuff in the country be best?"  Franky's eyes were full of hungry\par
solicitude as they rested on the small, pinched features.  "You look a\par
bit fagged, it strikes me!"\par
\par
She nipped her little lower lip, stung by the tone of sympathetic\par
proprietorship.  "Oh! very well.  A drive!" she told him, and they\par
passed together from the smoking-room. The sheath-skirt revealed, as she\par
moved, what she would have hidden.  Von Herrnung smiled, following the\par
little figure with bold, curious glances.  Other men stared, if more\par
discreetly.  Towering feathers nodded to each other as their feminine\par
wearers commented:\par
\par
"Poor little Margot, how quite too rough on her!"\par
\par
Said Lady Beauvayse, assuming the rip-saw Yankee accent in which it\par
pleased her to deliver her witticisms:\par
\par
"Say now! if we women could pick babies right away off the\par
strawberry-vines, it would save a deal of trouble, and a considerable\par
pile of self-respect."\par
\par
Everybody laughed.  A slender white and golden woman with a string of\par
sapphires very much the colour of her own eyes, picked up a toy\par
Pekingese that squatted near her, and said, cuddling the goggling morsel\par
under her chin:\par
\par
"I agree.  When I look at my two precious duckies I say to myself: 'You\par
little dears, for each of your sweet sakes I became a plain woman with a\par
shapeless silhouette and saucer-eyes.  Now that I've done my duty to\par
your pappy and Posterity, this is the only kind of baby I'll indulge\par
in."  She kissed the Pekingese on the end of its black snub-nose. "And\par
when I want a new one--I'll buy it at the shop!"\par
\par
"_Noch besser_.  Why not hire one? ..." suggested von Herrnung.\par
\par
Mrs. Charterhouse laughed and gave him the Pekingese to hold.  But it\par
snapped at him furiously and she took the little beast back again.\par
\par
"Dogs do not like me," said the big German.  "You will read perhaps in\par
novels that that is a bad sign, yes?"\par
\par
"I never read novels," returned Mrs. Charterhouse, with her famous\par
manner, "nor any books, only bits of the papers for the Sporting and\par
Society news.  And Reports of Divorce Proceedings, and the Notices in\par
Bankruptcy.  One likes to know what one's friends are doing, and where\par
they are to be found.  Don't you, Count?  Not that there is any great\par
difficulty in ascertaining your whereabouts, just now, I fancy....  Why,\par
what has become of Patrine?"\par
\par
"Miss Saxham went in there just now to write a letter," said the smiling\par
von Herrnung, pointing to the leather-covered swing-doors communicating\par
with the writing-room. "She comes now, I think!  Yes, it is she!"  He\par
rose with his air of exaggerated courtesy as the tall figure of Patrine\par
Saxham returned through the swing-doors and re-crossed the room.  She\par
carried her head high, and had a letter in her hand.  The alteration in\par
the colour of her hair made her whiteness almost startling.  There were\par
bluish shadows about her long eyes, and her rounded cheeks had lost a\par
little of their fulness, but her beauty had never been more apparent\par
than now.\par
\par
"She has dyed, therefore she is dead to me!" groaned Courtley, who was,\par
as usual, in attendance on Lady Beauvayse.  He added, plaintively: "It's\par
like--white-washing the Sphinx, or enamelling a first-class\par
battle-cruiser in some fashionable colour.  Why did you let her do it,\par
my lady fair?"\par
\par
Lady Beauvayse retorted:\par
\par
"Am I Miss Saxham's mother that I should meddle in her love-affairs?"\par
\par
"If I was acquainted with her mother," said Courtley, below his breath,\par
"and thought the good lady would take my tip seriously, I'd step in and\par
nip this affair in the bud. It's no go, even if Miss Saxham thinks it\par
is.  It's a dud. That German flying-chap is booked to marry a cousin; a\par
Baroness Something von Wolfensbragen-Hirschenbuttel. I've seen it in the\par
Berlin _Lokal Anzeiger_, and that's inspired, a sort of Imperial Court\par
Almanac.  And even if it wasn't true, there are reasons--"  His kind\par
grey eyes were worried, he tugged at his pointed black beard in a vexed\par
way.  "Take me seriously, Miladi, tell her what I've told you, before\par
it's too late!"\par
\par
"And bring on myself the fate of the interferer.... Couldn't you--since\par
you're so anxious?" Lady Beauvayse began.\par
\par
"Not possible," said Courtley.  "Too crushed with responsibilities.  Got\par
to brush up my seamanship, while my junior executive swots away in Docks\par
at Chatham, fillin' in the watch-bill and making out\par
commissioning-cards."\par
\par
"You've got a ship, do you mean?"\par
\par
Courtley nodded.\par
\par
"They call her one at the Admiralty just by way of being funny.  When\par
they've scraped off the dirt enough to get at her, she may turn out to\par
be a first-class protected cruiser. Twenty months out of commission--and\par
mobilised for the Spithead Naval Review."\par
\par
"Ought one to be glad? ... Does it mean that we're to congratulate you\par
on promotion?" asked puzzled Lady Beauvayse.\par
\par
"Well," Courtley admitted cautiously, "when I've got my full-dress\par
frock-coat and sword out of pawn, and hoisted my pennant and called on\par
the post Commander-in-Chief--I shall be something between a Rear-Admiral\par
and a Post Captain--or they'll have told me wrong."\par
\par
"And the Review--what do you call it?" persisted Lady Beauvayse.  "Can\par
one go and see it--whenever it comes off?"\par
\par
"It'll be big enough to see--with a stiffish pair of binkies," admitted\par
Courtley in his gentlest manner; "and the newspapers seem to have\par
arranged it for somewhere in the middle of the month.  As to what you're\par
to call it--if you called it an Object Lesson on the biggest scale for\par
the use of German Kultur Classes, perhaps you wouldn't be very wide of\par
the bull."\par
\par
He got up before Lady Beauvayse could rejoin, and had met Patrine, and\par
engineered her into his vacated seat next her friend upon the divan\par
almost before she knew.  She lowered her tall person upon the cushions,\par
studiously avoiding von Herrnung's glances.  She wore a white\par
embroidered gown of cobwebby material and extreme scantiness, a stole of\par
black cock's feathers was looped about her shoulders, and on her dead\par
beech-leaf-coloured hair sat a curious little hat of glittering silver\par
spangles, from which sprang a single black cock's plume.\par
\par
"What have you all been talking about?" she asked, looking about her.\par
\par
Lady Wastwood, who sat near, answered, balancing her long, slim, fragile\par
personality on the fender-stool before the hearth that was filled with\par
tall ferns and flowering plants in pots:\par
\par
"We were saying--what a wretched pity the process of racial reproduction\par
is so abominably unbecoming.  It really points to a loose style of\par
reasoning on the part of Nature--or whoever it is who arranges these\par
things!"\par
\par
Who does not know Lady Wastwood.  She affected, at this period, a\par
skull-cap of gold-green hair and a triangular chalk-white face, with a\par
V-shaped mouth, painted scarlet as a Pierrot's.  Her eyebrows were black\par
and resembled musical slurs.  Through her few diaphanous garments you\par
could have counted every bone of her frail person, so light that it was\par
a favourite vacation joke with her eldest boy--who was now at Sandhurst\par
qualifying for a Cavalry Commission--to sprint with his widowed mother\par
on his shoulder up and down corridors and stairs.\par
\par
Listen to Trixie:\par
\par
"I suppose--Nature.  She's so unreasonable--that must be why she's a\par
she, in literature.  She implanted in us poor women the raging desire to\par
be pretty under all imaginable circumstances....  At the same time she\par
says to us: 'You're immoral, unnatural, and selfish, if you don't\par
replenish the Race.  Go and do it!'  Consequently, when one is ordered\par
in that bullying way to choose between immorality and ugliness, one\par
calls out: 'Oh! do let me be pretty, please!'"\par
\par
A soldierly, good-looking man, sitting with a charming girl in a\par
particularly smoky corner, lazily propounded:\par
\par
"Why do women covet prettiness beyond everything?"\par
\par
"To please men, I rather surmise," said Lady Beauvayse, turning her\par
Romney head in the direction of the speaker, who queried:\par
\par
"Ah! but why do women want to please men?"\par
\par
"I can answer that," interrupted Mrs. Charterhouse. "Because she who\par
pleases is perfectly sure of having a gorgeous time."\par
\par
"It has been said by some inspired idiot," lisped Lady Wastwood "that\par
women make themselves beautiful for the sake of their own sex.  Give us\par
your opinion on this question, Count von Herrnung.  Did I put on this\par
perfectly devey frock for Miss Saxham, or for you?"\par
\par
"_Gnaedige Graefin_, for neither myself nor Miss Saxham. For your own\par
pleasure," said von Herrnung, "have you joy in making yourself\par
beautiful."\par
\par
"You feel like that when your tailor has done you particularly well?"\par
asked Lady Wastwood, wickedly, looking down her long, thin nose to hide\par
the sparks of humour in her eyes. Half a dozen pairs of ears were cocked\par
to catch the answer, in which von Herrnung's characteristic lack of\par
humour showed.\par
\par
"Gracious Countess, certainly.  It is _prachtvoll_ for a cultured man to\par
study and develop his physical advantages. To please women," he made his\par
little insolent bow, "who adore Beauty, and for the sake of ingratiating\par
oneself with men.  But above all for one's own sake.  For ugliness is\par
despicable," said von Herrnung.  His florid face paled, his hard blue\par
eyes dilated, he shivered as he spoke with uncontrollable disgust.  "It\par
is--_niedrig_!  There is no other word! No longer to be beautiful and\par
strong--that would be horrible!  There are many ugly accidents in our\par
German Flying Service.  Thus far I have escaped disfigurement. But when\par
my time comes I shall take care to be killed outright.  Better to die\par
than to be made hideous!"\par
\par
"Did you hear?" said the man in the distant corner to the charming girl\par
who shared it with him.  "The fellow's dead in earnest.  And he is\par
uncommonly good-looking, though I don't care about the German Service\par
type of man myself.  Don't like their clothes, don't like their\par
jewellery, don't like their tone when they're talking to women, and\par
simply loathe it when they're talking to me!"\par
\par
"It's a case of Doctor Fell," said his pretty friend.  "Now _I_ should\par
admire him--if he admired himself a little less, and his valet or\par
somebody with influence over him could persuade him to cut that awful\par
thumb-nail.  No, you can't see it now.  He's wearing a glove on his left\par
hand.  But it can't be under two inches long."\par
\par
"Queer kind of freak for a Twentieth Centurion," said the man\par
contemptuously.  "All very well for the Imperial Court of China, or a\par
Stone Age make-up for a Covent Garden Fancy Ball.  But for a London\par
drawin'-room in the year 1914 it is a little off the bull.  We must\par
approach Miss Saxham in the matter of cutting it.  She appears to be the\par
Ruling Star."\par
\par
His friend glanced across at the big knot of people gathered near the\par
ferny fireplace.\par
\par
"They go about together a good deal, and he does stare at her in rather\par
a possessive style.  She's so awfully good to look at, isn't she?"\par
\par
"She is; but she isn't quite so good for you to know!"\par
\par
"Why?"\par
\par
"Could we drop the subject?  I'll say why later.  Let's scoot now!  With\par
luck, we could nip in for the end of the second act of 'The Filberts' at\par
Ryley's Theatre, and see Jimmy Griggson do 'The Dance of the\par
Varalette.'"\par
\par
And they rose and sauntered away in search of entertainment, leaving\par
Cynthia Charterhouse drawing out von Herrnung, who seemed in a\par
particularly arrogant mood. Did he like England and London especially?\par
Did he find English women as nice, generally, as the friends he had left\par
at home?\par
\par
ue\par
\par
"Nice....  One is charmed with English ladies!" declared von Herrnung.\par
"So tall, willowy, and elegant, so independent of manner, and so amiably\par
ready to make a stranger feel at home!  True, they have not the\par
plumpness and repose of our German ladies ... at the theatres especially\par
they are rather thin than otherwise....  But they have _gehen_ and\par
_chic_"--he showed his white teeth--"and change is a delightful thing!"\par
\par
Patrine, silent in her settee-corner, wondered whether Trixie Wastwood\par
and Cynthia Charterhouse knew that he was insulting them?\par
\par
"Change from a fat woman to a thin one, is that what you mean?" asked\par
Mrs. Charterhouse.  She added: "I'm so glad we strike you as having lots\par
of go.  Perhaps it's a result of our being given to exercise, that\par
general effect of slimness you mention.  But if German women don't walk,\par
or ride, or skate, or fence, or swim, they do dance a great deal."\par
\par
"They dance a great deal, yes!" agreed von Herrnung. "One might say they\par
are passionately devoted to it.  Dancing is also one of the chief joys\par
of a German officer's life--when he has handsome partners to choose\par
amongst!"  He added: "When one is young, and the blood runs hot in the\par
veins, what more glowing pleasures can Life offer, than to ride a noble\par
horse, to drink glorious wine, or to dance all night with a beautiful\par
woman, to the sound of music voluptuous and exquisite!"\par
\par
Patrine, behind the shelter of a copy of the _Pall Mall Gazette_, was\par
shuddering uncontrollably.  Her life seemed driven back from the\par
extremities to centre about her heart. In that and in her brain were\par
glowing cores of fire.  All else was ice, rigid and heavy and cold.\par
\par
"Dear me!" came plaintively from Mrs. Charterhouse. She signalled with\par
her eyebrows to Lady Wastwood and continued, as the diaphanous Trixie\par
came drifting to her assistance: "Really, I shall have to seek a\par
delightful change by going to Germany.  I'd quite forgotten how\par
different you are!  The way you talk about your blood, and all that.\par
It's simply too awfully interesting!  Trixie, you've got to listen to\par
this!"\par
\par
"I need no telling, I assure you.  I have been drinking in Count von\par
Herrnung's eloquence at every pore," affirmed Trixie.  She added: "Like\par
you I have been deeply intrigued by his descriptions of his countrymen.\par
So, _so_ different from our poor creatures, who don't drink glorious\par
wine because they funk gouty complications, and leave their noble horses\par
eating their heads off in loose-boxes while they're scorching about the\par
country in racing-cars.  And as for dancing all night--"  She shrugged\par
her frail shoulders, and elevated her Pierrot eyebrows beneath the veil\par
that tightly swathed her white triangular face.\par
\par
"Doesn't it fire you to go to Germany?" gushed Mrs. Charterhouse.\par
"Why"--she demanded, raising her fine eyes to the genuine Adam\par
ceiling--"why can't my husband get a post in the Berlin Diplomatic,\par
instead of stupid old Petersburg?  One never _dreamed_ Germans could be\par
so interesting before!"\par
\par
"We are interesting, yes!" blandly agreed von Herrnung. He lighted a\par
fresh cigarette, balanced his magnificent person upon an inlaid Oriental\par
chess-stool, folded his huge arms upon his broad breast, and turned upon\par
Trixie and the impressionable Cynthia the batteries of his superb blue\par
eyes. "_Es mag wohl sein_--it may possibly be because the Englishman is\par
a human machine--a cold and formal, if intelligent being; while the\par
German is a child of Nature, whatever his calling may be.  His bounding\par
pulses throb under the official or military uniform as though it were a\par
fawn-skin worn by a young satyr.  He can sing.  He can revel.  He can\par
enjoy. He can love----"\par
\par
"He can love!  Now you're getting really quite too interesting!" Mrs.\par
Charterhouse exclaimed in seeming ecstasy: "Do go on, Count.  Pray, pray\par
tell us how German officers love!"\par
\par
"Yet this exuberance, and seeming-careless child-likeness," pursued von\par
Herrnung, "co-exists in the representative male of our glorious German\par
nation with an energy which is pitilessly indomitable, and a hardness\par
like that of diamond, or of the metal of the Hammer of Thor.  Scratch\par
the child, joyous and voluptuous"--the ladies nodded to each other\par
delightedly at this second reference to voluptuousness--"you will find\par
beneath its rosy skin the German Superman.  _Gnaedige Graefin_, may I\par
give you a cigarette?"  He pulled out a massive silver-gilt case, and\par
offered it to Lady Wastwood, who had thrown away the end of a tiny Pera.\par
\par
"Thanks," said the lady, "but it might turn out a super-cigarette and\par
disagree with me.  How astonishingly well-informed you Germans are upon\par
the subject of yourselves! I've met heaps of your countrymen whom the\par
subject seemed perfectly to obsess.  I suppose they begin to teach you\par
at a very early age, don't they?  Don't you suppose they would, Cynthia\par
dear?"\par
\par
Mrs. Charterhouse agreed.\par
\par
"Of course.  But I wonder if that sort of--might one call it--intensive\par
culture?--can be good for you?"  With her charming head on one side she\par
regarded von Herrnung pensively.  "Don't you _sometimes_ get fed up with\par
yourselves? One would somehow suppose you would!  Like the East End\par
Board School children whose mother had to write to the Fifth Standard\par
teacher to ask her not to tell Hemma and 'Arriet any more nasty things\par
about their insides."\par
\par
Courtley and Lady Beauvayse, who under cover of a separate conversation\par
had been listening, were seized with simultaneous attacks of coughing,\par
rose and escaped from the smoking-room.  Patrine Saxham remained,\par
seeming to study the newspaper she had picked up.  But only a confused\par
jumble of letters, big and little, danced up and down the columns she\par
held before her eyes.\par
\par
And yet there were lines scattered here and there throughout the\par
newspapers, that boded ill for the peace of the world. How little we\par
dreamed of what was coming while crowded London audiences applauded\par
Jimmy Greggson in the "Dance of the Varalette."  The River was ablaze\par
with multi-coloured sweaters, vast crowds planked their gate-money to\par
witness cricket-matches, lawn-tennis and polo-matches, Flying contests,\par
and bouts between International champions at the ancient game of\par
fisticuffs.\par
\par
Even while the handsome young French heavy-weight Carpentier was\par
whacking the Yankee Smith at Olympia, white-faced, weary-eyed men of\par
great affairs were spending the hot hours of the July days and\par
nights--_minus_ a stray half-hour for a meal and a snatched eyeful of\par
sleep now and then--in reading reports in cipher sent by lesser men,\par
agents of the Secret Intelligence Department--who were registered as\par
numbers and owned no names.\par
\par
These told of vast preparations long complete, and terrible designs\par
perfect and perfecting.  Poison-fruit, grown and matured in shade, now\par
bursting-ripe and ready to kill. The aerials thrilled, the long waves\par
travelled through invisible ether, carrying the despatches for the\par
weary-eyed men.\par
\par
The despatches were not all in cipher.  Thus little polyglot employes,\par
youthful radiotelegraphic operators in charge of ship-stations in\par
Territorial or foreign waters, or Wireless posts quite recently\par
established on foreign frontiers, found themselves sharers in the secret\par
councils of Ambassadors, Emperors, Kings, and Presidents.\par
\par
In their ear-pieces such words as "situation," "utmost gravity,"\par
"friction avoided," "Triple Alliance," and "Triple Entente," were\par
repeated over and over.  To them the tuned spark sang what the Tsar was\par
saying to his Cousin of Great Britain and the Dominions overseas.  They\par
heard the British Foreign Secretary talking from Downing Street to the\par
British Ambassador at Berlin, and the British Ambassador at Paris, and\par
the French President, on a visit to Tsarskoe Selo, replying to\par
_communiques_ from the Quai d'Orsay.  Also de Munsen from the Embassy at\par
Vienna, confirming Whitehall views as to the extreme gravity of the\par
Austro-Servian situation.\par
\par
Last, but not least, the voice from a certain guarded sanctum in the\par
Kaiserlicher Palais on the Schloss Platz, Berlin, saying in a cipher of\par
grouped numbers, the secret language of Hohenzollern intrigue not\par
understood of little operators--things that bleached the face of the\par
listener in London to the yellow of old cheese.\par
\par
"As Vicegerent of the World, charged by Almighty God with the supreme\par
duty of maintaining peace among nations ... warn these silly devils of\par
the danger in which they stand!  Just for the word 'neutrality'--a word\par
in War-time often disregarded--they risk annihilation of a dynasty by my\par
conquering sword, and the inevitable blotting-out of the British race.\par
Invasion Belgium indispensable.... Must strike the blow before Russia\par
could get to the frontier. Life and death as regards the Success of my\par
Plan.  Delay by diplomacy.  Promise anything for neutrality.  Obtain an\par
understanding of non-intervention.  Bluff for all you are worth!"\par
\par
Again in yet more groups of numbers, the vocal spark sang on and on:\par
\par
"Attention.  If the Secret Service agent who has managed to get into\par
Lord Clanronald's service as under-librarian at Gwyll Castle can secure\par
complete copies--or better still, the originals--of the old Lord's plans\par
for construction of the secret War-machine that hypocritical England has\par
kept up her sleeve out of so-called humanity since the days of the\par
British Regency--strike a deal with him at once.  To the _menu_ that\par
will presently be served to our enemies--beginning with\par
Super-Explosive--explosive bullets, incendiary shells, lachrymatory\par
shells serving as _entrees_--the bombardment of Dover from Calais--the\par
destruction of London and the chief Naval Ports of Great Britain by our\par
Zeppelin Fleet being the _piece de resistance_ of the banquet--the\par
Clanronald Death-engine will be added as fifth course!  Thou wilt pay\par
the rogue who has dared to stickle for higher terms ten thousand pounds\par
in English banknotes on account of the sum of twelve million marks he\par
presumptuously demands of us.  The balance will be paid him on personal\par
application at the Wilhelmstrasse--you understand!  Warn Prince Henry\par
and von Moltke not to risk bringing the Secret Plans personally.  Should\par
the loss of the documents be discovered, suspicion would instantly\par
attach to one of these two.  Trust not the thief; he may be tempted to\par
betray us.  Send the plans by Undersea Boat 18 now on coast-observation\par
duty in Area 88--fathoms 50--44, east of Spurn Head.  Annulled.  Forward\par
by air.  Squadron-Captain-Pilot von Herrnung of my 10th Field Flight\par
will be detailed for this duty, being now in London investigating the\par
value of a new stabiliser--rejected by the English War Office--which the\par
French Chiefs of the Service Ae are anxious to secure.  Tell him to\par
obtain a personal flying-test from the inventor.  I say no further! As\par
the Hohenzollern were noble robber-knights, so also were von Herrnung's\par
ancestors.  Let the eagle fly home to his Imperial master with booty\par
from across the sea.  England may suppose him drowned.  France also....\par
We shall know better....  A hearty welcome awaits the proud bird-knight\par
alighting on our German soil."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXIV*\par
\par
                             *DISILLUSION*\par
\par
\par
Rhona Helvellyn came stalking in, looked round, recognised Patrine, came\par
over and dropped down beside her on the divan, full to the brim of the\par
invariable subject, and suffering to talk.\par
\par
Through the good offices of a legal pal she had got in to hear the\par
Suffragette Trial at the Old Bailey that day.  Fan Braid and Kitty Neek\par
had been frightfully plucky.  Full of grit and vim, in spite of the six\par
weeks' hunger-strike.  Began shrieking like Jimmy O! the moment they\par
were brought into the dock by the warders and wardresses.  On being\par
rebuked by the Judge, Fan had bunked a bundle of pamphlets at the head\par
of his lordship, catching the Clerk of the Court, who was seated\par
immediately underneath the Bench, no end of a biff in the eye.\par
\par
"And then?"\par
\par
Patrine heard a strange voice from her own stiff lips asking the\par
question.\par
\par
"Then both of 'em were removed from the Dock.  It was done--in time!"\par
Rhona's light eyes danced with enjoyment. "Such a scrimmage!  Such a\par
rumpus!  Took three men and a woman to tackle each of 'em.  We could\par
hear 'em giving tongue all the way down to the cells.  Then they had to\par
go on with the Trial without 'em."  She chuckled. "You may guess there\par
were a lot of us at the back of the Court waiting--just for that!\par
Perfect wadge all together. Hell and trimmings when we started.  They\par
had to eject us before they could jog on with their gay old summing-up!"\par
\par
"But in the end they got through?"  The weary voice was so unlike\par
Patrine's that she wondered why Rhona did not jump and stare at her.\par
But Rhona was mounted on her hobby-horse, and unobservant of other\par
things.\par
\par
"Through right enough!  And Fan and Kitty--"  Rhona screwed up her lips\par
into the shape of a whistle, and winked away a tear that hung on one of\par
her fair eyelashes; "It's too brutal!  Three months each, and poor\par
little Kitty dying of lung-trouble.  They only brought her back from\par
Davos in May.  That riles me!"  She clenched her hands fiercely and went\par
on, cautiously lowering her tone: "So far I've taken no active share in\par
any Militant Demonstration. Partly because I'd be wiped off the Club\par
books if I got spouting in public, or was mixed up in any police-court\par
business, partly because I'm funky--there's the word!  But at last I'm\par
wound up!  It was Kitty's little peaky-white face did it! ... She--she\par
broke a blood-vessel as the warders were carrying her down to the\par
cells."\par
\par
A sob choked Rhona's voice, and a spasm of misery wrenched her.  She\par
controlled herself.  She was deadly in earnest--wound up to go, as she\par
had said.  She went on, talking rapidly, in a tone that only reached the\par
ear it had been meant for.  How many such secret disclosures the Club\par
divan had known.\par
\par
"I've thought....  A regular swarm of Distinguished French and Belgian\par
Big Pots and Little Pots--Mayors--Prefects and Deputies, Judges, Press\par
Representatives and Inspectors-General--are engaged in Discovering\par
England this week as ever is.  It's an echo of the Entente Cordiale.\par
Behind the badge of the International Advancement Association--I've got\par
one!--I might drop in at one of their farewell speechifications, I\par
believe the next's on Friday at Leamington--and heckle 'em like one\par
o'clock! Ask 'em why women don't have the Vote in France and\par
Belgium----"\par
\par
"Don't they?"\par
\par
"Nix a bit!  Not for all the fuss they make about the sex.  Or--to fix\par
the scene of my maiden effort nearer home--there's a Banquet of\par
Archbishops, Bishops and their wives at the Mansion House to-morrow\par
night.  Music just after the flesh-pots and before the speeches or\par
after--a select company of Concert Artistes, the gemmen in boiled shirts\par
and the usual accompaniments; the ladies in white with black sashes and\par
black gloves.  And that's where I shall come in--in white with black\par
trimmings.  Land of Hope and Glory!--when I get up and ask the\par
Archbishop of Canterbury to plump for Female Suffrage!--or shall it be\par
the Lord Mayor? ... Won't my Uncle Gustavus burst the buttons off his\par
episcopal waistcoat.  You've seen him. He's Bishop of Dorminster--and\par
they fasten 'em at the back."\par
\par
"Let the Bishop keep his buttons on!" said Patrine, suddenly and\par
savagely.  "What the--devil does it matter whether women get the Vote?\par
Would we keep it if we got it, or throw it away--oh! idiots--idiots!--to\par
gratify some vulgar vanity, or some beastly sensual whim?"\par
\par
"Gee-whillikins!"  Rhona whistled shrilly in astonishment. "Why, I\par
thought you were one of Us.  Not actively militant, but a sympathiser,\par
no end.  Didn't you get our Committee in touch with Mrs. Saxham, when\par
we'd set our hearts on having her speak at the Monster Meeting of Women\par
we're going to have in October at the Grand Imperial Hall?  She's\par
promised to address us on Suffrage and we're all over ourselves to hear\par
her.  That last article of hers in _The National Quarterly_--'The Burden\par
of Tyre,' has collared the literary cake.  People tell me who've read it\par
that she doesn't care a hang about the Vote for Women in any other sense\par
than that it'd open a gateway to legislation on the Sex Question of a\par
much more drastic kind.  She'd bring in a Bill to have moral offences\par
against children dealt with by a Jury of Mothers--a lot they'd leave of\par
the offender once they'd their claws on him!--and make it a Life\par
Sentence every time, for the fellow who seduces a girl."\par
\par
Patrine listened in stony silence.  Rhona chattered on. "Of course the\par
work she does amongst those unlucky wretches--young girls and women\par
who've come to grief--is topping.  But why waste herself rescuing\par
prostitutes and street-walkers?  Aren't any of us good enough--or bad\par
enough to interest her?  I'm going to ask her that when you introduce\par
me--remember you've promised to!"\par
\par
Patrine said in a voice jarred and harsh with anger:\par
\par
"Since your declared intention is to be offensive to Mrs. Saxham, whose\par
shoes neither you nor myself, nor any woman of our set is worthy to\par
unlace, I take back the promise, if it was ever given!"\par
\par
"What's up?"  Rhona turned and stared.  "I say!--but you look fearfully\par
seedy!  Worried about Margot, is that it?"  She was off on another tack,\par
carried by the light shifting breeze of her imagination.  "Poor little\par
Margot!--in spite of good advice and top-hole mascots--booked for the\par
Nursery Handicap--and out of the running for a year!"\par
\par
"Who told you--that?--about Margot?"\par
\par
"Melts--the head housemaid here--had it from Kittum's maid Pauline, who\par
dropped in to fetch away some stored luggage of her ladyship's....\par
They've furnished a house at Cadogan Place--Margot and her Franky-wanky.\par
West End enough, and quite exquie inside, but not as convee as the dear\par
old Club.  But--I believe I'm boring you."  Her nimble glance left\par
Patrine's face, and darted in the direction of von Herrnung.  "Who's the\par
big, good-looking, carroty man, gobbling you up with his eyes while he's\par
talking piffle to Cynthia and Trix?  Now I remember--I _have_ heard some\par
hints of your going over to the Common Enemy."  Rhona's sharp light eyes\par
sparkled like polished gold-stones.  "Is that the reason why you've\par
bleached your hair?  What a putrid shame of you!  And the Enemy's a\par
foreigner--a German!  Did he give you that gorgeous ring?"\par
\par
Upon the third finger of Patrine's left hand was the magpie pearl set in\par
platinum, gleaming to its wearer's fevered fancy, like some malignant\par
demon's eye.  Rhona caught the hand, and uttered a little squeak as\par
Patrine wrenched it away.  She--Patrine--was driven beyond endurance:\par
her self-command was breaking.  Her hair seemed to creep upon her\par
tingling scalp.  Down her spine and along the muscles of her thighs\par
passed slow recurrent waves of physical anguish.  She could have\par
screamed aloud, torn her garments, set her teeth in her own flesh.  But\par
she mastered herself sufficiently to say:\par
\par
"I won the ring over a bet in Paris.  You can see for yourself I don't\par
wear it on the engagement left.  Do not despair of me.  At this moment I\par
do not particularly esteem women.  But on the other hand, I absolutely\par
abominate men!"\par
\par
"Hope for you then, politically speaking," said the misanthropic Rhona.\par
"What, are you going?"\par
\par
Patrine had thrown aside her paper and risen, towering over her.  She\par
nodded without speaking, and went out of the smoking-room, crumpling the\par
letter she had written in her strong white hand.  She would not post it,\par
she told herself as she passed through the outer lounge.  She would go\par
and look up Uncle Owen at Harley Street.  She spoke a word to an agile\par
hall-boy in the vestibule and he skipped out, and signalled a taxi-cab.\par
\par
A handsome Darracq four-seater, enamelled bright yellow and fitted in\par
ebonized steel, was waiting by the kerbstone. As the taxi manoeuvred to\par
get round it, von Herrnung's voice said, speaking behind Patrine:\par
\par
"Stop the boy, that machine will not be wanted.... I have here a car\par
that is lent me by a friend."\par
\par
She turned and saw him, standing hat in hand.  His tone was pleasant,\par
and he was smiling.  He went on:\par
\par
"He--my friend--is a Secretary of our German Embassy. He has three\par
automobiles--why should he not lend me one?"  He replaced his hat and\par
pulled a curved gold cigar-case out of the breast-pocket of his\par
waistcoat asking: "I may light a zigarre after these stupid cigarettes I\par
have been smoking? It will not be unpleasant to _gnaediges Fraeulein_?"\par
\par
His courtesy insulted.  His smile was an outrage.  She controlled the\par
trembling of her lips with difficulty.  Whether he observed or not was\par
uncertain, he seemed to busy himself solely with the selection and\par
kindling of his cigar.\par
\par
"Pardon that I get in first, as I shall be driving!" he said, and threw\par
away the smoking vesta, pushed back the hall-boy who was wrestling with\par
the door-handle, got in and took his place at the steering-wheel,\par
beckoning to Patrine.\par
\par
"Thanks, but I cannot....  I am going to Berkeley Square."\par
\par
"I will drop you at Berkeley Square."  He met her eyes hardily.  "You\par
will not refuse me this pleasure, when I have not seen you since--"  The\par
slight significant pause stabbed as it had been meant to.  He saw her\par
wince, and finished: "Since two days.  Will you not get in?"\par
\par
She took the seat beside him.  He stretched his arm across her knees and\par
shut the door neatly.  She leaned back to avoid his touch, and he\par
smiled, feeling her shudder.  Her eyes were on his gloved left hand as\par
he drew it back.\par
\par
He manipulated the electrical starter and the yellow Darracq moved up\par
and out of Short Street.  Patrine stared before her, sitting rigid in\par
her place.  Not once did her glance visit _him_.  But every skilful\par
movement of his hands upon the steering-wheel, every creak of the\par
springy leather cushion under his great body, every tightening of his\par
mouth or twitch of his thick red eyebrows, were photographed upon her\par
brain.\par
\par
He was irreproachably got up in thin, loose grey tweed morning clothes,\par
cut by a West End tailor, and his feather-weight grey felt hat testified\par
to the make of Scott.  His knitted silk tie, a combination of electric\par
blue and vivid yellow, was a discordant note.  Patrine was certain it\par
must have been the work of some other woman in Berlin.  The heavy flat\par
gold ring through which the ends were drawn was set with a ruby and two\par
diamonds, another false note that jarred her painfully.  But he was\par
looking strong and well and in admirable condition.  His blue eyes were\par
bright, his red hair and his tightly-rolled moustache glittered in the\par
sunshine, there was a bloom of perfect health upon his florid skin.\par
\par
If Patrine did not look at von Herrnung, his eyes were less abstemious\par
with regard to her.  Under cover of their short red eyelashes, they\par
scrutinised her from time to time. There was unbridled curiosity in\par
their regard, and also a retrospective vanity, admiration, and\par
resentment as well. She rode the high horse.  She was hellishly sure of\par
herself. Sure of von Herrnung, it might be.  This passed in his mind as\par
he said to her:\par
\par
"Do you know that this car has had the honour to carry the Emperor of\par
Germany?  When _Seine Majestaet_ paid a visit to England in the year\par
1907, he used it every day."\par
\par
Patrine returned indifferently:\par
\par
"It seems to go smoothly."\par
\par
Von Herrnung said, as the car obeyed every motion of his practised hands\par
upon the steering-wheel:\par
\par
"It is a wonderful traveller.  It has been fitted with a Heinz motor,\par
three times more powerful to its weight than any other known\par
petrol-engine.  Some journeys, I can tell you, it has had with the All\par
Highest.  Travelling incognito, driven always by a--certain young\par
Prussian officer; then of Engineers--attached to the Personal Staff\par
specially for this work."\par
\par
"I daresay you mean yourself?"\par
\par
"That is a clever piece of guessing; I congratulate you, _gnaediges\par
Fraeulein_.  Well, it is now no secret.  I do not object to admit having\par
been the young _Leutnant_ in the case. So now you know how I gained my\par
_flair_ for English scenery and my violent penchant for English beauty.\par
A weakness of which I am rather proud, since it is one the Emperor\par
shares."\par
\par
The final sentence might have conveyed a jeer.  But Patrine was not\par
listening.  She called to her companion: "You are driving in the wrong\par
direction for Berkeley Square, but it does not matter.  Please put me\par
down just here at the corner of Harley Street.  I can leave this letter\par
at a house there instead of putting it in the pillar-post."\par
\par
"You are not getting out, _gnaediges Fraeulein_.  You are coming with me\par
to Hendon.  I have there a little business which will occupy an hour."\par
He added with a familiarity that stung, looking at the tense white\par
profile and the black brows knitted in anger: "You are yourself to blame\par
that I cannot part with you.  You are really as magnificent by day as by\par
evening--with your so-gloriously-coloured hair.  May I also congratulate\par
you on the effective costume?  Black and white are our Prussian colours.\par
I take that as a personal compliment."\par
\par
"Take it as you like, it will not make it one."\par
\par
"_Sehr gutig_.  I do not need telling.  When I want things I take them.\par
It is a habit of mine."\par
\par
He spoke sheer, brutal truth.  Oh God! what of Patrine's had he not\par
coveted and taken, only two horrible days ago. "So," he went on, "you\par
will have to post your letter.  I will stop at a _Postammt_ and drop it\par
in for you.  You see, I am greedy of your society.  At any moment I may\par
be recalled to Germany.  One must catch the Bird of Happiness and hold\par
it while one can.  Now tell me, is not that a pretty speech?"\par
\par
"Extremely, but it does not alter the situation.  I have a particular\par
appointment.  I cannot go to Hendon with you."\par
\par
"I have already told you that we are going there.  _Grosse Gott!_"  His\par
tone was savage.  "How is it that you are so confoundedly stubborn?  Do\par
you think such behaviour sensible--or wise?"\par
\par
"I am certainly wiser than I was two days ago."\par
\par
He slewed his head round to look at her with a greedy curiosity.  He saw\par
the lines of face and figure grow rigid, and her bare hands clench\par
themselves together in her lap.\par
\par
He glanced at her ringed hand, then transferred his regard to his own\par
left hand, the glove upon which he had retained at the Club.  The soft\par
dressed _suede_ bulged as though a bandage were concealed underneath.\par
She averted her eyes hastily as though she shunned some ugly, sickening,\par
spectacle.  He said:\par
\par
"I see that you honour me by wearing my mascot.  The magpie pearl most\par
excellently becomes your beautiful hand, my dear!"\par
\par
They had reached Regents Park Square and were turning into the Broad\par
Walk.  She plucked the ring from her bare finger, and held it out to\par
him, saying in a low tone:\par
\par
"Please take it back!"\par
\par
"I am to take it back? ... You are in earnest?"\par
\par
She repeated her words, holding out the bauble.  He released his gloved\par
left hand from the steering-wheel to take it.  His eyes were on the road\par
ahead and his face was hard as pink stone.  But she heard him give a\par
little sigh of relief as he slipped the ring into an inside coat-pocket.\par
He said, as though to excuse the sigh:\par
\par
"It was given me in April, when I made my raid on Paris from Hanover,\par
landing my _Albatros_ once only during two days' flight.  The weather\par
was magnificent.  My engine gave no trouble.  That is why I call the\par
ring my mascot, you understand.  Now that it has been worn by you, it is\par
more precious than when I first received it.  Whenever I look at it, it\par
will speak to me of you."\par
\par
"Don't let it!"\par
\par
"Why should it not speak of you?  Isis!  My heart's Queen!"\par
\par
"I have told you--don't revolt me with--piffle of that kind.  And don't\par
touch me, unless you want me to jump out of the car!"\par
\par
A voice that he barely knew had issued from the face she turned on\par
him--a face all violet shadows and haggard drawn lines, under the\par
burning splendour of the dead beech-leaf hair.  She vibrated like an\par
electrified wire, and round her pale pinched mouth and about her\par
blue-veined temples were little points of moisture, fine and glittering\par
as diamond-dust.\par
\par
"Am I to understand that my touch is unpleasant to you? That you are\par
angry with me?  That you do not love me any more?"\par
\par
"Love...."\par
\par
She laughed out harshly, hugely disconcerting him.\par
\par
"Lady Wathe said at that Grand Prix night dinner in Paris that you were\par
without a sense of humour.  But you must have a grain or so--to talk of\par
love to me!"\par
\par
She turned her face away, and the exquisite beauty of her small white\par
ear appealed to him provokingly.  He ground his teeth.  He could have\par
thrown his arm about her, and crushed the tall, full, womanly figure\par
against him.  How superb she was in her mood of hate.  The strapped-up\par
wound in his left hand was throbbing and smarting, just as when she had\par
writhed her head free from his furious kisses and bitten him to the\par
bone.\par
\par
He had made her pay richly for her bite.  He hugged himself as he\par
remembered....  Now the sting of desire was renewed in him and he eyed\par
her with greediness. Presently he stooped and said in her ear,\par
coaxingly:\par
\par
"Let us be friends!  Dine with me at the Rocroy to-night.  We will have\par
a box at the Alhambra, and sup again at the Upas.  Say you will come,\par
loved one!  Will you not, Patrine?"\par
\par
"No!"\par
\par
"No?  But I think you mean Yes!  Do you not?"\par
\par
"I have said No!  Is that not enough?"\par
\par
"You are mad!" he blustered at her--"mad as a March hare!"\par
\par
She answered him:\par
\par
"I have been mad, but I am sane now and I stay so."\par
\par
He said scoffingly:\par
\par
"You may not always remain as you are now!"\par
\par
If he launched a poisoned dart, its meaning glanced aside from her.\par
\par
"Shall you not write to me when I am back in Germany? Not one line?  Not\par
one single word?  Yet I have a few little notes from you that I\par
particularly value...."\par
\par
"Make the most of them.  I shall write no more."  And suddenly her hate\par
and loathing of him reached boiling point and ran over.  "My God!  Can't\par
you understand that I ask nothing better than never to see nor hear of\par
you again!"\par
\par
"_Grossartig_!  You are hellishly conciliatory."  His voice was thick\par
and shook with anger.  His smile mocked and the look in his eyes was\par
hateful as he pursued in a tone that was now quite gentle and purring:\par
"Just think a bit, my dear! Because--to burn one's boats behind\par
one--that is not prudent at all!"\par
\par
She did not answer, and he drove on to Hendon, planning fresh assaults\par
upon this unconquerable woman's pride.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXV*\par
\par
                          *THREE MEN IN A CAR*\par
\par
\par
When the yellow Darracq car turned in under the archway that advertised\par
Fanshaw's Flying School in three-foot capitals, the name revived no\par
associations in the mind of Patrine.  She had never visited the\par
aerodrome upon an afternoon in the mid-week, when as in the present\par
instance practice and instruction were being carried on.  The cafes, no\par
longer crowded by smart people, were thinly patronised by bronzed young\par
men in overalls, not innocent of lubricating medium, thirstily drinking\par
ginger swizzle or sucking iced-lemon squashes through yellow straws.\par
Business-looking middle-aged men discussed the market-prices of steels\par
and timbers, dope and fabrics, over bitter beer and ham-sandwiches,\par
while experimenting amateurs, male and female, discussed in loud,\par
relieved voices the experiences of the premier flight.  These, having\par
been previously warned not to experiment upon a crowded system, were now\par
ravenously putting in the solid, three-course lunches they had foregone.\par
\par
It was a perfect July day, hot and blue and green and golden.  To the\par
nor'-west, you glimpsed the elms and oaks and beeches of Boreham Wood,\par
westward the chestnuts of Bushey and Stanmore in fullest summer foliage.\par
The hawthorns of New Barnet were already browning in the sun. Hill and\par
common were plumy with the brake-fern.  Heather and ling were purpling\par
into bloom.\par
\par
Still looking westwards, you snatched a glimpse of Windsor. Eastwards, a\par
diamond set in emeralds, was the Crystal Palace at Sydenham.  Across the\par
whitish-grey scarp of Highgate and the verdant shoulder of heathy\par
Hampstead you saw the dun-coloured haze that is the breath of London,\par
the huge, black, formidable and formless monster, as, sprawling on her\par
ancient River, she keeps her envied place in the Sun.\par
\par
At the cafe end of Fanshaw's enclosure the Frogged Roumanian String\par
Orchestra were playing the "Dance Rhapsody" of Delius.  From a rival\par
establishment came the brazen strains of a German band in a\par
death-wrestle with ragtime.  Behind a straggling crowd of visitors,\par
where the cars that had brought them were parked in a double row, von\par
Herrnung stopped the yellow Darracq, leaned across Patrine's unwilling\par
knees and opened the car-door.\par
\par
As Patrine was getting out, a large hand in a white leather glove was\par
thrust forwards for her assistance.  The owner of the hand was a\par
square-faced, fair-haired, soldierly-looking servant of the somewhat\par
hybrid type that has replaced the carriage-groom.  He wore a dark blue\par
livery overcoat with silver braid upon collar, belt, and\par
shoulder-straps, black knee-boots, and a white topped cap with silver\par
braid, a shiny black peak and an enamel front badge in black, white, and\par
red.  Looking past Patrine, he saluted in military fashion and spoke to\par
von Herrnung in German, of which language Patrine possessed a\par
smattering:\par
\par
"Will the _Herr Hauptmann_ speak to the _Herrschaft_? Upon business.\par
_Er ist sehr wichtig_."\par
\par
Von Herrnung, at the first sound of the messenger's voice, had stiffened\par
to rigidity.  He glanced over his shoulder in the direction pointed out\par
by the big hand in the white glove, and answered:\par
\par
"Say to the _Herrschaft_ that I come!"\par
\par
The groom vanished.  Von Herrnung jumped out of the yellow Darracq and\par
went quickly over to the machine that had been indicated, a large,\par
superbly-finished F.I.A.T. touring-car of the landau-limousine type,\par
enamelled dark blue with a narrow silver line of finish.  The top was\par
open. A white-capped chauffeur in dark blue and silver livery sat\par
immovable at the steering-wheel, and three men, only one of whom was\par
plainly visible to Patrine, occupied the roomy body of the car.\par
\par
The visible man, sitting in the forward seat with his back to the motor,\par
his baldness topped, in deference to the weather, with a white felt\par
Newmarket, was a long-bodied, broad-shouldered personage, certainly over\par
seventy; clean-shaven, with staring eyes of light grey tinged with\par
bilious yellow, and skin of a prevailing yellow-grey doughiness, with a\par
huge wart in the middle of the cheekbone on the side next to Patrine.\par
His clothes were of yellowish-grey like his eyes and skin, his linen had\par
a yellow line in it and a huge, crumpled vest of buff nankeen threw into\par
relief a flaming crimson satin necktie confined within bounds by a flat\par
jewelled ring.  He had the air of an old actor of character parts, or of\par
a libertine monk who has foregone the cord and cowled habit.  Of the two\par
men sitting facing him little could be seen beyond the peak of a\par
gold-banded white yachting-cap pulled rather low over a bronzed and\par
rather aquiline profile with an upward-turned moustache and\par
slightly-grizzled beard of reddish-brown, and a Homburg straw with a\par
broad black ribbon and a slouched brim, overshadowing the face of the\par
man who sat on White Cap's left hand. An astute and cunning face, his;\par
long and sallow, with narrow, blinking eyes, a drooping nose, and a\par
drooping black moustache.  With this its owner played constantly,\par
twisting and pulling it with a delicate white hand that wore a diamond\par
solitaire.  He never looked up, when addressed by either of his\par
companions, but raised his eyes to the speaker, and pivoted, without\par
lifting his head.\par
\par
Von Herrnung's friends were nothing to Patrine, and von Herrnung's\par
person was by now intolerable, yet her eyes unwillingly followed the\par
tall, soldierly figure as he drew himself up, clicked his heels and\par
uncovered.  A brown hand went up to the peak of the white yachting-cap,\par
the wearers of the straw Homburg and the felt Newmarket slightly raised\par
their hats.  Von Herrnung did not speak first, he waited bareheaded to\par
be spoken to.  When the door of the big blue car was opened by the\par
servant at an imperious signal from the sallow man, von Herrnung got\par
inside, and sat down beside the personage with the wart on his\par
cheek,--leaning forwards deferentially to be addressed by the bearded\par
wearer of the white yachting-cap, who made great play with a brown right\par
hand that sported a heavy gold curb-chain watch-bracelet.  Once the hand\par
clenched and shook in vivacious threat or warning, very close to von\par
Herrnung's handsome nose.  That made Patrine laugh, and instantly she\par
was angry with herself for laughing.  She put up her long-sticked\par
sunshade, turned her back upon the blue F.I.A.T. car and moved away\par
towards the part of the enclosure where the visitors sat or promenaded,\par
drawing eyes as she went with her spangled silver headgear twinkling in\par
the sunshine, and its black cock's plume waving over her strangely\par
coloured hair.\par
\par
So changed, so changed.  She was sensible of an alteration even in her\par
gait and gestures.  A sickness of the soul weighed on her body as though\par
she walked in invisible fetters of lead.  The free space, the fresh air,\par
seemed to yield no physical stimulus.  She had bitten deep into the\par
apple of Knowledge, and found bitterness and ashes at the core.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXVI*\par
\par
                            *A PAIR OF PALS*\par
\par
\par
Among a dozen pairs of masculine eyes that followed the gallant womanly\par
figure, crowned by the plumed hat of silver spangles and displayed in\par
the frank unreticence of fashion by the semi-transparent sheath of\par
glistening white, a pair very blue, very shiningly alert and interested,\par
drew nearer until the elongated shadow of a small boy in Scout's uniform\par
mingled upon the sunlit turf with the longer shadow of Patrine.\par
\par
His thumping heart had said to him: "You know her!"  It was Pat and yet\par
not Pat.  Her tall, rounded figure.  Her walk.  The same face--and\par
another woman's hair.  The white gown and the long stole of black cock's\par
feathers he had seen before, and the hat had previously fascinated him.\par
He had asked Pat if it were not made of the twinkly stuff with which\par
they covered the Bobby-dazzlers on Christmas trees?  She had cried\par
"Yes!" and assured him that she would always hereafter call it her\par
"Bobby-dazzler chapper." ... And his Cousin Irma, whom Bawne secretly\par
abominated, had said it was too bad to talk costermonger slang to the\par
child.  "_The child_." ... A man must be ready to pardon an insult from\par
the unpunchable female.  But Bawne found himself wishing that Cousin\par
Irma had been a boy.\par
\par
He loved Pat.  You had to love a person who could keep secrets as\par
faithfully as Dad or Mother, and play tennis and hockey better than a\par
great many grown-up fellows.  Bowl you out at cricket, too, middle bail,\par
before you could wink. She could cycle all day without getting knocked\par
up, and swim a mile, easily.  For these reasons Bawne knew he loved her.\par
But he loved her most for the reasons that he did not understand.\par
\par
"Pat!"\par
\par
He had screwed up his courage to touch his crusher felt and speak the\par
name, but the tall lady with the electrifying hair did not seem to hear.\par
Her long eyes looked at him in a blind way without seeing him.  He had\par
never kissed this frozen, stranger's face.\par
\par
"I thought you knew me!  I most awfully beg your pardon!" he stammered,\par
in scarlet anguish, and the dull eyes suddenly came to life, and the\par
stiff lips smiled:\par
\par
"It's Bawne.  My sweet, I'm glad!  How did you come here?"\par
\par
"Dad brought me because he'd promised," the boy said joyously as they\par
shook hands.\par
\par
"Where is Uncle Owen?"\par
\par
"Over there."  Bawne pointed to two men talking apart beyond the\par
straggling line of spectators, and Patrine recognised the great frame\par
and scholarly stoop of the Doctor, standing with his side-face towards\par
her, a half-consumed cigar in the corner of his mouth, and his stick, a\par
weighty ivory-topped Malacca, loosely gripped in both hands behind his\par
back.\par
\par
"And the man he is talking to?  Why--of course!  It's Sir Roland--how is\par
it I didn't recognise him?"\par
\par
"The Chief Scout!"  Bawne's tone was one of incredulous wonder.  "But\par
you couldn't have forgotten _him_!  It--isn't possible!"\par
\par
Nor even to a stranger did he appear a personality to be easily\par
forgotten, the bright-eyed, falcon-beaked, middle-aged man, whose\par
feather-weight crusher felt was worn at an inimitable angle, and whose\par
slight, active figure set off his well-cut morning suit of thin blue\par
serge in a way to arouse envy in a military dandy of twenty-five.\par
\par
"You see," Bawne explained, "_he_ was talking business with Father, so I\par
just took myself out of the way."  He added: "They hadn't told me to,\par
but they might have forgotten.  And so"--the big word came out of the\par
childish mouth quaintly--"I acted on my initiative--you understand?"\par
\par
"I understand."  The formal handshake once over, their fingers had not\par
separated.  She held in her large, strong, womanly palm the hand that\par
was little, and hard, and boyish.  It squeezed her fingers, and the\par
squeeze was an apology.  It said:\par
\par
"I'd like you to have kissed me if there hadn't been lots of people\par
looking.  For, of course, you know I love you, Pat!"\par
\par
"And I love you, Bawne.  We'll always love each other, whatever\par
happens," said the answering pressure.  Her spoken utterance was:\par
\par
"So these are your holidays! ... How did you leave them all at\par
Charterhouse?  And--are you still tremendous pals with young Roddy\par
Wrynche?"\par
\par
He said, with a naive, adorable gravity:\par
\par
"Boys don't squabble like girls--and Wrynche is a frightfully decent\par
fellow.  We passed together from Shell into Under Fourth, and we've\par
promised always to stand by each other!"\par
\par
"Good egg!  And now, how is it you're here?  Has Uncle Owen given in at\par
last about the flying?"\par
\par
"Really and truly!  Man alive!"--Bawne's characteristic expletive--"I've\par
been up to-day in the air-'bus and--wasn't it first-class!"\par
\par
"Honour?"\par
\par
"Honour!  Twice round the aerodrome with the Instructor--and presently\par
I'm to have a longer flight with Mr. Sherbrand in his monoplane."\par
\par
"'Mr. Sherbrand' ..." Patrine repeated rather vaguely. "Sherbrand" had\par
somehow a ring that was familiar. Bawne explained:\par
\par
"He's a great friend of Father's.  He's splendid.  A regularly topping\par
chap!"\par
\par
"And you've actually flown?"\par
\par
"I've flewed--and I mean to go on with it."  He repeated the assurance\par
more sedately: "It's the profession I have chosen.  They say you've got\par
to begin young.  And my legs wobbled and the ground rocked a bit when I\par
got down on it. But I wasn't air-sick at all."\par
\par
"_Air-sick_....  Are people...?"\par
\par
Bawne said from the pedestal of superior knowledge:\par
\par
"Oh, aren't they just, like anything!  The Calais-Dover\par
steamer-crossing's nothing to it sometimes--the Instructor told me."\par
\par
Patrine laughed.  The latest circulating-library novel, _Love in the\par
Clouds_, had omitted to mention this fact. The heroine had donned an\par
aviator's cap and pneumatic jacket, and "leapt nimbly on board" the\par
aeroplane in half a gale of wind.  As the machine dipped and rose\par
gracefully upon the heaving element that cradled it, Enid had\par
experienced merely a delicious exhilaration.  Then a crisp moustache had\par
brushed her rosy ear.  The voice of Hubert, attuned to deepest melody of\par
passion, had murmured in the shell-like organ of hearing: "Little girl.\par
At last I have you! ... Mine, mine, my bride of the swan-path!--mine for\par
ever and aye!"\par
\par
Bawne continued, innocently discounting further statements on the part\par
of the author of _Love in the Clouds_:\par
\par
"He told me before we went up, you know.  Of course, when you're flying\par
you can't hear anything but the racket of the propeller.  It goes\par
roaring through you till your bones buzz, and the very ends of your\par
teeth hum.  So the other man has to yell at you through a trumpet, or\par
write to you on bits of paper, unless he's switched off the engine for\par
diving, and then you don't feel like talking--that's if you're a\par
beginner, you know....  But man alive! it's splendid. You must try it,\par
Pat!"\par
\par
She declared, laughingly:\par
\par
"While a single flight costs a brace of my hard-earned guineas, the\par
sport is not for me!  Why haven't I got a pal like your wonderful Mr.\par
Sherbrand?  I'm getting envious--you lucky infant, you!"\par
\par
It didn't hurt to be called an infant by Pat, because she never would\par
have done it in a stranger's hearing.  And it was ripping to have her\par
here, sharing his hour of joy.\par
\par
He told her: "Father brought me here as a reward for making a model\par
aeroplane.  Reminds me!--I've got to tell you all about that.  But it's\par
only a toy and this is the Real Thing.  There's nothing worth having in\par
the whole world," added the unconscious philosopher "unless it's real\par
and true!"\par
\par
"Am I not real?" Patrine asked, squeezing his shoulder.\par
\par
"Now you are!"  He said it with an effort of candour. "But when I saw\par
you a minute ago, I wasn't--quite sure."  He glanced up at her and asked\par
shyly: "Why are you different since you have been away in Paris?"\par
\par
"Different, how different?"  She whipped her hand from his shoulder.\par
Her black eyebrows knitted, and her face stiffened into the strange mask\par
that had puzzled him, under the scrutiny of his clear blue eyes.  "Do I\par
seem changed?" she queried.  And Bawne answered:\par
\par
"A little.  I was afraid at first you were somebody else, because\par
of"--he said it shyly--"because of your hair."\par
\par
"My hair?" she repeated blankly, and then said awkwardly: "The air of\par
Paris did that, darling, but it will soon be its old colour again!"\par
\par
"Will it ever be just like it was before?" asked Bawne, looking\par
innocently up at her, and something broke in Patrine's heart just then.\par
She gave a sudden gasping sigh, and a sudden spate of tears rolled over\par
her thick underlids, streamed down her pale cheeks, and fell upon her\par
broad bosom, heaving under its thin covering of filmy white voile.\par
\par
"Pat!  You're--crying!"  Bawne had never yet seen his friend weep, and\par
he was wrung between pity and bewilderment.  "Who has vexed you?  Who\par
has been hurting you?" he begged, and she answered brokenly:\par
\par
"No one! ... Someone....  It doesn't matter!" adding: "Would you punch\par
him, if anyone had--done as you say?"\par
\par
"_Wouldn't_ I?"\par
\par
"My sweet!"  Her arm went round his slight, square shoulders.  She doted\par
on the little amber freckles on his pure, healthy skin, the little\par
drake's tail of silky red-brown hair at the nape of his brown neck, the\par
half-shy, half-bold curve of his mouth as he smiled, the blue sparkle of\par
his eye glancing sidewise up at her.  She found in the pure warmth and\par
sweetness of the slight young body leaning against her, a healing,\par
comforting balm.\par
\par
"Why aren't you my little brother, Bawne?" she said, hugging him closer.\par
He answered after an instant's thought:\par
\par
"If my mother could be your mother too, it would be jolly!  Not unless!\par
..."\par
\par
He was not going to take on Mildred for anybody. Patrine sighed\par
pensively.\par
\par
"That's what I used to cry for when I was a little pig-tailed girl, my\par
sonny.  More than anything I wanted to belong to Aunt Lynette.  But\par
she's so young--only thirty-three.  She couldn't be my mother."\par
\par
"No."  His eyes considered her face gravely.  "Of course not.  You're\par
far too old.  How old are you, Cousin Pat?"\par
\par
"How old am I?"  A shudder went through her.  "Nineteen in August.  And\par
I feel about a hundred and one."\par
\par
"That's 'cos you're not well!"  His eyes were anxious and a little\par
pucker showed between his reddish eyebrows. "You're not going to be\par
ill--are you?" he asked in alarm.\par
\par
"Not I!"  She murmured it caressingly in her deep, soft voice.  "My pet,\par
don't worry.  Everything's all right with me!--perfectly all right and\par
O.K.!  Only talk to me. Don't let me keep on thinking.  Things are never\par
so--bally rotten if you can stop brooding over them."\par
\par
Why did she look like that?  What had somebody done to hurt her?  His\par
boyish hand clenched, the thumb well turned in over the knuckles.\par
Instinctively Bawne knew that the Enemy, who had stamped that dreadful\par
look of frozen misery on the face of his beloved, white as ivory or old\par
snow in its strange setting of flaming tresses--was of his own sex.\par
\par
\par
All the while, ever since Patrine had entered the gates of Panshaw's,\par
the song of the air-screw had not been absent from her ears.  The\par
tractor of the practice-engine roared fitfully, like a tiger being\par
prodded in its den by a spiteful keeper's meat-fork.  The propellers of\par
the double-engined passenger-buses kept up a steady droning as Fanshaw's\par
pilots followed the pointing arms of the red, white, and blue pylons\par
marking the limits of the air-circuit, or were silent as the machines\par
dropped to earth within the huge white circles where a giant T indicated\par
"Land."\par
\par
This was not a show day when visitors' half-crowns rattled unceasingly\par
into the boxes at the turnstile.  The rows of green-painted chairs\par
behind the whitewashed iron railings of the spectators' enclosure were\par
but thinly patronised by friends of people taking passenger-flights.  No\par
man with a megaphone announced events forthcoming or imminent.  No white\par
flag fell for the start, no pistol cracked signifying the conclusion of\par
a race.\par
\par
Three men occupied the Judge's stand behind the Committee enclosure.\par
One, small and dapper, in a frock-coat and topper, kept his eye on what\par
was probably a stop-watch.  Another, stout, bearded, and straw-hatted,\par
was absorbedly gazing at the sky through a big pair of Zeiss binoculars.\par
The third, in the uniform of a commissionaire, was an employe of the\par
School.  No one manifested any particular interest in them or their\par
occupation.  The sparse general public were not enlightened as to the\par
reason of their presence on the Judge's stand.\par
\par
"Talk," Patrine said, clinging to Bawne, her slender plank in moral\par
shipwreck.  "Tell me what Sir Roland and the Doctor are waiting to see.\par
What is that thin man doing with the stop-watch and the note-book?  And\par
the fat gentleman beside him, who never leaves off staring at the sky\par
through those big field-glasses.  Nothing is billed to happen--there are\par
no numbers up on the pylons--yet something seems to be going on!"\par
\par
"Rather!"\par
\par
The boy broke into a little gurgle of excited laughter, and began to\par
dance up and down under the arm that rested on his neck.\par
\par
"_Rather_!  Didn't you know?  How funny!  Why, man alive, we're waiting\par
for _him_!"\par
\par
"For him?"\par
\par
"For Mr. Sherbrand.  Father's friend.  The Flying Man I've told you\par
about."\par
\par
"Mr.----  Where is he?" Patrine asked vaguely, looking all about her.\par
In the tumult of her thoughts the name that had been upon a crumpled\par
card suggested no association with that so rapturously uttered by the\par
boy.\par
\par
"There!"  Bawne pointed upwards with another of the excited laughs.\par
"Carrying out a hovering-test.  The man with the stop-watch is timing\par
him, and the other with the binnocs is observing him.  He's French--no\par
end of an official swell!  The French Government sent him," went on the\par
boy, with infinite relish, "to see Mr. Sherbrand test his invention.  He\par
thought they didn't catch on, but the hoverer has fetched them.  If he\par
hovers for twenty minutes, ten thousand feet up, his fortune's made!--I\par
heard a fellow say so to the Instructor.  Man alive! isn't it topping\par
that you and I should be here to-day!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXVII*\par
\par
                       *SIR ROLAND TELLS A STORY*\par
\par
\par
While yet the Bird of War hovered invisible at ten thousand feet of\par
altitude, and the lungs of the men aboard her toiled and laboured, and\par
foam gathered about their nostrils and lips, Saxham stood talking with\par
the man who in his eyes ranked above all others, the tried and trusty\par
friend of fourteen years.\par
\par
In those unforgettable months of the Siege of Gueldersdorp you might\par
have noticed a crow's foot or so at the corner of the Chief's keen\par
falcon-eyes.  To-day, their hazel brightness undiminished, they looked\par
at Life from a network of fine criss-crossed lines.  But Time, the\par
spider, had spun no web in the fine alert brain, and the man's heart was\par
free from crow's feet or wrinkles.  Fresh and evergreen, it was as it\par
would always be, an oasis of kindness for the downhearted or weary,\par
watered by the twin wells of sympathy and enthusiasm.  He said, speaking\par
to Saxham of the invisible Sherbrand:\par
\par
"I wish we had a million like him!"\par
\par
Saxham answered:\par
\par
"I wish we had several millions.  He is a fine, energetic type.  A bit\par
of a hero-worshipper--a bit of a philosopher, a bit of a stoic: '_He\par
hath seen men rise to authority without envy, and schooled himself to\par
endure adversity, that he might bear himself the better when his time\par
should come to rule._'"\par
\par
"His time is coming, or I am no judge of capability.  And you quoted\par
from the _Encheiridion_ of Epictetus, I think? I've always found good\par
reading-meat in that and the _Discourses_.  Used to carry a little\par
sixpenny copy about in my pocket, until I wore it to rags."\par
\par
"I have often seen and noted its raggedness, and its uncompromising\par
Isabella-hue!"\par
\par
"It was negroid in complexion before the Relief of Gueldersdorp.\par
Perhaps you won't be astonished when I tell you I have got it now."  The\par
Chief's smiling eyes narrowed in laughter.  "My wife has bound it\par
gorgeously, and with other volumes of my Siege library, it occupies a\par
special and most sacred shelf near my writing-desk at home."\par
\par
He went on:\par
\par
"This fine fellow Sherbrand is an old correspondent of mine.  He would\par
say I might tell you the story, and I think it's worth hearing, in a\par
way.  It must be eight years ago, when he would be about seventeen, that\par
he wrote to me from an engineering college at Newcastle, to say he had\par
read some papers of mine on the subject of scouting, and proposed--if I\par
thought it would not be presumption on his part--save the mark!--to\par
enrol and organise a troop on the lines laid down.  He wanted a definite\par
code of Scout law to work on, and Rules and so forth, all of which I\par
supplied him, you may be sure.  Busy as I was drilling and cub-licking\par
the North British Territorials, I couldn't find time to spare to run up\par
and see the boy.  So he commandeered a holiday and motor-cycled to\par
Headquarters.  Rode all through one night in pelters of rain, and caught\par
me in my 5 A.M. tub."\par
\par
"He meant business."\par
\par
"Business--and it was up to me to encourage such grittiness and\par
enthusiasm.  So I ordered coffee for two, and bacon and eggs for half a\par
dozen, and when I had fed him I talked.  My book wasn't published, but I\par
lent him some proof-sheets.  He thanked me, but before he took them he\par
had to disburden his mind."\par
\par
The fine sunburnt hand went thoughtfully to the grizzled moustache, worn\par
rather longer than of old.\par
\par
"He had got something on his mind.  He had been reading that old\par
bogey-book, _Hales on Mental Heredity_, and the theory of the\par
transmission of base or criminal tendencies from the parents to the\par
children, had haunted him night and day.  He said to me, standing up\par
before me, looking about as long and thin as a fathom of pump-water: 'My\par
father was dismissed from the Army in War-time, for not backing up his\par
C.O.  Is there a kink in _my_ brain or a bacillus lying waiting in _my_\par
body that will one day make a slacker of me?"\par
\par
Saxham's square face was ashen, but the Chief's eyes were elsewhere.\par
\par
"And you told him----?"\par
\par
"I told him, that whilst physical disease and deformity are\par
transmissible from the unhealthy parents to their unlucky offspring, no\par
sensible Christian regarded the theory of inherited vice or crime, as\par
anything but the most pernicious lie that the devil ever invented to\par
spread as a net for the catching of bodies and souls.  That seemed to\par
buck him up!"\par
\par
"I do not doubt it!" said Saxham.  He breathed more freely, and his face\par
had regained its natural hue.\par
\par
"I reminded him," went on the Chief, "that our Army and our Fleet are\par
indebted for thousands of the finest men, morally and physically\par
speaking, to Reformatories and Industrial Schools and Homes.  'Think of\par
the character borne by Barnardo's boys,' I told him, 'and grind the\par
scorpion lie to pulp under your boot-heel, whenever and wherever you\par
find it cocking up its damnable tail!'"\par
\par
"So he went back," said Saxham, "cheered and strengthened by your\par
sympathy, as--other men have been before now!"\par
\par
"So he went back to the College, fortified by my bit of nervous English,\par
and worked at his troop for all he was worth.  Raked in seventy in the\par
first month, and kept on raking.  He is dandy at drill and organisation,\par
is Sherbrand.  When he left the College they mustered three hundred\par
strong."  The speaker struck his stick upon the turf and said\par
emphatically: "How it grows!--how the Movement spreads and gathers and\par
ramifies!  Do you know Saxham, that there are moments in my life when I\par
am tempted to be proud.  When rank upon rank of young, fresh, fearless\par
faces with bright eyes are turned to me. When thousands of active,\par
lithe, healthy young bodies run out into the open and rally about the\par
Chief Scout."\par
\par
There was a mist in the bright eyes that his manliness was not ashamed\par
of.\par
\par
"Years ago, when the officer in command of a certain beleaguered\par
garrison was doing his best to defend it, a great Fear came upon him in\par
the watches of a particularly anxious night.  Perhaps you will guess\par
what I mean, Saxham? The man had not slept for more weeks than I like to\par
remember, and the hours of rest in the day-time were hot-eyed staring\par
horrors of insomnia.  He was--up against it!  The shrunken lids would\par
not shut down over his bursting eyeballs, and his jaws were clamped so\par
that he could not yawn. Then, on this night, his Fear rose up and mopped\par
and mowed at him, and it had the kind of face that madhouse doctors and\par
keepers know.  He wasn't ordinarily a 'fearful man,' like Kipling's\par
immortal Bengali, but now he was horribly, sickeningly afraid!"\par
\par
"I guessed it," said Saxham.  "I realised what you were suffering, but I\par
did not dare to hint my knowledge to you. There was no danger of\par
madness.  But you were certainly on the verge of mental and physical\par
breakdown."\par
\par
"And being in such desperate case," said the other, "I prayed to God in\par
my extremity.  I promised Him if He would help me to carry out my duty\par
to Him, as to my earthly superiors, and those men and women and children\par
who looked to me as their protector and guide, that I would one day, if\par
He spared me, build Him a big Temple, made of the little temples that\par
are the work of His Hands."\par
\par
"And to-day the Temple is a reality!"\par
\par
"A grand reality.  East, West, North, and South, it spreads and widens\par
and towers.  It is built of boys. Clean-limbed, clean-minded,\par
self-respecting fellows, scorning vices, eager for service, sensitive in\par
Honour, chivalrous, patriotic, keen for Truth and Right.  It frightens\par
me, Saxham, when I think what a leaven is working through these boys of\par
mine, potential fathers of sons in the ripeness of Time, for the\par
ultimate regeneration of this vicious, degenerate world.  It makes me\par
understand how near old Coleridge got to the live heart of things when\par
he wrote the _Ancient Mariner_.  The service of Almighty God is the love\par
of your fellow-man.  But why to me, and not to another worthier, should\par
God have given this wonderful, glorious thing to bring about? ..."\par
\par
"Because you are worthy of doing His work for Him. Has He not used you\par
as His instrument in my own case?  Should I not own to this, I who owe\par
everything to you?"\par
\par
The other laid a hand on the great shoulder of the Dop Doctor.\par
\par
"If ever you owed me anything, Saxham, you paid me long ago!"\par
\par
He was silent a moment and said in a lighter tone: "I am not quite clear\par
yet as to how you met my whilom Scoutmaster."\par
\par
"Our acquaintance dates from a cross-Channel flight he made in the end\par
of June."\par
\par
"I know."  The Chief prodded the turf with his walking-stick. "A French\par
pilot-officer of their Service Aeronautique, a certain Commandant\par
Raymond, who flew here in the contest for the Ivor International Cup in\par
May, had heard of Sherbrand and his inventions from Lamond of the\par
Central Flying School.  He took a shine to the aerial stabiliser and got\par
his Chiefs to give it a trial.  That came off on the Grand Prix Sunday,\par
when Paris went wild over the Sarajevo affair."\par
\par
"And scenting War in the air," said Saxham, "Sherbrand promptly took\par
wing for England without waiting for the decision of the judges who were\par
present at the test."\par
\par
"Did he?  He has keen scent."\par
\par
"Better now," said Saxham laughing, "than when he came to me--on the\par
recommendation of an old patient--suffering from an aggravated form of\par
nasal catarrh.  He had had it at intervals for years, and suspected it\par
to be owing to what he described, in the language of the\par
engineering-shop, as "a defect in the air-intake."  He proved to be\par
right--and I sent him into the Hospital, where Berry Boyle performed a\par
slight minor operation which removed the trouble, and left him capitally\par
fit.  Then, when he came out of the Hospital, he found a letter from the\par
French Consul waiting at his office----"\par
\par
The Chief interpolated:\par
\par
"Ah yes.  The aerial stabiliser had gained the suffrages of Messieurs\par
the Chiefs of the Aeronautique Francaise.  I hope M. Jourdain's report\par
to his Government will induce them to buy the patent.  For, judging by\par
the interest that the representatives of another Power seem to take\par
in----"\par
\par
The Chief broke off.  The smiling lines about his eyes and mouth had\par
vanished as he queried: "Who is the lady my Scout over there is\par
squiring?  A superbly-shaped young woman, with hair of the fashionable\par
terra-cotta shade.  But for the hair, I should have said it was your\par
niece, Patrine."\par
\par
Saxham's eyes followed the direction of the Chief's glance. He said, and\par
his face looked hard as a mask of stone:\par
\par
"Your memory for faces is correct as usual.  The lady with the\par
terra-cotta hair is my late brother's daughter, Patrine."\par
\par
The Chief's familiar whistle filled in a space of silence, with a\par
pensive little fragment of Delius' _Spring Song_, while Saxham's frown\par
grew deeper and his jaw thrust out more angrily.  Then the well-known\par
voice said:\par
\par
"I am sorry that Miss Patrine has been tempted to follow the fashion.\par
But I regret still more her choice of friends! I refer to the German\par
officer in whose company your niece arrived here, in a yellow Darracq\par
car, about half-an-hour ago."  The speaker made sure, with a rapid\par
glance to right and left, that no listener was standing near them, and\par
added: "I know that I may trust you as myself in any private or official\par
matter.  Between ourselves frankly, I am here to-day for the purpose\par
of--keeping an eye on this particular man!"\par
\par
The Doctor's vivid blue eyes darted rapier-points at the other, from\par
caves that had suddenly been dug about them. The General went on:\par
\par
"The man himself is no common spy, though he may on occasion act as an\par
agent or post-box for Secret Intelligence communications.  He is an\par
extraordinarily able young officer, a squadron captain in their Field\par
Flying Service, with some astonishing records to his credit, though he\par
was an Engineer Lieutenant in 1907 when he came to England as\par
chauffeur-officer attached to the Kaiser's Personal Staff. For a\par
comprehensible reason his superiors desired him to improve his knowledge\par
of the topography of the British Isles.  He certainly did so, but"--the\par
keen eyes twinkled--"the record runs accomplished by von Herrnung with\par
the All Highest as passenger, were not unattended, or unobserved by us.\par
That he is well-born and well-looking is undeniable, and these\par
advantages, with other social gifts, may easily attract your niece, like\par
any other of Eve's daughters. But to say the least it is inadvisable\par
that she should encourage the advances of this man, or of any other\par
German officer,--when the next forty-eight hours may see Britain and\par
Germany at grips in War."\par
\par
"That is your opinion?"\par
\par
"It is my plain, unvarnished opinion, speaking as one of those who are\par
admitted behind the scenes.  Not that I am infallible, but the Signs and\par
the Tokens all lead one way."  He lifted his lean brown hand and pointed\par
eastwards.  "For years they have been making ready, but now--what a\par
frenzy of ordered preparation.  What secret councils, what reiteration\par
of orders, what accumulations of stores, what roaring of electric\par
furnaces--I'd give my little finger to know what chemical they're making\par
in huge bulk at the Badische Anilin-und-Soda Fabrik, and hundreds of\par
other dye and bleaching-powder works in Germany and Austria!--every one\par
backed up by the German Imperial State or the Dual Monarchy on the\par
understanding that at the signal, they are to turn to and turn\par
out--what?  Benzine for phenol, phenol for picric, and toluene for\par
Super-Explosive, that's understood.  But this stuff puzzles me.  Do you\par
see the Senile Arc in my eyes yet, Saxham?  It must be that I'm getting\par
old!"\par
\par
He smiled his whimsical smile and went on:\par
\par
"A day or two after the burial of the Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his\par
morganatic partner, murdered by some fanatics among the Greater Serbs, a\par
huge majority among the German military and naval officers doing duty in\par
their Colonies, or on political service in Africa, were recalled by\par
Wireless.  Leave has been stopped.  Rolling-stock in inconceivable\par
masses is being concentrated on the greater strategic railways, while\par
the official and semi-official Press prates and gabbles of peace and\par
neighbourly goodwill!"  He shrugged.  "Things were safer when they\par
yelled and foamed in convulsions of Anglophobia.  Then one doubted....\par
Now one is sure! ... Ah, I thought I wasn't mistaken. Here's Sherbrand\par
coming down!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXVIII*\par
\par
                       *THE GOD FROM THE MACHINE*\par
\par
\par
The dazzling turquoise of the sky was now streaked with milky bands of\par
haziness.  Dappled golden-white cloudlets at the zenith made what is\par
known as mackerel sky.  Trails of rounded stratus floated low in the\par
east and south-east. Long shadows of hangars, pylons, semaphore and\par
electric searchlight-stations, stretched over the turf from westwards,\par
crossed by perambulatory shadows of people moving about. These became\par
stationary, betokening that popular interest was newly awakened.\par
Umbrellas and sticks flourished towards the sky.  Bawne gave a little\par
crow of delight as the whitish-brown, shining shape of the monoplane\par
dived down out of the empyrean, travelling with a bold, beautiful\par
swooping glide that took away the gazer's breath.\par
\par
"Look, oh look!" Bawne gasped, leaning against Patrine. Now her tardy\par
interest was genuinely awakened.  Reaching the end of its glide, the\par
monoplane had regained the horizontal position.  As she flattened out\par
and hung well-nigh motionless in mid-air with the sunlight beating on\par
and drenching her fragile, lacy structure, she was a thing of beauty and\par
of wonder.  The humming of her tractor came to you mingled with the\par
buzzing of her gyroscopic hoverer, like the incessant vibration of\par
living, sentient wings.\par
\par
She seemed to tire of hanging between earth and heaven, ceased buzzing,\par
tilted a wing sharply and began to frolic after it as a kitten runs\par
after its tail on a hearthrug, or as a swallow gambols on a chase after\par
gnats, always turning towards the West, whilst her greyish shadow danced\par
and skipped upon the gold-white cloud-surfaces to eastwards a long way\par
below her, like the ghost of an aeroplane.  All the time she was\par
gobbling up distance, steadily descending. She presently reversed her\par
sun-worshipping tactics, dived, and spiralled, banking, from west to\par
east.  You saw plainly the helmeted heads and slightly hunched shoulders\par
of the pilot at the controls and the mechanic strapped in the forward\par
cockpit.\par
\par
Soon she hovered again and swooped, so suddenly that Patrine nipped\par
Bawne's shoulder, saying "Great Scott!" under her breath.  Another long\par
sweeping glide brought her close to the green turf of the aerodrome.\par
Then, with an adroit flexing of the wing-tips, she balanced, flattened,\par
and landed lightly within the huge white circle, rocking a little on her\par
tyred wheels.\par
\par
The man with the stop-watch checked the mechanism, the bearded man with\par
the big binoculars lowered and closed them, scribbled in a\par
memorandum-book and came down the judge's stand.  The Bird of War's\par
mechanic stayed in his place, the pilot unhitched his body-strap, pushed\par
up his goggled visor, threw a long leg over the fuselage and jumped\par
lightly to the ground.  He staggered as he reached it, recovered himself\par
and stood breathing quickly, as a man overcoming giddiness, or other\par
physical sensation of distress.\par
\par
Tall, young, and lightly built, with long active limbs and a physique\par
suggestive of youth and courage and enterprise, as he stood motionless,\par
his eared and goggled cap now in hand, the play of sunlight upon his\par
thin brown waterproof gabardine and overalls made him look like a statue\par
of Mercury wrought in pale new bronze.  And with a lifting of her sick\par
heart as though it had suddenly spread wings, and soared into a region\par
of unlimited space and glorious freedom, Patrine recognised her Flying\par
Man of the Jardin des Milles Plaisirs.\par
\par
\par
From what airt, of what world unknown, did it blow, that cool, fragrant\par
wind that then and always heralded for Patrine his coming?  It took her\par
by surprise; lapped her delicately about; enveloped her from head to\par
foot in its pure invisible waves.  The hot, sore places in her heart\par
were bathed and healed, the deep caverns filled, the little thirsty\par
rock-pools set awash and brimming.  When the sough of it was no longer\par
in her ears or the tug and flutter of it among the folds of her\par
garments; when she ceased to be conscious of the cool resilient pressure\par
upon cheek and neck and forehead--her brief sweet hour of joy was over.\par
Sherbrand had gone away again.\par
\par
\par
"_Cela ira-t-il, monsieur?  Je suis pret a faire une nouvelle\par
demonstration si vous n'etes pas satisfait._"\par
\par
His clear, strong voice speaking in laborious public school French gave\par
her a delicious shock of pleasure.  He was addressing the stout, bearded\par
Frenchman who, accompanied by the thinner man who had timed Sherbrand by\par
the stop-watch, now walked across the turf to shake the aviator's hand.\par
As Sherbrand spoke, he drew a white handkerchief from the sleeve of his\par
gabardine and wiped from the corners of his mouth some little blobs of\par
foam, slightly bloodstained.  The stout, friendly Frenchman glanced at\par
him, and uttered an exclamation.  Sherbrand shook his head in vigorous\par
protest and laughed, repeating his offer to demonstrate again.  Upon\par
which the bearded man, who had also a moustache with thick, stiff waxed\par
ends, and wore a large checked-pattern summer suit with a white drill\par
waistcoat, a low collar and a necktie with flowing ends, and was topped\par
with a high-crowned straw hat that suggested Trouville in mid-season,\par
negatived the proposal with a vivacious gesture, pouring forth a stream\par
of words:\par
\par
"_Au contraire, Monsieur, je suis convaincu que vous avez une idee\par
superbe.  Nous vous avons observe avec la lunette Zeiss, pendant votre\par
vol, et nous avons constate le temps tres soigneusement: vous avez\par
maintenu le bruit et la stabilite pendant cinq minutes de plus que les\par
vingt-cinq minutes stipulees.  Permettez moi comme une simple formalite\par
de voir votre altimetre?_"\par
\par
"By all means!" Sherbrand returned.\par
\par
They went back to the aeroplane together, and Sherbrand reached over and\par
unhooked the altimeter from a board in the pilot's cockpit, and the\par
bearded man examined it, and then cordially shook hands.\par
\par
"Within two days, at latest.  Possibly sooner!" Patrine heard the\par
straw-hatted, bearded gentleman say in English, pronounced with a strong\par
French accent.  "Believe me," he added, "I shall represent most strongly\par
the usefulness of your invention to the Chief of the Etat de l'Aviation.\par
_Au revoir, Monsieur, et encore mes felicitations!_"\par
\par
Then he went away with the small dark man who had used the stop-watch,\par
and who carried the Zeiss binoculars slung in their case.\par
\par
During this business interview Patrine had felt Bawne panting and\par
wriggling close beside her, like an excited, but well-mannered\par
fox-terrier waiting to be whistled for.  But Sherbrand, though he\par
glanced at the boy smilingly, had turned aside to engage in conversation\par
with Saxham, and the Chief Scout, whom Sherbrand saluted in orthodox\par
Scout fashion, flushing red under the clear sunburn that darkened his\par
fair skin.\par
\par
"He's one of Us!" Bawne whispered to Patrine, his own young face alight\par
with pleasure.  "He was Scoutmaster of a troop in the North, he told me,\par
and I know he must have been a splendid one.  He's the kind of chap\par
who'd be prepared for anything.  Don't you think he looks like that?"\par
\par
Patrine did not answer.  She was feeling "cheap," as Lady Beauvayse\par
would have expressed it.  She had put the man out of her thoughts\par
because she had taken it for granted that Fanshaw's instructor could not\par
be a gentleman.  Now, as she watched Sherbrand in conversation with the\par
elder man, his manner of quiet, well-bred deference, mingled with a\par
pleasant courtesy, showed her beyond all doubt that his place was above\par
the salt.\par
\par
He had looked towards her, when he had smiled at Bawne, and his glance\par
had swept over her without recognition.  She would have known him\par
anywhere, while he----!  She had forgotten her preposterously-coloured\par
hair.\par
\par
How sweet the breeze was, bringing from the west the smell of\par
strawberry-fields and red and white clover.  Yet she had not noticed it\par
until now.  Her mood had changed. She had put away the thing she most\par
hated to remember. She felt almost like the Patrine of two days ago.\par
\par
"Miss Saxham!"\par
\par
It was von Herrnung's voice speaking behind her, and with a shock of\par
loathing horror she remembered all.  She turned to see his tall figure\par
approaching.  The first impression was that he was ill; the next, that\par
he was furiously angry.  His florid complexion had bleached to an ugly,\par
greenish pallor, even the blue of his eyes had faded to a curious lilac\par
hue.  He carried in his gloved left hand, and with evident care, a flat\par
strapped wallet of brown leather, fastened with two Bramah locks and a\par
lock-strap.  He said to Patrine in a jarring voice of resentment and\par
impatience:\par
\par
"I have been looking for you.  Could one not leave you for a minute but\par
you must go off by yourself?  _Sapperlot_! Whom has one here?  Where did\par
you pick up the boy?"\par
\par
Her heart swelled as Bawne looked up at her in astonishment, then\par
transferred his stare to von Herrnung, puckering his brows in\par
disapproval of the rude, strange man.  She answered as calmly as was\par
possible:\par
\par
"This is my cousin, Bawne Saxham, Count von Herrnung."\par
\par
"Why did you leave me?" von Herrnung grumbled as Bawne stiffly saluted,\par
and she told him:\par
\par
"Because I saw you occupied in conversation with your German friends."\par
\par
"Women are incomprehensible creatures! ... How do you know that they\par
were German?  At any rate, whether they were or not, they have gone away\par
now!  You find me annoyed.  It is because they are--what shall I call\par
it?--perhaps a little exigent.  Now I will have a smoke.  I suppose you\par
do not mind?"\par
\par
He had not freed his hand from the brown leather satchel to remove his\par
hat when he had mopped his perspiring forehead, with a big cambric\par
handkerchief scented with the _tres persistent_ perfume that always\par
clung about his clothes. Nor did he relinquish it to help himself to a\par
cigar, but opened the gold case containing the weeds with the hand that\par
drew it from his pocket, extracted a cigar with his teeth, and returned\par
the case to his pocket; then produced a matchbox, opened it in the same\par
way, picked out a match, shut the box, and struck the match upon it,\par
saying to Bawne, as he blew out the first mouthful of smoke: "What do\par
you think of that, my fine fellow?  Should I not make a famous\par
one-handed man?"  But Bawne's suffrages remained unwon, although the\par
dexterity of the thing had secretly pleased him. He remained doggedly\par
silent, scowling with his reddish-fair brows, thrusting out his chin.\par
\par
"Should I not?  Tell me!" von Herrnung persisted. "Or is it that British\par
boys are cowards and afraid to answer when they are spoken to?"\par
\par
"I am not afraid--of anything or anybody!"\par
\par
Bawne reddened and looked the taunting big man between the eyes,\par
squarely.  The look added--_And least of all of anybody like you_!  He\par
went on:\par
\par
"But I think it takes more than--that kind of being clever--to make a\par
famous man."\par
\par
"_Nicht so schlimm!_" Von Herrnung nodded.  "But all the same these\par
little tricks are worth knowing.  You might be bound with ropes to a\par
post, or tree, or waggon by the enemy, and if he happened to have left\par
your matches on you--and you could get one hand free--there is no knot\par
man could tie that I could not wriggle myself out of!--you might burn\par
the rope and get away!  I did that once when I was a gunner-boy of\par
seventeen in South Africa----"\par
\par
Von Herrnung stopped short.  Bawne asked simply, and with the same\par
straight look between the eyes:\par
\par
"Did you fight with the Boers against us in the War?"\par
\par
Von Herrnung did not seem to have heard.  He had caught the drift of a\par
sentence spoken by Sherbrand, who was answering to a question of the\par
Chief Scout.\par
\par
"Oh yes!  I live here--have quarters over Mrs. Dunlett's restaurant--you\par
could communicate with me practically at any time.  We've the 'phone and\par
a private telegraph-office, and the wireless--under the usual licence\par
from the Postmaster-General."\par
\par
He pointed towards the well-known tall posts with the short\par
cross-pieces, china insulators and lines of thick wire, standing beside\par
the telegraph-cabin, over the roof of which straddled the wireless\par
installation's tall, latticed steel mast.\par
\par
"We find it useful for business as well as instructional purposes," he\par
went on.  "Macrombie--the man in charge--is a one-time Royal Navy Petty\par
Officer Telegraphist and a first-class operator."  Sherbrand's mouth\par
twisted a little at the corners as he added: "About twenty-four days out\par
of thirty, let us say!"\par
\par
The quick rejoinder came:\par
\par
"Then he's D.O.D. two working days in the month, not counting Sundays.\par
I've met plenty of Macrombies in my time.  This doesn't happen to be the\par
monthly pay-day, by any chance, or one of the other close days in its\par
neighbourhood?"\par
\par
"No, no!  He's as right as rain and as sober as a seal," said Sherbrand.\par
"And--this tall fellow with red hair must be the man who has written to\par
me upon business.  I shall have to go to him."\par
\par
They exchanged a left-handed grip, mutually smiling.\par
\par
"Good old habits stick," said the Chief, and Sherbrand answered:\par
\par
"Fortunately, they do.  Let me say again how much and how gratefully I\par
have to thank you for the teaching that has helped me to find myself!"\par
His clear light glance reverted to Saxham.  "The Doctor too, for giving\par
me this chance of meeting you.  Please tell him the story if you think\par
it would interest him.  I hope with all my heart, sir, that you will\par
soon come here again!"\par
\par
"I had already taken the permission for granted," the Chief said, as\par
Sherbrand saluted and went forward to meet "the fellow with red hair."\par
"There is big business in that gyroscopic stabiliser of his," he added\par
to Saxham, "and our friends at the French War Ministry have tumbled to\par
it as one might naturally expect.  So much the worse for our bungling\par
bigwigs at Whitehall, who've let a good thing slip, for the millionth\par
time, out of their claws.  But taking for granted the value of the\par
patent, and recognising the likelihood of the French bid stimulating\par
Teutonic competition----"\par
\par
The gentle, modulated voice broke off.  Von Herrnung had stepped out\par
upon the green and was striding towards the lightly-moving, less\par
stiffly-carried figure of Sherbrand, the approximation of the two\par
somehow suggesting a salute of gladiators previous to the fight.  Now\par
the big, grey-clad German was arrested in the middle of his stride by\par
the sudden kling-a-ling of a motor-gong, a sharp crystal vibration that\par
stiffened him to attention, and pricked his ears for a repetition of the\par
sound.\par
\par
It did not immediately come.  He raised the left hand that held the\par
leather satchel, and swung it from front to rear, so that it was for a\par
moment clear of the outline of his body, as who should signal: "_I have\par
it safe!_"  Quick, watchful eyes noted this.  Took in also the ornate\par
bulk of the dark blue F.I.A.T. touring-car, as with the characteristic,\par
noiseless smoothness of its make, it glided between the ranks of parked\par
and waiting automobiles, and stopped in the open, perhaps some forty\par
yards away.\par
\par
A fat yellow hand, with a twinkling solitaire upon it, waved.  A brown\par
hand, with a massive gold curb-chain watch-bracelet on the wrist of it,\par
beckoned imperiously. Something had been forgotten, something was still\par
to say. Von Herrnung wheeled, and went back in his traces as obediently\par
as the pointer that has been called to heel.  He did not uncover,\par
perhaps he had been told not to.  He saluted, and stood stiffly,\par
listening to a harsh German voice that yapped at him.  All his arrogance\par
and swagger seemed to have been juggled out of him by the gestures of\par
the brown hand with the flashing wrist-bracelet, emerging from the white\par
cuff with jewelled sleeve-links and the snowy sleeve with its broad\par
bands of glittering golden braid.\par
\par
"_S'th!_"\par
\par
The slight sound pulled Saxham's head round.  He had not been looking at\par
the occupants of the blue F.I.A.T. His eyes were intent on the tall\par
white figure of the woman standing beside his boy.  Her black lace\par
sunshade was closed.  She held the tall-sticked thing at arm's-length,\par
leaning upon it, and the westering light smote a myriad of\par
multi-coloured sparkles out of the tinsel spangles of the hat with the\par
single black cock's plume.  The queer headgear crowning her barbaric\par
hair, and her full white oval face with its wide, low, arched black\par
brows and long eyes, made her seem strange, alluring, as some\par
tall-stemmed, exotic flower, sprung at the incantation of an Oriental\par
conjuror, from a green stretch of English turf.\par
\par
In the same instant von Herrnung touched his hat, stepped back from the\par
blue car, wheeled and walked away toward the waiting figure of\par
Sherbrand, the sallow man in the Homburg hat gave an order, the\par
chauffeur touched the electric starter, and the F.I.A.T. turned and\par
smoothly bowled away.  But in the instant of its turning, the bearded\par
man in the white naval uniform rose in his place, and obtruding half his\par
short, bulky body across the lean person of his sallow neighbour,\par
scrutinised the face and figure of Patrine Saxham with a cool,\par
appraising deliberateness that tortured the wincing flesh it enveloped\par
like the cut of a carriage-whip.\par
\par
They were full, bright, and rather handsome grey-blue eyes shadowed by\par
the white cap-peak, and they had the indefinable expression of authority\par
and power.  Their glance said--and the face with the perfectly-trimmed\par
beard and the upturned moustache wore a curious smile that bore out the\par
glance's meaning:\par
\par
"So!  That's the woman!"  And a surge of scalding shame and bitter\par
resentment rose in the heart of Patrine Saxham and filled it to the\par
brim.\par
\par
She could not have explained why she felt certain that her shameful\par
secret was known to the man with the powerful eyes, the cast of whose\par
face with its pointed beard faintly reminded one of the King and the\par
Tsar.\par
\par
Patrine had always abominated cheap sentiment.  She had once laughed\par
until she cried at a revival of an old four-decker drama, whose hero,\par
waking to the knowledge of a committed, irrevocable deed cried in\par
throaty, stagy tones of anguish upon God to put back the dreadful clock\par
of Time and give him yesterday.\par
\par
Now she perceived the deep, vital interest of the common-place human\par
story.  If asking Him on whom that other sinner cried would wipe from\par
Time's register a span of hours between twelve P.M. and three o'clock in\par
the morning--blot one deed from the Roll of things that done, are beyond\par
Humanity's undoing--Patrine told herself that it would be worth while to\par
wear sackcloth, live on boiled field-peas, drink brook-water, and\par
pray--until her knees were worn to the bone.\par
\par
She caught Saxham's piercing glance across the intervening strip of\par
greensward.  He turned away his eyes, and a shudder went through her\par
frame.  Had he suspected--could anyone have found out and told him?  The\par
Doctor's head was bent now as the General talked to him.  It seemed to\par
her that a muscle in his lean cheek twitched, a characteristic sign with\par
him of excitement, or emotion.  She wondered what the General had said\par
to Uncle Owen to make him look like that.\par
\par
As a fact, the quiet voice was saying in Saxham's ear: "And prepare\par
against a surprise, Doctor--for though your nerves are tough as\par
aluminium bronze, a few million gallons of water have rolled under the\par
Thames bridges since you and I held Council of War....  As I mentioned\par
before, the interest taken by the French Government in Sherbrand's\par
gyroscopic hoverer may well have stimulated the interest of our Teuton\par
neighbours.  But it doesn't explain the presence on Fanshaw's Flying\par
Ground of Lieutenant-General Count Helmuth von Moltke, Chief of the\par
German Great General Staff, and--Grand Admiral Prince Henry of Prussia,\par
brother of the Kaiser--in a F.I.A.T. touring-car!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXIX*\par
\par
                           *A SECRET MISSION*\par
\par
\par
"Can it be possible----"  Saxham checked himself.  "You see how rusty I\par
am getting, General.  You refer to that machine that turned out from\par
where cars are parked just now.  The German fellow went up to it....  It\par
had a groom beside the chauffeur and three other men inside it.... While\par
I was looking--elsewhere--it must have moved away!"\par
\par
"It has only turned the corner of the cafe-restaurant," the Chief said\par
in his quiet tones.  He glanced in the direction of the squat block of\par
gaily painted wooden buildings devoted to the inner needs of Fanshaw's\par
clients.  "The awning hides it, but I can see a bit of it still.  Until\par
it moves, I can go on talking.  Frankly, I am in the position of the\par
High Church curate who went out wild-pig shooting in the territories of\par
the Limpopo with a single-bore hammer-gun of grandpapa's pattern--and\par
got his choice of pot-shots between a lion and a rhino.  Prinz Heinrich\par
is my royal lion and von Herrnung,--who counted for little more than a\par
bush-pig--has suddenly swelled into a rhinoceros."\par
\par
He pulled the grizzled moustache thoughtfully, keeping his eyes glued on\par
the back of the big blue car.\par
\par
"If I could get hold of Sherbrand!--but the chance is dead for the rhino\par
and lion winding me.  Old von Moltke with the big wart on his\par
ginger-coloured face, and the charming manner that makes you forget that\par
you don't like him!--would certainly recognise me--and the nautical\par
Hohenzollern and I have met once or twice before.  I must lay low like\par
Brer Rabbit, and take a single-handed chance. No, no, Doctor, you have\par
your patients to look after!  I am not going to drag you into this.  But\par
if I'd got a couple of my Boy Scouts handy----"  He broke off,\par
encountering Bawne's bright eyes.  "By George, Doctor!  I'm going to\par
chance it!  I'm going to give your youngster an opportunity to prove his\par
Saxham blood!"\par
\par
The Master-hand gave the Scout's Sign, and Bawne shot across like a\par
brownish streak of swiftness.  He drew himself up, gave the Full Salute,\par
and stood waiting, his rigid attitude in sharp contrast with his\par
dancing, expectant eyes. The Doctor looked at his watch and moved away\par
silently. The Chief said in a clear undertone:\par
\par
"You see that tall, red-haired man in grey clothes over there with Mr.\par
Sherbrand?  Don't look at him openly, or he will know we are talking\par
about him, but take a sidelong gliff, and say."\par
\par
"I see him, sir."\par
\par
"Do you know anything of him?  Stand easy and answer carefully."\par
\par
The hand came down from the hat-brim.  The boy said:\par
\par
"I've heard him talk, sir, and I think he is German. I'm learning that\par
and French at Charterhouse."\par
\par
"He is a German.  Do you speak enough of the language to understand him,\par
suppose he were talking to one of his countrymen?"\par
\par
"_Ich--kann--lesen, aber Ich kann es--nicht sprechen._"  The answer came\par
slowly.  "And if they weren't using crack-jaw words, sir, or talking\par
very quick, I might manage--I could make out a lot of what they said."\par
\par
"Very well, keep your man under close observation and--you see that\par
brown satchel he has in his hand?"\par
\par
"I've seen it close, sir.  A flat brown leather despatch case\par
thing--with a criss-cross pattern on the leather, and two locks, and\par
another lock on the strap that goes round. He hadn't it with him when\par
first I saw him talking to--a lady.  Then a man--a servant--came and\par
called him away to speak to some gentlemen in a big blue motor-car.  One\par
of them--fat and old and bald--with a wart on his cheek, who wore a\par
white topper, and yellowy clothes, and a red necktie, and looked rather\par
like a--like an Inspector of Sunday Schools in shooting-clothes--passed\par
him the leather case. That's how I know he didn't bring it, sir.  Oh!\par
and the yellow car he drives isn't British.  She's got an oval\par
International plate with the German 'D' in black on a white ground."\par
\par
"I am glad my Scout knows how to use his eyes!"\par
\par
The Chief's own eyes were crinkled with merriment. That Moltke, the\par
Chief of the German Great General staff, bosom friend of the All\par
Highest, should resemble a stout Inspector of Sunday Schools in the\par
estimation of a small British boy, was lovely in the extreme.\par
\par
"Well, I want to know what the big German officer--he is an\par
officer!--does with that leather satchel he's carrying so carefully.\par
Where he goes with it, whom he talks to, and what he says to them.  Find\par
out whether it is light or heavy, if it is what I believe it to be, you\par
might be rendering good service to your country in destroying it.  But\par
you'll be doing all I want or expect, if you stick to the man who\par
carries it!"\par
\par
"I'll do that, sir, on my Honour!"\par
\par
"Good!  Make your little German serve you.  I may have to leave here\par
upon this business, but I'll be back within, at least--half an hour.  If\par
he goes before I get back, find out where he is going.  If you can't\par
find out, follow him.  On foot if he walks, in a taxi if he doesn't.\par
Here are six separate shillings--in that case you'll want money for\par
fares.  Remember, if things take a puzzling turn and you find yourself\par
in a tight place, whisper a quiet word to Sherbrand, though I'd prefer\par
you to carry through on your own!  Report to me, in case he goes before\par
I get back here--at Headquarters, Victoria Street.  Have you got all\par
this tucked away safe in your head?"\par
\par
"Yes, sir."\par
\par
"Then quit yourself like a man.  My signal to you that I have left will\par
be a dog's yelping.  Ah!"  The keen bright eyes, glued on the distant\par
back of the blue car, had seen the rear wheels moving.  Before the\par
F.I.A.T. glided smoothly out of eyeshot the Chief had hurried away.\par
\par
In the opposite direction to the archway of exit, the slight, active\par
figure in the perfectly-cut blue serge morning clothes and pot hat of\par
Bond Street block, was rapidly walking. Bawne doubted his eyes for a\par
moment before he remembered that the Collingwood Avenue ran along that\par
side of the Flying Ground fence.  There was a smaller gate in charge of\par
a commissionaire, in the fence, about a hundred yards along it.\par
Taxi-cabs were standing outside the gate.  Any person on foot or awheel,\par
leaving the Flying Ground, must pass the gate and the taxi-stand.  You\par
could see through the chinks in the fence when they passed, nip out when\par
they were well by, and follow in a green-flagged chuffer.  Bawne had\par
settled this to his satisfaction before a wrench at the rein of duty\par
pulled his head round to the business on hand.\par
\par
"I'm not spying on Mr. Sherbrand," the boy told himself, gritting his\par
small square teeth doggedly.  "I've _got_ to listen, so as to understand\par
the German's game.  And I'm going to. This is how I'm going to!"\par
\par
He began to turn hand-springs after the fashion of the London street\par
Arab, thus lessening the distance between himself and the talking men.\par
They glanced at him, and Sherbrand grinned, but they looked back again\par
directly at each other.  Then Bawne threw himself down and panted,\par
rolled over and lay, still panting.  Now he was near enough to hear what\par
passed between the two.\par
\par
Sherbrand said:\par
\par
"No, I was not particularly solid in my conviction that the aerial\par
stabiliser would take the fancy of the Chiefs of the Service\par
Aeronautique.  An accident prevented me from witnessing the final test,\par
and I got what the Americans call cold feet and judged it no use staying\par
in France longer. So I flew back here, starting early by daylight the\par
next morning, with Davis, my mechanic, and found a cable waiting at my\par
office to say the working of the invention had been observed with\par
interest by the Chiefs of the S. Ae. F., and that if I could carry out a\par
satisfactory time-trial at my headquarters in the presence of the French\par
Consul, the authorities at the Ministry of War would be willing to buy\par
my patents for France.  As it happened, I was suffering from a slight\par
obstruction in the nasal passages, that spoiled my climbing.  It was\par
absolutely necessary to go into Hospital. That is why I could not give\par
M. Jourdain an earlier date for the hovering-test you have just seen\par
carried out."\par
\par
Von Herrnung demanded:\par
\par
"But did you not receive a letter containing a business proposal?  A\par
communication from Rathenau, Wolff and Brothers, Aeromotor Engineers of\par
Paris, 200, Rue Gagnette? I happen to know that it was posted, and the\par
date being that of the Paris trial, Herren Rathenau and Wolff certainly\par
possess the prior claim!"\par
\par
"Their communication reached me in Hospital, three days later than the\par
French War Office cable," Sherbrand answered.  "It had been forwarded\par
from the makeshift hangar I rented at Drancy--a mistake in the address\par
being the reason of the delay!"\par
\par
"That fellow Lindemann is a _Dummer Teufel_," said von Herrnung,\par
shrugging.\par
\par
"My German landlord....  Why--do you know him?" asked Sherbrand with a\par
look of surprise.\par
\par
"No, certainly.  But you--you said the fellow's name was Lindemann.  Not\par
so?  No?--then I am mistaken," said von Herrnung with another shrug.  He\par
hurried on as though to cover a mistake with a spate of sentences:\par
\par
"Of course, with Rathenau and Wolff I have nothing to do.  Save as an\par
old customer, of whom they have asked a favour--you understand?  Indeed\par
I--you will pardon me!--do not your hoverer regard as an original\par
invention.  In 1912 our German Ministry of Marine completed a gun-boat\par
fitted with a gyroscopic stabiliser to prevent rolling--you\par
understand--in stormy weather.  The device was hellishly effective."\par
\par
"So effective," rejoined Sherbrand, without the quiver of a facial\par
muscle, though there was laughter in his eyes, "that it broke up the\par
ship."\par
\par
"_Es mag wohl sein!_" returned von Herrnung, covering discomfiture, if\par
he felt it, with his imperturbable shrug and hard blue stare.\par
\par
Sherbrand went on, straightening his wide shoulders and clasping his\par
hands loosely at his back as he talked:\par
\par
"I don't claim that my patent is an absolutely new invention.  Far from\par
it.  But it is a new arrangement of some old ideas, and limited though\par
its use may be--it works. You have seen it working.  You agree that it\par
justifies its name?"  He waited for the assent, and went on: "Possibly\par
if I had described it as an aerial drag-anchor, I should have explained\par
its uses more clearly.  It is no good at all when your machine isn't\par
flying level--of course you understand that?  If you were ass enough to\par
try to dive without cutting out the power that drives the horizontal\par
screws you would drop to the ground like a plummet and break into a\par
million of little bits--or dig a hole in the earth big enough for a Tube\par
Station.  But--keeping an even line of flight--when you switch it on it\par
pulls against the tractor just sufficiently to give you--not\par
immovability--but poise.  Sufficient to take a photograph or drop an\par
explosive with a good deal of accuracy."\par
\par
The small boy lying outstretched on the warm turf near them, thought\par
dolefully:\par
\par
"_Dummer Teufel_ meant 'stupid devil' in German.  But this talk is\par
dreadfully business, I can't stow away much. Man alive!  I wish Roddy\par
Wrynche or some other fellow with a top-hole memory had got this job to\par
tackle.  And yet the Chief trusted it to _me_!"\par
\par
All this, while Sherbrand was explaining.\par
\par
"M. Jourdain declared himself completely satisfied.  His observer said\par
that I maintained poise and stability for five minutes longer than the\par
stipulated twenty-five.  He looked at the altimeter and said I should\par
receive a definite answer within a couple of days....  Unlucky brute!\par
Someone must have run over him!"\par
\par
The shrill yelp of a hurt dog had evoked Sherbrand's exclamation.  The\par
sufferer's plaint came from the Collingwood Avenue, on the other side of\par
the fence.  Thrice the excruciating sound ripped the ears, then died out\par
in a sobbing whimper....  _That was for me!_ Bawne told himself, as von\par
Herrnung went on:\par
\par
"Still, you are not pledged.  There is no definite understanding.  In\par
the interests of the wealthy firm I am asked to represent--solely as a\par
matter of courtesy, because they have been immensely civil to me in\par
business,--you would not refuse me a test?"\par
\par
Sherbrand said, drawing off his left glove and showing blood oozing from\par
under bluish-looking finger-nails:\par
\par
"I found it uncommonly parky to-day at 10,000 feet. There was a\par
nor'-east breeze, a regular piercer.  Found myself spitting blood rather\par
badly, and to be candid, I was uncommonly grateful that the French\par
Consul declined my offer, in case he was not satisfied, to do the thing\par
again. The fact is, the operation, slight as it was, has weakened me a\par
little.  I wouldn't care to repeat the performance without a good\par
night's rest to buck me up."\par
\par
Von Herrnung shrugged and agreed:\par
\par
"That it is cold at 10,000 I can credit easily.  I have had the oil in\par
my own gauges frozen at 7,000 in midsummer. _Da ist nicht zit strassen_.\par
Haemorrhage and dizziness are the chief enemies of the aviator.  One's\par
stomach betrays one also, the cursed beast!--after a good hearty meal!"\par
\par
"I don't give mine the chance!" Sherbrand returned, "but stave off the\par
pangs of appetite with milk-tablets and meat-lozenges.  Do all my flying\par
on these and chocolate. Keep a little store of the things and a Thermos\par
of hot coffee, in a _cache_ I've made for them, under the map-desk on\par
the left of the instrument-frame, facing the pilot's seat. If you will\par
come over to the Bird I'll show you, and explain the working of the\par
gyroscopic hoverer."  He added, looking squarely at von Herrnung: "Of\par
course the cutting of the double screw is the chief thing about the\par
invention.  I've registered every way I know and got a trade-mark.  They\par
tell me at the Patent Office that my international rights are secure!"\par
\par
"They should be, if you have those precautions taken. It does not do to\par
trust," said von Herrnung, "too much! The monkey proverb is law for most\par
men."  He shrugged. "It comes, by the way, from Namaland in German\par
South-West Africa.  '_Nuts in your pouch are nuts in mine!_'"\par
\par
The freemasonry of their calling had established a degree of\par
friendliness between them.  They were laughing over the monkey's\par
philosophy as they went over together to the Bird. The small boy who had\par
been idly sprawling on the hot turf near them, with his tilted hat\par
shielding his face from the westering sun-rays, got up and trotted after\par
them like a collie pup.\par
\par
"Coming too, young man?" Sherbrand said, glancing back and smiling.  The\par
boy nodded in answer, and thence-forward kept close at the heels of the\par
men, his ears industriously drinking in their conversation, while his\par
eyes were glued on the brown leather satchel depending from the German's\par
gloved left hand.  Both men, now leaning over the side of the pilot's\par
cockpit, examined the gearing of the hoverer, protected by a transparent\par
casing set in the tough ash, copper-riveted planking of the fuselage.\par
Then with the aid of sulky Davis they tilted the Bird, and inspected the\par
pair of thin circular plates of toughened steel with flanged edges that,\par
revolving at high velocity in different. directions, constituted the\par
horizontal screw.\par
\par
"Driven from the engine, as you see, by an endless chain-drive\par
arrangement.  By manipulation of levers, you can throw the tractor out\par
of gear, and hover, under favourable circumstances and in still weather,\par
by means of the horizontal screw alone.  But as a rule you keep the\par
tractor working, and the screw acts in one as a lifter and\par
floating-anchor. That's about all it amounts to!--I've said I don't\par
pretend to hang immovable in the air like the albatross and the condor,\par
not to mention the gull and sparrow-hawk and dragon-fly!  While I hover\par
I am making way--but way to an inappreciable amount.  One of these days\par
we shall find out the big Secret of Stability.  Until then we must rub\par
along as best we can!"\par
\par
Von Herrnung returned:\par
\par
"I am hellishly interested in your invention.  It now occurs to me that\par
as you happen to know my flying record"--he shrugged his great shoulders\par
and smoothed his tight red roll of moustache with a well-manicured\par
finger-tip--"that it is possible you would have sufficient confidence to\par
allow me to test your gyroscopic hoverer myself?"  He laughed again\par
pleasantly as he finished: "Whatever else I may do, I give you my word\par
of honour I shall not pile up your machine. Will you consent?  It may\par
lead--supposing you do not close with the French offer--to big\par
business--done with my friends!"\par
\par
Sherbrand had looked doubtful, only for an instant. Before the\par
twelve-year-old eavesdropper had recovered from the shock that had set\par
his brain spinning and his heart thumping, the situation had been\par
accepted by the owner of the Bird of War.  He held out his left hand,\par
and von Herrnung gripped and wrenched it, noting with inward amusement\par
that his grip had brought fresh lines of blood creeping about the edges\par
of Sherbrand's finger-nails.\par
\par
"You shake hands with the left," he commented, smiling. "Not for the\par
first time have I noticed the peculiarity in Englishmen of the younger\par
breed."\par
\par
"It is a custom," Sherbrand answered, "with--members of an organisation\par
to which I had, and still have, the honour to belong."\par
\par
His eyes, in speaking, went to the bright-haired boy in Scout's uniform\par
standing near them, but von Herrnung's glance had not followed his.  The\par
boy was staring wistfully at the round-faced clock on the front gable of\par
the cafe restaurant--ten minutes to the half-hour and no sign of the\par
Chief's returning.  Bawne's courage began to ooze away at the ends of\par
his fingers and toes.\par
\par
"Then," von Herrnung was beginning impatiently, when a sallow,\par
undersized young man, whose hollow chest and inky paper cuffs advertised\par
his clerical employment, came up, touched a pen sticking out from behind\par
his ear, and said as Sherbrand turned to him:\par
\par
"Beg pardon, sir, but the telegraph-cabin is locked up proper, and Mr.\par
Macrombie 'as carried orf the key."\par
\par
"Out of sorts to-day, is he?" Sherbrand asked meaningly, and the\par
telegraph-clerk answered:\par
\par
"I've never seen 'im so bad before--in the middle of the month!"\par
\par
As Fate would have it, Macrombie, ex-Petty Officer Telegraphist of the\par
R.N.--from whose sleeve the golden Crown and thunderbolt had been reft\par
by reason of his anti-teetotal habits, had received a visit that morning\par
from a friend who had repaid a debt.  Hence the licensed operator of\par
Fanshaw's experimental and educational Wireless-station had succumbed to\par
an attack of his intermittent complaint.\par
\par
\par
Hear Macrombie's assistant continuing the recital:\par
\par
"He's left the aerial connected to the transmitter and gone out for\par
lemon-squashes four times since one o'clock grub.  'That's the drink for\par
men who have souls to save, ye little fag-eater!' he says to me;\par
'Salvation for soul and body, sucked through a straw!  If thae deboshed\par
and hopeless drunkards at the Admiralty could be induced to swear off\par
their cursed alcohol and take to it, I wad no longer be deaved to the\par
point of steeping my tongue in profanity, by the kind o' eediots'\par
gibberish that is yammering at my lugs!'"\par
\par
"He'd been raking a lot of Admiralty strays in?" Sherbrand queried.  Von\par
Herrnung, who had been grinding his heel into the turf and gnawing his\par
lip with ill-concealed impatience, turned his head sharply, and listened\par
to the colloquy with all his ears.\par
\par
"Not so much X's as definites, sir," responded Macrombie's assistant.\par
"He was upset about ten minutes before he broke out by getting an\par
'Urgent' without a Preparative Call.  Then comes 'Important' in\par
International Code, and 'Administration' and 'Emergency.'  Then 'War\par
Office,' and 'Documents,' and 'Enforcement of the Law.'  By that time 'e\par
was purple in the face and 'arf crazy.  'If I had my way wi' you, ye\par
bung-nosed intemperates,' he says, groaning-like--'I wad keep ye on\par
grits an' caller watter for a fortnicht!  Oh, that men, as auld Hosea\par
says in the inspired Screeptures'--an' I 'appen to know myself it was\par
Shakespeare--'should pit an enemy intil their mooths to steal awa' their\par
brains!'  An' 'e snatches off the telephone 'ead-band and chucks it into\par
the corner, and just as my own instrument starts to tick out a call, he\par
ketches me by the neck as if I'd bin a tame rabbit, an' slings me out o'\par
the office an' locks the door.  'Out o' this!' 'e says, puttin' the\par
cabin key in 'is pocket.  'I will no' have your lugs, dirr-ty as they\par
are, polluted by the unclean counsels o' the wicked. I'm awa' to cool\par
the wrath o' the righteous wi' anither lemon squash!'  An' the winder is\par
blocked by the Morse key instrument, an' even if it wasn't, it's too\par
small for me to get in through!"  Macrombie's victim ended, with an\par
injured sniff.\par
\par
"Well, well!  Better hang about the cabin a bit and possess your soul in\par
patience.  If any pupils drop along, tell them they'll have to wait!\par
Perhaps Macrombie'll turn up sober enough to take them on by-and-by.  As\par
for the message in transmission, I daresay it will keep.  Mr. Fanshaw's\par
not expecting any particularly important communication that I know of.\par
Oh, hang it!"  Sherbrand whistled dismally.  "I'd forgotten.  That's\par
just what I am!"\par
\par
"Shall I go and see if I can find Rumball?" suggested Macrombie's\par
assistant helpfully.  "He's at the engine-sheds. He's been a locksmith.\par
'Twouldn't take him more than a sec. to open the office door!"\par
\par
"Cut then!" acceded Sherbrand, and the telegraph-clerk touched his\par
pen--discovering by a jab of the inky nib that he was wearing it--and\par
set off at a trot in the direction of the engine-sheds.\par
\par
You are to suppose that von Herrnung's sharp ears had gathered the pith\par
of the communication.  Some meaning in the isolated words the clerk had\par
repeated had had a palpable effect upon his nerves.  His face looked\par
bluish-grey and streaky, as he said to Sherbrand, stammering in his\par
eagerness:\par
\par
"So then, it is agreed about my flying your machine?"\par
\par
"I see no objection."\par
\par
"_Gut!_" Von Herrnung went on, concealing a huge joy under a careless\par
camaraderie: "Can you lend me a cap and coat and a pair of\par
_Schulzbrille_?  Goggles you call them, yes!  The coat should better to\par
be a large one"--he stumbled in his English now through sheer\par
excitement--"I am so much a bigger man than you!"\par
\par
"Certainly.  We keep Flying rigs in all manner of sizes. It's in the way\par
of business," Sherbrand said.  Then his glance fell upon Davis, whose\par
little black-avised countenance wore an expression of sulky resentment,\par
and he uttered a slight exclamation.  "I forgot, Davis!  I really am\par
very sorry!"  He turned to von Herrnung and explained in a tone of\par
finality that enraged the hearer: "This is Davis's afternoon off.  I\par
cannot ask him to repeat the climb."\par
\par
"It is hellishly annoying!  But see!  Listen, my fellow!"  He addressed\par
himself to little grimy Davis, unhelmeted and unbuttoned, leaning\par
against the Bird's flank with his hands in the pockets of his oily\par
overalls, chewing a blade of grass; "You will go up with me if I tip\par
you?  A sovereign!  Come then!  The gold does it!  You will go up with\par
me, will you not, yes?"\par
\par
Davis spat out grass and delivered himself:\par
\par
"Not even for my young guv'nor--and a Bank of England finnup, do I do\par
the soaring heagle hact again this blooming Wednesday."\par
\par
Welsh Davis had come to London from a mountain farm in Merioneth,\par
speaking nothing but his native Cymric, and had culled his Sassenach\par
from Cockney lips.  Von Herrnung bid another sovereign, and then two\par
more, ineffectually.\par
\par
"Naow!"  Davis was rock.  "I've done my day's stunt an' I'm nuffy.\par
D'yer tumble?  Nuffy!  Yer knaows wot that means--if you're a Flying\par
Bloke!"\par
\par
"Damn you, I will gif you ten pounds!"  Von Herrnung's face was wrung\par
and streaked with passion.  He breathed hard, and the brown leather\par
satchel jumped and wobbled in his shaking hand.\par
\par
"It isn't any use," said Sherbrand, "really!  Money doesn't count with\par
Davis where his off-time's concerned. Davis doesn't want to go up again,\par
and I've not another man of his weight available.  What do you turn the\par
scale at?  I should guess 16 stone or thereabouts?"\par
\par
"I weigh 16 st. 8 lbs. in my ordinary clothes."\par
\par
"Well, I tot 11 st. 6 lbs. in the fullest of flying-rig, and Davis only\par
8 st. 5 lbs.  And the Bird is built to carry in addition to her\par
engine--what with the instruments, so forth, and man-freight, a cargo of\par
something like 22 stone.  You see, even with Davis, you'd load the\par
machine a good bit over her"--he smiled at the conceit--"her Plimsoll\par
mark. Again, I'm sorry.  It's your luck!  No flying for you to-day!"\par
\par
"It is damnably annoying!  But"--von Herrnung's red-lashed blue eyes\par
were busily scanning Bawne's face and figure--"but suppose I could get a\par
boy of 6 stone to go up with me?  Merely as ballast, for I do not\par
require an assistant--the difficulty might be got over in this way?\par
What you say, my little English fellow?"  He turned on the boy with a\par
great air of jovial patronage.  "Are you plucky enough?  Shall we go for\par
a voyage together in the sky?"\par
\par
"Yes--please!"\par
\par
The dark blue eyes met the hard light ones bravely, though every vestige\par
of colour had sunk out of the young face.  Then back to lips and cheeks\par
the banished colour came racing.  Bawne flushed crimson, as von Herrnung\par
held up a bright bit of gold, and sharply shook his head.\par
\par
"_Was_?  Will you not take the sovereign?" von Herrnung demanded.  "Are\par
you a faint-heart after all?"\par
\par
The boy bit his lip and said, clenching his small fists desperately:\par
\par
"It's against the rule for Scouts to take tips.  So I don't want the\par
money.  But I'm ready to come with you!"\par
\par
"Look here, old fellow!" Sherbrand was beginning anxiously.  The boy\par
stopped him with:\par
\par
"Really and truly I'm not funky--and you said I was to have another\par
flight."\par
\par
"So I did, and so you shall," agreed Sherbrand.  "But this won't be just\par
a 'bus trip around the aerodrome.  It will be climbing and spiralling\par
and hovering, and all the rest!"\par
\par
Bawne persisted:\par
\par
"You could strap me in.  And I'm not afraid--really!"\par
\par
"And," von Herrnung interposed, "I shall not ascend higher than three\par
thousand.  Probably less will do for my purpose.  The boy will be quite\par
safe.  Surely you are able to trust him with me?"\par
\par
Sherbrand hesitated, then said to Bawne in a relieved tone: "Well,\par
there's the Doctor talking to a tall lady in white with a hat that\par
glitters.  Run across to your father and ask him whether you may go?"\par
\par
"I'd rather you asked him--if you must--and let me stop here!"\par
\par
"_Gut!  Sehr gut!_"  Von Herrnung's tautened nerves would have been\par
relieved by some hard Prussian swearing. He jangled out a laugh instead.\par
He caught hold of the boy under the armpits and lifted him high above\par
his head. "What is your weight?  Six stone?  Come now, have I not\par
guessed nearly!"  He had not relinquished his grip on the leather\par
satchel, and as it banged against his ribs, Bawne realised that it was\par
quite light.\par
\par
"Papers inside!" he said to himself.  Something quite hard was under the\par
leather at the corners, perhaps the thinnest of metal plates.  Its\par
contact with the boy's body seemed to sober von Herrnung's exultation.\par
He dropped Bawne unceremoniously, and straightened himself again.\par
\par
"How much petrol has been used?" he asked hastily of Davis, going over\par
to the Bird and mounting on the landing-carriage to look at the gauges.\par
"Because when I fly I never take risks.  You will have to fill up the\par
tank again.  Do you hear, my fellow?"\par
\par
"If Mr. Sherbrand orders me," Davis spat out another piece of grass,\par
"dessay I shall do it!"  He eyed von Herrnung with surly disapproval as\par
he craned over the Bird's fuselage, while audibly commenting to an\par
acquaintance who had strolled up:\par
\par
"Sheer blinders, I call 'em, these ere Fritzies!  Walk into Buckingham\par
Pallis next minute and ask to look into the Privy Puss.  'Ope the\par
Governor comes back before 'e gits Nosey Parkerin' into the 'orizontal\par
'overing gear!  Perish me if I ever met a bloke with such a nerve!\par
Watto, old sonny?"  He addressed himself to the boy.  "Ain't you feelin'\par
up to the posh?"\par
\par
"I am quite all right, thank you!" Bawne responded, while his heart\par
bumped against his ribs.  In his brain words and sentences kept forming:\par
\par
"I'm only a little chap.  And this is--a Big thing! Bigger than the\par
Chief expected, perhaps!  And he said he'd be back in half an hour."\par
Half an hour meant thirty minutes.  He glanced at the big round\par
white-faced clock above the entrance of the cafe restaurant.  More than\par
fifteen minutes of the half-hour had gone.\par
\par
To stick to the big, brutal German was his--Bawne's--Secret Mission.\par
And the inspiring, uplifting voice that thousands of boy-hearts thrill\par
to all the big world over had said to him:\par
\par
"Quit yourself like a man!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXX*\par
\par
                             *THE REAPING*\par
\par
\par
To Patrine, when the shadow of the familiar figure of the Doctor mingled\par
with hers upon the dry green grass, and Saxham's voice called her by her\par
name, it was as though his presence had a weight that physically\par
oppressed her, and his scrutiny seared her flesh like the approach of\par
white-hot iron.\par
\par
Through her mind passed swift sentences: "_Yet another of us has\par
disgraced him!  My father and mother are not the only traitors of our\par
name!_"  In the rawness of her mental anguish every sense was\par
unnaturally exaggerated.  The ticking of Saxham's watch, that the\par
furious beating of her heart could not drown, tormented by its\par
iteration.  And worst of all, was the consciousness of defilement in the\par
physical sense.\par
\par
\par
"Did not your mother give you my message?"\par
\par
Always pale, her pallor did not demand particular attention, save that\par
under their ruddy salve the edges of her lips showed white.  She\par
answered, forcing the lips to smile, compelling her eyes to meet\par
Saxham's.\par
\par
"About coming to see you?"  She remembered and drew from her gilt-chain\par
vanity bag the letter she had not posted: "This was written to you\par
to-day.  Then I thought I would have been able to look in at Harley\par
Street, and in the end----"\par
\par
"In the end you neither paid the visit nor posted the excuse.  Well, be\par
more considerate in future to those who love you.  Sincere, clean love\par
does not grow on every gooseberry bush, my dear!"\par
\par
The curt speech, made in the Doctor's brusquest tone, conveyed to\par
Patrine an impression of exquisite kindness. So many boons, so many\par
benefits had been conferred in that grim, curt way.  She had wept and\par
would not weep again, but her hard bright eyes grew misty as she thanked\par
him, and asked after Lynette, with a touch of wistfulness that recalled\par
to the Doctor that unforgettable time when greedy Death had threatened\par
to rob him of his joy.  He answered her cheerfully, and they found\par
themselves chatting of familiar, everyday matters across the gulf that\par
yawned between.  And then, warned by some swift change of expression in\par
her face, Saxham glanced up to see Sherbrand approaching.\par
\par
"Doctor!" he called.  "Sorry to interrupt, but would you listen a\par
minute?"\par
\par
The tall, lightly-built, lightly moving figure came swinging towards\par
them.  He still carried the eared cap with the goggled visor, his thick,\par
silvery-blonde hair was darkened at the temples with the dampness\par
generated under the close covering of waterproof.  His light grey-blue\par
eyes were smiling, yet there was a pucker of anxiety between his\par
eyebrows, as he put von Herrnung's case.\par
\par
"So," he ended, "instead of taking a second flight in the Bird with me\par
as we arranged, would you trust your boy to this foreign crack who's in\par
a hole for a passenger?  He is Captain von Herrnung of the German Flying\par
Service--winner of the two-days' flight from Hanover to Paris in\par
April--a famous run!"  He added, "I need hardly say that with such a\par
record as von Herrnung holds you cannot be apprehensive of any rashness\par
or neglect on his part. But I'll own I would rather take Bawne up\par
another day myself.  Still, von Herrnung----"\par
\par
"I am aware of the reputation held by the person you mention.  I am\par
going now to speak to him."\par
\par
The Doctor's face was devoid of all expression.  But he battled, as he\par
spoke, with a masterful desire to forbid Bawne the expedition.  To\par
assert parental authority on this point would have been the mode of\par
dealing approved by one of the two men who dwelt within the Dop Doctor.\par
The other Saxham said "Hold!"\par
\par
Dare you place your paternal love, that other Saxham asked--between your\par
son and his duty?  Because it would be so easy to do it, is the reason\par
why you should refrain! The Doctor had walked a few paces towards the\par
object of his troubled reflections.  He wheeled abruptly, returned, and\par
presented Sherbrand to his niece.\par
\par
\par
A faint blush rose in Patrine's white cheeks as her eyes met those of\par
the tall young aviator.  They looked at her without any sign of\par
recognition, and the conviction, "_He has forgotten!_" shot stingingly\par
across her mind.  "_He did not think me worth remembering_" came next.\par
And then she could have laughed, recalling that she had dismissed him\par
from her own thoughts on the discovery of his connection with Fanshaw's.\par
She had made so certain that a teacher of Flying couldn't be a\par
gentleman.\par
\par
Now, face to face with him again, in his upright easy bearing, in his\par
straight and fearless regard, in the pleasant well-bred voice that\par
addressed her in a brief conventional sentence or so, she read his\par
patent of gentlehood.\par
\par
From whatever root it sprang, the flower was noble.  Her swift eyes shot\par
a glance at the bigger figure in grey.  What a hoggish knight of the\par
dunghill, what a high-born clown had she not distinguished by her choice\par
and selection.  The smile of scorn that curved her mouth was suddenly\par
banished by the sudden recollection of Bawne.\par
\par
"Uncle Owen, you have not yet told Mr. Sherbrand whether Bawne may go up\par
again or not.  I am sure--if you won't think me--if you don't mind my\par
saying so!--that he has had enough for to-day!  I think it would be\par
better if you would not----"\par
\par
It was not the deep warm voice of Patrine's characteristic utterance,\par
but a weaker, thinner voice that hesitated and faltered and trailed\par
away.  It recalled nothing to Sherbrand.  He looked at her and\par
transferred his gaze to Saxham, who asked:\par
\par
"Does this German officer intend climbing to any high altitude, or\par
perpetrating anything in the nature of a display?"\par
\par
Sherbrand explained:\par
\par
"He does not want to go higher than three thousand. Just to try the\par
hoverer, regarding which some business friends of his are bitten with\par
curiosity.  My mechanic is not able to go up with him, and he wants a\par
light-weight passenger.  He is over sixteen stone himself, and the Bird\par
has been built to carry me with Davis.  I calculated her wing-area\par
to----"\par
\par
Sherbrand travelled into the realm of technicalities, using terms that\par
were Volapuk and Esperanto to Patrine.  He had supple, finely-shaped\par
hands, and used them as he talked with vivid illustrative gestures.\par
\par
"So," he ended, "as your plucky youngster asked to go, it seemed a way\par
out of the difficulty, provided you weren't dead against the thing.  Of\par
course we'll swadd the little chap in a sweater or so under the\par
pneumatic jacket.  It'll be a bit parky, even at three thousand, now the\par
sun's beginning to down."\par
\par
He added:\par
\par
"I'll see to the strapping myself.  You may rely upon it, Doctor."\par
\par
Saxham said with a look of kindness at the handsome face with the clear\par
candid eyes:\par
\par
"I am sure of that!"  He added, mastering that inward impulse: "I shall\par
not forbid the flight if Bawne is set on it.  But first, I must speak to\par
him!"\par
\par
And the great form with the stern thoughtful face and scholar's stoop\par
moved across the greensward, followed by the tall young figures of\par
Sherbrand and Patrine.  Of the two, the man was by a bare inch the\par
taller.  This Patrine realised in a swift side-glance.  Certain featural\par
characteristics of him, personal impressions received\par
half-unconsciously, retained their clear sharpness then and for many\par
days....\par
\par
The silvery-yellow hair toning into the pale brown skin. The powerful\par
sweep of the brows over eyes set flush with their large orbits,\par
prominent, brilliant, mobile as the eyes of a bird of flight.  The nose,\par
arched and jutting like a kite's beak, with large sensitive nostrils,\par
the somewhat sunken cheek and the sharply-angled jaw, the little ear and\par
the rounded skull superbly set upon the full muscular neck rising out of\par
the collar of the gabardine, made up a portrait upon which some happy\par
woman might well dote and dream.\par
\par
It was five o'clock and the breeze that smelt of heather and clover-hay\par
and strawberries blew more strongly, straight from under the westering\par
sun.  Patrine drank in deep draughts of the buoyant sweetness.  The\par
leaden gyves had fallen from her limbs, the leaden weight had lifted\par
from her bosom.  She had recovered something of her old, elastic grace\par
of movement, that even the sheath-skirt could not spoil.  Looking at\par
her, Sherbrand said to himself:\par
\par
"She walks like a Highland hill-woman or a native girl of the\par
Philippines.  And--did Heaven or a Bond Street specialist give her that\par
extraordinary hair?  I rather hate it, and yet I have to go on looking\par
at it.  Does she know? I wonder if she knows?"\par
\par
She felt his eyes on her.  And the buoyant sense of well-being that his\par
presence brought to her was mingled with an agony of apprehension.  Her\par
heart clamoured, like a brooding thrush attacked by the owl, that Bawne\par
should not be permitted to risk himself with von Herrnung.  "_Does any\par
other living being know him as I know him?_" she asked herself.  "_If by\par
some misadventure it came to a question of one life or the other, would\par
he scruple--no! he would not scruple for an instant to sacrifice the\par
child?_"\par
\par
Three words to Uncle Owen--if one only dared to speak them--would have\par
put the thing out of the question.  But at the thought of the dreadful\par
avowal to which such an utterance might lead, Patrine was stricken dumb.\par
She could not face the music.  This was one little ear of wild oats out\par
of the full field that waited for her reaping, sown in the hours that\par
lie between the midnight of pleasure and the dawn of the Day of Remorse.\par
\par
Perhaps she and Sherbrand had walked more slowly than it had seemed to\par
her.  She saw Saxham and his son meet, heard, indistinctly the exchange\par
of a few brief sentences, and then the boy, with a jump to hug his\par
father round the neck, ran to her as she came up.\par
\par
"Cousin Pat, I'm going to get into my flying-kit in a minute."  His\par
heart was thumping so that it shook him, and the short upper lip with\par
the gold-brown dust of freckles on it quivered, hard as he tried to keep\par
it stiff: "One doesn't do it before people generally--but I'd rather\par
like you to kiss me now!"\par
\par
"My precious, a dozen times!"\par
\par
She said it impetuously in the deep womanly baritone that Bawne loved,\par
and Sherbrand started as he heard it. She dropped her tall-sticked\par
sunshade, and caught the little boyish figure to her broad womanly\par
bosom, hugged him until he panted, and kissed his pale cheeks red.  You\par
do not need to be reminded that Patrine was a galumpher. "Don't go!\par
don't go!" she whispered in her darling's neck. "I hate your going! and\par
I don't believe Uncle Owen likes it....  Say you've been up once and\par
you're 'nuffy! Pretend you funk it.  Do, for my sake!"\par
\par
"I--can't.  Ouch!  You tickle!  Please let me go.  This is business!"\par
He squirmed, and she burst out laughing, and released him.  The act was\par
a wrench that tore her bleeding heart anew.\par
\par
He bounded instantly after Sherbrand, seeing him go forward to join von\par
Herrnung, who was standing watching Davis fill the Bird's tank with\par
petrol, and her reservoir with oil.\par
\par
There was no spurring these lazy devils of English into movement....\par
The accursed pig-dogs, the stupid sheep's heads!  If that fragmentary\par
Wireless message had really to do with _the business_, within the next\par
ten minutes everything might be ruined.  One walked perilously, as\par
amongst pebbles, holding a watch-glass of High Explosive in one's hand.\par
Here came the man and the boy.  He joined them with a noisy burst of\par
forced laughter.  Presently you saw all three moving in the direction of\par
a building where the "flying-kits of all sorts and shapes and sizes," of\par
which Sherbrand had boasted, were kept for the use of the patrons of\par
Fanshaw's School.  As they went in, Bawne cast a wistful glance up at\par
the clock on the front gable of the cafe restaurant, now supplying\par
afternoon tea served in brown teapots, and rolls and butter on thick\par
white platters, to a thin sprinkling of customers.\par
\par
"Three minutes to the half-hour," said the clock.\par
\par
Would the Chief come, or must this thing be carried out by a small boy\par
whose heart lay, a palpable lump of cold lead in the pit of his stomach,\par
and whose knees were turning to jelly as he went?\par
\par
If Cousin Pat, when she begged him not to go, had known how badly he,\par
Bawne, had wanted to hold her round the neck and beg her not to let him,\par
he would at this moment have been unheroically safe.\par
\par
She was so big.  He had most dreadfully wanted to cling to her and\par
cry--imagine a fellow of twelve doing anything so kiddish.  But he had\par
swallowed the unmanly tears, and wriggled out of her strong protecting\par
arms.\par
\par
He looked back and saw her tall white figure, standing near the hulking\par
black-clad shape of the Doctor, who had pulled his hat-brim low down\par
over his eyes, and did not seem to be talking or laughing at all.  Davis\par
was doing something with a spanner to the Bird's under-carriage, and the\par
long, thin shadow of her in combination with the squat shadow of the\par
little stooping Welshman, stretched eastwards over the dry green grass.\par
\par
He heaved a big sigh and followed his man in.  Von Herrnung was already\par
trying on pneumatic coats, swearing in nervous German when they were not\par
big enough.  At last he was caparisoned, in a heavy suit of\par
flannel-lined Carberrys and a buttonless hooded jacket.  He had stripped\par
the burst glove from his wounded hand, thrown it away, and replaced the\par
magpie pearl ring upon his little finger. He had put on a woollen helmet\par
and tied over that a flapped cap with goggles and ear-pieces.  While he\par
attended to his outfit, the leather satchel lay at his feet, or\par
sometimes between them, or he would keep a boot-toe on a corner of it.\par
And his hard blue eyes were vigilantly watchful against surprise.\par
\par
Sherbrand and the dresser--who presided over a long room of shelves and\par
pegs laden with queer garments, and who looked like a washed mechanic in\par
spotless blue overalls--put Bawne into a woollen sweater, and added to\par
the panoply he had worn already that morning, and which consisted of\par
leggings, slip-strapped to a webbing waistbelt, a pneumatic jacket, a\par
knitted helmet such as von Herrnung wore, and a pair of goggles.  They\par
looked like the Eskimo hunter and his little boy in the "Book of The\par
Arctic"--a volume specially beloved of Saxham's small son.\par
\par
It was five minutes past the half-hour when they emerged from the\par
dressing-shed.  Saxham came to meet them, turned and walked by his son's\par
side.  Davis, whose weakness as regards the sex we know, had pinched\par
from the visitor's enclosure a green-painted iron chair for Patrine. She\par
half-rose, stung by an impulse of escape, when she saw von Herrnung\par
approaching, and then controlled herself and sat down again.\par
\par
Nothing escaped her long eyes.  They saw Sherbrand glance from Saxham to\par
von Herrnung, and read the intention of an introduction in his look.  He\par
had just begun:\par
\par
"Doctor, I don't think you have met Captain----" when von Herrnung\par
lengthened his long stride, outstripped his companions, and went over\par
swiftly and stood beside Patrine.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXXI*\par
\par
                     *VON HERRNUNG BAITS THE HOOK*\par
\par
\par
She knew that he had interpreted her movement as an invitation.\par
\par
He saluted her and said, speaking thickly:\par
\par
"It is necessary that I have a word with you.  Walk with me for one\par
moment.  I shall not keep you more!"\par
\par
He bulked huge in his rig-out, but looked thoroughly at home, and deadly\par
workmanlike.  He pushed up his goggles as though conscious that they\par
discounted his personal attractions, and his blue eyes were stony and\par
glittering, and his full mouth showed pale and hard-set under the\par
scarlet roll of his moustache.\par
\par
"I shall not see you again to-day, and I have something important to\par
tell you."  He spoke rapidly and his breathing was harsh and loud.  "I\par
have been recalled by my Chiefs and return to Germany in--another two or\par
three days.  That we do not meet again before I leave is possible,\par
therefore I wish to give you my address."\par
\par
She did not look up.  A white hand with red hairs growing thick on the\par
back of it offered her a pencilled card. She made no movement to take\par
it.  He said, thrusting the card underneath her eyes:\par
\par
"It is printed here in German letters.  You read and speak my language\par
badly, so I will translate for you--'Squadron-Captain-Pilot Count\par
Theodor von Herrnung, Imperial Field Flying Service, Flight Station\par
XXX., Taubefeld, near Diebrich, West Hessen, Germany.'  Write your\par
letter to me in English.  The address copy from this. Will you not take\par
the card?"\par
\par
"There is no need to.  I do not mean to write to you!"\par
\par
"_Danke_.  You are candid," he said, "at least.  You give me to\par
understand that whatever happens--" he repeated the words with a\par
singular inflection "_whatever happens!_--you will have no more to do\par
with me?"\par
\par
"Have I not told you so twice already?"\par
\par
He gritted his teeth and said, controlling furious anger:\par
\par
"_Erklaeren Sie_!  _Was giebt es_?  Why are you so--rottenly furious\par
with me?  You have yourself to thank for--what has happened!  You led me\par
on.  You made me crazy about you.  And the devil of it is I am so still!\par
The sight of you maddens me!  Listen!  Do not be stupid--unkind to\par
yourself and to me!  In three days from now, you will get an envelope at\par
your Club with plenty of money.  Join me at my headquarters at Taubefeld\par
and then--you will see!  We will be happy--you shall have plenty of\par
money to throw about when we visit Berlin and other big cities, and\par
jewels, dresses, pleasure, admiration--everything a beautiful woman\par
wants!  _Grosse Gott_!  Can I offer anything more tempting?  What are\par
you saying?  'Yes!' or 'No!'"\par
\par
Her narrowed eyes looked like long black slits in her white face.  The\par
pale lips barely moved to answer:\par
\par
"Neither!  Are you proposing to marry me?"\par
\par
He laughed woodenly, and repeated:\par
\par
"Marry you!  Ha, ha!  What _verdammt_ nonsense are you talking?  What\par
has love to do with getting married? Nothing that I have ever heard!  Of\par
course I shall marry--my family have arranged all that for me.  But my\par
Countess will not interfere with my mistress--that I promise you!  Come,\par
be kind, my beautiful Isis!  Whisper now that you agree!"\par
\par
He bent his head to hear.  The whisper came from the pale lips:\par
\par
"I will see you in Hell first!"\par
\par
He started, taken aback.  Her own utterance had shocked her.  "Am I a\par
street-walker already," she asked herself, "that I begin to curse and\par
swear?"\par
\par
A whistle trilled.  He started and said:\par
\par
"So then, all is over between us?"\par
\par
She bent her head assentingly, and her glance fell guiltily on Bawne who\par
was standing near.  Von Herrnung, aware of him at the same instant,\par
turned on him with a scowl and the harsh demand:\par
\par
"What is this?  Do little English boys pry and listen?"\par
\par
Bawne returned, looking at the other squarely:\par
\par
"Beg pardon, but Mr. Sherbrand's calling you.  He says it's getting\par
jolly late."\par
\par
"_So!_"  Von Herrnung glanced at his wrist-watch, in the act lifting the\par
brown leather satchel into fullest view.  The boy queried with open-eyed\par
innocent curiosity:\par
\par
"Shall I carry that?  Are you going to take it with you?"\par
\par
"_Es mag wohl sein_," von Herrnung answered.  Then he clicked his heels\par
and bowed formally, and kissed Patrine's cold and heavy hand.  She felt\par
his teeth grit as he did it. She knew he was swearing in his way.\par
\par
"Adieu, then," he said, smiling at her maliciously.  "Will you not wish\par
me _Angenehme Reise_?"\par
\par
"Certainly.  A pleasant voyage, and a safe landing!"  Her eyes fell on\par
Bawne's little, oddly garbed figure and her woman's heart spoke in spite\par
of her.  "Take care of my dearest!" broke from her, and von Herrnung\par
answered:\par
\par
"He is your dearest?  Ah yes!  I will certainly take very good care of\par
him!"\par
\par
He bowed, wheeled about and walked from her with his long strides, and\par
the boy, with a face all flushed and quivering, suddenly jumped at her\par
neck and hugged her; bringing with the rough little embrace the queer\par
scent of water-proofed material and dubbined leather, knocking the\par
silver-spangled hat awry, loosening divers tortoiseshell hairpins and an\par
amethyst slide-buckle holding up the heavy tresses of the dead\par
beech-leaf coloured hair, as he whispered:\par
\par
"Remember I love you, Pat.  Don't mind!"\par
\par
And she shuddered as he freed her, and ran from her, asking herself: How\par
much had the child overheard of von Herrnung's proposal?  What had he\par
comprehended of what he had heard?\par
\par
Next, she was aware of the pleasant voice of Sherbrand calling, and saw\par
von Herrnung imperiously beckoning.  A cold sickness of dread assailed\par
her, and her knees trembled underneath her weight.  A mechanic came\par
running past, carrying away the chair Davis had brought her.  He set it\par
down at a safe distance from the aeroplane, and she staggered to it,\par
leaning on the long staff of her sunshade, and sat heavily down, feeling\par
chilly and old....\par
\par
\par
Saxham had squeezed Bawne's shoulder and kissed him, and then withdrawn\par
to a distance whence he could see all that took place.  He watched Davis\par
and Sherbrand help the boy into the forward cockpit, and fasten about\par
him the safety belt attached to the fuselage on either side of the fixed\par
bamboo seat.\par
\par
\par
"You are sure you really want to fly again?  Mind, I believe you're as\par
safe with him as houses, but if you don't want to go, say the word, and\par
you shan't!"\par
\par
Sherbrand whispered the words as he busied himself with the boy.  And\par
Bawne set his small teeth and squared his sturdy boyish shoulders,\par
registering an unspoken vow to go in spite of all....\par
\par
One had been told to drop a word to Sherbrand if one found oneself in a\par
tight place.  But could one ever hold up one's head again before the\par
Patrol, if one did this?  To share one's Mission with another when the\par
Chief had said "I'd rather you'd carry through on your own" wasn't to be\par
thought of.  Mother--he swallowed hard at the thought of her--would say\par
so too.\par
\par
It troubled his faithful little soul that he could no longer see von\par
Herrnung.  He heard him talking in his guttural English, to Davis, whom\par
Bawne could not see either--as he stood near the nose of the machine, in\par
readiness to start the tractor--any more than the two mechanics who\par
steadied the Bird, pressing each a toe on the axle of the under-carriage\par
as they held on to a steel rod that ran along under the rearward edges\par
of her single plane.\par
\par
His final directions sharply given, von Herrnung stepped up on the\par
under-carriage, threw a long leg over the bulwark of the fuselage, and\par
stepped into the pilot's pit.  Bawne screwed his head round and saw,\par
through and over a low talc wind-shield, the upright torso of the\par
German, big, hard, and indomitable, the leather satchel still gripped in\par
his strapped-up left hand.\par
\par
"Are you going to take that leather case along with you?"  Sherbrand's\par
voice had a note of surprise in it.  "You'll find it a handicap, let me\par
say.  You can't sit on it or lean against it, and if you tried to put it\par
under you, you'd find it dead-certain to foul the controls."\par
\par
To Sherbrand's voice, von Herrnung's answered harshly and rather\par
angrily:\par
\par
"Surely I shall be able to carry this?  It is nott-thing but a folding\par
camera, with a telephoto lens made especially for Survey and\par
Reconnaissance.  There is still a good light. If I fly with the sun\par
behind me, I shall be able to take quite a panorama of London\par
North-West.  It is not forbidden--no?  Your Government would not\par
object?"\par
\par
"I don't suppose my Government would care a little hang!" Sherbrand's\par
voice answered.  "But--this isn't one of your German Army Albatros's or\par
Kondors, and I don't see where you're to stow your camera, unless in the\par
observer's pit.  Of course the hovering installation takes up a lot of\par
room, and I can't possibly risk your hampering the controls."\par
\par
"_Ganz recht_!  Very good!" came von Herrnung's voice, giving in with\par
simulated heartiness.  In another moment his long legs, followed by his\par
great body, came scrambling into the forward cockpit, and his hands\par
busied themselves about the stout belt of pig-leather that secured the\par
boy in the observer's seat.\par
\par
"Look here, my fellow!  You will take care of this for me?  See, I have\par
passed the belt-strap through the handle. Do not touch it!"  The\par
guttural whisper had menace in it.  "I shall be sure to know if you\par
touch it, or try to unbuckle the strap."\par
\par
"What's up?"  Sherbrand's head and shoulders came thrusting over the\par
other side of the cockpit.  "Why did you unstrap him?" he demanded\par
brusquely of von Herrnung. "Don't you know that he is my friend's son,\par
and that it is my business to see to this?"  Sherbrand's hand felt over\par
Bawne's belts and bucklings before his head and shoulders vanished.\par
Then von Herrnung's big body withdrew itself. His voice, sounding from\par
the pilot's pit on the other side of the low wind-shield, gave a\par
peremptory order, and the tractor began slowly to revolve.  An instant\par
later, with a blinding flash, it began to roar and whizz round\par
furiously. The Bird, freed from the hands that detained her, leaped\par
forwards, hurtling over the smooth turf at the speed of a racing\par
motor-car.  The smooth floor of the cockpit unexpectedly tilted up, and\par
a rough cold wind buffeted Bawne about the head and shoulders, sent\par
eddies down about his dangling feet, bellowed in his covered ears and\par
made him gasp for breath.  Then--houses and people, trees, and hangars\par
fell suddenly away, and he knew that the Bird was rushing upwards at the\par
bidding of its "Gnome" motor--long superseded now, but then the latest\par
marvel in aerial engineering--towards the blue sky with its lines of\par
gilt mackerel clouds.  On each side of the roaring, flashing whirl that\par
meant the tractor, spread North Middlesex, with its fields fast\par
diminishing to the size of billiard tables. That patch no bigger than a\par
garden-lawn, with a row of wooden things like dog-kennels and\par
chicken-coops, must be--Bawne knew that it was--the aerodrome.  Deafened\par
by the noise and a little sick, for the roaring, striving, hurtling\par
Thing in whose body he sat fastened, stank horribly of castor oil, and\par
seemed to agonise and call on Bawne to suffer with it--he looked up and\par
took courage from the warm, blue, beautiful, cheerful sky.\par
\par
He was quitting himself like a man.  Nobody could say otherwise.  How\par
high, how much higher was the Bird going to climb?\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXII*\par
\par
                         *ADVENTURE IN THE AIR*\par
\par
\par
He looked down, and under his feet, left of the long transparent case\par
that housed the horizontal hovering gear, was a little steel-framed\par
glass port.  Seen through this, the ground with its trees, fields and\par
houses, hurried along beneath him as though a comet, travelling in the\par
opposite direction, had been harnessed to our old earth, and was towing\par
her away.\par
\par
The floor of the cockpit suddenly altered its angle.  It had tilted\par
upwards.  Now it tilted all to one side.  Sick and dizzy, but secure,\par
the boy hung in his straps as she lay over, and saw on his left hand a\par
wing of the Bird rising and blotting out the heavens, while on his right\par
hand the earth reared up so horribly that Bawne could only shut his eyes\par
tight and hold on to the arms-straps of his seat, and gasp out a little\par
prayer.  Then the cockpit floor became more level, and the wind buffeted\par
less.  The roar of the tractor and the twanging drone of the wires made\par
one's bones hum and tingle to the very ends of one's teeth and\par
finger-tips. But nothing had happened.  Perhaps nothing would!\par
\par
He drew a great breath of relief, and his heart left off bumping.  His\par
mouth was cold inside and his tongue felt dry and stiff.  Only Our Lord\par
and Our Lady and his guardian Angel had seen him funky, and for this\par
Bawne was grateful.  They understood, and--people--would not.\par
\par
He guessed it about a quarter to six o'clock.  By the genial warmth on\par
one cheek and shoulder, and the way his shadow stretched over the pale\par
grained ash-wood that lined the cock-pit, he knew the west must be upon\par
the left.\par
\par
He raised himself, craning his neck, and through the low wind screen\par
behind him, against the background of a sky all flaming and boiling with\par
molten gold and liquid amber, he saw the wide square shoulders and tall\par
helmeted head of von Herrnung, the hard eyes staring unflinchingly\par
through their round glass goggles, the mouth set in a straight\par
inflexible line under the tight red roll of the moustache.\par
\par
The red-moustached mouth opened, and von Herrnung shouted something.\par
Nothing reached the boy but a sort of muffled roar.  He shook his head\par
vigorously, and then--one does not wear the Signaller's Badge for\par
nothing!--released a stiff little gloved hand from its grip on the\par
arm-rest, and rapped out with his clenched right fist on the edge of the\par
fuselage:\par
\par
"_I--can't--hear!_"\par
\par
The Code was understood.  The helmeted head, some four feet distant,\par
nodded.  One of von Herrnung's gauntleted hands freed itself from the\par
steering-bar.  Its knuckles drubbed out the question:\par
\par
"Have you the brown satchel?"\par
\par
Bawne had quite forgotten the brown satchel.  He screwed back his head\par
and looked down and there it was, lying on the numb knees of him,\par
buckled to him by the tough strap of pigskin that held him in his seat.\par
He nodded assent, and signalled:\par
\par
"All right!"\par
\par
"Good!" von Herrnung signalled back through the hurly-burly of the\par
Bird's transit.  Bawne mustered courage to knock out:\par
\par
"Where are we?  When shall we go down?"\par
\par
Von Herrnung's right hand lifted itself, and described a sweeping\par
half-circle.  The brusque gesture answered Bawne's first question,\par
bidding him look and see.\par
\par
The boy, impeded in his view by reason of his small proportions,\par
wriggled in his straps so as to get his chin well over the gunwale of\par
the Bird's fuselage and the buffetting wind that was dug up and spaded\par
over her bows by the dizzying revolutions of the tractor, got hold of\par
him and pummelled and buffetted him again.  Her course was still north,\par
the sun was setting in great smoking lakes of gold and sulphur on her\par
left as she flew.  Thick patches of dark green bushes that probably were\par
woods, reddish-green blotches that might be heathy commons, shiny,\par
square patches that he guessed at as reservoirs, toybox villages that\par
were thriving suburban boroughs, specks that were villas, glittering\par
ribbons that suggested canals, and one broad shiny stripe that was a\par
river with tiny boats upon it, were swirling from right to left,\par
sweeping along in the opposite direction, under the rushing body of the\par
winged thing that bore him, ruled by the hand of von Herrnung upon the\par
steering-wheel.\par
\par
Behind her a chaotic, formless greyness brooded on the horizon,\par
innumerable spires rose out of it and a glittering haze hung over all.\par
That was London, the great grimy Mother of Cities tearing away from her\par
little son at eighty miles an hour.  The shriek of an engine and the\par
rumble of a train reduced by distance to infinite tenuity pulled the\par
boy's eyes downwards.  A weeny mechanical toy that meant one of the\par
double-humped colossi of steam traction, dragging a string of match-box\par
goods trucks, raced another locomotive, towing a crowded passenger-train\par
neck and neck along the spider-fine perspective of gossamers that meant\par
the Great Eastern Railway.  Now fear was swamped in the sheer joy of the\par
experience.  This thin air that kept you perpetually gulping and\par
swallowing saliva, made you feel more than ever how good it is to be\par
alive.\par
\par
Billows and billows of green, interspersed with patches of purple\par
heather, meant Epping Forest, though he did not know it.  A great\par
aggregation of grey walls and housetops, looking like a section of an\par
old wasp's nest, stood for Waltham Abbey as the Bird drove on.  Quite a\par
tangle of the shiny grey-blue streaks that were rivers meant Lea and\par
Orwell, Ouse, and their trouty tributaries.  East England rolled away\par
underneath like an endless carpet woven in irregular patches of many\par
hues.  Green and brown, grey and yellow, and innumerable shades of\par
these, so tempting in their suggestions of good things to eat that a\par
most unheroic hunger reminded the schoolboy of tea-time, hours and hours\par
gone by.\par
\par
He looked round in search of von Herrnung, who maintained unchanged the\par
same attitude, his shoulders level, his unseen hands steady as rock upon\par
the wheel of the steering-pillar, his mouth shut tightly, his hard eyes\par
ranging ahead or lowered, as he conned his course in masterly fashion by\par
aid of the roller-map, protected by its transparent, rainproof casing,\par
or the compass, clock, altimeter, and other instruments gimballed in the\par
wooden frame in front of the pilot's seat.\par
\par
"How long?" the small fist rapped out.  Von Herrnung detached a hand and\par
signalled in answer:\par
\par
"One hour!"\par
\par
"When do we go home?"\par
\par
"We go home now!" the hand signalled, and the boy settled down in his\par
seat to wait.\par
\par
\par
Between hunger and weariness he dozed, and soon slept soundly, his hands\par
hanging laxly over the leather arm-rests and his head nodding over the\par
brown satchel lying on his knees.  It figured in his dreams as something\par
huge, oppressive and uncanny, that suddenly took to itself malevolent\par
life, spread a pair of wide leathery bat-wings, and would have flown\par
away but that he gripped it fast.\par
\par
"No, no!  You shan't!  I promised!" he heard himself crying, and\par
suddenly the thing collapsed limply in his grasp and became nothing but\par
a satchel, and he was awake. Awake and very stiff and rather sick and\par
sleepy, and with the salt smell in his nostrils and the salt taste in\par
his mouth that meant--that could only mean the Sea.\par
\par
He looked over the gunwale and cried out in astonishment. For a vast\par
carpet of rounded woolly-grey-white clouds lay spread beneath.  The\par
carpet beginning to rise and the cockpit floor to incline downwards, a\par
thin clammy fog suddenly blotted out everything.  The Bird had dived\par
through a field of woolpack mixed with ground-fog.  Now flying some\par
hundred feet beneath it, she regained her level, in the clear light\par
stained by the sunset as water in which a dash of red wine is mingled,\par
the light that is the aftermath of a radiant summer's day.  And, with\par
the smell of the sea sharper in his nostrils, the boy became aware of\par
moving, muddy-grey water, with ships and boats and steamers on it, far\par
down below.\par
\par
Now the southerly breeze that had steadily tagged on some twenty-three\par
miles an hour to the Bird's eighty odd, began to veer and come in\par
strengthening puffs and gusts from the north-west.  Swirling eddies of\par
air came upwards from the water, rocking the machine as a swell takes a\par
boat at sea, and splashed upon the frail, silk-covered wings of the\par
aeroplane in deluges of invisible spray.\par
\par
On the right hand and the left were wide stretches of muddy grey salt\par
water, banks of sand, and drain-piped foreshore merging in patches of\par
potato and swede and yellow squares of unripe corn.  Clusters of white\par
dots, where shingle and sea-walls bordered the drab, restless water,\par
were fishing hamlets, villages and little coal-port towns. Upon the\par
north bank, rapidly receding in distance, could be dimly sensed, beyond\par
a dense fringe of masts standing close as pins in rows upon a\par
pincushion, the oblongs and squares and rectilinears of docks and\par
shipyards, stone quays, and piers and tide-basins, mixed up with blocks\par
and streets of sheds and warehouses, stations and goods-yards, and huge,\par
many windowed factories, whose towering chimneys yet belched forth thick\par
black smoke-gouts, licked by red tongues of flame.  Though even if the\par
Saturday noon steam-siren had not silenced the throbbing of pneumatic\par
rivetting-hammers and the roaring of steam coal-shoots, hydraulic grain\par
dischargers and oil-pumps, and all the hellish hubbub accompanying the\par
huge export and import trade of Yorkshire and Lancashire with North\par
Europe and the Continent, these sounds would not have reached the ears\par
of the boy in the aeroplane save as a dull and muffled murmur, vaguely\par
sensed, through the musical moaning of the stay-wires and the racket of\par
the tractor-screw.\par
\par
Now the sunset was behind.  The land was rushing back upon the right and\par
left-hand.  The two-mile-wide river was broadening to a great estuary,\par
vaster than the Thames, between Fort Victoria and Shoeburyness.\par
\par
Long crawling strings of linked-up barges, sailing vessels of the old\par
windjammer type and yachts of the latest rig, battered tramp and collier\par
steamers, high-sided rusty looking oil-tankers, pilot-cutters,\par
coastguard motor-launches, whole fleets of steam-trawlers, thrashed up\par
and down its broad south side fairways or cannily negotiated the\par
treacherous channels of the north bank.  Ocean-going giants of the\par
Merchant Service, flaunting the White Bordered Jack, or the Red Duster,\par
or under Admiralty Warrant, displaying the Blue Ensign.  Behemoths of\par
the North Sea passenger-service showing the three-striped merchant-flag\par
of Germany--or the tricolour of the Netherlands, or the Crosses of\par
Norway, Sweden and Denmark--with more rarely some big grey armoured\par
cruiser upon harbour and Coastal Defence Service, or a brace of stumpy,\par
square-ended patrol-boats, or a trio of the stinging black hornets we\par
have learnt to call torpedo-boat destroyers, ranging in company upon\par
some business of the Powers that order Britannia's naval affairs.\par
\par
Fascinating, wonderful to look down upon.  Alike, however diverse in\par
size, shape or uses, in the impression of flat unsubstantiality conveyed\par
to you--together with the doubt that the emmets crawling upon them could\par
possibly be life-sized men.  A drifting daisy-petal meant a smart\par
private steam-yacht.  You looked down from two thousand feet above, on\par
the open-lidded snuffboxes that signified the fire-control and\par
signalling-stations of some Leviathan of the Home Fleet, and a string of\par
black holes jabbed in an oval of floating white millboard represented\par
her funnels, black discs or white alternately stood for her ventilators;\par
and her imposing deckworks, her turrets or barbettes, her gun-houses and\par
casemates, and the terrible monsters bloodthirstily nosing out of them,\par
were reduced to a more or less symmetrical arrangement in thick or thin\par
black lines.\par
\par
The rosy light was greying.  The gusts came more fitfully. To the south,\par
upon the right hand, were stone-built fortifications with black muzzles\par
of big guns poking from the ramparts, over stretches of salty marsh,\par
drab-coloured mud-flats, and slimy rocks covered with blackened seaweed,\par
sticking up from pale silvery sand-shoals, licked by the restless white\par
tongues of the outgoing tide, and bumped by stranding buoys.  Black dots\par
and grey dots wheeled and scurried and settled.  Crows and gulls were\par
feeding ravenously as the tide drew off the flats and sand-shoals.  And\par
by the queer sensation in his empty stomach, Bawne knew that he too was\par
ravenous.\par
\par
From the beaconed north shore of the vast estuary basin, edged now by\par
low rambling cliffs, and belts of shingle and sand, a long curving\par
headland with two lighthouses at the crook-end, rushed now towards the\par
Bird at what seemed the speed of an express train.  Bawne winced as the\par
tall granite towers, topped with helmet-shaped domes of rust-red iron,\par
rose up like twin giants threatening to destroy.  An iron balcony with a\par
flagstaff and signal-mast ringed the base of each dome-top, a stairway\par
spiralled round each shaft to a railed stone platform well above\par
high-water mark.  And a shrimp-sized man in a red guernsey waved a speck\par
of blue handkerchief, and bellowed a disproportionately loud greeting\par
through what was presumably a megaphone.  In reality the\par
lighthouse-keeper was indicating the M. O. cone storm-signal which hung\par
point downwards from the west end of the yard-arm, presaging a\par
south-west or north-westerly gale. Whether or no this warning was lost\par
upon von Herrnung, proof of its value followed.  For a great upleaping\par
billow of brine-tasting wind caught the Bird as she flashed past the\par
twin lighthouses upon the headland, tossing her upwards like a withered\par
leaf.  And a curved iron shutter in the nearer of the two rust-red\par
dome-tops rolled down exactly as the nictitating membrane of a bird's\par
eye does--and with a wink of glass from the prismatic reflector, a broad\par
triple beam of blinding-white acetylene light leaped north, east and\par
south.  In the same instant upon each side of the flashing tractor, the\par
boy sensed a vast, shimmering, liquid restlessness.  Here was the Sea,\par
the very Sea.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXIII*\par
\par
                        *BAWNE LEARNS THE TRUTH*\par
\par
\par
Something in the blood of the child answered to the call of the Ancient\par
Mother.  He cried out, half in terror, half in delight, and the cockpit\par
tilted so suddenly that he was violently jerked against the seat-back\par
and the canvas bulkhead behind him.  Looking up he saw a large old moon\par
of luminous yellow, sailing away overhead through a sky all shot with\par
pink and grey as though hollowed out of a fire-opal. The Bird was\par
rushing through space at ninety miles an hour, and great lumps of cold\par
salt wind splashed over Bawne and took his breath away, and his hands\par
were numbed with bitter cold and his legs were legs of ice.\par
\par
So brave a spirit dwelt in his little breast, that the sob that heaved\par
it and the tears that stung his eyelids and dimmed his goggles, were\par
swallowed and blinked away as soon as shed.  The cockpit became level,\par
and there was an imperious rapping behind him, on the upper canvas deck.\par
He turned his head and met the hard unflinching stare of von Herrnung,\par
who held in the hand with which he had rapped a bitten piece of\par
chocolate.  Still munching he signalled:\par
\par
"Hungry?"\par
\par
He smiled grimly as the boy nodded in the affirmative, stuffed the bit\par
of sweetstuff into his mouth, produced from its cache below the level of\par
the upper deck another square of chocolate, tore off the silver foil\par
with his teeth, and crunched it greedily.\par
\par
He smiled, because of a queer tickling pleasure he felt as he did this,\par
akin to the sensation experienced when his taunts had tortured Patrine.\par
"Take care of my dearest!" he fancied he could hear her saying....  Not\par
until she had committed herself to that incautious utterance, had he,\par
von Herrnung, realised what rich vengeance on the desired, hated woman\par
might be wreaked by the simple act of carrying off the boy, whom he had\par
regarded until then as a mere bag of ballast; less useful, but certain\par
to prove less troublesome, than the Cockney-tongued Welshman, who might\par
or might not carry a cheap revolver in the hip-picket under his overalls\par
with which to enforce his protest against being taken away.\par
\par
Von Herrnung was himself armed with a Browning automatic pistol.  A\par
deadly shot, he would have been capable of dealing with half a dozen\par
Davises upon the solid ground. But, no lover of avoidable risks, he saw\par
himself steering with one hand and shooting with the other, while Davis\par
sat astride the chair in the observer's cockpit, and argued with an\par
eighteen-and-sixpenny Birmingham four-chamber, loaded with the cheap\par
little cordite cartridges, whose pea-sized bullet can kill a fine big\par
man.\par
\par
"What is this?  You are sick?"\par
\par
Even while keeping his ears open and his eyes skinned, as he negotiated\par
the Bird through a choppy cross-current, conning his course between the\par
compass and the roller-chart-map, now illuminated by an electric bulb,\par
his great shoulders shook with merriment as he saw the boy's head sink\par
helplessly against the side of the fuselage, and his small body\par
convulsed by throes of the sickness that is indistinguishable from the\par
dismal malady of the sea.  He had shut off the engine to shout to him.\par
And in the sudden cessation of the tractor's racket, the deep organ note\par
of the waters rolled in upon the hearing, mingled with the shrill piping\par
of the wires and the ruffle of the freshening wind.  As he switched on\par
power once more, the broad white ray from the Bull Light leaped forth\par
again and caught them as it ran eastwards over the tumbling\par
white-crested billows, flinging a huge shadow of von Herrnung over the\par
canvas-covered space of deck before him and showing him to the\par
white-faced boy who had twisted round once more to look at him, as a\par
featureless human torso shaped out of solid ebony with diamond specks\par
for eyes and gleams of grinning ivory teeth.\par
\par
"When are we going home?  Why are we over the sea now?"\par
\par
Von Herrnung shut off again for the luxury of hearing and answering:\par
\par
"I have told you because we are going home.  Our home is--Germany.  You\par
will not be an English boy but German, once I have got you there!"\par
\par
The shrill cry of anger that came from the open mouth of the white face\par
was lost to him in the necessity of switching on the engine.  He nodded\par
pleasantly to the white face and, in the darkness of his own shadowy\par
visage, there was the glimmer of a laugh.  Then he applied himself to\par
other business, for the tide would turn in an hour, and then the wind\par
might blow hellishly from the nor'-west.  Flying lower, he knew his\par
course the true one, for the white headlight and green starboard-lights\par
of a big steamer pricked twinkling holes in the thick grey dusk to\par
northward on his port beam.  He told himself she was one of the Elbe\par
Company's big bluff-bowed liners making from Newcastle for Hamburg\par
Docks.  The stern-lights of a sister-ship hailing from Grimsby, by her\par
steerings, were also discernible in the mirk ahead, while the lights\par
from her tiers of cabins made her look like a black water-beetle with\par
golden legs, hurriedly scuttling over the sea.  Following the course of\par
the Hamburg-bound liners, even if one failed to make connection with\par
one's accredited pilot, it would not be long before one picked up Borkum\par
Riff Lightship and in due course, spiring silver grey against the\par
pink-and-golden sunrise--the twin towers of Nordeich Wireless--marking\par
the journey's end.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXIV*\par
\par
                          *THE BROWN SATCHEL*\par
\par
\par
The journey's end.  A gust, tearing the mist that veiled the livid\par
waters, showed the shadowy shapes of a procession of battleships,\par
steaming southwards in single line.\par
\par
You see the German assailed by the wind, now hard on the aeroplane's\par
port beam, craning over, counting the speedlights passing diagonally\par
underneath.  Eight steel Leviathans, stabbing bright points of electric\par
light through fog and funnel-smoke, with an effect of diamonds seen\par
against a background of dull grey plush.\par
\par
Eight rushing, neutral-tinted shapes--conveying a formidable impression\par
of grim power, and force, and ruthlessness. A Squadron of Battle\par
Cruisers of the British Home Fleet, new from the brine of Lerwick\par
Waters, or the fierce green surges of Scapa Flow.  Bound for Harwich\par
Roads or Sheerness, or the Solent, to figure in the huge pageant of\par
steel and steam, electricity, and man-power that would be called the\par
King's Review.\par
\par
What a chance, supposing _Der Tag_ were come already, for the delivery\par
of a consignment of bombs!  It warmed like a draught of wine, to think\par
of the devastating effect of a couple of such German love-gifts,\par
exploded in the bowels of one of those steel monsters, packed with\par
complex machinery, high explosives, and inflammable oil.  True, there\par
might be a reverse to the medal, damping even to the spirits of a\par
Superman.  Wireless signals would go forth at the order of one amongst a\par
little knot of dark figures on the forebridge of the Flagship, warning\par
each of those grey monsters of its danger.  Not an armoured cruiser\par
scouting for them on the horizon, not one of all the torpedo-boat\par
destroyers in their vicinity, not a submarine nosing in the thick cold\par
darkness below the restless white crests, but would join in the man-hunt\par
that must ensue.\par
\par
How the dusk would spring alive with the eyes of foes, and long rays of\par
searchlight would go probing, and the mobile noses of guns great and\par
lesser would be thrust from their hoods of proof-armour, sniffing\par
bloodthirstily for the enemy up in the sky.  While from the Flagship's\par
mothering side, a Navy seaplane, armed with a Vickers' machine-gun,\par
might swing out and plop upon the water, rise from the white snarl of\par
waves with a vicious scream of her propeller, and, keen as a\par
gull-hunting sea-hawk, launch herself in chase.\par
\par
_Pfui_!  The thought made one sick at the stomach.  Cold, isolation, and\par
darkness tried a man, no matter how courageous. Buffeted by the bitter\par
wind, aching and stiff with weariness, lonely with the loneliness of\par
some small bird of the migratory order, outstripped by its companions on\par
the wild journey over the North Sea, the Kaiser's messenger drew energy\par
and cheer from the conviction that the dispatches entrusted to him by\par
Imperial favour were such as would hasten the arrival of The Day.\par
\par
The Day, to which all good German officers devoted the second toast on\par
Mess nights.  When the Black Eagle would swoop, and the nodding\par
witch-hag Britannia would awaken from her whisky-dreams of\par
World-Dominion to find her armour obsolete, her sword rusted in its\par
scabbard, the trident of Sea Power stolen from her hand.\par
\par
Hurrah! for The Day when the programme arranged by the All Highest War\par
Lord and his War Chiefs should be carried out in the complete overthrow\par
of British Supremacy, the seizure and domination of British territory,\par
the solution of the Great German Race Problem, in the transformation of\par
the United Kingdom into a German dependency,--the annexation of India\par
and the British Colonies--and the forcible Teutonisation of the hated\par
race.\par
\par
Aha!  Much to be locked in an Imperial messenger's letter-bag, thought\par
von Herrnung, greedily.  What in the way of guerdon might not be\par
lavished by a gratified All Highest upon the danger-braving and\par
to-duty-fearlessly-devoted Flying Officer who should accomplish the\par
Secret Mission, and lay the brown satchel at the Imperial feet.\par
\par
Probably the Second--tchah!--the First Class of the Iron Cross--with\par
military promotion, and a handsome sum in hard cash.  Laudatory articles\par
in the State-inspired Press organs and Service Gazettes presently.\par
Meanwhile, was it fitting that the future of von Herrnung should lie,\par
not upon the knees of the gods, but on the lap of a little, seasick\par
English boy?\par
\par
True, the brown satchel was firmly strapped to the boy, now lying in an\par
attitude of complete exhaustion, with one arm thrown over the gunwale,\par
and his small round head feebly nodding to and fro.  The child knew\par
nothing of the Imperial dispatches.  And yet--one would have been wiser\par
to keep the bag about one, in spite of the danger of fouling the\par
controls.\par
\par
It will be gathered that a chilly premonition of imminent disaster\par
crawled in the veins of the Kaiser's messenger. Hunger and fatigue were\par
spurring von Herrnung to imaginativeness unworthy of a Superman.\par
\par
Now he knew his frail winged craft beset by cunning, treacherous\par
enemies; the invisible air that cradled and supported her, only waiting\par
to destroy.  Other elemental forces, Gale, Lightning, Hail,\par
Waterspout--in collusion to bring about her swift and speedy ruin.  The\par
Sea, no less than these, was an implacable adversary, reaching up\par
innumerable greedy hands to drag her down and drown. The hawk-hoverer\par
would have been a help at this juncture if one had had some previous\par
experience in the use of it. As things were, it was wiser to leave the\par
Englishman's invention alone.  A labouring beat admonished the man's\par
quick ear of impending engine-trouble.  Ah, if the motor, that was the\par
living heart in the aeroplane, should break down at this juncture, or\par
the human intelligence perched behind the roaring tractor falter, the\par
game was up.  Kaput for von Herrnung, he very well knew.\par
\par
As though the very fear had brought on the catastrophe, the revolutions\par
dropped.  Below 1000, said the indicator's trembling finger, and there\par
was a miss.  The bang!--bang! of a back-fire followed.  If one had\par
believed in God, now, this would have been the time to pray to Him.\par
\par
But now the aviator's keen eye, peering downwards through Sherbrand's\par
binoculars, picked up something that had emerged with a sudden yeasty\par
swirl among the white-crested waves.  No handsomer nor bigger than an\par
under-sized steam-trawler, the casual observer might as such have\par
accepted her.  But a moment more, and fore and aft of the stocky little\par
pseudo-steamer, stretched the long snaky, whitey-brown hull of a\par
submarine.\par
\par
U-18, on observation-service off Spurn Head, or a Britisher?  An Evans\par
signalling-pistol, loaded, and with a supply of spare rockets, was fixed\par
in a cleat beside the instrument-board, within reach of the pilot's\par
hand.  The altimeter, illuminated by the electric bulb, gave an altitude\par
of six hundred, as von Herrnung snatched the pistol, and fired, aiming\par
towards the sky.\par
\par
The shot was followed by a second detonation, and a brilliant crimson\par
light illuminated the grey welter, throwing up orange balls of fire as\par
it ascended, to burst in showers of incandescent sparks.  Switching off,\par
von Herrnung strained both ears and eyes for an answer to his signal.\par
With the cessation of the motor the diapason of the North Sea rolled\par
upwards through the twilight with a threatening of storm. As the\par
weather-cone had presaged, a gale was coming.  It blew strongly from the\par
north-west.  The engine back-fired again, and von Herrnung swore at it,\par
trying to make out the nationality of the submarine running on the\par
surface six hundred feet below.  There were half-a-dozen tallish figures\par
on the narrow man-railed catwalk running along her hull forward, and one\par
upon the screened-in platform of her humpy conning-tower.\par
\par
Then the blue-white ray of a searchlight leaped forth illuminating her\par
bows and forward torpedo-tubes--revealing the long neutral-coloured hull\par
with the Wireless mast raised for use and soapy seas hissing off the\par
armour-plate. A backwash of brilliance picked out the\par
black-white-and-red Jack of Germany, fluttering from a short pole-mast\par
sternwards.  Signal-lights of white and two colours broke out upon\par
another slender mast aft of her conning-tower, and winked and jabbered.\par
U-18 was in touch with her man.\par
\par
It was quite time, for the Bird's engine hiccupped more and more\par
disastrously, and her pilot's frozen hands could only guess the\par
steering-wheel.  He grunted relief. _Sapperlot_!  One's star had not\par
deserted one.  Once more the Prussian Field-Flying Service would, with\par
reason, quote von Herrnung's hellish good-luck.\par
\par
Meanwhile the submarine's three lights chattered volubly in German Navy\par
Code.  Do Not Attempt Make Harbour. Heavy Weather Coming.  Original\par
Orders Cancelled. Heave To.  Will Stand By To Take You Aboard.  To which\par
von Herrnung, keeping pace with U-18, replied with long and short\par
flashes of an electric signalling-torch. Understood!  What Is the Sea\par
Like?  Keep Off and On.  Am Coming Down!\par
\par
And he came forthwith.  The Commander of U-18, standing on the little\par
platform over which furious seas were slashing, watched him critically\par
through a pair of Zeiss binoculars.  You, too, are asked to see him;\par
pulling round the Bird's head into the teeth of the nor'wester; shutting\par
off her hiccupping engine, implacably thrusting her nose seawards, and\par
diving with a splendid swoop into the widening paths of spirals that\par
ended amidst the angry surges below.\par
\par
Hitting the North Sea with so shattering a slap that the Bird's\par
landing-carriage crumpled and buckled, and the frail spars of her wings\par
crunched like the bones of a small bird in the jaws of a hungry cat.\par
\par
A fierce green sea leaped, towered, and broke, dumping a ton of water on\par
von Herrnung, and knocking the breath out of the man.  He tore open the\par
safety-belt as consciousness left him, and recovered in the warm\par
benzine-flavoured stuffiness of the officer's cabin aboard the U-18, to\par
the stinging of schnapps in his mouth and gullet, and the cheer of\par
German words in his ear.\par
\par
"Hey now, hey now, we are coming about.  That is well! Drink another\par
draught, comrade!  You have had a hellishly narrow squeak.  Another\par
time, when flying oversea with dispatches, start early, pick your\par
weather, and ship a life-belt, if you are wise!"\par
\par
Thus Lieutenant Commander Luttha of Undersea-boat No. 18.  You see him\par
as a spare, weather-bitten, black-bearded officer in a full panoply of\par
yellow oilies, and a sou'wester shading little eyes, sharp as\par
lancet-points and now twinkling with his bit of fun.\par
\par
But the word "dispatches," coupled with the jest about the life-belt,\par
volted through von Herrnung like the discharge from an electric battery.\par
He gulped and choked, collecting enough tinned air to talk with, and at\par
last got out:\par
\par
"The boy--the boy, with the satchel!  Where is he, in the devil's name?"\par
\par
Thus adjured the Commander answered pithily:\par
\par
"If you mean the half-drowned little English rat Petty Officer Stoll\par
found washing about in the bows of your aviatik, he's alive.  Don't\par
worry about that!"\par
\par
Through the churning foam upon his lips, von Herrnung spluttered\par
furiously:\par
\par
"_Himmelkreuezbombenelement_!  What is the _verdammt_ boy to me?  It is\par
the satchel that was strapped about the boy's middle I am asking\par
for--the Emperor's--_Herr Gott!_--I shall go mad!"\par
\par
He staggered to his feet, hitting his head a stunning crack against the\par
low white painted overdeck.  The incautious reference to the Emperor\par
electrified those who heard, squatting on the little folding bunks, or\par
kneeling on the palpitating deck of the little officer's cabin, into\par
desperate activity.  Von Herrnung found himself boosted up a ladder and\par
through a manhole, guided along a narrow slippery catwalk, washed by the\par
surges of the North Sea, to where a collapsible boat was being emptied\par
of a lot of shipped salt water, and the battered wreck of the Bird of\par
War, lashed to the U-18's forward man-rail, was waiting the Commander's\par
order to be finally abandoned to her fate.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XXXV*\par
\par
                           *NUMBER EIGHTEEN*\par
\par
\par
They launched the collapsible, and ransacked every cranny of the Bird's\par
waterlogged fuselage.  Not the ghost of a brown leather satchel rewarded\par
their feverish search. In the forward cockpit the belt swung loose, the\par
patent fastening had been opened by pulling the pin out.  Clearly the\par
boy had released himself when the Bird hit the sea.\par
\par
"Let us go look at this boy!" suggested the Commander, on receiving the\par
news that the Kind had breathed, and vomited sea-water.  Luttha promptly\par
led the way to the men's cabin, where Petty Officer Stoll and an\par
earringed first-class seaman were working over a little limp naked body,\par
outspread on the jiggetting deck-plates, in the raucous glare of the\par
electric light.\par
\par
Bawne was questioned, but nothing could be got out of him just then,\par
except North Sea, so they wrapped him in a blue Navy blanket, and left\par
him in charge of Petty Officer Stoll.\par
\par
"This is hellishly unfortunate, you must know, Count," said the\par
Commander, alone with von Herrnung in the vibrating steel box over the\par
upper accumulators, called the officers' cabin, and separated from the\par
men's quarters by a paper-thin sliding bulkhead of painted steel.  You\par
are asked to consider it furnished with seven narrow folding bunks, a\par
trestle-table about as wide and long as a coffin-lid, some folding\par
chairs, a marvellous array of charts on spring-rollers, fixed against\par
the steel walls, a row of wooden lockers, a chronometer and auxiliary\par
gyro-compass, several cylinders of oxylithe for respiratory emergencies,\par
an electric stove of small size, a log-book and writing materials, a\par
shelf of German literature, chiefly nautical reference-books; sets of\par
dominoes, a violin and a cornet, speaking-tubes and a telephone, a\par
gramophone and a giant cuspidor.\par
\par
Von Herrnung, having swapped his water-logged flying-kit and soaked\par
underclothes for dry flannels lent by the Second-in-Command, topped off\par
with a pair of the Commander's spare trousers, and a guernsey frock\par
belonging to the biggest man on board.  You can see him supplementing\par
the shortness of the trousers with a pair of long sea-boots: thrusting\par
his huge arms into the guernsey, beginning already to be superior to his\par
rescuers upon the strength of his family rank and wealth and his\par
flying-record, his bulk and handsomeness, and his magpie pearl.  He was\par
of the Prussian top-dog breed and let others know it, even whilst\par
smarting under his loss.  That he felt it was shown by the livid pallor\par
testifying to mental disquiet and physical exhaustion.  But he judged it\par
wisest to bluff, and did.\par
\par
"The cursed machine would have drowned me if you had not arrived in the\par
nick of time," he said suggestively, smiling under the red moustache\par
that hung uncurled over his full sensual lips: "Suppose you say you\par
found me swimming in the water--the aeroplane having foundered--it is\par
merely rewording a report!"\par
\par
"So many thanks!" ... returned the Commander, chewing hard at an\par
unlighted cigar, sending a jet of saliva into the cuspidor, and smiling\par
in a wry and dubious fashion. "But when I said things were hellishly\par
unfortunate, I meant unfortunate for you!"\par
\par
He moved to the green baize-covered plank that served as a cabin table,\par
and took from a weighted document-file a pencilled paper-slip.\par
\par
"As far as they concern you I will read you them as taken down by our\par
Wireless operator.  'To Undersea-boat No. 18, on observation-duty off\par
Spurn Head.  Stand by to get in touch with, act pilot, and render aid if\par
necessary to German Imperial Secret Service Messenger, crossing to\par
Nordeich in British aeroplane.'  The message comes from the German\par
Embassy in London and the sender is Grand Admiral Prinz Heinrich.  I\par
have carried out my instructions to the letter. There is only _one_ man\par
going to be broken over this affair!"\par
\par
Von Herrnung knew who the man was.  The Commander chewed some more of\par
his cigar, picked his oozing yellow oilskins off the deck, thrust\par
himself into them, crowned himself with his sou'wester, and said, taking\par
a farewell shot at the cuspidor:\par
\par
"And to brew more thunder-beer for you is not my desire! I am sorry for\par
you, _bei Gott_!  But to make game of those who command me is not the\par
purpose for which I am commissioned, Herr Count.  Nor have I any\par
experience in doctoring reports.  I rate only as Lieutenant in the\par
Imperial German Navy--a man born of plain people--without fortune or\par
even _von_ before my family name!"\par
\par
Von Herrnung sensed that he had bitterly offended the only human being\par
who could help him.  He apologised subserviently, and catching at the\par
straw afforded him by the Commander's admission of poverty, offered him\par
the pickings of the wrecked aeroplane.\par
\par
"For her instruments and signalling outfit--the seats and vacuum flasks\par
even--are well worth the having, and her engine and tractor will sell\par
for----" he named the sum in marks.  "There is a patent stabiliser under\par
her belly that I reserve for Majesty--the French have bought it or think\par
they have!"\par
\par
The speaker rubbed his hands.  The hoverer might yet prove a sop for the\par
All Highest.  Imperial displeasure thus averted, all would go well.  He\par
added, feeling that he might actually afford the luxury of grumbling:\par
\par
"As for me, I am what the English call 'fed up' with special missions.\par
Conceive it.  I am at a Hendon Flying School,--chatting with a handsome\par
Englishwoman who has taken me for her lover--as I am waiting to get an\par
inkling of the sort of invention the French War Ministry think worth\par
buying for use in their Service Aeronautique.  I am summoned by a groom\par
of our Embassy to speak to some Excellencies--I follow and find myself\par
clicking my heels before Prinz Heinrich, von Moltke, and Krupp von\par
Bohlen in an Embassy auto-car--to be sent off at a moment's notice in a\par
little cranky devil of an English monoplane--with secret dispatches for\par
the All Highest--on a journey over the North Sea.  With the barometer\par
falling and the hour past five meridian.  That's my luck!"  The speaker\par
paused for breath.\par
\par
Luttha said, pulling his black beard through his fingers with a crisp\par
sound, a trick of his when in meditation:\par
\par
"There was no time to lose.  And you have a wonderful record for\par
long-distance flying.  And luck it was!--if you had been of my mind.\par
Tell me, did not _they_ give you plain instructions?"\par
\par
"Do 'they' ever speak plainly?" von Herrnung scoffed; and Luttha\par
answered calmly:\par
\par
"Yes, to an ordinary man, who does not understand obscure language, they\par
would have said: 'Lieutenant Commander Luttha, here is a brown leather\par
satchel, with something inside it belonging to the Emperor.  You will\par
convey the satchel to Nordeich and deliver it to His Majesty's hands.\par
And from the moment I entrust it to yours, it shall be close as your\par
very skin to you.  If you meet Death upon your errand, die with it next\par
your heart!'"\par
\par
The speaker added with a wounding accent of irony:\par
\par
"Perhaps that marks the difference between a plebeian and a nobleman!  I\par
would have lashed it to my body, under my clothing.  You strapped it\par
about the boy!  By the way, what is the boy?"\par
\par
"The boy! ... Nothing! ... A piece of ballast, merely!"\par
\par
Von Herrnung, warmed by dry clothes and exhibitions of schnapps, was\par
fast recovering his characteristic arrogance. He added, with a shrug and\par
a wave of the hand:\par
\par
"As for the lost satchel, it may well be that duplicates of the\par
dispatches contained in it have been sent to the Emperor by another\par
messenger.  That is the usual method, perhaps you are not aware?"\par
\par
"Duplicates exist, but in only one place on earth will you find them,\par
and that place is the London War Office!"\par
\par
The Commander pitched his cigar-butt into the cuspidor, snapped the\par
three stud-clips that secured his yellow oilskin storm-coat, and dug his\par
piercing little eyes into von Herrnung's as he asked:\par
\par
"Have you never heard of the War-engine of Robert Foulis, the Scottish\par
sea-captain who first suggested to the British the use of steam as\par
applied to battle-ships, and invented the screw-propeller and the big\par
devil knows how many other things besides the mysterious, secret weapon\par
that Great Britain has kept hidden up her sleeve a hundred and\par
twenty-six years!  It was offered by Foulis, then Earl of Clanronald, in\par
1812, to the British Government, and it frightened people like the\par
drunken Regent and the Duke of York and Lord Mulgrave into refusing it.\par
It was offered again to their War Office at the time of their Crimean\par
War,--taken into consideration by the Duke of Newcastle and again\par
ejected,--because--_Grosse Gott!_--it was too inhuman! As though a\par
weapon that could end a War in a twinkling by sheer deadly effectiveness\par
could be anything but a boon to mankind.  _Pfui_!  Such hypocrisy makes\par
me vomit worse than thirty hours of submergence.  Not because of its\par
inhumanity has Britain stored up the old man's war-engine. Out of\par
diplomacy, to brutalise the great Germanic nation into subservience\par
under the rod of Fear!"\par
\par
Luttha and von Herrnung, otherwise antagonistic, were alike in their\par
rabid hatred of Great Britain.  Luttha had talked himself plum-coloured\par
and hoarse by now, but he went on, pounding the air with a knotty,\par
clenched fist:\par
\par
"Thus it was well done on the part of the Kaiser's secret agents to\par
steal Clanronald's War Plan, on the brink of The Day to which we have\par
drunk so long!  Not the duplicates buried in the Whitehall\par
strong-vaults, see you!--but the originals from the muniment-room of the\par
Welsh castle, the country-seat of the present Earl.  Less than an hour\par
after you took flight from Hendon, London was alive and buzzing with the\par
tale! ... How do I know? ... Does not a man know everything with\par
Wireless?  And you, with no inkling that you carried for\par
Germany--Victory in the World-War that is coming--you who have lost\par
Clanronald's secret, are a ruined man, _bei Gott_!"\par
\par
He added, as von Herrnung broke out cursing and raving:\par
\par
"As I have said, I pity you!--though you have tried to bribe me!--but it\par
will not do to talk of suicide, for I shall prevent that!  Your\par
cartridges are wetted--your revolver will not serve you.  And you will\par
not get a chance to drown yourself, for I am going to submerge.  My\par
fellows have got the flying-motor out of the stirrups and stowed it\par
away, with the auto-hoverer and the other things for the Emperor, whose\par
property they are!  Then we run, only periscopes showing, for the Gat of\par
Norderney.  There is a clear-dredged channel to Nordeich Harbour,\par
navigable in any tide.  You have to account there to the All Highest for\par
the satchel, or I, _bei Gott!_ must account to him for it and you!"\par
\par
And Luttha slid back the steel door, passed through the narrow gangway\par
and shot up the narrow steel ladder to attend to affairs on deck.  Two\par
of his subordinates instantly replaced him.  On no account was von\par
Herrnung, the living proof of the Commander's fidelity to his\par
instructions, to be left alone, you understand.\par
\par
One would have said the Superman believed in God, he blasphemed Him so\par
industriously.  When he was quite spent and voiceless, the lieutenants\par
offered him practical sympathy in the shape of gingerbread and lager\par
beer.  He accepted the beer, and sat on one of the sofas drinking it and\par
brooding lividly, while Undersea-boat No. 18, with hermetically-sealed\par
hatches, folded down her signal and Wireless masts, shut off her 2000\par
h.p. Diesel oil engines, sucked water into her ballast-tanks, and with\par
only her periscopes showing above the surface, ran under her\par
electric-motor power for Norderney Gat and Nordeich quay.\par
\par
Behind her as she sped, a red stain upon the angry waters gave back the\par
last rays of stormy sunset, smouldering out behind bars of drift-wrack,\par
beyond the bleak east-country beaches and the long blue-black, desolate\par
worlds.\par
\par
Von Herrnung's private, personal sun was setting somewhat after the same\par
fashion, amidst sable clouds of Imperial wrath.  It was to sink below\par
the horizon in deepest disfavour, rise again in The Day's gory dawning,\par
and fall, its evil fires quenched in a drenching rain of blood.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXVI*\par
\par
                             *HUE AND CRY*\par
\par
\par
Even as petrol and air mingled in the Bird's cylinders, and Davis\par
rotated the tractor and nimbly leaped out of the way of sudden death,\par
the buff broadsheets of the _Evening Wire_ edged the kerbs of Fleet\par
Street and ran up Kingsway to High Holborn.  And from Ludgate Hill to\par
Charing Cross, Pall Mall, and Piccadilly Circus, the raucous voices of\par
newsboys yelled through a pelting hail of pence:\par
\par
                   AMAZING THEFT OF A FAMILY SECRET.\par
                        STOLEN FROM GWYLL CASTLE\par
                        THE CLANRONALD WAR-PLAN.\par
                        AN ECHO OF CRIMEAN DAYS.\par
                     THIEF KNOWN.  POLICE SANGUINE.\par
              "COMMON CRACKSMAN'S ENTERPRISE OR DIPLOMATIC\par
                                STROKE?"\par
\par
\par
Strings of news-carts laden with bundles of papers were rattling east,\par
north, south, and west.  Trains were taking in the story by bales of\par
thousands and disgorging it at every stoppage, as Von Herrnung opened\par
the throttle, and the Bird raced a hundred yards or so, bumping like a\par
taxi going over a bad road, then rose into the air, as gracefully as a\par
mallard, and launched upon the first wide spirals of the aerial ascent.\par
\par
The small audience interested in the aeroplane, her freight, and her\par
behaviour, watched her as she dwindled in the sight and died upon the\par
ear.  The spectators in the enclosure had departed in dribbles, the last\par
three-seater air-bus had rounded the aerodrome, landed and deposited the\par
last passengers.  Two or three over-enthusiastic students lingered, but\par
the rest had shed their grimy overalls and betaken themselves home.\par
\par
The mellow light of late afternoon lay sweetly on the wide expanse of\par
treeless greensward and on the woods that tufted the horizon-line.\par
Rooks and starlings were wheeling over distant tree-clumps, the bands no\par
longer brayed or tootled, the mechanics were leaving the sheds and\par
hangars, the waitresses were hastening to other employments, such as\par
programme-vending at suburban music-halls and picture-theatres, the\par
selling of stale _boutonnieres_ about the entrances of restaurants, the\par
serving of drinks and suppers at night-clubs and so on.\par
\par
On the verge of the white-marked oval from which the Bird had taken her\par
departure, Saxham was standing with Patrine.  Their faces were lifted to\par
the sky as they talked together, and Sherbrand's eyes were irresistibly\par
drawn to them, so heroic in mould, and so curiously alike.\par
\par
There was a puzzled line between the Instructor's thick, fair eyebrows.\par
He was ready to swear it was the same girl. But the face that had looked\par
into his that night in Paris was somehow softer, younger....  It was not\par
only the alteration in the colour of the hair....  If you had taken the\par
big, hearty, smiling young woman of the Milles Plaisirs, and dipped her\par
into a vat of hydrogen peroxide, so that not only her hair but her whole\par
body had been bleached, you would not have accomplished such a\par
transformation--unless the chemical had possessed the power to change\par
the colour of her mind and soul.\par
\par
The girl of the Milles Plaisirs had looked at you frankly, and spoken to\par
you like a pal.  In that atmosphere of sexual excitement, amongst those\par
crowds of men and women, flushed with meat and wine and the desire of\par
sensual pleasure, she had appealed to Sherbrand like a heather-scented\par
breeze from the North.\par
\par
Beautiful and big and sisterly, she had seemed to him who had no\par
sisters.  He had often wondered how she came to be in that place.  But\par
it had never occurred to him to lump her with the ordinary\par
pleasure-seeker.  He had read--more correctly than von Herrnung, who\par
believed her from the first to have bitten deep into the Fruit of\par
Knowledge--Purity if not ignorance, in her wide curving smile, and\par
honesty in her clear unshadowed eyes.\par
\par
What eyes they were, long, brilliant, blackly-lashed, browny-green as\par
agate.  What a wonderful voice came out of the depths of her splendid\par
chest.  The arch of her breastbone reminded you of a violoncello.  How\par
splendidly her head was set upon its column of warm, living ivory!  Her\par
firm round chin had a dint in it that the old Greek sculptor had failed\par
to bestow upon the glorious Venus de Melos, the Lady of the Isle of\par
Music.  Everything about her was planned on the scale of magnificence.\par
Six feet tall, she walked the earth like a goddess, or as women must\par
have walked when the Sons of Light mated with the daughters of men.\par
\par
Thus Sherbrand, meditating on his Fate to be, while Destiny limped\par
towards him in the person of an undersized telegraph-clerk whose\par
complexion, previously pallid, had deteriorated to dirty green.  He\par
began, extending a shaky hand, from which dangled a slip of limp paper:\par
\par
"For you, sir.  Rumball 'adn't got a picklock among his tools, so 'e\par
burst in the door with a No. 10 spanner.  They rung us up about twenty\par
times while he was at the job. And the message is important, sir!"\par
\par
"I'll see!  Thank you, Burgin!"\par
\par
Sherbrand took the telegram from the jerky hand and read:\par
\par
"_Your--German--acquaintance--suspected--agent--\par
robbery--documents--national--importance.  At--all--\par
costs--keep--him--until--I--come._"\par
\par
The Chief's name at the end was the nail that clinched the thing.  But\par
the cry of Macrombie's undersized assistant was the hammer-blow that\par
drove the nail to the quick.  His sharp eye, following the climbing\par
aeroplane, had seen her flatten and swing about and leap forwards,\par
exactly as the carrier-pigeon strikes out its line of flight for home.\par
\par
"My Gawd," he yelped out.  "See there!  Blimy, if the --'s not done us!\par
Bunked it by air to Kaiserland while I was spellin' out the screed.\par
Gone with the Bird--the Bird and the 'overing gear.  My Gawd!  Wot's to\par
be done?"\par
\par
"Shut your head on what you know!" said Sherbrand's voice in the pale\par
clerk's ear as Sherbrand's hand fell ungently on his shoulder.  "You've\par
done your best!  It's not your fault if luck was on the other side!\par
But--"  His eyes went to the Doctor's great figure standing beside the\par
tall white shape with the hat of twinkling silver.  "But the boy!"  A\par
sickness swirled up in him and a dizziness overtopped it.  He caught at\par
and gripped the clerk's thin shoulder to keep himself upright.  "My God!\par
How shall I break it to the Doctor," Sherbrand asked himself, "if that\par
German fellow has carried off the boy?"\par
\par
"Steady-O!  Ketch on to me, sir....  Nobody's looking!" said the\par
telegraph clerk.  He was a hero-worshipper on a robust scale and\par
Sherbrand his chosen deity.  "This ain't our young Boss givin' in, but\par
just his empty inside playin' tricks on him," he assured himself.  To\par
Sherbrand he said humbly: "If you'd come over to the cabin there's hot\par
cocoa and toke there.  Grub'll steady you, if you'll excuse me taking\par
the liberty of saying so--and you can't do nothing till he comes!"\par
\par
The person to whom Burgin referred had passed the entrance-gates, almost\par
before the sentence left the lips of the clerk.  Now his alert, upright\par
figure came in sight, briskly turning the corner of the restaurant, and\par
wrought to the point of ironic merriment by the greatness of the blow\par
that had fallen on him, Sherbrand shook off his dizziness and faintness,\par
straightened his tall body, clapped both hands to his mouth, and gave\par
the huntsman's view-halloo:\par
\par
"_Stole away!  Stole--awa-aay!_"\par
\par
Small cause for mirth, and yet he laughed, pointing to the dwindling\par
speck high upon the north horizon that represented the worldly prospects\par
of Sherbrand, and a handsome sum in cash.  The Bird, just then entering\par
a broad belt of gold-white mackerel-cloud, was lost to view in another\par
instant.  But the Chief had wheeled upon the pointing gesture, and seen,\par
and understood.\par
\par
Then he was upon them, saying in accents jarred with anger:\par
\par
"How was this allowed to happen?  You were warned. You had my wire?"\par
\par
Sherbrand's mouth was wrung awry with another spasm of mirthless\par
laughter.  He fought it back and held out the crumpled slip of paper,\par
saying:\par
\par
"I did, but luck was on _his_ side.  Thanks to a relapse on Macrombie's\par
part, I got this after the Bird had flown."\par
\par
"The Bird..."\par
\par
The blue-grey eyes and the keen hazel met, and struck a spark between\par
them.\par
\par
"'The Bird.'  He has taken French leave--or, more appropriately,\par
German--by the help of your machine?"\par
\par
Sherbrand nodded, setting his teeth grimly.  The wailing voice of the\par
pallid clerk came in like a refrain:\par
\par
"'Ooked it.  Bunked--so 'elp me Jimmy Johnson! With our young guv'nor's\par
mono', and the gyro 'overer!"\par
\par
Said the Chief, moving sharply towards where the Wireless mast straddled\par
over the telegraph-cabin:\par
\par
"He has adopted the only means of exit by which it was possible for him\par
to escape.  All railways stations are being watched, all highways\par
patrolled by our agents, travelling in high-powered motor-cars.  We are\par
on the look-out for him at every ocean shipping-port.  One road we left\par
open, not having the means to block it--and that is the road of the\par
stork and the swan!  Decidedly, I might have guessed that he would play\par
Young Lochinvar after this fashion.  But until I left the ground an hour\par
ago I did not know of the theft of the Clanronald Plan."\par
\par
"The Clanronald--" Sherbrand was beginning, when the Chief cut him\par
short.\par
\par
"I had forgotten that you are as little wise as I was an hour back.\par
Better glance at this paragraph while I make use of your O. T.\par
installation and Wireless, and put the fear of Heaven into Macrombie,\par
incidentally and by the way."\par
\par
He thrust a tightly-folded copy of the _Evening Wire_ upon Sherbrand and\par
vanished into the rum-flavoured stuffiness of the cabin, with the pallid\par
telegraph clerk close upon his heels.  And upon Sherbrand, in the act of\par
unfolding the newspaper, rushed his Fate, in a hat of silver spangles:\par
challenging the knowledge in him with blazing eyes well upon the level\par
of his own.\par
\par
"Mr. Sherbrand....  Tell me what has happened? Why do you look so--queer\par
and--white?"\par
\par
She herself was whiter than her narrow dress, and the mouth the eager\par
rush of words poured from was pale under its rose-tinted salve.  She\par
hurried on breathlessly:\par
\par
"They show no signs of coming back--it fidgets me horribly.  And--I was\par
looking--from over there, where I was with Uncle Owen,--when you called\par
out, 'Stole away!' and waved your arm."  She glanced at the sky,\par
shuddered and looked back at him.  "Am I silly?  But all the same, the\par
General told you something!  I don't ask what!  But I funk--I don't know\par
why, but it's beastly--the sensation! Tell me I've nothing to be afraid\par
of--I swear I'll take your word!"\par
\par
That she was just then a creature full of fears was written large upon\par
her.  She might have quoted Queen Constance, who I think was also a\par
galumpher, meaning a woman of big build and sweeping gestures, and an\par
imperious temper withal.  Sherbrand feared also, and the pang of\par
solicitude for the pretty boy so unexpectedly dragged into the vortex of\par
a diplomatic and political felony was, to do him credit, quite as sharp\par
as the pang caused him by the rape of the Bird.\par
\par
He answered:\par
\par
"Miss Saxham, I do not believe that there is any danger of an accident.\par
But--that there will be delay--I shall not try to disguise.  The fact\par
is----"\par
\par
A guttural, Teutonic voice said close at Sherbrand's shoulder.\par
\par
"_Gnaediges Fraeulein_ will wish to return home?  It is getting late, so\par
very late!  I haf instructions from my master to drive the _Fraeulein_\par
back to her address."\par
\par
Sherbrand wheeled, to be confronted by the thickset figure of the\par
moustached and uniformed attendant who had occupied the seat beside the\par
chauffeur of the big blue F.I.A.T. car.\par
\par
"Who is this?" he demanded in a look, and Patrine, her pallor drowned in\par
a scarlet blush of horrible embarrassment, stammered:\par
\par
"I really--haven't the least idea!"\par
\par
"You hear!"  Sherbrand's tone was not pleasant.  "The lady does not know\par
you--that ought to be enough!"\par
\par
Patrine felt herself drowning in chill waves of horror. The man\par
persisted:\par
\par
"The lady is a friend of the gentleman who brought her here....  I haf\par
my orders to drive the lady home in the yellow car!"\par
\par
In his muddy eyes there flickered a leer or a menace. Patrine saw the\par
Doctor coming and flew to his side. Sherbrand said, looking sternly at\par
the German:\par
\par
"You understand, your orders are nothing to the lady. She does not\par
choose to be driven home by you!"\par
\par
The man protested:\par
\par
"But my master----"\par
\par
Sherbrand demanded:\par
\par
"Who is your master?"  Then a sudden light dawned upon him, and he\par
turned and knocked sharply at the cabin-door. At which the liveried\par
attendant, as a man who finds hesitancy a double-edged weapon, wheeled\par
in military fashion and retreated, casting a surly glance over his\par
shoulder, and quickening his heavy footsteps to a jog-trot as the\par
General's active person appeared at Sherbrand's side.\par
\par
"That man, Sir Roland!"  Sherbrand's slight gesture indicated the\par
thickset figure now getting hurriedly into the yellow Darracq.  He\par
added, as the car swirled round the corner of the restaurant and\par
vanished in the direction of the entrance-gates, "Ought I to have\par
grabbed the brute, and hung on to him?  He was certainly with a party of\par
foreign-looking people, who interviewed von Herrnung just before he got\par
away.  You saw them?"\par
\par
"I certainly saw them.  And I agree with you that their unexpected\par
appearance has had to do with their countryman's sudden departure," said\par
the Chief.  "But to grab an orderly of the German Embassy would be--only\par
less risky than grabbing a Kaiser's messenger, on suspicion of his\par
carrying stolen War Secrets in his official bag."\par
\par
"A Kaiser's messenger!" Sherbrand's mouth shaped a soundless whistle,\par
"Why, now I remember, he had a dispatch-case or valise with him.\par
Wouldn't hear of leaving it behind!"\par
\par
"I--daresay not," the Chief's dry smile commented.\par
\par
Sherbrand went on:\par
\par
"I developed muscle in persuading him to let it go in the observer's\par
cockpit for fear of it fouling the warping-controls. No wonder he stuck\par
to it.  War Secrets!"\par
\par
"It is plain you haven't glanced at the _Evening Wire_.  It tells the\par
story rather pithily, beginning with an outbreak of fire on Tuesday\par
night at Gwyll Castle, Denbigh, caused by a short-circuit in the\par
electric-lighting apparatus of the North Tower."\par
\par
He went on:\par
\par
"I waste no time telling you, for all that's possible has been done now\par
in setting our agents on the track of the flying thief!  The North Tower\par
at Gwyll holds the priceless Clanronald library, and the Muniment\par
Chamber, where they bottle up the original MSS. detailing the War Plan\par
of the old Earl.  The short-circuit that set up the blaze was--the kind\par
that any amateur can arrange for with rubber gloves, a pair of pliers\par
and a bit of soda-water wire."\par
\par
"Is it known who the amateur was?"\par
\par
"There is reason to suspect one Heir Rassing, an under-librarian of\par
German nationality, who behaved like a hero, according to the local Fire\par
Brigade!  He it was, who suggested--Clanronald being absent on a\par
yachting-cruise in the Fjords of Norway--that the contents of the\par
Muniment Chamber should be transferred to the strong-room in the\par
basement of the East Wing.  He superintended the removal, armed with\par
knowledge, enthusiasm, and a large-sized Webley Scott revolver, with\par
which he volunteered to keep solitary guard till morning, outside the\par
strong-room door!"\par
\par
"And when daylight came--" hinted Sherbrand.\par
\par
"It discovered the zealous Herr Rassing to be missing, and a\par
corresponding hiatus in the treasures of the Muniment Chamber.  Item, a\par
sharkskin case inlaid with ivory figures, Japanese, antique and\par
valuable,--containing the original diagrams--chemical _formulae_ and so\par
on--embodying the famous Plan."\par
\par
Sherbrand asked.\par
\par
"Was it as tremendous as they tell one?"\par
\par
The crisp voice answered:\par
\par
"Tremendous it not only was, but Is.  The most terrible and effective\par
method of annihilating an enemy, that has ever been conceived by the\par
brain of man."\par
\par
Sherbrand said, drawing a deep breath:\par
\par
"And that is what von Herrnung carried in the brown leather valise-thing\par
that he took away with my machine! Not that I trouble about the Bird.\par
She was old, and I've got the stuff to build a new one.  But my\par
patent--the hawk-hoverer--that's another pair of shoes!"\par
\par
"The hawk--!  Phee-eew!"\par
\par
The Chief whistled a rueful note and his keen eyes softened in sympathy:\par
\par
"I had forgotten your invention.  So von Herrnung has scooped for\par
Germany the gyroscopic hovering-apparatus that the French War Ministry\par
were proposing to buy. Now I understand the something about you that has\par
puzzled me.  You wear the look of a father, Sherbrand, bereaved of an\par
uncommonly promising son."\par
\par
Saxham's stern face rose up in Sherbrand's thought, stamped with that\par
look, and his throat contracted chokingly. The Chief asked:\par
\par
"What sort of man is the mechanic von Herrnung has commandeered?  A\par
fellow easy to bribe, or intimidate?  It would be worth while to know?"\par
\par
"It's a boy--not a man!" broke from Sherbrand, hurriedly and hoarsely.\par
"General, no more unlucky thing could have happened! ... Dr. Saxham's\par
twelve-year-old nipper took a tremendous shine to von Herrnung,\par
and--and--he's gone with him!  That's the news the Doctor's got to hear\par
by and by!"\par
\par
There was a silence.  The Chief's face was turned away. Then he said\par
quietly:\par
\par
"There was no question of 'a shine.'  My Scout was obeying an order.\par
His Chief Scout had said, 'Keep this man under observation; and if he\par
leaves the Flying Ground--follow him, if you can!"\par
\par
Sherbrand could not speak for pity of the small white face that had\par
grinned at him out of the clumsy woollen helmet. He understood now, that\par
when he had bent to strap the safety-belt about the little body swathed\par
in the flannel-lined pneumatic jacket, he had felt a terrified\par
child-heart bumpity-bumping under his hand.  And he struggled with his\par
grief and rage in silence, broken by an utterance from the other man.\par
\par
"So he followed him into the air, seeing no other course before him.  My\par
old friend Saxham has good reason to chortle over such a son.  I said\par
to-day, 'I am proud of my Scouts!'  Well, to-night I am ten times\par
prouder.  I shall tell the Doctor this--when I get a private word with\par
him--and wind up with: 'Thanks to Bawne!'"\par
\par
"Then the Doctor--" Sherbrand began, a weight lifting with the hope that\par
the news might not have to be broken:\par
\par
"The Doctor knew.  I had said to him, doggily: 'I'll give your pup a\par
fighting chance to prove his Saxham breed.'  It's a stark breed--hard as\par
granite, supple as incandescent lava,--with a strain of Berserk madness,\par
and a dash of Oriental fatalism.  They can hate magnificently and\par
forgive grandly, and love to the very verge of death."\par
\par
Could _she_, Sherbrand wondered, letting his eyes travel to the tall\par
white woman standing by the Doctor, as the Chief went over to them and\par
grasped his old friend's hand.  Then both men moved away across the\par
dusky ground together. Those words of thanks and praise were being\par
spoken. Coming from such a source they must be heartening to listen to.\par
But presently when their glow had paled and faded, and the boy did not\par
come back...\par
\par
Presently, when the empty chair and the vacant bed, and the little\par
garments hanging in the wardrobe should be filled and occupied and worn\par
only by a shadow-child wrought of lovely memories.  By and by, when the\par
silence in the house should clamour in the tortured ears of the woman\par
and the man...\par
\par
Then, Sherbrand knew no praise of their lost darling would console\par
Bawne's parents....  Dry-eyed they might smile until their lips cracked,\par
but their hidden hearts would weep.  Their tongues might be silent, but\par
their hearts would cry always; Did we wish our child to be heroic? Had\par
he been a craven we would have had him now beside us!  Give us our\par
living boy again!  O! keep your empty words!\par
\par
\par
A cry from Patrine prodded Sherbrand to active sympathy. So at last they\par
had told her.  She knew all.  And true to her type she was raging at the\par
Doctor and the Chief like a very termagant; upbraiding them with a spate\par
of words rushing over her writhing lips and lioness-frenzy in her\par
blazing eyes.\par
\par
"I begged you not to let him go!"  This was to the Doctor.  "Faint!  Do\par
you take me for a bally idiot--to faint when there's something to be\par
done!  Follow that man and get him back!  If he takes him away to\par
Germany--don't you know we shall never see Bawne again!  Oh! why--why\par
can't I make you understand!"\par
\par
The raging voice grew hoarse with sobs, though her furious eyes were dry\par
as enamel.  She added with an inflection that made Sherbrand blink and\par
gulp:\par
\par
"Don't you know--don't you _know_ it will kill Aunt Lynette?  And I\par
shall be guilty--I who love them so!  Oh, God, I must do something or\par
die raving mad!"\par
\par
The Doctor's great arm held her firmly round the body. Saxham was strong\par
as an oak-tree, but who can control a woman in the frenzy of hysteria,\par
standing six feet tall in high-heeled No. 7 shoes?  She wrestled and\par
fought, and her tawdry hat of silver spangles tumbled off, and her\par
superb hair shed its pins of tortoiseshell, and rolled, yellow-tawny as\par
a South African torrent in flood-time, down over her heaving shoulders,\par
over the supple back and writhing loins, reaching nearly to her knees.\par
Then her strength went from her, and her tears came.  She dropped into a\par
chair Sherbrand had got her, and crumpled up there, crying bitterly.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXVII*\par
\par
                          *PATRINE CONFESSES*\par
\par
\par
With her hat off and her hairpins out, and her tawny-coloured mane\par
tumbling over her heaving shoulders, the superb illusion of maturity\par
vanished.  The three men viewed Patrine with clear, unprejudiced eyes.\par
Stripped of the magic cloak of Circe, here was no transformer of Man\par
into the hoofed and rooting mammal, but a great galumphing schoolgirl,\par
pouring out a heartful of trouble, without the least concern for her\par
complexion; mopping her streaming eyes with a little sopping\par
handkerchief; temporarily ending its brief career of usefulness with a\par
dismal blast upon the nose.\par
\par
"Take mine!" said Saxham, thrusting the large-sized square of cambric\par
upon her.\par
\par
"Th--thank you, Uncle Owen!"\par
\par
She said it in the voice of a child.  The torrent of tears, so different\par
from those shed earlier, had washed her heart clean.  Something hard and\par
cynical and evil had passed out of her.  She was Bawne's dear Pat again.\par
\par
A lean brown hand that wore a chipped and ancient signet was next held\par
out to her.  She grasped it and was straightway hauled upon her feet.\par
\par
"Are you better?" said a friendly voice, in a crisp way.\par
\par
"I--think so.  Thank you, Sir Roland!"  She added in a tone as\par
tear-soaked as her handkerchief, while Saxham offered her her hat, and\par
Sherbrand tendered tortoiseshell hairpins:\par
\par
"I'm awfully afraid I have behaved like a fool!"\par
\par
"Like a woman!" said the friendly voice even more crisply.\par
\par
"Do you think women are fools?" she was beginning, when she caught his\par
eye and broke off.  For she had met Sir Roland's mother and she knew his\par
young wife quite well, and her Aunt Lynette, the one living being whom\par
she worshipped, was one of his closest friends.  No!  To this man women\par
were sacred.  Why had she uttered such a banality? For the life of her\par
she did not know.\par
\par
She drew a sobbing breath, and looked about her vaguely, and suddenly a\par
mist rolled away from her brain.  The net of Tragedy whirled high and\par
fell upon her, and the steel trident was driven deep between her ribs\par
again:\par
\par
"I--had forgotten!"  She stared upon them.  "What must you all think of\par
me?"\par
\par
Saxham's arm came round her, and Saxham's voice answered:\par
\par
"Nothing, my dear, but that you are human, and have had a tremendous\par
shock!"\par
\par
She leaned against the Doctor's great shoulder, sighing:\par
\par
"Thank you! ... I'm all right now!  Not going to cry any more....  But\par
Bawne!  If we wait long enough there will be news of him?  We--shall get\par
him back?"\par
\par
She felt Saxham's iron muscles jerk, and his ribs heave as though the\par
trident had found a home between them.  Perhaps he could not find his\par
voice, for it was the Chief who said:\par
\par
"We are doing everything possible.  Mr. Sherbrand is helping.  He has\par
been good enough to place the telegraph installation at our disposal and\par
the Wireless also.  A call, Burgin?"\par
\par
The undersized clerk had waved a hand from the threshold of the cabin.\par
The Chief vanished.  Patrine sighed:\par
\par
"Oh, if there should be news!"\par
\par
"You are too sensible to be bowled over if there happens to be no news,"\par
said the Doctor's voice.  But his arm was tense about her waist and she\par
felt the beating of his heart.\par
\par
"Uncle Owen!"\par
\par
Sherbrand had withdrawn out of earshot.  She squeezed the kind\par
responsive hand, turned her mouth towards the Doctor's ear, and\par
whispered tremulously:\par
\par
"Uncle Owen!  You don't know _him_ as I do.  That's why I am\par
so--horribly afraid for Bawne!  He would be cruel to anyone you liked,\par
if he hated you.  And he is furious with me!  I have thwarted him\par
in--something he wishes!  He is bad!--dangerous!--do you understand?"\par
\par
"He cannot be a bad pilot with such a record.  And in such calm weather\par
there is little danger of an accident.  We must be patient; there is\par
nothing else to do at the moment, but wait!"\par
\par
Saxham had feigned to misunderstand her, for very pity, you can\par
conceive.  Blurting out her miserable secret in this moment of unselfish\par
sorrow, his heart was wrung in him to an anguish of compassion for\par
Patrine.  But no less was he wrung by the truth her words conveyed.  His\par
son and Lynette's was in the power of an evil man!  What was David's\par
daughter saying?\par
\par
"Uncle Owen!"  The tall figure of Sherbrand had moved away into the\par
reddish twilight, and a wild desire of confession spurred on the girl to\par
desperate frankness of speech. She hurried on, nerving herself to the\par
change that would presently show in Saxham.  "Uncle Owen!  I think you\par
had better know!  Since I met _him_ in Paris I----"\par
\par
"Stop!" said Saxham.  But she would not stop.  She had his blood in her,\par
and went on, though to have set her naked foot on glowing iron would\par
have been easier than to tell.\par
\par
"I have flirted with him!--gone alone with him to restaurants and\par
music-halls!--let him take me to the Upas!"--there was a tightness like\par
knotted whipcord about her throat; "That's--not the worst!"\par
\par
"I guessed it.  Stop!" Saxham repeated:\par
\par
"Who told?"--she faltered brokenly, and shivered at the deep stern\par
whisper:\par
\par
"No one told, but the reputation of the--man is known to me.  His type\par
does not hesitate where a woman's virtue is concerned."\par
\par
A great sigh burst from her.  "And you can speak to me and touch me\par
kindly--you don't hate the sight of me?"\par
\par
"No, my poor girl, God forbid!"\par
\par
"How good!--" she began, broke off and said, shuddering: "But--Aunt\par
Lynette!  How could I bear it, if she were ever to know----"\par
\par
Saxham said harshly:\par
\par
"She shall not know!  Who do you dream will tell her? Not I!  So set\par
your mind at rest, my girl.  You are a girl--though you talk like a\par
woman of thirty!"\par
\par
She said with a miserable catch in her throat:\par
\par
"Nineteen _is_ rather young, isn't it?  Perhaps things would have been\par
different if only Dada had lived!"\par
\par
The utterance was as inapposite as it was sentimental. If David had\par
still been in existence his daughter would have had no less cause for\par
regret.  But Saxham, inwardly quivering and wrung with pity, could only\par
acquiesce:\par
\par
"Perhaps things would!  What you have got to do now is--Forget!  Do you\par
hear me?  I order you, and I will be obeyed!  And I will have you leave\par
this titled lady who employs you, and who is all kindness and no\par
discretion. Resign your post to-morrow!  You need not return to your\par
mother.  My house is your home!"  He went on in his rare tone of\par
tenderness, "You need no telling that I care for you as a daughter.\par
Come to me, and to Lynette who loves you dearly.  She will want\par
comfort--now that--"  His voice broke and his mouth twisted.  He fought\par
with his anguish, in silence, turning his grim white face away.\par
\par
"Who will tell Aunt Lynette?  Oh!  who will tell her?" he heard Patrine\par
whisper.  He commanded himself to answer:\par
\par
"For the present, I have telephoned her that we may be detained here\par
until late.  Suppose you twist up your hair now, and put your hat on.\par
Sherbrand!"\par
\par
A sweet, manly voice answered out of the dimness of the Flying Ground:\par
"Here, Doctor!  You called me?"\par
\par
In the madder and umber light of the dying sunset Sherbrand's tall brown\par
shape came towards them.  Saxham said as Patrine swept her tawny tresses\par
into one rough rope:\par
\par
"I am going to ask you to find out whether the people at the\par
refreshment-place could give my niece something by way of substitute for\par
dinner.  A cup of coffee, or cocoa with milk, a roll and butter, and a\par
slice of cold beef or ham?"\par
\par
Sherbrand said eagerly:\par
\par
"I am sure Miss Saxham can get anything like that. Mrs. Durrant keeps\par
open house till nine o'clock, or later, if there is reason.  She caters\par
for the School Staff, respectably, by contract.  I lodge--a very decent\par
berth--over the dining-room, where I have my grub.  Noisy by day but\par
quiet enough at night-time.  Will you come this way, Miss Saxham?  You\par
too, Doctor?"\par
\par
Saxham declined.  They left him standing there, in the wide expanse that\par
was filling up with brooding shadows, with his back to the dying rose of\par
the sunset, looking fixedly to the north.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                           *CHAPTER XXXVIII*\par
\par
                             *THE REBOUND*\par
\par
\par
Patrine, that magnificent animal, had passed unknowingly through the\par
painful ordeal which accompanies in the human the evolution of a soul.\par
No doubt she had had one before without suspecting it.  Now she was\par
conscious of the presence of the guest.\par
\par
Through the big barbaric halls of her nature, glittering with tinsel\par
over plaster backed with canvas, thronged with vanities, appetites,\par
desires, and ambitions, jostling at the glittering fountains, buying at\par
the tawdry counters, flocking to the dubious restaurants, swooping down\par
the water-chutes, wandering through the painted landscapes, drinking in\par
the dubious atmosphere, had passed a ray of light, pure, vivifying and\par
cleansing, had blown a breeze of crystal mountain air.  And through the\par
blare of brass a note had sounded that would never cease to vibrate in\par
Patrine's ears. Having partially confessed, she experienced a\par
disproportionate rebound of spirits.  Her fears for Bawne weighed on her\par
less heavily, Saxham's reference to cold ham had awakened in her the\par
pangs of healthy appetite.  The proximity of Sherbrand was a vividly\par
keen pleasure.  She had always wished for a brother, and here was the\par
very _beau ideal_ of one!  She meant to ask him if he had sisters--she\par
was sure they would be awfully nice girls!\par
\par
One or two electric lights were switched on in the big room full of\par
little white-covered tables, with the counter at the far end piled high\par
with thick white plates.  The big nickel urns were cold and empty, but\par
Mrs. Durrant, the stout and smiling proprietress of the restaurant,\par
produced hot coffee and milk in a twinkling, bread and butter, the cold\par
ham, and a cold pigeon-pie.\par
\par
With her own very fat, very pink hands Mrs. Durrant ministered, voluble\par
the while in sympathy....  The lady had been upset because the dear\par
little boy hadn't come back.  People were sometimes kept for hours\par
through a Loose Nut, or a Slack Wire, or a Carburetter, or some little\par
thing or another going wrong.\par
\par
"You remember when Under-Instructor Davis took Mr. Durrant for an Air\par
Beano all the way to Upavon, Mr. Sherbrand? Flares burning 'alfway\par
through the night, and pore me!--new to the Flying then--wasn't I, Mr.\par
Sherbrand?--going from one fit of astericks into another, and running\par
out to meet Durrant, when he dropped down calmly 'Ome at four in the\par
mornin', with my hair all untidy and hangin' about me--"  Patrine\par
swiftly put up a hand to assure herself that her own tawny coils were\par
securely fastened--"for all the world like an Indian Squawk."\par
\par
"Wives had their feelings, it was only to be expected," said Mrs.\par
Durrant.  Mothers had also theirs, and, that was natural too!  Patrine\par
found the idea of her own maternal relationship to Bawne so firmly fixed\par
in the mind of Mrs. Durrant, it was barely worth the trouble to\par
endeavour to explain it away.  Mrs. Durrant had none of her own, worse\par
luck! but here, just coming with the salad and some fried potatoes, was\par
Mr. Durrant's married niece, Ellen Agnes, and nobody knew better what it\par
was to lose a darling child.\par
\par
Ellen Agnes, wan-eyed, anaemic, slipshod, and overworked, supported the\par
statement.  Only in April it 'ad 'appened, and Ellen Agnes 'ad never\par
'eld 'er 'ead up properly since.  And little Elbert the 'ealthiest of\par
children. Rising three and never a nillness till the pewmonia carried\par
'im orf.  'Ad only 'ad 'im phortographed three days before it 'appened!\par
with 'is lovely little limbs and body naked, sitting on a fur rug, the\par
blessed dear!\par
\par
Ellen Agnes not appearing to recognise any connecting link between the\par
nude pose and the pneumonia, Patrine suppressed the obvious suggestion.\par
Both women meant well, but their talkative sympathy oppressed her.  She\par
imagined how, when Sherbrand ate alone, the stout aunt and the thin\par
niece would hover round his table, assailing his ears with their Cockney\par
voices, making their common, vulgar comments on the happenings of the\par
day.\par
\par
Perhaps her disrelish showed, for the kind women presently slackened\par
their attentions.  There was nothing then to divert Sherbrand's\par
attention from his guest, beyond the undeniable attractions of the\par
hastily spread board.\par
\par
So they ate the pie, all of it.  Patrine cried, in frank astonishment at\par
the evaporation of her second plateful:\par
\par
"But I am a wolf or something.  No!  Not even salad. What must you think\par
of me?  Crying my eyes out one minute and stodging pigeon-pie the next!\par
Do the rest of the friends you feed here behave as badly as that?"\par
\par
Sherbrand returned, ignoring the mention of other guests:\par
\par
"Now, what should I think?  Nothing but that you wanted something to\par
buck you, and I was pretty ravenous myself.  It was pretty parky up\par
there at 10,000."  He answered to her question how high that was: "Why,\par
comparatively, you might imagine it about nine times as high as the top\par
of St. Paul's Cross from the level of the ground."\par
\par
Little the speaker dreamed then of aerial battles to be fought at\par
20,000.  She asked whether he had "felt giddy" and he shook his head,\par
saying:\par
\par
"If I had felt inclined to giddiness I should have put off climbing\par
until I felt fitter.  I sympathise with Opera Stars who disappoint full\par
houses, because some high C or lower G is a hairsbreadth off the bull.\par
The singer can't afford a false note.  It's death to a reputation.  And\par
the Flying Man can't risk brain-swim, because it means possibly\par
nose-dive and smash.  So I stay out of my sky unless I'm sure of myself.\par
There's nothing on earth like being sure."\par
\par
He had a way of saying "my sky" that was queer and rather beautiful.\par
Just as though he had been a lark, occurred to Patrine.  And indeed, in\par
the beaky, jutting nose, and the full, bright eyes set forward and flush\par
with the wide orbital arches, there was some resemblance between the man\par
and the bird.\par
\par
Patrine sunned herself in the lighter moment.  She who had lain through\par
the night sleepless--had risen still a bond-slave--realized that her\par
fetters were broken now that her evil genius had flown.  Taking with him\par
her beloved, she fully believed in malice.  Piercing though that\par
knowledge was, it could not mar the blissful sense of freedom, mental\par
and physical.\par
\par
Bawne would be brought back.  Meanwhile, one's blood sang through one's\par
being, mere living was riotous ecstasy, mere breathing sheerest delight.\par
The joy of life radiated from her.  And to Sherbrand, sitting opposite\par
at the little coarse-clothed table, she grew momentarily more and more\par
like the girl of the Milles Plaisirs.\par
\par
True, instead of cloudy black, her hair vied in tone with the banner of\par
coppery flame that streams from the crater of an active volcano, or\par
burns above some giant crucible of molten metal ready to be poured\par
forth.  Her long eyes under her wide level brows looked the colour of\par
peat-water, in the electric light that contracted their pupils to\par
pin-heads, and brought out against the yellow-distempered walls the\par
creamy whiteness of her wonderful skin.  When she leaned her round\par
elbows on the table-cloth and smiled at him, it was the frank, generous\par
smile that had warmed his heart when he stood solitary and unfriended on\par
the rose-pink carpet near the gilt turnstile on the Upper Promenade.\par
\par
He would put it to the test.  He beckoned the pallid Ellen Agnes, asked\par
for the bill, slipped his hand into a breast-pocket and drew from it a\par
tiny white silk purse.\par
\par
"Oh!  You found ..."\par
\par
With an indescribable emotion, half pain, half pleasure, she saw her\par
missing property in the broad extended palm. He said:\par
\par
"It flashed on me, even as I blackguarded Davis, that you must have paid\par
that Commissionaire-fellow at the turnstile or he'd have been breathing\par
vengeance at my back.  So I ran back to find you and ask for an address\par
where I might send the money.  You were gone!  He had got this purse in\par
his hand.  So I--bluffed the brute for all I was worth, and got him to\par
give it me!--a stroke of luck--for I'd no money left to bribe him with!\par
Be kind and tell me how much you gave the fellow!"\par
\par
The deep dimple Sherbrand remembered showed in the full oval of one of\par
her white cheeks.  Slowly the pale rose-flush sweetened and warmed the\par
whiteness.  Her eyes were dusky stars under the barbaric wealth of\par
beech-leaf tresses. A slow smile curved her mouth, the scarlet lips\par
parted widely, showing two perfect rows of gleaming teeth.\par
\par
"Two half-jimmies!" said the rich, mellow woman's baritone.  Why did it\par
talk such awful slang?  "Half my screw for one whole week of\par
letter-writing, running errands, doing shopping, and generally\par
sheepdogging for my friend, Lady Beauvayse!"\par
\par
"Then please take this!"  This was a fat bright sovereign. "And be kind\par
and say that I may stick to the purse?"\par
\par
"If you care to--" Patrine began, dubiously.\par
\par
"I care--most awfully!"  He went on quickly.  "Lady Beauvayse--your\par
friend--I've seen her--if she's very pretty and tremendously American?"\par
\par
She nodded.\par
\par
"You've spotted her!  That's Lady Beau--the dear thing!  But she only\par
talks Yankee Doodle to bounders or fogies, or people who seem to expect\par
it from her.  Her English is as good as mine."\par
\par
"You don't mean it!"  His keen face crinkled with laughter.  She was\par
superbly unconscious of its cause.  He went on, rather ashamed of having\par
made fun of her: "That accounts for the Old Kent Road-_cum_-Whitechapel\par
I've heard from the august lips of British duchesses.  At\par
cricket-matches when Eton and Harrow were playing 'Varsity."\par
\par
"Does it?  I think not!  The duchesses weren't amusing themselves, or\par
trying to snub swankers.  They were just mothers--_real_ mothers--trying\par
to talk cricket to their boys.  And the boys--the sweets!--grinning up\par
their blessed young sleeves, and saying 'Yes'm!' and 'No'm!'  How I do\par
love boys!  Don't you?"  Her smile contracted with a spasm of anguish.\par
"And I'm sitting here, gobbling and gabbling, when my darling!--"  She\par
rose taller than ever, from the little table, caught up her feather\par
stole from a chairback near and slung it vigorously round her,\par
straightened the tinsel hat with a side-glance at the strip of a\par
looking-glass nailed in a frame of cheap gilt beading on the\par
matchboarded wall at her right hand, picked up the vanity-bag and the\par
long-sticked sunshade, and declared herself ready to go.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XXXIX*\par
\par
                           *A NIGHT IN JULY*\par
\par
\par
She reached the door before him.  He had turned to say considerately to\par
the good woman of the restaurant:\par
\par
"We shall be late....  Frightfully, I expect!  Promise me you won't sit\par
up!"\par
\par
"Oh! but I can't promise!  One never knows!  Best to have people up an'\par
ready when there might be need of 'em!" Patrine heard, as she wrenched\par
at the handle of the green curtained glass door.\par
\par
"No--no!  Let me!"\par
\par
His hand touched hers and she drew it away, not before a keen, sharp\par
thrill had traversed her.  "_Vile, hateful creature!_" she said to the\par
Patrine von Herrnung knew--the other woman within her, whom she loathed.\par
"_Is not it enough that you have done what you have done?_"  Then as she\par
passed out into the night, feeling beneath her feet the roughness of the\par
gravel walk that led between grass-plats studded with green painted\par
chairs and little iron tables, a strange roaring filled her ears and\par
hellish tongues of fire licked a sky of vivid blackness.  She recoiled,\par
saying in awed and shaken tones:\par
\par
"Why!  What has happened?  What does it mean? ... How horrible!"\par
\par
The door had shut behind them.  Now the round dome of the sky showed not\par
black, but velvety purple.  Away in the south-east a fierce red moon\par
drifted like some derelict vessel burning away to embers on a waveless\par
midnight sea. And sheaves of dazzling blue-white flames, leaping and\par
roaring, fenced in, or seemed to fence, a dreadful lake of Stygian\par
darkness, upon the surface of which figures--were they men or\par
devils?--moved....\par
\par
"Don't be scared, Miss Saxham!  It's nothing ... though I ought to have\par
wanted you...!"\par
\par
Not with intent, her heaving shoulder pressed against the breast of the\par
man who had followed her.  Perhaps the contact thrilled him, for his\par
voice was unsteady as he went on:\par
\par
"I was rather a brute to forget! ... It's a night-flare to\par
guide--possible home-comers! ... Wads of tow dipped in petrol, burning\par
in iron buckets round our landing-place.'\par
\par
"I ought to have guessed," she said ruefully.  "Forgive me for being\par
such an idiot!"\par
\par
His answer was unexpected.\par
\par
"On condition that you'll leave off saying 'Great Scott!' and things\par
like that."\par
\par
"All right!  But what's the matter with the expression, anyhow?" she\par
demanded.  "Do you always get riled when women use slang?"\par
\par
They had been standing within the gate that led upon the Flying Ground,\par
still girdled by its Valkyr-ring of leaping flame.  He said, holding\par
open the gate to let her pass through:\par
\par
"I use slang myself, habitually, like every other man I know.  But I\par
don't know a man who really likes to hear his wife or sweetheart copy\par
him in that respect.  For myself who have neither wife, sweetheart, nor\par
even sister, I can only say what I feel.  It is--that a beautiful woman\par
should use beautiful language.  One of the old Greek poets put the whole\par
thing into two lines.  I've forgotten the original, but the translation\par
runs like this:\par
\par
    "From the goddess the speech of Olympus,\par
    From the herd-maid the language of the cows."\par
\par
\par
"I'm no goddess, God knows!" said Patrine, sorrowfully and sincerely.\par
\par
Then a light scorching flame seemed to envelop her whole body.  She felt\par
Sherbrand's breath upon her cheek.... He said, speaking swiftly, and\par
close to her ear:\par
\par
"No, you are not a goddess, but something far better! You are a woman\par
one could worship!  You could hate magnificently and forgive greatly,\par
and love to the very verge of death!  That was said to me of the Doctor,\par
and you are like him!"\par
\par
"Don't!" she said, wincing.  "You don't know me!"\par
\par
He answered firmly:\par
\par
"But I do know you!  I knew you the moment I saw you in Paris.  You're\par
the girl I have been waiting for ever since I read Morris's\par
'Eredwellers'.  You're The Friend!  Now I've found you I shall never let\par
you go again!"\par
\par
What midsummer madness was this, prompting him to sweet audacity?  His,\par
"I shall never let you go!" had a convincing, manly ring.  She quickened\par
her steps, wading through a shallow sea of shadows, through which the\par
warm short turf came up to meet her feet.  He kept by her side, and\par
together they moved towards the Valkyr-ring of fire, changing as they\par
advanced into isolated pillars of towering flame outlining the huge\par
white oval of Fanshaw's landing-place. Here and there the goblin-like\par
shapes moved, stirring the flares with rods, feeding the blaze with\par
something from vessels they carried.  And two other figures stood in\par
talk by the telegraph-hut, recognisable, outlined against the oblong of\par
electric radiance framed by the doorway, as Saxham and the Chief.\par
\par
"This is a bit previous, you think?  Headlong--ill-considered on my\par
part--to have spoken like this to a girl I've only met once before?  You\par
must understand--a man who follows a risky profession gets into the way\par
of not waiting for to-morrow, because to-day may be the wind-up. Say you\par
are not angry!" Sherbrand pleaded.\par
\par
"No, you poor dear boy!  But you're so awfully mistaken!"  There was a\par
rich and exquisite tenderness, it seemed to Sherbrand, in the deep,\par
full, breathy tones. "I'm not a bit what you think me!  There is nothing\par
worthy of worship in a woman like me," said Patrine.\par
\par
He asked, as they walked side by side from patches of brilliant\par
blue-white light into deep oases of shadow:\par
\par
"May I say more?  May I tell you that I've thought of you ever since\par
that Paris night....  What things I've called myself--if you only\par
knew!--for not getting your address.  But I swore I'd find you somehow,\par
and I would have!  I'd know your voice among a thousand.  If I were\par
blind, and forgot other people's faces, I should always see yours\par
painted against the dark.  At night--now! when I shut my eyes ... there\par
it is!  You are not angry?"\par
\par
"No--I'm only sorry for you!" she said in her deepest, sweetest tone.\par
\par
"Sorry?"  There was keen anxiety in the face that was illuminated by the\par
petrol-flare they were passing.  "You're not--married--or going to be?"\par
he asked.\par
\par
"Neither!"\par
\par
"Thank God!" said Sherbrand simply and sincerely. "Now I'll go on!  My\par
rank bad luck gives me a kind of right.  This morning I got up solid in\par
the conviction that you and I were meant for one another; that we should\par
somehow be brought together; that the French Government would make it\par
possible for me to marry you by buying my hawk-hoverer--for with only\par
the two hundred a year my uncle left me, and the two hundred my\par
Instructorship here brings me--how could I possibly have the nerve to\par
ask you to be my wife?  And--"  He caught his breath, "And everything\par
I'd dreamed came real.  The test succeeded!  I dived down out of my sky\par
to find You!  Miracle of miracles. And not twenty minutes later--I found\par
myself nearly, if not quite--a ruined man.  For if my invention has been\par
swiped off to Germany, France will never buy, for money--what her\par
neighbour gets for nought!"\par
\par
"I understand.  My poor Flying Man, you've been plucked of some of your\par
wing-feathers!"\par
\par
"I don't care, if you'll wait for me until they grow again!"\par
\par
How grim a day had been followed by this night of wonder!  Woven of the\par
shining stuff of dreams it seemed, then and for long years after, to\par
Patrine.  Their intimacy grew and ripened like a magic beanstalk in the\par
light of the red moon and the fierce blue petrol-flares.  She said with\par
a catch in her breath--like Sherbrand's:\par
\par
"You must be serious!"\par
\par
"I never was more so!"\par
\par
She amended:\par
\par
"We must be sensible!  Oh! but this has been a close-packed day!"\par
\par
"Hasn't it!" Sherbrand agreed, as they moved on side by side, from\par
islands of raw, glaring light into broad pools of lustreless darkness,\par
their tall heads level, for Patrine carried her hat of silver spangles\par
swinging from the top of the sunshade with the lengthy stick.\par
"Sometimes, for weeks, the days slip by smoothly as the beads of a\par
Rosary over a baby's finger.  Then--bang-bang-bang! they explode--like a\par
rocket fired by a signal-pistol--until things fizzle out into dulness\par
again."\par
\par
"It's true!"  Her bosom rose in a sigh.  "But it's possible to get\par
awfully fed up with banging and fizzling.  One can learn to long--just\par
for a little dulness, as long as it means quiet and rest, and peace of\par
mind."\par
\par
That Patrine should voice such an aspiration was incredible even to the\par
speaker.  "_How changed I must be!_" she said to herself, as Sherbrand\par
answered her:\par
\par
"With heathery moors and towering scaurs, and galloping trout-rivers\par
brabbling over lichened boulders--and Somebody one loves to talk to--one\par
calls that kind of dulness a happy honeymoon!"\par
\par
She thrilled as his hand, swinging freely by his hip, touched hers,\par
lightly, enclosed, and then released it.  He was no tardy lover, this\par
Flying Man.  He knew a thousand times better than von Herrnung how a\par
girl should be courted and wooed.  For, with her heart in joyful tumult,\par
and her usually pale cheeks warmed and rosy with shy blushes, it was a\par
girl who walked beside Alan Sherbrand that night.  I am sorry she could\par
forget so easily the slip that had led her over the frontier line, the\par
Rubicon that can never be recrossed.  But in fact she did forget, just\par
as a young man would have forgotten.  Though she was to remember as only\par
a woman can remember, and to suffer as only a woman can.\par
\par
In the midst of the new, wonderful happiness, so strangely threaded not\par
only for Patrine, with bitter loss and tragic possibilities, she\par
suffered a quite intolerable twinge of memory in the sudden recollection\par
of the boldly-scrutinising look cast upon her by the bearded man in the\par
white Naval uniform.  She did not realise that an imperious gesture of\par
the brown hand, whose wrist had sported a massive gold watch-bracelet,\par
had whisked von Herrnung off the scene.  But she guessed that the huge\par
red-haired Prussian, bowing at the side of the big blue F.I.A.T., had\par
clicked his heels before a master who could break him at his will.\par
\par
He had boasted....  They _knew_!  Not only the bearded man whose look\par
had stung so, but the close-shaven old Colossus with the\par
tortoiseshell-mounted pince-nez on his thick heavy nose and the huge\par
wart on his yellow cheek. And the sallow diplomat in the Homburg hat\par
shadowing the sly glance and the moustache tucked up by a sinister smile\par
under his drooping Oriental nose.  They all knew.... Even the servant\par
had worn the leer that is born of knowledge, as he said in his Teutonic\par
gutturals:\par
\par
"The lady is a friend of the gentleman who brought her here..."\par
\par
Horrible!  But she would not remember.  She banished the hateful,\par
knowing faces with a gallant effort and turned to Sherbrand, asking\par
whether he had been an Eton, or Rugby, or Harrow boy?\par
\par
For had her Flying Man borne the cachet of the Public School Patrine\par
Saxham would have infinitely preferred it. That it is possible to be a\par
snob even in the most tragic or romantic moment of one's existence, she\par
had not realised before she discovered herself to herself in this way.\par
\par
"Downside was my school," he said quite proudly. Patrine had no\par
acquaintance with Downside.  "My father would have liked me to go to\par
Harrow; but my uncle--my mother's brother--who paid for my\par
education!--being a Catholic, naturally preferred the place where the\par
Faith was taught.  And my mother--as naturally--shared his preference.\par
I was happy at Downside.  The Fathers were thundering good to me.  I\par
worked hard--and I played hard--and when it wasn't Swot, or cricket, or\par
football, or fives, or boxing, it was the making of flying-sticks, just\par
shaved laths with paper wings, at first--and then a dodge much more\par
ambitious, a model Wright in varnished card, with a propeller worked by\par
a rubber release....  My father was pleased at my being a chip of the\par
old block in my turn for mechanics.  But when I wouldn't go up for\par
Woolwich--when I entered at Strongitharm's College of Engineering on\par
Tyneside, and spent two years at Folsom's Works at Sunderland--he rather\par
gave me up, I fancy, as a low-minded kind of cad."\par
\par
He shook himself as though to shake off the adverse paternal judgment.\par
\par
"I had my reasons for not going in for the Army, though I love it.  They\par
weren't easy to explain, and so I didn't try. But my father never liked\par
the idea of my being a civil engineer.  Even my mother, and my\par
uncle--dear old fellow--he understands me better now!"\par
\par
"Why?"\par
\par
"Because he's dead!" said Sherbrand simply, "and the Holy Souls know\par
everything!"\par
\par
"The Holy Souls?"  By the glare of the flare-light her puzzled eyes\par
questioned him.\par
\par
"The Holy Souls in Purgatory.  They're privileged to help us.  We help\par
them--by praying for them.  It's--a spiritual intercommunication--a kind\par
of endless chain. A circuit of influence, received and transmitted, not\par
by etheric flashes, but by a medium more subtle.  Prayer--in a word!"\par
\par
His bright-winged intellect had outstripped her heavier, duller\par
intelligence.  She suddenly felt like a caterpillar on a cabbage-leaf,\par
slow-moving, groping, but dimly conscious of a distant affinity with the\par
jewel-winged butterfly hovering high in golden air....\par
\par
"_Prayer_," she repeated dully, "do you believe in prayer?"\par
\par
"Naturally!" said Sherbrand--"since I believe in God. Do not you? ..."\par
\par
"I hardly----"\par
\par
In the ensuing pause Patrine had a brief retrospective vision of the\par
curate who had prepared her for Confirmation, and who had talked of the\par
Almighty as though He were a crotchety but benevolent old man.  And last\par
time she had been to Church--a fashionably attended High Church in the\par
West End--another curate in a black cassock and tufted biretta had\par
preached about the 'Par of Card, the baptismal dar of Grace, the bar of\par
flars,' in which our first parents dwelt in Eden, 'the fatal ar' in\par
which they sinned, and the 'shar of tars' with which Eve lamented her\par
fall.\par
\par
"No," she said bluntly, "I don't think I believe in God at all now,\par
though it sometimes seems as though there must be Somebody behind\par
things!--Somebody who punishes--Somebody who laughs!  As for a religion,\par
I don't suppose I've ever had one.  Oh, yes!--my religion is Aunt\par
Lynette!"\par
\par
A mental picture of Lynette, years ago in the Harley Street nursery,\par
teaching a curly-headed baby Bawne to say his evening prayer, while a\par
great galumphing girl stood in the doorway and looked and listened, rose\par
up and brought with it the horrible choking sensation.  She fought with\par
it as Sherbrand said:\par
\par
"I think you are speaking of Mrs. Saxham?  Well, one must have a star to\par
hitch one's waggon to.  And she is a star--if ever I saw one!  A woman\par
with a face like a Donatello Madonna, or a tall lily growing in the\par
garden-cloisters of some Italian mountain-convent, and who has the\par
Faith,--ought to be able to teach you to believe in God!  Why not ask\par
her?  I once knelt in a Church near her, and saw her praying.  She\par
seemed--very close to what Norman or someone else called the Eternal\par
Verities."\par
\par
"She will be nearer still," said Patrine with sudden, savage roughness,\par
"if anything happens--if Bawne is killed! She will die of a broken\par
heart!"\par
\par
"Then why not pray," argued Sherbrand, "that she may get him back again?\par
Why not try it?  There's nothing else that helps so well!"\par
\par
"Pray!"  The tall girl stopped short and swung round on him, facing him.\par
A moment since they had walked like lovers.  Now the spell was\par
broken--at all events, for the time.\par
\par
"Pray--pray!" she mocked.  "Am I a sneak?--to pray when I don't believe\par
in prayer!  And if I did believe, God--if He exists--would not hear me.\par
Even the parsons own He has His favourites.  I am not one of them....  I\par
am one of His forgets!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER XL*\par
\par
                         *MACROMBIE IS SACKED*\par
\par
\par
Tall, lithe, vigorous, masterful, they confronted each other across the\par
gulf that suddenly opened between them--the bottomless chasm that yawns\par
between Faith and Unbelief.\par
\par
In the fitful uncanny light, the darker side of Patrine started into\par
sinister prominence.  Her defiant face was masked by shadow, but the\par
fierce vibrating voice and towering shape had something of the fallen\par
angel.  Had wide sable pinions sprung and bannered from her shoulders,\par
Sherbrand would hardly have been surprised.\par
\par
"Let us draw the line at that.  If we are to be friends--and I would\par
like us to be!--agree to it!  But since you have what I have not--you\par
would call it Faith, no doubt," he guessed the wide mouth curving in a\par
jeering smile, "there is nothing to prevent _you_ from praying for Aunt\par
Lynette and for Bawne too!  Unless you are the kind of physician who\par
draws the line at taking his own drugs!"\par
\par
If she had thought to disconcert Sherbrand she erred. He said instantly:\par
\par
"I give you my word of Honour that I will pray for them! But there is\par
one other person much dearer to me than either. You don't ask me for\par
_her_, but all the same..."\par
\par
"You kind, dear boy!  Pray for me all you want to!"\par
\par
She was his big, smiling girl of the Milles Plaisirs, and the Pat young\par
Bawne worshipped, as she stretched out her beautiful, massive arm and\par
offered him a cordial hand.\par
\par
"Shake, Mister!  Making love to me one minute and bally-ragging me the\par
next! ... Great Scott!  Ah!--I've said it again--and I gave you my word\par
I'd not!"\par
\par
He took the hand in a close grasp, sought for the other and took it\par
also....\par
\par
"Thank you!  Why, how you're shivering!  You have nothing but that\par
feather thing over your thin gown!  Wait half a minute--I'll get you a\par
wrap!"\par
\par
He was gone in an instant, leaving her standing on the border-line of\par
one of the oases of black-velvet shadow, swayed by the violence of her\par
emotion as some tall young birch might have been shaken by the fury of a\par
south-west gale.\par
\par
His touch....  She had not dreamed....  Her head drooped, and a long\par
sigh went fluttering after him into the darkness, like some night-moth\par
whose wings are wrought of hues more gorgeous than the peacock\par
butterfly's, whose scent is on the alert, and whose diamond eyes pierce\par
the blackest midnight in search of the partner of its kind.\par
\par
\par
A footstep she knew approached.  A familiar voice called her:\par
\par
"Uncle Owen."  The spell broke.  Her mind leaped up alert and quivering.\par
"Have you any news--of Bawne?"\par
\par
"I have news!"\par
\par
"Not----"\par
\par
"Not the worst news," said Saxham's harsh voice, "but not--hopeful!"\par
\par
"They are not coming back?"  She strove to set her heel on the\par
treacherous hope that he would say No!  For how could she bring herself\par
to desire the enemy's return.  And yet the thought of Bawne was a stab\par
of anguish in her bosom.  What was the Doctor saying?\par
\par
"The last definite intelligence received of them confirms the certainty\par
that Captain von Herrnung is now over the North Sea.  He alighted\par
nowhere; that we have positively learned from many different\par
news-centres.  A tractor-monoplane answering to the description and\par
carrying two-passengers passed the Bull Light on Spurn Head, at a few\par
minutes before eight.  The lighthouse-keeper signalled that bad weather\par
might be expected.  The pilot paid no attention.  And later on----"\par
\par
As Saxham spoke, with that strange hoarseness, Patrine took his arm\par
tremblingly.  Her heart plunged as though it would burst its prison as\par
the Doctor went on:\par
\par
"An hour or more later a Wireless came in.  It had been sent on to Sir\par
Roland from the Admiralty!--I will not puzzle you with technical\par
details.  But at 8.30 the officer on duty on the upper-bridge of the\par
second-in-line of a Battle Squadron steaming through Northern Waters on\par
the way to a Southern rendezvous, reported having heard an aeroplane\par
pass overhead, crossing the course of the Squadron\par
diagonally--apparently flying due east----"\par
\par
Saxham added:\par
\par
"The aviator made no signal for assistance.  But the engine-beat told of\par
trouble developing....  There is nothing to do but wait and hope!"\par
\par
\par
What had really happened on board H.M.S. _Rigasamos_, maintaining her\par
appointed speed of fifteen knots, and her statutory two-cable-lengths\par
from the stern of the Flagship ahead, and the bows of the sister-ship\par
following her, had been that as the ship's band struck into _The Roast\par
Beef of Old England_, and the Owner took his place at the head of the\par
Ward-room mess-table, his Second in Command on the fore-bridge got a\par
speaking-tube message from the Navigating Lieutenant on the\par
upper-bridge, to say that the drone of an aeroplane, flying at about\par
four hundred overhead, had been picked up by Warrant Officer So-and-So,\par
of the gun and searchlight control, _per_ medium of the microphone.\par
\par
The Second in Command called back through the voice-tube:\par
\par
"An aeroplane....  You're sure?  Could hear her racket myself, without\par
assistance.  But put it down to a Fleet Seaplane taking a flip round the\par
Squadron for exercise, or one of the Goody-Two-Shoes from the R.N.A.S.\par
Station at Rosforth, blown out to sea doing Coast Patrol."\par
\par
An answer rumbled down the pipe:\par
\par
"It was an aero all right, sir!  The rattle of her floats 'ud have given\par
away a Goody....  Travelling east against the side-drive of a\par
forty-mile-an-hour north-west gale.... And with engine trouble well\par
developed.  Missing and back-firing like the gayest kind of hell!"\par
\par
The Second in Command took his ear from the mouth of the speaking-tube,\par
and with a glance that included the figures of his Sub-Lieutenant, the\par
Midshipman, signalmen, and lookouts at their posts swung into the\par
chart-house and logged the occurrence in the plain language of the sea.\par
The clock told 8.35 P.M. as he finished, capped his fountain pen, and\par
slipped it in an inside pocket, soliloquising:\par
\par
"Travelling east against a forty-mile-an-hour gale from the north-west,\par
and with engine-trouble to top up with ... Little Willie will be seeing\par
the angels pretty soon at this rate!  Or piling himself up somewhere on\par
the coast of Holland!  Wonder who the bally idiot is?"\par
\par
\par
Saxham continued, and now he croaked as hoarsely as a raven:\par
\par
"Sir Roland has little doubt that the aeroplane heard on the _Rigasamos_\par
was Sherbrand's 'Bird of War.'  If so, there would be very little hope\par
left, unless it had been previously arranged that a vessel belonging\par
to--a foreign Power!--was to watch for and give help if she should\par
require it.  Now you know as much as I do.  I have telephoned to both\par
Lady Beauvayse and your mother that you return with me to Harley Street.\par
We shall go presently.  First, I want you to speak on the telephone to\par
Lynette."\par
\par
"To--Lynette!" Patrine breathed.  The Doctor told her: "I have kept the\par
worst from Lynette hitherto.... I shall do so until the ultimate hope is\par
abandoned.  My wife knows my voice so well....  You understand.... She\par
would suspect something ..."\par
\par
His voice stumbled and broke.  And clinging to the arm of the big man\par
standing quietly beside her, potent in inertia as a lump of raw iron,\par
Patrine realised that her anguish was a drop in the ocean of his.  She\par
took his hand and said in a tone he had never before heard from her:\par
\par
"Come, dear!  We will go and speak to her now."\par
\par
\par
So they went across to the telegraph-cabin, raw with unshaded electric\par
light and littered with papers.  The Chief was there, looking livid and\par
careworn, leaning one elbow on the edge of the stand that supported the\par
Wireless, and wearing the telephone head-band with the ear-pieces, as he\par
dictated to the pallid clerk who occupied a Windsor chair at a stained\par
deal desk, and wrote with a spluttering pen on a depleted paper-pad.  At\par
first sight there seemed to be nothing else in the place but a low voice\par
speaking, a Railway Key instrument, a file for telegrams and an\par
overpowering odour of rum.\par
\par
The odour of rum consolidated to Patrine's view into a stocky thickset\par
man with a square heavy yellow face set into a tragic mask of despair.\par
It was Macrombie, ex-Petty Officer telegraphist, whom the Royal Navy had\par
spat forth for being D.O.D. fifteen full years before.  Sacked now from\par
his civil employment, for the old glaring, unblinkable offence.\par
\par
The liquor had barely faded out in him; his breath came across the\par
little cabin like a flaming sword, and his eyes under their beetling\par
coal-black eyebrows looked burnt-out. He rose from the debilitated\par
office-stool he had been sitting on, saluted Patrine stiffly and said:\par
\par
"Mem, this is no place for a leddy, wi' a drucken wastrel like mysel' in\par
it.  Ay!  I hae lat ower a drap too mony, I am awa' the noo wi' my\par
weicht o' wyte.  But no wi'oot a warstle have I yielded to the Enemy!"\par
His anguish broke the flood-gates in a rumbling roar.  "Like Job I hae\par
cried oot in the nicht-watches to my Creator, speiring o' Him why He\par
made weak men an' strong rum?  He didna' gie me ony answer--and I am\par
ganging down the Broad Road's fast as my bluidy thirrst can carry me--a\par
disgraced and ruined man!"\par
\par
"Mr. Sherbrand will give you another chance.  I know he will!--I'll ask\par
him!" came impetuously in the big warm womanly baritone.\par
\par
"You're a grand woman to luik at, and the lad'll gie in--an' the haill\par
deil's dance to begin ance mair....  Na, na, my bonny leddy!" said\par
Macrombie, "ye can never lippen to the promises o' a drunkard.  Best lat\par
me gang my gait to muckle Hell.  Ay!  I'll no' be lonesome there for\par
want o' company....  Toch! what a regiment o' Macrombies deid an' damned\par
will answer 'Present' to auld Satan's rollcall!  Guid-nicht, my leddy,\par
an' thanks to ye a' the same."\par
\par
He took his cap from a peg, and from the corner a bundle of\par
miscellaneous possessions, rolled up in apparently a worn alpaca\par
office-coat, and girt about with knotted string.  He saluted the Chief\par
and Saxham, and nodded to the telegraph clerk, and went out of the cabin\par
in a plodding kind of hurry as though no grass should grow under his\par
feet before he set them for good upon the dreadful downward Road.\par
\par
His vice had played into an enemy's hands, and he would trust himself no\par
longer.  He meted out judgment to rum-soaked Macrombie, assuming for\par
himself the prerogative of the One Judge.  But he got his chance in\par
spite of himself, when Britain's Hour came.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLI*\par
\par
                             *SAXHAM LIES*\par
\par
\par
At Saxham's nod Patrine rang up Lynette, and the familiar voice that\par
came back, spun out to a spider-thread of sweetness across the distance,\par
stabbed the listener to the heart like a delicate blade of gold-wrought\par
steel.  It said, with a quiver in it:\par
\par
"Of course, I am not nervous at all.  And I know how much Bawne would\par
enjoy the night-flying.  But if Owen were not there, perhaps I might\par
be--afraid that something was wrong.  Owen!"\par
\par
"Say that I am here," the Doctor signed, and Patrine obeyed.\par
\par
"Tell my darling to speak to me," said the voice, and Patrine, dropping\par
the microphone from suddenly useless fingers, saw Saxham take it and\par
force his stiff white lips to speech:\par
\par
"It is not possible--just at this moment.  You forget----"\par
\par
"Of course ... The fireworks!"\par
\par
"Just so.  The fireworks.  Expect us in another hour. And--Patrine is\par
here and coming back to Harley Street. To stay.  Please tell Mrs. Keyse\par
and Janey to get a room ready."\par
\par
The cordial answer came:\par
\par
"I will at once.  Dear Pat! how glad I shall be to have her!"\par
\par
"This is Patrine speaking now!"\par
\par
\par
Saxham's steady hand touched Patrine's in transferring the receiver of\par
the telephone, and the chill of it stung like the touch of death.  She\par
could not control her trembling as she answered:\par
\par
"You are always so kind to me, dear Aunt Lynette!"\par
\par
"No, dear!  In an hour, then?  Take care of my precious," the sweet\par
voice pleaded, "until I see you both..."\par
\par
"Yes--yes!"\par
\par
Saxham's hand hung up the receiver, rang off, and steadied Patrine,\par
whose knees were melting under her weight:\par
\par
"Don't ask me ... any more ... I--can't!" she begged of him brokenly.\par
He said, and with those deep lines that showed in his hard grey face,\par
and his light eyes staring haggardly from caves that grief had dug about\par
them, Saxham looked older by twenty years:\par
\par
"I know it was hard, but the thing had got to be done. How could I\par
bludgeon her with the truth, whispered over a wire?  Once face to face,\par
the first glimpse of me will show her that I have lied to her.  God help\par
me!" said the Dop Doctor; "I told her I had stayed on here with Bawne to\par
give him the treat of seeing a night-flying display."\par
\par
"How--horribly clever of you!"\par
\par
"So clever," Saxham answered harshly, "that I shall probably regret it\par
to the end of my days.  In the whole of my practice I have never known a\par
well-meant deceit do any good--rather the opposite.  Consequently, I\par
preach to my patients Truth before everything--and break down and lie\par
when my own turn comes--like the damned coward I am."\par
\par
"We shall leave here now in a few minutes," went on the Doctor,\par
glowering at his chronometer.  "I sent Keyse away with the car upon a\par
message.  He will be here to take us home to Harley Street at half-past\par
nine.  You have ample time to telephone to Berkeley Square for your\par
clothes and so on....  Lady Beauvayse's maid can pack them for you, I\par
presume?"\par
\par
"Oh, yes.  She's decent in the way of doing things for me."\par
\par
"Very well."\par
\par
The Doctor left the telegraph-hut, and Patrine 'phoned to Berkeley\par
Square.  Then, with a sudden recollection of an appointment which must\par
be cancelled, she gave the number that meant Margot's newly-furnished\par
mansion, and presently heard the little bird-like voice chirping:\par
\par
"Yes, this is 00, Cadogan Place.  I'm Lady Norwater! ... Is that you,\par
Pat?  Yes?  What cheer? ... I'm having a long, deadly domestic evening.\par
Franky's reading an improving book aloud to me--at least he was when you\par
rang up--'Matrimony for Beginners.  A Handbook to Happiness,' it's\par
called.  But I don't believe the man who wrote it ever had a live wife."\par
\par
"Probably not.  Margot, pet, I can't possibly lunch with you to-morrow!"\par
\par
"Don't say you back out because of the book!  Fits has got it now under\par
the sofa."  Fits was Franky's lady bull-terrier.  "And by the time she's\par
done with it there won't be much left.  Say you'll come!" Margot urged.\par
"Franky's got to test a new car--so Rhona Helvellyn's coming with two or\par
three Militant pals of hers.  I'll give you lobster _Americaine_ and\par
cold lamb in mint aspic--and strawberry mousse.  There!"\par
\par
"I'm frightfully sorry, my dinkie, but it simply can't be!"\par
\par
"What tosh!  And we're going to talk over ideas for speeches at the\par
Monster Meeting of Women in October at the Royal Hall.  And Rhona has a\par
Grand Slam in the way of surprises--did she say anything to you about\par
the Mansion House Banquet demonstration she's thought of for Monday\par
night?"\par
\par
"Yes, and I'm down on it--like houses!" declared Patrine.  "Is Rhona\par
really spoiling for a taste of skilly and yard-exercise?  Don't you get\par
mixed up.  Think of Franky reading the paragraphs: 'POPULAR YOUNG\par
PEERESS ON THE SUFFRAGE WAR-PATH.  SOCIETY BEAUTY HECKLES THE LORD\par
MAYOR!  VISCOUNTESS NORWATER BURSTS UPON BANQUETING BISHOPS, IN THE\par
CHARACTER OF A WOMAN WHO WANTS A VOTE!'"\par
\par
Patrine called good-bye and rang off, turning with the smile upon her\par
lips to see Sherbrand standing behind her with a long white coat upon\par
his arm.\par
\par
"I have brought you a wrap.  A lady forgot it here the other day.  Let\par
me help you to put it on."\par
\par
Patrine shivered as he drew the large loose garment round her.  It was a\par
white Malta blanket-coat, very soft and fleecy and warm.\par
\par
"Shall we have another turn on the Grounds before the Doctor's car----"\par
Sherbrand was beginning, when the Chief removed the Wireless head-band\par
and came forward.\par
\par
"Miss Saxham, I must detain you for a minute, I am afraid."\par
\par
Sherbrand went out of the hut.  At a sign the pale clerk evaporated.\par
Sir Roland moved nearer to Patrine.  How old he looked! she thought.\par
\par
"You are done up!  _Esquinte_, aren't you?'\par
\par
"I am tired, but neither done up nor the other thing. Miss Saxham, you\par
just now put me in possession of the details of a Suffragist plot.  The\par
friend of a friend of yours, backed by some other viragoes of the\par
militant order, intends--I quote your own words!--to a bid for a diet of\par
skilly, and prison-yard exercise, by interrupting the after-dinner\par
speakers at the Mansion House Banquet on Monday night. Kindly let her\par
know from me that the stewards will be prepared to prevent her doing\par
so,--and tell her that women will never make successful conspirators\par
until they learn to hold their tongues!  Now, good-night.  Your\par
incautiousness has rendered Miss Helvellyn a service.  She will bless it\par
one day if she doesn't now."\par
\par
He took Patrine's hand in his frank, strong clasp.  The haggard lines on\par
the keen bronzed face did not mar the beauty of its kindliness.\par
\par
"You have given her a chance.  Let's hope she makes the most of it.  To\par
herd with the--wild she-asses isn't the way to serve her sex.  Rowdiness\par
and shrieking will never get the Vote for Women.  Burning down empty\par
country-houses won't land a female Member in the House of Parliament. It\par
isn't Propaganda to--behave like an improper goose.  Mind you tell her!\par
That you, Saxham?" as a tall figure came towards them out of the\par
glimmering darkness fitfully splashed by the petrol-flares now burnt\par
down and dying out.  "Best take your niece home to Harley Street, she is\par
thoroughly tired.  Sherbrand and myself and Mr. Burgin here are good for\par
hours yet."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLII*\par
\par
                        *SAXHAM BREAKS THE NEWS*\par
\par
\par
"Owen! ..."\par
\par
Lynette was dressed in a delicate, filmy black chiffon dinner-gown, and\par
as Saxham's latch-key clicked in the front door-lock and she rose up out\par
of the tail carved armchair that stood beside the large hall fireplace,\par
her paleness seemed to diffuse light, like the whiteness of the moon.\par
\par
"Owen ... He is not ... What ..."\par
\par
Her wide bright glance went past the tall wrapped-up figure of Patrine\par
to the taller shape that bulked behind her. No small active boy-form\par
danced in its wake.  She put out her arms, groping blindly--swayed and\par
would have fallen, but that Saxham strode past Patrine, caught the\par
slender figure in his powerful embrace, turned and carried his wife away\par
down the short corridor that led to the consulting-room.\par
\par
"Miss Pat, my dear!  There's cold supper all laid an' ready waitin' in\par
the dining-room.  By the Doctor's special orders, and I was to see you\par
eat."\par
\par
Thus Mrs. Keyse, now for years housekeeper at Harley Street, a little\par
light-haired woman, common of speech and innocent of grammar, but a\par
pearl of price in the Doctor's estimation and her mistress's right hand.\par
\par
"Don't say they fed you at 'Endon on 'am and salad an' pigeon-pie.\par
Trash is the word," said Mrs. Keyse, "for resturong pastry, and them\par
there piegeons, if language could be given 'em, would bear me out in\par
what I say."\par
\par
But Patrine refused baked meats, submitting to be escorted to her room\par
and tenderly fussed over by the kind, Cockney-tongued little woman, and\par
yellow-haired pink-cheeked thirteen-year-old Janey, out of whose small\par
triangular face looked the honest grey eyes of W. Keyse.\par
\par
Both Mrs. Keyse and Janey had been crying, for Keyse, who acted as the\par
Doctor's chauffeur, had broken bad news in the kitchen-regions.  Master\par
Bawne, according to Keyse, had been taken for a trip in one of them\par
Hairos by a German flying-bloke, and it was feared--not having returned\par
or been heard of--that Something or Other had gone wrong.\par
\par
Mrs. Keyse, a born optimist, rejected the idea of accident or casualty\par
with ringing sniffs of incredulity.  Master Bawne, the blessed dear! had\par
prob'ly bin kidnup' by some foreign Nobleman wanting a Nair.  Trust a\par
German, Mrs. Keyse would never! having when a young woman in service at\par
Alexandra Crescent, Kentish Town, N.W., been treated something frightful\par
by a young man who travelled in shaving-sets of German silver and other\par
fancy articles of Teuton origin.  Keyse must often have heard her\par
mention That There Green?\par
\par
Keyse responded, lighting his pipe, for his wife and daughter had\par
accompanied him to their own private parlour in the basement, looking\par
out across the yard to the garage over which Billy and Janey had been\par
born:\par
\par
"Twice a day since you and me stood up before the dodger to git married.\par
But you never tipped me as 'ow the bloke was a bloomin' Fritzer before.\par
'Ow do you make it out?  Switch me on to the notion!  'Cos o' somethink\par
in the German nickel 'e drummed in gettin' into 'im an' affectin' 'is\par
blood?"\par
\par
Mrs. Keyse, impervious to sarcasm as incapable of grammar, maintained\par
that the subject under discussion had spoke wiv' a Naxent particularly\par
noticeable when upset.  Broken English, in moments of passion, with red\par
eyes and white 'air simpular to one o' them Verbenas, had in conjunction\par
with a decided bent towards bigamy, and an appetite for other people's\par
savings, distinguished That There Green.\par
\par
W. Keyse and Janey went off to bed, and the other servants, instructed\par
through the Doctor's consulting-room speaking-pipe, shut up the house\par
and retired, all save the night-maid who answered the telephone, and\par
attended to the midnight rings at the hall-door.  But Mrs. Keyse did not\par
follow the household.  The Doctor and Mrs. Saxham were still shut up\par
together in the consulting-room. Mrs. Keyse owned to herself that she\par
had talked all that rubbage about That There Green and cetra, to hide\par
that her heart was as water in her bosom, and that she trimbled and\par
shook all over after the fashion of them Fancy shapes of Chicken in\par
Haspeck, or Coffin cream, or Blue Mange coloured with Scotch Anneal.\par
\par
\par
It grew late and later.  The flares on the Flying Ground, many times\par
renewed, had died down to greasy black ash in the scorched and dented\par
buckets, before there was a movement or a sound in the dark\par
consulting-room.  Then the woman who sat in the chair sighed, and the\par
long quivering breath she drew, stirred the thick hair of the man who\par
knelt upon the floor before her, holding her in his arms.\par
\par
"Owen!"\par
\par
"My wife!"\par
\par
The sigh that had escaped her seemed to flutter through the unlighted\par
room like some dusky-winged creature of the darkness.  She leaned her\par
face upon his brow, pressing her lips upon the smooth place above the\par
broad meeting eyebrows. The first kiss she had ever given Saxham had\par
been placed just there.  Now the sweet lips were cold.  He could feel\par
how the delicate white teeth were set behind them. Had she relaxed her\par
grip upon herself he knew she must have cried aloud.  Nor could he help\par
her save by his sustaining hold, and the silence of a grief only\par
equalled by her own.  Thus they had remained, speechless through the\par
hours; drawn closer than ever by the anguish of mutual loss.\par
\par
Now she stirred in Saxham's arms, and spoke collectedly:\par
\par
"Tell me Bawne is not--dead!  Give me courage to go on waiting.  And\par
yet, do not help me to deceive myself or you, with a false hope."\par
\par
"If the worst had happened," said Saxham, almost appealingly, "should we\par
not have known it?"\par
\par
She breathed between stiff lips, trying to control her shuddering:\par
\par
"Twice to-night I have heard him call me: '_Mother!_' and then again,\par
'_Mother!_'  Now I feel"--she closed her eyes and opened them widely,\par
staring through the darkness--"that he is wanting me!--wanting you!--as\par
he never has before.  We were always near till now--he could not realise\par
what parting meant!"\par
\par
She fought with sobs, and the tears she could not keep back fell in the\par
darkness on her husband's face.  His own were mingled with them.\par
Perhaps she knew it, as she wiped them away with a touch that was a\par
caress, saying:\par
\par
"We must not give in!  We must not fail him!  To abandon hope too soon\par
would be to fail!"\par
\par
Courage had come to her with the paling of the stars and the greying in\par
the East that meant the dayspring.  She was full of solicitude for\par
Saxham's weariness, as he rose up stiffly as a knight who has watched\par
his armour through the long hours, kneeling on the threshold of the\par
Sanctuary, and knows with the waning of the flame in the lamp before the\par
Tabernacle that his vigil is over and done.\par
\par
"You are tired--so tired!  Dear Owen, go to bed now, if only for an hour\par
or two.  There will be news of him very soon now--there _must_ be news!"\par
\par
Saxham took a delicate fleecy wrap from a chair and put it about her,\par
for she shivered in the raw chill of the unsunned morning air.  Then he\par
touched the blind, and it rolled up upon a vista of backyard and garage.\par
The shriek of an engine and the vibrating passage of an early train\par
through Portland Road Tube Railway came into their ears, standing\par
together at the open window, as Dawn in her streaming crocus veil peeped\par
shyly through the vast smoke-bank that broods upon the morning face of\par
London, engendered by the innumerable little fires of those among her\par
five millions who must rise and eat, and go forth to labour ere yet it\par
is fairly day.\par
\par
"Owen, tell me!  What is coming?  What is it I feel, here and here?"\par
\par
She turned upon her husband suddenly with the question, touching her\par
brow and heart lightly and fixing on him her widely opened eyes.  The\par
haunted look of Beatrice had come back to them.  His wife's strange\par
likeness to the Guido portrait in the Barberini Palace Gallery--the\par
tragic face with the wistful eyes, that despite the asseverations of the\par
learned and critical will be associated as long as its canvas hangs\par
together with the Daughter of the Cenci--leaped up in her at this hour\par
to startle him afresh.\par
\par
"What is in the air?" she asked.  "What changes are taking place about\par
us?  What great and horrible Thing is moving,--moving towards us as we\par
stand together here?"\par
\par
Saxham's powerful arm went round her protectingly.  He answered:\par
\par
"You shall know, my love, my comrade.  In confidence--I am permitted to\par
tell you this much.  We stand upon the very brink of international War!"\par
\par
She looked at him and in the golden eyes he read courage, endurance and\par
tenderness.  Love that would be changeless. Fidelity through life beyond\par
Death to the Life that is for evermore.\par
\par
"You mean that Austro-Hungary will attack Servia, and that Russia will\par
intervene?"\par
\par
"As Austria intends, no doubt," said Saxham shrugging, "prompted by her\par
Mentor and Ally at Berlin.  In him we have a personality blatantly vain,\par
immensely egoistic, feverishly energetic, imbued to the verge of\par
monomania with the idea of his own appointment by the Almighty--as they\par
understand Him in Germany--to be Imperial leader of nations and arbiter\par
of the destinies of Kings!"\par
\par
He went on:\par
\par
"Suppose the Great Powers of the World a row of straw bee-skeps,\par
susceptible of being upset by a Hohenzollern kick!  Will the mailed toe\par
of Imperial Germany refrain from giving it--invading France through the\par
lost Alsace-Lorraine provinces, the moment Austria-Hungary gets to grips\par
with the Russian bear?  Britain is France's ally, bound in Honour to\par
support her.  Now you understand what vital questions the Chancellories\par
of the world were burning electric light and brain-power and eyesight\par
over, the long night through, while you and I----"\par
\par
She stopped him:\par
\par
"You make me think!--You have told me--That man who has taken my darling\par
is a German Flying Officer.  He may have had some urgent, secret reason\par
for quitting England at once!"\par
\par
"It is more than probable that he carried dispatches of importance.  But\par
I can answer no questions on that point. I should be verging, if I did,\par
on a betrayal of confidence."\par
\par
Lynette Saxham looked at her husband earnestly, and the change wrought\par
in her by the long night's vigil of sorrow sent a pang through the man's\par
heart.  That line of anxiety between the slender eyebrows and the bluish\par
shadows round the golden eyes came to him, like the sorrowful sweetness\par
of the exquisite lips, out of the past.\par
\par
"Why do the Germans hate us?" she asked, and he answered wearily and\par
sombrely:\par
\par
"As the nation with which Germany runs neck and neck in military\par
armament, national wealth and influence, Germans pay us British the\par
compliment of dislike. German ambition, spreading rank and high, is\par
checked in the attainment of its ends even by our geographical position.\par
We carry in our veins too large a share of Teutonic blood, to be\par
ingratiating or subservient to our arrogant and domineering neighbours.\par
What hatred is bitterer than racial hatred? Where is enmity deadlier\par
than that one finds existing between women and men of kindred blood?"\par
\par
The face of David, fair and debonair, rose up before Saxham as he said\par
it.  Strange! that even while he thanked his stars for David's ancient\par
treachery, the fact of the betrayal should rankle in the Doctor still.\par
\par
"Nowhere is there hatred more terrible.  Listen, Owen--there is\par
something I want to tell you----"\par
\par
Lynette shivered and drew the fleecy shawl more closely about her white\par
bare throat, and the slender shoulders and arms that were revealed\par
through the laces of her filmy dinner-gown:\par
\par
"In the first days of the Siege of Gueldersdorp, a woman from the native\par
stad, the wife of a Barala herd, who came to the Convent for medicine\par
and soup for a sick _piccanin_--told the Mother that long before the\par
Orange Free State threw in its lot with the Transvaal--long before Oom\par
Paul and Vader Steyn ordered that all _rooinek_ soldiers sent by Groot\par
Brittanje to South Africa should quit the country--the Barala could not\par
sleep in their kraals at night '_for the going of the creatures_.'  Not\par
all the creatures of prey--the Eaters of Flesh--the crows and the\par
_aasvogels_, the wild dogs and jackals, the _aard_-wolves, and hyaenas.\par
But the hartebeest and springbok and prongbuck and rietbuck; with the\par
little gazelles and tiny antelopes, the _dassies_ and hares, and all the\par
shy, wild harmless things that are stalked and shot for what is called\par
sport, by most men and some women--they passed away in multitudes each\par
night until just before the dawn.  Even the _meerkat_ and the leopard\par
went, the baboons and snakes and the big lizards.  Barala trackers\par
followed the trails North to the Marches of the Okavango--and farther\par
still into the Mabunda country--the woman told us--and their wise men\par
had warned them that it was a _teeken_ of War to come."\par
\par
Her wistful eyes strained towards the East, where between the crowded\par
roofs of the vast City and the shadowy purple day-brow, showed a clear\par
wide band of crocus-yellow, melting into exquisite hyacinth-blue.\par
\par
"Perhaps I am like the antelope and the hares and the wild-bucks and the\par
other creatures.  It may be that this nameless Thing that I have felt\par
coming nearer and nearer is War," said Lynette.  Then she winced as\par
though the net had whirled and fallen, and the trident pierced, and\par
cried out irrepressibly: "If so--Bawne will be out there unprotected--in\par
the midst of it!  Owen!--do you hear me?  How can you stand there so\par
calmly when such a thing may be? How--oh!--how could you consent to his\par
going?"\par
\par
Saxham's square face was set like a mask in the stern effort for\par
self-control.  He was in spirit with the Navigating Lieutenant on the\par
upper bridge of H.M.S. _Rigasamos_, hearkening to the drone of an\par
aeroplane struggling against the thrust of a north-west gale....  He\par
heard the double knock of a back-fire, and heard men talking about\par
engine-trouble.  Even as he brought himself back to say quietly:\par
\par
"I did as you would have done in the same circumstances. If the same\par
voice that spoke to me had virtually said to you: '_Here stands your\par
only son, a child in years and yet a man for England!  Will you let him\par
go?_'  Would you not have consented?  If you deny, I shall tell you that\par
I know my wife better than she knows herself!"\par
\par
"'_A child in years--a man for England_....'"  The fold between her\par
slender eyebrows deepened and the delicate sensitive upper-lip lifted,\par
showing the white, slightly irregular teeth.  "I do not understand," she\par
said piteously; "Was there any question of an order to be carried\par
out?--a duty to be done?"\par
\par
"There was a question to be settled," said Saxham, "involving Bawne's\par
whole future.  Here and Hereafter--and the question was this: Whether\par
the son you have given me is worthy of his mother, or whether he has\par
inherited any twist of brain, any degenerate and wretched weakness from\par
the father whom your pure hand saved and led back, my guardian Saint, my\par
heart's beloved!--from the very threshold of the gates of Hell."\par
\par
"Owen!  Don't speak so of yourself.  I will not hear it. You had been so\par
wronged--driven beside yourself by misfortune and betrayal.  You were\par
not responsible----"  She covered the little ears with the slender\par
hands.  He took the hands down and kissed them, and held them in his\par
own, as he went on:\par
\par
"That is what I should like to believe.  But--the truth is very\par
different.  There was--there is still, I suppose--a spot of weakness in\par
me.  A bubble of air in the casting--a flaw in the wrought steel."  He\par
looked like wrought steel as he spoke; "I had to be sure our boy is\par
sound, mentally and morally as he is physically.  Fit--in the fullest\par
and highest sense of the word.  Rather than have the doubt," said\par
Saxham, "or the knowledge that confirms the doubt, I would----"\par
\par
"No, no!"  She tried to free her slender hands, but the Doctor's hold\par
was as inexorable as gentle.  "You must not say--that!  I cannot\par
bear----"\par
\par
"Ah, my poor love, you, too, have feared lest the sins of the father\par
might some day be visited on the son!" said Saxham with a strange\par
mingling of pity and sorrow and exultation.  "Well, now for your\par
comfort, believe they will not be.  Bawne is all yours, Lynette.  Young\par
as he is, he has learned to master Self and conquer Fear.  Obedience,\par
Duty, and Honour are welded into the metal of his character.  If I had\par
not been my boy's father, I should have envied that man--knowing what I\par
have learned to-day. And therefore I do not grudge--I give freely----"\par
\par
"You give--you do not grudge----"  She suddenly wrenched away her hands\par
and said in a tone that chilled Saxham:\par
\par
"Owen, do you speak like this because you believe Bawne is--dead?"\par
\par
The Doctor made answer:\par
\par
"I believe that if God so decree our boy will yet be given back to us.\par
As far as knowledge goes--except for one fact I am little wiser than\par
you."\par
\par
"I must know what that one thing is!  You will tell me now, and all!"\par
\par
The sun was rushing up over East London in a gloriole of golden fire.\par
To her husband's thought she was like some slender Roman patrician at\par
the stake, as she stood up against the background of flaming splendour,\par
and waited to hear the worst.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XLIII*\par
\par
                          *THE PLUNDERED NEST*\par
\par
\par
If that story of the aeroplane over the North Sea in the thickening\par
dark, fighting East against the side-thrust of the nor'-west gale, with\par
the dropping revolutions and the hiccuping engine, had seemed desperate\par
before, it was ghastly now.  Saxham's last hope died as he told.  When\par
he had done, Lynette said with strange, unnatural composure:\par
\par
"Perhaps I have loved our child too much, and that is why he is taken\par
from me....  And yet how can a mother love by measure and by rule?  Did\par
Our Lady withhold any part of her love from her Divine Child?  Did not\par
the dearest of all earthly mothers say to me--in that waking Vision, the\par
God-given reality of which I have never doubted--'_Be to a son of Owen's\par
what I have been to you!_'"\par
\par
Her strained composure gave way.  Her face quivered and the tears broke\par
forth.  She nipped her trembling lips close and shut her quivering\par
eyelids with her fingers, but the fountains were unsealed, and she wept.\par
Perhaps it was better so.  She dried her eyes presently, and yielding to\par
Saxham's persuasions in that she consented to go and lie down, she came\par
into his embrace and laid her arms about his neck and kissed him with\par
wifely tenderness, saying:\par
\par
"I will answer now, what you said a little while ago.  You shall see\par
under the only leaf of my heart, Owen, that has ever been folded down\par
over a secret kept from you.  When my boy was to be born, and I was weak\par
and suffering, the doubt--the dread, that has haunted and tortured you,\par
assailed me and made me wretched--for a little while. Then I gathered\par
together, jealously, every noble, true and brave thing you had ever done\par
for me or for others; every good deed of kindness, every unselfish\par
tender thought.  I asked you to take me with you to visit your poorer\par
patients. I saw their hollow eyes brighten and heard them bless you when\par
you turned from their bedsides to carry comfort and help elsewhere.  And\par
I wrote down these things in a book. They shine from its pages like\par
jewels.  When I die it was to be given to Bawne....  It will be if he\par
lives to come back to us....  There is a prayer at the end that, in His\par
goodness, God might give me in my boy a man like you!"\par
\par
He went with her to the door and looked after her earnestly as she\par
passed down the corridor out of his sight.\par
\par
Then he locked himself in, and went back to his chair at the\par
consulting-room table.  The bright boy had stood there beside him a few\par
short hours before.  He was there now, pleading with a silent voice,\par
coaxing with unseen looks, tugging with invisible hands.  He always\par
would be. Though Time softened the mother's anguish of loss, there would\par
be no forgetfulness for Saxham, the grim stern man whose nature was\par
Fidelity.  Other children might yet call the Dop Doctor father, but\par
their little fingers would never blur the imprint of the firstborn's\par
babyish hand upon his heart.\par
\par
Perhaps you can see the man, wan and haggard and unshaven, trying to\par
attend to the pressing correspondence that had accumulated since the\par
previous noon.  Even as, to the shrill crying of the Fleet bugles, a\par
windy grey day broke over the choppy Solent, showing the huge pageant of\par
Sea Power ready for the King.\par
\par
Down forty-mile avenues of floating steel fortresses one might follow\par
Majesty, with a great muster of Naval sea-planes and aeroplanes\par
manoeuvring somewhat wildly overhead.\par
\par
As Saxham sat there with Fate's trident rankling in him, those lights he\par
had spoken of were burning behind closely-curtained windows at the\par
Admiralty and at the Foreign Office, and at the Belgian and German\par
Embassies.  In Berlin and Vienna, in Brussels and Paris and St.\par
Petersburg--later to cast off its Teutonic name in loathing and be\par
Petrograd--similar phenomena might have been observed. "Austria was\par
going to take some step," as Prince Lichnowsky had nervously stated to\par
Britain's Foreign Secretary, adding that he regarded the situation as\par
very uncomfortable. And the German Foreign Secretary ingenuously\par
confided to the British Charge d'Affaires in Berlin that it was the\par
intention of Austria-Hungary to offer Serbia a pill which she could not\par
swallow, in the Note demanding the removal of all officers and\par
functionaries guilty of propaganda against the Dual Monarchy, presented\par
by Baron Giesel at Belgrade, on the 24th of July.  The ultimatum was to\par
be accepted or rejected within forty-eight hours, a sweeping proviso, in\par
which one recognises the Hohenzollern touch.\par
\par
The world trembled on the brink of Armageddon.  Men even then were\par
doubtful as to the issue.  It might yet, some said, be Peace.  But if\par
Man, who arrives at conclusions by intellectual processes, was unsure,\par
not so things that are guided merely by Instinct.  Like the wise\par
creatures of Natal and the Transvaal and Bechuanaland in 1900, these\par
knew quite well that War was in the air.\par
\par
It is on record that in these days preceding the Great Calamity, huge\par
droves of wild pig, great herds of deer and small bands of the rarer\par
elk, with bears, hares, martens, and foxes, evacuated the forests of\par
Bavaria and South Germany for the mountain fastnesses of Switzerland.\par
Immense flights of birds not usually migratory, partridges, pheasants,\par
grouse, plover, wild-doves and water-fowl went South with the animals.\par
Under cover of night the colossal game-preserves of East Prussia emptied\par
into Poland--their furred and feathered peoples passing into the\par
labyrinthine swamps of the Russian Dnieper and Dniester--spreading the\par
news, sending the alarm before them:\par
\par
"_Man is coming, and with him War!_"\par
\par
Man was coming.  That strange trembling of the earth had warned its\par
creatures, even before the tramp, tramp, tramp of millions of marching\par
feet, the rumbling that betokened the slow but sure approach of Titanic\par
death-engines, told Fine Ears to seek safety in flight, before the\par
cataclysm of human flesh and iron and steel, and chemicals a thousand\par
times more deadly, rolled down to overwhelm, and destroy.  Hence through\par
those July nights the sound of rushing wings above, and stealthy pads,\par
and trotting hoofs, and heavy bodies crashing through sedge and brake\par
and underbrush, hardly for a moment ceased.  Puffs of sweet wild breath,\par
and musky odours from hidden lairs; tufts of hair upon the thorns, and\par
crowded spoor upon the dust of the forest-paths or the mud of the\par
river-banks, told of their going, to those who were skilled to read such\par
signs. But the same mysterious instinct that urged them to flight, bade\par
the eagle and vulture that prey upon carrion, the raven and owl and\par
crow, the wolf and lynx be on the alert, for the table of Earth would\par
shortly be spread for them as never before in the whole History of War.\par
And their hoarse croaking and hooting and baying and barking answered:\par
War, War, War!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLIV*\par
\par
                          *PATRINE REMEMBERS*\par
\par
\par
Patrine knelt beside the bed in her charming chintz-draped,\par
white-enamelled room at Harley Street, and clumsily thanked God for\par
having taken away von Herrnung. She petitioned that darling Bawne might\par
be quickly found and brought back, and that if he were not, Lynette\par
might not die.  And she wound up with 'Our Father,' rather imperfectly\par
remembered, and got into bed wondering whether Sherbrand would be\par
pleased if he could know her not quite as irreligious as she had\par
boasted--and lay revelling drowsily in the comfort of cool\par
lavender-scented linen, until she fell asleep.\par
\par
She had not tasted sleep for nights: age-long nights of broad staring\par
wakefulness.  Now Somnos, the gentle brother of Thanatos, took her and\par
lapped her divinely round. She felt herself drifting away on a\par
wide-flowing tide of deep sweet restfulness.  Then it was as though an\par
electric light were suddenly switched on in the dark galleries of her\par
brain. Insomnia, the malevolent hag-witch, jests thus merrily with her\par
victims, suffering them to taste sleep, and then whisking the cup away.\par
Like many other practical jests, this ends in breakdown and brain-fever,\par
or drives its victims to the chemist for sleepy drugs, and to the\par
madhouse subsequently.\par
\par
\par
In the middle of the dazzling cocoon-shaped patch of brightness thus\par
created, Patrine recognized the outlines of an ornamental fountain that\par
occupied the centre of the vestibule leading to the supper-room of the\par
Upas Club. Executed in the New Art style of sculpture, of white and\par
black, and tawny marble, it was shaded by tall palms with gilded leaves.\par
\par
On low pedestals rising from the rim of the shallow oval basin of the\par
fountain were three nude life-sized shapes delicately tinted, with gilt\par
hair, carmined lips, darkened eyebrows, vague round eyes of pale blue.\par
They had the flattened breasts and narrow hips of masculine adolescence\par
with women's faces and shoulders, arms and thighs.  One held a finger\par
hushingly on its lip; another was putting on a black vizard through\par
which its pale eyes peeped slyly, the third was smiling over the rim of\par
a golden drinking-cup.  The Three were sharing a pleasant secret between\par
them--or so it had seemed that night to Patrine.\par
\par
After complying with certain formalities, and paying a heavy fee for\par
admission, Patrine with her friend had passed through to a wonderfully\par
decorated supper-room with a big grill at the end, where white-capped\par
cooks were busy with savoury things.  Wind and strings filled the room\par
with great waves of music.  Liveried attendants were serving champagne\par
in crystal jugs to men and women seated supping at the\par
daintily-appointed tables.  The hot eyes and lividly-pale or\par
purple-flushed faces of many of the revellers, already told their tale\par
of excess.\par
\par
The champagne at a guinea a jug, a speciality of the Upas, had seemed\par
excellent to Patrine.  She was out for enjoyment, and fizz made you feel\par
top-hole.  They had supped--was it lobster Americaine or grilled oysters\par
that had preceded the other things?--when there came a change in the\par
music.  The unseen orchestra sighing and thrilling forth the amorous\par
phrases of _Samson et Dalila_, leaped all at once into another familiar\par
theme.  To wit, the dance of the Jaguars in the Jungle, with its wail,\par
clang, clash and growl as of strange, discordant, exotic instruments.\par
\par
"Drums covered with serpent-skin, gombos of elephant-tusk, human\par
skull-rattles and all the paraphernalia of Voodoo," to quote Lady\par
Beauvayse.\par
\par
Couples rose, and began passing out through a wide curtained exit at the\par
farther end of the supper-room.  The music grew madder.  Patrine,\par
laughing, took von Herrnung's offered arm.\par
\par
"Now," he told her, "you are going to see something that is very _chic_!\par
We shall dance in the Hall of the Hundred Pillars!"\par
\par
"How frightfully ripping!" said Patrine.\par
\par
Thus they joined the mob of people--a singularly quiet mob,--and passed\par
through the heavy, curtained entrance. The much-talked-of Hall was\par
merely a big circular ballroom, lighted by groups of electric lilies,\par
set about with pillars of tinted glass, slanting from a dado of black\par
marble, ending at a broad frieze of black beneath the ceiling-dome.\par
Theatrical and tawdry, gaudy and glittering, the scheme of decoration\par
reminded Patrine of the inside of a solitaire marble.  The walls of\par
fierce bright orange were striped in curving oblique and transverse\par
lines of black-and-silver, the silver dome was decorated with similarly\par
curving lines of orange-and-black.\par
\par
To the strange barbaric music of the dance from Sao Paulo men and women\par
were gyrating and posturing, gliding and pausing, as other men and women\par
had done at the Milles Plaisirs.  Presently Patrine and her friend were\par
revolving like the others, in the Valse with the hesitations and the\par
Tango steps in it.  You had only to know Tango and the thing came\par
easily--or you imagined it did, after so much champagne.  Reflected in\par
the wall and ceiling-mirrors the girl had seen herself, twisting and\par
twirling amidst the mob of dancers, with her head thrown back, and her\par
long eyes blazing, and her wide red mouth laughing wantonly, before the\par
black-and-orange-and-silver walls, the silver-and-black-and-orange dome\par
spun giddily round her with the mob of dancers. Dazed, she had shut her\par
eyes.  She had felt herself being hurried somewhere--out of the pillared\par
dancing-hall....\par
\par
\par
She shivered, lying there in the sunshine remembering.... She recalled\par
von Herrnung's face as they had passed out of velvet-curtained,\par
soundless darkness into a tapestry-hung, softly-carpeted corridor.  The\par
inner angles of the eyebrows were lifted, the laughing mouth under the\par
red-rolled moustache displayed the big white teeth in a tigerish way.\par
The pupils of his eyes were dilated, the irises pale as water.  He had\par
looked at her curiously, and said with a strange accent:\par
\par
"So, Isis, you are mine now!"\par
\par
"I suppose so!"\par
\par
"I did not suppose so.  The experience has been very real for me.  Shall\par
we go back--or would you prefer----"\par
\par
She said with her face turned from him sullenly:\par
\par
"I should prefer to go--to where I live!"\par
\par
He had been loth to let her go.  Then under a promise of renewal of\par
those strange, shameful, secret relations, he had wrapped her\par
theatre-mantle about her, and helped her arrange her lace scarf about\par
her head, and taken her through a passage back to the vestibule where\par
the three ambiguous statues stood about the central fountain, upon whose\par
restless jet of water played shifting lights of different hues. By some\par
arrangement of those who had planned the Upas, there faced you as you\par
issued with your companion from the furtive side-passage the figure that\par
had its finger on its smiling, carmined lips....\par
\par
And then--the stale air of London at dawn in midsummer. In the shabby\par
side-street where long ranks of private cars stood waiting, von Herrnung\par
had signalled the chauffeur of one of them--could the man have been the\par
German who had leered at her that day at Hendon?--and then he had put\par
her in, and followed her, and taken her back to Berkeley Square....\par
\par
It irked her to remember that she had told to the sleepy manservant who\par
had admitted her at 3 A.M. an absolutely supererogatory falsehood to\par
account for her return at that belated hour.  For Lady Beau wouldn't\par
have bothered if you'd arrived with the milkman, so long as you turned\par
up smiling at her bedside with your fountain-pen, and her coroneted\par
paper-pad, when she'd had her early grape-fruit, and roll, and coffee,\par
and was ready to tackle her morning mail.\par
\par
\par
Patrine must be discreet.  Cautious.  Must tell no lies of the\par
unnecessary kind.  For even though von Herrnung had been removed, just\par
when his attitude had become formidable and menacing--there might yet be\par
pitfalls of her own digging to brave and shun.\par
\par
Pitfalls ... Perils ... As she lay wakeful, conscious through shut\par
eyelids of the white mouldings of the ceiling her face was turned to,\par
suddenly a keen sharp terror ran her through.  She had heard her own\par
voice say to von Herrnung:\par
\par
"My God!  Can't you understand that I ask nothing better than never to\par
see nor hear of you again!"\par
\par
He had mocked her with his hateful smile, and she had not understood.\par
\par
"Under no--possible conditions?  Just think a bit, my dear!  Because--to\par
burn one's boats behind one--that is not prudent at all!"\par
\par
And later:\par
\par
"You give me to understand that whatever happens--_whatever\par
happens_--you will have nothing more to do with me?"\par
\par
Idiot!--besotted idiot!  She leaped up in the bed, visualising the\par
peril, clearly as though a shutter had snapped back within her brain.\par
Horror froze her, realising the shame she might live to bring upon those\par
who loved Patrine.  Uncle Owen ... Lynette ... Bawne....\par
\par
Mildred and Irma were minor considerations, shadowy silhouettes,\par
negative quantities.  Neither Irma nor Mildred had ever loved Patrine.\par
Dad had though.  Poor, dear Dad! She was glad he wasn't alive now.  And\par
Margot ... Would Kittums cut one if--that happened?  And--Sherbrand! A\par
blush burned over her, and she flung herself face downwards, burying her\par
scorching face among the pillows, stifling the scream that the sheer\par
torture wrung from her, by nipping a fold of the smooth linen in her\par
teeth.\par
\par
So she lay and writhed on the red-hot griddle of her anguished\par
recollection, until a neat housemaid knocked at the door and brought her\par
morning tea.  And as she set down the emptied cup, someone else knocked,\par
and opened the door softly, and Patrine turned--to meet the look of\par
Lynette.\par
\par
And then, though her struggling conscience warned her that she was\par
unworthy to be held in arms so pure, she cried out wildly, and felt\par
herself enfolded, and the fierce emotional tumult within her broke forth\par
in wild sobs and drenching tears.  She heard herself saying:\par
\par
"I would have given my life over and over to have saved you from grief\par
like this!"\par
\par
And yet these were not the words she would have spoken. We are actors\par
often and often when we least suspect ourselves, even when Calamity with\par
one swift stroke of the scalpel has divided the palpitating flesh and\par
quivering nerves down to the living bone.\par
\par
"I would have given my life!" she wept, and Lynette seated by the\par
bedside and bending over her, answered tenderly:\par
\par
"I know it, my kind heart!  You have always loved him.  You wished him\par
not to go--you begged Owen not to allow----"\par
\par
There was unutterable loyalty in the breaking of the sentence: "He\par
thought it best.  I trust my husband," said the sweet voice.  "But yet I\par
thank you, dear one, for your loyalty to me."\par
\par
"Don't touch me!  I'm not fit!" Patrine stammered, resisting the\par
mothering, encircling embrace.  But the cup of pure sweetness was held\par
to her feverish lips, she craved it too much to thrust it from her.  You\par
can see her coming out of the bed in a galumphing outburst of\par
passionate, remorseful tenderness:\par
\par
"Here is my place!--here!" she gulped out brokenly, hiding her wet face\par
on the elder woman's knees.  Together they made a group not unlike\par
Bouguerau's great canvas of the Consolatrix, save that there was no\par
dead, lovely boy lying amidst the scattered petals of the fallen roses\par
on the stone.  Perhaps if there had been and the worst known, Bawne\par
Saxham's mother could hardly have suffered more.\par
\par
Not to understand ... not to be sure.  To be bereaved, and never to know\par
just how the Beloved was taken from you....  Can there be anything more\par
fantastically horrible than this, the fate of thousands of sorrowing\par
women since the beginning of the Great War?\par
\par
It was Sunday morning, brilliant and hot even for July weather.  The\par
clangour of church-bells mingled with the clashing of milk-cans, and the\par
scent of pot-roses mingled with the hot smell of London in midsummer.\par
Lynette shivered in spite of the sultriness, and looked down at the\par
girl, spilt out at her knees under the meretricious splendour of her\par
dead beech-leaf hair.  She did not--how could she?--fathom the secret of\par
such wretchedness, but love and pity flooded her heart, thawed out of\par
its frozen misery by the vital warmth of the contact.  She drew the\par
unresisting arms upwards and about her, and lifted the prone head and\par
took it to her bosom, saying:\par
\par
"My poor girl!  My dear Patrine!"\par
\par
They were silent awhile.  Then Lynette asked, her soft breath stirring\par
the heavy tresses:\par
\par
"Why did you do this, dearest?  Wasn't it sufficiently beautiful?"\par
\par
Patrine choked out, blazing crimson to the tips of her little ears:\par
\par
"No!  At least!--It is hideous now and he hated it!  I--I had to tell\par
him," a sob and a laugh tangled together, "it was the effect of Paris\par
air!"\par
\par
Lynette smiled, though the golden eyes were running over: "Bawne thinks\par
so much of you, always!"\par
\par
"I don't deserve that any one should!"\par
\par
"Nobody shall speak ill before me of any one I care for! Why did you\par
start?"\par
\par
For a vision had flashed into the brain of Patrine, of all the world\par
mocking and jeering and vilifying, and Saxham and Lynette upholding and\par
defending David's daughter, who had brought disgrace upon them.  She\par
lifted her head and released herself almost roughly from Lynette's\par
embrace. She stooped down and took the hem of Mrs. Saxham's gown and\par
kissed it, and rose up looking wonderfully big and stately, and\par
extraordinarily tall.\par
\par
"I love you!" she said in her large warm voice.  "You are the best woman\par
I ever met or shall meet, and I am a rotten bad hat!  Not worth a\par
penn'orth of monkey-nuts, take my word for it!  But--if somebody like\par
you had been my mother--perhaps there'd have been something to show for\par
it to-day."\par
\par
Lynette might have replied, but just then through the quiet house,\par
unnaturally still without the boyish voice and the boyish laughter, and\par
the clumping of little thick-soled brogues upon the stairs and in the\par
passages, there sounded the sharp whirring ting-a-ting of the hall\par
telephone-bell. She turned and was gone with no more noise than a thrush\par
makes in departure.  Left alone, Patrine threw on her bathrobe over the\par
thin nightgown of revealing transparency, lined with the opulent beauty\par
that captures the desires of men, and looked at her fair reflection in\par
the long cheval-glass, smiling with something of the subtlety of the\par
androgynous genius of the Upas Club fountain--the figure that faced the\par
guests as they entered, tying a vizard over its mocking eyes.\par
\par
"You're worse even than I thought you!" Patrine said calmly to Patrine,\par
"but now you know what he meant by what he said, you're not going to\par
trust to Chance and Luck. You're going--for Uncle Owen's sake, and Aunt\par
Lynette's, and Bawne's--and Mother's and Irma's and your own--don't\par
pretend you're a victim!--to marry Sherbrand, the Flying Man!"\par
\par
Not a notion of any possible or eventual wrong or injury to Sherbrand\par
troubled her conscience.  She had yet to develop on the side of moral\par
sensitiveness.  Responsibility towards God, and duty towards her\par
neighbour--the sense of these two obligations that are the foundation\par
and cornerstone of Christianity--had not as yet awakened in Patrine.\par
\par
She liked Sherbrand.  It troubled her more that he had not the _cachet_\par
of one of the great public Schools, than to know him poor, with his four\par
hundred _per annum_--as the proverbial church-mouse.  But she herself\par
was not altogether penniless.  There would be a hundred and fifty pounds\par
a year for Patrine when she married; derived from moneys bequeathed to\par
his daughter's children by Grandpapa Lee Hailey, strictly tied up and\par
protected by various legal provisos, from depredations on the part of\par
the unknown possessive male.\par
\par
Five hundred and fifty between them.  Anyhow, she told herself, that was\par
better than a jab in the eye with a burnt stick.  How soon might the\par
marriage be brought off? One must bend one's energies to the solving of\par
that question.  How many sleepless nights--they were horribly\par
unbecoming!--lay between Patrine and Security?  The Fear that lurked in\par
her dried her palate at the question. She felt like the runner of a\par
Marathon fainting in sight of the goal.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLV*\par
\par
                      *FLOTSAM FROM THE NORTH SEA*\par
\par
\par
On Monday morning, July 20th, under a flying double-column of Naval\par
Goody Two Shoes and aeroplanes, the King led forth his Fleets for\par
tactical exercises in the Channel. There were pictures on the screens at\par
the music-halls that night and for many nights after, that evoked from\par
huge audiences tremendous outbursts of patriotic clapping. Hence first\par
blood in the Great War scores to Lil, belonging to the most ancient of\par
all professions--who had accepted the invitation proffered by a Teutonic\par
stranger to join the familiar crowd on the Empire Promenade.\par
\par
The German paid for drinks.  A friend joined him. There were more\par
drinks, and the two men began to talk, discussing the _ultimatum_\par
expected from Austria-Hungary, and the inevitable refusal of Belgrade to\par
eat Vienna humble-pie.  War with Russia must ensue.  They were cheering\par
in Berlin that night for _Krieg mit Russland_.\par
\par
"It must come sometime," said Lil's patron in an undertone to his crony.\par
"Why then should it not happen now?"\par
\par
"War with Russia means war with France!" the other returned in the same\par
key.\par
\par
"And war with France a reckoning with these pig-dogs!" snarled Lil's\par
temporary owner.  "If the Serbians and Russes are to be smacked--good!\par
If the French--good also!  If the English, a thousand times the better!"\par
\par
"Let us hope," said the more placable Teuton, emptying his second\par
liqueur-glass of Kuemmel--"that it will not be this time as at the\par
affair of Agadir!"\par
\par
"We are ready!" said Lil's patron with an oath.  "We have seven millions\par
of men ready, and two thousand millions of cartridges, and for\par
shell--one would not have dreamed the world held so much steel packed\par
with super-explosive.  No, no!  _Diesmal wird es nicht sein wie in der\par
Agadir!_"\par
\par
He inquired as they left the bar and moved to where Lil, steeped in the\par
Pictures, was standing at the front of the Promenade:\par
\par
"What are these _Gottverflucht_ jackasses braying about?"\par
\par
The jackasses were lustily cheering the portrait of Admiral Sir John\par
Rushworth Jellicoe, Commander-in-Chief of the Grand Fleet--now flung\par
upon the screen.  And the jackasses got upon their feet with a sound as\par
though the packed house were tumbling to pieces, and the Orchestra\par
changed on the final bar of "Rule Britannia!" and the more belligerent\par
of the two Teutons leaned over the barricade and hissed malignantly, as\par
wind and strings crashed tumultuously into "God save the King!"\par
\par
The row broke out in the Promenade as the Royal portrait flashed out and\par
faded.  A German voice swore shrilly, another expostulated, and a woman\par
screamed and screamed....\par
\par
"'Ere!  What's up, what's up now along o' you, young woman?" demanded a\par
burly gold-braided Commissionaire, thrusting through the staring crowd\par
that had gathered. He dragged Lil, still screeching and clawing, from\par
the windpipe of her dishevelled patron, adding, "Do you call this pretty\par
be'aviour?  I'm ashamed o' you--I am!"\par
\par
"He hissed....  The ---- hissed the King!" Lil gasped, scarlet and\par
vituperative and still clawing.  "Let me git at 'im!  Let me----"\par
\par
"No, hold her tight!  It is a lie!  She is drunk!" snarled the German\par
who had hissed.  His necktie, a choice thing in Berlin haberdashery,\par
much sported on the Unter den Linden, was plucked up by the roots, and a\par
broad bleeding scratch adorned his flushed and angry features.  But at\par
the suggestion that he should give the offender in charge of the Police,\par
he melted with his companion into the thinnest of thin air, and Lil did\par
not spend the night in the cells at Wine Street Police-Station.  There\par
ought to have been a paragraph in the _Daily Teller_ or the _Morning\par
Wire_, but it was crowded out by the report--in leaded type--of von\par
Herrnung's death and that of the boy, his volunteer passenger, the only\par
son of Dr. Owen Saxham, M.D., F.R.C.S., M.V.O., whose distinguished\par
share in the Defence of Gueldersdorp would always be remembered, etc.,\par
etc., even now that the frank, manly, and courageous policy of General\par
Botha had established permanent and solid ties of friendship between the\par
Briton and the Boer.\par
\par
A sudden freak, perhaps a private bet, had induced the deceased officer,\par
Captain Count von Herrnung of the Prussian Field Flying Service, son of\par
a distinguished official of the German Imperial Foreign Office, and hero\par
of the two days' flight from Hanover to Paris in the previous April,--to\par
essay the crossing to Germany at a late hour, and in the face of a\par
threatening gale.  Another paragraph recorded how the wreck of the\par
monoplane, "Bird of War" (wrongly described as "the property of\par
Fanshaw's Flying School"), "had been found by a passenger-steamer of the\par
Hamburg Line, bound for Newcastle, floating derelict in the North Sea."\par
\par
A telephone-call followed the ring that had heralded the stroke of\par
Fate's scimitar on that thick bull-neck of Saxham's.  He answered it\par
through the roaring in his ears of the North Sea waters that had drowned\par
the boy.\par
\par
"Are you there?" came in the voice of the friend so toughly tried, so\par
faithfully trusted.  "You have heard the report?  Your voice tells me\par
you have!  Hope, man!--hope!--against everything go on hoping!"\par
\par
The thick slow answer came stumbling over the wire:\par
\par
"Have I--grounds for hope?"\par
\par
Came the prompt reply:\par
\par
"I say yes!  Dare to despair, when you hear that from me!"\par
\par
"God bless you, General!"\par
\par
"Have you--you have not told her?"\par
\par
Saxham answered, steadying his twitching lips:\par
\par
"No!--I thought I should like to keep my wife for another hour or two!"\par
\par
There was a crisp, sharp order:\par
\par
"Go to her now, and steel her with this from me--that the aeroplane,\par
when found, had been thoroughly gutted. The First Officer, who is\par
English and one of our men, swears positively to this.  The 'Gnome'\par
engine had been taken out of the stirrups, and the gyroscopic\par
hovering-gear removed wholesale.  Do you comprehend that this means--a\par
pre-arranged thing?  Listen!--I'll pound it into you, confound you!\par
Once--they have been picked up!  Twice--they have been picked up!  Three\par
times--they have been picked up!  Go to your wife and tell her so from\par
me!"\par
\par
The speaker rang off.\par
\par
But he knew discouragement.  The rapid march of events across the page\par
of History since the Saturday of von Herrnung's flight from Hendon had\par
elicited a check from Official Headquarters.\par
\par
Without signing the book that all visitors must sign, and cooling your\par
heels in the anteroom, you are to be admitted to the private sanctum at\par
the War Office, Whitehall, and the presence of Britain's Secretary of\par
State for War.  See him, seated square and upright in a high-backed\par
leather-covered arm-chair behind a big green cloth covered mahogany\par
desk, a thinnish, wide-shouldered man, with a nose of the beaky type,\par
brown crisp hair sprinkled with grey receding from tall sunburned\par
temples, and deep-set smallish blue eyes, a little weakened by much\par
recent poring over State documents by electric-light.\par
\par
The British Government found it incompatible with its present line of\par
Foreign Policy to take steps towards the recovery of the Foulis Papers.\par
For forty-five years their duplicates had lain in safe-keeping at the\par
War Office. They were there now.  That was the Minister's chief point.\par
\par
The Foulis War Engine had never been patented--never acquired by the\par
British War Office.  Such distinction or favour as the tenth Earl had\par
received from Government had been conferred in recognition of the dead\par
man's gallant services to his country, not as the reward of his\par
inventive gift.  _Ergo_, the British Government could not concern itself\par
with the theft of the original Plans from Gwyll Castle.  To pursue and\par
arrest the thief was the affair of the Head of the Clanronald family.\par
If his lordship chose to drop the matter!--the Colonel's celebrated\par
Parliamentary shrug and smile conveyed the rest.\par
\par
\par
There was another point still.  If the Plans of the War Engine of\par
Clanronald had once been seen by--alien eyes, the possession of the\par
formulas did not matter two pence. The cat that had grown grey in the\par
bag was out of it for good.  In the Colonel's opinion--a priceless asset\par
in the highly delicate condition of International Politics--a more\par
formidable document than the Foulis Plan was the Note which was even\par
then being placed by Austria's Representative at Belgrade before the\par
Serbian Council of Ministers. This, in conjunction with Germany's\par
deferred answer to our proposal of a Conference of Representatives of\par
the Great Powers, and the sudden, secret return of the Emperor of\par
Germany to Berlin--"justifies Admiralty orders that have been issued,"\par
said the Minister, "directing our First--ahem!---Battle Fleet,\par
concentrated--as it happens!--at Portland, not to disperse for Manoeuvre\par
Leave."\par
\par
The speaker, who had pushed back his chair and crossed his legs, looked\par
very steadily at Sir Roland as this last sentence very quietly left his\par
thin lips.  Not a muscle twitched in the other's lean, keen face.  The\par
Minister went on:\par
\par
"Thus I may hope I have made clear to you my view of the situation.  As\par
for the Flying-officer, Count von Herrnung--we may presume him to have\par
been--for no doubt he is drowned--a military spy.  The German General\par
Staff have a preference for employing men belonging to the higher social\par
circles for work of this kind.  Wonderfully organised, their system of\par
strategical and political investigation!"\par
\par
Sir Roland agreed:\par
\par
"Wonderfully organised, when one goes closely into its\par
ramifications--tracing and following them to their Headquarters in a\par
certain underground office at the Wilhelmstrasse! But they fail in one\par
thing.  The kind of operations they contemplate can usually be deduced\par
from the line of their reconnaissance!"\par
\par
"And yet in the instance under consideration," hinted the Minister,\par
"Count von Herrnung's intention of commandeering a machine from the\par
Hendon Flying Ground seems to have been fairly well disguised!"\par
\par
"Pardon me!" opposed Sir Roland, with quiet assurance. "He had no such\par
intention when he arrived at Hendon. His orders were conveyed to him on\par
the ground!  And the haste with which he was got out of England with the\par
brown satchel proves that his superiors did not dare to delay even for\par
the precautionary measures, and that no copies nor photographs have been\par
made of the Foulis MSS. and plans! Take it from me that the cat, if she\par
has not already got to Germany, remains in the brown bag!"\par
\par
"And the bag is somewhere in the North Sea.  But it may be recovered,"\par
said the Minister, "with the body of von Herrnung."\par
\par
The General returned, with a deepening of the lines upon his forehead,\par
and at the angles of his mobile nostrils:\par
\par
"It may be recovered, as you say.  But _if_ so, it will be found upon\par
the body of the boy."  He added, meeting the question in the tired eyes\par
of the other man: "Some objection was made by Mr. Sherbrand--the owner\par
of the now wrecked aeroplane--to von Herrnung's taking the satchel with\par
him in the pilot's pit.  So--Mr. Sherbrand informs me--von Herrnung\par
strapped it to the safety-belt that secured Saxham in his seat."\par
\par
A gleam of interest warmed the frostiness of the Ministerial\par
countenance:\par
\par
"The boy ... Ah! yes, as I think I mentioned before, I sympathise deeply\par
with the boy's parents.  He is a son of a personal friend of your own, I\par
understand?"\par
\par
"Dr. Saxham, sir, late attached to the Medical Staff at Gueldersdorp."\par
\par
"Saxham--that is the name--and the child is the only one?  Most sad and\par
regrettable.  And I think the paragraph in the _Wire_ mentioned--one of\par
your Boy Scouts?"\par
\par
"One of my Scouts!"  The Chief's bright eyes snapped as he added, "Very\par
much to the honour of his troop.  Very greatly to the credit of the\par
Organisation--as I mean to prove to him should he happily survive to\par
return!"\par
\par
"Indeed?  You interest me!  Pray tell the story."\par
\par
It was told, succinctly and crisply.  He said quite warmly:\par
\par
"I could hardly have credited!  What pluck and energy! And to dare the\par
thing--on the strength of a second flight! A boy like that should have\par
lived!  Good-bye, my dear General!"\par
\par
He added, accompanying the visitor to his door:\par
\par
"These are pleasant summer evenings to be wasted in London!  A shower or\par
so--and one could do a great deal of execution with the White Coachman\par
on our Hampshire trout-rivers, sir!"\par
\par
He spoke like an angler mildly peeved by deprivation of the sport he\par
loved best, and even paused to tap the glass of a barometer hanging by\par
the wainscot, on his way back to the writing-table littered with State\par
papers, in defiance of the thin, shrill summons of the\par
telephone-bell....\par
\par
\par
So the General went away, owning to himself that the thing looked\par
desperate.  It was better for England that the Plans of the Foulis War\par
Engine should lie at the bottom of the North Sea, but what of his\par
friend, what of his friend's wife?\par
\par
The keen eyes were unwontedly dim as he reached the wide Turkey-carpeted\par
landing, and the messenger caught a snatch of _The Flowers o' the\par
Forest_ whistled in slow time as his hurrying footsteps overtook the\par
General.  Would Sir Roland please to go back, was the gist of the\par
message.  The Minister had something further to communicate.\par
\par
The War Minister was not alone.  Two persons were with him--a tall man\par
in civilian clothes who stood looking out of the window as one who had\par
temporarily removed himself out of earshot, the other a slim and dapper\par
Naval Secretary.\par
\par
The "something further" proved to be the pith of an Admiralty\par
communication just imparted.  Early that morning a British Submarine on\par
North Sea Patrol duty (we will call her E-131), upon returning to the\par
surface to ascertain the cause of defective submergence, had discovered\par
a brown leather lock-strap to be entangled with her aft diving-plane on\par
the starboard side.  A leather satchel firmly attached to the other end\par
of the strap was jammed under the plane, and subsequently extricated by\par
one of the men, from the collapsible.\par
\par
Perhaps you can imagine the Lieutenant Commander stooping over the\par
retrieved bit of flotsam, lying under the shaded electric light hanging\par
over the narrow sliding table that pulled out from under his bunk in the\par
officer's cabin--a place of privacy again, the steel bulkhead-doors\par
being shut. For when you submerge they are all thrown wide so that the\par
Commander's eye may traverse the whole length of an elongated\par
engine-room, and see what every man is doing at his particular post, in\par
a single flash.\par
\par
The Commander's eye was screwed up in the vain endeavour to see under\par
the flap of the locked satchel.  He took up the thing and turned it in\par
his hands, while the strap, soaked and twisted by sea-water and\par
engine-power, flapped upon his knees like a long frond of wet seaweed.\par
\par
"Wonder who cut the strap?"  Clumsily, as though by a blunt knife\par
wielded by a numb hand--it had been hacked through, and the satchel\par
scratched badly in the process. He went on: "Looks like some rich\par
American globe-trotter's travelling-satchel.  No picking these locks!\par
One might negotiate 'em with the oxygen flame-puff--if it wasn't for the\par
risk of damaging the wads of dollar bills that might possibly be inside.\par
Nothing to be done but rip or cut the leather--and that seems to be made\par
strengthy with metal, somehow!"  He slipped the lean blade of a penknife\par
between the strongly stitched edges.  The satchel proved to be lined\par
with thin plates of aluminium.  "As easy to get inside as the Bank of\par
England!" he grumbled, and so it proved, if the Bank of England has ever\par
been negotiated with a bull-head tin-opener.\par
\par
Inside the leather case lined with aluminium, a little sea-water had\par
penetrated, patching with damp a small antique portfolio of pearly,\par
bossy shark-skin exquisitely painted with birds and foliage by some\par
old-world Japanese master of Art. The quaintly feeble lock, and\par
corner-guards were of bronze, gold-inlaid with scowling fox-masks, and\par
the inevitable chrysanthemum.\par
\par
The Japanese lock gave at a twist of the penknife-blade and then the\par
portfolio disgorged its loose sheaf of yellowed papers strung together\par
by a clew of faded silken twist. Drawings to scale and plans: sheets of\par
manuscript and pages covered with the symbols used in chemical formulas,\par
scribed in a clear small rounded hand.\par
\par
"Great Scott!--what's this?"\par
\par
The ash from the Commander's neglected cigarette fell upon the topside\par
of the precious manuscript.  He blew it reverently off, and dug himself\par
into the pile:\par
\par
"H'm, hum!"\par
\par
"_By Me, Robert Foulis, Seaman, Tenth Earl of Clanronald, G.C.B.,\par
Admiral of the Red, Rear-Admiral of the British Fleet, and Marquis of\par
Araman, etc., etc.  Invented & Conceived Not in Hatred of Mankind, but\par
in Defence of my Country and the Rights Beloved by Every True\par
Briton----_"\par
\par
"Marvellous old cock!  And in 1854, when he was eighty if a day, he\par
offers it for the fifth time to the British Government!"\par
\par
"Busy, Owner?  See you've got inside the prize-packet! My Christmas!\par
what is it?  Miss Araminta's Diary; 'FOUND AFTER FORTY YEARS!' or 'HOW I\par
BROKE MY ENGAGEMENT WITH THE CURATE!'"\par
\par
This from a young, exceedingly wet, and dirty Engineer Lieutenant, fresh\par
from an interview with the damaged diving-plane, and smelling potently\par
of castor-oil.\par
\par
The Commander looked up, and strange things were in his eyes.\par
\par
"You're pretty wide!"  He added, speaking partly to the other man inside\par
the Commander: "Jolly good thing we're on the Home trip.  That main\par
motor gives a lot o' trouble, and--suppose some purblind sailing ship\par
crashed into us--and sent us to the bottom with THIS aboard.  Great Sea\par
Boots!  It makes me crawl all down my back to think of it!"\par
\par
The Second clattered down the steel ladder and filled the doorway with\par
his burly personality.\par
\par
"What makes you crawl?  Don't say the leg o' mutton we bought Saturday\par
from the skipper of that Grimsby trawler has gone back on us!  Is that\par
what the liar means by fresh meat?"\par
\par
"If I told you, you'd crawl too.  Or you'd think it a case of\par
sunstroke--or D.T. of the deferred kind."  The Commander stowed the\par
papers back in the sharkskin case with gingerly carefulness that\par
provoked the query whether he thought he had got hold of a new kind of\par
floating mine, and elicited the retort:\par
\par
"I don't think!--I know it!"\par
\par
No one got anything more out of the speaker, who, presently, declining\par
stewed mutton, whose wholesome savour amply certified to the moral\par
character of the trawler's skipper, went to the Wireless and dispatched\par
a pithy message to the Commander of E-131's particular Coast Defence\par
station, and the news was flashed to Whitehall, to go forth ere long\par
from thence over the world.\par
\par
\par
Sir Roland said, with that unwonted cloud dulling his bright eyes, and a\par
certain huskiness of utterance:\par
\par
"There's no other solution of the puzzle.  Remembering that I had said\par
to him, '_In an emergency, you might do good service to your country by\par
destroying this!_' my Scout took the only course open to him--and dumped\par
the satchel into the sea!"\par
\par
The Minister admitted with characteristic reticence:\par
\par
"Whether I concur with your theory or not, I must admit to you that the\par
report received specifies that the strap had been cut.  'Hacked through'\par
is the actual expression--and the back of the leather outer case\par
scratched as though by a knife."\par
\par
"It is vital that I should examine the strap and see those scratches!"\par
\par
The Minister answered:\par
\par
"To-morrow morning by twelve o'clock--I can obtain you an opportunity.\par
The recovered valise, or wallet, or satchel, will be brought up to the\par
Admiralty by the officer commanding E-131.  She has not yet arrived in\par
harbour. But the Commander will doubtless receive instructions as soon\par
as he reports himself."  He continued, gracefully ignoring his previous\par
statement that the Government had decided not to interfere: "In the\par
absence of the Earl of Clanronald, now yachting in Northern waters, it\par
is obligatory that the War Office should take the matter in hand."\par
\par
The very tall stranger had wheeled, and advanced to Sir Roland with a\par
smile and an outstretched hand of greeting. We know how great a heart\par
beat in its pulses.  Its short, hard grip spoke sympathy and\par
understanding, though the voice was harsh and the light grey eyes stared\par
out of the brick-burned, heavily-moustached face with the old sagacious,\par
indomitable regard.  He said after a word or two had passed, the\par
Admiralty Secretary temporarily occupying the attention of the War\par
Minister:\par
\par
"By the way, you will be interested to hear something I have at\par
first-hand from Clanronald.  He has been, as perhaps you know, cruising\par
with two ancient cronies, Lord Gaynor and Colonel Kaye, in his\par
steam-yacht _Helga_, along the Danish West Coast of Jutland.  He returns\par
the richer by--what I may term a unique experience!"\par
\par
Sir Roland said, meeting the Sirdar's eyes with great certainty:\par
\par
"If I may guess at the nature of the experience, I should hazard that it\par
was--an attempt in the kidnapping line?"\par
\par
The other gave his short, gruff laugh:\par
\par
"You have hit it.  They carry a Wireless installation on the _Helga_,\par
and sparked the story _via_ Cullercoats to Bredingley, who was stopping\par
a week-end at Doome.  The yacht was at anchorage in the outer harbour of\par
Esbjorg, some twenty-eight kilometres from the frontier of\par
Danish-Germany.  It was midnight.  Everybody on board, including the\par
watch, seems to have been asleep except Clanronald, who was roused by\par
something scraping the side of the yacht.  Presently he heard stealthy\par
footsteps on deck, and whispering.  He was squatting on his bunk with a\par
brace of loaded revolvers and a Winchester repeating-rifle, when the\par
intruders opened his cabin door!"\par
\par
"Did any of them survive the intrusion?  If so, Clanronald has--very\par
much changed!"\par
\par
The Sirdar returned, with the quirk of a smile lurking under the heavy\par
moustache whose brown was getting flecked with grey:\par
\par
"Well--the _Helga_ has recently been re-enamelled, and Clanronald is\par
faddy on the point of his new paint.  Besides"--the quirk deepened into\par
a laugh--"he thought it would be more useful to take them as live\par
specimens of the kind of material that goes to make up the crew of a\par
German submarine."\par
\par
They looked at each other, laughing.  Sir Roland inquired:\par
\par
"I venture to hope that while Clanronald was about it--he collected the\par
submarine?"\par
\par
"Unfortunately, no!  And, very regrettably, the collapsible boat in\par
which the raiders had made their midnight visit was swamped when the two\par
others--there had been four of them!--jumped into her to make off.\par
Presumably they could swim and were picked up by the submarine--Undersea\par
Boat No. 14--according to the testimony of one of the prisoners.  The\par
other of whom--an officer and leader of the foray--took poison, and was\par
found dead in the cabin that served for his prison-cell.  The other, a\par
mere seaman, is too dazed with terror to be intelligible--according to\par
Clanronald.  But the whole thing is interesting!"\par
\par
"Hugely and instructively.  As shedding," said the General, "a certain\par
light upon a mystery that baffled the wiseacres in 1913.  I refer to the\par
mysterious disappearance of the engineer-inventor Riesl from his cabin\par
aboard a Hamburg Line, Leith-bound steamer.  With a contract in his\par
pocket for the supply of crude-oil-consuming marine motor-engines to the\par
Navy of a Power--other than the German Government!"\par
\par
"Possibly!--possibly!  One never knows what forces are working beneath\par
the surface."  The set, brick-dust face and grave sagacious eyes of the\par
great soldier seemed to testify to his complete innocence of anything\par
like a _double-entendre_.\par
\par
He ended as the War Minister dismissed the secretary from the Admiralty,\par
and turned again to Sir Roland, saying in his most pompous tones:\par
\par
"Twelve o'clock to-morrow, then, General.  Meanwhile, pray convey to his\par
parents my admiration--in which I feel the First Lord will concur--of\par
the remarkable qualities manifested by young Saxham!  Astonishing\par
devotion to duty, and courageous self-reliance!  He should have\par
lived!--he would have made a noble man!"\par
\par
Came the curt reply:\par
\par
"He is alive now!  I am convinced of it!"\par
\par
The Minister gave the speaker a glance of incredulity. It was so very\par
clear to the War Secretary's logical mind that the child and the man\par
were drowned.  But the harsh voice of the great Field Marshal, England's\par
most faithful friend, who was to succeed him in his place of power,\par
answered for him:\par
\par
"One would expect you to stick to your guns, General. Should you prove\par
right before I sail for Egypt, bring him to see me!"\par
\par
"I promise that, faithfully, my lord."\par
\par
They shook hands and parted.  It seemed a long week until the morrow\par
when the secret of Robert Foulis came home to roost at Whitehall.  But\par
it ended, and twelve o'clock brought that keenly-desired opportunity of\par
examining the cut lock-strap and the empty, knife-scored satchel in the\par
official sanctum of the First Lord Commissioner for the Admiralty, and\par
in the presence of that functionary.\par
\par
"There seems--ah!" the First Lord mounted a pair of gold-rimmed\par
pince-nez, "to be something in the nature of an address scratched upon\par
the leather!"\par
\par
Sir Roland corroborated, after a brief inspection:\par
\par
"There is, most undoubtedly.  And the address is that of the London\par
Headquarters of our Organisation, No. 1000, Victoria Street."\par
\par
"Dear me--dear me!  Most remarkable!  Now here," said the Right Hon.\par
gentleman, breathing asthmatically and twinkling through the gold-framed\par
pebbles, "is something not so easily deciphered.  A rude symbol,\par
something like a _fleur-de-lis_ with letters at either side, and a few\par
other meaningless scrawls!"\par
\par
"It is not a _fleur-de-lis_," Sir Roland answered, "but a fox-mask, with\par
the number and signature of my Scout.  He belonged to the Fox Patrol,\par
331st London.  Here is his troop-number, 22, and here are his initials,\par
B.M.S.--Bawne Mildare Saxham.  It is perfectly in order!  In this way he\par
would be expected to sign a communication to his fellow-Scout.  And the\par
marks below, I can assure you, are not meaningless.  They convey that\par
there is trouble of a very definite kind.  In addition the arrow, here,\par
taking the top of the satchel for the North as in a map--signifies,\par
'Road to be followed East.'"  He added with a stiffening of the facial\par
muscles that made the keen face as hard as a mask carved in boxwood:\par
\par
"And followed it shall be!"\par
\par
\par
It had been decided amongst those who controlled such matters that the\par
British Public were to be fed with the tale. The tapes began to run out\par
at the newspaper-offices as the General took leave of the First Lord and\par
the War Minister and got into his waiting car, and sped away to Harley\par
Street to tell the Dop Doctor how the Saxham pup had proved worthy of\par
his breed.\par
\par
The evening papers made great marvel out of the story, and at all the\par
street corners of London and the suburbs broadsheets lined the gutters,\par
proclaiming in huge inky capitals:\par
\par
"MYSTERIES OF THE SEA.  EXTRAORDINARY ATTEMPTED CAPTURE OF BRITISH\par
YACHTSMAN BY PIRATES IN DANISH WATERS!  MIRACULOUS RECOVERY OF\par
CLANRONALD WAR-PLAN! SUBMARINE IN NORTH SEA FOULS BAG CONTAINING\par
PRICELESS HEIRLOOM STOLEN FROM GWYLL CASTLE!  LAST MESSAGE OF HERO BOY\par
SCOUT!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLVI*\par
\par
                         *AT NORDEICH WIRELESS*\par
\par
\par
In the face of the outrunning tide, Undersea Boat No. 18 had nosed her\par
way from Norderney Gat to Nordeich, by the deep-dredged low-water\par
channel of which Luttha had told. The boy had been roused by the kick of\par
a foot shod with a heelless rubber boot, out of a dog-sleep on the\par
vibrating deckplates of the men's cabin, under the white glare of the\par
electric globes.  The man who kicked him hauled away the blue blanket,\par
and pitched him his clothes, yet moist and heavy with sea-water,\par
ordering him in broken English to get into them quickly to go ashore.\par
\par
The boy obeyed, stiffly, for he yet ached in every limb from the\par
resuscitative rubbing administered by Petty Officer Stoll and his\par
assistant--and his temples throbbed, and there was a singing in his\par
ears.  Perhaps that was from the smell of the petrol!  One breathed\par
petrol--devoured tinned meat stew petrol-flavoured, and drank soup and\par
coffee made with petrol--judging by the tang upon the palate--on board\par
the German submarine.\par
\par
The hatch at the top of the dripping steel ladder was open, letting in\par
the smell of the sea tanged with the odours of fish and rotten seaweed\par
and sewage.  One emerged through the manhole into a strange, windless\par
woolly world. Through a weeping woolly-grey mist, grey, greasy-looking\par
water lapped and licked against a weedy jetty of grey stone alongside\par
which U-18 lay with the fog smoking off her whitey grey painted steel\par
skin.  A bluff-bowed galliot, a yacht or two, and some lighters laden\par
with bricks and cement sat on the blue-grey mud of a small harbour; grey\par
and white seagulls were feeding on the mud, gaily-painted row-boats were\par
lying on the shelving beach of weedy sand.\par
\par
To the right-hand a lighthouse or beacon made a yellow blur in the\par
prevailing woolliness.  Behind one, the foggy land seemed mixed up with\par
the foggy sea, even as the yellow-white curd mixes with the whey in a\par
dish of rennet. North, the intermittent beam thrown from a lighthouse\par
came and went in sudden winks.  Facing to the mainland again, one made\par
out east of the quay an aggregation of tiled roofs and chimneys, and a\par
wooden church-spire with a quaint gilded weathercock.  Running south\par
were black and white signal-posts, buffers, and a big, barn-like railway\par
station.  Beyond, the fog came down so like a curtain, that the shining\par
metals of the permanent way ran into it and ended as sharply as though\par
they had been cut off.\par
\par
There was a trampling of feet on the steel ladder.  Heads showed through\par
the manhole, and a rough hand caught the boy by the collar of his\par
pneumatic jacket and jerked him out of his betters' way.  Luttha\par
appeared in his panoply of yellow oilskins, passed aft and went up on\par
the platform, where his second officer and another stood together at the\par
rail.  Von Herrnung followed, dough-pale, and wearing an old Navy cap in\par
place of his goggled helmet, and a junior officer came after.  They\par
brought the tang of schnapps with the smell of their oilskin coats.  The\par
boy had seen them drinking and nodding to each other at the narrow table\par
in the officer's cabin, as he had hurried into his clothes.\par
\par
"_Gute Reise!  Viel Glueck!_" Luttha had shouted to von Herrnung, and\par
waved his hand with a heartiness that did not seem quite real.\par
\par
"_Auf wiedersehen, besten Dank!_" von Herrnung had called back to the\par
Commander, and set his foot upon the one-rail gang-plank by which a\par
seaman had connected the submarine with the quay.  And then he had drawn\par
it back, as though the salty plank had burned him.  For a party of tall\par
grey soldiers with brown boots and belts, and spiked helmets covered\par
with grey stuff like their clothing, came tramping along the quay with\par
bayonets fixed, and halted at a harsh order from their officer--and von\par
Herrnung, with a shiny grey face, and grey lips under his red moustache,\par
had croaked out meaningly to Luttha:\par
\par
"My thanks for this, Herr Commander!  We will settle the score one day!"\par
\par
He went on then, and the officer arrested him.  And while Bawne stood\par
staring, taking in the scene, another brutal hand had grabbed him by the\par
scruff--lifted him as a boy lifts a puppy--and slung him on to the\par
stones of the quay.\par
\par
"You come with us!"  Somebody spoke to him in English.  It was von\par
Herrnung, and his eyes were poisonous with hate.  "You bear your share\par
in this, Her Dearest."  This was a curious nickname by which the Enemy\par
was often to address Bawne.  "Where I go you will go also!--do you\par
understand?"\par
\par
The officer said something harshly, making an imperious gesture with his\par
drawn sword, and von Herrnung saluted and fell silently into place\par
between the grey files.  Then the party marched along the quay between\par
rows of storehouses with doors painted in broad horizontal stripes of\par
black and white, and passed through a yard and a big open gate at the\par
end of it, with a black and white sentry-box, and a grey-uniformed\par
spike-helmeted sentry on duty outside the gate.  The sentry presented\par
arms, and the party swung through, and struck into a wide main-road that\par
crossed the railhead, a sandy road with a dyke at either side of it,\par
that followed the curve of the shore-line east.\par
\par
Beyond the shore-line the North Sea fog came down, blank and drab as an\par
asbestos curtain, waiting a westerly breeze to roll inland and blot out\par
everything.  Between shore and road were the clumped houses of the\par
fishing-village, and a church with a wooden spire, shaped like an\par
old-fashioned needle-case.  Sand-dunes, covered with sea-holly and bent\par
grass, came up to the road.  But on the other side of the road, beyond\par
the dyke, the eye traversed a wide expanse of dead, flat fenland,\par
drained by a many branched creek.\par
\par
Set in the midst of the fenland were buildings of some kind.  One\par
thought of barracks in the same enclosure with a martello tower or a\par
powder-magazine, like that in Hyde Park.  But two strange landmarks\par
sticking up into the foggy sky altered the character of the flat-roofed\par
structure of grey stone standing in a wide expanse of gravel enclosed by\par
a strong wooden fence, stained with some drab weather-resisting\par
composition, and entered by an imposing pair of spike-topped gates.  A\par
wide dyke full of sluggish water girdled the fence.  You crossed by a\par
wooden swing-bridge leading to the big gates.  When you approached them\par
by the road that branched from the main-road at right-angles, you\par
realised the immense height of two hollow triangular towers of\par
grey-painted steel latts and girders that straddled over the flat roof\par
of the squat stone building--the shorter tower nosing up three hundred\par
feet into the air, and its big brother more than double that height,\par
sheathing its sharp point amongst the leaden-hued clouds, bellying full\par
of moisture sucked up from the North Sea.\par
\par
They looked alive to Bawne in a queer ugly way, throwing out their\par
mile-long antennae to the supporting poles, linking their metal\par
guy-ropes to solid structures of stone and concrete, like colossal\par
web-spinning insects, half-spider, half-mantis, wholly horrible.  And\par
they reminded him of the three tall Wireless masts rearing over the\par
Admiralty at Whitehall, and Marconi House, in the Strand, and the little\par
one that straddled over the telegraph-cabin on Fanshaw's Flying Ground.\par
And at the remembrance the salt tears overbrimmed his raw and burning\par
eyelids, blotting out the muscular, vigorous backs of the men who walked\par
in front of him, and his throat felt as choky as though he had swallowed\par
a whole bull's-eye.\par
\par
There was a sharp order to halt, and boots marked time on sandy gravel.\par
A grey-uniformed soldier of the two on guard outside the big\par
spike-topped gates, flanked with a black-and-white sentry-box on either\par
side, brought his bayonet to the slope and challenged sharply.  A\par
sergeant-major of the party stepped out and answered; the sentry\par
bellowed:\par
\par
"_Raus!_"\par
\par
And with the ruffle of a side-drum, the gates swung open, the guard\par
turned out of a stone guardhouse within, and the armed party with the\par
prisoner and the boy marched into the gravelled courtyard.  The gates\par
shut, and von Herrnung was taken off to a block of buildings distant\par
from the central erection with the Wireless towers.  There was a clock\par
over the doorway of the guardhouse.  The hands indicated a quarter to\par
four.\par
\par
Bawne, standing shivering in the morning rawness, heard the infantry\par
officer commanding the party say in a loud, harsh voice that the boy was\par
to be kept close and sharply looked after.  Then a heavy hand gripped\par
him roughly by the collar, and the voice belonging to the grip shouted:\par
\par
"At the Herr Lieutenant's orders!"\par
\par
Whereupon the boy was summarily thrust before the gruff-voiced speaker\par
to a shed behind the guardhouse--a shed whose planks were a-tremble at\par
all their lower edges with glittering drops of North Sea fog.  He was\par
helped in with a kick, scientifically administered--the big key crashed\par
in the lock--and one was free to sob one's bursting heart out, lying\par
face downwards among the hard, clean, shining straw-trusses that covered\par
the floor of beaten earth.  Somehow the tears relieved, and merciful\par
sleep came to the child, and presently he awakened under the oilskin\par
coat that served for bed-covering, to the rustling of the straw under\par
his head, and through one unglazed aperture that admitted light and air,\par
shone a large, lucent moon--in her last quarter, with Saturn, blazing\par
like a great blue diamond, at her pale and silvery side.\par
\par
In the shed, which had been destined but luckily not used as a kennel\par
for the Adjutant's Pomeranian boar-hound, the boy remained in durance\par
vile for a period of several days. The drills and parades, the buglings\par
and drummings that marked the ordinary course of garrison life, alone\par
enlivened the cramped monotony.  He was given coarse food and drink\par
three times a day, and permitted to exercise for half-an-hour in charge\par
of a corporal within the limits of the gravelled courtyard.  Soldiers\par
were drilling there on most of these occasions, big men in the brand-new\par
green-grey uniform that seemed a kind of Service kit, and who regarded\par
Bawne with looks of quite incomprehensible malignancy, and when their\par
mouths were not closed by Prussian military discipline, made coarse or\par
beastly jokes at his expense.\par
\par
You are to suppose a pitifully unequal struggle on the part of the boy\par
to maintain decency, cleanliness, and self-respect under these\par
conditions, which would have ended in hopeless lethargy had the Saxham\par
pup sprung from a feebler race. Two things helped him at this juncture.\par
The Rosary he said in his straw lair at night, and certain stimulating\par
reading contained in a sea-stained and grimy-paged Scout's Notebook,\par
that nobody had seen him with, or having seen had thought it worth their\par
while to take away.  You can see him on the sixth morning of captivity\par
squatting on his straw, poring over the Alphabet of the Morse Signalling\par
Code, the Rules for First Aid, and so on, following the ten precepts of\par
Scout Law.\par
\par
"_Rule No. 7_.  A Scout obeys orders of his patrol-leader or\par
scout-master without question."\par
\par
He nodded his head as he read the words and his heavy eyes brightened.\par
He pushed back the dulled and rumpled hair from his forehead and\par
straightened his hunched back.\par
\par
"_Rule No. 8_.  A Scout smiles and whistles under all difficulties...."\par
\par
The smile was bravely forced.  He held up his head, filled his lungs\par
with air, inflated his chest, pouted his lips, and began to whistle\par
_Rule Britannia_.  And at the second bar, somebody booted the door\par
heavily and a thick voice bellowed:\par
\par
"_Halt den Mund!_"\par
\par
It was the voice of the soldier who was Bawne's jailor, and the whistle\par
quavered and broke down.  And as the boyish heart swelled to bursting\par
and the irrepressible tears brimmed over, a musical motor-horn, some\par
distance off, sounded clearly and sweetly:\par
\par
"Ta-rara-ta ra!"  And a Prussian officer's voice drowned out the\par
sweetness of the answering echo, shouting:\par
\par
"_Achtung!  Wache heraus!_"\par
\par
Bugles sounded, side-drums beat, there was a crunching of heavy boots\par
upon stone and gravel, followed by the click of presented arms, and the\par
groaning of the heavy gates swung back.  Amidst all these significant\par
noises, you caught the purr and crackle of pneumatic tyres rolling over\par
the wooden bridge into the courtyard.  As they stopped short, a bugle\par
sounded imperatively, and hoarse voices gave the order:\par
\par
"_Helm ab!_"\par
\par
And a multitudinous shout answered--a thick, short, crashing utterance\par
that suggested the fall of a tree.  Three trees fell crashing, and then\par
in a little still of awe a sharp, hollow voice answered:\par
\par
"_Danke, meine Kinden!_"\par
\par
And the boy squatting, listening in the straw, was conscious of a queer\par
tingling sensation that made his hair stiffen on his scalp and sent odd\par
little waves of shuddering down the whole length of his spine.  The\par
voice was not melodious or powerful.  But it set the nerves on edge, and\par
made you wonder what he could be like--the man to whom it belonged. And\par
the question made a picture in the mind, of a mouth with thin lips that\par
were parched and discoloured, a cruel mouth, matching the harsh and\par
hollow utterance.\par
\par
The time crawled on and the sun climbed high.  It must have been noon or\par
nearly when measured steps approached the shed, and the door was\par
unlocked.  This time a non-commissioned officer who had kicked Bawne\par
yesterday caught hold of the boy, hauled him out of the shed, and made\par
at the double towards the squat stone building bestridden by the pair of\par
Wireless towers.  Their intolerable shadows, the sun being nearly\par
overhead, barred the big courtyard with wide lateral and diagonal bands\par
and stripes of blackness.  It was as though two Brobdingnagian spiders\par
had spun there a pair of webs of incredible size.\par
\par
There were soldiers on guard with fixed bayonets at the open doors, that\par
led into the square low-ceiled stone vestibule.  Before the two wide\par
steps stood a bright yellow motor-car.  It was big, roomy, and\par
luxurious, with the Prussian eagle in black and red on both doors.  A\par
young officer in field-grey and flat cap sat immovable at the\par
steering-wheel.  At a little distance waited two other cars. Their\par
chauffeurs wore a dark blue livery with silver braid and buttons, and\par
these cars were black-enamelled and studiously plain.\par
\par
Inside the vestibule were more sentries and a small body of soldiers,\par
all with fixed bayonets.  Also three dubious individuals in black\par
uniform who might have been detectives or not.  They were grouped\par
outside a heavy door on the right hand as you entered.  Despite the\par
presence of so many persons a singular quiet reigned.  Footfalls made no\par
noise on the floor, presumably of stone, covered with thick, resilient\par
red rubber.  There were no windows, light being admitted from overhead\par
by a skylight of thick opaline glass.\par
\par
\par
I have said that quiet reigned, but as the corollary of a sharp harsh\par
voice that talked without cessation.  It upbraided, denounced,\par
interrogated; interrupted conjectural answers with contradiction; burst\par
out anew into shrill denunciation, and switched off the current of abuse\par
to pelt its object with questions again.  It rasped the nerves. Of the\par
men who heard it some grew pale, others were red and sweated freely.\par
When it broke off in a scream like a vicious stallion's neigh, a\par
susurration of horror passed from one to another of the erect, silent,\par
and rigid men waiting in the vestibule.  The neighing scream was\par
followed by a small commotion.  The door opened, and a tall,\par
grey-moustached, grey-cloaked cavalry officer, in a silver helmet\par
crested with a perching eagle, demanded--Bawne's little German serving\par
him once more at this juncture:\par
\par
"Water!  Immediately--a glass of water!" and vanished again.\par
\par
An orderly got the water, passing out by another tall door in the centre\par
of the vestibule and coming back with a filled tumbler on a china plate.\par
One of the men in black snatched it from him and knocked officiously.\par
But the harsh shrill voice had begun to rate again, and when the door\par
was opened, a thick-set officer in a spiked infantry helmet, with a\par
glittering gold moustache and sharp blue eyes twinkling through\par
glittering gold pince-nez, waved the water away as though it had never\par
been asked for.\par
\par
"The boy!" he said, in a shrill falsetto whisper.  "_Seine Majestaet_\par
wants the boy!"\par
\par
Then it seemed as though twenty zealous hands propelled the boy towards\par
the mysterious room's threshold.  The officer in pince-nez grabbed his\par
arm and pulled him briskly in.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XLVII*\par
\par
                         *THE MAN OF "THE DAY"*\par
\par
\par
You were in a square, singularly light, though windowless room\par
immediately underneath the lower, pointed end of the biggest Wireless.\par
The room was lighted along the top of the walls on two sides by oblong\par
slabs of thick opaque glass with many ventilators controlled by levers.\par
The huge metal ribs and supports of the colossal steel tower overhead\par
were built deep into the solid stone masonry.  Through a massive block\par
of crystal glass--the insulator on which the pointed end of the mast\par
rested, your vision was snatched up--up dizzily--through the vertical\par
labyrinth of metal ribs and girders, until it ended at the inner\par
extremity of the apex, seven hundred and fifty feet above.  The shrill\par
song of the wind amongst the steel ribs, and spars, and guy-ropes, whose\par
ends were linked to reinforced steel beams or ground-anchors, sunk in\par
heavy outside foundations of masonry, hardly reached one here.  But from\par
the dynamo-room that absorbed the space between this and the second\par
Wireless chamber, you heard the deep moan of the Goldberg Alternator,\par
its rotor speed maintained by a 500 horse-power Krafit engine, sunk, to\par
lessen the tremendous vibration, in a solid steel and cement lined\par
power-house, deep below the level of the soggy ground.\par
\par
The boy's wide blue eyes took in the wonder and the strangeness of his\par
surroundings.  Lightness and whiteness, a ship-shape neatness, a\par
scrupulous freedom from dust, a dazzling polish and burnish on surfaces\par
or knobs or handles of wood, brass, or copper, characterised the place.\par
About the walls were metal cylinders with pipes and induction-coils,\par
frames supporting reels of wire in rows, and brass things like pincers\par
in rows above them; and above these, rows of shining crystal bull's-eyes\par
like port-lights, and yet others with stars and circles of electric\par
bulbs.\par
\par
At the distant end of the long, light, shining room, the deck-like run\par
of the polished boards was broken by a step leading to a platform where\par
the rigidly-erect figures of three men in dark blue uniform sat at the\par
middle, and at either end of a long narrow table burdened with\par
instruments whose use Bawne partly knew.  The midmost operator, sitting\par
with his back to you, wore a head-band with receiver ear-pieces, beyond\par
which his ears, large, thick, and red as quarter-pounds of beefsteak,\par
projected in a singularly grotesque way.  The man seated on the right of\par
the table had a paper-pad and pencil, and the man on the left sat in\par
front of a typewriter, with lowered intent eyes and fingers crooked\par
above the keys, as one waiting to type off a Wireless message, and the\par
tingling desire to approach and see the apparatus more closely evoked a\par
wiggle on the part of the boy that was grimly checked by a big hard hand\par
that gripped his arm.  This reminded him that he was a prisoner.  Like\par
von Herrnung, Bawne thought and--then upon his right he became aware of\par
von Herrnung, green as a drowned man--and with all the stiffening gone\par
out of him--wilting over the supporting arms of two officers of the\par
garrison.  And then a voice said something shrilly and harshly--and\par
Saxham's son found himself looking into a pair of steel-blue, shining,\par
flickering eyes, with whites curiously veined with red.\par
\par
\par
The man to whom the eyes belonged sat immediately facing you, on the\par
opposite side of a big kneehole writing-table with rows of drawers in\par
its pedestals, and official-looking ledgers upon it, also files of\par
papers, dispatch-cases, three big inkstands, and the shining metal\par
pillar of a telephone transmitter, the base of which the officer gripped\par
with his right hand as he leaned forwards, sharply scrutinising you.\par
The hand was large and muscular, with short, thick, crooked fingers,\par
covered with jewelled rings that sparkled in the sun.\par
\par
Half a dozen other officers stood at some little distance behind the\par
seated personage....  Five out of the six wore the Service dress of\par
grey-green serge, with spiked helmets covered with the same material.\par
Badges, buckles, chain-straps, and the hilts of swords curved or\par
straight were dulled to rigorous uniformity, and belts, gloves, and\par
boots were of earth, not tan-coloured, brown.  Thus much Bawne grasped,\par
but of these individualities, save one, he got no clear impression.  You\par
were obliged to look at, and think of, the man sitting in the chair.\par
\par
Those strange eyes stung as they fastened on you and sucked at you,\par
somehow making you think of a tiger lurking in a cave of ice.  They were\par
shadowed by the peak of a grey-green field-cap, with an edge of vivid\par
crimson showing above its deep band of silver lace, oakleaf and\par
acorn-patterned. He wore a loose grey overcoat with silver buttons,\par
thrown open to reveal a grey-green single-breasted Service jacket with a\par
turn-down collar edged with silver lace and faced with crimson, and a\par
glittering decoration dangling below the hook.  But as he was of the\par
short-necked, fleshy type of man, and kept his head well down and thrust\par
forward, staring you out of countenance over a grizzled moustache with\par
upright, bushy ends--and all the light in the room came from overhead,\par
the decoration was obscured by the shadow of his chin.  A sharp chin,\par
meagrely modelled, with a cleft in the middle, suggesting petulance and\par
vanity. The chin of a mediocre actor of romantic parts.\par
\par
"So you are the boy?"\par
\par
The tobacco-stained teeth in the mouth under the dyed moustache were\par
filled and patched with gold that glittered when he spoke to you.  There\par
was a flash of yellow metal now as he added:\par
\par
"You do not answer, no?  Come nearer, boy!"\par
\par
His legs, short, thick legs in grey riding-breeches and brown boots with\par
beautiful spurs of gold and steel, stuck out towards you under the\par
table.  As you stepped out briskly to lessen the distance between you,\par
he pulled the legs back sharply, and a handsome, dark young officer,\par
standing on his right, put out a brown-gloved hand warningly, as though\par
the border of the big Turkey rug on which stood the kneehole\par
writing-table were a frontier-line that must not be crossed.\par
\par
As he did this, the seated man glanced round at him, nodding approval,\par
and the pale, jagged seam of a scar on his left cheek showed plainly\par
against the dark, harsh, fever-dry skin.  With the slewing of his head\par
the decoration hanging by a swivel at the collar of his single-breasted\par
Service jacket flashed into the light.  Bawne saw a large Maltese Cross\par
eight-pointed and blue-enamelled, having a black eagle, with outspread\par
wings, between each arm. Crossed swords in diamonds were above,\par
surmounted by a diamond Crown Imperial.  And a black and white ribbon\par
supported another Cross of plain black edged with silver, at a\par
buttonhole of the Norfolk-cut jacket of grey-green. Possibly the boy had\par
guessed in whose presence he stood, even before the young officer, at an\par
impatient signal from his master, said in excellent English:\par
\par
"I am commanded to tell you that you are in the presence of the Emperor\par
of Germany."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER XLVIII*\par
\par
                          *PATRINE IS ENGAGED*\par
\par
\par
"Don't tell me--not that you ever have--that there ain't such a thing as\par
Providence!"  Thus Franky, after lunch upon the fateful Third of August,\par
from the hearthrug of the drawing room at 00, Cadogan Place.  "When," he\par
went on, "just as I'm on the point of sendin' in my papers to please\par
you--good old England kerwumps into War!"\par
\par
He continued, as Margot shrugged her small shoulders:\par
\par
"All right, best child!  Bet you twenty to one in gloves it comes\par
off!--as sure as the Austrian monitors were shellin' Belgrade, and the\par
British Cabinet were sittin' on Sunday, and the weekly rags selling like\par
hot cakes, when you and me and the rest of the congregation were slowly\par
oozin' out of Church.  Why, the Kaiser and the Tsar have been at\par
loggerheads since Saturday.  German troops are swampin' Luxembourg, and\par
the next move will be the Invasion of France.  There We come in--and the\par
rest of the big European Powers!  Like a row of beehives kicked\par
over!--all the swarms mixed and stingin', and Kittums' little Franky in\par
the middle of the scrum!"\par
\par
"Why are you so--frightfully keen about it?"\par
\par
Margot's great dark deer-eyes were vaguely troubled. She got up from her\par
writing-table, a lovely thing in Russian tulip-tree, the shelf of which\par
was graced by a row of mascots: Ti-Ti and the jade tree-frog, Jollikins,\par
Gojo, and half a dozen more.\par
\par
"Best child, I'm not keen!" asserted Franky.  "But I'm pattin' myself on\par
the back--gloatin' over the knowledge that I'm not a bally Has Been--but\par
a real live soldier--just when I'm likely to be wanted to be one!\par
Switch on?"\par
\par
He added, as Margot shook her head: "My grammar's a bit off, but I know\par
what I mean if I can't express it.  Here's a telegraph-kid on a red\par
spider.  Two to one in cough-drops that yellow screed's for me!  Callin'\par
me to Headquarters just as I'd got into my civvy rags to spend the\par
afternoon with my wife!"\par
\par
The prophecy proved correct.  Franky vanished upstairs to peel, plunge\par
into his Guards' uniform, and whirl away, borne by a taxi, into the dim\par
conjectural regions known as Headquarters.\par
\par
Margot went back to her desk to re-read a type-written letter from the\par
Secretary of the Krauss and Wolfenbuchel Fraueenklinik at Berlin,\par
counselling the honoured English lady whose introduction, supplied by a\par
former lady-client, was specially satisfactory!--to secure a room at the\par
Institute, by the payment of a moiety of the fee in advance. The crowd\par
of applicants desirous to subject themselves to the wonderful "Purple\par
Dreams" treatment, was so large, the accommodation, by comparison, so\par
restricted, that to follow this course would be the only wise plan.\par
Similar treatment could be obtained in Paris and Brussels, but to ensure\par
success beyond doubt it was wisest to seek it at the German\par
fountainhead.  One hundred guineas would secure admission to the Berlin\par
Fraueenklinik.  By cheque made payable to the British Agent of\par
Professors Krauss and Wolfenbuchel, Mr. Otto Busch, 000, Cornhill,\par
London, E.C.  It would be advisable were the English client to follow\par
her remittance, taking up residence in Berlin within the next few days.\par
Travelling might not be so easy in October, mildly hinted the Secretary\par
of the Institute.\par
\par
Why, bosh! what utter piffle!  Good old England wasn't going to toddle\par
into any European War in a hurry, decided Margot.  She had had enough\par
bother over the South African biz.  Perhaps if Germany was having a rag\par
with Russia, and a tiny bit of a scrap with France, one would have to\par
get a passport, and travel by a different route to Berlin.  Perhaps the\par
best thing would be to go now--and stick the boredom of a three months'\par
residence in the Kaiser's capital!  Why not?  Under the existing\par
circumstances, one would be bored anywhere.\par
\par
She drew the cheque, and enclosed it to Mr. Busch's address, and wrote a\par
little letter in a huge hand to the Secretary, saying that she had done\par
this and was obliged by his advice.  Then she 'phoned to the Club to ask\par
Patrine to come round to tea at 00, Cadogan Place.  Miss Saxham was not\par
there, according to the hall-porter, but might be found at AA, Harley\par
Street.  There Margot ran her to earth.  Yes, Pat would come with\par
pleasure! but upon condition that Lady Norwater was alone.\par
\par
"Of course!" Margot remembered.  "She's in mourning for the pretty\par
kiddy-cousin!  I must be getting stupid, or I'd have thought of that!"\par
\par
But when the tall figure passed under the Persian portiere of the\par
Cadogan Place drawing-room, it was arrayed in a revealing gown of pale\par
rose lisse with the well-known stole of black feathers and a\par
tall-crowned hat of golden braiding topped the Nile sunrise hair.\par
\par
"Why, I thought--" Margot began:\par
\par
"I know!  Do you think it horribly unfeeling?"  The speaker stooped to\par
kiss the soft cheek of the little creature curled up in the corner of a\par
favourite sofa in a favourite attitude which conveyed an impression of\par
Margot's having no feet.  Patrine did not look at all horrid or\par
unfeeling as she said, winking back the tears that had overbrimmed her\par
underlids, "My heart is in crape if my body isn't!"\par
\par
\par
"I know!" Margot's lovely eyes looked sympathy. "I remember how fond\par
you've always been of the little cousin."\par
\par
"Uncle Owen and Lynette won't believe that the darling's drowned,"\par
Patrine went on.  "But I can't hope!  I'm not of the hoping kind!  When\par
I shut my eyes I seem to see Bawne fighting to keep afloat--then\par
sinking.  It's as though he called me, and--it's horrible!"  She\par
shuddered. "It's horrible!"\par
\par
"And--Count von Herrnung?  The German Flying Man?"  Margot touched the\par
large white hand next her. "You know what a bad hand I am at saying\par
things that are consolatory and cosy.  Couldn't rake up a single text\par
for my life--or if I did I'd quote 'em wrong end topside.  Like the\par
callow curate who assured the weeping widow that 'Heaven tempers the\par
wind to the lorn sham!'"\par
\par
"I'll let you off the texts, not being a weeping widow!"\par
\par
But Patrine's pale cheeks burned.  Margot pursued, not looking at them:\par
\par
"Rhona Helvellyn told me there was nothing serious between you.  Indeed,\par
she said you rather hated him than otherwise.  But of course you're\par
sorry he's drowned, naturally!"\par
\par
There was a silence.  Then:\par
\par
"Yes," Patrine agreed, "I rather hated him than otherwise!"\par
\par
"Ah!"  Margot's little face was sage.  "It's a pity you don't care for\par
some nice man or other!"\par
\par
"Isn't it?"\par
\par
"But you will one day.  It's much nicer to live with your husband than\par
with your sister.  Though I never had a sister," added Margot.  Then her\par
mind, light and brilliant as a humming-bird, flitted to another subject.\par
"Rhona and her two Militants lunched with me on Sunday.  Awfully down on\par
their luck, all three.  The Grand Slam they'd planned--the\par
surprise-packet for the Mansion House Banquet had had the lid put on it\par
by the Police.  Fancy Scotland Yard finding out anything!  But it had,\par
for Rhona got a mysterious note warning her that she'd be dropped on\par
before she could open her head.  So--the Bishops toddled through their\par
speeches without being interrupted!  Sit down and light up.  These\par
Balkan Sobranies are tophole!"\par
\par
"I can't stay!"  But Patrine sat down on the sofa, dipped in the\par
ever-brimful silver box, and kindled a cigarette.\par
\par
"Where's His Nibs?" she asked.  For not even the chastening of\par
bereavement could cure Patrine of slanginess.\par
\par
"Called to B.P.G. Headquarters suddenly."  Margot blew rings.  "Or doing\par
duty for some pal or other at the Tower.  Don't bother about him!  Tell\par
me--why can't you stay with me?"\par
\par
"Aunt Lynette wants me, for one thing.  And----"\par
\par
"And who for the other?"\par
\par
"A man!"  Patrine sent a thin blue spiral of cigarette smoke twirling\par
upwards from her pursed lips.  Intently she watched it climbing and\par
spreading.  When it faded between her absorbed eyes and the Futurist\par
mouldings of the lapis lazuli-grounded ceiling whereon a silver comet\par
swung in a great elliptical orbit about a golden central Sun, she\par
resumed:\par
\par
"A man----"\par
\par
"That makes two men!" said Margot shrewdly,\par
\par
"No, only one.  A man I'm going to marry.  Rather soon, too," said\par
Patrine calmly, and put her cigarette into her mouth again.\par
\par
"PAT!"\par
\par
Margot was staring at her blankly.\par
\par
"Well, my dinkie?"\par
\par
"Isn't this frightfully previous?"\par
\par
Patrine removed the cigarette to say:\par
\par
"It depends on how you look at things."\par
\par
"But--when did you meet?"\par
\par
"In Paris."\par
\par
"Do I know him?"\par
\par
"No, luckily for me!"\par
\par
Margot's small, amazed face dimpled a little at the compliment.\par
\par
"Is he nice?"\par
\par
"I think so!"\par
\par
"In Our Set?"\par
\par
"I don't think so!  He's a Flying Man by profession. Now you know nearly\par
as much as I do," said Patrine. "And I've to be getting back to Harley\par
Street."  She rose from the sofa, towering over her small, indignant\par
friend.\par
\par
"You're not going out of this room until you tell me the rest of it!\par
What is his name, and when did--it--come off?"\par
\par
"His name is Alan--and he only asked me on Wednesday, when he came to\par
Harley Street.  He has called every day since that horrible 18th of\par
July, but this time he came to bring"--Patrine choked a little--"Bawne's\par
Scout staff and smasher.  They had been forgotten in the dressing-shed\par
at the Flying School.  Lynette was too ill to go down to receive them.\par
I had to instead--and the sight of them broke me up."\par
\par
"I--see!"\par
\par
"And," Patrine went on, "he--Alan--was being sympathetic, when Uncle\par
Owen came in."\par
\par
"My hat!"  Margot sat up, her small face alight and sparkling.  "The\par
Doctor-man with the chin and eyebrows! Did he give you unlimited wigging\par
or relent and bless you like the heavy uncle in a proper French Comedy?"\par
\par
"He saw how things were between us.  He wasn't astonished.  He was very\par
kind.  He is always kind!" said Patrine, swallowing.  "If I really\par
believed God were as good as Uncle Owen, I shouldn't be afraid to die."\par
\par
"He makes me feel like an earwig under a steam-roller," affirmed Margot.\par
"And the charming aunt.  Does she cotton to the engagement?"\par
\par
"Lynette is not, for the present, to be told.  I asked that. It seems so\par
cruel to be happy when she is so broken-hearted."\par
\par
"Umps!  Then--Irma and your gay and giddy mater? How do they take it?"\par
\par
"They haven't been asked to take it any way."\par
\par
"Oh well!  Love is good while it lasts," Kittums said from the summit of\par
a pedestal of experience, "but if I could change back to Margot St. John\par
again----"\par
\par
"You wouldn't!"\par
\par
"Wouldn't I, that's all!  This horror that November brings--that's\par
coming every day closer! ... Pat--I haven't told Franky yet, that's to\par
be got over!  But I've definitely settled to go to that Institute in\par
Berlin where women can have babies without knowing anything about\par
it--under--Bother!  I never can remember the name of that drug!"\par
\par
Patrine sat up.  Her face was curiously expressionless. She said,\par
crushing out the last spark of her cigarette-end against the face of a\par
Chinaman on the lacquer ash-tray that occupied a little stand beside the\par
sofa with the silver Sobranie box:\par
\par
"You told me something--you showed me the pink book with the pretty\par
title, 'WEEP NO MORE MOTHERS'--wasn't that the name?  You've made up\par
your mind?  Does it cost the earth?"\par
\par
"Two hundred for patients of the superior class--_wohlgeboren_ clients.\par
Half paid in advance!  Stiff!--but to make sure of not suffering I'd\par
plank a thou'!  It's a nightmare, and a Day-mare, that haunts me all the\par
clock round. That's why I'd change--and be Margot St. John again! That's\par
why I can't whoop with joy when my friends tell me they're going to be\par
spliced!"\par
\par
Patrine got up.\par
\par
"Oh!--well!  Perhaps I shall escape.  After all--it's a lottery!"\par
\par
"Not for big, splendid women like you.  You were made to be a mother,\par
Pat!"\par
\par
"_Don't!_"\par
\par
She kissed Margot hastily and went to the door.\par
\par
"Stop!"  Margot scrambled off the sofa.  "You've forgotten the most\par
important thing of all.  Hasn't 'Alan' got a surname by any chance?"\par
\par
Patrine looked back over her shoulder with something of the old smile.\par
\par
"Rather!  What do you think of Sherbrand?"\par
\par
"What do I think of Sherbrand?  How odd!  It's Franky's family name!"\par
\par
"Queer coincidence.  But _my_ Sherbrand hasn't any relatives in the\par
Peerage!--or if he has, he hasn't told me! I'll butt you wise when I\par
know him well enough to ask him about them.  You see, the whole thing\par
has been beautifully sudden!"\par
\par
"Bring him to lunch at the Club to-morrow.  You're not in mourning, and\par
if you were it wouldn't matter.  It's simply a family affair, if he's\par
really Franky's cousin.  So, say yes."\par
\par
"Very well, if he'll come!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER XLIX*\par
\par
                         *THE WAR CLOUD BREAKS*\par
\par
\par
Patrine kissed her friend again, and went, leaving Kittums in a whirl of\par
astonishment.  To Franky, presently returning from the conjectural\par
region known as Headquarters she announced:\par
\par
"Here's something like news!  Pat Saxham--the girl with the Nile sunrise\par
hair that you don't like!--is going to marry a Flying Man.  And his name\par
is--the same as yours!"\par
\par
"By the Great Snipe! you don't say so!"\par
\par
Franky, slim and dapper in the scarlet Guards' tunic and crimson sash,\par
divested himself of his sword, dropped his immaculate buckskin gloves\par
into his forage-cap, and sighed with undisguised relief as the attentive\par
Jobling, who had been hovering in the background, disappeared with these\par
articles.  Then he proceeded carefully to choose a cigarette from the\par
silver box of Sobranies, lighted it up, bundled Fits out of her master's\par
corner of the sofa, and dropped into it with a sigh of relief.\par
\par
"Sherbrand....  Must be the aviator-fellow we met in Paris.  The chap\par
whose hoverer was bein' tested by the swells of the French S. Ae!  Saved\par
your life and snubbed me for askin' him to dine with us!  Well, that's\par
what I call a cannon off the cush for the Saxham girl!"  His dislike of\par
her betrayed itself in his tone.  "Must be the same man! supposin' him\par
short of a father!  Hilton of Ours showed me an advertisement in the\par
B.M.D. column of _The Banner_ this afternoon briefly announcin' my Uncle\par
Sherbrand's death. Never read _The Banner_--that's how I missed it.\par
Can't say I feel much like puttin' crape on my sleeve in any quantity,"\par
went on Franky.  "My Uncle Noel has been the Family Skeleton, poor old\par
chap! since that affair in 1900.  No doubt his son's cut up--wouldn't be\par
decent of him not to! But at any rate it brings him nearer these--"\par
Franky stuck out a beautifully-cut pair of red-striped auxiliaries\par
ending in dazzling patent-leather Number Eights, and craning over Fits,\par
who had jumped upon his knees, regarded them critically, ending after a\par
pause--"By one life out of the three that stand between.  Don't be so\par
gushin', old girl!"  The rebuke was for Fits, who had taken advantage of\par
her master's attitude to lick him on the chin.\par
\par
Margot crinkled her slender eyebrows and moved restlessly among her big\par
bright, muslin-covered cushions as she asked:\par
\par
"Is this Volapuk or Esperanto?  For mercy's sake don't be obscure!  Why\par
is this Flying Sherbrand nearer your shoes by one life out of three?\par
What has he got to do with your shoes at all?"\par
\par
"Don't you switch on?"  He lifted his sleek brown head and turned his\par
neck in the setting of the gold-encrusted collar badged with the\par
Scottish Thistle, and stared at Margot with the brown eyes that had\par
seemed so beautiful under the awnings of the Nile _dahabeyah_, and were\par
only stupid now.\par
\par
"Have you forgotten?  Don't you twig, best child? Suppose--for the joke\par
of it--there's War, and I get wiped out tryin' to keep up the fightin'\par
traditions of my family and get a bit of gun-metal to hang on a ribbon\par
here."  He glanced down at the left breast of the red coat, guiltless of\par
anything in the decoration line.  "Then--unless the child"--his tone\par
grew gentle--"our kiddy that's coming, happens to be a boy--my Cousin\par
Sherbrand steps into my billet. He's the next heir to the Norwater\par
Viscounty.  Look in Burke or Whittaker if you don't believe me!  Get\par
down, old lady, you're coverin' me with white hairs!"  He bundled Fits\par
off his knees, got up and rang.  "A man ought to be here from Armer's,"\par
he told the servant who responded. "Armer and Co., Pall Mall, Military\par
Tailors.  Send him up to my room and tell Jobling to help him with all\par
those cases and things.  No! don't send Jobling!--send Dowd!"\par
\par
The said Dowd being Franky's soldier servant, between whom and the\par
civilian Jobling reigned a profound mutual contempt.\par
\par
"What is Dowd going to do?"\par
\par
"Oh! only goin' to help overhaul my Service kit and so on," Franky\par
responded lightly.  "What with gettin' leave and bein' married I've\par
hardly sported kharks since last Autumn Slogs.  Wouldn't do to find\par
myself too potty to get into the regulation tea-leaves in case my\par
country called."\par
\par
"What rot! ..."\par
\par
But Franky had swung out of the room and clattered upstairs with Fits\par
close upon his heels.  Fits, who, ordinarily unwilling to be out of\par
sight and sound of her master, now adhered to him like a leech, or his\par
shadow; whining and fidgeting in his absence, as though her feminine\par
mind were beset by haunting apprehensions of some sudden parting, or\par
impending loss....  Long afterwards Margot wondered: "If I had loved him\par
as Fits loves him--should I not also have felt that foreshadowing\par
dread?"\par
\par
But she was conscious only of her own physical discomfort and the\par
increasing weariness that movement brought her. Sharp discontent peaked\par
and pinched the tiny features. She caught a reflection of them in a\par
screen-mirror and shuddered.  With every day that dawned now, their\par
wild-rose prettiness faded.  By-and-by--\par
\par
"If I were as good to look at as I used to be in June--or even a month\par
ago!" she wondered--"would _he_ leave me as he is leaving me\par
to-night--to go down to the House?  Don't I know that the House means\par
the Club, or the music-hall, or a card-party!  Why do men get the best\par
of everything and never have to pay the bill?"\par
\par
She dined in a tea-gown, and when Franky, still in that strange mood of\par
suppressed excitement, attired to four pins in the magpie evening garb\par
of civilized life, had kissed her and said: "So-long, Kittums, little\par
woman!  I'm going down to the Big Talk Shop for a bit.  Expect me back\par
on the doormat when the Mouthpieces of the Nation have done swoppin' hot\par
air!" she tucked up her feet on the big sofa in her charming\par
drawing-room and read "WEEP NO MORE, MOTHERS," until the pink pamphlet\par
with the gilt sunrise stamped upon it grew heavy in the tiny hand. Then\par
she rang for Pauline and betook herself to bed.\par
\par
The bedroom was blue-green as a starling's egg, its painted walls\par
adorned with delicate lines of black and silver.  Perhaps you can see\par
Kittums, under her Brittany lace coverlet amongst the big frilled\par
pillows in one of the narrow black oak bedsteads standing side by side\par
on a carpet of deep rose.  A silver night-lamp burned under a dome of\par
sapphire glass on her night-table, and an electric clock noiselessly\par
marked the hours.  Lying thus, wrapped about with all the swaddlings of\par
Civilisation, this dainty daughter of the Twentieth Century strove in\par
blind revolt against Nature, the huge relentless Force that was slowly\par
grinding her down.  The ant that gets fed into the mill-hopper with the\par
grain might resent the millstone after the same fashion.  Ridiculous,\par
but infinitely pathetic, the tragedy of an infinitesimal thing.\par
\par
What did Franky comprehend of her terrors, her forebodings?  Even\par
Saxham's counsels were a man's counsels, his advice a man's advice.\par
"_Face your ordeal! do not flee it, lest you encounter something even\par
more terrible!_"  Not more terrible for oneself, mind you! but for that\par
unknown, conjectural being, referred to by Franky with such foolish\par
tenderness.\par
\par
The child always!  Never Margot!  She set her little teeth, staring out\par
into the blue-green dusk from among her pillows.  What if it were to be\par
always so?  "My boy," "My son," for ever, instead of "My wife."\par
\par
It was a breathless night.  A hush of suspense brooded over the huge,\par
hot city, such as prevails before the breaking of a storm.  Sentences\par
from the Secretary's letter came back to her as she tossed under the\par
cool light coverings:\par
\par
"_Wiser not to delay, lest travelling should become difficult. It will\par
be advisable indeed for the gracious lady to start as soon as may be.\par
English bank-notes are negotiable here to some extent.  A sum in gold is\par
most convenient to bring._"\par
\par
Why hang back?  Why hesitate because one expected opposition from\par
Franky?  Why not slip off on the quiet without a hint to him?  What a\par
perfectly tophole idea! One could pack secretly, get funds from one's\par
Bank, and skip with Pauline via Ostend to-morrow!  Berlin was a dull\par
place, but anyhow one had got to be dull for some months yet.  The thing\par
could be arranged while Franky was absent on duty at the Tower, or on\par
one of his mysterious errands to Headquarters.  One could cable to him\par
afterwards from the _Fraueenklinik_ at Berlin.\par
\par
An electrical thrill of energy and purpose volted through the\par
humming-bird brain under the silken brown waves. Margot tossed back her\par
coverings and sat up suddenly in bed.  Her great eyes gleamed like a\par
lemur's in the light of the night-globe.  She would steal that march on\par
Franky, she told herself, to-morrow, or at the latest, the day after.\par
Wouldn't it be A1?\par
\par
The small face dimpled into mischievous smiles.  She caught a glimpse of\par
it in a mirror on the opposite wall and kissed her little hand to Margot\par
with saucy gaiety.  If Franky, down at Westminster, could only know what\par
Kittums was planning!  She had a vision of the Houses of Parliament\par
under the white-hot August moonlight, outlined in bluish-green and\par
dazzling silver against a background of glittering black.  Like a\par
Limoges enamel, she told herself.  The long lines of electric arc-lights\par
stretching over the bridge, up Whitehall and down Victoria Street--all\par
along the Thames Embankment--strings of diamonds--crowds and crowds of\par
people ... talking bosh about War when there wouldn't----She was asleep.\par
\par
Asleep, while packed thousands waited under the blue glare of the\par
arc-lights for the rising of the Curtain on the World Tragedy, of which\par
four yearlong Acts have been played out.  For the tag of which Humanity\par
is waiting with held breath, too weary even to cry out: "_How long, O\par
Lord?--how long?_"\par
\par
\par
Prone to assume strange, angular attitudes when speaking, the Foreign\par
Secretary hung over and clutched at the dispatch-box before him, as\par
though it literally contained that most malignant of all the swarm of\par
Evils that issued from the Box of Pandora, as he told his hearers of the\par
rejection of the German bribe and warned them of the imminence of a\par
Declaration of War.  Then, amidst increasing, deepening excitement, the\par
Prime Minister read the appeal of the King of the Belgians, and told of\par
Great Britain's ultimatum to Germany....\par
\par
No wonder those close-packed crowds of sturdy Britons waited under the\par
blue glare of the arc-lamps to hear Big Ben bell the midnight hour.  As\par
the great voice boomed Twelve from the illuminated square of the dial\par
amidst the striking of the countless clocks of London, a tremendous roar\par
of cheers acclaimed the pipping of the egg of Fate and Destiny, echoed\par
by other crowds in distant thoroughfares, spreading in waves to the\par
unseen horizon, whose East was pregnant with the Kaiser's Day.\par
\par
That Fourth of August; Eve of the Feast of British Oswald, King, soldier\par
and Saint, whose Address to his Northumbrian warriors before the battle\par
of Denisburn, fought against Pagan Cadwalla in 633, the Catholic Church\par
enshrines in Her Chronicles:\par
\par
\par
"_Let us all kneel and jointly beseech the true and living GOD ALMIGHTY\par
in His Mercy to defend us from the doughty and fierce enemy.  For He\par
knoweth that we have undertaken a just War...._"\par
\par
"Whereupon," says the Venerable Bede, "all did as the King commanded.\par
And advancing towards the enemy with the first dawn of day, they won the\par
victory their Faith deserved."\par
\par
\par
And before midnight of this pregnant Fourth of August, from the great\par
Wireless Station of Eilvise in Hanover, Germany flung round the world\par
this vital message to all her mercantile Marine:\par
\par
"War declared on England!  Make as quickly as you can for a neutral\par
port!"\par
\par
On the outbreak of War the British Navy cut the All German cables.  One\par
by one the German Colonial Wireless Stations were dismantled.  When the\par
great station at Kamina in Togoland fell, the only remaining link in the\par
system was between the Fatherland and the United States.\par
\par
\par
Dawn outlining the silken blinds, vied with the blue glimmer of the\par
night-lamp as Margot wakened, to hear, in the hush that precedes the\par
Brocken-hunt of Sloane Street motor-traffic, Franky's low, urgent\par
appeal:\par
\par
"_Kittums_!  Kittums, best child!"\par
\par
"What on earth did you wake me for?" said a sleepy and distinctly cross\par
voice.\par
\par
"Couldn't help it!  I simply had to tell you!" Franky began.\par
\par
The little hand touched the electric clock-button and on the ceiling\par
wavered a gigantic dial of yellow brightness.\par
\par
"_Had_ to!  At three o'clock in the morning!  When I was having such a\par
tophole dream!  Thought I was back at the Club in my three dear rooms\par
with the Adams doors and chimney-pieces--and Pauline came in with a huge\par
basket of white flowers--and I asked: '_Who are they for?_'  And she\par
said: 'For Mademoiselle!'  And I was Margot St. John--and had never been\par
married!"  There was infinite wistfulness in the little voice.\par
\par
"White flowers mean death, don't they, when you dream of 'em?  And I'm\par
sorry your dip in the Bran Tub of Matrimony has turned out such a bad\par
investment.  What I came to tell you should revive your hopes of making\par
a better one, my child!"\par
\par
That jarring note of mingled resentment and irony, how new and strange\par
it sounded to Margot!  Until this moment Franky's voice had never been\par
anything but gentle.  It was gentle now as he said, at his dressing-room\par
door:\par
\par
"Finish your sleep.  I was rather a brute to wake you!"  He was going\par
without a backward glance.\par
\par
"Come back!  Come off it!  Don't be dignified!" Margot called after the\par
retreating figure.  "I'm quite awake now, so you'd better tell.  What's\par
on?"\par
\par
He came back to the bedside, looking tall and shadowy in the blue\par
dimness.  Margot put up a little hand and patted his cheek.  There were\par
wet drops upon the smooth, warm skin....  Perhaps he had walked home,\par
and it had been raining.  Or--\par
\par
"_Franky_!  You're not----"\par
\par
He captured the little hand and took it in both his own, and squeezed\par
it.  He said in a cheerful but rather choky voice:\par
\par
"Of course not!  And--the news could have waited. Only--since midnight\par
England and Germany have been at War.  The Big Scrap is three hours old.\par
First battalion of Ours is under orders for the Front--I've exchanged\par
out of the Second with Ackroyd--too sick a man for fightin' just now,\par
luckily for me.  You know Ackroyd.  Used to flirt with him\par
frightfully--to give me beans when I'd vexed you when we were first\par
engaged.  When do we go, did you ask? Liable to be off at any old\par
minute.  By-bye, little woman. Too late to go to bed--heaps of things to\par
attend to.  God bless you!  See you at brekker--or lunch, if I've luck."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER L*\par
\par
                        *THE EVE OF ARMAGEDDON*\par
\par
\par
Kittums, upon that fateful morning, coming down to breakfast and finding\par
no Franky, was annoyed.  One might just as well have had breakfast in\par
bed.  She didn't want any, but Cook would be hurt if the chowder and\par
eggs, and croquettes of chicken weren't eaten.  Therefore Margot ate--to\par
avoid wounding the cook.  The daily papers she left untouched, knowing\par
that War would leap out from the huge capitals heading the columns and\par
strike her in the eyes.\par
\par
She had herself dressed and 'phoned for the car.  The house did not seem\par
a place to stay in, somehow.  Dowd was busy in his master's room,\par
ordering Jobling about in loud authoritative tones and being waited upon\par
by the maids. Even Pauline, ordinarily scornful, referred to him as\par
"Monsieur Dowd" instead of "_zat man Dow!_"\par
\par
Once in Sloane Street, the War rushed at you.  Groups of men, young, old\par
or middle-aged, stood talking at every street-corner, newspapers rustled\par
in every hand.  You couldn't escape the papers.  Huge flaring headlines\par
shrieked from the broad-sheets in the gutters and on the railings: "WAR\par
DECLARED!  ULTIMATUM EXPIRED.  BRITISH FLEET READY FOR BATTLE.  INVASION\par
OF BELGIUM BY GERMAN ARMY CORPS!"  The drapery salesman who was to win\par
the Victoria Cross, called from the top of a Knightsbridge motor-bus to\par
the grocer's assistant who was to receive the Medaille Militaire at the\par
doughty hands of Joffre.... The budding airman who was to bring down a\par
Zeppelin single-handed chuffed past on a motor-cycle--the girls who were\par
to make shells for British guns, or pack made ones with T.N.T. and\par
kindred explosives, tripped along in their transparent hobble-skirts, to\par
meet Alf and Ted at the Tube. And neither Alf, who subsequently took\par
five Huns prisoner by the single hand, shepherding them back to the\par
British lines with dunts of the gun-butt and sarcasms more pointed, nor\par
Ted, who threw himself down over the exploding bomb, dying that his\par
comrades in the trench might live, dreamed what kind of chick would pip\par
Fate's egg for him or her presently.  Yet the dullest face wore a new\par
expression, in the tamest eyes burned the light of battle!  Unquenched\par
it burns in them still, after four dreadful years of War.\par
\par
The Club, already deserted by August holiday-makers, would be utterly\par
abandoned to chimney-sweeps, charwomen and window-cleaners, and yet\par
Margot told the chauffeur to drive to the Club.\par
\par
Turning out of Piccadilly she discovered Short Street to be blocked by\par
taxi-cabs.  An endless procession of telegraph-boys plunged in and out\par
between the thudding swing-doors of the vestibule.  The vestibule was\par
congested with battered, dusty ladies, ladies' maids even dustier and\par
more battered, and travelling bags battered and dusty to the _nth_\par
degree.\par
\par
Some of the bags were bursting, not a few of the maids were hysterical.\par
All the returned travellers were telling their adventures at once.  The\par
air was thick with exclamations, explanations, cries and ejaculations.\par
Unfed, unslept, baggageless and penniless in many instances, the members\par
of the Ladies' Social--seeking health, or novelty, in half the\par
pleasure-resorts upon the map of Europe--had come hurtling back to Short\par
Street like leaves driven before the furious blast of War.\par
\par
"Has anything happened?"\par
\par
Lady Norwater addressed this query to the Club hall-porter, a bald\par
person of habitually slow movements and singularly bland address.  The\par
man gnashed his teeth at her, uttering a sound between a groan and a\par
snarl--made as though to tear non-existent hair,--leaped with\par
astonishing nimbleness over a pile of luggage, and vanished.  Margot\par
would have made a note of his conduct in the Complaints register, but\par
that the hall-table was obliterated by heaps of rugs, dust-cloaks and\par
waterproofs.  Wondering, she made her way into the big General Room on\par
the ground-floor.\par
\par
Here travel-creased, dust-smeared members sat in voluble rows on the\par
comfortable sofas, or reclined speechless in the capacious armchairs.\par
Medical men, hastily summoned by 'phone, moved noiselessly from patient\par
to patient. Husbands and male friends listened not unmoved, to piteous\par
recitals of adverse experiences undergone on enemy ground.\par
\par
Kittums, snatched into the whirl, moved from friend to friend, gathering\par
experiences.  Mrs. Charterhouse, with her Pekinese pug and her maid, had\par
just arrived at Homburg to undergo treatment for a twenty-two-inch waist\par
when the War Cloud gathered monstrous on the horizon.  Had not her Swiss\par
doctor written a warning instead of a prescription the white and golden\par
Cynthia, Mademoiselle Mariette and Chin-Chin, would at this moment have\par
been languishing on rye bread and bean coffee in a Teutonic jail.\par
\par
"As it is, we've spent a whole week, and every sou we had on us making\par
the journey!" said Cynthia, in her plaintive tones.  "They held us up at\par
Frankfurt, Basel, and Geneva! What inquisitions, what scowling\par
suspicious looks! To be hunted and suspect makes you wicked, I've found\par
out! When we got to Paris at four yesterday morning and took a rickety\par
_fiacre_ to the Palais--all the taxis have vanished!--I could have\par
_prayed_ for a cup of tea and a roll!  But at the Palais all was\par
confusion.  The hotel was shutting up--every male servant called to the\par
Reserve.  We got to the 'Spitz'--the same experience there!  Exhausted,\par
I sat on something in the vestibule--it moved, groaned, and I found it\par
to be the wreck of Sir Thomas Brayham.  He and Lady Wathe, his man and\par
her maid, who have been all through July at Franzenbad in the\par
Egerland,--reaching Paris after awful adventures, had all four been\par
hurled out in the same way. One of those jiggety motor-omnibuses took\par
all of us to the Couronne.  They were full to the roofs and cellars, but\par
they wedged us in, somehow!  Then, for two days Sir Thomas tore round\par
Paris trying to get _laissez-passers_."  She turned her lovely eyes upon\par
a large, stertorously-breathing but otherwise inert object reclining\par
with closed eyes and folded hands in the biggest of the Club armchairs.\par
"Didn't you, Sir Thomas?"\par
\par
"Beparr?"\par
\par
Brayham, waking with a bewildered stare, regarded the charming Cynthia\par
uncomprehendingly until the Goblin, sitting opposite, centre of a knot\par
of bosom friends, repeated the query:\par
\par
"Didn't you run about Paris for passes for two days?"\par
\par
"No!" bounced out Brayham, now aroused, and purpling under the coal-dust\par
that begrimed his large, judicial visage. He added, with a vestige of\par
his King's Bench manner, as the Goblin stared at him in concern for his\par
mental state: "I retain the use of my reason, dear friend!  But I WILL\par
NOT consent that the varied tortures of the abominable ordeal I have\par
undergone could possibly be packed within the nutshell limits of\par
forty-eight hours!  Mph!"\par
\par
So dust-covered was the ex-Justice that the very act of shaking his head\par
rebukingly at the Goblin, raised a cloud that made him sneeze.  He\par
uttered the curious composite sound that heralds sternutation, drew out\par
a voluminous, coal-dusty handkerchief, stared at it indignantly, and in\par
the very act of returning it to his pocket--fell asleep again.\par
\par
"A perfect wreck, as I said just now!" whispered Mrs. Charterhouse to\par
Kittums.\par
\par
"_How_ I congratulate you, dear Lady Wastwood," said the Goblin, "on not\par
having gone abroad!"\par
\par
"Was it so horrid?" asked Trixie, sympathetically, arching the eyebrows\par
that resembled musical slurs.\par
\par
"Was it so--"  Lady Wathe shrugged her thin shoulders and gave the ghost\par
of one of her rattling laughs.  "If to fight your way back, stage by\par
stage, amidst inconceivable difficulties, obstacles and insults, is\par
horrid!--if to travel for two long days and nights in trains crowded to\par
suffocating excess merits the term--" She loosened the quadruple string\par
of superb Oriental pearls that tightly clipped her stalk-like throat and\par
went on: "If it comes under the heading to find yourself and your\par
friends--in tatters after a suffocating struggle!--packed with sixty\par
other squalid wretches in a luggage-van _en route_ for Dieppe!  If to\par
sit for three hours on your jewel-case, waiting, in a crush of congested\par
humanity, for the arrival of the Newhaven boat--if to fight as with\par
beasts at Ephesus to gain its gangway--if to fall in a heap on the\par
sodden deck--to lie there lost to everything but the fact that the waves\par
that drench you are British waves, and the British coast is slowly\par
crawling nearer!--if all this and how much more, can be lumped under the\par
term of horrid, it has been, dear Lady Wastwood, horrid in the extreme!"\par
\par
Lady Wastwood's small, triangular, white face with the V-shaped scarlet\par
mouth, looked enigmatical.  She arched the thick black slurs that were\par
her eyebrows again, and said not without intent, to her crony Cynthia\par
Charterhouse:\par
\par
"_Who_ would have _dreamed_ only three weeks ago, when that excessively\par
long-legged and extremely good-looking Count von Herrnung sat here and\par
talked to us about German women and German Supermen--that we should be\par
at War to-day with Germany?"\par
\par
"Poor Count Tido!"  Something rattled in the Goblin's meagre throat as\par
though she had accidentally swallowed some of her pearls.  "That\par
dreadful report in _The Wire_ made the Franzenbad treatment disagree\par
with me horribly! To be drowned in that commonplace North Sea crossing,\par
upon the very eve of realising the one ambition of his life!  For he\par
hated us so thoroughly!  His Anglophobia was a perfect obsession.  Poor\par
dear Tido!  One might call it a wasted career!"  The speaker dried a\par
tear and continued: "His family will be frantic.  You know he was to\par
have been married in October!  Baroness Kriemhilde von\par
Wolfensbragen-Hirschenbuttel.  Immensely rich!  Her father has large\par
interests in the pearl-fisheries of German New Guinea.  Her betrothal\par
gift, a superb black and white pearl, the Count always wore as a mascot.\par
Poor Baroness! She will be inconsolable.  Marriage means the first\par
draught of real freedom to young German girls!"\par
\par
Mrs. Charterhouse said in her sweetly venomous way:\par
\par
"Miss Saxham bears up--under the circumstances!"\par
\par
"Under what circumstances, might one presume to ask?"  Then, reading\par
aright the ambiguous smile of Mrs. Charterhouse, the Goblin rattled out\par
her characteristic laugh:\par
\par
"What absurdity!  You refer to a mere dinner-table flirtation in Paris.\par
The mere _rapprochement_ of _atomes crochus_!  Miss Saxham and Lady\par
Beauvayse dined with us on the night of the Grand Prix.  Poor Tido was\par
certainly struck with her.  I remember he talked about her eyes and\par
figure afterwards.  But her hair being so black and growing so\par
heavily--did not please him.  He found the effect--I think his term\par
was--'too crepuscular.'"\par
\par
"Ah!  You throw a ray," said Mrs. Charterhouse in that sugared way of\par
hers, "on a mystery that has intrigued me. Now I know why Miss Saxham\par
went to the Atelier Wiber in the Rue de la Paix and got her crepuscular\par
tresses changed to terra-cotta!"\par
\par
"_Not_ saffron?  Now," said Lady Wastwood, pensively tilting her own\par
green-gold head and elevating her arched black eyebrows, "I should have\par
called that shade saffron or tumeric.  Who do you suppose footed the\par
bill for the process?  The wretch Wiber simply won't look at you under\par
four hundred and fifty francs!"\par
\par
"Perhaps Vivie Beauvayse--" suggested Mrs. Charterhouse.\par
\par
"I think not.  Vivie preferred the crepuscular effect.  It contrasted\par
capitally with her own style of colouring.  You must have noticed, they\par
are seldom seen going about together as they used.  Dear Lady Wathe, do\par
you feel faint? Can I get you anything?"\par
\par
For something had clicked behind the Goblin's pearls, and she had\par
suddenly stiffened in her seat.  The superb figure of Patrine Saxham had\par
entered by the swing-doors leading from the vestibule followed by a\par
tall, broad-shouldered young man in loose grey tweeds, whose left sleeve\par
displayed a band of black significantly new.\par
\par
"Can that be Miss Saxham?  It must be!--her type is so unusual!  Not\par
having seen her since the night of the dinner I referred to I did not\par
quite grasp the meaning of your references to ingredients common in\par
Indian curries. Of course, I understand now!"  The Goblin surveyed the\par
tall, pliant figure with the dead beech-leaf hair through her lorgnette\par
before she leaned forwards and roused the sleeping Brayham by a brisk\par
application of the instrument.  "Look, Sir Thomas!  Would you have known\par
Miss Saxham?"\par
\par
"Beparr! ... Wharr? ... God bless my soul, no!"\par
\par
Brayham, turning in the armchair as the Zoo walrus turns in his concrete\par
pond, surveyed Patrine with a bloodshot stare.\par
\par
"Silly girl!  Spoilt her looks!" he snorted.  "Handsome as the dooce\par
with her gipsy-black tresses.  Won her bet. Won't get her ring now\par
though, unless von Herrnung paid before he flew!"\par
\par
"Was there a bet between them?  How is it you never told me?  Have I\par
deserved this from you?" demanded Lady Wathe indignantly, as Mrs.\par
Charterhouse and Lady Wastwood exchanged glances and smiles.\par
\par
"Sorry! ... Forgot! ..."  Brayham gobbled apologetically. "Man I know\par
happened to be close to 'em leaving Spitz's Restaurant that Sunday night\par
in Paris.  Told me he heard von Herrnung lay Miss Saxham his magpie\par
pearl solitaire against a bit o' Palais Royal paste she was\par
wearing--that she wouldn't change the colour of her hair! Made the\par
appointment for her, with Wiber--'_Pastiches Artistiques_,' and so on,\par
_Rue de la Paix_.  He bragged of it afterwards at the _Cercle Moderne_!\par
Dam Germans! no idea of decency!  Why do Englishwomen intrigue with 'em?\par
Bounders that kiss and tell!"\par
\par
There was a significant pause, broken by the Goblin's shrillest peal of\par
laughter.  The ex-Justice, whose vitality was low, folded his hands and\par
dozed again.  Then----\par
\par
"Now we _know_ who footed the bill," said Cynthia Charterhouse in\par
dove-like accents.  While Trixie murmured in the vexed ear of Margot:\par
\par
"Kitts, my dinkie, you are a pal of the Saxhams.  _How_ far had the\par
affair _really_ gone?"\par
\par
"There was no affair!" said Margot's sweet little voice, very clearly.\par
"Pat rather hated Count von Herrnung than otherwise!"\par
\par
"Judging by the mute evidence of her hair--" began Mrs. Charterhouse,\par
languidly.  How Margot loathed these women, erstwhile her chosen friends\par
and associates, tearing with greedy beaks and vicious claws at the\par
reputation of an unmarried girl....\par
\par
"Her hair belongs to her!  She can bleach it if she wishes!"  The little\par
figure rose to its altitude of four feet seven inches and surveyed the\par
scandalmongers with wrathful eyes.  "I have said that there was nothing\par
between Miss Saxham and Count von Herrnung"--the little voice was\par
crystal-cold: "I should be extremely obliged to all of you if you will\par
understand this clearly!  Miss Saxham is engaged to my husband's cousin,\par
Alan Sherbrand."--Had Franky heard that stately reference to my husband,\par
he would have been "bowled," to quote himself.  "Franky likes him, and\par
so do I, tremendously!  We're both keen on their bringing off the\par
match!"\par
\par
There was another resounding silence.  Brayham snored melodiously.  Then\par
Trixie Wastwood said with her Pierrot smile:\par
\par
"Really, Kitts, it was--hardly cricket not to have warned us!"\par
\par
While Mrs. Charterhouse added in tones of iced velvet:\par
\par
"Regard me also as prone beneath Miss Saxham's Number Eight shoes.  Did\par
you say her _fiance_ was a cousin of Lord Norwater's?  Not possibly the\par
son of the uncle who died quite recently?  Captain the Hon. Noel\par
Sherbrand, late of the Royal Gunners....  My husband used to know him\par
before--people left off!"\par
\par
"It is the same.  He muddled his career somehow, and--most people cut\par
him!  But he is _dead_," said Margot very deliberately, "and his son, if\par
we have no--" the delicate cheeks flushed with sudden vivid\par
crimson--"his son is perfectly tophole and Franky's next heir.  We met\par
him in June in Paris, and so did Pat Saxham!  How do any of you know she\par
didn't tint her hair to please _him_."\par
\par
"Possibly she did!  But, according to Sir Thomas--it was the other man\par
who paid!"\par
\par
"Odd, isn't it?  In this world," said the Goblin with her crackling\par
laugh, "the other man invariably pays the bill! And so the young\par
gentleman over there--who is quite remarkably good-looking in the blond\par
Norman style--and who is going to marry Miss Saxham--succeeds to Lord\par
Norwater in--a certain eventuality!  May one be permitted to hope, dear\par
Lady Norwater, that Fate and yourself will combine fortuitously, to keep\par
the cousin out of the House of Peers!"\par
\par
"Rude, ill-bred, horrid woman!" thought Margot, clenching her little\par
teeth to keep back these epithets. "How dare she twit me with--_that_!\par
How dare--"  Then her hot flush sank away and a mist came before her\par
eyes, and she would have fallen, but that Trixie Wastwood jumped up from\par
the sofa and threw about the little figure a kind, supporting arm.\par
\par
"I've got you!  You're not going to faint, Kittums, are you?  Forgive\par
us, my dinkie!  What _pigs_ we have been!"\par
\par
"Heckling the tomtit for defending the saffron-crested blackbird!  I\par
rather agree with you," admitted Mrs. Charterhouse as Margot freed\par
herself, saying it was nothing, and proudly moved away.  "We women are\par
horribly spiteful," continued Cynthia.  "Yes, we are spiteful, Lady\par
Wathe!  I am perfectly in earnest.  What is the reason?  Will anything\par
cure us?  Do somebody tell me! Colonel Charterhouse would say it is\par
because we eat too much rich food, walk too little, automobile too much,\par
and want some useful work or other to occupy our minds!  He is coming\par
here to lunch with me--he was quite touchingly anxious to be invited!"\par
Her beautiful eyes widened as the swing-doors thudded behind three\par
entering masculine figures, "Why, here he is with Lord Norwater, and\par
your boy, Trixie!  All three in khaki!  What a day we are having!"\par
\par
She added, as her handsome middle-aged Colonel made his spurred way\par
through the ever-thickening crush to her:\par
\par
"I am enlightened!  So _this_ was your surprise!"\par
\par
"Not half as big as mine when I found they were willing to take me.\par
'Fit as a fiddle,' according to the M. O. Gad!"--he went on, as his wife\par
made room for him on the settle by her side--"as willingly as though he\par
had been somebody else's husband," Lady Wathe said subsequently--"It's\par
to my golf I owe it--these A.M.S. sawbones finding me in the pink!  And\par
instead of a back-seat, what do you think they've given me?  Command of\par
the Third Reserve Battalion of the blessed old Regiment, the Loyal North\par
Linkshires, _vice_ Crowe-Pinckney, kicked out by a gouty toe! ... How's\par
that for an oldster of fifty-five, ... Eh, what?"  His chuckle was that\par
of a Fourth Form athlete picked to supply a gap in the School Eleven.\par
And Cynthia's beautiful eyes, as she slipped her hand into the\par
Colonel's, looked at him as the boy's mother's might have looked upon\par
her son.\par
\par
Lady Wastwood's Pierrot smile might have played upon the reunited couple\par
mockingly, but that the unexpected apparition of her boy Wastwood in\par
single-starred khaki, adorned with the badge of a crack Hussar Regiment,\par
girt with the Sam Browne and narrow officer's shoulder-strap, and clad\par
as to the legs in spurred brown butcher-boots--dimmed her bright green\par
eyes and brought a choke into her throat.  Wastwood the son was so like\par
Wastwood the father--killed at Magersfontein in 1900,--whom Trixie, for\par
no reason apparently, had romantically adored.  A burly young man, pink\par
as a baby, with thick fair hair growing down within two inches of his\par
eyebrows, small, fierce blue eyes, and a huge roaring voice, softened\par
down now to a tender bellow as he answered a pale girl's eager question\par
with:\par
\par
"Well, I can't say exactly when we're going to the Front, but I hope to\par
Christmas it'll be soon!"\par
\par
Wastwood's engagement to the girl had been announced only the week\par
previously in the Society Columns of the leading dailies.  Now, while\par
Wastwood's younger brother Jerry anguished in the throes of a final\par
Exam, at Sandhurst, the said Jerry being set upon getting a Commission\par
in time to go to the Front with one of the First Divisions--his elder\par
sat on a Club sofa and made love to the girl Jerry was subsequently to\par
marry.  For not only Wastwood's title, but his vacant Commission as a\par
Lieutenant in the Dapple Greys and his sweetheart went to his junior\par
after Mons.\par
\par
There was a lot of family and regimental re-shuffling and re-dealing,\par
you will remember, after Mons.\par
\par
The leaven of the Great Awakening was working in the souls of these\par
worldly-minded, ultra-modern men and women, even as the crowd in the\par
rooms grew denser, as the buzz of talk became almost solid, and khaki\par
mingled with the brilliant toilettes.  Hardly a man but wore dead-leaf\par
brown.  Wives were entertaining their husbands, mothers were lunching\par
their sons, that day, at the multitudinous little tables in the great\par
and lesser dining-rooms,--there was a revival of old code-words, an\par
interchange of almost forgotten pet-names, a resurrection of ancient\par
jokes, when the atmosphere seemed dangerously charged with emotion. How\par
many Last Sacraments of renewed love were eaten and drunk by husbands\par
and wives who, estranged for years, were to be reunited by the War, and\par
parted by the War until the Day when Wars shall be no more.\par
\par
\par
That a tall young man in grey tweed with a crape armlet should sit\par
opposite Patrine that day at Margot's special table was one of the\par
thousand miracles already wrought.\par
\par
Sherbrand had shelved all recollection of that June adventure in the\par
Bois de Boulogne, when a flushed young husband in immaculate top-hat and\par
frock-coat had thanked an angry young man in waterproof overalls and\par
gabardine for not chopping his wife into kedgeree.\par
\par
Could one be angry any more when this little human dragon-fly was what\par
Patrine called "a frightful pal" of hers.  Thank Heaven!  Patrine had\par
known nothing of the cousinship when she had answered Sherbrand's plain\par
question, "Will you marry me?" with an assent:\par
\par
"_Sooner than not!_"\par
\par
"Then--it is settled?"\par
\par
"Yes, you poor dear!  If you think me worth having!"\par
\par
_Worth having_!  Sherbrand's glorious Patrine.  Whom to be near was\par
heaven on earth.  Whom to obey was a lover's luxury, even when she had\par
issued the mandate:\par
\par
"Now, you must come to the Club and lunch with me, and meet my friends.\par
Do be decent to them!"\par
\par
Perhaps you can see Sherbrand bowing rather stiffly to Margot and\par
accepting with the briefest hesitation the smallest of offered hands.\par
\par
"I thought it must be the same!--I was certain there couldn't be two\par
Flying Sherbrands.  Pat!--Mr. Sherbrand can't deny the relationship,\par
though he disapproves of Franky and me most fearfully.  You'll have to\par
teach him," went on the coaxing little voice, "that we're lots and lots\par
nicer than he thinks us!  For we've got to be friends," said Kittums,\par
"if you and my dear Pat are going to be married! No time like the\par
present!  Can't we begin now?"\par
\par
What a vivid little face it was, though there were tired marks like\par
faint bruises under the great dark eyes, and the rose-flush in the\par
cheeks was less bright than it had seemed in June.  He released the tiny\par
jewelled fingers, and found himself presented to the husband.\par
\par
"Frightfully glad to meet you--more reasons than one!"\par
\par
Franky, slim, sleek-headed, and dapper in unblemished Regulation\par
tea-leaf, held out his hand, saying as he looked the other squarely in\par
the eyes:\par
\par
"If I had known your Home address, I should like to have dropped a line\par
to you, when I--when I saw the newspaper yesterday."\par
\par
"My mother lives at Bournemouth.  My father had been an invalid for\par
years.  I go down to-day by the afternoon train."\par
\par
"Ah!  Please remember me to my--Aunt Jeannette."\par
\par
From what dusty shelf of memories had Franky reached down the name of\par
his uncle's unknown wife?  But it sounded pleasantly to Mrs. Sherbrand's\par
son.  The cloud upon his forehead cleared away, and his cold sea-blue\par
eyes began to thaw into kindness:\par
\par
"I'd like a word with you in private.  Do you mind comin' out of this\par
clackshop into the vest*i*bulee?" Franky went on, quoting his favourite\par
Jimmy Greggson, and with a word to Margot and a glance on Sherbrand's\par
part at Patrine, the two men passed through the swing-doors.  Here\par
Franky wheeled, and said with effort:\par
\par
"This is a bit subsequent! but--if there's time available and the date\par
of my uncle's funeral doesn't happen to be fixed, I should like to\par
say--"  He grew furiously red and began to stammer: "My father ...\par
myself ... Dash! how brutally I bungle!  But my uncle has a right to--to\par
lie in the family vault with his ancestors.  It's at Whins--the Church\par
is in the Castle grounds.  I can guarantee that my father--every\par
facility--sympathy--proper respect--"  He broke down.  Sherbrand\par
answered, now the cooler of the two:\par
\par
"You are very kind, Lord Norwater.  My mother has already received a\par
telegram from Lord Mitchelborough conveying a message to the same\par
effect."\par
\par
"I engineered that!" thought Franky complacently.  But he was fish-dumb.\par
Sherbrand went on:\par
\par
"She would thank you, as I do, gratefully.  But my father--would have\par
preferred to be buried where he died!"\par
\par
"Good egg!  And now there's another thing to get off my chest," said\par
Franky.  "You know you stand in for the Viscounty when I succeed my\par
father, or if I get knocked out in this scrap--supposing I should kick\par
without heirs! That being so, suppose you bury the hatchet and lunch\par
with us?  Wouldn't in Paris--perhaps you will now?  The War seems to rub\par
up old saws like new somehow.  That copy-book tag about Blood bein'\par
thicker than water! that's one of the ones I mean.  In case my wife got\par
left--do you tumble?"--the ambiguous term was quite expressive--"I'd\par
like to think that you were--would be kind to her!"\par
\par
"I should certainly--in that case--try to do what I could."  A certain\par
physical and mental resemblance showed between these two long-legged,\par
lightly-built, clean-made Sherbrands, standing together talking of grave\par
matters, with candour and simplicity and British avoidance of sentiment\par
and excess of words.\par
\par
"But,"--Sherbrand found himself yielding to an impulse of confidence in\par
the owner of the brown eyes that were some inches below his own, "this\par
War is my chance!  I'm a certified pilot-aviator and constructor and\par
engineer. Perhaps there'll be a chink in the Royal Flying Corps for\par
me--and many another fellow like me--before long--I hope, not very long!\par
For my father's sake as much as for my own, I'm bound to make good--you\par
understand?"\par
\par
The brown eyes understood.  His kindred blood warmed to the look in\par
them.\par
\par
"He knew--my father knew that he had failed in life through his own\par
fault.  He did not resent his brother's attitude.  He bore the\par
consequences more or less patiently, and when he died he left the\par
cleansing of his name to me.  Not that he was as badly to blame as\par
people thought.  He was born without sufficient of the quality\par
called--objectivity. It's the power that keeps a man slogging, slogging\par
in one groove without getting mechanical or stupid, as long as he attain\par
his ends or can serve his country by keeping on.  It's\par
_indispensable_!"--he emphasised the word, his strong blue-grey eyes\par
full on Franky's--"as indispensable as lymph in your inner ear-tubes.\par
Without it you can't keep a level balance--whether you stand, or walk,\par
or fly!"\par
\par
"Miss Saxham--knows, I suppose?"\par
\par
A flush crept up through Sherbrand's tanning:\par
\par
"I have told her.  It wasn't pleasant.  But she--likes me enough to\par
overlook it.  She--seems to think I should never fail in that way!  I\par
hope to God I never shall!"  The old boyish terror of inherited weakness\par
cropped up in the tone of the man grown.  "It would be horrible to\par
suspect the bacillus of slackness lurking in my blood!  If there is--the\par
sooner I get scrapped, the better for her and for me!"\par
\par
"Well, you've chosen the--kind of career that is going to use up a good\par
many men pretty quickly."  Franky was warming more and more to this big\par
blond, candid cousin. "Not that I think there's much of the slacker\par
about you. Few chaps more fit and nervy--that is, going by looks, you\par
know!  But if the Kaiser's Flying Men can shoot on the wing as well as\par
they brag they can"--his brown eyes were watchful for a change in the\par
other's face--"then----"\par
\par
"Then I tumble out of my sky, a dead bird!" said Sherbrand, squaring his\par
broad shoulders, "and someone luckier fills my place!"\par
\par
"Thumbs up!  Ten to one you'd come down with a broken wing or so."\par
There was something that touched Franky's latent quality of imagination\par
in the fellow's queer way of saying "my sky."  "This cousin of mine is a\par
handsome fellow," he said to himself, "and a plucky one.  And--by the\par
Great Brass Hat!--now I come to think of it--the livin' image of old Sir\par
Roger Sherbrand--his and my great-grandfather--goin' by the portrait in\par
the gallery at Whins."\par
\par
"So you're firm on joinin' the Flying Corps..." he went on, feeling for\par
the moustache which had been reduced to Regulation toothbrush size.\par
"Good egg You! Wish you all the sporting chances----"\par
\par
"And better luck," said Sherbrand drily, "with Bird of War No. II. than\par
I had with No. I.!"\par
\par
"You're building a new 'plane?"  The brown eyes were alight with\par
interest.\par
\par
"Rather!  Come and have a look at her one day."\par
\par
"Like a shot, if only I'd time!  Did she tot to a hatful of money?"\par
\par
"Something under L700.  L500 of that goes for the new 'Gnome' engine.\par
You see that German--"  Sherbrand broke off.\par
\par
"I remember!  Pretty rough on you, that North Sea crossin' business.\par
Must have been an awful loss.  Look here!"  Franky reddened again and\par
began to flounder. "Could I--couldn't I--help with the boodle?  Got L700\par
lying by idle.  Frightfully glad if you'd let me chip in!--just in a\par
cousinly sort o' way!"\par
\par
"I am much obliged to you, Lord Norwater."\par
\par
Confound the fellow! how he froze at the least hint of patronage.  He\par
went on, holding his head high:\par
\par
"You are very kind, but I am not poor, unless as poverty is understood\par
by people of your world.  Apart from what my profession brings me I have\par
something in the way of income.  My mother's brother left me a sum of\par
money that brings in yearly over L200."  He went on as Franky regarded\par
with unaffected interest the man who wasn't poor on two hundred _per\par
annum_: "The principal--I suppose it tots up to L6,000--I shall\par
naturally settle on my wife."\par
\par
He warmed and brightened with the utterance of the word.  His cold eyes\par
grew soft and his brows smoothed pleasantly.  He said with a glowing\par
pride, and a kind of brave shyness that a woman who loved him would have\par
adored:\par
\par
"I have said nothing yet to Miss Saxham about my hopes of a\par
Commission--I suppose for fear of not pulling the thing off.  But the\par
moment it comes along I shall persuade her to marry me.  We'll be man\par
and wife before I fly for the Front."\par
\par
As cocky as though he had landed the biggest catch in the matrimonial\par
waters, thought Franky, instead of that great, slangy, galumphing young\par
woman without a halfpenny at her back.  But he did the amiable, in a way\par
characteristic of Franky, ushering the guest back to the luncheon-room,\par
introducing "my cousin" to people worth knowing, doing the honours with\par
a pleasant cordiality that won upon Sherbrand more and more.\par
\par
Sherbrand took leave directly after lunch, saying that he had to catch\par
the afternoon express for Bournemouth.  He had left his bag and\par
suit-case in the hall-porter's care. Would Patrine?--Patrine read the\par
entreaty in the hiatus and yielded to it, saying Yes, she would drive\par
with him, and see him off from Waterloo.\par
\par
"It's lovely of you!" Sherbrand said to her gratefully as they rose.\par
She gave him her cordial smile and a soft glance from the long eyes.\par
They took leave of their hosts and passed out together, heads slewing as\par
the tall young figures went by.\par
\par
Once in a taxi, spinning down Short Street, Sherbrand possessed himself\par
of the hand he coveted.  Its warm strong, answering clasp thrilled him\par
to speechlessness.  He looked at the long white fingers intertwined with\par
his own, and asked himself whether he were deserving of a happiness too\par
great to be credited.  When her shoulder touched his, its warm creamy\par
whiteness gleaming through the dead-white of her thin sleeve, his heart\par
drummed until it seemed as though she could not but hear it.  But his\par
was not the only heart that beat....\par
\par
"Thank you."  It was her rich warm voice speaking close by his ear.\par
"Thank you for being so nice to my Kittums!  She is the truest little\par
soul going.  We have been chums ever since I joined the Club.  Never\par
quarrelled once--until she made up her mind to marry Franky----"\par
\par
"And now you're going to marry Franky's first cousin."  Sherbrand\par
laughed rather breathlessly.  "'Marry' ... 'Marriage.'  Two splendid\par
words with meanings and meanings beyond meanings packed into them.\par
Isn't it wonderful? ..."  He gripped the warm white hand in his strong\par
brown one.  "Pat, your pulses are playing a tune!"\par
\par
"So are yours," she answered in a low tone.\par
\par
"What is it?"  He bent his head and set his lips in a swift caress to\par
the back of the white hand.  Then he turned it gently over and looked\par
earnestly at the blue wrist-veins. They were full and throbbing\par
tumultuously.  Her blood was answering to the call of his.  He set a\par
second swift kiss upon them and his voice was unsteady as he said:\par
\par
"I know the name of the tune, my wonder.  Patrine! Love!--it's the\par
_Wedding March_!"\par
\par
"Whose?  Grieg's, or Wagner's in _Lohengrin_, or Haydn's?"\par
\par
"Neither Wagner's nor Haydn's nor Grieg's.  Yours and mine!  I told Lord\par
Norwater to-day that I meant to make sure of you before I fly for the\par
Front."\par
\par
"You're going to the Front?  Oh!--why?"  Her long eyes looked at him\par
with sharp terror in them.  He answered:\par
\par
"When the Powers that be offer me a Commission in the Royal Flying\par
Corps."\par
\par
"I see."  She breathed freely.  "And so--we are not to be married until\par
then?"\par
\par
"Would you--to-morrow, if I----"\par
\par
"You know I would!"  Her voice broke over him in a wave of tenderness.\par
"You've made me love you--so dreadfully, Alan.  Now if the little tin\par
gods hear us--the spiteful little gods who spoil people's\par
lives--something will happen to part us, soon."\par
\par
His arm went round her and gathered her against him. He said with a\par
great thrill of triumph:\par
\par
"If the Great God is for us we can defy the little tin devils!  It was\par
He who made us for each other, brought us together--will bring us closer\par
still!"\par
\par
He added, as a handsome boy of nineteen or twenty, dressed at the zenith\par
of the fashion, and already showing the worn lines of habitual\par
dissipation, flashed by driven in a silver-grey Lanchester, with a\par
notorious Cyprian enthroned at his side:\par
\par
"How can I thank Him enough for what He has done for me?  How many\par
temptations He has helped me resist, that I might come to you clean\par
to-day!"\par
\par
"Were any of the temptations like Mrs. Mallison?"  She had freed her\par
hand from his, and now leaned forwards, hiding her clouded face from\par
Sherbrand under the pretext of following the grey car with her eyes.\par
"That was little Wyvenhoe with her....  How young he is!  And how old\par
she must be!  Why, I've seen her portrait in a Book of Beauty dated\par
forty years back--with a chignon and waterfall. They called her the\par
Marble Marvel in those days, didn't they?  Before she pitched her cap\par
over the windmill, and made hay of the Prunes and Prisms.  Now she acts\par
in Music Hall sketches--has a voice like a raven's, paints a\par
quarter-of-an-inch thick, and exploits Eton boys.  Is anything the\par
matter?"\par
\par
Sherbrand had suddenly started and pulled his watch out. Now he rapped\par
on the glass at the back of the chauffeur, leaned out of the window and\par
spoke to the man, and resumed his seat, answering:\par
\par
"The matter is that I had forgotten an important appointment.  I can\par
manage to keep it by the skin of my eyelids by taking the three o'clock\par
train to Bournemouth instead of the two-thirty Express.  You won't mind?\par
You'll come with me and wait for me?"\par
\par
"Not a little bit! ..." she answered to the one question and to the\par
other: "Of course I will!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER LI*\par
\par
                           *THE INWARD VOICE*\par
\par
\par
The taxi, arrested and reversed on its way to Piccadilly Circus, was\par
soon speeding Westwards.  It whirred up Berkeley Street, traversed\par
Berkeley Square, and turned into a short street ending in railings,\par
enclosing grass wonderfully green for August, clipped bushes of\par
evergreens, and some autumn-foliaged planes.\par
\par
"We'll keep the man.  I'll take his number.  He'll look after my kit for\par
me.  Let me help you out, dear!"\par
\par
He opened a gate in the railings and let her through.  A large double\par
house, with many windows, severely screened with brown curtains and wire\par
blinds, loomed behind them, commanding the oblong patch of London green.\par
The Modern Gothic porch of a lofty building of smoke-darkened freestone\par
rose up before them.  Patrine said under her breath, realising the\par
ecclesiastical character of the edifice:\par
\par
"Great Scott!  It's a church!"\par
\par
But Sherbrand, who had stayed to shut the gate in the railings did not\par
hear the tabooed expletive.  He caught her up and turned the massive\par
iron handle of the porch-door which was braced by bands of iron with\par
trefoil heads, and studded with heavy nails.  They went down two shallow\par
steps into an oblong, vaulted chamber, very cool and dark and fragrant,\par
tesselated with squares of black and white stone.  Slabs of black marble\par
lined the walls to the height of a tall man.  An inscription in Early\par
English lettering, cut into the black background and gilded, caught\par
Patrine's eye in passing.  She read beneath the symbol of the Cross:\par
\par
[Illustration: Cross. "Sodality of the Blessed Sacrament"]\par
\par
\par
Under were lists of names, all male, ranged alphabetically. Her quick\par
eye dropped to the initial S. and found Sherbrand there.  But when she\par
looked for her companion, he was waiting hat in hand, at a door some\par
distance beyond them.\par
\par
"You will come in and wait for me?" he whispered as she came towards\par
him.\par
\par
"Why not?  As well here as anywhere!"  He opened the door and she passed\par
in.\par
\par
To Patrine's left hand, close to the door by which they had entered, was\par
a small unpretending altar supporting the tinted image of an emaciated,\par
bearded monk in a black robe girdled with a white cord.  A clustered\par
pillar of red and white marble supported a shallow basin containing a\par
little water.  Patrine shrugged as Sherbrand dipped his fingers and made\par
upon brow and breast the sacred Sign.  Then he seemed to\par
hesitate--dipped again and held the wetted finger tips towards her,\par
evidently courting her touch.  She shook her head hastily.  Her eyes\par
swept purposely past his, scanning the vast interior.  They were\par
standing in the shorter southern transept of what was _some_ church.\par
\par
The vast nave was dark and cool, full of silence and shadow and the\par
perfume of flowers and incense, mingled with a fragrance far subtler\par
than these.  Pillars of richest Modern Gothic design supported the roof,\par
whose forest of rich dark timbers showed little adornment, except at the\par
Sanctuary end.  Here coffering, diapering, and gilding made for\par
splendour; rich marble cased the pillars and floored the stately choir\par
with its rows of stalls, wrought in dark wood, elaborately carved.  The\par
north transept housed the organ, a towering instrument of many pipes.\par
The scarlet cushion on the vacant organ-bench, the book of chants left\par
upon the rack, the black and yellow-white of the well-used keys, the\par
numbered heads of the stops, showed through the lattice-work of a high\par
wrought-iron screen, wonderfully painted and gilt.  Between Patrine and\par
the nave was a pulpit of red and white marble like the pillars, with a\par
carved sounding-board of obviously ancient work.  Rows of pews flanked\par
the wide central aisle and the two smaller, and on the right of a lofty\par
oaken screen that masked the west door, with the mellow light of a great\par
rose-window falling on it, towered a huge Crucifix in black marble,\par
upholding a white tortured Figure whose drooping thorn-crowned Head,\par
like His hands and feet and side, dripped with crimson....  Patrine\par
winced at the sight, and turned hastily away.\par
\par
Now she was looking over the head of Sherbrand, who knelt before her\par
upright and motionless,--at the High Altar, backed with a noble\par
triptych, its three panels displaying the Annunciation, the Visitation,\par
and the Nativity.  A silver lamp depending by chains from the centre of\par
the Sanctuary roof burned with a small steady flame before the\par
Tabernacle--standing between tall tapers burning in gleaming\par
candlesticks, and vases of huge white golden-anthered August\par
lilies--hiding behind its broidered curtains and golden doors, the\par
Ineffable Mystery.\par
\par
"Come!" Sherbrand's whisper said, close at her ear as he rose up.  She\par
turned and followed him down a side-aisle. "Sit here!" he signed to her,\par
pointing to a narrow bench. He waited until she was seated, laid his hat\par
and stick beside her, gave her a grave smile, bent his knees once more,\par
looking towards the High Altar and moved noiselessly away.\par
\par
Turning her head to follow him with her eyes, Patrine saw that the large\par
dark church was not as empty as she had supposed.  Kneeling or seated\par
figures of men and women were scattered here and there amongst the\par
wilderness of empty pews.  The serried rows of rush-bottomed\par
kneeling-chairs in either side-aisle showed aggregations of people, ten\par
or a dozen together, chiefly in the neighbourhood of certain narrow\par
wooden doors appertaining to small structures that might be little\par
chapels or vestries, set between groups of pillars in regular sequence\par
down the length of the side-walls. Still following Sherbrand's figure\par
with her eyes she saw him knock at one of the doors, wait as though for\par
an answer, and enter.  As the door swung towards her, she saw that it\par
bore a name in gilt letters within an oval on the upper panel.  Each of\par
the doors, a questing glance satisfied her, bore a name.\par
\par
Of course the little wooden chapels were confessionals. Was Confession\par
the important business that necessitated Sherbrand's losing a train and\par
foregoing the company of Patrine to the station, a favour so eagerly\par
sought and so ardently received?  Her red lips curled a little at the\par
corners as she turned her face back towards the High Altar, rising\par
within the low barrier of the red and white marble Communion-rail.  So\par
remote and pure and set apart with its tall, shining lights and gleaming\par
vases of pure white lilies, its snow-white silk frontal embroidered with\par
a golden ray-surrounded Chalice, its fair white linen Altar-cloth, with\par
a running border of Old English lettering in dark rusty red:\par
\par
             "He had borne our Infirmities and Carried out\par
              Sorrows.  He was Wounded for our Iniquities.\par
                     He was Bruised for our sins."\par
\par
\par
The words seemed to have a physical as well as mental force and\par
impressiveness.  It was as though they swept from the high white Table\par
through the fragrant, wax-lit stillness of the Sanctuary, winnowing the\par
still, spicy air of the dark nave and the lighter side-aisles as with\par
wide, powerful, unseen wings.  And despite the presence of nearly a\par
hundred people scattered about the great building, the stillness was\par
extraordinary.  It got on the nerves.\par
\par
Almost awfully upon the nerves.  For a long way behind her, where the\par
shadowy dusk brooded thickest, and the white tortured Figure of the\par
Crucified hung drooping from the great Cross of black marble against the\par
background of the towering oak screen, it was as though the first great\par
drops of a thunder-shower were falling, _pat, pat, pat!_ upon the\par
pavement below.\par
\par
Merely a trick of imagination--and yet it tortured.  One knew by\par
sensations like these that one had been frightfully overstrained of\par
late.  One had done lots of things one regretted--several things one\par
disliked to think of; one thing that made one hate oneself sometimes\par
with a very fury of intensity, when one wasn't too busy hating _him_.\par
But since he was drowned, one had felt it scarcely cricket to go on\par
expending fierce resentment and savage disgust and acute loathing in\par
that direction.  One heaped it on the living of the two gross, sensual\par
offenders.  Oh God! when Sherbrand had said in that tone of triumph:\par
\par
"_I come to you clean!_"\par
\par
How inexpressibly one had abominated oneself.  How one had shrunk\par
against the side of the taxicab, pretending to look after wretched\par
little decadent Wyvenhoe and the unquenchable Mrs. Mallison--feigning\par
sudden absorption in the Piccadilly shop-windows, to escape those clear\par
undoubting eyes that pierced one to the very soul.  To be thought good\par
when one was wicked, pure when one was the other thing; believed candid\par
when one was a living lie. Ah!--that not only pierced but scorched.\par
\par
\par
If anybody, a month or so back, had asked Patrine: "Are you a\par
Christian?" she would have retorted: "What are you playing at?  Of\par
course I am--I suppose!"  Of late that conjectural Being she had called\par
God had receded, faded, grown dimmer, and vanished.  But here in the\par
stillness, looking towards the Altar, she was conscious as those\par
candle-flames went up like prayers from faithful souls, that Good and\par
Evil were living warring Forces.  You chose White or Black deliberately,\par
and when Death came--it was anything but the end.\par
\par
Her hair stiffened slightly on her scalp and a light shudder thrilled\par
through her.  She felt with a growing awe, and sense of dreadful\par
certainty, that Someone was looking at her. And to relieve the\par
insupportable tension she stretched out her hand, and took a squat,\par
thick little book from the shelf below the seat in front of her.  It was\par
a copy of the Douai translation from the Latin Vulgate of the Bible, and\par
there was a purple marker where she opened it, in the middle of the Book\par
of Job.\par
\par
"_Power and terror are with Him...._"\par
\par
That was the first line that caught her eye.  A little lower on the page\par
came:\par
\par
"_Was it not Him that made life?  Hell is naked before Him and there is\par
no covering for destruction....  He stretched out the North over the\par
empty place, and hangeth the earth upon nothing._\par
\par
"_He hath set bounds about the waters, till light and darkness come to\par
an end...._\par
\par
"_The pillars of heaven tremble and dread at His beck.  By His power the\par
seas are suddenly gathered together, and His Wisdom hath struck the\par
proud one._\par
\par
"_His Spirit hath adorned the heavens ... and seeing we have heard\par
scarce a little drop of His Word, who shall be able to withstand the\par
thunder of His greatness?_"\par
\par
It was like a Voice speaking--a Voice of inconceivable magnitude.  It\par
made one go cold, asking oneself the question: What if sin were an\par
insult to Him?  A scrap of filth flung in the Face of One who created\par
the atom, the protoplasm, the cell, and the bacillus, and built from\par
these in His own Image, Man.\par
\par
\par
Sitting in the stilly duskiness the woman He had made shut her eyes and\par
tried to envisage Him.  He was not the God of the Curate's\par
Confirmation-class, nor the God the Anglican Vicar of the West End\par
Church preached about, but a Being the hem of whose garment extends\par
beyond the confines of Space, and in whose lap lies Eternity.  Infinite\par
Goodness, infinite Love, infinite Purity, infinite Beauty, He could\par
stoop to care for the little beings of His Workmanship so much, that for\par
them He did not hesitate to sacrifice Himself in the Person of His Only\par
Son.  Did not love such as this make wilful sin an insult to Him in that\par
Son's Person? Wasn't it--pretty rough on Our Saviour--to have poured out\par
His Blood upon the Cross of Calvary as an atonement for the sins of men\par
like dead von Herrnung, and women like Patrine Saxham, and know them\par
still so beastly, so prurient, so base, so vile? ... It began to dawn\par
upon Patrine, still possessed by that strange hallucination of the Blood\par
that dripped heavily from the tortured Body on the great black Cross\par
behind her, how it might be that evil wilfully committed, opened its\par
Wounds afresh.  Drove the thorns anew into the drooping Head of the\par
Crucified, pierced once more the Heart, that inexhaustible fountain of\par
love....\par
\par
"_O! all you that pass by ... attend and see if there be any sorrow like\par
unto My Sorrow._"\par
\par
The words came cropping up through layers of sentences heard and\par
forgotten, clearly as though a voice had spoken them at her side.\par
\par
\par
This afternoon the headlines of papers had shrieked of horrors.  You\par
remember that at seven o'clock in the morning two German Army Corps had\par
poured into Belgium by the eleven strategic railways that provided for\par
The Day. The vast grey-green flood of marching men, the huge python-like\par
columns of machine-guns, the splendidly-horsed batteries of field\par
artillery, the Brobdingnagian siege howitzers thundering behind their\par
traction-engines, the miles of motor- and horse-drawn transport-waggons,\par
carts, and lorries, blotted out the familiar features of the landscape,\par
as, preceded by massed brigades of cavalry, with squadrons of Field\par
Flying Service aeroplanes reconnoitring three thousand feet overhead,\par
the hosts of Germany rolled down towards the banks of the Meuse.\par
\par
Directly in line of them rose the fortified City of Liege, termed "the\par
Birmingham of Belgium," holding in the suburb of Seraign, five miles\par
distant from the city, the huge Cockerill machine-plant and foundry, one\par
of the largest ironworks in the world.  They had stayed three hours at\par
the frontier station of Vise, a Belgium Custom House town of less than\par
4,000 inhabitants, where a few squadrons of Belgian Cavalry and the\par
Belgian 12th Line Regiment, aided by some heroic peasants, farmers, and\par
townspeople had risen up with desperate gallantry to oppose their\par
inevitable advance.\par
\par
They had written the sign-manual of the Hun upon the ashes of Vise in\par
the blood of its massacred inhabitants. Frightfulness, the many-headed\par
hydra, was uncaged and let loose ere they rolled on to Liege peeved by\par
their three hours' intolerable delay.  While I who write and you who\par
read far from the sound of fusillades, or the crash of shells or the\par
yells of peasants dying amongst the flames of burning houses, learned of\par
these deeds from the shrilly clamorous headlines, and asked one another\par
with raised eyebrows, in incredulous voices: "_Can these hideous things\par
possibly have been done?_"\par
\par
Patrine had no doubt that they had been done!--were being done even\par
while she sat waiting in Sherbrand's church for Sherbrand.  Did she not\par
know von Herrnung?  Were not his fellow-officers and the soldiers he and\par
they commanded, lustful, brutal, cruel, rapacious, arrogant, and\par
pitiless even as he?  He was a Type--not the isolated example of a new\par
species.  It would not be easily stamped out; its dominating\par
characteristics would write themselves upon a conquered race.  Those\par
outraged wives, those violated daughters of Belgium would live to see it\par
reproduced in the living fruit of their humiliation.  What honest man\par
could bear to stoop over his wife's bedside and meet the eyes of the\par
Enemy looking at him--from the face of a new-born child!\par
\par
A rigor of horror seized upon her body and shook it.  Her jaw dropped,\par
her eyes closed as though they shrank and withered under their\par
contracting lids.  She slid from her seat and fell upon her knees\par
helplessly.  Her head sank forwards upon the hands that rose\par
instinctively to hide her face.  In the same instant Sherbrand's low\par
voice speaking behind her turned the heart in her bosom to ice.\par
\par
"Dearest--I am ready, that is if you are?  My keeping you was\par
unavoidable.  I am going to Communion with my mother, before the Funeral\par
Mass to-morrow, and I wanted to make my Confession first.  Has the time\par
seemed long?"\par
\par
"Not long.  Shall we go now?"\par
\par
He bent the knee to the High Altar and moved with Patrine down the nave\par
towards an altar dedicated to the Virgin Mother, that was on the south\par
side of the church near the great west door.  Wax tapers of several\par
sizes burned in a brass stand beside the tiny altar-rail.  Sherbrand\par
lighted three tapers and placed them, felt in his waistcoat-pocket for a\par
bit of silver and balanced it on the slotted top of the money-box too\par
gorged with pennies to admit of the slender sixpenny bit.  Then with a\par
beautiful, devotional simplicity he knelt upon the narrow blue\par
golden-starred cushion for a moment, looking up at the gracious veiled\par
head that bent above.\par
\par
But for the modernity of the tweed clothes, the pose of the athletic,\par
lightly-built body would, with the mellowed light from the great rose\par
window falling on the keen bronzed face and thick fair hair, have\par
suggested a knight at prayer.  In a moment he rose.  They returned as\par
they had come, passed through the chapter-house of the Sodality, and\par
issued through the door into the garden.  She said, as he triumphantly\par
possessed himself of the dear white hand:\par
\par
"Tell me, when you lighted and placed those three candles and knelt\par
down--what did you intend--what was it for?  A practical insurance\par
against a railway-accident?"\par
\par
The dull, ill-timed gibe was no sooner uttered than she sickened with\par
self-contempt.  For Sherbrand answered with direct simplicity:\par
\par
"Well, no!  Call my three candles a reminder that I have asked Our\par
Lady's help and protection and guidance for three dear people.  My\par
father, my mother, and my wife that is to be.  For myself I asked that I\par
might never disappoint you.  You don't know how I shall try to live up\par
to your belief in me!"\par
\par
"You dear boy!"  Touched to the quick response of tears she could barely\par
falter: "You're a million times too good for me, if only you knew!"\par
\par
"I know this--that the wide world doesn't hold another woman like my\par
woman!  Why, Pat, the very sound of your voice lashes all the blood in\par
me into big red roaring waves of love."\par
\par
"'Big red roaring waves.'  Oh, Alan!"\par
\par
She laughed, driving back the hot salt tears that stung her eyelids.\par
The taxi was waiting at the corner of Blount Street, patiently ticking\par
out twopence.  Sherbrand whistled and it approached them.  But this time\par
Patrine did not enter.  She could not now drive to Waterloo. It was\par
much, much too late.  She refused even to be dropped anywhere.  She\par
infinitely preferred walking. It was quite a pleasant stroll from there\par
to Harley Street.\par
\par
So they wrung hands and looked in each other's eyes and parted.  When\par
the taxi vanished round the corner of Blount Street, the tall,\par
gallantly-borne figure in the golden-braided hat and pale rose gown\par
began to walk swiftly towards Grosvenor Square.  Suddenly it paused,\par
wheeled, and returned upon its paces, passed through the gate in the\par
railings and disappeared into the church.\par
\par
In bed that night in the chintz-hung room at Harley Street, Patrine,\par
recalling the experience that had followed the yielding to that\par
irresistible prompting, wondered if it had actually taken place, or were\par
woven of the tissue of dreams.\par
\par
Kneeling upon a bast matting-covered hassock behind the door of the\par
narrow little wooden cell into which she had slipped as a tall,\par
grey-haired officer in Service khaki passed out,--she had rested her\par
elbows upon a narrow ledge before her and peered through a close grating\par
of bronze wire at a figure dimly descried beyond.\par
\par
The priest was white-haired and of small stature.  A meagre ray of light\par
falling from above upon the hands clasped over the ends of the narrow\par
stole of violet-purple that hung loosely about his neck, showed them\par
wasted and yellow-white and deeply wrinkled.  By the testimony of the\par
hands he was an old man.  Something in the manner of her address must\par
have struck him as unusual.  She had not spoken six words in her quick,\par
hot, stammering whisper before he lifted a hand and said\par
authoritatively:\par
\par
"Stop!"\par
\par
And as she had arrested the rush of her words, he had continued, in a\par
grave, dry voice, quite devoid of unction or sympathy, cautiously\par
lowered and yet wonderfully distinct:\par
\par
"You say that you wish to 'confide something' to me 'under the seal of\par
Confession,' and you are not a Catholic!"\par
\par
"No, I am not!  I suppose I would be called--a sort of Christian,\par
though."  She said it haltingly.  "Does my not being a Catholic prevent\par
you listening to anything I ... want to say?"\par
\par
The dry voice came back:\par
\par
"I do not refuse to hear what you have to say.  But Confession,\par
Absolution, and Penance are Catholic Sacraments. I cannot extend the\par
benefits of the Church to one who stands without her pale."\par
\par
"I'm sorry! ... I suppose, I really haven't got the right to ask advice\par
from you, or to expect you to keep anything--secret?"\par
\par
There was a little old man's cough.  The dry voice followed:\par
\par
"I did not say that.  As a priest, I am bound to give good counsel to\par
those who ask it.  And I promise you, also as a priest, to respect your\par
confidence....  Now if you desire to go on--for I have several penitents\par
waiting--I will ask you to do so.  Be clear and truthful and brief.\par
Mention no person by name.  Let there be no exaggeration. Now begin!\par
..."\par
\par
"It's like this..."  And she had blurted out the ugly, sordid story,\par
that in the plain, unvarnished narration grew uglier and more sordid\par
still.\par
\par
He had listened without the movement of an eyebrow or the twitch of a\par
muscle.  At certain points where she had deviated from the sheer fact by\par
a mere hairsbreadth the dry little cough had interjected: "Think again!"\par
When she touched upon the circumstances that had resulted in "another\par
man's" offer of marriage:\par
\par
"You have accepted this other?" he had asked, and followed her\par
affirmative by saying, quietly, just as he had told her she was not a\par
Catholic: "You have not told him of--what has taken place.  Is he an\par
honourable, upright man?"\par
\par
"Very!"\par
\par
"H'mm!" said the dry cough.  "What is his religion?"\par
\par
"He is a Catholic."\par
\par
"H'mm! ... A devout Catholic?"\par
\par
"He seems--awfully keen on his Church!"\par
\par
A silence had followed, during which the beating of Patrine's heart and\par
the singing of the blood in her ears had seemed to fill the clean little\par
wooden place.  Then:\par
\par
"Do you intend to tell this keen Catholic," asked the merciless voice,\par
"that you do not come to him--pure?"\par
\par
"No! ... At least..."  The heave of her bosom against the little shelf\par
before the lattice made the dry wood quiver and creak.  A deep sigh\par
broke from her.  The priest's voice continued:\par
\par
"You have made it quite clear why you have applied to me.  To be\par
encouraged not to tell!  But even for your own sake I advise you to make\par
confession.  Do you expect God's blessing upon a marriage that is--upon\par
your side--a fraud?"\par
\par
"Men aren't angels!" Patrine burst out rebelliously. "How do I know that\par
he--Yes, I do know!"\par
\par
His face had risen up before her, and his voice was in her ears saying\par
with that note of gladness in it: "I come to you clean!" and shame and\par
compunction choked her, as she added:\par
\par
"He's straighter than I should have believed it possible for any man to\par
be."\par
\par
"H'mm!"  The dry hacking old man's cough came again.  He sniffed twice,\par
sharply.  Now he was speaking again.\par
\par
"You have not known many--or any Catholic men before this one.  Your\par
doubt as to the existence of masculine purity proves with what type of\par
persons you have hitherto mixed.  For your own sake you will be wise to\par
tell the truth to this gentleman.  If you loved him you would tell him\par
for his.  Now you must leave.  I have given you too much time as it is.\par
Repeat after me as I dictate."  He clasped the withered hands and began\par
briskly: "_Oh, my God----_"\par
\par
After a brief ineffectual hesitation, Patrine echoed him. He went on\par
trailing after him a voice that stumbled and dragged:\par
\par
"_Oh, my God!  I am very sorry that I have offended Thee by the sin of\par
fornication, and have yielded up my body to uncleanness, instead of\par
keeping myself pure as Thou commandest. I beseech Thee for the love of\par
Thy Son my Saviour Jesus Christ to bestow upon me the grace of a genuine\par
sorrow for my sin; and while I implore that Thou wouldst mercifully\par
spare me the ruin and disgrace I have merited by my own act, I\par
faithfully promise Thee to profit by the bitter lesson I have learned.\par
But if I find myself as the natural consequence of my wickedness----_"\par
\par
"_--of my wickedness----_"\par
\par
The dragging echo failed.  A mist came before her eyes.\par
\par
"Go on," said the stern voice from the other side of the grating.  It\par
went on dictating:\par
\par
"_But if I find myself as the natural consequence of my sinfulness about\par
to be the mother of a child, I vow not to be guilty of any violence to\par
the innocent.  But to bear my bitter punishment meekly, as coming from\par
Thy Hand.  Amen._"\par
\par
She said the words.  He blessed her with some such words as these:\par
\par
"Now may God bless and forgive you, and bring your soul from darkness\par
into His Light.  Leave me now.  Please shut the door."\par
\par
She heard the dry little hacking cough again as she closed it after her.\par
But she did not go away thinking him harsh and merciless.  She had seen\par
great shining tears dropping, dropping upon those withered hands.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LII*\par
\par
                                *KHAKI*\par
\par
\par
Remember how upon the great grey canvas of London, broadly splashed in\par
with khaki, from the becoming dead-leaf of the Regular troops to the\par
deadly ginger of the newly mobilised Reserve or the hideous\par
mustard-yellow of the latest recruit to the newest Territorial\par
unit--Recruiting posters of every shape, size, and method of appeal to\par
patriotism, suddenly flared out, ranging from the immemorial\par
red-and-blue printing on white to the huge pictorial hoarding-plaster in\par
monochrome.  Dash in as values the glow of re-awakened patriotism, the\par
resounding silences in which Royal Messages to British Citizens and\par
lieges were delivered by grave officials in scarlet gowns and curly\par
white wigs, and the singing of the National Anthem by huge crowds\par
gathered in front of Buckingham Palace, to cheer, over and over again\par
the King, the Queen, and the Heir to the British Throne.\par
\par
Recall how keenly-curious Britons densely thronged the neighbourhood of\par
the Houses of Parliament, eager to ascertain the British attitude\par
towards France and other Continental Powers; while immense aggregations\par
of people blocked the entrance to Downing Street, surging outside the\par
wrought-iron screens protecting Ministerial windows; congesting\par
Whitehall until omnibuses proceeded at a snail's pace.\par
\par
Revive the strange newness of things, the snap and tingle of seeing not\par
only Royal Palaces and Government Offices, but vital places such as\par
Arsenals, Docks, Railway, and Electric Power stations, Powder-magazines\par
and Munition Stores closely guarded by men in tea-leaf or ginger-brown.\par
Sickly the hot flush of things so new with the pale dread of ruin, the\par
ugly rumours of Invasion.  Shadow in broad and black, a panic on the\par
Stock Exchange, the dizzying fall of prices on Continental Bourses, the\par
record slump on Wall Street, the frenzied stampede of the run upon the\par
Banks, the Proclamation from the steps of the Royal Exchange of the\par
strange thing called by nearly everybody--anything but a Moratorium; as,\par
for example, a Monatorial, a Monoroarium or Honorarium, and so on.\par
\par
Who could ever forget the excitement attendant on the sailing of famous\par
passenger and cargo-liners with quick-firers and Maxims nosing through\par
steel shields abaft the lower bridge?  How the Red Cross notified its\par
surgeons, nurses, and ambulance-helpers to hold themselves ready for\par
business, and a neat khaki rig-out that had puzzled us in several\par
unfamiliar details, turned out to be the Service uniform of the Royal\par
Flying Corps.\par
\par
German and Austro-Hungarian Reservists of all classes, summoned home by\par
the strident bellow of Fatherland, surged round their respective\par
Consulates.  Prince Cheraowski, Representative of Germany, having had\par
his passports handed him, shrugged the shrug of a disgruntled man,\par
lighted a cigarette, and took a farewell constitutional through St.\par
James's Park.  And, on the Declaration of War with Austria-Hungary a few\par
days later, Count Lensdorff received his walking-ticket, and gracefully\par
vanished from the scene.  And from the hall-doors of one Embassy in\par
Carlton House Terrace and another in Belgrave Square, British workmen,\par
cheerfully whistling, unscrewed the massive brazen plates.  Crowds\par
watched the operation in phlegmatic silence; the single individual who\par
loosed a "boo" being promptly bonneted by a disapproving majority, and\par
moved on by the police, while the windows of the British Embassy at\par
Berlin were being shattered by brickbats, as were those of divers\par
British consulates and Legations throughout the Fatherland.  On the mud,\par
stones, and verbal filth lavished on their inmates, of the Yahoo-like\par
usage undergone by Englishmen and Englishwomen, we may not dwell, but I\par
do not think we are likely to forget.\par
\par
Recall again, how vast public spaces carefully kept and tended by\par
Committees and boards and Councils, became, as at the stroke of a wand,\par
huge training camps of young, keen, healthy if pale-cheeked Britons in\par
ill-fitting gingerbread or mustard-coloured clothes.  How groups of\par
unoccupied London houses, or large vacant stores, or the head-centres of\par
the Y.M.C.A. in various districts, would suddenly overflow with bronzed\par
and sturdy warriors of the Regular Forces, and as suddenly empty again.\par
The platforms of railway termini, closely guarded and barred from the\par
public, would be dotted with neat stacks of Lee Enfield rifles, while\par
regularly-breathing sleepers in khaki pillowed on their packs, shielded\par
by the peaks of their tilted caps from the blue-white electric glare, or\par
the yellow dazzle of the morning sun.  A whistle--a snort and clank of\par
two big locomotives--and the platforms under the reverberating glass\par
roofs would be empty again, under the dusty yellow sunshine, or the\par
blue-white electric glare.\par
\par
Remember all this to the daily accompaniment of those huge shrieking\par
headlines, the trotting of innumerable iron-shod hoofs, the ceaseless\par
rolling of iron-shod wheels, the clatter and vibration of huge\par
motor-lorries, vans, and waggons commandeered for the use of the\par
Auxiliary Transport (brilliantly painted in thousands of instances, and\par
proclaiming in foot-long capitals the virtues of Crump's Curative\par
Saline, or Bango's Extract of Beef), mingled with the steady tramp of\par
marching men, all through the days and nights.  By night you lay and\par
listened to these sounds, mingled with the bleating of flocks of sheep,\par
and the bellowing of herds of cattle, until the hoofs and wheels and\par
marching boots mingled into the roar of one great ink-black, awful\par
River, whose ice-cold woe-waters--sprung from some mysterious\par
source--swept through our villages and towns and cities, carrying with\par
them millions of lives, brute and human, towards the blood-red dawn of\par
Death.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LIII*\par
\par
                      *FRANKY GOES TO THE FRONT!*\par
\par
\par
With the First Infantry Brigade of the First British Expeditionary Force\par
went the First Battalion of the Bearskins Plain.\par
\par
Exchanging with Ackroyd, "too sick a man for fighting" (who parted with\par
several superfluous inches of appendix and convalesced in time to go out\par
with the Second Battalion and meet a glorious end at Ypres), Franky was\par
swallowed up in the vortex of Aldershot.  000, Cadogan Place saw him but\par
once more before the roaring flood whirled him away, like a slim brown\par
autumn leaf, to the Unknown.\par
\par
His gift to Margot on the night of their parting was a silver elephant\par
of truculent aspect, having ruby eyes and mother-o'-pearl tusks and a\par
howdah on its back, accommodating a "Gladsome Days" pull-off kalendar.\par
\par
"You're such nuts on mascots and gadgets, best childie, I thought I'd\par
get you this beggar for a keepsake.  Saw it in a shop in Bond Street.\par
It goes like so!"--Franky demonstrated by sticking a penknife-blade\par
under the liberal whack of leaves that had become obsolete since the\par
First of January.  "Rather a neat notion.  Something appropriate for\par
every day o' the week," he continued, indicating a rhymed distich\par
appearing beneath the current date.  This, the first of many utterances\par
on the part of the Silver Elephant, ranging from the idiotically\par
inappropriate to the appositely malign, ran as follows:\par
\par
    "_Be very kind to Pussy-cat_\par
      _And handle her with care:_\par
    _You would not pull her by the tail_\par
      _If her claws grew out of there!_"\par
\par
\par
"Well, if that's the best this beast can do--" began Margot, sternly\par
surveying the proboscidean.  Then she softened, meeting Franky's\par
disappointed eyes, and said it was a lovely present and she would always\par
keep it on the table by her bedside.  She and Franky were almost lovers\par
again for the brief time that yet remained to them.  She even endured\par
without open resentment his continual references to the child.\par
\par
"Take care of you both for my sake, won't you, Kittums? Of course, long\par
before Christmas I hope to be back with you! But"--he tenderly crushed\par
the little figure to him as he sat on the bedside holding it\par
embraced--"but if by any old chance I get sent in--remember what kind of\par
man I'd like my boy to be.  Sanguine, ain't I?--on the point of his\par
being a boy--putting a pink geranium in the front window before the\par
house is built, but still----"\par
\par
He laughed awkwardly, and brushed off a shining drop of moisture that\par
splashed on the slender brown leather strap that marks the officer's\par
caste.  A third star showed on his khaki sleeve, but he had made no\par
reference to it, and Kittums omitted to ask what it meant.  He kissed\par
her gravely on the eyes and lips and forehead, unwound the slender arms\par
that clasped his neck, and gently laid her back upon the pillows.  Then\par
with: "Good-night and God bless you!" he went quietly out of the room.\par
The hall-door shut and a servant put the chain up, and the waiting car\par
slid away to the Tower.  For "I'm to kip down at the old shop for\par
to-night," Franky had explained, "and shepherd five hundred strengthy\par
foot-sloggers--fat as prize bullocks every one of 'em!--to Nowhere in\par
Particular in the morning."\par
\par
Margot cried a little when the hall-door shut, and then fell soundly\par
asleep among her big pillows.  Waking as a ray of five o'clock sunshine\par
penetrated between the blue-green silk blinds and the lacy curtains, to\par
realise that Something had gone out of her life.\par
\par
Something wilful, petulant Kittums had not valued until the hall-door\par
had shut behind it.  Something that--crawling, shuddering\par
thought!--might never return.  She sat up in bed, hugging her knees and\par
staring into a Future without any Franky in it, a tragic little picture\par
against the background of the big frilled pillows, her great dark eyes\par
wide and wild under her tumbled gold brown hair-waves, her paleness\par
enhanced by the rose-silk night-sheath, a maelstrom of thought,\par
emotions, apprehensions, terrors, whirling in the humming-bird brain.\par
\par
The ray of sunshine presently touched the face of the electric clock and\par
elicited a malicious twinkle from the ruby eyes of the Silver Elephant.\par
Remembering her promise, Kittums put out a hand, pulled off the\par
paper-slip bearing the date of the previous day and read:\par
\par
                         "_May All Your Hours_\par
                         _Be Bright As This!_"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LIV*\par
\par
                          *OFFICIAL RETICENCE*\par
\par
\par
The First British Expeditionary Force was in France. Thus much after\par
considerable delay was vouchsafed us. Some studiously unenlightening\par
Field post-cards, some industriously Censored private letters, some\par
Press narratives and photographs were permitted us, of Highlanders,\par
Guards, Scots Greys, Middlesex, Worcestershires, Gordons, and others,\par
brought in upon the midnight tide and debarking from huge transports at\par
Boulogne and Havre and Rouen, under burning blue skies and a sizzling\par
sun.  The illustrated weeklies and the cinematograph showed them, with\par
battery after battery of R.F.A. and R.H.A. and R.G.A., Ammunition parks\par
and columns, and Engineers with pontoons on motor-waggons, and Field\par
Ambulance units, endlessly streaming into or out of the canvas cities\par
erected on the sites of the old Napoleonic camps.  Showed also Comic\par
Relief, in the familiar form of British Tommy, grinningly appreciative\par
of the welcome accorded him by command of the French Republic; meekly\par
submitting to be plucked bare of buttons and badges, by sirens who\par
sought these with offerings of chocolate, wine, and fruit.  This meagre\par
pabulum we champed, possessing our souls perforce, in patience; sitting\par
before the great iron curtain of official reticence that had glided down\par
into its grooves as though it never meant to go up again.\par
\par
Then, with the whiffling swoop of the Jabberwock--the Food Scare was\par
upon us.  Letters showered from venerable maiden aunts in remote country\par
districts, describing economies practised by our great-grandmothers in\par
1801 and 1814.  Hot-eyed friends buttonholed one and whispered of Famine\par
that was coming, and pressed crumpled pamphlets, dealing with Food\par
Values, into one's unwilling hand.  The Specie Scare came next, rousing\par
the most phlegmatic to frenzied indignation.  What!  In lieu of the\par
smooth plump British sovereign and half-sovereign welcomed in every\par
corner of the civilised world, must we perforce accept the "magpie," or\par
One Pound note, and the "pinky" or ten-shilling bill!\par
\par
People frothed and vituperated.  We were all frothing, what time the\par
stocky Kalmuck-faced von Kluck with 130,000 Germans of the Kaiser's\par
First Army came rolling down in overwhelming force upon the First and\par
Second British Army Corps.  Eighty thousand men of our blood holding the\par
line of the canal from Conde to "a place called Mons" with, as the\par
flanking angle, another place called Binche.\par
\par
The 5th French Army was in full retreat from Namur and Charleroi; borne\par
back by the resistless pressure of von Buelow, Chief of the Second Army\par
of Attila, 250,000 strong. The 4th French Army was retiring before von\par
Hahsen and a third tidal wave of armed Germanity--humping its huge snaky\par
columns after the fashion of the looper caterpillar--along the menaced\par
line of the Meuse.\par
\par
The Krupp and Skoda motor-howitzers that had crushed Belgian fortresses\par
like eggshells were coming into position; the circling enemy aeroplanes\par
were directing with smoke-rockets the uncannily excellent shooting of\par
the German Artillery.  We who thought we had no more than a couple of\par
Army Corps in front of us, and possibly a Division of Cavalry, were\par
beginning to realise the ugly truth.  As the frightful blizzard of iron\par
and flame broke upon the British batteries, and the shallow trenches\par
made in desperate haste and crowded with the flower of the British Army,\par
began to lose the shape of trenches, to melt--to become mere scratches\par
in the earth, littered with human scrap....\par
\par
We did not suspect, we never dreamed of grave disaster to our Forces,\par
though some of us were strangely haunted by well-loved looks and dear\par
familiar touches before the Iron Curtain of official silence lifted that\par
quarter-inch and the thick red stuff oozed slowly underneath.\par
\par
\par
An hour or two before the Great Awakening, Margot had 'phoned asking\par
Patrine to come round.  Arriving, her friend found Kittums sorely\par
exercised in spirit.  The housekeeper, in tears, had sought an interview\par
on the Food Question and entreated her lady to lose no time in\par
provisioning the domestic citadel with Flour, Sugar, Bacon, Tea, Coffee,\par
Potatoes, Cereals, and tinned meats against the approaching days of\par
famine.  She begged to submit a List. It would be well to lose no time\par
for all the Banks were breaking.  She felt it her duty to mention the\par
fact.\par
\par
"And so I told Wallop to dry her poor old eyes," explained Kittums, "and\par
I'd go and buy up the Army and Navy Stores as soon as I'd had a look in\par
at what Franky calls the Dross House, just to ask the Manager, as man to\par
man, if there's any chance of the Bank going biff?  Your adorable\par
Lynette and your Uncle Owen may say that hoarding things to eat isn't\par
playing the game and all that.  Well! When you're too sharp-set to think\par
Imperially, come round here and I'll grub the lot.  How is your Flying\par
Man?"\par
\par
"Doing some Army Coaching.  Out Farnborough way," said Patrine.  "I've\par
not set eyes on him twice since that Club lunch."\par
\par
"When Franky cottoned to him so," said Margot. "You've not had a\par
scrimmage?"\par
\par
"God forbid!"\par
\par
"Engaged people always squabble."\par
\par
"Alan and I don't," asserted Patrine.\par
\par
The car came round and they drove to the Bank.  Most Banks had enjoyed a\par
Run and a few had experienced the combination of a Run with a Panic.\par
There had been a severe Run on Margot's bank.  Now it was over and a\par
huge majority among the people who formed queues at the doors and\par
crowded the counters were paying in the deposits they had nervously\par
withdrawn.  Relieved in mind, Kittums cashed a cheque of magnitude, and\par
the respectable Williams turned the car in the direction of the Stores.\par
\par
On this Day of the Great Awakening, Woman stormed the departments.\par
Kittums and Patrine plunged into the scrum, to emerge after having\par
achieved a modified success. Lady Norwater's explanation, that she\par
required provisions in wholesale bulk because of a yachting-trip she\par
meditated, had been hit upon by several thousands of other\par
terminological inexactitudinarians.  The mounds of bacon, the castled\par
tins of tea and coffee, the sacks of sugar, rice, and cereals, the\par
raisins, currants, and tinned comestibles--had been nearly all picked up\par
by these knowing early risers.  Still enough had been secured to relieve\par
the mind of Mrs. Wallop, and scare the wolf from the threshold of 00,\par
Cadogan Place.\par
\par
"Beg pardon, m' lady."  The sedate face of the respectable Williams\par
looked over the last Brobdingnagian parcel transferred to his embrace.\par
"I think if your ladyship 'as no objection it would be better to close\par
the car."\par
\par
"If it will close," began Margot, looking with interested speculation at\par
the mountainous accumulation of bulky, whitey-brown string-tied bags and\par
packages upon the front seat.\par
\par
"FOOD 'OGS!" bellowed a man in a rusty bowler hat and soiled shirt\par
sleeves, so suddenly and powerfully that Kittums jumped.\par
\par
"Garn 'ome!" vindictively shrieked a fiery-faced female. "Greedy-guts!\par
Yah!  Git along 'ome!"\par
\par
"FOOD 'OGS!" reiterated the Stentor in shirt sleeves, backed by an\par
approving murmur from a crowd of dingily-clad men and women gathered\par
upon the pavement right and left of the imposing entrance to the Stores.\par
\par
"Now then, move on 'ere!" came from a policeman, and the crowd began to\par
dissolve, with lowering glances. Motorcars were moving away, carrying\par
their owners embedded in groceries.  Others were driving up to the door.\par
\par
"Move on, please!" repeated the Man in Blue.\par
\par
"Not till I've got rid of these things.  Call the Commissionaire.  Tell\par
him my name and number!--say the orders were given by mistake! ..."\par
Margot went on, when the Alpine range of parcels had melted away under\par
the combined efforts of chauffeur and Commissionaire: "Poor old Wallop\par
will wail, but I've purged myself of the contempt of being a Food Hog.\par
Great Snipe! to think of deserving to be called such an awful name.  It\par
made me feel all of seventeen stone, with a row of chins like\par
saddle-bags!"  She pinched her own dainty chin between a tiny finger and\par
thumb. "Still, I've enjoyed the scrum," she went on, as the car slid\par
towards Piccadilly.  "It's bucked me splendidly!  I shall know what to\par
do now, when I want to lay my ghosts. You know one of them"--the little\par
fingers twitched in Patrine's--"what's coming in November.  The other\par
started haunting me only a few days back."  All the new-won colour had\par
died out of the small oval face and the great dark eyes were tragic in\par
their terror.  "You're too good a pal to laugh.  Well, then--I'll own\par
up.  Franky's my latest ghost of all!"\par
\par
"But you have heard?  You have had letters?"\par
\par
The answer was strangled between a laugh and a sob.\par
\par
"Letters.  Three post-cards from Somewhere in France and a queer epistle\par
all squares of blacking.  Not much between--except that he is tophole\par
and coming Home at Christmas and sends love to us both!  That's Franky's\par
way.  He always talks as--"  A shudder went through the little figure,\par
and shadows were about the great wild eyes, and the pale lips quivered:\par
\par
"Poor little Kittums!" said Patrine's big warm baritone. She slipped an\par
arm tenderly about the little thing.  Who could have dreamed that\par
Kittums could care so about Franky--or any other man.  "Are you worrying\par
so badly, my dinkie?" she went on, soothingly: "Try not.  It isn't\par
wise!"\par
\par
"I'm not worrying," came the weary answer.  "I'm being haunted--that's\par
all.  Day and night since it started, his hands are on me and his eyes\par
are looking at me.  When I sleep, I'm wandering through desolate places\par
looking, always looking for him!  And thousands of other selfish, silly\par
women are being haunted in the same way.  Oh, Pat, be always kind when\par
you're married to your Flying Man!"\par
\par
"_When!_"--Patrine echoed.  But what of sorrow or doubt her tone\par
conveyed was lost upon Margot.  She had told her own grief, and the\par
telling had relieved her.  Like the child with the kissed bruise, she\par
could prattle of other things.  She was twittering and chirping in the\par
gay little voice Franky knew so well, as Williams, the respectable,\par
turned smoothly into Short Street.  There was a dense block at the\par
corner by the Aldebaran Hotel, and amidst the swishing of the\par
motor-engines and the fidgeting of plump carriage-horses, loathful of\par
the sudden release of the pungent exhaust from escape-valves under their\par
noses--a little piece of dialogue between two Cyprians on the near\par
sidewalk drove home to both the occupants of the car.\par
\par
One Cyprian was well-to-do, past thirty-five and expensively caparisoned\par
for conquest, from the tall feather topping her stove-pipe hat and her\par
burnished wig of Angora goat-hair, to her silk stockings of\par
liberally-open pattern and the tips of her high-heeled, buckled shoes.\par
Her hard eyes under their painted brows took critical stock of the\par
other, younger woman, whose make-up could not hide ill-health, and whose\par
flaunting fineries were the worse for wear.\par
\par
Said Hard Eyes, indicating with a jerk of her powdered double chin, a\par
procession moving down Piccadilly Circus-wards--a publisher's catchpenny\par
advertisement of "WEEP NO MORE, MOTHERS!" ingenious in its employment of\par
robust-looking matrons as bearers of the sandwich-boards plastered with\par
posters of rose-colour and gold:\par
\par
"You could give some of the swell West End ladies a tip or two, I\par
reckon, Lallie, about that Purple Dreams dope?"\par
\par
"Honest to God, I could!  But I wouldn't!"  The haggard eyes leapt\par
viciously out of their languor.  "Let 'em run up against it--same as me!\par
Me that went all the way to Brussels to get the new treatment.  Great\par
Scott! When I came to I was black and blue and green all over. And my\par
face!  It was a fair scream!"  She threw an appraising side-glance in a\par
shop window.  "No!  My skin'll never be what it used, I reckon."\par
\par
"But the"--the hard eyes between the elder woman's blued lids were\par
hideously significant--"the Trouble, eh?"\par
\par
"The Trouble"--Lallie's still girlish shoulders shrugged.--"Oh, that's\par
all right!  I heard no more of it!  There's the one comfort.  Good-bye,\par
ducky.  I got to meet somebody at the Cri."\par
\par
"Well, better luck!"  And as the block broke and the car moved on, the\par
women nodded and parted.  Margot and her friend Patrine did not look at\par
each other as the car stopped before the Club.\par
\par
A glance showed the vestibule crowded, the second pair of swing-doors\par
thudded momentarily as members and their guests passed on into the Club\par
rooms, without relieving the congestion that fresh arrivals renewed.\par
Some doors above, a piano-organ in charge of two men was jolting out the\par
last bars of the Russian National Anthem.  One of the men,\par
olive-skinned, grey-haired, and dressed in threadbare black, sang the\par
words with perfunctory fervour in a cracked tenor voice.  As the last\par
chord banged out and the organist jerked the changing-lever over, and\par
the Marseillaise summoned jangling echoes of its lyrical frenzy from the\par
pavement and the surrounding walls, Patrine, meeting Sherbrand's eyes\par
over the crowded heads of people, knew a sudden shock of apprehension in\par
the strangeness of their regard.\par
\par
For day and night since that strange, impulsive visit she had made to\par
the Confessional--"You must tell him.  It is your duty to tell him!" had\par
sounded in her ears.  She set her teeth and determined that she would\par
never tell him, none the less knowing that the revelation would be made.\par
A Power infinitely stronger than her woman's will was bearing upon it.\par
Her treasure was in peril, her fairy-gold at any moment might turn to\par
withered leaves at a breath from her own mouth.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER LV*\par
\par
                            *NEWS OF BAWNE*\par
\par
\par
"Pat!--what luck!"\par
\par
Sherbrand was standing before her, tall and lean and masterful, saluting\par
her with the touch of three fingers to a soldierly forage-cap with three\par
buttons, set jauntily atilt on the broad tanned brow.\par
\par
Ah! the delight of seeing the cold grey glance warm into sea-blue, the\par
lean, eagle-face flash into smiles.  For a little while yet he was hers,\par
she told herself, as the hard hand gripped on hers that answered the\par
swift fierce pressure, and her blood that the sickly chill of fear had\par
stagnated, whirled on its crimson circle singing for joy.  And then--a\par
second glance, sweeping from the top to the toes of the tall manly\par
figure, stopped the song.\par
\par
"Alan!  You--in khaki!"\par
\par
"I suppose so," he said a little clumsily, echoing thousands of other\par
men.  "It's the universal wear just now, isn't it?  We fellows must make\par
good while we can--and we're all of us joining.  Even Macrombie--you\par
can't have forgotten Macrombie--has got his rating, and is acting a\par
P.O.T. on a Destroyer in the North Sea."\par
\par
\par
Do you see the dour drunkard standing up, under the eye of the smart\par
young inspecting Fleet Surgeon, naked save for the leather bootlace that\par
held a battered silver locket round his harsh and swarthy scrag.\par
\par
"Your age? ..."\par
\par
"Ye micht ca' me forty," said the subject, with caution.\par
\par
"I might, but I'd be a liar!" said the Fleet Surgeon, "so try again, my\par
man!"\par
\par
"Ye micht pit twa to the forr-ty," came rumbling from the hairy chest.\par
\par
"And tack eight on to that," thus the Fleet Surgeon, tucking the hooked\par
ends of the stethoscope into his ears, and deftly applying the\par
microphone.  "And then I'd be wide of the actual!  Breathe deeply, will\par
you!"  The effort provoked a volley of coughs sounding like half-bricks\par
pitched against the sides of an empty cistern and the Fleet Surgeon\par
shook his head.\par
\par
"_Hough--hough--hough!_--why didna' ye--hough! lat weel alane?" gasped\par
Macrombie, with eyes blazing hell-fire through the moisture engendered\par
by the cough.  "Dinna ye ken I'll never no' be wanting to breathe deeply\par
whaur ye're needing to send me?  There is nae room whatever for\par
lung-play oot o' the ordinar'," he added scornfully, "aboard ane o' thae\par
kittle, cranky, tinpot Destroyers!"\par
\par
"Hold out your hand!" commanded the arbiter of Destinies. He\par
contemplated the extended member, wavering and fluttering like the\par
indicator-needle on the dial of an atmospheric pressure-gauge.  "Pretty\par
wobbly, what?" he commented to the owner with the sarcastic inflection\par
that advertised a keen advocate of Temperance.\par
\par
"Man, O! man!" broke from Macrombie in a harsh rattling whisper,\par
desperate appeal flashing in his burnt-out eyes, "you that are young\par
enough to be my son, tak' me or leave me, ane or the tither--but shame\par
me nae mair!"\par
\par
Telegraphists were sorely needed, so Macrombie of the racking hoast and\par
the shaky hand was passed as fit for Service, and duty rated as Petty\par
Officer Telegraphist aboard one of the contemned tin-pots.\par
\par
The Crown and winged double-thunderbolt must have nerved the arm they\par
came back to.  For, on the day of the Battle of Jutland, when a\par
point-blank salvo from an enemy cruiser wrecked the bridge and\par
searchlight platform, carrying away the forward mast and funnel of\par
Macrombie's particular tin-pot, and men in respirators were fighting the\par
smothering fumes of the fire caused by German shells of the incendiary\par
description, a dour, stark man whose clothes were alight and burning on\par
him, stuck grimly to his post among the wreckage of the shattered\par
Wireless room, sending out the message last dictated by the officer who\par
lay dead across the blistering steel plating--for the short circuit set\par
up by the smashed searchlight had created its own separate\par
conflagration, and the electricity was "running out of everything like\par
oil."\par
\par
When the tin-pot heeled over, and, having duly buried her steel chest\par
and secret documents, went down with colours flying in a smother of oily\par
steam, men who were saved on the rafts told this tale of Macrombie, who\par
sleeps well, after Life's thirsty fever, at his post in the Destroyer's\par
battered Wireless cabin, on the deep-ridged, sandy bottom of the wild,\par
shallow North Sea.\par
\par
\par
Patrine felt her heart crushed as in the grip of a cold steel gauntlet.\par
Her apprehensions had not been unfounded. She and Alan were to be\par
parted, if not as she had feared.\par
\par
"I--suppose I ought to congratulate you--"  Her unwilling eyes admired\par
the tall manly figure in the plain workmanlike uniform.  The buttonless\par
tunic with its Lancer plastron, the riding-breeches of ampler cut than\par
the cavalryman's, the high spurless boots of supple brown leather, and\par
the belt that carried a revolver and no sword. "What--what are you in?"\par
she asked draggingly, and he answered with a smile and a flash of his\par
grey eyes:\par
\par
"I hope I'm in for some of what's going on!"\par
\par
"How glad you are!"\par
\par
"Rather.  I should think so!  Now that they've let me into the Royal\par
Flying Corps as a T.S.L.  Look at my wings!"  He touched the white\par
outspread pinions on the tunic-breast with a reverent finger-tip and\par
went on pouring out his story without a break.  "It's cost me some\par
badgering of High Officials of Military Aeronautics at Whitehall, and a\par
lot of time wasted in baby tests.  Squad drill, Harris tube,\par
bomb-dropping, air-signalling, Webley and Scott practice, and so on.\par
Now I'm teaching trick-flying to Army aviators from 4.30 A.M. till 11\par
P.M.  The Powers that Be have taken over the Flying Schools--Durrant's\par
Cafe is our Officer's Mess now.  You should see old Durrant in his glory\par
as Head Waiter.  And Mrs. D--"  His white teeth flashed as he laughed.\par
\par
"And they have known of this"--she nodded at the eagle-wings--"while I\par
have been kept in ignorance!  How long?"\par
\par
"Not quite a fortnight.  Don't be unreasonable, dear!"\par
\par
The new tone stung.  Did a yellow star upon the cuffs and\par
shoulder-straps and a pair of white wings on the left breast mean so\par
much to him that her just claims upon his confidence seemed wanting in\par
reason now?  Anger and resentment choked her as he added:\par
\par
"I am here now, as it happens, because I'm crossing the Channel\par
to-morrow at peep o' day."  Something in her pale face made him add:\par
"Don't worry!--I'm likely to be back again by nightfall.  That's what\par
I've rushed in here to tell you, though I've a man in tow, a Wing\par
Commander of the French S. Ae.  Hot from the Front and just landed at\par
Hendon.  I had to take him in my car to his Embassy, and now I've got to\par
find him a room at an hotel.  When I've done it I'm coming back here to\par
talk to you.  Where on earth has my man got to?  Why, there he is,\par
talking to Lady Norwater.  The little chap with the grey moustache and\par
the gold-banded _kepi_."\par
\par
"I am honoured by Madame's gracious remembrance," the person indicated\par
could be heard protesting, during an instant's lull in the Babel of\par
voices round.  "But my own--a thousand pardons! is less accurate."\par
\par
"Oh!"  Margot expostulated, "but you can't have forgotten.  That Sunday\par
of the Grande Semaine--when you were in the Bois, timing a Flying\par
Officer who was testing an English invention--a sort of a----"\par
\par
"But assuredly, Madame!"  His quick nod and the gesture of his gloved\par
hand summoned up the scene vividly. "I remember, but perfectly, though\par
much water has rolled under the bridges since that day.  And\par
Milord--Madame's husband?"\par
\par
"He's at the Front," Margot explained, "wherever the Front is!"\par
\par
"Unfortunately at the moment," returned the suave voice, "the Front is\par
everywhere.  It is easy to find without binoculars.  _Adieu, Madame_.\par
_Merci bien de la souvenir si gracieuse, dites mes amities a Monsieur._"\par
And in another moment he arrived beside Sherbrand, exclaiming with his\par
vivacious shrug and gesture: "My faith, my friend, your London _Cercle\par
des Dames_ is a veritable Paradise of Mahommed.  Now in Paris, at least\par
before the War--instead of ten thousand houris to every true Believer,\par
one counted at least three Adams to every Eve.  But I observe your\par
search has been successful.  Will you not present me to Mademoiselle\par
your _fiancee_?"\par
\par
And the dapper middle-aged Wing Commander in the gold-banded _kepi_,\par
whose dark plain uniform displayed the gold badge of the Service\par
Aeronautique under the Cross of the Legion of Honour, was introduced as\par
Captain Raymond by an off-hand young Briton who comprehended not in the\par
least the immense condescension that had prompted the request.\par
\par
"_Sapristi!_" thought Raymond, as Patrine gave him her large hand and\par
assured him in her big warm voice that she was frightfully pleased to\par
meet a friend of Alan's.--"A magnificent type of the human female animal\par
to have paired with this bluff, simple English boy.  Part _femme du\par
monde_, part romping hoyden, part _cabotine_, she should have been a\par
Duchesse of the old Napoleonic regime, or at least the effect that lies\par
behind a _cause celebre_ of the Paris Law Courts of modern days.  And\par
she will be expected by this honest fellow to live in a stucco villa at\par
Kensington or the Crystal Palace, and bear and rear his children, and\par
live and die in all the deadly respectability of the British\par
middle-class _milieu_!"\par
\par
But he made his beautiful bow and murmured some civil phrases.  In the\par
spring, at the Hendon Flying Grounds of M. Fanshaw, he, Raymond, had\par
been interested to meet the friend of Mademoiselle.  Had been profoundly\par
impressed by the displayed inventions of a young man so gifted as\par
aviator and engineer.  Had had the good fortune subsequently to obtain\par
the consent of his own Chiefs of the S. Ae. F. to a test of an\par
invention--the value of which had been hall-marked by the approbation of\par
Messieurs les Allemands.  True, M. Sherbrand had been the victim of\par
their unscrupulosity.  But Fortune, who knew? might be kinder in the\par
near future.  This War so grievous, so brutal, so deplorable, waged by\par
the Prussian against Civilisation and Progress, would open up not only\par
_le metier des armes_, but countless other avenues of prosperity to\par
thousands of ardent and gifted young men.  Like M. Sherbrand.  To whom\par
Raymond said with an authoritative glance of his blue eye: "My friend,\par
we keep your auto waiting at the door!"\par
\par
"Ah, but stay!" Patrine began, with a sense of hatred towards the\par
well-used little Ford runabout standing in much grander company by the\par
kerb outside the Club: "do stay and lunch and smoke and tell us things\par
about the War, won't you?"\par
\par
"A thousand thanks, but impossible, Mademoiselle!"\par
\par
Raymond shrugged, conscious that her look of disappointment was for\par
Sherbrand, and pleaded fatigue as an excuse.\par
\par
"For these are iron times, Mademoiselle," he went on in his smooth,\par
musical accents, "and we who live in them are unfortunately of flesh and\par
blood.  When the War is done perhaps there will again be social\par
pleasures like the lunch you were so kind as to offer me.  That I am\par
tempted to accept I will not conceal from you.  I have not eaten since I\par
flew from France at _la pointe du jour_--one of the smallest of the\par
little hours of this morning, and then I broke fast on two fingers of\par
little red wine, and a hunch of soldier's bread."\par
\par
"You mean to say you're fresh from flying the Channel?"\par
\par
"Crossing the Channel came near the end of my journey, Mademoiselle.  I\par
should have arrived earlier"--he shrugged indifferently--"had not some\par
German aviators caused delay."\par
\par
"Oh-h!"  Her vexation passed like a breath from a mirror.  Her long eyes\par
danced with delight under her hat-brim.  Her breath came quick, her red\par
lips curled, and a sweet faint pink showed under her creamy skin.\par
"You're a knight of the skies hot from a fray with two flying\par
dragons--and you were going without saying a word!  What do you think we\par
Englishwomen are made of?"\par
\par
"Very desirable flesh, some of you, at least, Mademoiselle," occurred to\par
Raymond, but he suppressed the equivoque and answered with professional\par
brevity:\par
\par
"Mademoiselle, I regret there is but little to tell you. The enemy\par
possesses an aerial organisation of great effectiveness which is being\par
chiefly employed in the killing of harmless civilians and the\par
destruction of unfortified towns.  But small success has hitherto\par
attended his efforts in the Channel.  Your British Expedition was\par
conveyed across the water without the loss of one _piou-piou_, or any\par
damage received by the explosion of a German bomb.  As for the German\par
aviators of whom I speak, their attitude towards myself and my pilot was\par
modest.  Flying their double-seated military Taubes, of which the wings\par
and tail resemble those of the dove after which they have been named,\par
they pursued our biplane half-way from Calais to Dover before deciding\par
to attack."\par
\par
"Then--"  She hesitated, softly clapping her palms together and dimpling\par
like a big child over the telling of a new fairy tale.\par
\par
"Then one climbed, possessing the advantage of a powerful engine, and\par
dropped a bomb from a height of some 600 _metres_ which exploded without\par
hitting us and went to the bottom of the sea.  While the second aviator,\par
who was armed with a repeating-carbine, wounded my pilot so severely\par
that it was only by a miracle of endurance he preserved consciousness\par
long enough to land without a crash. So I left him at Dover and--with a\par
pilot mechanic from the Air Station, completed my passage, descending at\par
Brooklands at twelve _demie_."\par
\par
"Was your pilot hurt very badly?  Will he be able to fly back to\par
France?"\par
\par
"Mademoiselle, being a pious Catholic, he has already flown to Heaven."\par
\par
"He is dead....  And you can joke!" Patrine reproached him.  His face\par
was very wrinkled as he smiled.\par
\par
"Mademoiselle, if a soldier could not jest at Death upon occasion, Life\par
for a soldier would be impossible!  Of verity, the loss of a good\par
pilot-_aviateur_ is not a thing to joke about, but fortunately I have\par
your friend to fill his place."\par
\par
"_Alan_!  You must not--I will never consent to it!"\par
\par
All taken aback, her colour banished, she fixed Sherbrand with blazing\par
imperative eyes.  He reddened to the hair and his mouth shut firmly.\par
For the first time there was a clash of wills between the pair.\par
\par
"Alan, why didn't you ask me?"\par
\par
He was redder than ever.\par
\par
"Because it wasn't for you to say.  It is an order from my Chiefs--don't\par
you understand?"\par
\par
She did not care that the French officer was smiling. She would have\par
liked to have struck him in his merrily-crinkled face.  Wretch! to have\par
blurted the truth at her that Alan had hidden.  What was he saying:\par
\par
"Permit, Mademoiselle, that I make my _adieux_.  I go to secure an\par
apartment where I may repose myself."  He looked at Sherbrand, saying in\par
his cool tone of authority: "The Aldebaran,--that is in the next street\par
and a good hotel, is it not so?  A little sleep will not come amiss\par
after a cutlet and a _demi-bouteille_.  And whilst I eat we will settle\par
our _affaires_.  Eh, mon lieutenant?"\par
\par
His gloved hand took Sherbrand neatly by the elbow. He was skilfully\par
steering him towards the doorway when Patrine, white and flaming, placed\par
herself in their path.\par
\par
"My affairs come first!" she was beginning.\par
\par
"_Shut up!_" came from Sherbrand, in an exasperated aside whisper.  "My\par
duty comes before you--or anything in the world.  It should come first\par
for you if you cared a damn for me!"\par
\par
No one but Raymond had overheard the curious, fierce colloquy.  She felt\par
literally scorched by the hot look of anger.  She knew an agony like the\par
tearing of the tissues of the flesh when Sherbrand passed her and went\par
out with that gloved hand of authority upon his arm.\par
\par
"Women are the devil!" he thought bitterly, as he opened the door of the\par
runabout Ford to admit the French Staff Officer.  "She'd had a shock in\par
being told the news so suddenly; but to ballyrag me--to make me look\par
such a thundering idiot before _him_!"\par
\par
He swung the crank with violence and wrenched angrily at the levers when\par
he took the driving-seat.  A gloved hand patted his arm, and Raymond's\par
voice said in his ear:\par
\par
"Bah!  You are chagrined, my friend, because a handsome woman has made\par
you a little drama.  Think no more of it!  I have forgotten, for my\par
part."  He added, as they got out at the Aldebaran: "I propose to detain\par
you but a little while, _mon ami_.  When we have completed arrangements\par
for the start to-morrow, you will be free to return and make your peace\par
with Mademoiselle."\par
\par
"Thank you, sir.  She was rattled at my telling her so suddenly about my\par
Commission," said Sherbrand, still beclouded.  "Women are all like that,\par
I suppose?"\par
\par
"Except in France," said the agreeable voice of Raymond, "where the love\par
of Country is stronger in our women than the love of lover or even of\par
child.  It was so before 1870.  They have remembered through the\par
centuries, as their sisters of Britain have not.  They--the women of\par
England are patriotic--oh yes! but patriotism is not yet a religion to\par
them.  It will cost millions of lives, and of blood an ocean to kindle\par
that flame within their souls.  Then, they also will hold the bayonet to\par
the grindstone with their soft white hands and say: 'Become sharp, to\par
drink the blood of Germans!'  And they will mend the soldier's ragged\par
breeches and clean the soldier's dirty rifle, and when they do they will\par
not be less womanly.  No, by my faith! nor less beloved by men.  Try one\par
of these.  You will not find them too bad."\par
\par
He offered Sherbrand a cigarette and took a light from him as they stood\par
under the Aldebaran's tall Corinthian portico.\par
\par
"One should always be accurate.  When I told you that in France there\par
lived no woman who was not patriotic, I was in error.  Such a woman\par
existed since three or four days."\par
\par
He blew out a puff of smoke and watched its mounting spiral.  Then he\par
resumed:\par
\par
"She was very young, very pretty, the bride of a month, and passionately\par
enamoured.  When her husband received orders to proceed with his\par
Regiment of Chasseurs to the Belgian Front, she made him a scene of\par
desperation.  She would do this and that mad thing if he did not take\par
her. Then she became calmer.  She had exacted a promise from her doting\par
cavalryman.  She should visit him at the Front at a suitable\par
opportunity.  She chose her own moment, my faith!--and what a moment!\par
She appeared in her husband's quarters in the French cavalry camp near\par
Antoineville when the Germans were attacking Dinant.  When the Cavalry\par
Division of the Prussian Guards, and the Cavalry of their First\par
Division, with some infantry battalions and machine-gun companies\par
crossed the Meuse, and we were to attack, she was lying in his arms, the\par
little idiot!  He told her to go and she would not.  Then he entreated\par
her--a fatal error that!"\par
\par
The cigarette was burning crookedly, forgotten between Raymond's\par
fingers.\par
\par
"Then he commanded her.  She laughed, and kissed him.  He gave back the\par
kiss, drew his revolver and shot her dead.  Then he ran out--in time to\par
mount and wheel to his place as second in command of his squadron,\par
before the Regiment swept on to the charge.  Fate was kind to him. He\par
charged like a Centaur, and died like a soldier of France the Beloved.\par
Tell the story to Mademoiselle Saxham. She is magnificently handsome,\par
but forgive me! not a patriot. And a woman without patriotism is--an\par
altar without a Sacred Host and a lamp without a flame."\par
\par
They went into the hotel.  When the Frenchman had secured a quiet\par
bedroom on the fourth floor, and intimated that no German was to serve\par
him, they went together into the dining-room.\par
\par
"_Pfui_!  It smells of soot, and petrol, and drainage, this London air\par
of yours," said Raymond, as he chose a table in a quiet corner.  "You\par
will eat with me?  No!  Then smoke and share my wine."  He ordered\par
cutlets, _petit pois_, a sweet omelette, and a bottle of Beaujolais,\par
and, filling his own glass and one for Sherbrand, touched brims gaily\par
and said with a smile: "To France and her Allies, Victory!  On earth," a\par
clink, "by sea," a clink, "under the sea," another clink, "and in the\par
Air!"\par
\par
He clinked three times, and emptied the glass thirstily. Sherbrand\par
asked:\par
\par
"Was the battle near Dinant a big affair?"\par
\par
"Not big."  He broke a roll and munched bread. "Not on the grand scale.\par
A _spectacle tres interessante_, regarded from the--archaic point of\par
view.  An example of the ancient _mode de bataille_ that will be dead as\par
the Dodo in three months.  _Chasseurs a cheval_ and German Imperial\par
Guard Regiments charging and meeting with shocks like thunder.  Much\par
slaughter.  So fierce was the onslaught upon our side that the Germans\par
were driven back across the Meuse.  Many missed the bridge and were\par
drowned.  One French regiment followed them in pursuit for several\par
_kilometres_.  They were led by the man of whom I have told you.  A\par
glass to his memory--and _hers_!"\par
\par
They touched full glasses and drank.  Raymond went on.\par
\par
"My Flying Centre was near Maubeuge on the 16th. Some _escadrilles_ of\par
my command were engaged that day near Dinant.  My faith! those\par
_cotellettes_ are slow in arriving."  He munched more bread, and his\par
blue eyes narrowed smilingly.  "We had only the little bombs we used in\par
Morocco, but yes!--we did some good work with the _balles-bon_. Flying\par
low, at ordered distances--for to make War by Air successfully the\par
science of tactics must assist the aviator.... What says your great\par
Field Marshal, who has bent his neck to the collar-work of\par
Administration--who has conjured an Army of trained soldiers out of your\par
shops and counting-houses, and playing-fields,--and will make another\par
and another when the time comes?"\par
\par
Sherbrand quoted the words uttered by the great voice now quenched for\par
ever in the bitter waters of the North Sea.\par
\par
"_Until aviators learn to fly, manoeuvre, and attack in regular\par
formation, the Fifth Arm will remain a useless limb._"\par
\par
"_Tonnerre de Dieu!_ but that goes to the point," said Raymond,\par
"straight and sharp as a thrust from his sword. If we possessed that man\par
we should make use of him.  He should be Marshal of France, or President\par
or Emperor--all we should ask of him would be to lead us.  _Notr'_\par
Joffre would not be jealous--they would agree like the hilt and the\par
hand.  But I was telling you of an attack by the _flechette_....  You\par
may imagine how the Uhlans loved that rain of steel.  It changed the\par
retreat to a rout.  Only it spoiled so many German horses.  Right\par
through the man, you understand, into the animal! ... Sieves on four\par
legs are useless as Remounts for French Chasseurs."\par
\par
"And the German Field Flight?" Sherbrand interrogated.\par
\par
"Their Fifth Arm was represented," said Raymond, sipping his burgundy,\par
"by many Taubes and Aviatiks armed with the machine-gun and some\par
ordinary bombs of _schrapnel_,--also a dirigible of 'Parsifal' type\par
dropping big bombs.  We were hampered in our offensive by a prejudice\par
which does not trouble the Germans.  To throw bombs upon friend and foe\par
alike--that is not our idea of War.  It annoyed me, and I wasted on that\par
flatulent brute of a 'Parsifal' all my remaining _flechettes_ and little\par
Morocco bombs.  Aha, the _cotelettes_!"\par
\par
A waiter set them before him.  He tucked his napkin under his chin, and\par
helped himself, and said:\par
\par
"Thus, though I had damaged her steering-gear and riddled her outer\par
envelope, and the Flying Pig wallowed in difficulties below me, I could\par
not pursue the advantage I had got.  When the pilot of an Aviatik\par
launched himself to the rescue, all the ammunition of my carabine was\par
exhausted. I had one cartridge left in my automatic revolver, and not a\par
single bomb with which to return the compliments of the German's\par
_mitraille_.  My petrol-tank had been perforated. My single bullet\par
missed him.  The duel was too unequal, so I withdrew from the field,\par
leaving him to cavalier the Flying Pig.  We may meet again upon terms\par
more equal, when French military aviators fight with machine-guns. And\par
now to business.  It concerns your gyroscopic stabiliser, the patent of\par
which my Chiefs desired to buy for the use of our _Service\par
Aeronautique_.  You demanded, according to M. Jourdain's statement,\par
L8,000 and a royalty for the world-patent.  We will buy it of you\par
outright for L12,000. Is it agreed?"\par
\par
Sherbrand straightened in his chair, and said, looking the other\par
squarely in the eyes:\par
\par
"No, sir, thank you!  You see, though the War Office wouldn't have\par
anything to say to me----"\par
\par
"It occurs to you that now you may find a market for your invention?"\par
_To the devil with this smug young British tradesman!_ thought Raymond\par
behind his knitted brows. "Come!" he said.  "Another proposal.  Will you\par
make and supply us with your hawk-hoverer?  Or sell us the right to\par
manufacture a thousand for the sole use of the S. Ae.? Name your\par
price--I shall not be frightened.  It is not State money, but my private\par
fortune that I draw upon--with the approval of my Chiefs.  It has been\par
my whim to lavish on my _escadrille_ what other men hang in jewels upon\par
their mistresses.  Efficiency is my vice.  I have heard of worse!"  He\par
scrawled some invisible figures with a polished finger-nail upon the\par
tablecloth and exclaimed, with a laugh and a shrug: "_Sapristi_!  At\par
even a hundred pounds apiece you would soon be a millionaire, even\par
without the fortune you expect from your War Office!  Upon occasion it\par
pays to be a patriot.  Decide, Monsieur, lest my patience run dry before\par
my purse!"\par
\par
"I've not asked you a hundred, sir," Sherbrand said with his disarming\par
simplicity.  "I can make and sell the hoverers at a profit for L60.\par
It's the cutting and welding of the horizontal flanged screws with the\par
acetylene flame that eats up that money.  But for the cost of the\par
process, hang it!--I'd have had more than seventy ready by me now."\par
\par
"You have seventy, you say, laid by in readiness?"\par
\par
"Laid by in grease," said Sherbrand, "at the aerodrome."\par
\par
"Waiting the moment when the authorities at Whitehall awaken to the fact\par
that you are a genius, _mon ami_!  _A la bonne heure_!  We buy your\par
seventy equilibrisers!"\par
\par
"I'll sell you ten," said the British tradesman doggedly. "And I'll give\par
the Belgian Government another ten, if you think they'd honour me by\par
accepting them?"\par
\par
"_Parole d'honneur_!  I can guarantee they will.  And of the other\par
fifty?"\par
\par
"They are for England to take or leave," said Sherbrand. "No doubt I'm\par
an ass, but a man must act according to his lights."\par
\par
"They are stars, your lights," said Raymond with a crackling oath, "and\par
they point the path of Honour!"  He pulled a cheque-book and a\par
fountain-pen from a pocket within his tunic and wrote a cheque on the\par
Credit Lyonnais for the price of the ten stabilisers, their packing,\par
carriage and duty, saying as he signed, and tossed the lilac slip of\par
paper across the tablecloth: "Your endorsement is my receipt.  For the\par
stabilisers--they must be sent not later than to-morrow.  I would give\par
something if I could fly back to France with a couple in my valise.  But\par
patience!  In a week at most we will give the Germans news of us.\par
Perhaps I shall have the good fortune of a _rencontre_ with my Boche\par
pilot-aviator.  For--listen, lieutenant!  He too possessed the device\par
that solves for the _avion_ the problem of stability.  And--listen\par
well!--he carried a young boy with him in the _nacelle_.  It was the man\par
who robbed you. Von Herrnung!  Could you not have guessed before?"\par
\par
It seemed to Sherbrand that he had always guessed. Raymond went on:\par
\par
"When I read of the finding of the wreck of your 'Bird' in the North\par
Sea, I knew what _coup_ the Prussian and his confederates had carried\par
out.  We had met in Berlin, and at the Hanover aerodrome, and at Paris.\par
And--I could have shot him the other day if it had not been for the\par
child. The legions of the modern Attila employ women and babes as\par
bucklers and breastworks, by their Emperor's order. Perhaps he carried\par
the boy for protection!"  His moustache bristled like an angry cat's as\par
he added:\par
\par
"A beastly idea, but the German Idea is bestial.  Well, _au 'voir_!\par
To-morrow, six _demie_, we start from the aerodrome!"\par
\par
He rose, whisked his napkin over his mouth, and said, giving Sherbrand a\par
hearty hand-grip:\par
\par
"I shall be punctual.  Do not forget.  My compliments to Mademoiselle!"\par
\par
But Sherbrand was occupied less by thoughts of his angry love than by\par
Raymond's story of the boy in the German warplane.  He telephoned to Sir\par
Roland and to Saxham before he drove back to the Club thinking:\par
\par
"Bawne!--It must be Bawne!--out there in the midst of all those horrors.\par
If I could only meet that fellow von Herrnung! ... I've owed him no\par
grudge because he robbed me....  But--for this--I could kill him now!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LVI*\par
\par
                            *LA BRABANCONNE*\par
\par
\par
"You saint, Pat!"  Margot, amidst Raymond's polite excuses, had\par
recognised Sherbrand's hatchet-face under the khaki cap.  "You've stolen\par
a whole morning for me from your Flying Man.  Why didn't you tell me\par
he'd come back to town?  How perfectly tophole he looks in tea-leaves!\par
Franky and I came across that French officer who was with him, last\par
June, in Paris.  We're been rubbing noses on the strength of having met\par
before.  Is Alan going to the Front? My poor Pattums, it'll be your turn\par
to be haunted.  Here's Rhona Helvellyn.  Cheer, Rhona!  Do tell us why\par
you look so smudgy?  Have you been hiding up the chimney of the House of\par
Commons, or bombarding a Minister's front door with coal?"\par
\par
She beckoned, and Rhona came stalking through the crush of marvellously\par
got-up members, the round, fair, freckled boy-face that topped her long\par
swan-neck and deceptively sloping shoulders pinched with weariness under\par
the wreck of a Heath hat, her usually immaculate tailor-mades covered\par
with the dust of what might have been a Claxton Hall conflict or a\par
Downing Street Demonstration, and strange fires burning in her\par
light-lashed eyes.\par
\par
"Am I such a sweep?  I feel one!  But so'd you be grubby if you'd done\par
the crossing from Folkestone to Ostend and back again to London without\par
a dab of a puff.  I'd an appointment here at three-thirty."  Beyond\par
anything in life Rhona plumed herself on her punctuality. "Mrs.\par
Saxham--_the_ Mrs. Saxham, had promised to meet me in the Chintz Room."\par
The Chintz Room is the first-floor drawing-room securable for private\par
teas and interviews.  "We got in too ravenous even to wash for lunch.\par
You should have seen us eat.  My hat! the scrum on those boats.  And the\par
dirt.  Nothing but a Turkish bath will get me clean again.  As for\par
Brenda, she's a nigger."  Thus Rhona in her loud young accents.\par
"Nobody'd believe she'd been born a white girl!"\par
\par
"Is she here?"\par
\par
"My Christmas!  I should rather hope so!  Upstairs scraping off the\par
top-crust before I take her to Eccleston Square.  Don't do to startle\par
the Mater.  She's been frightfully off-colour with worry over her\par
precious youngest. You see, Brenda was due home for the Autumn holidays\par
from the Convent of the Dames de l'Annonciation at Huin on the Sambre,\par
when the War broke out.  And--Huin's near Charleroi, where they say the\par
Germans are--and we'd nary a letter, and no answer to a hailstorm of\par
wires from the Mater.  So I got passes and permits on the Q.T. and\par
skipped over to Ostend--to see what might be done."\par
\par
"And you got through?"\par
\par
"Did I?  Not much!  We don't get things properly rubbed into us--tucked\par
away in our blessed old island.  I forgot that Belgian trains wouldn't\par
be running from Ostend to Brussels, now the Germans have got a grab on\par
there.... As for getting South-East by Courtrai and Valenciennes--all\par
trains were required by the Allies for military purposes. Perhaps if I'd\par
been a hefty War Correspondent or an Army Nursing Sister or a V.A.D. in\par
diamond earrings and a Red Cross armlet, I'd have had a chance.  But I'm\par
doubtful!  Transport officers, English and Belgian, keep their mouths\par
shut--and once they've opened them to say "No!" they never open 'em\par
again.  And"--Rhona breathed as though she had been running--"there were\par
Official War News placards stuck up at the Customs Office, and on the\par
quays and at the Prefecture.  They said that the Germans under von\par
Buelow have been having a scrap with the 5th French Army on the\par
Sambre--from Namur to Charleroi--and that the French have been beaten\par
back.  And the hospitals are crowded with Belgian and German\par
wounded"--she gulped and something twinkled on her pale eyelashes--"and\par
trains crammed with more keep coming in and in. I've seen some sights, I\par
tell you, that gave me horrors. That showed me, even more than those\par
Ostend quays and wharves and squares and Places--packed solid with\par
refugees--Great Christmas!--shall I ever forget 'em!--the devilish,\par
hellish work of War!"\par
\par
"Refugees....  Common people?"  Margot was a little puzzled.  Rhona\par
nodded and repeated:\par
\par
"Refugees.  Swells and mechanics, rag-pickers and shopkeepers, sweeps,\par
schoolgirls, lacemakers, and students. Professors, priests, and\par
prostitutes.  Madame la Comtesse and her gardener's wife, wheeling the\par
babies in trams and go-carts.  Dust-covered, dirty, done up, desperate,\par
with faces that make you think of the damned in the Tartarus scenes of\par
Orpheus and Eurydice.  And someone squealed my name, and there was\par
Brenda.  Just got in, with three of the Sisters, and a baker's dozen of\par
English pupils and a herd of other miserables, evacuated from Charleroi\par
and Huin. Three-and-a-half days on the journey, travelling by fits and\par
starts on branch-lines--tramping when trains weren't available.  Eating\par
whenever anything was to be had, and going without when there wasn't!\par
Sleeping in barns and on the floors of railway-station platforms, or\par
waiting-rooms, when they were lucky--such a pack of tramps you never saw\par
in your life.  But Great Scott! how thundering glad I was to get hold of\par
Brenda and whisk her away from that Chorus of the Damned in Orpheus,\par
pent up like cattle behind ropes, and moaning and stretching their arms\par
out to the sea!"\par
\par
"Why on earth the sea?"\par
\par
A foreign voice, resonant and rather nasal, startled Margot by\par
answering:\par
\par
"Pardon, Madame.  Because these most unhappy fugitives believe that\par
salvation and safety may be found in England, from whence come those\par
strong brown English soldiers who are fighting in Belgium now."\par
\par
"Are there--" Margot was beginning.  But Rhona was introducing the\par
speaker at length as Comte d'Asnay, Capitaine Commandant and Adjutant of\par
the Belgian General Staff, Attached to the General Staff on the Third\par
Division of the Belgian Army, and d'Asnay was saying with a smile:\par
\par
"Mademoiselle bestows upon me all my titles, possibly because we\par
Belgians have so little else left."\par
\par
"Except Honour," snapped Rhona.\par
\par
"Except our Honour and our self-respect, and a few other non-negotiable\par
securities," he said, "that do not bring us much of credit on the\par
Bourses of Vienna and Berlin.  But Madame was asking of the refugees.\par
Many from Liege have escaped to Antwerp or into Holland, thousands are\par
rushing from Namur into the bosom of France.  But from Louvain and\par
Brussels and Tirlemont they flock to Ostend. The steamers of the Channel\par
service are crowded with those who have money and can obtain the\par
necessary _laissez-passers_. Your town of Folkestone is encumbered with\par
arrivals.  Were stones pillows there would be a head for every stone.\par
But those who have neither money nor passports--and many of these were\par
rich a week ago--remain, as Mademoiselle has told you, to weep, and\par
stretch their arms towards the sea."\par
\par
"They'd rush the boats," declared Rhona, "only that the Companies keep\par
up the gangways.  I suppose," she grimaced, "the authorities at Ostend\par
don't want a scare.  They believe--I hope they may get it!--there'll yet\par
be an Autumn Season.  Hang these profit-hoggers!  If I'd my way I'd\par
lower every blessed gangway and let everyone who wanted walk on board.\par
If Belgium hadn't faced the music there'd be Germans in England now,\par
murdering and burning....  They've a right to come.  Let 'em all come!\par
Britain's big enough, I should hope!"\par
\par
"_Brava, Mademoiselle.  Bis!_" d'Asnay applauded noiselessly. "That is\par
what you said to me on the deck of the steamer.  Say it again, say it\par
often, and the people will be let come!"\par
\par
"Oh, I've my plan."  Rhona's light eyes sparkled wickedly.  "People here\par
want waking up.  They're kept in cotton-wool.  Eyes bunged up and ears\par
stuffed.  What they want is--to see and hear.  Well, a few of 'em are\par
doing it.  That," she nodded knowingly at d'Asnay, "is where my\par
Distinguished Visitors come in."\par
\par
The lips under the fiercely-waxed moustaches smiled. Margot liked the\par
look of this officer of the Belgian General Staff, with the savage eyes\par
and the smooth olive skin, the pointed chestnut beard, fiercely-waxed\par
moustache, and the cool, polite manner.  He wore the uniform of the\par
Belgian Chasseurs a Cheval, and the vulture-plumes of his high shako\par
were cut and broken and scorched in places, the gold braiding of his\par
dark blue tunic was tarnished and weather-beaten, and the grey,\par
blue-striped overalls and spurred black knee-boots were rusty with old\par
mud and white with new dust.  "You're from the Front?" she queried, as\par
she moved with Rhona and the Belgian towards the glass swing-doors,\par
giving access from the vestibule to the Club's big ground-floor\par
drawing-room.\par
\par
He answered:\par
\par
"There are several Fronts--and I have the honour to come from one of\par
them, Madame."\par
\par
"With dispatches?"\par
\par
"Possibly with dispatches, Madame!"  He answered with an amused\par
side-glance at the small, vivacious face. "Though there are swifter\par
methods of transmitting intelligence than by entrusting letters to a\par
messenger's hands."\par
\par
As he moved beside her, courteously replying, she saw the crimson and\par
green enamelled, purple-ribboned Cross of the Belgian Order of Leopold\par
shining upon the dark blue tunic-breast.\par
\par
"How are--things--getting on?  Nobody tells us anything," twittered the\par
humming-bird.  "We might live at the North Pole."\par
\par
"Madame might find even at the North Pole compensations for the low\par
temperature and the lack of society."  The vulture-plumes on the dark\par
blue shako nodded as he turned his face to her.  "In the fact that there\par
are no Boches there," he added, and the smile that had curved the\par
soldierly moustache vanished as though the word had wiped it from his\par
mouth.\par
\par
"Do tell me what are Boches?" Margot begged, kindling to interest.  He\par
answered with an intensity that dug deep lines at the angles of his\par
nostrils, and puckered the corners of the eyes that burned under his\par
frowning brows:\par
\par
"They are a nation of beings, Madame, that are no longer men!"\par
\par
"Germans you mean, don't you?" she asked after a little pause of\par
bewilderment, staring with shocked, dilated eyes at the left side of\par
d'Asnay's close-cropped head, now revealed to her as he removed his\par
shako, and standing a little in advance of the two women, held back with\par
the thrust of his broad shoulders a leaf of the drawing-room\par
swing-doors. The four-inch square of white surgical plaster adhering to\par
a place whence the chestnut-brown hair had been shaven, showed the\par
outline of a deep, jagged gash.  "You are hurt! You have had some awful\par
accident! ... Was it a motor-smash? Doesn't it pain you?" Kittums asked\par
breathlessly. For d'Asnay had touched the surgical strapping with his\par
gloved hand, and his smiling face had winced.\par
\par
"It is nothing, Madame," he assured her, "and it was not caused by an\par
accident.  It is merely a whiff of _schrapnel_--a love-gift from\par
_Messieurs les Boches_."\par
\par
"You are _wounded_?"\par
\par
"Madame, that is what one calls it, when one suffers _a coup d'obus_.\par
They are common, these little tokens, on our side of the North Sea.\par
Mine has procured me a visit to London, and the pleasure of meeting\par
you."\par
\par
She looked at him like a grieved child, and her lips so quivered that he\par
softened to her behind the crinkles of his smiling bearded mask.\par
\par
"You speak like this because you think I am heartless and indifferent.\par
Perhaps I have been--until to-day!  We are so far from things.  We see\par
nothing.  And we hear so little about the War!"\par
\par
"Alas, Madame!" came the answer.  "Forgive the cruel prophecy, that the\par
moment approaches when you will hear too much!"\par
\par
The swing-doors thudded behind them like guns at a great distance.  The\par
capacious ground-floor drawing-room, not usually crowded before\par
luncheon, was thronged nearly to the walls.  A vacant space in the\par
centre presumably accommodated the Distinguished Visitors.  But between\par
these and Margot's quickening curiosity intervened a solid wall of\par
backs.\par
\par
The Distinguished Visitors must be Royalties, decided Margot, as she\par
skirted the barrier, looking right and left for a peephole, recognising\par
the vast back of Sir Thomas Brayham, the skeleton back of the Goblin,\par
the willowy back of Trixie Wastwood, the backs of Lady Beauvayse,\par
Cynthia Charterhouse, Tota Stannus, and Patrine Saxham with other backs\par
pertaining to divers dear friends, consolidated into the rampart of\par
humanity over which the towering feathers of Vanity Fair nodded and\par
bobbed and waved.\par
\par
"They're taking it in," Margot heard Rhona mutter, behind her.\par
"'_Somebody's playing off a joke on us_,' would be the first thing\par
that'd come into their blessed heads. Well!--let 'em think what they\par
choose.  Ask me why I did it, Comte, and I swear I couldn't tell you.\par
Blue murder! how my arms ache.  But so must yours.  You nursed the\par
biggest of the babies all the way from Ostend to Charing Cross."\par
\par
"Mademoiselle is right!"  The swift, fierce undertone was d'Asnay's.\par
"They do not comprehend yet.  Not yet!"  He breathed hissingly through\par
his nose.  "Wait--and presently the Truth will leap at them and strike\par
them _entre les yeux_.  But a place must be found for the friend of\par
Mademoiselle!"  He came noiselessly to the side of Margot. "A chair, so.\par
A footstool, so.  Madame will step on the one and mount to the other.\par
Permit, Madame, that I offer my assistance!  Now Madame commands an\par
excellent view of--shall I call it--the spectacle?"\par
\par
The speaker's voice was drowned in an outburst of strident music.\par
Barely two doors from the Club the piano-organ had broken out with "_La\par
Barbanconne_."  And as the walls vibrated to its shrill cries of\par
triumph, and the wild disonances of a joy that touches frenzy, the\par
cracked but vigorous tenor began to sing:\par
\par
    "Apres des siecles, des siecles d'esclavage\par
      Le Beige sortant du tombeau\par
    A reconquis par son courage\par
      Son nom, son droit et son drapeau.\par
    Et ta main souveraine et fiere\par
      Peuple desormais indompte\par
    Grava sur ta vielle banniere\par
      Le roi, la Loi, la Liberte!"\par
\par
\par
"_Sapristi_!  It is strange that!" d'Asnay muttered at the first bars.\par
"Mademoiselle Helvellyn devised the tableau, certainly, but who arranged\par
the _entr'acte_?"\par
\par
The shrill, unbearable frenzy of the piano-organ abated, the voice of\par
the singer was more plainly heard.  It chanted in thin nasal tones, with\par
missed-out notes in each bar that were like gaps where teeth had been in\par
an old sorrowful singing mouth:\par
\par
    "O Belgique, O mere cherie,\par
      A toi nos coeurs, a tot nos bras,\par
    A toi noire sang, O Patrie----"\par
\par
\par
While Margot, a-tiptoe en her chair, peered through the screen of\par
towering feathers at the Club's Distinguished Visitors,--wondering that\par
within the wall of absorbed faces there should be so little to attract\par
or interest.  Nothing more intriguing than the homely figure of a\par
Flemish peasant woman, with four young children huddled round her, and a\par
baby at her breast.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LVII*\par
\par
                           *THE BELGIAN WIFE*\par
\par
\par
Desolate advance-guard of the vast army so soon to invade the shores of\par
Britain, how familiar the figure is now that was then so strange to us\par
in the quaint old-world fashion of its homely garments, the thick white\par
dust and travel-stains that covered it, from the linen coif to the\par
wooden shoes.\par
\par
She was not old, the woman who sat with her little flock gathered about\par
her, on the Indian stool that had supported the superb person of von\par
Herrnung, what time he had held forth to Mrs. Charterhouse and Lady\par
Wastwood upon the loftiness of German Kultur, the perfection of German\par
female beauty, and the overwhelming mental and bodily superiority of the\par
German Superman.  A Walloon peasant from a village near Jodoigne where\par
she and her husband had worked upon a tiny farm.\par
\par
Perhaps a dozen words of French were hers: "_Tout brule!_" and "_En\par
Angleterre ou il n'y a pas de Boches!_"\par
\par
We were to learn to reap terrible meanings from that hoarse, faint\par
parrot-cry.  Truths that raised the hairs upon the flesh and chilled the\par
blood were to be imaged for us in the blank vacuity of her unseeing\par
stare.  We were to learn why all her children squinted, from Vic, the\par
sturdy man of seven, and Josephine, his junior, possibly by a year, down\par
to Georgette of the chubby cheeks and crinkly, roguish eyelids, and\par
Albert, of the round blue stare, the big white-haired head, and the\par
marvellous bow legs.\par
\par
In their dull stunned quietude and their clayey pallor, the mark of the\par
Beast was branded upon them, down to the livid baby in its little cap of\par
soiled linen, swaddled in the old red shawl, that bound down its arms.\par
You might have thought it dead, but for the flutter of a muscle in the\par
cheek, and the faint movement of its lips, feebly sucking at the breast\par
that had been large and bounteous, and now was lax, and flabby, covered\par
by a network of darkish violet veins.\par
\par
\par
"_Who are they? ... What are they? ... Where do they come from? ... Why\par
were they brought here? ... Does no one know? ... Will no one tell?\par
..._"\par
\par
\par
The silence of amazement was now breaking.  The mouths belonging to the\par
faces under the nodding feathers, old and young, handsome and ugly,\par
vacuous and clever, silly and intellectual, were all prattling\par
interrogations like the above.  Pride of Place and Joy of Life, Thirst\par
of Pleasure, Lust of Power, Gaiety and Weariness, Wisdom and Folly,\par
Humbug and Sincerity, Meanness and Generosity, ringed-in the dusty group\par
of wooden-shod mysteries and most frightfully wanted to know!  And\par
nobody offered any solution of the puzzle.  The piano-organ was playing\par
half a dozen doors below the Club, the cracked old tenor quavering to\par
its accompaniment:\par
\par
    "_Nous le jurons tu vivras!_\par
      _Tu vivras toujours grande el belle_\par
    _Et ton invincible unite_\par
      _Aura pour devise immortelle----_"\par
\par
\par
The music suddenly broke off.  A policeman had ordered the organ to move\par
on....\par
\par
"_Tout brule!_"\par
\par
Hitherto the Belgian woman had not looked up, nor changed her listless\par
attitude.  Now she lifted her empty expressionless eyes, and hoarsely\par
iterated her parrot-cry. The suckling at her breast whimpered and let go\par
the nipple. She glanced at it, saying in her own thick Flemish tongue:\par
\par
"_Daar is geen melk._"[1]\par
\par
\par
[1] "There is no milk."\par
\par
\par
She rocked the baby for whom she had no milk.  Its feeble whimper was\par
not stilled.  She went on to that accompaniment:\par
\par
"_De Duischer kwamen.  Zy hebben alles gebrand!  De geburen,--mijn\par
voder--mijn man is gedood!  Zy hebben hem in het vuur geworpen!_"[2]\par
\par
\par
[2] "The Germans came.  They burned everything.  The neighbours, and my\par
father, and my husband are dead.  They threw them into the fire."\par
\par
\par
The baby's whimper became a wail of feeble protest.  It fought and\par
struggled frantically under the old red swathing shawl.  The shawl\par
loosened, slid to the floor, and the wizened arms rose free and jerking.\par
One arm, tightly bandaged below the elbow, ended in a raw and bloody\par
stump.  She regarded it with her drained-out stare, not trying to\par
replace the strappings that had bound it, saying in the heavy voice of a\par
sleep-walker:\par
\par
"_Dees ook hebben ze gedaan.  God sta ons bij!_"[3]\par
\par
\par
[3] "This too they did.  God help us!"\par
\par
\par
And sobs and weeping broke out around her, as though that little\par
handless arm had been a veritable rod of Moses bringing water from the\par
living rock.  But no sigh lifted her bosom, nor were her dry eyelids\par
moistened with the dew of tears.  Prussian militarism had wrought its\par
work upon her. She and hers had been trodden as grapes in the\par
Hohenzollern Winepress.  Those emptied eyes had seen things done that\par
might well make devils laugh in Hell.\par
\par
\par
The Club walls vanished away as we looked, and behind that stricken\par
figure spread the devastated plains of Belgium, the Sorrowful, the\par
Glorious, who has endured agony and shame unutterable, that her\par
neighbours might go free.  We had a vision of the Son of Man descending\par
in a blood-red, rainy dawning, and heard Him saying to the apostles of\par
German Kultur:\par
\par
"_Inasmuch as ye have done it unto the least of these ... ye have done\par
it unto Me!_"\par
\par
And not a woman among us who had a man with the British Expedition, but\par
prayed in her soul, fervently:\par
\par
"Vengeance is Thine, for Thou hast said it.  But make _him_ Thy scourge,\par
O Lord!"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER LVIII*\par
\par
                      *SHERBRAND BUYS THE LICENCE*\par
\par
\par
The spell of silence was broken.  Excitement seethed as Patrine escaped\par
out of the crush in the drawing-room and returned to the vestibule.\par
There, subsiding into one of the tall-backed chairs beside the table\par
that held the Members' Register and Visitors' Book, she waited, hoping\par
against hope that the tall figure in khaki might reappear under the Club\par
portico.\par
\par
"Patrine!"\par
\par
"Oh, Alan!--you came back after all!"\par
\par
Her gloom changed to radiancy.  She rose up as the tall figure of\par
Sherbrand passed under the portico, and hurried to him, emptying her\par
budget of regrets.  "I've behaved like a cad.  Do forgive me!  Don't be\par
wrathy.  But you can't be--or you'd never have come back."\par
\par
"You dear, it's all right!"  He caught the outstretched hands in both\par
his and wrung them.  "Forget--and let's be happy."  The truth about\par
Bawne tugged at him as he said the words, but he had determined not to\par
torture her with that horror.  He went on, with the frankness that she\par
found so lovable, "I was vexed, but it was idiotic of me not to have\par
told you about the Commission before."\par
\par
"And the man.  Your French sossifer," she went on, "who looked at me as\par
though I ought to live in a cage at the Zoo?  What must he have thought\par
of your taste in young women?  What mustn't he have said when he got you\par
out of the way?"\par
\par
"Oh, not much!"\par
\par
"Go on.  Rub it in!"\par
\par
"Well then"--Sherbrand's mouth was steady, but the laughter in his eyes\par
was not to be controlled--"he saw I was fearfully sick at your having\par
shown temper before him. And he told me not to be chagrined because a\par
handsome woman had made me a little drama."\par
\par
"F'ff!"  She winced and set her teeth on her crimson underlip.  "He knew\par
I'd ask and you'd tell me.  He saw me--squirming--in his mind's eye.\par
Oh! and how he's hit me off.  For I _was_ awfully like the heavy leading\par
lady of a tin travelling theatre-company.  Aren't you ashamed of me?\par
Don't you loathe me?" she wooed with entreating eyes.\par
\par
"Frightfully.  Tell me--where can we have a cosy talk together?  I've\par
got a whole hour before I'm due at Hendon," he said.\par
\par
"The Rose-and-Green Divan--but there are sure to be people smoking\par
there.  Oh!--I know.  The Little Library. Nobody ever goes in, and it's\par
got a door opening into the Divan.  Friends of Members aren't admitted\par
into the Library--but if you're caught there--you say you were coming\par
out of the Divan, where outsiders are allowed--and opened the wrong\par
door--do you switch on?"\par
\par
He nodded, repressing the desire to ask in whose company she had been\par
caught there, and followed the tall lithe figure down a short corridor\par
leading to the back of the ground-floor.  The corridor ended in the\par
Little Library, a studious apartment of bathing-machine dimensions,\par
walled with curiously new-appearing books of information and reference,\par
and containing two small writing-tables, each supported by a\par
rosewood-stained Windsor, a brace of baskets, and two deep, cushiony,\par
Rothmore chairs.  A Member of mature years and mountainous proportions\par
slept placidly in one of these, with Whitaker's Peerage balanced at a\par
perilous angle on the vanishing indications of what must once have been\par
her lap.  The subdued murmur of voices trickled in from the adjoining\par
smoking-room with vaporous wisps of Turkish and Virginia.  Save for the\par
stout slumbering Member the lovers were beautifully alone.\par
\par
"Good!  Oh, boy!--to have got you back again," Patrine said breathlessly\par
after their kiss.  She dropped down noiselessly into the springy\par
embraces of the vacant Rothmore, and Sherbrand smiling, perched upon the\par
chair's broad arm.\par
\par
"This is an unbecoming contrast--isn't it?"  She leaned her beech-leaf\par
tinted head against the plastron of the khaki tunic as his strong hand\par
crept behind her supple waist. "But I don't care, I can't think of\par
anything but you, Alan. When do you start to-morrow, and from where?  I\par
suppose you mustn't tell me?"  She sighed, rubbing her cheek against him\par
as the strong arm embraced and held her.  "Oh me!  What it is to be the\par
sweetheart of a soldier.  Why--Alan!"\par
\par
She lifted her head and looked at him, frowning, and her long eyes were\par
black between the narrowed lids.  "Do you know how your heart jumped\par
when I said 'soldier'?  Does it mean as much to you as all that?"\par
\par
He began to stammer a little.\par
\par
"Oh--well!--you see--we Sherbrands have worn the King's coat for ages.\par
Ever since there were any Sherbrands--going by the portraits in the\par
gallery at Whins--where my father lived when he was a boy.  He used to\par
describe them to me until I knew them as well as he did from the Sir\par
Alan who fought with Talbot against the French at Castillan Chatillon as\par
a boy, and got killed at Bannockburn thirty-five years later, down to\par
the jolly old Sir Roger, who fought like a Trojan at Badajoz.  He was my\par
great-grandfather, so I suppose I've always had a secret hankering for\par
the Service. Like the inherited nostalgia Hillmen's children have for\par
the mountains, or sailors' for the sea.  The kind of feeling that sets\par
the little Arctic foxes in the Zoo howling at the first sprinkle of snow\par
in December.  Only I knew I mustn't yield to it.  You know the reason\par
why!"\par
\par
"You told me, and I answered that that kind of reason couldn't affect\par
you."\par
\par
"Now you shall hear a plan I've been nursing."  His arm again engirdled\par
her.  "Do you know Seasheere?  It's a little grassy, cliffy, shingly\par
village on the South-East coast, three-hours' journey from Charing\par
Cross.  There's a Naval Air Station there that was a Seaplane School not\par
long ago.  We used to send 'em pupils from Hendon: there's a cottage\par
where they take lodgers not far off.  I spent three weeks there last\par
summer, fishing and motor-boating when I wasn't making friends with\par
Goody Two Shoes----"\par
\par
"Who's Goody Two Shoes?"\par
\par
"The hydroplane!"  His voice broke in laughter.  "Did you think I meant\par
a girl?"\par
\par
"I'm an idiot.  Go on about your plan, dear."\par
\par
"Oh--well!  The cottage I stayed at was jolly comfortable, and the\par
landlady the tidiest old woman that ever grilled a chop.  Now\par
suppose--to-morrow, or a week, or two months hence you got a wire from\par
Somewhere in France or Belgium saying:\par
'_Seasheere--such-a-day-and-such-an-hour--Alan_'--would you pack your\par
kit for a week-end and hop into the train, and come?"\par
\par
"Without asking--without telling--Aunt Lynette or Uncle Owen?"  She\par
asked the question breathlessly.\par
\par
"We'll tell the Doctor and Mrs. Saxham directly afterwards."  He leaned\par
his cheek on the beech-leaf hair and his arm tightened about her waist\par
possessively.  "You said my heart jumped just now when you called me a\par
soldier.  How it will jump when I pick you out with the glasses, a tiny\par
black speck on the cliffs at Seasheere, waiting with the sunset behind\par
you, or the dawn in your eyes to welcome me back from over the sea.  Oh,\par
my girl!"--his voice wooed her irresistibly--"I've dreamed wide awake of\par
the joy of such a greeting....  It's up to you to make my dream come\par
true!"  He kissed her hair.  "And we'll watch the day die, and sup\par
together, and you'll sleep at my nice old woman's cottage.  And I'll\par
turn in at the Air Station--and next morning we'll be married at\par
Seasheere Catholic Church!"\par
\par
"Married--that's your plan?  Ah, Alan! shall we ever be married?" she\par
sighed.\par
\par
He laughed softly, pressing her against him.\par
\par
"The little Catholic Church I've mentioned was built for the very\par
purpose.  Perched on the cliffs as though it might spread its rafters\par
any minute and flap away to sea."  He kissed her hair again.  "Don't\par
think I'm spinning fairy-tales. I've got a Special Licence, so there's\par
no need to bother about time, or previous residence in the district, or\par
anything stuffy.  Nothing's wanted but Opportunity, the church, and the\par
priest.  And that the local Registrar should put in an appearance.\par
That's necessary, as we're not of the same faith--yet!"\par
\par
She freed herself from his embrace, rose to her superb height, and stood\par
over him.\par
\par
"You've arranged all this--without consulting me for a minute.  You and\par
your landlady--and your Licence and your Registrar!  Boy, I am sensible\par
of a great desire to box your ears soundly for this!"\par
\par
"I'd rather take a clout from you than a kiss from any other woman."\par
\par
She tapped him lightly on both ears, and said, putting a butterfly touch\par
of lips in the middle of the broad, tanned brow:\par
\par
"There are both clout and kiss.  Now show me the Special Licence."\par
\par
He thrust his hand into a pocket behind the plastron of the khaki tunic\par
and pulled out a note-case she had bought and given him.  The shiny\par
square of parchment-paper bearing the signature of his Grace the\par
Archbishop of Canterbury drew both their heads together over it.  In a\par
compartment meant for stamps was a hard, thin, metallic circle, shining\par
yellow through tissue paper folds.\par
\par
"The--Ring?" she whispered.\par
\par
"The Ring!"  He nodded, smiling, as she bent her face over it, kissed\par
the tissue paper reverently, stuck the Licence back in its compartment,\par
and gave him back the case.\par
\par
"And you had these in your pocket this afternoon when I was such a\par
horrid beast to you?"\par
\par
"They were burning a hole right into my chest.  Why, Pat,\par
you're--crying!"\par
\par
She half turned away, mopping her wet eyes with her flimsy little\par
handkerchief.\par
\par
"Because--because--it's so blessedly sweet and dear of you to have\par
planned this.  Do you--do you really want it so much?"\par
\par
"More than anything under the sky," said Sherbrand. "And, don't you see,\par
it settles the question of providing for you, splendidly!  If we're\par
married, and I get--pipped--Somewhere at the Front--"  He stopped short,\par
for one of her large hands firmly covered his mouth.\par
\par
"I won't have it.  You're not to speak like that, ever!" said a muffled\par
voice above his head.  "If you were killed--don't you\par
understand--everything'd be over for me!  It's a kind of nasty little\par
Death--only to have you hint at it."\par
\par
"All right!" he mumbled penitently, and kissed the hand. It was\par
withdrawn, and he went on:\par
\par
"I have my little fortune, though Flying has made a hole in it.  And I'd\par
naturally like--as my mother is provided for--the stuff to go to my\par
wife."\par
\par
"Oh! if I only were--good enough, I would be your wife to-morrow!" she\par
groaned.\par
\par
He got up and took her masterfully in his arms.\par
\par
"No more of that.  I can't stick being made out a--bally pattern.  You\par
are a hundred times too good for me!"\par
\par
"_But not at all patriotic_," came drifting back upon him in the voice\par
of Raymond.  His embrace never slackened, but he asked of her a\par
question, looking for the answer to lighten in her eyes: "Pat--you've\par
not said yet that you're glad they've given me my Flying\par
Commission!--that you're British enough to give your man, if it came to\par
giving--for the Old Shop!  I know you are!--of course you are!--but say\par
it--I'd like to hear you."\par
\par
"I--I----"  She caught her breath and her eyes wavered miserably under\par
his steady gaze.  "I'm not a little bit o' good at telling decent proper\par
lies.  I love England--but I love you heaps, heaps, _heaps_ best!"  He\par
felt her pant between his arms....  She writhed her long white neck like\par
a creature in desperate agony.  "I want to eat my cake and have it!" she\par
wailed, evading his eyes.  "Now you know me, you'll despise me.  But\par
it's the truth--anyway! I'd like a man to send to the War--and a man to\par
keep for myself!"\par
\par
His arms wrapped her closely and his heart plunged madly against her\par
bosom.  He kissed her on her yielded mouth, and the kiss was a living\par
flame.\par
\par
"That will be when we are married and you have a son!" he whispered, and\par
a drowning horror enveloped her.  She cried out and thrust him back, and\par
might have sunk down at his feet and told her dreadful story then....\par
\par
Whitaker's Peerage intervened, sliding from the lap of the obese,\par
reposeful Member, and falling to the carpet with a resounding thump.\par
The indignant eyes of the awakened lady glared at Sherbrand over her\par
gold-rimmed spectacles. She demanded, snorting:\par
\par
"Since when has this room--_hr'runk!_--been thrown open to visitors?"\par
\par
"I'll inquire," Sherbrand stammered, and the guilty couple fled.  That\par
night Patrine wrote on a card "Seasheere," and thenceafter wore it in\par
her bosom.  But many weeks were over her head before the Call came.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LIX*\par
\par
                         *THE WOE-WAVE BREAKS*\par
\par
\par
Meanwhile everybody who could get near the Belgian refugees excitedly\par
pressed hospitality upon them....  The desolate mother was termed "Poor\par
Dear" in a dozen different keys of sympathy.  But she only looked with\par
dull vague eyes in the faces of would-be philanthropists.  When kindly\par
hands tried to draw the little ones away, she grabbed them and held on.\par
\par
"She doesn't understand us, the Poor Dear Creature!"  Thus the Goblin,\par
gulping within her rows of pearls, red-eyed under her towering osprey\par
_panache_.  "What she has suffered!  It shatters one to realise.  Can\par
one credit that dear Count Tido could have belonged to such a race?\par
Miss Helvellyn claims her by right of discovery, I believe, so farewell\par
to my plans for her benefit!  But Belgians, I understand, are to be had\par
in any quantity, and Belgians I must and will have!  Think of those rows\par
and _rows_ of new cottages standing empty at Wathe Regis, and that huge\par
caravanserai that nobody can live in at the corner of Russell Square!\par
Do you hear me, Sir Thomas?  Oh, how clever of you, Lady Eliason!  Sir\par
Thomas, listen!  Lady Eliason _positively promises_ that Sir Solomon\par
shall interest himself in this.  _Of course_ there must be a Fund, and a\par
Committee, and a Headquarters!  The Fund must be Huge, the Committee\par
Representative....  Dear Lady Beauvayse is to be our Hon. Secretary....\par
With your legal knowledge and influence, and your passion for\par
philanthropy, Sir Thomas, don't tell me You are going to keep out of\par
this! You are damned if you do! did you say?  Bless you!  Who are these\par
queer people coming in?"\par
\par
Two nuns in the familiar habit worn by Roman Catholic Sisters of\par
Charity, little black-robed figures with starched white coifs, broad\par
white guimpes and flowing black veils, had passed the Club windows a\par
moment previously.  A tall, slight woman in Quaker grey had seen and\par
hurried in pursuit of the Sisters, recognised as members of a Belgian\par
Community, to whom Mrs. Saxham explained the situation, speaking in her\par
exquisite French.  The Sisters replied in a less polished accent, their\par
discreet eyes ignoring curious glances as their guide ushered them into\par
the crowded drawing-room.\par
\par
The crowd parted before them, revealing Rachel and her children.  The\par
nuns moved forwards and stood within the radius of those heavy, vacant\par
eyes.  Life leaped into them. She cried out in her thick Flemish tongue\par
and was answered, and rose up, the children clinging to her.  In a\par
moment the Sisters had advanced upon her, taken the baby from the\par
cramped arms that now resigned it, taken the mother also into a pair of\par
black-sleeved arms.  And she was weeping on the bosom of Charity, and\par
telling them the dreadful story that is told anew every day.  Presently\par
she and Vic, Josephine, Georgette, and Albert the big-headed, were\par
eating cake and drinking coffee under the sheltering wing of the\par
Sisters, but though some elderly Members still hovered in their\par
neighbourhood, the question of a Fund and a Committee had usurped the\par
attention of the Club.\par
\par
Lady Eliason and Lady Wathe were selecting a Quorum.... Rhona Helvellyn\par
had proposed to Lynette an adjournment to the Chintz Room.  They had\par
reached the swing-doors of the drawing-room, when with violence they\par
banged open to admit Brenda Helvellyn in the maddest spirits, escorted\par
by Doda Foltlebarre and Sissi Eliason and half a dozen of the wilder,\par
younger members of the Club.\par
\par
Said Rhona, barring her junior's way with a long thin arm as Brenda\par
rollicked past her:\par
\par
"Mrs. Saxham, let me introduce my sister Brenda. Brenda admires you\par
frightfully!"\par
\par
Brenda, staring with wide bright eyes at the object of her alleged\par
admiration, offered a pink, moist, recently washed hand to Lynette.  At\par
Rhona's indignant exclamation she started and pulled away the hand,\par
stammering:\par
\par
"They wouldn't let me! ..."\par
\par
"Wouldn't let you change into decent clothes when I'd 'phoned Home to\par
have some sent here?  Tell me another!"\par
\par
"Well, then, the things hadn't come!"\par
\par
"And if they haven't, why not have stayed upstairs until they do come?"\par
\par
"All alone....  Oh!  I couldn't!  Anything awful might happen up\par
there...."  The peach-face of sixteen winced and the eyebrows puckered.\par
"And Doda and Sissi simply _love_ me in these things.  They said I must\par
come down and be seen!"\par
\par
Doda and Sissi and the guilty six exchanged rapturous winks and\par
grimaces.  Certainly a damsel of sixteen, whose superb crimson tresses\par
are crowned with the squashed ruin of a muslin "Trouville" hat, and\par
whose slender form is draped in the wilted wraith of a light green\par
aquascutum, is more than likely to create a _succes fou_, on her\par
appearance in a London drawing-room.\par
\par
"'Seen!'" Rhona snorted.  "Well, you are a sight, there's no denying.\par
From your head to your feet--My merry Christmas! what _have_ you got on\par
your feet?"\par
\par
Brenda tittered nervously, poking out a slim foot in a huge golosh lined\par
with wearied red flannel.\par
\par
"They're the Mere Econome's.  There wasn't time to dress properly.  We\par
were turned out of the Convent, haven't I told you!--just as we stood.\par
It was early in the morning.  Seven o'clock Mass was just over.  We were\par
trooping in to the _Refectoire_ for coffee.  We went to Mass and did our\par
lessons, in spite of the awful guns.  Then ... all at once--"  She began\par
to laugh, and a mask of fine glittering dew broke out over her peachy\par
face from the temples to the upper lip.  "The earth began to shake. The\par
French were retreating from Charleroi.  They streamed past and past,\par
horsemen and guns and marching men, just as they'd gone by two days\par
before when we waved and cheered them from the garden.  Only this time\par
there were wounded men....  The ambulance waggons were heaped with\par
them--all bloody and dreadful....  Oh!  And then the shells began to\par
fall ... among the waggons and on the Convent!  "The Germans are\par
coming," the soldiers called to us.  'Fly while you have time!'"\par
\par
"Shut up!" Rhona ordered the girl.  "Haven't I told you not to talk, you\par
stoopid!  There weren't any shells--it's all your silly nerves.  There\par
might have been--but there weren't!"\par
\par
"But the shells were hitting the Convent walls ... and bursting.  The\par
house was on fire.  And the French Commandant said to the _Maitresse\par
Generale_: 'It will be _rase_ over your heads if you remain, Madame.\par
_On n'y fait quartier a personne--les Allemands_!  They are advancing in\par
incredible numbers.  The road to Calais lies open before them because of\par
the Great Catastrophe of yesterday.  Our hearts are sad, not only for\par
our own losses, but for the misfortunes of our friends across the----'"\par
\par
"WILL you be silent!  He never said so!"\par
\par
With her scarlet head surmounting the shiny waterproof, Brenda rather\par
reminded one of a Green Hackle, the likeness to the splendid\par
gauze-winged fly being increased by the brightness of her eyes.  Very\par
round, very wide open, and with strange lines radiating from the\par
pin-point speck of pupil to the outer band ringing the hazel irids, they\par
stared from that crystal-beaded mask of hers.  "But, Rhona," she\par
reiterated, bewildered by her senior's vehemence of contradiction, "he\par
_did_ say so!  And the Convent was burning when we left!"\par
\par
"If it was, you're to forget it--d'you hear me?  And look here, if you\par
dare to talk like this at home----"\par
\par
"I won't.  I know the Mater mustn't be upset!  Look here, I'll swear I\par
won't, if that'll do!  Only don't say I've got to stop upstairs, will\par
you?  They're so gay here," Brenda pleaded humbly--"it'll help me to\par
forget!"\par
\par
"All right!" and with a warning scowl from Rhona the sisters parted.\par
Lynette Saxham asked, looking after the little bizarre figure of Brenda\par
with wistful tenderness in her eyes:\par
\par
"Will she recover from the shock of the horrors she has seen the more\par
quickly because you forbid her to speak of them?"\par
\par
"I don't know....  I haven't thought....  It's my mother I bother most\par
about....  You see, Roddy's Battery--Roddy's my brother--has gone with\par
the Expedition.  If Brenda talks rawhead and bloody-bones--but I'll take\par
care she don't, the little fool!"\par
\par
The eyes of both women followed the funny little figure. Lynette said as\par
it was absorbed in a crowd of laughing friends:\par
\par
"Would you prefer that we finished our talk here?"  She glanced at the\par
settee in a glass-screened angle near the fireplace, and Rhona assented\par
with evident relief.  Her Chiefs of the W.S.S.S., she explained, were\par
anxious that Mrs. Saxham should consent to speak at the Royal Hall Mass\par
Meeting of Protest Against the Delay of Parliament in passing the Woman\par
Suffrage Bill.  The Meeting was fixed for the middle of October.  Mrs.\par
Saxham's sympathy with the Movement was to be gathered from her\par
writings.  A personal expression would be valued by the W.S.S.S.\par
\par
"I am in sympathy to the extent of joining in any form of protest or any\par
description of organised Demonstration that is not characterised by\par
violence," said Lynette.  "To brawl at public meetings"--Rhona wondered\par
whether she had heard of her own baulked attempt to heckle the Bishops\par
at the Guildhall Banquet?--"to assault public personages and damage\par
private or public property is not the method by which the Franchise will\par
be gained.  To make war upon men is not the way, I think, to win their\par
suffrages for women. But I will gladly speak at the Meeting, please be\par
kind enough to tell the Chiefs."\par
\par
"It's awfully sporting of you--when you've been in such trouble.  It\par
must have been quite too awful," bungled Rhona, "about your boy!"\par
\par
"About my boy! ..."  Lynette caught her breath and nipped her lower lip\par
between her teeth to keep back the cry that else must have escaped her.\par
"You are kind.... You will be infinitely kinder if you say no more!"\par
\par
"I beg your pardon.  I'm frightfully clumsy!" apologised Rhona.\par
"Roddy--my brother who's at the Front--once told me that I had the tact\par
of a steam-cultivator and the discretion of a runaway motor-bus."  She\par
added: "I'm afraid you think I was rough on Brenda.  But the Mater's\par
heart-trouble keeps us all on tenterhooks, and for her sake--no matter\par
what horrors are hinted or whispered--nothing shall make me\par
believe--anything but the Best, until the Worst is brought to my door!\par
You understand, don't you? ... What's that?  Young Brenda----"\par
\par
A gust of laughter drew the eyes of both women to the Green Hackle, who,\par
surrounded by an appreciative circle, including Margot and Trixie\par
Wastwood, Cynthia Charterhouse, Doda and Sissi, was performing the\par
maddest _pas seul_ that ever held the floor.  One huge golosh flew off,\par
shaving a gilt-and-crystal electrolier as she finished with a daring\par
high kick, and dropped down breathless and panting between Margot and\par
Cynthia Charterhouse.\par
\par
"You crazy child!" cooed Mrs. Charterhouse, patting one of the pink\par
hands.\par
\par
"I feel crazy!" gurgled Brenda, while Doda picked up her battered\par
Trouville hat and Sissi retrieved hairpins scattered over the Club\par
carpet.  "Oh, my stars!  You don't know, you'll none of you ever guess\par
what it is to me to find you all so gay!"  She bounced on the springy\par
seat until her red locks tossed like the mane of a Shetland pony.  "Now\par
I really can believe--really!--that the whole thing's been a bad dream!\par
Like you get when Sisters have been too busy to boil the potatoes soft,\par
or take the cores out of the stewed apples."  She turned her head and\par
the sparkling mask of tiny beads broke out again over her flushed face.\par
"Who are those _Soeurs de Charite_?" she asked, for the circle of\par
elderly Members had melted away and the two Religious were now going,\par
taking with them the Belgian mother and her children, to whom--of course\par
at the Club's expense--they were to afford a temporary home.  "What are\par
they here for?  Why, that's the woman who came with us on the boat from\par
Ostend! Ah, my God!--it's all true!  I can't tell lies any more!  Do you\par
hear, Rhona?" and the bizarre little figure leaped up and stood before\par
them, defiant and panting.  "Not even for you and Mother!"  The voice\par
broke in a wail.  "Oh! how can you bear to see everyone so gay when the\par
Guards and Gunners have been killed at Mons?  Seven thousand lying dead,\par
the French Commandant told us.  Thousands taken prisoners--and we sit\par
laughing here----"\par
\par
Lynette Saxham caught the little body as it doubled on itself and\par
dropped like a shot rabbit.  She carried it to one of the settees, and\par
knelt by it, loosening the clothes, working with swift and motherly\par
hands.\par
\par
The piano-organ had come back, or another like it,--and was jolting out\par
the popular pseudo-pathetic strains of "Good-bye, Little Girl,\par
Good-bye!"  The swing-doors had thudded behind the nuns and their\par
charges.  Lady Wathe was just saying to Lady Eliason:\par
\par
"Then you, dear, will personally apply to the Foreign Office and the\par
Home Office and the Belgian Ambassador and the County Council.  Pray\par
count on me for _all_ the rest! Sir Solomon is a Tower of Strength!  You\par
agree with me, don't you, Sir Thomas?  Mercy on us!  _What_ a commotion!\par
Who has had a telegram from the Front?  Who says the Guards and Gunners\par
have been annihilated?  Who says the British Expedition has been\par
overwhelmed by numbers and forced to Retreat?  Will nobody stop that\par
horrible organ? Will nobody answer me?"\par
\par
\par
It was the tragic crowning of that day of trivial happenings that the\par
Iron Curtain that had baffled us so persistently should rise to the tune\par
of a music-hall ballad at the touch of a schoolgirl's hand.  Long before\par
the huge funeral broadsheets broke out in the gutters of Fleet Street,\par
the Strand, Pall Mall, and Piccadilly, screaming of the RETIREMENT OF\par
THE FRENCH FORCES FROM NAMUR AND CHARLEROI, DISASTER TO THE BRITISH\par
EXPEDITIONARY ARMY, DECIMATION OF FAMOUS REGIMENTS, AND THE RETREAT FROM\par
MONS, the Tidal Wave of Mourning that was to sweep the United Kingdom\par
from end to end had crashed down upon the Club.\par
\par
\par
Ah! how one had underrated them, those dead men who, living, had seemed\par
to hold themselves so lightly.  Who, submitting to be outclassed in\par
Sport even while holding it the thing best worth living for, had\par
smilingly accepted those hateful records of 1912-1913.\par
\par
Theirs is a glorious record now.  Above the huge Roll that is wreathed\par
with bloodstained laurels, droop the Flags of the Allied Nations, their\par
heavy folds all gemmed with bitter tears.  Each nightfall finds the\par
endless Roll grown longer. Each day-dawn sees the Hope of noble houses,\par
the pride and stay of homes gentle and simple swallowed up in the abyss\par
that is never glutted!  How long, O Lord? we cry, yet comes no nearer\par
the End for which the smallest children pray.\par
\par
And the women....  In the Book of the Prophet Ezekiel we read of a\par
valley of dry bones over which the Spirit of the Creator breathed.  When\par
that Wind from Heaven stirred them, the dead white bones put on Life and\par
rose up. A change as miraculous has been wrought in Woman since the\par
Black Deluge left a deposit of new-made widows and mourning mothers,\par
red-eyed sisters and silent wan-faced sweethearts, sitting about the\par
little tables where the empty places showed as awful gaps.\par
\par
The bereaved did not shed many tears.  Their grief was too deep to be\par
emotional, their newly-awakened spirit too lofty for complaint.  Their\par
pride in their dead men was their upholding.  Their bleeding hearts they\par
only showed to GOD.  Before then, He was for many of us non-existent:\par
for many more a remote, passively observant Personality but tepidly\par
interested in the affairs of the human race. Would these have learned to\par
know Him, think you, if there had been no War?\par
\par
And those whom every newspaper unfolded, every knock at the door might\par
smite with dire intelligence, right bravely they bore themselves through\par
that fortnight-long, hideous pipe-dream of the Long Retreat South.  For\par
many of these the torture of suspense was to give place to cruel\par
certainty, after that unforgettable Sunday of the Sixth September, when\par
at a distance of twelve kilometres from Paris the retirement of the\par
Allied Armies suddenly changed to an Advance, and the columns of German\par
Guard Uhlans in hot pursuit of the British Force, were routed by\par
Generals Gough and Chetwode with our 3rd and 5th Cavalry Brigades.  For\par
many, many others, the strain has never since slackened. They lie o'\par
nights as they lay through those nights of September, 1914, and feel the\par
bed shaking, and the floors and walls vibrating, as the outer rings of\par
vast concussions spread to them through the troubled ocean of\par
atmosphere.  And in the mornings they will tell you calmly:\par
\par
"Oh, yes.  _He_ is alive, but where he is there is terrible fighting.  I\par
heard the guns." ...\par
\par
No arguments of people whose sons or husbands are not with the Army in\par
Belgium, or France, Italy, or Palestine, will convince them that they do\par
not hear the guns.  Or that, borne upon the waves of a subtler medium\par
than air are not conveyed to them finer, more mysterious vibrations.\par
\par
Thoughts that meet thoughts.  Mental appeals--demands--entreaties....\par
The hands of their souls, reaching out through the dark hours, clasp\par
those of other souls in greetings and farewells.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                              *CHAPTER LX*\par
\par
                               *KULTUR!*\par
\par
\par
The Belgian village-town had been so sorely knocked about that the names\par
of its faubourgs, boulevards, and thoroughfares were obliterated.\par
Hence, one is fain to substitute others, such as the Street Where The\par
Naked Body Of The Little Girl Hung Up On Hooks In the Butcher's Window,\par
the Passage Of The Three Dead British Soldiers With Slit Noses And\par
Pounded Feet,--The Square Of The Forty Blindfolded Civilian Corpses, and\par
the Place Of The Church Of The Cure They Crucified For Warning The\par
British By Ringing The Bells.  Of this sacred edifice--Romanesque and\par
dating from the tenth century--little remained beyond the crypt and the\par
stump of the tower.  Some calcined and twisted bones, a scorched rag of\par
a cassock, represented M. le Cure, that faithful shepherd of souls.  Of\par
M. le Cure's flock, not one remained to tell the story of the tragic\par
episode that had reared the grim pile of blackening corpses in the\par
Market Square, and added seven hundred homeless refugees to the rivers\par
of human wretchedness ceaselessly rolling South.\par
\par
In the bright sunshine of the fine October morning that had followed a\par
night of rain and thunder, the grimly-altered shadows of shell-torn\par
buildings lay black on the ripped-up pavements and shrapnel-pocked\par
walls.  A sandy-white cat lapped gratefully at a puddle, a dishevelled\par
fowl pecked between the cobblestones, a pigeon or two preened on the\par
broken ridge-tiles.  To the eye of a skilled observer hovering hawk-like\par
in the hot blue heavens, raking the streets through high-powered Zeiss\par
binoculars, nothing human remained alive in this Aceldama.  Yet when the\par
two-seated bomb-carrying Taube with the big man and the small boy in it\par
had banked and climbed, and hummed away Southwards on its aerial mission\par
of ruin and destruction, one British officer, sorely wounded, lay in\par
what had been the ground-floor living-room of a well-to-do baker's shop.\par
\par
\par
A Captain of a Guards infantry battalion belonging to a Brigade of the\par
First Division of the First Army Corps. Marching, counter-marching,\par
digging, and fighting rearguard actions had kept the Brigade's hands\par
full during those blazing days and drenching nights of August and\par
September, whilst the battered Divisions that had borne the brunt of the\par
huge German offensive, reduced to one-twentieth of their effective, had\par
hurried Southwards, leaving a trail of blood.\par
\par
"Those other beggars have had all the luck!" the Brigade had growled\par
when it had any time for growling.  But it had won shining honours at\par
the Marne, and had been heavily engaged at the Aisne, losing many of its\par
men and officers. In the Aisne battle, particularly, the man we are\par
concerned with had won special mention in Dispatches for a deed of great\par
gallantry.  Three days previously, an order from General Headquarters\par
had moved his battalion on the little village town.\par
\par
\par
Their R.F.A. Battery had been posted a quarter-mile distant, commanding\par
the north-east and east where the Germans were known to be.\par
Machine-guns were placed at the principal road-ends debouching on the\par
west where the Germans might be: the main streets had been barricaded\par
with transport-waggons and motor-lorries, all the Maxims left had been\par
hidden behind the sand-bagged windows of a factory--a gaunt, brick\par
sky-scraper, long a thorn to the beauty-loving eye of M. le Cure--the\par
walls of houses ending streets leading to the country had been loopholed\par
for musketry, and a howitzer from the battery and a machine-gun had been\par
spared to protect the bridge south of the town, a little place resting\par
in the elbow of a small babbling river. Watches and patrols had been set\par
and pickets placed, and then these war-worn Britons had dispersed into\par
billets, or gone into barracks, too weary to eat, craving only for\par
sleep.... That big mound of blackened ruins near the railway station,\par
left intact for strategic purposes by the enemy, now stood for the\par
barracks--just as that calcined heap of masonry, and twisted iron\par
girders at the town's north angle now represented the hospital.  Both\par
had blazed, two huge, unquenchable, incendiary-shell-kindled pyres, to\par
light the retreat of the battalion south.\par
\par
\par
Secure on those points of menace, north-east, east, and west, the\par
exhausted battalion had slept like dead men.  The townspeople, relieved\par
in mind by the presence of so many English soldiers, slept like\par
Flemings--very nearly the same thing.  The Burgomaster slept; M. le\par
Maire followed his example.  M. le Docteur and M. l'Avocat slumbered\par
profoundly too.  Only M. le Cure, being restless for some reason or\par
other, resolved to spend the night on the church-tower in the company of\par
his breviary, an electric reading-lamp, a bottle of strong coffee, and a\par
battered but excellent night-glass, the property of his late maternal\par
uncle, an Admiral of the French Navy.\par
\par
Four hours they had slept, when a furious clangour from the church bells\par
awakened the sleepers.  Shrill whistles screamed, bugles were sounded,\par
Staff officers and company commanders clattered out of their\par
quarters--the battalion jumped like one man to its feet.  Voices talked\par
over the wires of the field-telephones.  An artillery patrol-leader had\par
ridden into the advance of a column of heavy motor-lorries approaching\par
the bridge that crossed the river, carrying the highway that had brought\par
the battalion from the south.  Lorries heavy-laden with--French\par
infantry!--for an outpost's flashlight on the advance had revealed the\par
Allies' uniform.  Well, what of it!  French troops were in the east upon\par
the Yser.  But still the crazy church-bells jangled and clanged and\par
pealed, shrieking:\par
\par
"REVEILLEZ-VOUS, MESSIEURS LES ANGLAIS!  VOUS ETES SURPRIT, LES\par
ALLEMANDS SONT ICI!  REVEILLEZ-VOUS! AUX ARMES!  AUX ARMES!"\par
\par
\par
And another broad arrow of dazzling blue-white light showed\par
motor-lorries packed with spiked helmets and green-grey tunics, behind\par
the _kepis_ topping men in blue coats and red breeches.  The gunners of\par
the howitzer, spared for the point commanding the road south of the\par
bridge, were picked off by German sharpshooters before they could fire.\par
The officer with the machine-gun was bayoneted and the gun itself\par
seized.  Revolvers cracked and spat incessantly, bayonets plunged\par
through the darkness into grunting bodies.  Britons and Boches strove in\par
a melee of whirling rifle-butts and pounding fists.  And by the light of\par
star-shell, shrapnel, and machine-gun-fire from the other side of the\par
river began to play indiscriminately on the assailants and the assailed.\par
Under cover of this fire, the Germans would have rushed the bridge, but\par
for the Factory stuffed with machine-guns, pumping lead from its\par
windows, and the howitzer--Oh! bully for the howitzer! thought the\par
wounded man.\par
\par
His company had been entrenched as a reserve near the bridge in the\par
mouth of a faubourg running westwards. They had doubled out to support\par
the bridge-party in the moment of alarm.  He had been shot then in the\par
right arm and had gone on using his revolver with the left hand.  It was\par
not until some well-timed shrapnel from the R.F.A. battery north-east of\par
the town began to burst among the green-grey uniforms, and the Kaisermen\par
took to their motor-lorries and went off, carrying their wounded and\par
leaving many dead--that Franky had been sensible of any pain.\par
\par
"You've been pipped, old man," had said the commander of the\par
bridge-company, mopping a smudged and perspiring visage with a\par
handkerchief that shrieked for the wash.\par
\par
"By the Great Brass Hat! so I have, but I'd forgotten all about it,"\par
said Franky, surveying the carnage in the golden sunlight of the\par
newly-minted day.  "Look at these fellows in French uniforms.  It's an\par
insult to the Allies to bury 'em like that.  Couldn't we take off the\par
blue coats and red baggies before we stow 'em underground?  And the\par
prisoners.  What beauties!  Whining 'Kamerad!' to our chaps, and putting\par
their hands up for mercy.  Do they suppose----"\par
\par
The speaker ceased, for the brother-officer who had commanded the\par
bridge-company was absorbed in looking through his binoculars at a\par
silvery speck in the western heavens.  It grew into a British R.F.C.\par
scouting biplane, that came droning overhead at 4,000, circled, fired a\par
white rocket for attention, dived nearer, circled again, and dropped a\par
scrawled message in a leaded clip-bag.\par
\par
"_Enemy-column--infantry with motor-lorries and two guns crossing\par
river--bridge a mile to the West of you--hurrying hell-for-leather\par
North.  Dropped them two bombs.  Bigger column advancing from North with\par
more motor-lorries and howitzers.  Look out for squalls that direction.\par
Roads to South all clear._"\par
\par
\par
"Those crossing the bridge to west of us will be the gentlemen who came\par
round that way to leave their cards!" said the Lieutenant-Colonel\par
Commanding as the biplane sang itself away.  "Probably a column detached\par
for the surprise from the bigger force to the north.  Well, we seem to\par
have finished top-dog.  Let's hope they won't tackle us again until the\par
men have had their coffee.  'Phone the Brigadier at Zille!  And\par
'wireless' the news of the scrimmage to the Divisional Commander at Baix\par
and Marwics thirty miles south of us, and get a message through to Sir\par
Kenneth"--he named the General Officer Commanding the A.C. to which the\par
Brigade belonged.  "And give details to the G.H.Q. at St. O., don't\par
forget!  Not that we'll get much credit over this."  The Colonel\par
scowled, surveying from the sandbagged window of Headquarters, situate\par
in the Factory, the long lines of stretchers being trotted off by the\par
R.A.M.C. bearers to the town Hospital.  He rubbed his finger under the\par
bristles of his close-clipped moustache with a rasping sound that\par
conveyed his irritation as he went on: "That's the worst of these rotten\par
little Advance-guard actions!  They're expensive, infernally expensive.\par
The casualties are heavy and the credit _nil_."\par
\par
"Possibly, sir, but at any rate we've wiped out a lot of these Boche\par
beggars," said the Battery Commander, optimistically.  "Halloa!  Bird\par
over!  And it's a Boche plane!"\par
\par
A two-seated Taube, shining silver in the morning sunshine, had come out\par
of the golden mists to northward, rolling up the landscape under its\par
steel belly with wonderful steady swiftness.  At some 3,000 above the\par
town, it hovered, making a queer buzzing noise.\par
\par
"I've heard that song before," said the Adjutant, his eyes glued to his\par
binoculars.  "You remember, sir, at Fegny?"\par
\par
"The spotter our fellows christened the Buzzard.  At his old\par
smoke-signalling tactics."  The Colonel snatched the Field-telephone,\par
spoke, and from a gaping skylight at the top of the tall, square,\par
many-windowed Factory an extravagantly-tilted Maxim began to pump lead\par
skywards in a glittering fan-shaped stream.  "Queer effect, uncommonly!\par
Looks as if it were raining upside down....  Gad!--I believe that hit\par
him!" he added, as a small dark object fell from the Hunnish monoplane.\par
But it was only the inevitable miniature parachute with the smoke-rocket\par
attached to it belching gouts of black vapour.  The Buzzard ceased\par
buzzing, banked, and climbed gracefully out of view.\par
\par
And then, with a leaping of green-white tongues of flame away in the\par
north, beyond a long sunlit stretch of level country fringed with\par
poplars and streaked with canals, and patched with brown cornfields and\par
golden-tinted woods and apple-laden orchards, and dotted with little\par
towns and villages, the heavy German field-guns and 11.2-inch Krupp\par
howitzers began to shower shrapnel and big steel shells of High\par
Explosive upon the devoted little town.\par
\par
The Kaisermen had got the range from their spotter. Half of the single\par
Field battery of 18-pounder quick-firers were put out of action in the\par
twinkling of an eye.  The little town became a storm-centre, canopied by\par
soot-black smoke, stabbed by the fierce blue glares of the shell-bursts.\par
The houses were toppling.  The ruins were blazing.  The gasometer near\par
the station was hit and blew up with a fearful explosion.  The streets\par
were full of shrieking, stampeding, dying townspeople and children.\par
"Save us!  Take us with you!" they screamed to the Englishmen.  For the\par
Divisional Commander at Baix and Marwics had telegraphed "Retire," and\par
the battalion was preparing to evacuate the town.\par
\par
A great shell wrecked the Factory, killed the Adjutant and many of the\par
machine-gunners, and slightly wounded the C.O.  The Romanesque\par
church-tower, whose bells had shrieked alarm in the little hours of the\par
morning, rocked, staggered, and collapsed over its famous chime.\par
\par
Again, men had melted as you laid your hands on them, blown into crimson\par
rags as their mouths opened shouting to you.  It had been Hell, Franky\par
remembered, sheer, absolute, unvarnished Hell.  The Battalion\par
Surgeon-Major had been dressing his wounded arm in the open street when\par
the Death-blizzard had broken upon them.  A lump of shrapnel hit Franky\par
in the ribs on the right side and some R.A.M.C. bearers carried him,\par
vomiting blood, into the baker's shop. Possibly they were killed--for a\par
shell hit and burst, and wrecked the house in the instant of their\par
leaving it--and they never came back again.  Their charge, in his\par
helplessness, had escaped death by a narrow shave.  The plank flooring\par
of the upper room, dropping from the broken joist at the fireplace end,\par
had formed a penthouse over him--lying on the blood-soaked stretcher on\par
the tiled flooring--shielding him from the avalanche of household\par
furniture, glass and crockery, descending from overhead.\par
\par
Thus he had lain, partially unconscious, when what was left of the\par
battalion marched out of the town.  Most of the population followed on\par
the blistered heels of the British soldiers, helping to carry the\par
stretchers of the wounded and crippled men who under that blizzard of\par
fiery Death had been got out of the burning Hospital.  Not all had been\par
got out.  Franky, lying bloody and smothered with plaster, and helpless\par
under the penthouse of planking that had saved him, had heard the\par
screams of these--such pitiful, heart-rending screams.\par
\par
Then the bombardment had stopped, and the mere relief from that\par
intolerable torture of outrageous sound was Heaven.  The screams from\par
the burning Hospital had ceased, but when the earth had shaken with the\par
approach of a great host, and German cavalry in green-grey uniforms with\par
covered helmets had ridden through the ravaged streets, and the\par
tottering walls had trembled at the passage of colossal motor-tractors\par
dragging 11.2-inch Krupps and carrying huge loads of German gunners,\par
engineers, and infantry--and German voices had shouted harshly up and\par
down the streets--and German heads were thrust from open windows--and\par
the work of Pillage, so dear to the German heart, was being carried out\par
with German thoroughness--the screaming had begun again.--Cries of women\par
and children, shouts of men; pleas, expostulations, prayers for mercy in\par
French or Flemish, brutal laughter, German oaths, threats, and orders;\par
subsequently, to the accompaniment of "_Deutschland, Deutschland, ueber\par
Alles_"--the popping of corks and the breaking of glasses--Hochs for\par
Kaiser and Kronprinz, fierce disputes over the divison of booty, more\par
shrieks of women and girls....  To the funeral adagio of picks and\par
mattocks upon the cobblestones of the Market Square.  A volley then, and\par
shots and more shots.... Subsequently Private of Infantry, Max\par
Schlutter, made these scrawled entries in his note-book; testifying to\par
the Sadism prevailing among the troops of the Attila of To-day:\par
\par
"_October --th, 1914.  Great day of loot and plunder! We shelled the\par
cowardly English--a whole Army Corps with a brigade of heavy\par
Artillery--out of the village of H----.  The Hospital, Barracks, Church,\par
and many houses destroyed by our guns.  The Mayor, the Burgomaster, and\par
the Registrar shot for harbouring our enemies.  The priest tied up to\par
his church-door, tortured, and then burnt, for ringing the bells to warn\par
the English of our approach.  Lieutenant Rossberg had a little girl\par
butchered like a pigling, and pounded the feet of some lame English\par
soldiers we found hiding, to teach the swine how to dance.  They too\par
were shot.  Decidedly the Lieutenant is a funny fellow.  All the people\par
who had not run away brought out of their houses and shot.  They filled\par
the air with their lamentations.  After a grand gorge and a big swill,\par
we now all drunk and slept on the pavements by the light of a\par
magnificent silvery moon.  Burned more houses, and continued the march\par
next day with a hellishly bad head._"\par
\par
\par
"How long before they find me out?" Franky had wondered.  But the\par
plaster-whitened brown boots sticking stiffly out under the penthouse of\par
broken flooring must have looked as though they clothed the rigid feet\par
of a dead man. "Presently they will come!" he had promised himself.  But\par
though they had sacked the baker's shop and visited the other rooms in\par
the dwelling, no one had entered the ravaged little parlour, split open\par
from floor to ceiling by the upburst of the High Explosive, and offering\par
its ravaged, worthless interior to the scrutiny of every passing eye.\par
\par
Worn and spent with fierce exertion, hard fighting, and loss of blood,\par
delirious with the rising fever of his wounds, he was conscious in\par
whiffs and snatches.  The conscious intervals made fiery streaks across\par
broad belts of murky shadow, a No Man's Land wherein Franky wandered,\par
meeting things both beautiful and hideous, knowing nothing real except\par
thirst, racking cramps, and stabbing pain.\par
\par
The second day passed.  At sun-high a distant fury of guns broke out.\par
Through the terrible drum-fire of Prussian Artillery he fancied he could\par
hear the British field-guns, hammering out Death in return for Death.\par
Suffering agonies for lack of water, he sustained life with scraps of\par
chocolate broken from a half-cake carried in a breast-pocket. To move\par
one hand and carry it to his mouth was possible at cost of ugly pain.\par
Night fell, a night that was rainy, and windy, full of cool drippings\par
that wet Franky's clothes without visiting his baked lips, and still the\par
cannonade went on ceaselessly--so that the crazy walls that sheltered\par
him shuddered and the earth vibrated, and the eeriness was made more\par
eerie with the sliding of tiles from broken rafters, and the creaking\par
and banging of broken doors, slammed by ghostly, invisible hands.  Pale\par
splashes of light,--reflected stabs of fire from the muzzles of those\par
unsleeping guns in the south and west, made the darkness yet more\par
dreary. Rats scrambled and squeaked, close to him in the obscurity,\par
evoking horrible suggestions of being gnawed and bitten as one lay\par
helpless there....  He gritted his teeth to keep back the cry that\par
nearly broke from him as one rodent crossed him, its hooked claws\par
rattling against his straps and buttons, its cold hairless tail sliding\par
snakily over his hand. He fancied that he saw its eyes shining in the\par
darkness--he was certain that it had moved and lopped round behind\par
him--he felt its whiskered snout cautiously approaching the throbbing\par
artery beneath his ear....  Then his nerve left him, and he croaked out\par
feebly, though it seemed to him that he shouted:\par
\par
"S'cat, you brute!  Get, you beggar!  Halloa; Halloa! _Belges au\par
secours_!  _Ici un Anglais, grievement blesse_!  Is anybody there?"\par
\par
But there came no answer save the muffled thunder of guns in the\par
distance, the crackle of fire in houses that were burning, the gurgling\par
of a broken water-main, and the distressed miaowing of a cat.  It came\par
nearer.  There was a rustling sound, and the light descent of a furry\par
body on padded feet; Pussy had jumped in where the window had been,\par
alighting not far from Franky.  He could see a pair of green eyes\par
lamping in the darkness, and called, seductively:\par
\par
"Pussy, pussy!  Come here, old girl!"\par
\par
The purr came near.  Franky, with infinite torment reached out a hand,\par
felt and stroked a warm, furry body.  He said, cautiously feeling for\par
the appreciative, sensitive places at the nape of a cat's neck, and\par
under the jaws:\par
\par
"Good old girl.  Don't know what they call cats in Flemish, but Pussy\par
seems to be good enough for you.  Stop and scare the rats away, give 'em\par
fits, eh, Pussy?  You're agreeable?  Good egg!  Oh--I say!"\par
\par
For Pussy had walked, loudly purring, on to the chest that heaved so\par
painfully, and proceeded to knead the surface scientifically,\par
preparatory to curling down.  Franky set his teeth, and bore the ordeal.\par
Thus they kept company until morning, when Pussy, who proved to be a\par
lean white Tom with patches of sandy tortoiseshell on flanks and\par
shoulders, withdrew by the fanged opening where the window had been.  A\par
moment later Franky heard his late companion lapping noisily from a\par
street-puddle and knew envy, in the anguish of his own unrelieved\par
thirst.\par
\par
He wandered then for a space of hours or instants, in the days of his\par
own lost childhood.  He was in the night-nursery at Whins, suffering\par
from some feverish ill.  He felt the prickling as of innumerable ants\par
running up his limbs and the sweat upon his forehead, and called\par
meaningly to Nurse for drink.  But it was his mother in her\par
dinner-dress, with shining jewels crowning her dark hair, and wreathing\par
her neck and starring her bosom, who came to the bedside and leaned over\par
him, put the rumpled hair from his hot forehead, and held to his lips\par
the cup of milk.  Then a droning sound made the room vibrate, and he was\par
back with his company in the hastily-dug trench across the mouth of the\par
west-running thoroughfare, and church-bells were clanging and the\par
telephone-buzzer was calling for the reserve to double out and reinforce\par
the men in the trench enfilading the bridge....\par
\par
\par
Then he was awake and the sun was high.  Those guns in the west were\par
silent now, though from the south and south-east came heavy thuds and\par
long vibrations.  Through the rents in the flooring above him by which\par
the rain had dripped upon him in the night, he was looking at the blue\par
sky.  A big white bird hovered there.  Not a bird--a Taube.  _The_\par
Taube, and he had not dreamed the buzzing after all.\par
\par
Oh, but it was queer to lie there under the keen scrutiny of that eye in\par
the heavens!  It made the prickly ants swarm up Franky's thighs and\par
sides until the sensation grew unbearable.  Hate, fierce hate of the\par
murderous, beautiful thing droning up there in the azure sky above its\par
curious misty circle made him see everything red, made him want to yell\par
and shriek.  For Margot was in danger, somehow--somewhere--while one lay\par
helpless as a log....\par
\par
"Steady, old child!" whispered Franky to himself, warningly. "You're\par
going off your chump.  Hold still!"\par
\par
And he held still.  The Buzzard ceased to buzz, and floated on, droning.\par
He fancied that he felt its shadow darken and pass over him, moving from\par
his head to his feet. The noise of the tractor stopped.  Reflected in\par
the area of a skewed wall-mirror he saw the machine volplane down, and\par
alight without a falter in the Market Place--before the smoking ruins of\par
the Town Hall.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXI*\par
\par
                            *LYNETTE DREAMS*\par
\par
\par
Upon that same night in October nearly five weeks following the breaking\par
of the Woe Wave, Lynette Saxham had a strange dream.\par
\par
It seemed to her that she saw piled up in one colossal heap the riches\par
of all the world, the world we know and the world we have forgotten; the\par
treasures of all ages piled up higher than Kilimanjaro, or Aconcagua, or\par
the cloud-mantled peak of Mount Everest.  To her feet as she stood\par
spell-bound amongst the foothills, rolled jewelled crowns, and huge\par
barbaric torques and diadems of rough gold, precious cups, vases, and\par
chargers; outpoured treasures of precious stones and wrought gems of\par
inconceivable beauty and vileness, wondrous fabrics, marvels of\par
sculpture, weapons, armour and coins of age beyond the ages--rude discs\par
of tarnished gold, stamped with the effigies of forgotten kings.\par
Orders, decorations, the paraphernalia of Pomp, the stage-properties of\par
Power, the symbols of every religion, save One, were mingled in the\par
stupendous pile, and a terrible Voice cried:\par
\par
"Gone is the age of pride in possession!  Chattels and fardels are no\par
more!  The days have spilt like pearls from a broken necklace!  Time has\par
eaten the years as the moth a garment of wool!  Foredone, foregone,\par
finished!  Who now will gather riches from the Dustheap of the World?"\par
And as new avalanches of treasure rolled downwards to the reverberation\par
of that thunderous shout, a Hand of Titanic proportions hurled down upon\par
the heap a war-chariot of beaten gold, with great scythed wheels, and\par
jewelled harness; and that vision changed, and the dreamer was drowning,\par
deep down in clear green seas, under the rushing keel of a huge barbaric\par
War-galley that was all of gold, arabesqued and bossed with jewels, and\par
coral, and pearl.\par
\par
And the sense of suffocation passed, and a wonderful cool peace flowed\par
in upon Lynette.  She seemed to be led by a beloved hand that had been\par
dust for years, into a bare walled place through which a thin breeze\par
piped shrilly. Someone was there, doing some manual labour.  He turned,\par
and with a shock of unutterable rapture Lynette was looking in the face\par
of her lost boy.\par
\par
Bawne had grown thin and seemed taller.  His temples had hollowed, his\par
plume of tawny-gold hair hung unkempt over his wide white forehead.  But\par
his blue eyes were as sweet as ever.  She had never realised how like\par
they were to Saxham's in shape and colour, and in expression, until now.\par
He thrust his lower jaw out and knit his brows slightly, as though her\par
face were fading from his vision, and he wished to fix in mind the\par
memory of its well-loved features:\par
\par
"Stay, Mother!  Oh!  Mother, don't leave me!" he cried, and stretched\par
out his hands to her, and she awakened, weeping for sorrow and joy.\par
\par
It was broad day.  Her husband was not there.  She rose and bathed in\par
the cold water she loved, and dressed in the simple Quaker-like grey\par
that set off her fairness, and went out to Mass....  The day's\par
Preparation was taken from the noble prayer of St. Ambrose, Bishop and\par
Confessor:\par
\par
"_And now before Thee, O Lord, I lay the troubles of the poor; the\par
sorrows of nations, and the groanings of those in bondage; the\par
desolation of the fatherless; the weariness of wayfarers; the\par
helplessness of the sick; the struggles of the dying; the failing\par
strength of the aged; the ambitious hopes of young men; the high desires\par
of maidens; and the widow's tears.  For Thou, O Lord, art full of pity\par
for all men: nor hatest aught of that which Thou hast made._"\par
\par
He even loved von Herrnung, who had taken her boy, and kept him in\par
slavery, and robbed the joyous light from his sweet eyes, and set\par
amongst his red-brown hair one sinister streak of white.  She saw the\par
bleached forelock dangling before her eyes when she shut them and tried\par
to pray for the Enemy:\par
\par
"Oh God! forgive that evil man, and turn his heart towards mercy and\par
pitifulness, and give me back Thy precious gift, for the love of Her who\par
is Thy Mother!"\par
\par
It was yet early when she returned to Harley Street and passed at once\par
into the Doctor's consulting-room.  There, where her lips had first\par
kissed him, sleeping in his chair, she found Saxham sitting at his\par
table, with his sorrow of heart revealed in the stoop of his great\par
shoulders, and his greying head resting upon his hands.  Not a sound did\par
he utter, but the attitude was more than eloquent:\par
\par
"Oh my son!" it said.  "Oh me!--my little son!"\par
\par
"Owen!" she said, coming to his side and touching him. Then, as he\par
started and looked up: "Bawne is alive!" she cried.  "I have seen him in\par
a dream, and he has spoken to me.  He was in a bare high place with\par
corrugated iron walls, whitened.  It made me think of the Hospital at\par
Gueldersdorp in the old days, and of a hangar....  His clothes were\par
soiled and torn, and his hands were blackened.  One other thing I\par
saw--but I will not wring your heart by telling you....  It is enough\par
that I have seen our boy.... alive.  Oh! thank God!"  She stopped, and\par
the rose of joy faded from her cheeks, and only the tears were left\par
there. Her eyes widened with a terrible doubt.  "You _knew_! ... It is\par
in your face!  You had heard ... something, and you did not tell me!"\par
\par
"I had not the courage.  Despise me, for I deserve it!  I had news of\par
Bawne at the end of August.  He is with that man who stole him--"  He\par
clenched the hand that rested on the table until the knuckles showed\par
white upon it and his hair was wet upon his forehead and his mouth was\par
twisted awry.  "Taken with him on errands of aerial\par
reconnaissance--carried helplessly into battle as a Teddy bear or a\par
golliwog might be fastened on the front of a racing-plane. And, when I\par
remember that I bade him risk that journey--"  Saxham broke off, and\par
turned his face away.  She came nearer to him and said:\par
\par
"But he is alive!--alive, even though he be in danger. My dream was sent\par
to tell me so.  Did not the Mother come to me in my sleep and lead me to\par
him?  Just as when she came and sent me here to you.  Now I will atone\par
for these days of selfish grieving.  Only give me work to do!"\par
\par
"Have you not enough upon your hands already?  Too much, I have\par
sometimes feared."\par
\par
"Only the Hospice and the Schools," she answered eagerly, "and the\par
Training Houses for the elder women. And, thanks to you, these are\par
excellently staffed.  If I were to die it would make little difference.\par
Things would go on just the same."\par
\par
"Would they?"\par
\par
She stooped, lifted his hand to her lips and kissed it.  He looked at\par
her keenly as she did so, and the over-bright flush upon the thin cheeks\par
and the hollows about the beautiful eyes, like the burning touch of her\par
hand and of her lips, told him their tale of woe.\par
\par
"Not for you.  Nothing would ever be the same for you or for Bawne.\par
Therefore--give me more work."\par
\par
"There is plenty of work, unhappily," he said, "because of this calamity\par
that has fallen upon the nation.  We have notice that a hundred wounded\par
men from the Front--many of them cot-cases--will arrive at SS.\par
Stanislaus and Theresa's at three this afternoon."\par
\par
"I shall be there!"\par
\par
"I am not going to try to dissuade you.  I will not keep back what God\par
has given to me from those who have given so much for England.  There is\par
another quarter where you will be of use."  His eyes were on the\par
triptych frame before him.  "I speak of that little Lady\par
Norwater--Patrine's friend--I think you have not met?"\par
\par
"Oh, but I have.  We were made acquainted with each other some weeks ago\par
at the Club."  Her delicate face contracted.  "That day when the news\par
came about the British losses.  Just before that poor child Brenda\par
Helvellyn blurted out the dreadful truth.  Owen, it was tragic.  She had\par
known it from the beginning----"\par
\par
"And the sister forbade her to breathe a hint of it.  That is the\par
attitude of the fashionable Sadducean," said Saxham bitterly, "who not\par
only denies the Atonement and the Resurrection, but will not admit of\par
Death."\par
\par
"But," she asked him, "what of Lady Norwater? Patrine tells me she is\par
ill."\par
\par
"She is ill.  Lord Norwater--at first reported missing after an action\par
north of Ypres on the --th is now said to have been killed."\par
\par
Lynette was silent.  Her husband knew why her head was bent and her\par
white fingers sought a little Crucifix she wore.  She was praying for\par
the dead man.  Presently she said:\par
\par
"He was very brave, I believe?'\par
\par
"He had been recommended for the Victoria Cross for a special service of\par
great gallantry--rendered during the Battle of the Aisne.  He was a\par
brave and simple young man, and very lovable.  His wife received the\par
official intelligence of his death yesterday.  They 'phoned Patrine, as\par
you know, and sent for me later.  Lady Norwater is expecting her\par
confinement at the end of November--and they were alarmed for her."\par
\par
"Poor little soul!  Her baby will be a comfort to her!"\par
\par
Saxham remembered under what circumstances he had made the acquaintance\par
of Lady Norwater, and his look was rather grim.  In his mind's ear he\par
heard again the sweet little voice saying in its fashionable slang\par
jargon:\par
\par
"Oh no!  I rather cotton to kiddies.  It's the bother of having 'em\par
doesn't appeal.  It puts everything in the cart for the Autumn Season."\par
\par
Still, the recent remembrance of her piteousness softened the Doctor's\par
never very adamantine heart towards her, the humming-bird broken on the\par
wheel of implacable Fate.  Not unnatural, after all.  More of a woman\par
than one would have thought her.  How she had clasped her tiny hands\par
together and entreated him, when the worst was feared for her, to save,\par
to save her child.\par
\par
"Franky's child.  Perhaps--the boy he hoped for.  Oh! to have to say\par
_hoped_, hurts so dreadfully.  Yes, yes!  I will be brave and good and\par
quiet....  I will do everything that you say.  Ah, now I know why all\par
these days I have felt Franky near me, and seen his eyes looking at me\par
out of every stranger's face."\par
\par
Margot did not cry out in her pain and loneliness for her friend Patrine\par
to come to her, though she sent loving, grateful messages whenever Pat\par
called or 'phoned.  But she had said to Saxham, only that morning:\par
"Doctor, I met your wife at the Club not long ago.  She is more\par
beautiful, but so much sadder than the portrait you showed me.  Ah, yes!\par
I remember why.  When I am better, would she come and see me?  Perhaps\par
it is inconsiderate that I should ask.  But the world is so huge and\par
coarse and noisy and empty"--the little lip had quivered--"and there is\par
something in her face that is so sweet, I have been fancying that it\par
would"---she hesitated--"be good for me and for my baby if she would\par
sometimes visit me.  Do you think she would mind?"\par
\par
Saxham had answered:\par
\par
"I will ask her."  Now he gave the piteous message, and Lynette warmly\par
agreed:\par
\par
"Of course I will go.  Whenever you say I may!"\par
\par
"Not for some days.  She is to see no one yet, and your hands are full\par
with Madame van der Heuvel and Marienne and Simonne."  The Doctor\par
referred to an exiled Belgian lady and her young daughters, who had been\par
received at Harley Street as guests.  "And--there is the Hospital--and\par
to-night you have to address this Meeting of Suffragists at the Royal\par
Hall.  It is the only decision of yours, let me tell you," said Saxham,\par
"that I ever felt tempted to dispute.  My wife upon the same platform\par
with Mrs. Carrie Clash and Fanny Leaven!  A triple force of Metropolitan\par
Police on duty, and detectives at all the exits and amongst the\par
audience.  It's--"  Words failed Saxham.\par
\par
"It is unspeakably hateful in your eyes.  Dear Owen, I know it.  But I\par
should be hateful in my own sight if I were to break my word.  On the\par
day I first met you we spoke of these views of mine.  I hold them still.\par
Marriage has not altered them.  It is not in me," said Lynette, "to\par
change!"\par
\par
"You are the soul of faithfulness in all things!"\par
\par
"Then do not be grieved that I keep to my given promise. Those who have\par
honoured me by asking me to address them are aware that my convictions\par
are opposed to theirs at points.  But while I oppose I admire their\par
ruthless devotion and their magnificent, unswerving policy of\par
self-sacrifice----"\par
\par
"But these felonies," he protested, "these incendiary attacks upon\par
property----"\par
\par
"In nine cases out of ten, and I believe the authorities know it as well\par
as the W.S.S.S., such outrages have not been committed by Suffragists at\par
all."\par
\par
"By whom, then?"\par
\par
"Have we no enemies without our gates even now when we are at War?"\par
\par
"Germans...."  A light broke in upon Saxham. "It's not impossible.  As\par
for scattered literature being evidence--that can be bought anywhere.\par
But granted the blackest sheep of the W.S.S.S. to be proved--piebald,\par
that will not make me less anxious for you to-night."\par
\par
He touched a heavy plait of the red-brown hair with a tender hand and\par
said to her:\par
\par
"I grudge that the pearls of my wife's eloquence should be thrown before\par
Suffragists."\par
\par
"We disagree, dear love," she said to him, "but we do not love the less\par
for it.  When the Franchise is accorded to Women, should I vote for one\par
Party and you for another, will that matter a whit to you?"\par
\par
"Not a whit," he said, as he kissed her.  "You may give your vote to\par
whom you choose, so long as the voter remains mine.  Who was that?"\par
Saxham's quick ear had heard a footstep in the hall.  "Madame van der\par
Heuvel coming back from Mass?"\par
\par
"It is Patrine!"\par
\par
"Patrine off and away at this hour?"\par
\par
"I told her I would explain to you."\par
\par
"She has explained to you," said Saxham, "and that should be enough."\par
\par
"Dear Owen! ... I am sure she wished you to know of it....  She has gone\par
down to Seasheere, a little Naval Flying-station on the South-East\par
coast, to meet Alan Sherbrand on the home-flight from Somewhere in\par
France."\par
\par
"I see in to-day's _Wire_ that he has been gazetted Lieutenant," said\par
Saxham.  "One rather wonders, all things considered, that it has not\par
happened before."\par
\par
For not once nor twice in the past weeks the big smudgy contents-bills\par
hung upon railings and worn as a chest-protector by newspaper-vendors,\par
since paper became too scarce an article to line street-gutters with,\par
had trumpeted the name of Sherbrand; and the big black-capitalled\par
headings had set forth his deeds of daring.  Only to-day they had\par
announced:\par
\par
"SHERBRAND OF THE R.F.C. STRAFES ANOTHER HUN-BIRD. BAG BROUGHT UP TO\par
NINE, AND TWO ENEMY KITE-BALLOONS. POPULAR YOUNG AVIATOR NOW VISCOUNT\par
NORWATER, HEIR-PRESUMPTIVE TO BRITISH EARL."\par
\par
\par
"He may be sent back to the Front at any moment--it is natural that they\par
should wish to be together, don't you think?"  The speaker added, as\par
Saxham made no immediate rejoinder: "As they are engaged to be married,\par
and what is more, engaged with your consent."\par
\par
"She has told you so?"\par
\par
"No!"  A shadow of the old smile hovered upon the sensitive mouth.  "I\par
told her, and she could not deny it.... Oh, Owen!  Do you really believe\par
I have been blind all this time?"\par
\par
"I should have known that women have clairvoyance in these matters.  But\par
Patrine feared that you would think her unfeeling or inconsiderate----"\par
\par
"And why?  Because when God sent me a great grief He gave my poor girl a\par
great happiness?  The best earthly happiness, save one, that He holds in\par
His gift."\par
\par
"I thank Him that you still think so, after thirteen years of marriage!"\par
\par
"I shall always think so, Owen.  And it is a great thing that Patrine\par
has chosen so well.  He is true and brave, and loves my dear sincerely.\par
And her love is beautiful and disinterested.  There is no taint of\par
baseness in her----"\par
\par
"She has nothing of Mildred or of David, then," flashed through the\par
Doctor's mind.  Lynette went on:\par
\par
"No one will ever be able to charge her with venality or mercenariness.\par
The succession that they _will_ talk of in the newspapers was not\par
dreamed of when she and Alan fell in love."\par
\par
"The succession!  Ah, of course!" the Doctor said; "There is a possible\par
succession to a Viscounty now that Lord Norwater's death is proved fact,\par
but only in case Lady Norwater bears no male child.  But a title would\par
not spoil Sherbrand, and I agree with you that it has never influenced\par
Patrine."\par
\par
"How tired you look!" Lynette said, noting the look of heavy care and\par
the deep lines of weariness traced on the stern visage.\par
\par
"I have several critical private cases, and a long list of operations\par
for this morning at SS. Stanislaus and Theresa's. Now go and dress, my\par
sweet, for I have work to do."\par
\par
And Lynette went with a happier look than she had worn since the\par
crushing blow fell.  And Saxham shot the bolt of his consulting-room\par
door and went back to his chair at the big writing-table, and leaned his\par
head upon his hands.\par
\par
An Atlas burden of care cracked the sinews of the Doctor's huge\par
shoulders.  It had not occurred to Saxham when Patrine had gulped out\par
her pitiful story, and he had heartened her by bidding her forget, that\par
forgetfulness would speedily be accomplished at the cost of an honest\par
man.\par
\par
Now, what to do?  Must Sherbrand take the stranger's leavings or David's\par
girl be twice the loser by the stranger's lustful theft?  It was a\par
problem to thrash the brain to jelly of grey matter, thought the Dop\par
Doctor, drilling his fingertips into his aching temples--were there no\par
cause for anxiety elsewhere.\par
\par
Ah! how much more stuffed the pack that burdened the big shoulders.  The\par
boy had been taken and the mother would die of grief.  You could see her\par
withering like a white rose held near the blast of a smelting-furnace.\par
Yet there was nothing to do but look on and play the game.  A bitter\par
spasm gripped the man by the throat, and slow tears, wrung from the\par
depths of him by mortal anguish, splashed on the paper between his\par
elbows and raised great blisters there. Truly, when the spark of Hope\par
burns dimmest, when the grain of Faith is a thousand times smaller than\par
the mustard-seed--when God seems most far away, He is nearest.  We have\par
learned this with other truths, in the War.  Blood and tears mingle in\par
the collyrium with which our eyes have been bathed, that we might see.\par
\par
Saxham battled down his weakness, and rose up and went to duty.  None\par
might guess, looking at the Dop Doctor, that those hard, bright eyes had\par
wept an hour ago.  Later on, a moment serving, he went to the telephone.\par
\par
"Halloa!  Is this New Scotland Yard?  M.P.O.?  Halloa! ... I am Dr.\par
Saxham, speaking from SS. Stanislaus and Theresa's Hospital, N.W.  Can I\par
get word with Superintendent-on-the-Executive, Donald Kirwall?  Halloa!\par
... Thanks, I'll hold the line."\par
\par
He waited a minute, and the Superintendent answered:\par
\par
"Halloa!  Dr. Saxham?  Anything we can do for you, sir?"\par
\par
"Yes.  Put me on six good plain-clothes men at this Mass Meeting of\par
Suffragists at the Royal Hall to-night. Can you? ... Halloa! ... I could\par
do with eight or ten!"\par
\par
"Halloa! ... Well, sir, we'll do what we can.  We'll be pretty strong in\par
force there, as it happens, Marylebone and Holborn and St. James's\par
Divisions...."  Something like an official chuckle came over the line.\par
"Mrs. Petrell in the chair, and the Clash and Fanny Higgins.  We've\par
learned to look for trouble when they get up to speak. Halloa!  Beg\par
pardon!  I didn't quite hear! ..."\par
\par
Saxham had cursed the popular leaders.\par
\par
"Yes, I was aware they'd prevailed on Mrs. Saxham to address 'em....\par
Indeed, they're advertising her all over the shop....  Halloa? ...\par
Certainly we'll put you on the plain-clothes men you ask for.  But even\par
without Police to protect her, Mrs. Saxham don't run much risk. Halloa!\par
... Why! ... Oh! because an uncommon big percentage of the audience on\par
these packed nights are out-and-out loose women.  Soho and Leicester\par
Square, and all that lot....  Others come up from Poplar and Stepney and\par
Bethnal Green and Deptford to hear Fanny Higgins. Halloa?  Do they want\par
the Vote?  Well, naturally these gay women like the idea of being\par
Represented in Parliament. If respectable females are going to get good\par
of it, naturally the prostitutes want the Franchise.  They hold that\par
Woman Suffrage 'ud improve their conditions.  Halloa! ... You don't know\par
but what the gay women have as good a right to vote as the gay men who\par
employ 'em?  No more don't I! But whatever they are, they appreciate\par
those who spend their lives in trying to help the unfortunate.  And,\par
West or East-Enders--the most chronic cases among 'em wouldn't suffer a\par
finger to be laid on your wife.  All the same, I'll attend to your\par
instructions.  Doors at 7.  The men shall be there.  Don't worry\par
yourself!  Four ready back of the Platform and four more posted right\par
and left of the proscenium.  Don't mention it!  Very proud to....\par
Good-afternoon!"\par
\par
"Good-afternoon and thanks, Superintendent!"\par
\par
And Saxham rang off, more relieved in mind than he would have cared to\par
own.  Then the horn of a motor sounded below in the Hospital courtyard,\par
and another and another followed.  Tyres crackled on gravel.  The\par
running feet of men pattered on pavement.  The hall-porter whistled up\par
the speaking-tube into the Medical Officer's Room, and Saxham went down,\par
meeting the black-robed Mother Prioress and the Sister Superintendent on\par
their way to the great vestibule.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXII*\par
\par
                        *WOUNDED FROM THE FRONT*\par
\par
\par
The wide-leaved front doors stood open.  Doctors and surgeons,\par
theatre-assistants, students, white-habited Sisters,\par
blue-and-white-uniformed nurses and probationers, were swarming in the\par
great vestibule.  Already a double stream of canvas stretchers, laden\par
with still figures swathed in iodined gauze and cotton-wool padding,\par
were being carried up the wide steps, from the big grey-painted Red\par
Cross motor-ambulances, by R.A.M.C., and blue-uniformed bearers of St.\par
Theresa's Association, while omnibuses, private cars, taxis from Charing\par
Cross and Victoria were hauled up behind, waiting to disgorge their\par
loads.  And cheer upon cheer went up from the packed sidewalks and\par
roadway; handkerchiefs waved from the windows of the nearest houses, and\par
the passengers on the roofs of the omnibuses passing up and down\par
Wellington Road, Edgware Road, and Praed Street, stood up and craned\par
their necks in the fruitless endeavour to glimpse the reason of those\par
frantic cheers.\par
\par
For the first convoy of wounded from the Front had reached the Hospital.\par
These unwashed, begrimed, hairy brigands, these limping tramps in\par
tattered khaki, these bandaged cripples leading blind comrades, were our\par
Guards, our Gunners, our Highlanders, Kents, Middlesex men and Munsters,\par
our Rifles and Northamptons, our Welsh and Gloucesters, our Scots Greys\par
and Lancers, our immortals of those red-hot days of August, and their\par
compeers, the terrible fighters of the Marne and the Aisne....\par
\par
They were back, full of cross-nicked, nickel-coated Mauser bullets, bits\par
of shell and lumps of shrapnel, cheap jokes, music-hall choruses,\par
vermin, and spunk.  The reek of lysol and carbolic, the sickly whiff of\par
dysentery and the ghastly stench of gangrene, brought back to Saxham the\par
Hospital at Gueldersdorp, as he passed back and forth between the\par
stretchers, issuing swift orders, briefly wording directions,\par
marshalling his trained forces with the generalship that had\par
distinguished him of old.\par
\par
"Doctor!"\par
\par
"What is it, Ironside?"  Saxham turned to speak to the Resident Medical\par
Officer.  "You look off-colour, man!"\par
\par
"I feel off, sir.  They're so damned full of grit, and cheerful! Not\par
only the cases from the Base Hospitals, but those casualties they've\par
sent us direct from the trenches.... Two days in the train getting to\par
Calais--and Lord! the straw and filthiness in their wounds!  And we've\par
been told our next War'd be carried out on an absolutely Aseptic Basis,\par
and here we are back in 1900!"\par
\par
"Not quite," said Saxham.  "Wounds like these were never made by Boer\par
shrapnel.  Human bodies shattered beyond imagination by High Explosive,\par
rank among the triumphs of Modern Science.  After the Stone Age and the\par
Iron Age, the Golden Age and the Age of Shoddy has come the Age of\par
Militant Chemistry.  Martianism, in a word."\par
\par
"It's an ugly word....  Doctor, that man over there," the speaker\par
indicated a pair of hollow eyes staring hungrily over a huge\par
iodine-smeared gauze muffler, "wants to know if we can save his lower\par
jaw?  Not that there's much left of it.  His pal, who interprets for\par
him, says a wounded German officer shot him in the face with his\par
revolver, 'cos he went to give the blankety blank a drink out of his\par
water-bottle.  One of the Gunners--and not long married, according to\par
the pal."\par
\par
"All right, tell him!  Name him for one of my beds," Saxham said\par
brusquely, and nodded to the owner of the desperate eyes, saying, as\par
they flared back their gratitude: "Even if it had been 1821 in the\par
cattle-truck, we're in the Twentieth Century here.  Warn Burland," he\par
named the anaesthetist, "for duty at once.  Gaynor Gaynes and Frost to\par
be ready with the X Ray on Flat I.  Mr. Whitchett and Mr. Pridd to act\par
as Assistant Surgeons.  We'll take the worst cases straight away----"\par
\par
"But, my God, sir! most of these men are beyond Surgery," groaned\par
Ironside, cracking his finger-joints.  "Broken and mashed and rent as\par
they are, what they need is to be re-created! ... If Christ were to look\par
in here just now," the Medical Resident cried in his bitterness,\par
"there'd be plenty of work in His line.  New tissues to make, bony\par
structures to re-build.  Organs to replace where organs have been\par
destroyed.  He'd have done it by mixing earth with His saliva and\par
anointing.  We might as well spit on twenty per cent. of these\par
fellows--for all the good we can do!"\par
\par
"Give them liquid nourishment--brandy where necessary, and send those\par
I've tagged up to the theatre.  No waiting to wash--in their cases.  And\par
remember my Gunner gets the first look-in!"\par
\par
\par
Saxham turned and ran at speed, making for the nearest elevator, found\par
it just going up full of stretcher-cases lying close packed as sardines,\par
turned and shot up the stone staircase three steps at a time to the\par
first floor, glittering with white enamel, polished oak, brass fittings\par
and cleanliness, under the discreet radiance of shaded electric lights.\par
The centre space was occupied by the tribune engirdling the domed\par
Sanctuary of the Chapel.  Short corridors tastefully adorned with\par
red-enamelled buckets, blue glass bombs of chemical fire-extinguisher,\par
and snaky coils of brass-fitted hose, led to long wards running east,\par
west, north, and south.\par
\par
"Eh, Doctor!"\par
\par
A fair-faced, gentle-eyed Sister of Mercy, in the wide-winged starched\par
linen cap and guimpe, and white twill nursing-habit with the black\par
Cross, stood near the lift, talking to a tall, raw-boned, white-haired\par
Surgeon-General of the R.A.M.C.  She greeted Saxham's appearance with a\par
little womanly cry:\par
\par
"Eh, Doctor!  Never it rains buddit pours."  There was a hint of\par
Lancashire in her dialect.  "The R.A.M.C. have sent us ten more cases.\par
Dear, dear!--but we'll have our hands full."\par
\par
"Then you'll be happy, Sister-Superintendent.  I've never known you so\par
beamingly contented as when you were regularly run off your feet, and\par
hadn't a minute to say your Rosary.  Anything specially interesting, Sir\par
Duncan?"\par
\par
"Aweel!"  The broad Scots tongue of Taggart droned the bagpipe-note as\par
of old.  "Aweel!  There's an abdawminal or twa I'd like ye to throw your\par
'ee over--an' a G.P. that ye will find in your line.  Fracture o' the\par
lumbar vairtebra from shrapnel--received ten o'clock yesterday\par
morr'ning!--an' some cases o' shellitis, wi' intermittent accesses o'\par
raging mania an' intervals o' mild delusions--an' ane will gar you draw\par
on the Medical Officer's Emergency List o' Abbreviated Observations I\par
supplied ye wi' a guid few years agone."\par
\par
"I've not forgotten."\par
\par
"I'm no' dootin' but ye have found it unco' useful."  Taggart's frosty\par
eyelashes twinkled.  "It has saved my ain face from shame mair times\par
than I daur tell."  He quoted, relishingly: "M.B.A.--Might Be Anything!\par
G.O.K.--Guid Only Knows!  L.F.A.--Luik for Alcohol. A.D.T.--Any Damned\par
Thing!  'Toch, Sister, I beg your parr-don!  The word slipped oot--I\par
have nae other excuse! But my case o' shell-shock, Saxham.  What say ye\par
to an involuntary simuleetion o' _rigor mortis_?  A man sane an' sound\par
an' hale--clampit by his relentless imagination into the shape o' a\par
Polwheal Air-Course Finder, or a pair o' dividers.  Half open, ye ken.\par
Ye may stand him on the ground upo' his feet, an' his neb is pointing at\par
the daisies--or ye may lie him o' his back in bed--an' his taes are\par
tickling the stars.  Am thinking it long till I'm bringing ye thegither!\par
But ye are busied.  I'll no' keep ye the noo."\par
\par
Racing for the second lift, just emptied of its sorrowful burden, the\par
big shirt-sleeved Doctor checked in his stride and touched the handle of\par
a sliding door.  The door shot back noiselessly in its grooving.  Saxham\par
was in a cushioned tribune high above the level of the chapel Altar.\par
The scent of flowers and the perfume of incense hung like a benison on\par
the still air of the sacred place.\par
\par
In one of the carved stalls of the nave the figure of a priest in\par
cassock and biretta sat reading from a breviary. It was the Chaplain,\par
waiting in readiness to be called to administer Holy Unction and\par
Viaticum to some Catholic soul about to depart.  In the choir behind the\par
high Altar a slight girl, in the frilled cap and prim black gown of the\par
Novitiate, knelt on a rush-bottomed prie-dieu absorbed in meditation,\par
her black Rosary twisted round her clasped hands.  Prayers that are most\par
earnest are frequently incoherent.  Saxham formulated no petition as he\par
knelt there in the tribune, but the cry of his heart to the Divine\par
Hearer might have been construed into words like these:\par
\par
"_If Thou wert here in the visible Body as when of old Thou didst walk\par
on earth with Thy Disciples, Thou wouldst heal these broken sons of\par
Thine with Thy look.  Thy Touch, Thy Word! Yet art Thou here--for Thou\par
hast said it, ever present for Thy Faithful in Spirit, Flesh, and Blood.\par
Help O Helper!  Heal O Healer!  Lord Jesus, present in the Blessed\par
Sacrament of the Altar, give power and wisdom to Thy servant.  Aid me,\par
working in the dark by my little flame of hard-won knowledge, to\par
preserve life, Thou Giver of Life!  Amen._"\par
\par
\par
So having prayed, the Dop Doctor went up to the theatre and wrought\par
mightily, doing wonderful things in the way of patching and botching the\par
broken bodies of men.  Later, as he sat in the Harley Street dining-room\par
playing the courteous, attentive host to sad-eyed, wistful Madame van\par
der Heuvel and her two pretty daughters--for Lynette had dined earlier\par
on account of the Suffrage Meeting--he heard a latch-key in the\par
front-door and Patrine's well-known step in the hall.\par
\par
He excused himself, rose and went out, and spoke to his niece.  She made\par
a croaking sound in answer, as unlike the voice of Patrine as the\par
pinched and sunken face revealed by the hall electroliers, resembled the\par
face of dead David's handsome girl.  The mouth hung lax.  The cheeks had\par
fallen.  The eyes stared blank and tearless, from hollow caves under the\par
broad black eyebrows.  He said with a pricking of foreboding:\par
\par
"You have had a long day! ..."\par
\par
"Not long enough to tire me.  I am made of india-rubber, I think, and\par
steel."\par
\par
He considered her a moment with grave, keen eyes that had no gleam of\par
curiosity.\par
\par
"Sherbrand is well?  He returned from France in safety?"\par
\par
"He was quite in the pink when he arrived--and ditto when he left.  Not\par
that he had much time.  A wireless came, ordering him to replace an\par
aviator of the Royal Flying Corps, killed on observation-duty--or\par
whatever it is they call it--with our fellows on the new Front.  Rough\par
on him, but he took it smiling.  No, thanks!  I'm not keen on dinner....\par
You won't mind if I go to my room?"\par
\par
"One moment.  Have you had food to-day?" he asked her.\par
\par
"I forget....  Yes, of course!  There was luncheon at one o'clock.  The\par
people at the Air Station did us tremendously well."  Her mouth twisted.\par
"I think it better to tell you and Lynette that Alan Sherbrand and I\par
have said ta-ta!"  She tried to smile.  "I'm back on your hands like a\par
bad penny!"  Her eyes seemed all black between their narrowed lids.\par
\par
They were quite alone, no servant within hearing, and the dining-room\par
door was shut.  Came the Doctor's low-toned question:\par
\par
"Has any--third person made mischief between you two?"\par
\par
"No, nobody has blabbed to him about anything.  But--he's wise enough\par
now, as regards this child.  Particularly wide-O!"  The black,\par
glittering eyes looked dry and hard as enamel.  Her teeth again showed\par
in that mirthless grin. "I don't suppose he has the ghost of an illusion\par
left.... Women--most women would say I was a howling fool to make a\par
clean breast of it.  I never meant to--I can swear!--when first we got\par
engaged.  I used to call his goodness stodgy.  I think I despised him\par
for it in certain moods of mine.  You've never realised the kind of\par
beast I can be. But more and more, I got to respect him!  And\par
suddenly--I knew that if I married him under false colours--letting him\par
believe me to be what I amn't--even though he never found me\par
out--I'd--never have been able to shake hands with myself again!"\par
\par
She moved to the stairs, the sleeve of her coat brushing the Doctor's\par
great shoulder.\par
\par
"Don't you suppose God had it all his own way," she said in that odd,\par
strangled voice that wasn't like Patrine's. "There were minutes when the\par
World, and the Flesh, and the Devil were jolly well to the fore.  Alan\par
would marry me to-morrow if I used the power I _could_ use.  But I\par
won't! I won't!  It'd not be playing the decent, straight game. So I let\par
him call me heartless, and piffle like that, and then the game seemed\par
hardly worth playing.  I'd have thrown up my cards--only the Recall\par
came.  And we said good-bye, and I saw him fly away like a great white\par
bird, over the water.  And I'm so strong--so horribly strong--that I\par
stood it and didn't die....  Even if Alan's killed at the Front I shan't\par
die....  _Ah-h!_ ... You mustn't touch me!"  Her hands plucked\par
themselves violently from Saxham's that would have enfolded them.  "I\par
could stand anything better than pity.  Being pitied would kill\par
me--though I'm so awfully strong!"\par
\par
"Then trust us not to pity you--only to love you.  That I look upon you\par
as a daughter is no secret to you, I think?"\par
\par
"No, dear!"  She stroked his sleeve, not lifting her pitifully reddened\par
eyelids, and then he felt her start. "Uncle Owen!"  Her hand clenched\par
upon his arm, and her tear-blurred eyes sought his.  "I must tell\par
you....  He had news to give me to-day--of Bawne!"\par
\par
"Nothing worse, thank God!--than what I know already," Saxham commented\par
when she had told.  He stood in silence a moment, mastering himself, and\par
the electric hall-light showed in his harsh square visage the ravages\par
that grief had wrought.\par
\par
"How you have suffered!  If only I could do something to comfort you!"\par
she muttered.  "And Lynette.  Do you know--there are days"--a sob caught\par
her breath--"when I daren't even look at Lynette."\par
\par
"It is so with me!"  His voice was deep and quiet and sorrowful.  "Old\par
Webster probed deep with his Elizabethan goose-quill, when he wrote of\par
the\par
\par
    "Greyfe that wastyth a faire woman\par
    Even as wax doth waste yn flame."\par
\par
Pray for us both, my dear, and believe that you are a comfort to us."\par
\par
She said with a laugh that was half a sob: "I might have made a hole in\par
the water at Seasheere, or jumped out of the train on the way back, I\par
daresay, but for the thought of you both.  Or, if it wasn't that stopped\par
me, my joss was on the job."\par
\par
"I had rather say your Guardian Angel."\par
\par
"Do you think any self-respecting Guardian Angel could possibly bother\par
about a regular bad egg like me?"\par
\par
"Mine did--when my wife married me and I was a peculiarly bad egg."\par
\par
"You, you dear!"  She suddenly caught him round the neck and hugged him\par
strenuously.  "Do you think I don't know--haven't always known how my\par
father and mother treated you!"\par
\par
"Time heals wounds of that kind," said Saxham, as they turned together\par
from the foot of the staircase, and, still keeping a protecting arm\par
about David's daughter, he reached his hat and stick from the\par
hall-stand, "though you may doubt the statement now."\par
\par
"I can't.  I'd only have to look at mother to----"\par
\par
"To remember that she is your mother!"\par
\par
His tone was final in its closure of the subject.  But in his heart he\par
thanked frail Mildred once again for her ancient treachery, as he went\par
out to the waiting car, and sped through London's murky streets to the\par
North-West suburb where stands the Hospital.\par
\par
Patrine went upstairs, holding by the balusters and feeling chilly and\par
old.  In the prettily furnished sitting-room, communicating with her\par
chintzy bedroom, were her letters, and a deep cardboard box stood upon a\par
table.  It had been sent on to Harley Street from the Club, and bore the\par
address of a Regent Street florist, whose showy establishment boasted a\par
German name.\par
\par
The fragrance of roses with a musky after-tang in their sweetness\par
permeated the atmosphere.  There were no roses amongst the flowers on\par
the chimney-shelf and cabinets.  It occurred to Patrine that there must\par
be roses in the box.\par
\par
Her head was throbbing and her eyes smarted.  She threw off her hat and\par
coat, pitched them down upon the chintzy sofa, switched off the electric\par
lights, let up the blinds, pulled a chair close to the open window, and\par
sat down, resting her folded arms on the clean, dustless sill.\par
\par
Sitting there, staring out into the semi-obscurity of Harley Street,\par
with the late cabs and motors sliding past and the distant roar of\par
Oxford Street in her ears, she asked herself:\par
\par
"Have I behaved like an honourable woman or--a blithering idiot?  That's\par
what I want to know?"\par
\par
She waited.  Not one pat on the back was vouchsafed by an approving\par
Conscience.  The indicator of the dial slowly travelled in the direction\par
of the blitherer.  Patrine shut her hot, dry eyes, and began to conjure\par
up the day that had gone over.  Its sweetness was rendered infinitely\par
sweeter, its bitterness a hundredfold more poignant by the knowledge\par
that it was the last, the very last.\par
\par
If she lived to be old, old, old, she knew she would never live to\par
forget Seasheere.  The smell of the hot thyme and sun-baked grasses of\par
the cliffs, the rhythmic _frrsh!_ of the salt waves upon its shingle,\par
the shrill piping of its gulls, and pale blue of its skies would never\par
fade, never cease, never be silent, never alter....  For on Seasheere\par
cliffs her Wind of Joy had blown for the last time.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER LXIII*\par
\par
                         *BAWNE FINDS A FRIEND*\par
\par
\par
The machine that could hover like Sherbrand's "Bird of War" had come\par
down in the Market Place.  A big grey two-seater monoplane, with the\par
rounded cleft bird-tail and wings of the German Taube type.  You could\par
see a number on its side and three big black Maltese crosses, and the\par
profile heads of pilot and passenger showing up in strong relief against\par
the blackened ruins of the Town Hall.\par
\par
A bomb hung in its wire cage-holder on the visible side of the fuselage.\par
It struck Franky that the airman must be profoundly sure of himself, or\par
culpably reckless to have come down before getting rid of the thing.  A\par
swivel-mounting like a barless capital A supported a machine-gun above\par
the radius of the tractor, and well within reach of the pilot's hand.\par
\par
The pilot got down.  He was tall and big, with a red moustache; a man\par
whose natural height and bulk were so augmented by the padded helmet\par
topped with the now-raised goggles, the pneumatic jacket girt in by a\par
broad band of webbing, supporting a brace of large revolvers, and the\par
heavy bandolier he carried, that the figure of his companion, scrambling\par
after him, seemed that of a mere dwarf.\par
\par
The man who saw, _per_ medium of the rakishly-angled looking-glass yet\par
hanging on the wall of the wrecked parlour, conceived a horror of the\par
Troll-like creature in its big helmet, and the full-sized oilskins that\par
hung in folds about its diminutive body, the skirts reaching nearly to\par
the ground.  When the two passed beyond the mirror's area of reflection,\par
the doubt whether they might not have discovered his whereabouts and be\par
stealthily creeping up from the rear to attack him, made him shudder,\par
and brought the perspiration starting in the hollows of his sunken\par
temples and cheeks.\par
\par
Minutes passed.  He waited with his eyes upon the mirror.  Someone was\par
approaching from the direction of the Market Place, keeping well under\par
the broken walls of the houses fringing the narrow _trottoir_.  Where an\par
avalanche of tiles and brickwork had fallen, he must perforce skirt the\par
obstacle, and thus for an instant be reflected in the glass. Meanwhile\par
the sound of nearing footsteps--sometimes muffled in thick dust, or\par
clicking over cobblestones, or tripping and stumbling among bricks and\par
rubble--grew more distinct.  The red-moustached giant could not walk so\par
lightly.  It must be the Troll--could be no one but the Troll!  The\par
suspense of waiting had tensed into unbearable agony when the sound of a\par
voice crying broke out in the deathly silence of the place.\par
\par
"Oh, oh!"  Like a woman or a child's uncontrolled wailing.  "Oh--the\par
poor men!  Oh, the poor women and the li-ittle ch-ildren!  Oh!" and _da\par
capo_, working up to a crescendo of agony, and dying away in\par
heartbreaking sobs. It was so strange--not that there should be weeping\par
in these razed and ravaged streets, but that the voice that wept should\par
be a voice of England--that it begot in the helpless man who heard\par
doubts of his own sanity, and a reckless desire to dissipate such\par
doubts.  He heard himself call out: "Who is crying there?"\par
\par
And a treble voice piped back, and stumbling over the moraine of\par
_debris_ tongueing from the avalanche of broken tiles and masonry,\par
came--not the Troll-dwarf in his huge disguising helmet and outsized\par
pneumatic jacket--but an urchin of twelve or thirteen--in the familiar\par
dress of a Boy Scout--minus the smasher hat and staff.\par
\par
"Me for the gay old life!" meditated Franky.  "Thought I was getting\par
groggy in the upper works--and now I know it!  A British Boy Scout in\par
his little khaki shirt, with a row of gadgets on his left sleeve, and\par
ribbon tags to his little garters, all on his little lone in the middle\par
of this--Gehenna!"  He spoke to the fever that galloped through his\par
veins in the tone of a patron presiding at the test-display of a Cinema\par
Film Company: "Pretty good, but you can do better.  Roll along with a\par
troop of blue-eyed Girl Guides, old Touch-and-Go!"\par
\par
\par
The Scout's figure vanished out of the glass.  There was a sound of\par
scratching and scrambling.  The broken floor jarred to the impact of a\par
light body, and a boyish treble called:\par
\par
"Is--is anybody here?  Anybody--English?"\par
\par
The voice quavered on the last word.  Franky knew that this was\par
delirium.  He grinned under his four-days' beard, and the grime and soot\par
and plaster that masked him, and answered in a series of Bantu clicks,\par
so leather-dry was his tongue:\par
\par
"Me as per descrip: to fol: Young British sossifer of good fam: irrepro:\par
ref: and tophole edu: badly dam: by Hun shell!  Greatly in need of the\par
com: of a ref: Chris: ho: Mus: in the eve: and intell: conver: greatly\par
appre:"  He shut his stiff eyelids and opened them again, but the\par
imaginary Scout had not gone.\par
\par
"You're dreadfully--hurt.  Couldn't I do--something?" the treble voice\par
piped.  Its owner was now squatting on his heels in the shade of\par
Franky's penthouse of planks.  The knuckles he rested on the floor were\par
cracked and grimy, and his deeply-freckled, fair-complexioncd face was\par
lined, and anxious and thin.  His blue eyes were swollen with crying,\par
though his sensitive lips wore a wistful, crooked smile. "You _are_\par
real?" he asked wistfully, and Franky answered, huskily:\par
\par
"Rather!  In fact, I'm a lot more real than you.  Who are you, since\par
we're gettin' personal?"  He repeated slowly after the boy:\par
\par
"'Bawne Mildare Saxham, Scout No. 22.  Fox Patrol, 331st London W.'\par
Seems good enough."  He shut his hot eyes wearily.  "But if you're\par
solid--you'd get me a drink!"\par
\par
There was a little stir.  The Scout had gone.  Franky knew it without\par
opening his eyes, yielding to the deadly sinking faintness engendered by\par
the effort of speech.  A mountainous weight crushed his chest, and his\par
legs were cold and heavy as ingots of pig-iron.  It occurred to him that\par
at this rate the--wind-up--could not be far off.  And a great horror\par
fell upon him like a pall, and cold sweat broke forth and streamed upon\par
his haggard face and broken body. Death for one who so loved Life and\par
the pleasant things of a commonplace existence....  A cricket-match, a\par
day with the hounds, a funny _revue_, a game of polo, a break at\par
billiards, a clinking run with the car, a fine cigar.  Mess in camp\par
after the hard day's march, long, cool drinks with bits of ice tinkling\par
in the tumbler.  That new, fierce pleasure tasted in his first\par
experience of real fighting....  And oh! how much sweeter than these the\par
scent of Margot's hair, the light of Margot's eyes, the clasp of her\par
arms about his neck, the hope of fatherhood, never now to be\par
realised....\par
\par
"My little chap!" he muttered, and his heart wept, but no tears came to\par
his arid eyes.  Then something cold touched his mouth.  The rim of a cup\par
with water in it. "Thank you!" he said, after a gulping draught, opening\par
his eyes with the sense of reviving coolness stealing through his\par
parched vitals.  "That's--absolutely IT!"\par
\par
The boyish treble said with a quaver in it:\par
\par
"If I set this can beside you--I got the water from the pipe that is\par
running--and the broken cup near it, could you manage to dip it in?  Are\par
you able to move this hand?"\par
\par
"First class!" whispered Franky, lifting the member a very little way\par
and dropping it again.  "The--the other arm came in for it when the\par
shrapper hit me in the ribs.... Halloa!  Chocolate," for a bit touched\par
his lips and was gently pushed between them.  "That reminds me. I've an\par
iron ration somewhere about me.  No--they took my pack off when I got\par
crumped up."  It had seemed only--decent to Franky in those days of\par
endless foot-slogging, to carry a pack and a Lee-Enfield and fare no\par
better than his men.  "Frightfully obliged.  But I won't take this."\par
This being another scrap of chocolate.  "Is thy servant a Boche that he\par
should stodge kid's grub?"\par
\par
"You're English!"  The blue eyes were full of hungry worship.  "Man\par
alive!" quavered the boyish treble, "you don't know how I've wanted to\par
hear an English voice again. Tell me"--he panted and was pale under his\par
multitudinous freckles, and the beating of the childish heart shook the\par
thin young frame--"the Germans haven't beaten England--and sunk our\par
Navy, and wiped out our Army--and killed the King, and Lord Roberts, and\par
the Chief Scout, and Lord Kitchener, and--and my father and mother and\par
everyone?"\par
\par
"No!" said the wounded man, and his faint whisper was as convincing as\par
though the negative had been shouted with the full strength of vigorous\par
lungs.  "Is that the kind of lie they've been pitching you?  Perhaps it\par
does 'em good to believe it!  Let 'em, if they like.  It'll never be\par
true!"\par
\par
"I knew it couldn't!"  The clear treble had lost its quaver.  "And yet\par
there were times when I was funky.  _He_ seemed so awfully sure at--the\par
beginning!  And--the Enemy never stops--rubbing it in!"\par
\par
"Who is the Enemy?"\par
\par
"His name is von Herrnung.  And--and I must go now, for--for your sake."\par
The eyes flickered, and their pupils dilated to wide circles of\par
frightened blackness.  "He might wake up--and come--and find you.  And\par
if he found you----"\par
\par
When the arteries have been almost depleted by haemorrhage, and the\par
strength of the body has ebbed to vanishing point, the brain is\par
sometimes dazzlingly clear.  Thus, though the faint whisper barely\par
reached the ear of the other, the haggard eyes looking out of the\par
begrimed and unshaven face of the man lying in the blood-soaked\par
stretcher were alert and observant.  He said reassuringly:\par
\par
"He won't come just yet.  Tell me more about him, and all about\par
yourself."\par
\par
How strangely lined and pinched and puckered was the young face with its\par
clear red-and-white sprinkled over with brown freckles.  Fine dust of\par
dew-beads started upon forehead and temples and cheeks, the half-opened\par
mouth twitched nervously, though he thrust out his under-jaw and knitted\par
his reddish brows in a gallant effort of self-control.\par
\par
"His name is von Herrnung.  He is the German Field Flight officer who\par
took me away from England.  I wrote down the date in my Scout's\par
pocket-book so that I mightn't forget.  It was July 18th.  He was trying\par
Mr. Sherbrand's hawk-hoverer at Hendon.  He asked me to go up with\par
him----"\par
\par
"Great Snipe!" panted Franky weakly.  "Are YOU the boy who dropped the\par
wallet with the Clanronald Papers and the scratched message in the North\par
Sea?"\par
\par
The blue eyes understood.  "There was a wallet," said their owner.  "I\par
don't know what was inside, of course. But he----"\par
\par
A spasm of trembling went through the slender body.  He bent his head,\par
and blinked his eyes, and the muscles of his throat and jaw worked as\par
though he fought down an hysterical access of tears.  And a broad shaft\par
of golden light, falling on the young bare head, showed how the shining\par
red-brown hair had been roughly clipped in ridges, leaving a\par
forehead-tuft oddly streaked with white.  Amongst the crowds of homeless\par
exiles endlessly streaming along the roads of this scourged and tortured\par
country, or crouching amongst the wreckage of its ruined villages and\par
battered towns, heads even younger than this boy's had displayed the\par
tragic sign.\par
\par
"Poor kid!" Franky muttered, recognising it as the result of\par
overwhelming physical shock and unnatural mental strain.  "He knew what\par
was inside? ..."\par
\par
"I don't think so!  If he had known when the submarine picked us up in\par
the North Sea--I think he would have killed me!  He would like to kill\par
me now, he says"--the apple in the boy's throat jerked--"because through\par
me he has been _degradiren_--reduced from Captain to Supernumerary\par
Officer Pilot--and has had his Third Class of the Red Eagle taken away!\par
That was done at the big Wireless Station--Nordeich, they called it----"\par
\par
"Nordeich....  Of course ... in German West Friesland. Thrash along--I'm\par
following you.  Did they Court Martial the Flying Man?" Franky\par
whispered; and Bawne whispered back:\par
\par
"The Emperor punished him! ..."\par
\par
"The Emperor, did you say? ..."\par
\par
"Yes.  He came to Nordeich--in--I've forgotten what they call it when\par
great people want to move about without red carpets and lots of fuss."\par
\par
"Incognito."\par
\par
"Incognito.  He'd broken off his yachting-trip in Norwegian waters--and\par
landed at Kiel only that day.  I heard men whisper it....  He was\par
dressed in the field-grey, like his War Minister von Falkenhayn---and\par
his generals of the Imperial Staff--and all the other officers and men.\par
But he 'stripped off the War-harness,'--that's what they called\par
it!--before he got into the Potsdam train."\par
\par
"Go on! ... What did he look like? ... They say he has changed a lot o'\par
late."\par
\par
"I couldn't tell.  I'd only seen photos that made him look younger and\par
hid his short arm.  But even if he hadn't sat while the others\par
stood--and worn the Iron Cross, Grand Class--and the Black Eagle with\par
diamond swords and a Crown Imperial--I'd have known it was the Emperor,\par
by his eyes."\par
\par
"By his eyes, you say! ..."\par
\par
The boy's heart throbbed visibly, the breath came in short puffs through\par
his nostrils, and his lips were twisted awry as he smiled.  The smile\par
stiffened out as he nodded. "By his awful eyes! ... When they looked at\par
you they made you feel tired, and empty, and--queer.  But when they got\par
angry--you were reminded of--of a tiger lurking to spring out of a cave\par
of ice!"\par
\par
"Ah!  So he got angry, did he?"\par
\par
Bawne nodded.\par
\par
"When I wouldn't answer the questions he asked me--he talked\par
English--about how the brown satchel had come unstrapped and tumbled\par
into the sea.  And he said to an officer: 'Show him your whip!'--and he\par
did--and it was short-stocked and covered with leather, like a\par
dog-whip--with three thongs strung with little balls of lead.  Man\par
alive!  you ought to see my back.  Though they only hit me once!"  He\par
winced, and flushed, and paled.  "I was a coward to squeal--though when\par
they asked: 'Will you tell now?' I _did_ say: 'Not to stop you from\par
killing me!'"\par
\par
"Good egg you!  Great Snipe!--if I'd been there.  With a Service\par
Revolver--!  Never mind....  Go on!"\par
\par
"I forget....  Oh!--they pulled on my shirt and gave me some strong\par
stuff to drink.  Corn brandy, I think it was--and then He got up and\par
came round the table and began to talk to me.  He said I must not be an\par
obstinate boy, for in another few days there would be War.  Our pitiful\par
little Army'd be wiped out and our Fleet sent to the bottom of the sea.\par
The British Isles would be _Deutsch Brittanien_--and English people who\par
would not swear to be good Brito-German subjects of their new Emperor\par
and Overlord would be instantly put to death.  But if I told up about\par
the brown satchel I would be permitted to live, and possibly my parents\par
also.  If I said No!--nothing would be left but to call back the officer\par
with the whip."\par
\par
"Coaxin', wasn't he?  And what did you tell him?"\par
\par
"I said: 'You've only said you're going to conquer England, Sir.  You\par
haven't done it yet!"\par
\par
It was not merely the treble voice of a courageous child answering.  It\par
was the utterance of a race untamable and indomitable.  Franky could\par
hear the metal balls on the whip clink one against another as the loaded\par
thongs were shaken out....  He whispered with dry lips:\par
\par
"Then----?"\par
\par
"Then I don't quite know.  I was sick and sleepy, and the blood was\par
running down my back under my shirt.  If they had killed me I wouldn't\par
have cared much.  Perhaps he saw that, for he called up von Herrnung.\par
He was not to be dismissed from the Field Flying Service--because of the\par
War that was coming!--but he was to forfeit his Order of the Red Eagle\par
and rank as a Supernumerary Officer Pilot. Man alive!--you should have\par
seen how that big man squirmed and crawled and blubbered."  The young\par
lips curled, and the jaw thrust out contemptuously.  "'Thanks!\par
Gratitude! ... My blood to prove devotion! ... All I ask--the service of\par
danger--the reconnaissance under enemy fire!'  And the Emperor----"\par
\par
"Kicked him, I hope!"\par
\par
"No, he said: 'Supernumerary Officer Pilot von Herrnung you will now to\par
your Flying Headquarters return.  Let it be your task to win back at the\par
cost of a thousand lives--if you had them--the lost esteem of your\par
Emperor.  Take this boy with you.  Make of him a decent German.  It is\par
"up to you," as the English say.'  And then the Wireless went '_S'ss!\par
Crackle!  Pzz!_' and the telephone-bell said '_Pr'rr!_' and the room was\par
cleared--they said because of a Call from the Winter Palace at\par
Petersburg."\par
\par
"And where did they take you after you left the Wireless Station?  Go\par
on--I'd like to hear you tell!"\par
\par
The boy glanced round uneasily and then mastered his apprehensions.  The\par
grimed hands went to his stocking-top and pulled out a squat little\par
book.  The coloured presentment of a Boy Scout adorned its soiled\par
leather cover, and the thumbed leaves of the diary within were pencilled\par
from end to end.  The Odyssey of a Saxham Pup, one might have called the\par
story whispered into the ear of the wounded man by the boy squatting at\par
his side.\par
\par
\par
One had been taken by train to Bremen and thence to a place called\par
Taubefeld, in West Hessen.  Flight Station XXX was here on a vast\par
stretch of heath.  There were rows of great hangars, and a vast army of\par
motor floats and lorries, upon which machines, hangars,\par
telegraph-installations, workshops, mess-houses, and quarters for\par
officers and mechanics, could be placed when the mobilisation-order came\par
and transported by road or rail.\par
\par
One had fallen sick at Taubefeld--the effects of that North Sea ducking.\par
One had waked up with a skin-cropped head wondering where one was.  A\par
woman who helped in the cookhouse had given one broth and gruel and the\par
medicine prescribed by the doctor.  One had crawled off one's straw\par
palliasse weakly and shakily, and so won forth into a new, unfriendly\par
world.\par
\par
One's parole had been taken--and one was thenceforth free to move about\par
and see things--when one was not wanted to help oil or clean wires or\par
sweep up the hangars. There was grub enough: bacon-soup, potato-salad,\par
and sausage, queer but not uneatable.  Nobody was really brutal as long\par
as one didn't speak English, or even German with a British accent, too\par
much at one time.  _Keine Unterhaltung da!_ ("No conversation there!")\par
some officer or N.C. would yell at one, and the rebuke was generally\par
accompanied by the swishing cut of a cane.\par
\par
Consequently the Saxham Pup had bent himself to acquire German, as\par
spoken by Germans, and schooled himself to employ his eyes and ears\par
while maintaining economy in the use of his tongue.  He had found out\par
his whereabouts from an envelope he had picked up, and other things from\par
listening to the officers' conversation, and the talk of the mechanics\par
in the big hangars.\par
\par
War was the thing everybody talked about.  There was going to be bloody\par
War in a twinkling.  The German Navy was going to smash the British Navy\par
into matchwood, everybody was quite sure.  The German Army was going to\par
walk over the miserable little British Army--and then would be expiated\par
the sins of the British Government and the diabolical plottings of Sir\par
Edward Grey.  Throat-cuttings, shootings, and hangings were mentioned in\par
connection with the above, and other personages whom British Boy Scouts\par
hold in reverence.  But one had had to bear it and hold one's tongue,\par
and keep smiling.  That was the method of the Chief who had said to one:\par
"Quit yourself like a man."\par
\par
Brave advice, possible to follow by day when alien eyes were watching.\par
One could choke down weak tears and the ache of the lonely heart that\par
cried for Home and the dear familiar faces, when the Birds of War were\par
roaring and whirring up the night-field or down out of the sky.  But at\par
night, in the grim, unfriendly dark of the sleeping-cupboard, without\par
other witness than the thin, sore-eyed white kitten that shared one's\par
meals and slept beside one on the hard straw mattress under the\par
foul-smelling grey blanket,--things were harder.  One had got through,\par
after a fashion, by "rotting" and making believe.  One did not set down\par
in the Scout's Note-Book or tell the wounded friend on the stretcher how\par
one had kissed the back of one's own hand, and whispered, "Good-night,\par
Mother!" and touched one's cheek with the tips of two fingers and\par
whispered, "Good-night, and God keep and bless you, my darling boy!"\par
\par
Amongst other things of interest picked up by day, one found out that\par
Supernumerary Officer Pilot von Herrnung was cold-shouldered by the\par
officers of the Flight Squadron, which he had captained before his fall.\par
No longer top-dog, he was made to pay for his domineering and\par
swaggering. He resented this, by swaggering more.  The men talked of\par
this in the hangars, as they tuned-up wires or cleaned the engines.  Von\par
Herrnung was _Ungluecklich_.  Nobody liked him.  The Squadron would not\par
stand him long.  Hadn't he insulted the Herr Squadron-Captain Pilot who\par
had succeeded and challenged him, and got his cartel back again?\par
\par
"Colossal insolence!" he had fumed.  "A challenge from a person of my\par
rank confers an honour on him who receives it.  Not a man among you\par
stands upon my level.  Deny it if you can!"\par
\par
"True, very true!" the Lieutenant-Observer who had brought back the\par
challenge was reputed to have retorted. "Not a man among us has ever\par
been degraded, therefore, Herr Supernumerary Officer, you stand alone.\par
And we of the Field Flight do not regard your presence among us as a\par
distinction.  You may possibly conceive that?"\par
\par
He had said it just as though he had had a stink under his nose,\par
according to the narrator.  And he had dropped von Herrnung's letter on\par
von Herrnung's table, wiped his fingers ostentatiously upon his\par
handkerchief, given the ghost of a salute--wheeled and gone out.  After\par
that the whilom favourite of Fortune had turned sullen and solitary, and\par
developed such desperate recklessness that men funked to fly with him.\par
Subsequently the Bird of War hovering-gear having, after due examination\par
by Government experts, been relinquished to its captor, he had had the\par
mechanism adapted to a Taube monoplane, and thenceforward made Her\par
Dearest the sharer of his flights.\par
\par
You are to suppose Bawne snatching fearful joys in the realisation of\par
cherished ambitions.  Loathing and fearing, he yet admired the big\par
red-haired man, so superbly brave in the air that seemed his natural\par
element.  Equally the man, detesting the child, grudgingly acknowledged\par
his courage and obedience.  No queerer companionship may have been than\par
this between the Enemy, and the son of Saxham and Lynette.\par
\par
When the Flight Squadron shifted to Aix-la-Chapelle, a huge seething\par
caldron of military preparation,--"Does England declare War against us?"\par
people asked the Flight officers.  "It is probable," they answered,\par
"_Gott sei danke!_"  Upon the Third of August, starting at night, Bawne\par
had made a long flight with the Enemy.  At midnight the Taube had\par
hovered over a great, beautiful city twinkling with millions of electric\par
lights.\par
\par
"That is Brussels you see down there," shouted von Herrnung through the\par
voice-tube.  "The city is _en fete_ because of the agreement arrived at\par
between the Emperor and the Belgian King.  That means England has lost a\par
friend, and made another enemy.  Do you understand, little English\par
swine?"\par
\par
And von Herrnung, who had brought a Wireless outfit, had busied himself\par
in picking up messages from a low-powered installation at the German\par
Embassy and transmitting them to Somebody, high in authority, who waited\par
at Berlin.  He had grown more and more peeved as he went about his\par
business, Bawne could not tell why but Franky understood quite well.\par
\par
Belgium had not been content that the Red Cock should perch upon her\par
British neighbour's roof, while her own house remained unscathed by\par
fire.  Franky smiled, knowing this to have been the burden of the song\par
sung by the tuned sparks.  Broad day had found the big city humming with\par
mobilisation, enormous placards printed in the National Colours, with:\par
"BELGIUM REFUSES!" and "ROI, LOI, LIBERTE," posted in all the public\par
places--and a park of heavy Artillery concentrated round the Etterbeek\par
Barracks, as von Herrnung had flown back to Aix-la-Chapelle on the\par
morning of August 4th.\par
\par
Bawne went on:\par
\par
The Flight Squadron had been attached to a Field Artillery Division of\par
the Second Corps, under a General named von Kluck.  A huge man he, with\par
a square head and a big mouth full of broken teeth.  Bawne had\par
previously seen him at the Wireless Station where he had been taken on\par
landing from the submarine.\par
\par
They had seen little of the aviation-base, from the beginning of\par
hostilities.  The Powers that Were had promptly taken von Herrnung at\par
his word.  For him were the long-distance flights, the delicate and\par
risky missions, the dangerous reconnaissances over the Allied batteries.\par
Driven by that gadfly of desire to regain the lost distinctions, he\par
seemed to have lost all sense of fear and to bear a charmed life.\par
\par
Thus, while von Kluck's Advance was opposed at Mons by the stubborn\par
thrust of the British Forces, the Buzzard earned his nickname by his\par
tireless quest for Death.  It eased his grudge against mankind to hunt\par
men--and he hunted; hovering and observing, wirelessing and spotting,\par
utilising one machine for many purposes,--in those days when War Flying\par
was as yet in its infancy--sniped at by the sharpshooters of four out of\par
seven British Divisions--often waging, with automatic pistol and Krupp\par
machine-gun, fierce battles with other Paladins of the Wing, on the\par
boundless lists of air.\par
\par
How many times the boy's heart had cried for pity when some brave bird\par
crippled by a spout of lead, or fired by an explosive bullet, had gone\par
spinning earthwards, showing the Three Crosses of the Union Jack, or the\par
blue-white-red circles of France's tricolour--or the red-black-yellow of\par
the Belgian Flag upon its upper and under-wings as it fell.\par
\par
They had bombed Paris two days before, and bombed Ypres that morning,\par
starting from a Flying Base near the city of Bruges.  Bawne knew the\par
place was Ypres because it was marked in red on the roller-map.  The\par
British General Headquarters were supposed to be there.  All the bombs\par
had been used except two, and the Enemy must have forgotten to get rid\par
of these before he landed.  He was generally careful, but not so when he\par
drank much.  And lately he had drunk a good deal, there was so much wine\par
in the country.  He had come down and gone into the restaurant to quest\par
for food and champagne.  If he found, he would eat hugely and drink\par
heavily, and then sleep himself sober.  He always slept after a bout\par
before taking to the air again.  But sometimes when he had mixed drinks\par
he got savage instead of sleepy, and then----\par
\par
"Do you mean that he thrashes you?" Franky interjected here.\par
\par
"Rather!  Just look!"\par
\par
There were bright red, newly-made weals and brown and purplish old ones\par
on the little muscular, boyish arm from which the speaker stripped the\par
sleeve.\par
\par
"My back and legs are lots worse," he volunteered with the air of a\par
showman.  "I sometimes think he'd like to kill me.  But he won't"--the\par
blue eyes were shrewd under the white-streaked forelock--"because of\par
what the Emperor said."\par
\par
"'Take the boy with you and make of him a decent German.'  For fear of\par
your being sent for, he--  Yes, I understand! ... My Christmas!" Franky\par
whispered, opening his haggard eyes, and the fire that burned in them\par
scorched up the water, "If I only had the use of this bashed-up body I'd\par
jolly soon put the fear of God into the howling brute!"  His uncertain\par
hand fumbled about the butt of his Webley and Scott revolver.  "Shoot\par
him--and make tracks for Headquarters with you in his Taube.  Can't fly\par
for monkey-nuts though.  Can you?"\par
\par
"A little."  There was a lightening of pleasure in the sombre depths of\par
the blue eyes.  "He lets me do plain, straight flying when he's sending\par
Wireless, or photographing or observing.  I've never started from the\par
ground yet, or done a landing, though I'm sure I could if I tried.  _He_\par
has shown me lots and lots.  And I do what he tells me."  The forehead\par
knitted under the ragged piebald forelock.  "He bluffs about shooting me\par
if I don't obey.  But before I drink brandy or do other things that are\par
blackguardly--or throw bombs on the British and the Allies, he _shall_\par
kill me! I've told him--and he knows I'll keep my word."\par
\par
"I pipe.  And can't you manage to do a flip on your own," came back in\par
the nearly extinguished voice from the sunken chest of the helpless\par
figure on the blood-soaked stretcher.  "One o' these fine days when von\par
Thingamy isn't wide?  What's to hinder your getting away now and pushing\par
South to meet the British Advance-guard?  We blew up the bridge when we\par
left the town, but it's up to you to swim the river.  Or cross with a\par
barrel or a plank."\par
\par
"Yes.  And I've often planned to bunk it!  But--Man alive!--he's\par
frightfully clever.  He knows a Scout sticks to his Word of Honour--and\par
he always asks for my Parole."\par
\par
"F'f!  That's a poser, old son."  Franky considered. "If I were in your\par
shoes I'd take to givin' the strictly limited parole.  Two hours--or\par
three--or four....  There's a chance if the time expires without\par
renewal--of being able to--perpetuate a strictly honourable bunk.  So,\par
best Kid, live in hopes and watch out for chances, and one day----"\par
\par
The speaker's voice trailed off into indistinctness.  A deadly vertigo\par
came upon him.  He sank amidst swirling waves of grey nothingness, to\par
emerge after aeons, to consciousness of the morning sunshine, and the\par
warm rain dropping on his clammy cheek and hand.\par
\par
"Oh, oh!  I thought you were dead!"  It was the wailing voice he had\par
heard long ages back.  "Like all the other people....  The poor men and\par
women and the little children----"\par
\par
"Dead!  Not a bit of it!  Only shamming for a drink," Frankly whispered,\par
as the cup with its blessing of cool water revisited his baked lips:\par
"Look here.  Where did you tell me your Flying Devil was?"\par
\par
The boy said, with a scared glance through the breached front wall of\par
the baker's parlour, out into the street where the golden sunshine\par
played upon War's havoc and desolation:\par
\par
"I said he went into the restaurant in the square where the--the dead\par
people are piled up--to hunt about for wine."\par
\par
"I remember.  What's that?"\par
\par
The gaunt eyes rolled towards the yawning gap where once had been the\par
window.  The white lips whispered, "Did you hear?  I'll swear somebody\par
laughed."\par
\par
Both held their breath.  Not a sound reached them except the sliding of\par
some _debris_ from a pile of shattered masonry, and the gurgling of the\par
water in the broken street-main.  Franky mustered breath and went on:\par
\par
"And now shake hands and scoot, my son, for this spot isn't healthy.\par
Say 'Good-bye and God bless you!'  And--if you didn't mind--you might\par
kiss me"--the uninjured hand lifted clumsily and pointed--"here on my\par
forehead.... Steady on!  Hold hard!  Thumbs up, old man!"\par
\par
For sobs were racking the thin young frame, and the bright tears were\par
running.  He gasped out:\par
\par
"I--I--can't go away and leave you--to--to die all alone!"\par
\par
_Die...._\par
\par
The dreadful word, at last, dropping with a dull shock through the\par
wounded man's consciousness as a heavy stone sinks through deeps of\par
black water.  Swirling rings of mist in Franky's brain, threatened to\par
close down and blot out all things.  He thrust back the grey menace of\par
unconsciousness with a brave effort, whispering:\par
\par
"Die....  Rats!  What are you--talking about?  It's me for the gay life\par
every time!  All I've--got to do is to lie here--and--wait until they\par
fetch me....  They're coming--before to-morrow morning--give you my\par
solemn word!"\par
\par
"You're sure?"\par
\par
"Dead sure.  Look here--can you remember my name was Norwater?  Captain,\par
First Battalion Bearskins Plain?"  The stumbling voice went on as the\par
boy nodded: "Well then, I'd like you to put in a word for me when you\par
say your prayers, sometimes.  I might have a little chap of my own,\par
by-and-by, to do that for his Pater.  What's this, best child?"\par
\par
A black wooden Crucifix with the Figure of Our Lord in white plaster was\par
being held close to the dimming eyes.\par
\par
"It's a Crucifix.  I think it must have fallen down from the room that\par
was above here.  Won't you keep it--to help you through the\par
night-time--just as the one on my Rosary helps me? ..."\par
\par
"Good egg!  Do you pray to it--and kiss it?"\par
\par
"We pray--not to it, but to Our Lord who died for us and lives in\par
Heaven.  We kiss it--because even if it isn't pretty it is His\par
Image--and has been blessed by a priest."\par
\par
"Wipe my mouth first, please.  You'll find--hanky in my pocket.\par
Thanks!"  He asked, after his discoloured lips had touched the Feet of\par
the Crucified: "Isn't there something one ought to say?  A prayer--or\par
something!  Not much time now--before they fetch me.  Tell quick--what\par
words say!"\par
\par
"You couldn't have anything better than Our Father. Our Lord made that\par
prayer Himself.  But there are lots of others.  The little ones are\par
easiest.  Say: '_Jesu, have mercy upon me!_'"\par
\par
The weak voice came stumbling after.\par
\par
"Jesu, have mercy on me!"\par
\par
"_Jesu, help me!_"\par
\par
"Jesu, help me!"\par
\par
"_O Thou who didst die for sinful men upon the Cross, have mercy upon me\par
a sinner!_"\par
\par
The glassy eyes stared upwards and past the boy, and a thin scarlet\par
thread began to trickle from the corner of his mouth....\par
\par
"O Thou who didst die--upon the Cross--mercy--me a sinner!"\par
\par
The stumbling voice trailed away into silence.  The glazing eyes,\par
meeting Bawne's, said plainly: "Now go!" And as the boy, blind with\par
tears, turned in obedience to their order, a dull flame leaped into\par
them.  They had seen the tall half-length of a big man, panoplied in the\par
goggled helmet and pneumatic jacket of the aviator, bulking in the\par
window-gap, even before Bawne knew that the Enemy was there.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXIV*\par
\par
                             *AT SEASHEERE*\par
\par
\par
The narrow white footpath had suddenly led nowhere. Patrine had found\par
herself standing at the edge of a four-foot bluff, looking down upon a\par
grassy plateau that gently sloped to the brink of the cliffs.  A wire\par
fence enclosed an aggregation of stone-grey wooden buildings dominated\par
by a flagstaff and the latticed steel tower of a Wireless installation.\par
The White Ensign flapped lazily from the halyards of the flagstaff,\par
there were three hangars at a little distance away. A row of seaplanes\par
sat on the grass before them, and some figures of men in overalls or the\par
familiar Naval uniform moved in and out and about the machines busily as\par
ants. Where the grassland stopped at the cliff-edge the roofs of other\par
hangars showed, that were built upon the shingle. A little way out\par
beyond the line of foam where the long green lips of the sea mumbled at\par
the wet pebbles, another row of seaplanes lashed to buoys, rocked like\par
gulls drowsing after a gorge of fish.  And far out to sea, where the\par
heavy trails of smoke bannering from the funnels of rushing grey hulls\par
betokened the War activities of the Fleet in the Channel, and the\par
conning-towers of big submarines sometimes pretended to be little stocky\par
steamers sitting on the swell, two strange bat-like things rose and\par
circled and swooped, and were hidden in grey-blue mists to rise again,\par
and swoop and circle....  And a little dinghy with two blue figures in\par
it was pulling out from the beach in the direction of the anchored\par
planes.\par
\par
"Beg pardon!  But--aren't you Miss Saxham?"\par
\par
She craned her long neck, looking for the speaker, and found him in a\par
youthful Flight Sub-Lieutenant, who, standing below the grassy bluff,\par
was looking up with very brown eyes at the tall figure in the narrow\par
skirt of tan, white and rose-pink chequers, the low-cut blouse of\par
guipure lace, and the knitted silk coat of rose-pink.  Buckled pumps\par
adorned the well-arched feet, clad with navy blue silk stockings of\par
liberal open-work.  She sported a buff sunshade lined with rose, and a\par
hat of rough tan straw, trimmed with quills of navy blue and rose-pink,\par
sat coquettishly on the beech-leaf hair.  She gave the boy one of her\par
wide smiles, evading the "Yes" by nodding, and with a cat-like leap and\par
scramble, he was up the grassy bluff and standing before her, blushing\par
and saluting and holding out a scribbled paper-pad.\par
\par
"For me?"\par
\par
"For you--if you're Miss Saxham.  It's a Wireless came this\par
morning--from your--from a great friend of yours. Somewhere in France."\par
\par
"Oh--thank you!"\par
\par
She pulled off a loose buff glove and stretched a large white hand for\par
the paper-pad.  The message ran:\par
\par
"6 a.m.  Now leaving Compiegne for Calais.  Seasheere in five hours,\par
barring accident.  All my love to you.  Alan."\par
\par
\par
And the Lieutenant had thought her pale....  She kissed the paper and\par
smiled at him bewilderingly.  "Lucky beggar, Sherbrand," thought the\par
Lieutenant.  "What a glorious woman!"  He extorted from Patrine, who\par
would not be twenty until next August, the penalty for being built on a\par
grander scale than other daughters of Eve.  But she was asking:\par
\par
"Whom have I to thank for bringing Mr. Sherbrand's message?"\par
\par
"Flight Sub-Lieutenant Dareless--and the thanks are quite on my side."\par
He phrased the trite civility punctiliously, while the bold brown eyes\par
beamed and twinkled: "For you're IT," they said; "just--clippingly--IT!"\par
\par
"How did you know me?" began Patrine.\par
\par
"Picked you up through the binnics from the bridge, ten minutes ago."\par
The slim brown hand flourished, indicating a T-square-shaped space of\par
well-watered turf marked off in whitewash lines upon the green aerodrome\par
below.  "We call things by their proper names so as not to lose touch,\par
you understand?  The short stretch is the Bridge, and the long strip aft\par
at right angles--that's the Quarter-deck.  The big hut No. 1 is our\par
Wardroom--the Wing-Commander's cabin is divided off from it.  The\par
officers' cabins are in the small hut, No. 2, and the Warrant Officers\par
and men divide No. 3.  Of course we keep watches and post sentries--just\par
as if we were at sea.  That Territorial on guard near is relieving a man\par
of ours, do you see?"  He jerked his chin towards the moving brown\par
figure.  "What have we to guard?  Oh, well, the hangars, and our\par
Wireless"--another jerk indicating the latticed steel mast surmounting a\par
telegraph hut wedded to a vibrating dynamo-shed.  "We get reports from\par
our patrols--most of 'em are fitted with radio-apparatus--and we receive\par
and transmit messages.  Long distance?  Well, rather!  We're frightfully\par
swanky about our Wireless plant.  It's Number One, H.P.  Not big, but\par
jolly powerful.  A----"\par
\par
Six clear, silvery double-notes had sounded from a brass bell, hung\par
beneath a little white-painted penthouse sitting on the blue strip of\par
shadow on the westward side of the Wardroom hut.  The Petty Officer who\par
had rung the bell exchanged a brief word with the Territorial, and went\par
back to the hangars from whence he had emerged.  Patrine, with her heart\par
in her mouth, asked the Sub-Lieutenant:\par
\par
"Was that a signal?"\par
\par
"Only ship-time," said the brown-eyed one.  "Six bells. Eleven A.M.  And\par
our man ought to be looming up in sight. He might hit Seasheere now at\par
any minute.  In fact, he's nearly an hour late."\par
\par
"You don't--you don't suppose----?"\par
\par
Fear had pinched and drawn and bleached her so that she looked forty\par
behind her white veil with blue chenille dragonflies.  Her pale mouth\par
twitched and her black brows knotted over the haunted eyes that strained\par
out to sea.  The paper-pad, crunched to a mere wad, dropped from the\par
hand that unconsciously released it.  The boy picked it up, thrilled by\par
this peep behind the scenes of another's romance.\par
\par
"No, no!  There's no fear of an accident, Miss Saxham. Perhaps a bit o'\par
engine-trouble--you've got to travel slowish if she vibes too much.  Or\par
he might have spotted an Aviatik and delayed to have a biff at him--on\par
the principle that ten Hun-birds make an evener bag than nine.  We know\par
what a terror he's getting to be with the Maxim. But what puts the fear\par
of God into the flighty Taube quicker than anything is our R.N.A.S.\par
Vickers' gun."\par
\par
Ah, did he know how horribly he tortured her!  But a grey speck showed\par
upon the delicately-misty distance eastwards, growing bigger, coming\par
nearer, putting miles of green white, heaving water under its throbbing\par
engine with effortless speed.  Her glance leaped to Dareless, studying\par
the oncomer between narrowed lids, and the hope that had kindled in her\par
died out as he shook his head.\par
\par
"One of ours, on the Home-flight from Belgium, Miss Saxham.  Your man\par
will pick up much higher, and to the south-east."\par
\par
And presently the latest type of Fleet hydroplane, a two-seater Batboat\par
carrying two bareheaded young gentlemen, moaned into view, chasing its\par
own wave-skipping, flying shadow at full stretch for the shore, came\par
down in a long mallard-like glide, skidding over the water as the\par
wild-duck does, and in a ruffle of glittering spray, continued the\par
home-journey in the character of a motor-boat.\par
\par
Then there was a sharp squib-like crack, and from one of the anchored\par
hydroplanes, a rocket went up and burst in a smoke-puff that hung in a\par
little cloud of violet-grey upon the sunny air, and from the hangars on\par
the shingle under the bluff streamed figures in blue overalls or grimy\par
shirt-sleeves, and cheered and waved, standing ankle-deep in refluent\par
water, topped with creamy sheets of foam.  As the Batboat with her\par
joyous navigators rushed spluttering to the shallow anchorage and tied\par
up beside the Station planes, megaphones bellowed, motor-horns tooted,\par
somebody banged on the ship's bell, a cornet struck up "Rule Britannia!"\par
very much out of tune....\par
\par
"Well done, you two beggars!  Oh! well done!" trumpeted Dareless,\par
through his hollowed hands, and turned a beaming face on Patrine to\par
explain that the hatless navigators of the Batboat were Lieutenants of a\par
Flight stationed at Antwerp, and had shared in the Air Raid on the\par
Zeppelin-sheds at Duesseldorf--early on the previous day.\par
\par
And then a droning song had come drifting down out of the sky to the\par
south-eastward with a buzzing undernote in it that Patrine remembered\par
well.  Dareless had lifted his head for a rapid upward reconnaissance,\par
and said with a flash of white teeth in his brown face:\par
\par
"Thumbs up, Miss Saxham!--this is your particular bird!"\par
\par
And Patrine had seen, small and high, and shining palely golden in the\par
sunlight, the shape of the biplane that carried her lover, and her heart\par
knocked twice in her bosom, heavily, as they knock behind the curtain\par
before they ring up at the Comedie Francaise.  A Clery's\par
signalling-pistol had cracked and been answered from the Air-Station.\par
Mechanics in overalls had appeared upon the green.  Then the buzzing had\par
stopped, and the second Bird of War, rising higher to escape the\par
backwash of light airs from the cliffs, had launched into a splendid\par
sweeping spiral, ending in a long glide, and alighted on the well-rolled\par
Station aerodrome--and Sherbrand had come home.\par
\par
\par
Surely never until the thought of Flight,--formed in the brain-cells of\par
Man and fertilised by the lust of Adventure,--hatched out in the Bird\par
that bears the Knight of To-day upon the air-path, did lover return to\par
his lady after a fashion so wonderful as this.\par
\par
The Flying Men have always been coming.  In the Book of Books you will\par
read of them.  Ecclesiasticus, the Preacher, foretold of the day when a\par
Bird of the Air should carry the Voice, and That Which Hath Wings should\par
tell the matter; and how these Winged ones rush and roar through the\par
prophetic pages of Ezekiel and Daniel, you have but to open them to\par
learn.  Their shapes like locusts, their armoured bodies with great-eyed\par
headpieces "like those of horses prepared unto battle," the noise made\par
by their wings in flight "like the noise of chariots and horses running\par
to battle," the wheels beneath their wings, the human faces appertaining\par
to them, the inward fire that issues from them in scorching\par
vapours,--are described with fiery eloquence in the Apocalypse of the\par
Apostle of St. John, when the Fifth Angel sounds the Trumpet, and the\par
King whose name is Exterminans, the Destroyer, reaches the culminating\par
point of his terrific reign upon earth.\par
\par
Flight makes the world no more joyful, being mainly used for purposes of\par
destruction, but nothing can rob the Flying Man of his shining gloriole\par
of Romance.  The boy who was building toy aeroplanes of card and elastic\par
a few years back has rediscovered the Flying Dragon of the Cretaceous\par
period, broken and tamed the winged monster into a War steed, and\par
thundered down the forgotten roads of the Pterodactyl and the Rukh, to\par
reap shining honours upon the battlefields of the mutable Air.  And if\par
the girl who chaffed the boy of old worships him to-day as St. George,\par
Sir Lancelot, Sir Galahad, and Le Bon Sieur de Bayard rolled into one,\par
who shall blame her?  Not I, for one!\par
\par
\par
In the instant of reunion, when the tall brown figure came swinging to\par
meet her, and the strong hard hands gripped her own, Patrine loved him\par
more than ever.  Sherbrand's was not a romantic greeting, but it\par
thrilled her nevertheless.\par
\par
"They've asked us to lunch here, but it's ready at the Cottage.  Shall\par
we accept?  It's for you to decide."\par
\par
His tone had indicated his keen desire for the _tete-a-tete_ in\par
preference.  Disappointment had shadowed his clear eyes when Patrine had\par
voted for luncheon at the Air Station, inwardly longing to be alone with\par
him--to be alone.\par
\par
And yet, despite the longing, the haunting sense of a sword of Fate\par
hanging over her, Patrine found the Wardroom lunch a jolly banquet.\par
They were so young, those sunburnt faces, laughing about the\par
plainly-furnished board. The Wing-Commander in charge of the Station\par
proved to be something under thirty.  To Patrine, occupying the place of\par
honour on his right hand, he did the honours like a veteran.  One of the\par
navigators of the Batboat sat upon her other side, and Sherbrand was her\par
_vis-a-vis_.\par
\par
Sherbrand was altered.  She knew him older, harder, sterner....  Thinner\par
to the verge of haggardness, with a deep vertical furrow graved between\par
the thick eyebrows that made a bar of blonde fairness against the red of\par
his deeply-burned skin.  He had gone away a splendid youth. Now he\par
returned with two silvery-yellow stars on the cuffs and shoulder-straps\par
of his khaki tunic, a man seasoned and tempered as a bar of steel in the\par
furnace-blast of War.\par
\par
The pleasant meal ended, and the jolly party broke up. Their hosts\par
accompanied them to the gate of the Station enclosure, and the warmth\par
and heartiness of Naval tradition had been in the farewells that had\par
sped the departing guests upon their way:\par
\par
"_Au revoir_!  All happiness!"\par
\par
"So-long!  We'll look after the 'plane all right!"\par
\par
"_Adios!  Buenas noches!_"\par
\par
"_Sayonara!_"\par
\par
"_Sieda!_"\par
\par
"Good-bye and good luck!  Now all together.... Hip--hip--" and a rousing\par
British cheer.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXV*\par
\par
                    *GOOD-BYE, DEAR LOVE, GOOD-BYE!*\par
\par
\par
They had looked back to smile and wave their thanks, and an aged\par
tennis-shoe, scientifically hurled by Dareless, had knocked the cap out\par
of Sherbrand's upraised hand, and raised a cloud of chalky dust from the\par
surface of the sunken road.  Under cover of this they had crossed the\par
road and climbed a slope together and found themselves standing in\par
heavenly loneliness, with the sea beside them and their feet upon the\par
thymy grasses blotted by the short shadows of their tall figures, under\par
the almost vertical sun.\par
\par
"Look!" Sherbrand had said, pointing to a whitewashed, red-tiled cottage\par
cuddled in a hollow some quarter of a mile distant, girt with a gay\par
frivolous little garden full of bachelor's buttons and sunflowers,\par
lavender bushes and nasturtiums yellow and red.  He slipped his hand\par
within her arm and pressed it, whispering: "There's our Eden--and my\par
dream has come true!"\par
\par
Her heart choked her.  They moved on together shoulder to shoulder, her\par
elbow resting in the bend of his strong arm, and her hand lying in his.\par
The air they breathed was sweet with heady, nameless fragrance, the\par
burning golden light that haloed them seemed the effluence of their\par
love. Anguish and rapture mingled in the chalice of the perfect hour for\par
Patrine.  Nothing but rapture was in the draught for Sherbrand, though a\par
faint fold showed between his eyebrows as he said suddenly:\par
\par
"Hang it!  I've forgotten to ask the Station fellows to give me a\par
night's shakedown.  However, there's a decent hotel in Seasheere.  My\par
bag is still in the machine, by the way....  Did you send someone on to\par
the cottage with your traps?"\par
\par
"I----"\par
\par
She began to falter.  It was coming....  But his eagerness delayed the\par
moment of revelation.  The track they followed dipped down and they\par
found themselves in a grassy basin.  The turf cupped up on every side\par
and they were alone, lidded by the blazing turquoise sky.\par
\par
At the bottom of the green nest he stopped, and next moment his embrace\par
enveloped her.  She forgot, as an answering flame burned in her blood,\par
all the things that she had meant to say.  "I'll have my hour," shot\par
through her whirling brain, "I must have something of him to keep in\par
remembrance.  He has never loved me--nor I him--so passionately as now.\par
Oh, my God!"\par
\par
He released her with a happy sigh, and they sat down on the shadowed\par
side of their green nest, a deep dimple in the cheek of the sunny,\par
smiling Earth, and looked in each other's eyes.  He said, as she took\par
off her hat and threw it aside and turned her unveiled, unshadowed face\par
back to his:\par
\par
"Your dear cheeks are thinner, I fancy, Pat.  Have you been worrying\par
much about me?"\par
\par
She nodded, thinking of her sleepless nights passed after reading his\par
few letters, or when his letters had failed to come.\par
\par
"Pretty badly--in the days of the Retreat from Mons. You piloted that\par
French officer over the Channel and--whiff!--you vanished.  What has\par
become of him?"\par
\par
"Wing Commandant Raymond?  He's riding the storm and directing the\par
whirlwind somewhere on the French Front. I got my orders to join the\par
R.F.C.-unit acting with a rearguard battery of the Second Army Corps as\par
soon as I'd dumped him.  As for the work with the battery, it was always\par
the same thing.  We flew out against von Kluck's advance, spotting their\par
gun-emplacements and getting the range for our gunners.  And under us a\par
dark-brown river with five branches rolled South.  And that was the\par
Retreat."\par
\par
His arm was round her, her cheek was pressed to his, her bosom heaved\par
against him.  She turned her lips to his in a quick kiss, and whispered:\par
\par
"And when you came down out of your sky 'like pigeons homing at\par
nightfall'--that's a sentence in one of your letters--d'you recognise\par
it?--the river went on rolling still?"\par
\par
"Just the same, without a break.  And what a--welter. Remnants of crack\par
infantry brigades tangled with the rags of cavalry squadrons--grimy,\par
hairy, ragged chimney-sweeps with bandaged feet and empty bellies, and\par
blackened tongues hanging out, and blind, blank, staring eyes....\par
Imagine all the toy soldier outfits in the kiddy-shops of Regent Street\par
emptied into the gutters and you'll get an idea of what the thing was\par
like....  And Transport and Supply-columns jumbled with bits of R.G.A.\par
batteries and R.F.A.--three dying horses to a howitzer, and one gunner\par
left out of six!  Bands of refugees and troops of stragglers. Lunatics\par
led along howling and gibbering.  Lorries, carts, and motor-vans crammed\par
with swollen-footed cripples--cheek by jowl with bloody spectres\par
evacuated from Field Hospitals that were reddening the sky with their\par
burning in the rear.  A day-and-nightmare to haunt one for ever if the\par
end had been different--"  He caught his breath. "But when I remember\par
that we straightened the muddle--brought Order out of Chaos--turned on\par
the Germans and bit to the bone--I pray that the memory may stay with me\par
always, so that I may teach your sons and mine what it means to be\par
Englishmen!"\par
\par
"Oh, Alan!  My poor boy! ..."  She caught him in her arms with sudden\par
passion, strained him to her and then freed herself from him, and moved\par
away, signing to him that he must not approach.  "What you hope for can\par
never be! I'd have told you this before if I'd been decent, but I wanted\par
your kisses--I was hungry for the touch of you--and the sound of your\par
voice in my ears after all these weeks and weeks----"\par
\par
"Then why do you say it can never be--and tell me in the same breath\par
that you long for me and love me?"  His light brows were drawn into a\par
heavy line over his stern grey eyes. "Aren't you and I going to be\par
married?  Is it possible that you'd draw back--_now_?"\par
\par
"Because your wife should be a pure woman, and I am not, it is possible.\par
Don't move!  Don't come nearer!  If you do I'll never have the courage\par
to tell--what must be told!"\par
\par
And he had sat still, as a figure in carved khaki-coloured stone with\par
his knees apart and his knotted hands hanging between them, and his\par
eyes, curiously hard and pale against the strong red sunburn of his\par
face, fixed immovably upon her mouth.  When she ended there had been a\par
great silence; and she had looked up at the azure dome lidding their\par
green nest, wondering why the burning, perfumed breeze had suddenly\par
turned cold.  His voice recalled her:\par
\par
"Why have you told me this?"\par
\par
"To be honest."  She hugged her knees.  "To give you a chance for\par
freedom before you were handicapped with me for life, poor boy!"\par
\par
"And how do you suppose it makes me feel?"  He breathed roughly, and\par
gritted his teeth, wringing his hands in one another so strongly that\par
the knuckles started death-white against the reddened skin.  She heard\par
herself saying lamely:\par
\par
"I knew you'd be horribly sick about it and hate me!"\par
\par
"I don't hate you.  But I want to kill _him_!  He took you to that\par
damnable place and--"  He bit his lip and swallowed.  "How long was that\par
before I met you at Hendon?  Three days--and our day of meeting--the\par
meeting I thanked God for!--was July 18th.  This is October--the\par
14th--to be particular.  You must know what I'm driving at.  Is\par
there--any danger----"\par
\par
She said in a level voice, looking at him steadily:\par
\par
"I have deserved it--but I think God is going to be kinder to me than\par
to--punish me in that way."  Her eyes flickered and fell from his.  "It\par
was because--I was so awfully afraid at first that I made up my mind to\par
marry you.  And now--and now you know the very worst of me."\par
\par
"Hardly the worst."  He drew breath roughly, and the cloud upon his\par
forehead lightened a little.  "We'd have been man and wife before I flew\par
for France--if you'd let me have my way.  Why didn't you?"\par
\par
"I--Oh!--It seemed so mean....  A kind of child-stealing. You were so\par
unsuspecting, and so generous, and so _clean_!"  She bit her lips, and\par
the tears welled over her underlids....  "You shamed me into being\par
straight with you.  I'd loved you from the beginning.  But it was as\par
though my love had left off crawling and grown a pair of wings."\par
\par
"Answer me straight."  He turned so as to face her. "Did you ever love\par
that German?"\par
\par
"To my shame be it spoken--never for an instant!  After that night at\par
the Upas I hated him unspeakably.  Only when I thought he was dead, I\par
began to let up a little on the hate."\par
\par
He looked at his hands and unknotted them and knotted them, and said\par
suddenly:\par
\par
"You may be interested to know that he is not dead, but very much the\par
other thing.  He is scouting and spotting for von Kluck's gunners on\par
their south and west Fronts, and sometimes bombing positions he has\par
skried out--and doing it all superbly, damn him!  He has been degraded\par
to the rank of a Supernumerary Flying officer for some breach of duty\par
that got to the Kaiser.  And he has evidently made his mind up to make\par
good in this War.  They pick him for all the dangerous missions.  He\par
seems unkillable--and we've tried our hardest.  And wherever he\par
goes--until now I've kept this from you--he takes--the Saxhams' son!"\par
\par
"Bawne! ..."\par
\par
She shaped the name dumbly, with lips that were pale as poplar leaves.\par
"God forgive me!" her conscience whispered.  "How little I have thought\par
of Bawne!"\par
\par
"Yes.  I mean Bawne!"\par
\par
So odd was the contrast between the speaker's grim, set face and the\par
bald simplicity of his language, that her white lips twitched with a\par
crazy desire to laugh, as he added:\par
\par
"I've been keen for a long time on coming across the man who pinched my\par
hawk-hoverer and kidnapped my friend's son--and putting the fear of God\par
into him with an automatic revolver, or a Maxim....  But now that I\par
know--this!"--the deadly contempt in the voice is inconveyable--"a clean\par
death hardly meets his case.  Good cartridges seem wasted in killing\par
that fellow.  One wants to set one's heel down--hard on him--and\par
scrunch!"\par
\par
He had sat silent, staring before him yet a moment longer. Then he\par
gathered himself together and got up from the grass, glanced at his\par
wrist-watch and said, holding out his hand to assist her in rising:\par
\par
"Well, let's be going.  It's half-past three.  They'll expect us to tea\par
at the cottage.  By the way, you haven't told me.  Did you send on your\par
bag from the station when you came?"\par
\par
She shuddered violently, and leaped up without touching the offered\par
hand.  The west was all dappled with tiny pearly cloudlets, their\par
shadows were lengthening momentarily, the salt smell of the sea was on\par
the breeze that came in languid puffs.  But the wine of joy that had\par
brimmed their green bowl had been emptied out by her own hand, and the\par
draught now held to her flinching mouth was bitterer than hemlock and\par
blacker than Styx.  That change in his face and voice--\par
\par
"What do you suppose?  I brought no bag.  I am going home by the next\par
train."  She glanced at a little jewelled wrist-watch he had given her\par
and back at the mask-like face, that said:\par
\par
"You mean we part here, for good!  Is that it?"\par
\par
"For good--or bad.  My poor boy----"\par
\par
He put her "poor boy" from him with a gesture of the hand.  He asked in\par
a flat, toneless voice:\par
\par
"Am I a blackguard like von Herrnung?  You came down here to marry me.\par
What will be said afterwards--if----"\par
\par
"I'm past caring what people think or say!" she flashed at him angrily.\par
"I've told you that I will not marry you!--that I'm not fit to be your\par
wife.  Oh! if you suppose it didn't hurt----"\par
\par
A rush of tears drowned out his altered visage.  She turned away,\par
fighting for composure, summoning all her woman's pride to help her at\par
her need.  That swaying grace, that alluring physical perfection--had\par
never appealed to Sherbrand's senses so irresistibly....\par
\par
"Patrine!"\par
\par
She heard his eager footsteps following her.  She was snatched into his\par
masterful embrace, assailed by his stormy kisses, wooed by his\par
passionate words of love beyond her power to resist.  The flood in the\par
veins of both was rising, the force of the warm rushing torrent was\par
bearing them away, she cared not whither, so that she might keep those\par
arms about her still.\par
\par
"Patrine!  My woman of women--do you think I'd let you go from me?  Not\par
I!  I'll have you for my wife whether you will or no!  We'll forget--all\par
that!  We'll be happy in spite of it.  Won't we?"\par
\par
"No!" she gasped out.\par
\par
"We will, I tell you!"  He laughed out with ringing triumph and bent his\par
head, seeking her evasive mouth with his own.  Hard pressed she had\par
panted:\par
\par
"Don't ask me to marry you!  I'd never, never do it! Unless you were\par
poor and sick and a nobody--and wanted a woman to nurse and work for\par
you....  Then--the wag of a finger or the wind of a word would bring me\par
to you. But--I swear it before God!--I won't marry you as you are!"\par
\par
"You will!"\par
\par
"I've sworn I won't.  But--"  She had whispered it in a kiss of fire--"I\par
will give you--what that other man took!"\par
\par
And Sherbrand had uttered a hoarse sound like a sob, and unwound her\par
arms from about his neck, and said, holding her hands close in his and\par
looking sternly in her swimming eyes:\par
\par
"I'm no saint, God knows!--but I'm a better man than to take what you\par
offer.  Halloa!  That's Davis.  What's up now?"\par
\par
A distant whistle had made him prick his ears.  He whistled back and ran\par
lightly up to the brink of the grassy punch-bowl in time to meet the\par
little black-avised Welshman--hero of the Paris episode in connection\par
with the girl with the goo-goo eyes.  Davis had handed him a paper-pad.\par
Sherbrand had read it, scrawled a reply on the blank side to be\par
dispatched by the Station's Wireless, and hurried back to Patrine.\par
\par
"We--couldn't have been married to-morrow anyway. The man who undertook\par
to replace me while I went on leave has been killed doing reconnaissance\par
on our new Front in North-West France.  I'm recalled."\par
\par
"Recalled?"\par
\par
He nodded.  The British Force had been deftly transferred from its\par
position on the Aisne to a base at St. Omer, you will remember, thus\par
blocking the Calais Gate.  The New Offensive was taking shape.\par
Sherbrand had continued:\par
\par
"So--if you're to catch the three-fifty from Fearnchurch to Charing\par
Cross--we'll have to run!"\par
\par
And as the screech of a distant engine had sounded from the direction of\par
Fearnchurch Station, he had caught up the veiled hat and thrust it upon\par
Patrine, grabbed her thin rain-coat and vanity bag and sunshade, and\par
hurried her back to the flinty railway-station by the way she had come.\par
And with the banging of the carriage-door, her woman's heart had broken.\par
She had felt it bleeding drip, drip, drip! as Sherbrand's tall bare head\par
and grave sad eyes had receded out of sight.\par
\par
And the train had been delayed at the next station, waiting for the\par
passage of a troop-train crammed with eager faced young men of\par
Kitchener's Army, concrete answers to the famous Call to Arms and the\par
First Five Questions--nearly half an hour.  So that rounding the curve\par
beyond the last signal-cabin for the clanking journey through the short\par
tunnel, Patrine had seen, some miles to seaward of the glittering white\par
prow of the North Foreland, a biplane with its wings reddened by the\par
sunset, flying south-east.\par
\par
"Oh! good-bye, Alan!" she had whispered, knowing that she would never\par
see her Bird of War again.  He had been caught and dragged back into the\par
fiery whirl of the cyclone without the hope that nerves and supports and\par
brings adventurers back.  Sorrowful and stern, baulked of his heart's\par
desire, grimly bent on meeting von Herrnung, and wreaking retribution\par
for a horrible wrong, upon the red head of the Kaiser's Flying Man.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXVI*\par
\par
                             *MORE KULTUR*\par
\par
\par
The boy's slight figure seemed to shrink upon itself as the stony eyes\par
looked at him, and the teeth showed under the red moustache, not tightly\par
curled now, but stiffened and pointing to the eyes.  Von Herrnung set a\par
foot upon the broken wall and leaped into the baker's parlour,\par
staggering slightly as he alighted amongst the rubbish on the broken\par
floor.  He had been drinking, but not to excess, for the\par
restaurant-cellars having been thoroughly gutted by his countrymen, the\par
wreckage of the bar behind which Madame had sat, busy with her\par
embroidery, had yielded barely a half-tumbler of Cognac and a single\par
bottle of Champagne.\par
\par
Having drunk enough to spur memory and not to lull his snarling\par
grievances to slumber, he had come forth to blunt the tooth of his\par
bitter hatred on the boy.  For, since that queer tickling, pleasurable\par
sensation experienced in his first tantalization of Bawne's hunger,\par
every new weal marked upon the wincing body, every fresh bruise\par
inflicted on the shuddering soul of Her Dearest, imparted to von\par
Herrnung a ferocious pleasure in comparison with which mere vicious\par
indulgence palled.\par
\par
"So, there you are, little English pig-dog," he said in German, as the\par
blue eyes met his own and fell away before them and the colour sank out\par
of the young face.  "Get you back to the _Market-platz_ there and wait\par
for me.  I have some business with your friend."\par
\par
He stretched out a long arm, picked up the boy by the slack of his\par
garments, and with a turn of the wrist dropped him into the street.  His\par
ears were pricked for the cry that should follow the slight scrambling\par
fall of the light body on the rubbish.  It failed to come, and he\par
frowned. Presently--  Meanwhile here was game of a larger kind.  He\par
looked down from his superb height upon the bloodstained figure in the\par
stretcher.  Its eyes were closed, and the haggard face beneath the grime\par
and bristles had the yellowish-white of old wax.  He spoke to it\par
harshly, in his English, and the brownish lids split apart and the gaunt\par
sick eyes glimmered up at him.  But no reply came from the livid lips.\par
He rapped his foot sharply on the floor, repeating:\par
\par
"I suppose you know you are my prisoner, sir?" and a strange spasm of\par
mingled amusement and irony twitched the muscles of the haggard mask.\par
The faded negatives of eyes regarded him with the ghost of a smile in\par
them.  The dissolving voice said in tones no louder than a sigh:\par
\par
"Possibly.  But not--for long!"\par
\par
The voice stopped short.  As von Herrnung took a step nearer to the\par
stretcher, his toe stubbed against and caught in the strap of a leather\par
case lying on the littered floor.  He picked up the case and smiled as\par
he drew out a costly pair of Zeiss binoculars.  His own, though hailing\par
from the Jena workshop, only magnified to 12x.  These registered 25x. On\par
the metal rim of the larger lense was engraved the style and title of\par
the owner: "Capt. Rt. Hon. Viscount Norwater, Royal Bearskins Plain."\par
\par
A find in the dual sense.  He restored the binoculars to their case,\par
unbuckled the strap and slipped it under his heavy bandolier of\par
cartridges, hanging the case beside his own, loosened the upper\par
stud-clips that fastened his goggled helmet, and pushed it back so as to\par
reveal his whole face. The gaunt eyes were open, looking at him\par
attentively.  He asked them:\par
\par
"May it not be that we have met before?  In Paris, yes? On the night of\par
the Grand Prix.  At the Hotel Spitz, _ja, ja, gewiss_!  A dinner given\par
by Sir Thomas Brayham for Lady Wathe and a few friends.  You were one of\par
the friends.  I another.  How is the old woman, do you know?"\par
\par
_Kreutzdonnerwetter!_ what inconceivable insolence!  The eyes looked\par
through him as though he had not been there. His hard blue eyes, already\par
injected with blood, grew savage, and a purplish tinge suffused his\par
florid skin.  He reflected an instant, pulled a capacious silver\par
spirit-flask from the deep side-pocket of his pneumatic, half-filled the\par
drinking cup that capped it, and knelt down beside the stretcher, saying\par
quite pleasantly, in his gutturals:\par
\par
"See, here is some capital Cognac.  Let me give you a sip, eh?  Then you\par
will feel better."  He poured a dram between the teeth, and waited\par
through a spasm of coughing, wiped the blood and mucus from the gasping\par
lips with a rag of the torn clothing, then pulled a stool from amongst\par
the rubbish, sat down near the feet of the wounded man, facing him, and\par
took a long pull of the belauded brandy from the neck of the big flask.\par
\par
"That does more good than canteen coffee," he said, and sucked his red\par
moustache appreciatively.  He set down the flask on the floor between\par
his feet, found his case, and carefully chose a cigar.\par
\par
"A zigarre?  No!  You will, then, perhaps not object to my smoking?  We\par
of the Field Flight have to comfort ourselves with snuff when in the\par
air.  To burn tobacco and blaze up like a star-shell and come down like\par
a charred rocket-stick, that is not at all agreeable or _praktisch_.\par
_Sapperlot!_ you are not a very amusing companion.  Nevertheless, my\par
fellow, I drink to your jolly good health!"\par
\par
He knocked off the ash of his cigar, cleared his throat, and spat, just\par
clearing Franky's shoulder.  The flicker of anger in the sunken eyes\par
brought a glitter of malice into his own. He sent out a long swaggering\par
stream of smoke, and knocked the ash from his cigar with the little\par
finger of his ringed left hand, continuing:\par
\par
"You see, I have cut the long thumb-nail that amused you when we met in\par
Paris.  The Day has come--though you would not join me in drinking to\par
its dawning!--and the German eagle has dipped his claws in English\par
blood.  We Prussians have beaten out the iron sceptre of World Power\par
with giant blows upon the War Anvil, and the sun that never set upon the\par
swanky British Empire, has already risen to find the Roast Beef of Old\par
England in danger, and the Triple Entente a bankrupt syndicate."  He\par
shrugged and twisted his red moustache, tilted his big body sidewise,\par
and spat at a carefully-calculated angle, missing the other shoulder of\par
the victim as he pursued:\par
\par
"But you do not know ... _Donnerwetter!_ how should you?--lying here\par
like a stuck pig!  Yesterday--in the neighbourhood of Ypres--took place\par
the ultimate, conclusive battle, in which the German mammoth pounded the\par
British Lion into pulp.  Your little British Expeditionary Force may be\par
said to exist no longer.  Your Brigade of Guards, who boast that, like\par
the Samurai, they do not surrender while yet unwounded, is practically\par
extinct. Maddened by despair the officers shot the few men who remained\par
and then blew one another's brains out.  Your Commander-in-Chief is our\par
prisoner, Sir Rothesay Craig has been killed, also General Callonby and\par
General Jones-Torrian. The French Generalissimo has surrendered, with\par
the 5th French Army.  The 6th French Army has been chopped into\par
sausage-meat.  So, all is over!  Total Kaput!"\par
\par
"If what you say is Gospel," said the weak voice, and the faded eyes had\par
the ghost of a smile in them, "why do I keep on hearing our guns?"\par
\par
For the hurly-burly of battle in the South had broken out afresh as\par
though in contradiction.  The crazy floor vibrated, the tottering walls\par
shook with the distant fury of sound:\par
\par
_Thud--thud--thud--thud!_ and the muffled _Boom!--Crash!_ of immense\par
explosions.  And through all the steady slogging of Royal Garrison\par
Artillery howitzers, and the tireless, dogged hammering of Field\par
Artillery eighteen-pounders.\par
\par
"_Macht nicht!_"\par
\par
Von Herrnung shrugged contemptuously, though his keen ear did not miss\par
the fact that the guns were coming nearer: "That must go on--for a\par
little!--until the last show of resistance is broken down.  If it be a\par
military virtue not to be aware when you are beaten--your big-jawed,\par
dull-brained, short-headed British bull-dogs of soldiers have that\par
virtue, of course.  But comes the awakening!  The Russian Navy has been\par
blown off the Baltic, the Czar has accepted our Kaiser's ultimatum--the\par
Belgian Government has made its submission--the Belgian Army has laid\par
down its arms. Our 17-inch siege-howitzers are bombarding the shores of\par
England from their emplacements at Calais.  The Army of Invasion is\par
embarking--your British Navy--the floating bulwark of your Empire--lies\par
at the bottom of the North Sea.  Ministers run from one end of England\par
to the other, begging, coaxing, persuading--your proletariat.  There is\par
panic in the English War Office, and despair at Buckingham Palace;\par
rebellion in the streets of London, _debacle_ in the City, and stampede\par
in the West End.  To-morrow the Emperor of Greater Germany and the Crown\par
Prince, Viceroy of the Brito-German Possessions, will, with the Empress\par
enter Paris.  Ten miles of films will record for all Posterity this\par
colossal and magnificent scene.  The London pageant of triumph follows.\par
Well may you weep, my unlucky fellow, over the collapse and ruin of your\par
proud country"--for tears were really trickling from the puckered\par
eyelids of the now flushed and quivering face.\par
"_Himmelkreuzbombenelement_! You are not weeping.  You are laughing, you\par
dirty English swine!"\par
\par
"What else do you--expect--when you're so--dashed amusin'?" gasped\par
Franky painfully.  "Roll along with some more of it--why don't you,\par
Anatole?"\par
\par
"You do not believe me, no?  You think that I am rotting," von Herrnung\par
shrugged his huge shoulders and laughed with forced heartiness.  "Always\par
to rot, that is the English custom."  He added, with a cruel relish:\par
"_Desto besser_, you will die more pleasantly.  For of course you will\par
die.  This is the third day you have lain here, _Alter junge_, and you\par
have the smell and colour of gangrene.  You are a lump of carrion,\par
Norwater, not worth the taking away!"\par
\par
"Possibly not!"\par
\par
The eyes met his calmly, though their laughter had died out.  It angered\par
von Herrnung to be baulked of the ferocious enjoyment he had promised\par
himself.  He finished the Cognac slowly, seeking in the fiery drink a\par
spur to inventiveness, and sucked his moustache slowly as he capped and\par
pocketed the flask.\par
\par
"I am hellishly sorry, I assure you, Norwater," he said, adopting a\par
bluff and hearty manner as he sucked the stump of the nearly finished\par
cigar.  "One is hardened to death and wounds in War, but one is human.\par
And I have been on friendly terms with many Englishmen and _Angenehme\par
Englanderinn_ such as Lady Wathe, whom I have known for years, and that\par
superb brunette, Mees Saxham.  We flirted desperately that night in\par
Paris.  Later on, in London, she became my mistress----"\par
\par
"You lie, you aeroplane-stealing cad!" said Franky, feebly but with\par
great distinctness.  Von Herrnung swore and spat, full in his face.  Its\par
nostrils winced disgust, but the brown eyes were indomitable.  And from\par
the blue lips came a mere thread of human utterance, pregnant with\par
scathing irony:\par
\par
"I--say to you what the--Belgian woman said to your Kaiser--when\par
his--horse splashed her.  '_This kind of filth--wipes off!_'"\par
\par
"You think so, eh?  You----"\par
\par
Von Herrnung clenched his fist, and might have dashed it in the eyes\par
that defied him, but for a sudden, significant change in the sound of\par
those distant guns.  The barrage of the German Field Artillery was\par
becoming intermittent. The slogging of the British had increased in\par
energy.\par
\par
A flare of red spurted into the Kaiserman's pasty cheeks, and his hard\par
eyes lighted eagerly.  He forgot his rule of sleeping off liquor before\par
again taking to the air.  With a confidence in his own powers largely\par
justified by his successes, his mind leaped to the scene of conflict.\par
Now, when the German batteries were weakening, was the moment for the\par
arrival of a pilot-aviator of the Imperial Field Flight, skilled as\par
aerial observer and signaller, and known to be indifferent to risk.\par
\par
Here was the chance one had hoped for.  Restitution of the forfeited\par
decoration.  Restoration to the Emperor's favour.  Reinstatement in the\par
lost place upon the regimental roster.  Promotion--the bestowal of new\par
honours--danced before him like little, gaudy demons, drowning with\par
their buzz the voice of prudence, luring him to the essay.\par
\par
"I am compelled to leave you now, Norwater," he said smilingly to the\par
man on the stretcher; "thanks so much for our interesting chat!  I shall\par
carry away a pleasant recollection, and leave you also a memento in the\par
shape of a bomb, which I shall drop on you when I have climbed to a\par
suitable height.  So _Gut Abend, Alter junge_.  Though before I go there\par
is a trifling formality----"\par
\par
He knelt down by the stretcher, and without unnecessary gentleness\par
rifled the pockets of the wounded man.  The victim had swooned when von\par
Herrnung rose, transferring to his own person a small purse, heavy with\par
English sovereigns, and a pigskin case full of crisp French banknotes,\par
with a thin gold wrist-watch that had a luminous dial, and a coroneted\par
monogram upon the back.\par
\par
Sheer waste, according to the German War Book, issued by the Great Staff\par
for the use of German officers, to leave upon the person of the fallen\par
opponent articles likely to be of use to the conqueror.  He rinsed his\par
hands in the water-can, and dried them on his clothing, pulled up his\par
helmet, fastened it, and buttoned his pockets, straightened his\par
bandolier, nodded pleasantly at the reflection of his giant person in\par
the skewed wall-mirror, jumped lightly through the window-gap, and went\par
upon his way.\par
\par
\par
The slight figure lying so still upon the stretcher had never been\par
remarkable for beauty of proportion.  The sharpened face with its hue of\par
old wax, the discoloured stains and the hair and grime upon it, had\par
never been handsome even in health.  But thrown back and tilted upwards,\par
with the rosy glow of the setting sun touching the high brow, and violet\par
shadows framing the sealed eyelids and close-shut mouth, it did not lack\par
the quality of nobility.  There was something knightly about the still\par
form.\par
\par
He revived to pain and loneliness and burning thirst, the squalor and\par
abomination of desolation, the louder, nearer thudding of the German\par
drum-fire, and the dogged reply of the unweakening British guns.  He\par
might have deemed the events that had taken place illusions born of\par
weakness and fever, but for the testimony of the looking-glass that hung\par
away upon the wall.  There was the familiar vista of the Market Square,\par
with the charred ruins of Town Hall and Clock Tower, yet sending up thin\par
columns of bluish smoke into the radiant air.  You could even make out a\par
corner of the great stack of stiffened, blackening bodies.  Nothing was\par
wanting but that the Taube should still be resting on the cobblestones\par
like a drowsy white vampire-bat glutted with human blood.\par
\par
But the Taube was not there.  From high overhead the buzzing note of the\par
hoverer came down to Franky.  He could see through the rents in the\par
penthouse of broken flooring the white, winged shape hanging poised\par
overhead. He even fancied he could descry the helmeted, goggled head of\par
von Herrnung peering over the bulwarks of the bird-body, the jut of his\par
elbow and the pear-shaped wire cages in which the bombs hung ready to\par
his hand.\par
\par
The thought of Margot and the child was an exquisite agony.  The thirst\par
for life, delectable life, revived in Franky ragingly.  In dreadful\par
expectation of the deafening crash, and the rending pang, and the\par
burning bite of the greenish flame, the haggard eyes were straining\par
upwards, when the terror went out of them, and their lids flickered\par
down.... Let the fellow do his worst.  Where was the good of hating?\par
Christ had prayed for His murderers when they nailed Him on the Tree.\par
The numb hand feebly made the Sacred Sign, and the tension passed with\par
the terror.... There was a dull boom high overhead, and some heavy\par
objects fell in a neighbouring backyard.  Little bits of metal rattled\par
on Franky's plank penthouse, and some warm drops pattered on Franky's\par
face and wetted the hand that lay upon his breast.  Not rain, but\par
something sticky and thick, with a sickly, well-known odour.  He lifted\par
the hand. Oh, horrible!  The heavens were raining blood.\par
\par
\par
Too weak to even guess at what had happened, he fell again into a\par
stupor.  The hollowed chest heaved at longer intervals beneath the First\par
Aid bandaging over which had been thrown the khaki coat.  Long cold\par
breaths expired through the panting nostrils, the eyes showed a glassy\par
line of white between the parted lids.  He was dreaming....\par
\par
Dreaming of being borne along in a shadowy boat under starless skies,\par
through clear lucent darkness, over another darkness unfathomable, and\par
yet diamond-clear.  Perhaps no more water than the atmosphere above it\par
was air, both possibly, elements unknown....  The boat crowded with\par
seated shapes, three of them feminine....  A tall, black-hooded,\par
black-mantled figure in the sternway seemed to impel the vessel with a\par
single oar.\par
\par
"Is this stuff water?"\par
\par
The quiet voice of a man seated beside Franky had asked the question.\par
Franky slipped his hand over the boat's low side and withdrew it\par
shining, but not dripping, thinking:\par
\par
"It is and it isn't.  Fairly odd!  Wonder where we're bound for?  That\par
fellow sculling....  Reminds me of old Charon, in the Sixth AEneid, when\par
I swotted Virgil at School."\par
\par
"Me too!"  Thought seemed to pass current as speech, for though Franky\par
had not voiced his reflection, the tall man who sat next him had\par
answered instantly:\par
\par
"But if this is the Ninefold--what about the '_cold and venomous waters,\par
consuming iron and breaking the rarest vessels_.'"  The speaker dipped\par
his hand over the side and brought it up all shining but not dripping,\par
and touched his lips with it, and went on, smiling: "Besides, if you and\par
I are alive, where are our golden boughs, and if we're dead, where are\par
our oboli?  We ought to have 'em!  It wouldn't be good form not!"\par
\par
"Why, you're Braythwayte of Ours!  How is it I didn't know you?  Why did\par
I suppose--"  Franky broke off, for Braythwayte's very recent exit from\par
the stage of life had been performed after a highly coloured fashion,\par
when the Germans had showered heavy shells of high explosive upon the\par
little Belgian town.  "That fellow sculling," he said to cover the\par
slight embarrassment.  "Somehow I fancy I've seen him before."\par
\par
"Ah!  Now I recollect."  Braythwayte was answering the thought of the\par
previous moment.  "I did get crumped up pretty badly.  Should have come\par
off lots worse hadn't it been for Cruse.  He threw himself in front of\par
me when the shell dropped so near us."  He spoke of the Sergeant-Major\par
of his Company who had been killed at the same moment. "Don't you\par
recognise him?  Cruse is the man who's sculling. I caught a glimpse of\par
his face just now--it can be nobody but Cruse."\par
\par
"Beggin' yer pard'n, Sorr."  The soft South Irish brogue sounded more\par
apologetic than contradictory.  The thick, sturdy figure of the speaker,\par
uncertainly descried in the clear obscurity, leaned anxiously over from\par
the opposite seat. "'Tis Father Walsh--may Those Above reward him for an\par
ould, bould gentleman!--that kem crawlin' out on his four bones to the\par
Advanced threnches at a place they did be callin' La Bossy or\par
suchlike--to give Holy Absolution to meself and Hanlon an' two other\par
boys av' the Loyal Irish Rifles that wor' in a bad way.  Wouldn't I\par
swear to his skin on a gate, or the bend of his beak anywhere"--the\par
voice hesitated--"barrin' for the mimmory I have that Thim Wans was\par
afther pluggin' him through the head--and himself just layin' the\par
Blessed Sacrament on me tongue!"\par
\par
"Beg pardon."  A woman's voice joined in the conversation. "Sorry to\par
interrupt, but I know him, really.  It isn't the Surgeon-Major--or\par
Father Anybody!"  Franky recognised in the clear obscurity the flowing\par
white head-dress and grey Red-Cross badged cape of an Army Nursing\par
Sister, as she went on: "It's just our Civil Surgical Specialist--who\par
died of double pneumonia (septic) at the Harfleur Military Hospital.\par
Had a touch of influenza--and would get out of bed to operate on one of\par
the Sisters--a sudden case of appendix trouble with typhoid thrown in.\par
Oh, yes! the operation was successful, but the Sister didn't recover.\par
Still, the C.S.S. gave his life for hers all the same!"\par
\par
"Good egg, him!  But are you quite sure there's no mistake with regard\par
to our friend there?"  Franky nodded towards the tall, black-hooded,\par
black-mantled figure plying the oar, upright in the stern.  "Because\par
just now I caught a glimpse of his face, and I could have sworn it was\par
my grandfather--by a long sight the finest man I've ever come across!\par
He dived over the yacht's side and saved my life when I was drowning.\par
It was the Cowes Season of 1894. I was a cheeky nipper of eight--and he\par
was seventy-one. And the chill and the excitement brought on a stroke or\par
something.  He was dead in his cabin-berth next morning, when his man\par
went in with the mail."\par
\par
"Oh, you funnies!"  This with a clear little trill of laughter in the\par
voice of a small girl--Franky could see her bright eyes dancing as she\par
peeped at him from her niche between the Army Nurse and the small,\par
black-habited elderly figure of a Sister of Charity in a deep starched\par
_guimpe_ and wide-flanged cornette.  "As if it could be anybody but my\par
Dada--who pulled the soldiers out of the train that was all smashed up\par
and burning!  When me and Mummy----"\par
\par
"_Taisez vous donc, Raymonde!_" whispered the nun reprovingly.  "It is\par
not _convenable_ that _petites demoiselles_ should interrupt their\par
elders thus.  Remember where you are, and in what Presence!"\par
\par
"Please don't scold her!" coaxed Franky, the devout lover of children.\par
The nun smiled, meeting his entreating eyes.  He smiled back and went\par
on: "Right or wrong--we seem all agreed that our friend in the stern is\par
a near relation--or a close acquaintance of nearly every one of us.  In\par
every case a supreme benefactor----"\par
\par
"Surely, monsieur!" she gave back in a hushed tone. "But surely,\par
monsieur!  The Helper--the Benefactor of us all!"\par
\par
As the keel grated on unseen bottom, she folded her hands with a\par
beautiful devoutness, and sank upon her knees, drawing with her the\par
child.  The man of the Loyal Irish followed her example.  Franky found\par
himself kneeling with the others--and as the boat's prow ploughed into\par
sand or shingle, and the Ferryman, shipping his oar, moved shorewards\par
with a shepherding gesture, the voyagers rose with a thrill of\par
expectancy, and followed with one accord.\par
\par
He stepped ashore--dropping the great black mantle--turned and faced\par
them, spreading out His Arms.  Beauty Divine, glory unspeakable----\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER LXVII*\par
\par
                             *THE QUESTION*\par
\par
\par
"Have I been honest?" Patrine asked herself over and over, kneeling by\par
the open window, staring into the darkness. "Have I been just towards\par
the man who never was a friend even when he played the lover?  Did not\par
my own attitude of cynical curiosity towards secret, hidden things, bias\par
his line of conduct towards me?  Might not even von Herrnung have\par
respected a girl who showed no inclination to flutter moth-like, about\par
the flaming torch of Sin?  No! he would not.  But I could have saved\par
myself even from scorching--I, who approached the flame too closely, and\par
shall carry the scars of my burning to the grave."\par
\par
Drip, drip, drip!  Water, oozing from the box that stood upon the table,\par
was dropping on the carpet with the small, insistent sound....  At the\par
west end of the Catholic Church where Patrine had told her story to a\par
priest in the Confessional there was a great black Crucifix, bearing a\par
white thorn-crowned Figure gashed with gory-seeming wounds.  She had\par
fancied that the blood from them dripped down upon the pavement as she\par
had sat staring at the High Altar, and wondering whether it were true\par
that wilful sin committed by men and women for whose salvation Christ\par
had bled and died might not cause Him suffering even now?\par
\par
She had been willing to sin for Sherbrand, and said so in her hour of\par
madness.  Yet the renunciation of her lover as a husband had been an act\par
of the purest love.  Perhaps God would overlook the one thing for the\par
sake of the other? Perhaps He had really spoken by the mouth of that old\par
priest whose tears had dropped upon his withered hands....\par
\par
Drip, drip, drip!  Patrine began to suspect the source whence the sound\par
proceeded.  The people who had packed the roses--they must be roses--had\par
wetted the cotton-wool too heavily, the fools!  The inlaid table and the\par
carpet would suffer if the wet were not mopped up.  One ought to ring\par
for Mrs. Keyse or Janey, or better still, see to it oneself.\par
\par
She half-rose with this intention, then sank down again nervelessly.  It\par
was half-past ten.  The October night leaned close over London, Harley\par
Street was muffled in velvet darkness.  The veiled gleam of electric\par
lights showed at its junction with Cavendish Square.  The rumble of the\par
tube train came from Portland Place, the faint shriek of the Northern\par
Express sounded from Euston.  A Brocken Hunt of motor-buses screeched\par
and clanked up the Marylebone Road and faded into distance.  The rumble\par
and roar of Oxford Street showed signs of diminution.  It was possible\par
to hear stray sentences spoken by people passing upon the pavement\par
below.\par
\par
"I don't care!"  This from the shorter of two female figures that had\par
halted before the house.  The edge of light-coloured skirt showing below\par
her cloak, and the gleam of white cuffs framing the gloved hands with\par
which she gestured, suggested a Hospital nurse to Patrine.  "Taxation\par
without Representation is a crying injustice--and the men will wake up\par
to it one of these days....  And Mrs. Clash may be a noisy person--and\par
Fanny Leaven may drop her haiches--I do myself when I get stirred up.\par
But they're in earnest--and they've suffered--cruel!--for their\par
convictions. Look at this Petrell--that one that always takes the Chair.\par
She's a physical wreck--with the treatment she's had--and I know what\par
I'm talking about!  Haven't we had Suffragettes brought to the Hospital\par
for treatment over and over--after they'd been pitched out of Political\par
Meetings by Stewards and half-throttled by Police.  What I say\par
is--Moses! how late! ... We shall get locked out of the Home if we don't\par
run for it!"\par
\par
And their light hurrying footsteps and the unmistakable frou-frou of\par
starched print accompanying, passed away up Harley Street.  They must\par
have come from the Mass Meeting of Suffragists that had taken place at\par
the Royal Hall.\par
\par
\par
It had been a memorable evening.  The atmosphere of the Royal Hall,\par
thronged not only with the members of the W.S.S.S. but with\par
representatives of many other Women's Unions and Associations and\par
Societies and Leagues, was highly charged with electricity.  Mrs.\par
Petrell, resolute-lipped, quiet-eyed, clear of diction and composed of\par
manner, knew, as she sat in her chair beside the little table in the\par
middle of the crowded platform, and better even than the plain-clothes\par
police among the audience--that at any moment the storm might break.\par
\par
She had advocated with all her much-tried strength an armistice for the\par
War-period, involving a temporary abandonment of militant methods and\par
inflammatory addresses, in favour of a policy of active help and\par
practical sympathy, alike honourable to her head and heart.\par
\par
Other Societies, Unions, Leagues, and Associations might have followed\par
the lead of their Presidents.  But would the W.S.S.S. accept her\par
programme?  Militancy had been its motto and the breath of its nostrils\par
through all these troubled years.  Since the outbreak of War, Flaming\par
Fanny had busily sown the whirlwind, advocating fresh Demonstrations in\par
conjunction with a system of Unlimited Strikes. Woman must hold her\par
hand, now that her help was needed. Man, the Oppressor of all time, must\par
be coerced by Woman's flat refusal to take part in Relief Work, or War\par
Work, or Work of any kind whatever, into yielding the withheld right.\par
And Mrs. Clash sided with Fanny--and others, nearer home.\par
\par
Little wonder then that Pressmen, sensing the imminence of riot, had\par
turned out in their shabbiest tweeds and left their watches and tie-pins\par
at home.  Little wonder that Medical Students, who had not already\par
joined the Service, with betting-men and patrons of the pugilistic Prize\par
Ring, found themselves baulked of anticipated entertainment, or that\par
loafers and crooks, pickpockets and rowdies, disappointed of a\par
pleasurable evening, expressed themselves in unmeasured terms regarding\par
that Mass Meeting at the Royal Hall.\par
\par
A melodious speaking-voice can be a magical wand, wielded by the mouth\par
of a plain woman.  But when the woman is beautiful and intellectual,\par
when soul breathes through her words, and strength and tenderness, then\par
she becomes a Force to reckon with, a Power to move mountains and bring\par
water of tears from the living rock of the hardest human heart.\par
\par
The officially-checked lights of the Hall shone down upon a sea of\par
threatening faces.  The electric battens over the speaker's head showed\par
her to be a tall, fair, slender woman, dressed in filmy grey, veiling\par
soft clinging silk of the same shade.  The simplicity of her dress was\par
unrelieved by ornaments other than a chain of pearls about her long\par
throat. The red-brown hair seemed heavy for the little Greek head, the\par
lovely pale face with the sensitive lips, wore a look of patient sorrow,\par
the eyes she turned upon the audience--a seething mixture of\par
irreconcilable elements--had in them courage, sympathy and\par
understanding, and knowledge too.  Before she spoke she had created an\par
impression. Strangers were ingratiated by her beauty and evident\par
refinement.  Those who best knew her were among the wildest and most\par
reckless there.  They had quieted, when she had risen up in her\par
unnoticed corner of the platform, and moved forwards to the speaker's\par
place opposite the Chair, as though oil had been cast upon the waters of\par
a stormy sea.\par
\par
\par
"When God Willed this War that we call Armageddon," she had said to\par
them--"for without the permission of the Most High the earthly Powers\par
that planned and prepared it could not have plucked the fruit of their\par
desire--it came in time to prevent the declaration of a War even more\par
terrible.  War, to the Death, between Woman and Man."\par
\par
In a few trenchant words she painted the dire results of such hostility.\par
\par
"That unnatural horror has been mercifully averted," she said to them.\par
"The old sore is healed, there is no hatred nor rancour left.  We women\par
have learned what a price has to be paid for the Franchise of Manhood.\par
It is the brave blood that is drenching the soil of Belgium and France\par
and Poland--that will flow in rivers as wide as the Thames at Vauxhall\par
Bridge before Peace is proclaimed again.  They have answered the Call.\par
They are pouring into the recruiting offices--in thousands of\par
thousands--those who have given up their loved ones, their homes, their\par
hopes of success in Arts or Sciences, professions or businesses or\par
trades. Will women be as unselfish and as generous when their Call\par
comes?  For it will come.  It is coming while I stand here!"\par
\par
They were strangely quiet, under the spell of the beautiful voice, and\par
the eyes that were luminous and deep with tenderness:\par
\par
"There are faithful Christians among you; brave earnest souls who have\par
prayed to GOD for guidance among the difficulties that beset the way for\par
working-women, and weaker souls have been maddened to frenzy and plunged\par
into unbelief by the intolerance and the injustice, the shrieking wrongs\par
and the unpurged evils that Man, who enters upon his heritage the world,\par
by the Gate of Motherhood, has ignorantly accumulated upon the shoulders\par
of the sex he professes to respect."\par
\par
There was a murmur of approval at this.  She lifted a hand, and they\par
were silent.\par
\par
"I say to those who have despaired, 'Despair no longer!'  I say to those\par
who have prayed--'Your prayer is answered!' Take up the work that has\par
dropped from the hands that are busy with the rifle.  Prove your right\par
to the Parliamentary Franchise.  Take your place amongst the World's\par
Workers, for good and for all.  The Vote will be granted: it cannot be\par
denied!  But if you had it now, passionately as you desire it, and the\par
choice were offered you--Oh! my sisters!--would you not yield it up with\par
gladness to bring those dead men back to life again?"\par
\par
And after a pause of unbroken silence she added:\par
\par
"For they have fought even better than they knew. They have re-conquered\par
Woman.  Freely and willingly as comrade and helper she takes her place\par
and her share of the burden.  Peace is proclaimed.  The War between the\par
sexes is at an end!"\par
\par
We know how truly the speaker prophesied.  Quietly as the vast Atlantic\par
flows into and fills a labyrinth of empty, echoing, rock-caverns, the\par
vast body of unemployed women took the places of the male workers called\par
away to the Front. They had clicked into the slots before the world was\par
well aware of it, or they themselves understood that a miracle had been\par
wrought.\par
\par
Said the breeched and gaitered lady-conductor of a North-West tram the\par
other day:\par
\par
"Now the ones that was brought up active has got their chance to do a\par
bit, and the ones that was brought up idle 'ave found out that they like\par
work, will they ever be content to sit and twiddle their thumbs again?\par
I don't think!"  She clipped pink tickets with zeal, and when a\par
red-nosed, watery-eyed elderly man who had offered her a pewter shilling\par
cursed her venomously as she thrust the coin back on him: "'Ere you! ...\par
'Op it!" she said to the offender, and caught him neatly by the scruff,\par
hauled him down the cork-screw stairway, and deposited him in the Camden\par
Road without turning a hair.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER LXVIII*\par
\par
                            *THE DEVIL-EGG*\par
\par
\par
Von Herrnung had quitted the earth sober, to discover at the height of a\par
thousand metres that his potations had dulled his brain.  As he ceased\par
to climb and brought down the nose of the Taube to the level, he\par
realised that he was dizzy, and that at the pit of his stomach squatted\par
the aviator's deadly foe, the demon of nausea.  He pictured it as a\par
yellow, frog-like thing with frothing leathery lips and green eyes that\par
squinted.  This image vexed him, and would not be driven away.\par
\par
He switched on the hawk-hoverer and sensed the drag of the twin\par
horizontal flanged screws against the thrust of the propeller, adding to\par
its drone the vibration of the endless travelling-chains running in\par
their sheath of transparent talc. To make room for its long groove in\par
the floor of the bird-body, the thick glass port beneath the pilot's\par
feet had been removed by the sergeant-mechanic of the Flight Squadron.\par
Now there were two ports, one on either side.  Through these the German\par
looked down upon the shell-pounded ruins of the village-town, its\par
roofless homes and broken enclosures giving the effect of a wild-bees'\par
nest laid open by the gardener's shovel after the gardener has smoked\par
out the bees.  As von Herrnung located the baker's house by aid of his\par
recently acquired binoculars, another swirl of sickness took him, and he\par
shuddered and spat bile over the side.\par
\par
Those distant voices of guns had not ceased their sullen calling.  In\par
the rose-flushed south towards which the Taube faced as it hovered above\par
the ruins of the village, black columns of vapour swelled and towered,\par
and acrid flashes stabbed through the murkiness.  One should be there,\par
his manlier self said to him.  Better to be a brave German bird dodging\par
Death amongst the puffs of shrapnel, dropping devil-eggs on the British\par
batteries, winning back the forfeited Cross and the lost Imperial\par
favour, than to be here, hanging like a carrion-vulture over the maimed\par
body of a dying man.\par
\par
Perhaps.  But one had promised oneself revenge for the scorn that had\par
stung like fire.  And one had bragged to the English boy of what one\par
meant to do.  He looked back, and called through the speaking-tube that\par
traversed the canvas over-deck between the pilot's seat and the\par
passenger's:\par
\par
"Unstrap yourself and come to me and take the control-stick.\par
_Schnell_--do you hear?  What is that you say?"  He put the voice-tube\par
to his ear and heard the shrill pipe answer through it.  "You think it\par
best to tell me that you take back your parole?"  The big teeth grinned\par
under the red moustache.  "All right!" said the Enemy.  "While we are in\par
the air, you are free to jump out if you like, and run away.  When we\par
get to the ground again, that is another matter.  Come now, sit in front\par
of me and take over the controls!"\par
\par
And as the boy obeyed, creeping beneath the intervening deck and under\par
the canvas partition, the Enemy moved back upon the pilot-seat, keeping\par
his feet on the lower controls, and separating his knees so as to leave\par
a ledge for Bawne to occupy.  Still laughing, he took spare\par
safety-straps that hung on each side against the bulwarks, and clipped\par
the patent pneumatic studs to the belt that girt the boy.\par
\par
It did not do to run risks.  Some day, it might occur to the Emperor to\par
order von Herrnung to deliver up his captive. And--the little devil was\par
useful--hellishly!  He had come into the world, twelve years\par
ago--possessed of the Flying Gift.  He had taken to the air as naturally\par
as a young crow or pigeon.  A tap on the shoulder, a word shouted in his\par
ear--and he knew what you wanted!  He understood now why his overlord\par
required the unrestricted use of his arms at this moment.  The small\par
hands twitched as they gripped the lever, and shudders convulsed the\par
slender frame. Noting this von Herrnung grinned.  His qualms had left\par
him for the present, he was once more master of his stomach and lord of\par
his cool and steady brain.  Through the back of his head the boy could\par
see him--leaning his big body sidewise--craning his neck over the edge\par
of the fuselage--his hand hovering over the bomb hanging near in its\par
wire holder, his keen hard eyes calculating distance--his red brows\par
knitted, his full mouth smiling under its thatch of red hair.  The\par
devil-egg would burst upon its impact with a roof or with the ground, a\par
thousand metres under the Taube. How many times since the red dawning of\par
the Aggressor's Day had he, von Herrnung, not plucked out the pin and\par
lifted the latch, and sent Death and Destruction speeding earthwards!\par
Why should this particular devil-egg have exploded five seconds after\par
its release?\par
\par
The detonating mechanism had been wrongly set, or the explosive had\par
suffered some chemical deterioration.  With the volcanic upburst of\par
flaming gases and the fierce blizzard of rending steel splinters, the\par
Taube was shot upwards like the cork from a bottle of champagne.  The\par
Enemy had cut out the hovering-gear when he had dropped the devil-egg,\par
and the thrust of the tractor had sent the Taube rushing on. Thus,\par
though she had been bumped about on waves of rising gases--though\par
daylight shone through holes in her wings and body,--a wheel had dropped\par
like a stone from her under-carriage--and a piece of her tail had gone\par
fluttering and swerving earthwards, no serious damage had been done to\par
the machine.\par
\par
Bawne's cheek was bleeding from the scratch of a splinter, but he stuck\par
manfully to the controls.  "Steer south," he had been told, "when I\par
switch off the hoverer," and he had waited, his teeth set, his brows\par
knitted, his eyes on the compass, and his heart crying out to God to\par
save his new-found friend.\par
\par
He knew it was because he had prayed so hard that the bomb had exploded\par
prematurely.  Would the Enemy try again with the one that yet remained?\par
But the Enemy made no sign.  One dared not look round or speak to him.\par
Was he in a fit, or sick, or merely shamming?  One could feel the big\par
body heaving at one's back as it lay huddled against the canvas\par
partition, with rolling head and arms spread wide, and knees that\par
straddled and sagged.\par
\par
Jerk!  The Taube heaved her after-part as a cow gets up, and nose-dived.\par
Von Herrnung's feet had slipped from the controls, and her rudder was\par
flapping free.  As Bawne toed the bar and gripped the guide-wheel, and\par
brought the keel to a level, the blood in his veins tingled and he knew\par
a thrill of joy.\par
\par
One had borne a lot, but--Man alive!--a moment like this was worth it.\par
What Boy Scout could deny the greatness of this boy's reward?  To be\par
master of this giant Bird, rushing at the speed of an express-train over\par
woods and fields and villages, diminished to the patches on a\par
crazy-quilt by the height at which one sped.  To hear the shrill breeze\par
harping in the wires and the roar of the flashing tractor, and change\par
the din at a finger-touch to the silence of a glide.\par
\par
West, where the sun was setting in red fire were signs by now familiar.\par
Linked specks that were big grey German troop-trains ran over the\par
shining gossamer-lines of the railways, going south.  Where the shining\par
lines looked like scattered pins, the railways had been blown up by the\par
Belgians, or the British.  Things like caterpillars crawling over the\par
white ribbons of the highways were German motor-lorries dragging great\par
howitzers, or Army Supply and Transport, or marching columns of robust,\par
bullet-headed German infantrymen.\par
\par
A blot of grey upon a town was where a Division rested. Strings of grey\par
spiders hurrying south, would be brigades of cyclist telegraphists or\par
sharpshooters, and processions of drab beetles scuttling along, Field\par
Ambulances, or Staff motor-cars.  One would have said that a green-grey\par
blight had fallen upon Belgium, swiftly advancing, stayed by nothing,\par
devouring as it moved.\par
\par
East, where the shadow of the Taube raced beside her like a\par
carriage-dog, black streaks that were barges still crawled on the\par
canals, and peasants' carts crept over the roads--and there were no\par
columns of troops in view, nor uglier tokens of the War.  Though the red\par
and brown towns showed scant signs of life, late root-crops were being\par
harvested; plough-teams were breaking up the stubbles, factory chimneys\par
were smoking, and acres of linen-web yet spread to bleach along the\par
river-banks.\par
\par
Later in the month the grey-green blight was to sweep over all this\par
region as the Boche retreated before the thrust of the 1st and 4th\par
British Army Corps, from Houthulst Forest to Menin-on-Lys.\par
\par
\par
Those voices of the guns were nearer now.  They talked on incessantly.\par
You felt the air that carried you vibrating as you flew.  The solid\par
earth heaved up in waves under the dusty golden smoke-drifts veiling the\par
south horizon. Black pillars of smoke and _debris_ climbed and collapsed\par
against the dusty gold.  Grey Imperial Staff cars were parked in the\par
courtyard of a chateau with pepper-box towers. Officers sat at tables on\par
the vine-covered terrace, while a farm close by was doing duty as a\par
casualty-clearing station. You could pick out the flutter of the Red\par
Cross Flag on a broken tree beside the gateway--and the come and go of\par
the bearers carrying laden or empty stretchers--and the white armlets of\par
the _Sanitaetskorps_ men who drove the ambulance-cars. To have seen over\par
and over again what grown folks learned from newspapers was to be a man\par
seasoned in War, whilst yet one's bones were young.  Well worth the\par
hardships one had borne, this sheaf of ripe experience.  Good to know\par
one had obeyed the Chief who said, "_Quit yourself like a man!_"\par
\par
So Bawne flew on.  The fiery chrism of a strange second baptism was on\par
his forehead.  Gates of wonder seemed opening on the horizon towards\par
which he hastened, guided by the big broad arrow of the reinforced\par
compass and the thudding of those nearing guns.\par
\par
Some perception of great issues at stake and marvellous impending\par
changes, ushering in the revival of the forgotten days of Chivalry, may\par
have come at this hour to the child so strangely caught and whirled into\par
the dizzy circles of the maelstrom of International War.  Did a voice\par
whisper to him that as of old by his Pagan forefathers, babes were\par
sacrificed to Bel and Odin--so for the cleansing of the sick world of\par
to-day from the War-madness begotten by greed and materialism a torrent\par
of rich, warm, generous blood was to be shed from the veins of the\par
young?  Could he dream that the lower mankind sank, the higher men were\par
to rise--mounting on stepping-stones of obedience and courage, to those\par
heights where the human may walk with the Divine? That through long\par
years to come, bright boys in myriads would drain the wine of Death from\par
the chalice of Self-Sacrifice, and pass to God who kindled in those\par
clean young souls the fire that made Him burn to die for men.\par
\par
\par
The Enemy was rousing from his doze or dwam, or swoon, or whatever had\par
been the matter with him.  The big body was heaving into an upright\par
posture, the big foot was knocking in Morse on the bottom of the\par
fuselage.  The boy looked down and saw blood running there--or was it\par
the red of the sunset?\par
\par
"Shut--off--and--look--at me," rapped the foot, and its thrall obeyed\par
and shrieked at the sight of the horror he was strapped to, glaring with\par
wild eyes, and spitting unintelligible sentences with bloody splinters\par
of shattered teeth and red rags of palate and tongue.\par
\par
"I am damaged, is it not so?  Something hit me when the bomb exploded."\par
Something like this came in strange sounds from that inhuman face.  And\par
the boy shrieked again and again, straining at the belt that bound him\par
to his terrible companion, conscious of nothing but overmastering fear--\par
\par
"_Quit yourself like a man!_"\par
\par
\par
He heard the words through the drumming in his ears and his heart left\par
off leaping.  His brain cleared.  He realised that the Taube was diving\par
to the ground.  He switched on power and brought down her tail and\par
pulled up her nose gamely.  They passed through a suffocating mist of\par
burned chemicals that deposited red powder on your hands and face, and\par
the glass of your flying-goggles, and parched your lungs like burning\par
Cayenne pepper--and were over the battle-zone.\par
\par
As far as the eye could take it in the face of earth was moving.  Death,\par
like a many-handed mole, seemed working underground.  Huge geysers of\par
dirt and mud and stones heaved up in thick black smoke and vapour.  The\par
air shook incessantly with reduplicated concussions.  Buildings tottered\par
and sank away, and railway bridges melted, and spurts of blinding fire\par
leaped from invisible mouths of guns.\par
\par
The revolutions were slowing down.  The Taube travelled painfully.\par
Beneath her bobbed a row of sausage-shaped observation-balloons\par
straining at their spidery cables, beyond these were the third and\par
second German lines--whitish furrows stretching East and West, with\par
little zig-zags, that were communicating-trenches, between.  A thin blue\par
haze of rifle and machine-gun fire hung over the pitted ground.  The\par
Advanced lines behind their smear of rust-red barbed wire might have\par
been sixty yards from the parapet of the British trenches.  Friend and\par
foe were dying there--and over the hurly-burly, dodging Death in puffs\par
of woolly vapour, belched from vertical mobile muzzles, directing fire,\par
signalling, wirelessing, scouting, fighting others who assailed\par
signallers or scouters--wheeled and circled the Birds of War.  Their\par
sharp eyes picked him out flying far down beneath them.\par
\par
"There goes a Hun somebody's shrapbozzled!" said the pilot of a\par
R.A.F.B.E., shutting off to speak to his observer.\par
\par
"Going to crash in a minute," said the observer of the Bleriot\par
Experimental.  "Where, do you suppose?"\par
\par
"If he keeps on at that angle," said the pilot from behind his glasses,\par
"he'll pass over that nest of Hun machine-guns in the big shell-pit\par
behind the German Advanced Line, at about a hundred and fifty--and pile\par
in that ploughfield behind our Gunners."\par
\par
The Taube was flying low and crookedly--the high crescendo whine of\par
shell passed over it--heavy metal sent from German batteries--and other\par
shells from British guns were crashing and bursting near.  The wind was\par
getting up in the west, and the drift of the machine was trending\par
eastwards, in spite of anything Bawne could do.  Could one keep flying\par
long enough to pass the first line of British trenches?  And how would\par
one come to the ground, knowing nothing about landing--and with a bomb\par
on board!\par
\par
One must get rid of the devil-egg.  Should one drop it on the enemy's\par
trenches?  As he flew towards them a rag of white fluttered, and Bawne\par
caught his breath.  A long line of grey-green men were jumping like\par
grasshoppers over the parapet.  They went forwards with their hands up,\par
waving a White Flag, and from the British trenches came men in khaki\par
doubling out to take their prisoners....\par
\par
_Rat-tat-tatt!_\par
\par
The khaki figures began to fall.  The grey men were cheering....  The\par
_rat-tatt_--came from the German machine-guns, pumping out jets of\par
murderous lead.  Then in a flash Bawne understood, leaned to the right,\par
and seeing the machine-gun pit beneath him--pulled out the pin, jerked\par
up the latch, and dropped the devil-egg.  Horrible to think, it would\par
kill Germans!--but then--to save one's own dear Englishmen----\par
\par
"Good Night!  Did you see that?" asked the pilot of the R.A.F.B.E.,\par
shutting off to address his observer, and immediately switching on\par
again, for a geyser of earth and stones and fire, and bits of things\par
that had been men and guns had spurted up from the spot where a moment\par
since had been the gun-pit, and troubled waves of heated air reached\par
them at 5000.\par
\par
"He knows he's got to come down crash, and jettisoned the lollipop to\par
improve his chances! ... Civil of him to drop it just when the\par
Deershires were getting it hot and hot! ... Deserves thanks from the\par
British C. in C., though his Kaiser won't be particularly pleased with\par
him," reflected the R.F.C. observer, as the Taube, flying like a bird\par
with a wounded wing, crossed the lines of the British trenches, dived\par
staggeringly, and crashed down in the ploughed field behind the slogging\par
guns.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXIX*\par
\par
                       *A MENACE; AND GOOD NEWS*\par
\par
\par
_Drip, drip!_ ...\par
\par
The slow dropping of water on the carpet and the sweet, heavy fragrance\par
of roses, brings me back as it brought Patrine.  She got up and pulled\par
down the dark blue blinds with the precaution that was becoming habitude\par
with us at this date, in view of that often bragged-of menace from the\par
sky.  She switched up the lights and moved to the table, roughly pulled\par
off the string that tied, and lifted the lid of the cardboard box.\par
\par
A rich, sweet fragrance that was almost musky enveloped her as she\par
lifted the thin paper.  A sheaf of roses of flaming sanguine crimson,\par
tied with black-and-white striped ribbon lay beneath.  Black and white\par
are the Prussian colours. Black, white, and red the standard of the\par
Hohenzollern. Patrine knew that von Herrnung had sent the roses, even\par
before she recognised his writing on a thick white envelope pinned to\par
the ribbon binding the flowers.\par
\par
"_If Isis desires news of 'her dearest', she will open and read the\par
letter.  From one who does not desire to forget._"\par
\par
\par
The letter contained a lock of hair, jaggedly cut--she knew from whose\par
sweet head.  Half blind with tears, she lifted the lock to her lips and\par
kissed it passionately, before she bent herself to read the careful\par
English sentences that revealed the man in all his vanity and\par
lustfulness, insolence, and tyranny, as though the burin of Strang or\par
the brush of Sargent had etched him upon copper or limned him upon\par
canvas, to show the world what depths of infamy can be plumbed by the\par
Superman.\par
\par
"_Strong Woman of the race of moral weaklings, have you not yet learned\par
to be proud that a Prussian soldier prized your beauty, and took it for\par
his own?  When the fierce men in the proud German Field-grey have\par
swarmed over the soil of England,--when, amidst the squadron of\par
night-birds whose feathers gleam mysteriously in the pale moonlight, thy\par
lover flies onward, singing his war-song, laden with his cargo of\par
explosives--when the Red Cock crows on the roof-trees of London's\par
wilderness of houses and London's fire-bells, amidst terrific\par
explosions, ring out the last battle of the century, will Isis then\par
think of me? Revolvers, carbines, bombs, and poisoned arrows are among\par
the gifts I shall bring thee in the hand that wears the mascot pearl of\par
black and white.  Coloured signalling-balls set in the silver of the\par
searchlight, shall be thy tiara; for thy arms and thy white bosom there\par
will be strings of rubies outpoured from the broken coffers of the House\par
of Life.  Our second nuptials will be celebrated by a mitred Death,\par
amidst the smoking ruins of Westminster Abbey, to the roaring strains of\par
the German Anthem, 'Now Praise Ye the Lord.'  Till then au revoir! shall\par
one perhaps say?_\par
\par
"_Ah, were Isis of the burning beech-leaf tresses not only beautiful but\par
wise, she would place her hand in the hand that stretches yearningly\par
over the North Sea.  I wish love more than vengeance; is not that\par
unnatural for a Hun?  A golden consciousness of happiness yet to come\par
wells up within me.  Would Isis taste that happiness, let her go to her\par
window and open it on the night of the day that brings this letter.\par
There are no Germans in England who are not in prison or under\par
espionage. No, possibly! yet go to thy window!  A word to him who waits\par
there, and Isis is once more mine.  But beware of turning my tenderness\par
by scornful rejection to hatred.  Cold devil!--I should then strike, and\par
frightfully, at the head whence came this hair.  Look at it well and\par
answer.  T.v.H._"\par
\par
\par
She could turn no paler, her hue was that of death already. She dropped\par
the loathsome letter from her hand upon the roses and thrust the lock of\par
hair into her bosom, and went to a window and touched the spring of the\par
blind.  It flew up and revealed her tall shape standing there\par
silhouetted against the electric radiance in defiance of that boasted\par
menace from the sky.\par
\par
The street seemed empty, within the radius of her vision, save for the\par
dark bulk of a motor-car, standing before a house on the same side some\par
way down.  Its headlights flashed, once, twice, and again, as though in\par
answer.  It slid forwards with a low hissing sound: "_Ss'sh!_" it said,\par
as if in gluttonous anticipation, and stopped opposite the hall-door.\par
Again the headlights flashed, there was a gleam of yellow enamel.  She\par
recognised the Darracq car in which von Herrnung had driven her to\par
Fanshaw's Flying Ground on that unforgettable eighteenth of July.\par
\par
Holding her breath, narrowing her long-sighted eyes for better focus,\par
she scrutinised the driver, recognising in the thick-set figure hunched\par
over the steering-wheel, wearing a peaked cap pulled low over his\par
forehead, and a wide white muffler twisted round his throat, the German\par
who had brought the message from the Three in the blue F.I.A.T. car. She\par
was sure of him when he touched his cap, looking furtively up at the\par
window, and switched on a small electric bulb, illuminating the clock\par
upon the dashboard as though to afford her a view of his face.  Its\par
bloodshot pale eyes, thick broad nose, and the unwholesome, purplish\par
colour of the complexion, barred with a big light yellowish moustache\par
with waxed ends, had stuck in her memory as ugly personal traits will\par
stick.  Of the slenderer man beside him she had no recollection.  He was\par
buttoned up in an overcoat with a fur collar, and wore a soft felt hat.\par
She felt the eyes it shadowed were fastened on her, and recoiled as\par
though from the touch of something unclean and horrible, roughly\par
dragging down the blind.\par
\par
She was brave, but the sense of being almost alone in the house with\par
those alert, observant eyes outside, spying upon her movements, made her\par
heart beat suffocatingly, and brought chill damps of deadly terror to\par
the surface of her skin.  She moved to a chair with a clogging sense of\par
ultimate effort--the nightmare feeling of striving against a powerful\par
hypnotic influence, bidding her creep downstairs and open the\par
street-door, step into the car waiting at the kerbstone, and be borne\par
away by rushing wheels and whirling screws, or even swifter wings,\par
perhaps, to that War-torn land where von Herrnung was waiting to exact\par
his price for sparing the beloved head.\par
\par
She drew the lock of hair from her bosom and whispered inarticulate\par
tendernesses to it, stroking its red-gold beauty with fingers and lips.\par
Not until now those bread white strands amongst the reddish-gold\par
conveyed their sinister meaning.  When it came it was like a blow\par
delivered full between the eyes.  She swayed forwards and fell upon her\par
knees beside the table, her forehead resting on the clenched hand that\par
held the boy's hair.  All that was maternal in her fierce, undisciplined\par
nature urged her now to make the sacrifice.  Remorse for having\par
forgotten the child in her absorbing love for Sherbrand, was a scourge\par
of fiery scorpions that urged her to the leap.\par
\par
Its uselessness, the certainty that von Herrnung would keep no hinted\par
promise to restore the hostage, would have been no argument to deter\par
her.  Sherbrand's influence might have counterpoised, but she had sent\par
away Sherbrand for his own sake.  Now she would go to Bawne, buy him\par
back with body and soul, if need be, from the hands of the torturer, or\par
at least share his agony and die by his side.\par
\par
Madness was near enough that night to sweep her tattered robe before the\par
eyes of Patrine, and beckon enticingly with her sceptre of plaited\par
straw.  She was alone and she had borne so much, and nothing else could\par
save Lynette's boy--unless it were a miracle!  Where was God--where was\par
God now?  Upon that July night of the child's spiriting away Sherbrand\par
had bidden her pray that Bawne might be restored to them.  She had\par
petitioned in a perfunctory way when she had thanked God for taking away\par
von Herrnung--that the child might be traced and brought back.  Now she\par
clenched her hands until the nails dug into their palms, and groaned\par
out, as the dry sobs racked her body, words that sensed after this\par
fashion:\par
\par
"Save him, save him!  For Christ's love save him--and give him back!\par
For the dear sakes of those to whom I have been so ungrateful! hear\par
me--only hear me! and I will--be different.  I will serve Thee, O God,\par
who have ignored Thee!  I will confess Thee, I who have denied! ..."\par
\par
Mean, base, said her pride, to kneel and entreat Him whom you have\par
neglected and insulted.  Even though He heard, do you think that He\par
would answer now?  But with desperate effort she thrust away the thought\par
from her. The Hound of Heaven had leaped upon her, flying.  She felt his\par
teeth in her garments, holding her back from the invisible hands that\par
dragged at her.  She knew that unseen forces of Good and Evil were\par
engaged in furious battle for her soul....  And strangling, she gasped\par
out incoherent sentences, wild appeals to the Divine Pity....  In the\par
midst of these, startling her like a thunderclap, came a hurried\par
knocking at the door.\par
\par
"Miss Pat!"\par
\par
It was the voice of Mrs. Keyse, and as Patrine stumbled to her feet and\par
stood wild-eyed and shaking, the little, matronly figure in the black\par
silk gown of housekeeperly dignity appeared upon the threshold of the\par
room.\par
\par
"You--wanted me, Mrs. Keyse?  Is it about the--the yellow car?  Have\par
they----"\par
\par
The hoarse voice and the white, wrung face conveyed to an ardent lover\par
of Patrine that something was wrong with her Doctor's niece.  Tragedy\par
was in the air--but Discretion is the better Part of Value, and nobody\par
knew better than Emrigation Jane what fierce passions could boil in the\par
Saxham blood.\par
\par
"No, Miss Pat.  It's not the car, yet, though I fancied I 'eard one stop\par
here a minute back.  It's the telephone in the consultin' room ringin',\par
and ringin',--and Chewse gone to bed," Chewse being the trained maid who\par
admitted patients and received messages.  "And me with the best will in\par
the world never could make 'ead or tail of them tellermessages--except\par
the 'ulloing!  And pre'aps you'd come and write down for the Doctor\par
whatever it is they've got to say...."\par
\par
"Very well.  Don't wait, I'm coming directly!"\par
\par
Mrs. Keyse vanished, and with that dreamlike sense of unreality upon\par
her, Patrine followed downstairs and passed along the silent corridor.\par
The electric lamp above the Doctor's table had been switched on.  She\par
took the Doctor's chair and rang-up and waited, sitting where Saxham had\par
sat when Lynette's sweet lips first touched his forehead--where the big\par
man had planned self-murder in the darkest hour of his despair.  The\par
frayed patch on the Persian rug beneath her feet had been worn by\par
Saxham's usage.  The triptych frame that held the portraits of Lynette\par
and Bawne drew Patrine's eyes as she sat waiting, and the clench of her\par
big white hand upon the table-ledge, the bend of her black brows and the\par
stern sorrow stamped upon her face made her likeness to the Doctor more\par
than ever apparent now.\par
\par
"Halloa!" she called, and the brusque harshness of her own voice was\par
startlingly like Saxham's.  A sense of Destiny oppressed her.  She felt\par
as one stifling in a vacuum--drowning for lack of air.  Her prayers had\par
rolled back upon her soul unanswered.  The sense of spiritual desolation\par
intensified her desperate loneliness.  No good to pray and cling until\par
you broke your nails to that great Rock that upholds the Crucifix.\par
Better let go, and be carried away by the torrent.  Signs and wonders\par
are not wrought in these days!--said that other Patrine within\par
Patrine--and if any were, there would be no miracle.  You fool, you\par
fool, to dream of one!\par
\par
She was sorry for herself as she sat there waiting.  This little duty\par
done, she would rise and obey that sinister summons from the outer\par
darkness.  Nothing on earth nor in Heaven could help or prevent.  The\par
sudden tinkle of the bell came at this juncture.  The call was in Sir\par
Roland's well-known voice.\par
\par
"Halloa! ... Is that you, Saxham?"\par
\par
"Halloa!" she called back in that voice so strangely like _his_ and\par
unlike her own.\par
\par
"Good!  Well, my true friend and faithful coadjutor of old time," said\par
the crisp voice, shaken a little as though by some irrepressible emotion\par
or excitement, "some news has been communicated to us by Wireless that\par
will lift up your heart and your wife's.  Are you listening? ... To-day,\par
about six P.M., near Langebeke, north-west of Ypres, at the moment of\par
the White Flag ruse that cost the Deershire Regiment two hundred men, a\par
two-seater Taube, flying low, as though something were the matter with\par
her engine, came wobbling over the British lines.  Nobody shot at\par
her--she had just given our side sufficient reason for consideration by\par
dropping a highly-effective bomb on a wasp's nest of German\par
machine-gunners--and she crashed to ground behind a battery of First\par
Corps R.F.A.  Her German pilot had been frightfully wounded.  His\par
passenger, who sat in his lap to steer--and dropped the bomb!--escaped\par
with a shake-up.  You've got the story?  Then, here's the tag of it.\par
WE'VE GOT YOUR BOY!  Bawne was the lucky fellow who only got a shaking.\par
He arrives at Charing Cross to-night at twelve sharp!"\par
\par
He added, as a stifled cry travelled over the wire:\par
\par
"Congratulations with all my heart, to you and Mrs. Saxham.  And to Miss\par
Pat, though I'm afraid she pays, poor girl, in sorrow for your joy.\par
There is a report that Sherbrand's Bird of War No. 2 has been shot down\par
by a Zeppelin he encountered returning to the Front from England to-day,\par
to supply the place of an R.F.C. pilot--killed while on\par
observation-service near St. Yves--for Callenby's Cavalry Corps."\par
\par
There was a stifled sound of interrogation or an exclamation. The Chief\par
continued:\par
\par
"He had no bombs.  It was madness to attack with only a Maxim and their\par
magazine-revolvers, but glorious madness worth a thousand sane,\par
reasonable acts.  As it is, the Zeppelin--supposed to have been on her\par
way from Ostend to bomb St. O--was badly crippled and compelled to turn\par
back.  It was a shell from one of her Q.F.'s that exploded Sherbrand's\par
petrol-tank and set the Bird on fire.  The machine was seen to fall in\par
flames near Dixschoote--held by the Germans.  Sherbrand and his observer\par
must be prisoners--that is, supposing they're alive.  Hard luck!  Break\par
it gently to the poor girl!  Good-night!"\par
\par
There was no answering Good-night, only a faint thud and rustle.  Sir\par
Roland did not guess what he had done as he rang off and hung the\par
receiver up.  And Lynette, coming into the consulting-room, noiselessly\par
as a pale moonbeam, found a big galumphing girl she loved lying huddled\par
between the chair and table, with her white face pressed against the\par
spot worn threadbare by the Doctor's feet.\par
\par
\par
Coincidence, you say, perhaps.  Well, but what is Coincidence? Is it a\par
Dust-wind careering over the Desert in the neighbourhood of the\par
Pyramids, playing with straw and twigs and dead locusts' wings, and one\par
stray fragment of printed paper, as a Mounted Division of the British\par
Expeditionary Force encamped upon the slope not far from Gizeh, ride out\par
with the dawn to exercise their horses on the plain that is partly\par
flooded by the Nile?  Or is it the ragged quarter-sheet torn from an\par
English newspaper, that wraps itself about the spurred ankle of the big\par
blond young Englishman who rides the vicious chestnut mare?\par
\par
Long lines of horses marching in threes for miles, black and\par
coffee-coloured natives in flowing jubbehs mixed up with tanned young\par
British Centaurs in sun-helmets and khaki shorts--and the rag of paper\par
clings to the leg of the one man there whom its news concerns.  She who\par
is dearer than all save Honour is once more a free woman,--and his faith\par
and constancy are to meet their reward.  His letter lies before me; a\par
sentence pencilled more blackly than the rest stands out upon the\par
yellowish paper:\par
\par
"_If this be accident it is incredible.  If Design, it is miraculous.\par
And I had rather thank Heaven for a miracle vouchsafed than owe even\par
such happiness--to Chance._"\par
\par
\par
When the deep swoon gave place to semi-consciousness, the pale lips\par
uttered nothing but broken words.  Locked away safely behind them was\par
the glorious news that would have changed two people's lives.  Thus\par
Lynette was still ignorant of her own great happiness, when having\par
helped Patrine upstairs to her room and put her tenderly to bed, she\par
dismissed Mrs. Keyse to her own slumbers, and took her place beside\par
Patrine's pillow, listening to the sighing breaths that were growing\par
deeper and fuller, keenly alert for the sound of the Doctor's latch-key\par
and the Doctor's step in the hall.\par
\par
It was close upon the smallest hour.  Something had detained Saxham.\par
Sitting in the darkened room beside the long prone shape beneath the\par
coverings, Lynette was free to lean her head against the back of the\par
chair she sat in and yield herself to the bitter sweetness of memories\par
of her lost boy.\par
\par
What the sorrow of Shakespeare wrought in deathless lines no halting pen\par
like mine dare strive to portray. Enough that the beloved little ghost\par
that haunted the woman whose heart was breaking, was closer than ever to\par
Lynette on this night.  All day the sweet obsession had thrust itself\par
between Bawne's mother and solid, tangible things.  The red-gold sheen\par
of the boyish head, the gay blue challenge of the laughing eyes, the\par
coaxing tones of the treble voice had tortured the senses they deceived.\par
She had thrust him away with both hands, for ordinary, commonplace\par
duties claimed, and yielding led the way to madness.  He had come back\par
again and again, to be driven away once more.  Now that her hands lay\par
idle in her lap--now that she was withdrawn from the world and its\par
realities, the beloved little ghost returned and had his will with her.\par
\par
Sitting in the haunted gloom, a strange conviction came to Lynette.\par
This was not Grief, travestying in the figure of the absent, but a\par
visitation from the World Unseen.... Bawne was dead, and had been\par
dragged back from the threshold of the Beyond by her own unbridled\par
yearnings. Could there be a punishment more terrible than this? Only\par
those who have loved and lost, and clinging to their faith in a Future\par
Life, strive to bear patiently the burden of bereavement, can comprehend\par
the torture of this woman in this hour.\par
\par
The Presence grew more torturingly tangible.  The empty shell of the\par
house that had been Bawne's home was full of his callings, his\par
movements, his play, his laughter. She heard his quick soft breathing\par
behind her chair in the darkness.  Once she could have vowed that a hard\par
little boyish hand brushed against her cheek.  Then she was alone once\par
more, except for the unconscious sleeper.  And then the torture began\par
all over again.\par
\par
Bawne was coming home, late, from the Hendon Flying Ground.  The long\par
months of misery--the horror of the War--had been a dreadful dream.  She\par
heard the long _br'r'_ of the electric hall-bell under the impetuous\par
insistent finger--the small scurry of his entrance, a squawk from the\par
maid who answered night-calls--a whispered word or two, and the clumping\par
of the heavy little brogues upon the stairs.  Would he trip at the\par
corner where he always stubbed his toe? she wondered--and she plainly\par
heard him stumble. Then her hair stiffened upon her head, and a long\par
shudder rippled through her.  The little clumping brogues had stopped\par
before Patrine's bedroom door.\par
\par
"_Mother!_"\par
\par
His voice called, and his well-known thump came on the door-panel.  The\par
handle clicked.  She controlled her shuddering and forced her stiffened\par
tongue to speech.\par
\par
"_Come in, my own!_"\par
\par
The tall door swung slowly inwards.  A wedge of brightness from the\par
lighted landing threw his shadow over the white-enamelled door-post....\par
The darkness of the room soaked it greedily up.  Then the doorway was a\par
square of radiance with a little ghostly figure framed in it. All the\par
light was behind him.  She could not see his face, but she felt his eyes\par
upon her....  Then the voice that her ears were sick for said with a\par
quaver in its treble:\par
\par
"It's dark, but I can hear you breathing! ... Mother, why didn't you and\par
Father come?  I thought when I got there I'd be sure to see you! ... But\par
amongst all those faces and faces not one was yours--and--Man alive!--I\par
wanted to blub a bit!  I'm not quite sure that I didn't, you know!"\par
\par
She stretched her arms to the beloved little ghost, whispering:\par
\par
"My poor, poor love, my baby, my treasure!  Mother knows how much it\par
hurt.  But be patient a little longer. Soon--soon--your father and\par
I----"\par
\par
The woe-wave rose and swelled in her bosom, tears began to run over her\par
stiff white face.  The clasped hands she stretched to him were\par
quivering, but she controlled them like the trembling of her voice.\par
\par
"Go back to Paradise, my little son!  Wait patiently, my love, my Angel!\par
I have been wrong, but I will grieve no more!  I will be patient:--O!\par
believe----"\par
\par
A man's footsteps sounded on the staircase and the great shadowy figure\par
of the Doctor appeared behind Bawne's little shape.  With a swift\par
movement Saxham caught up the bewildered boy, made one long stride\par
across the threshold, and put the warm, living treasure into the\par
mother's outstretched arms...\par
\par
\par
Once again big black-lettered contents-bills shrieked from the railings\par
and were worn after the fashion of heralds' tabards by the vendors of\par
newspapers, and the editions were snapped up as fast as they came out.\par
Here are some of the headlines:\par
\par
"THRILLING ESCAPE OF KIDNAPPED BOY SCOUT FROM THE HANDS OF THE HUN.\par
YOUNG HERO OF NORTH SEA ADVENTURE LANDS BEHIND BRITISH LINES AT\par
LANGEBEKE IN TAUBE WITH A BOCHE PRISONER.  FULL STORY OF HOW SCOUT WHO\par
SAVED THE CLANRONALD PAPERS BOMBED THE GERMAN MACHINE-GUNS.  DECORATION\par
OF SCOUT SAXHAM WITH 'GOLDEN WOLF' BADGE BY ROYAL PRESIDENT AT\par
ASSOCIATION HEADQUARTERS.  PROBABLE TESTIMONIAL FROM BRITISH PUBLIC.\par
AFTERNOON TEA WITH THE WAR MINISTER AT WHITEHALL.  EXPECTED INVESTITURE\par
WITH EDWARDIAN ORDER OF MERIT.  WHAT YOU GET BY BEING PREPARED!"\par
\par
\par
And again:\par
\par
"SPLENDID PLUCK OF BRITISH AVIATOR.  FIGHTS ZEPPELIN ON WAY TO BOMB\par
BRITISH HEADQUARTERS.  AIRSHIP CRIPPLED. SHERBRAND R.F.C. KILLED.  FALLS\par
IN FLAMES OVER GERMAN LINES.  HEROIC END OF SOLE REMAINING HEIR TO\par
PENINSULAR WAR EARLDOM, AND INVENTOR OF THE HAWK-HOVERER THAT SOLVES\par
PROBLEM OF STABILITY.  WILL WAR OFFICE ADOPT GREAT INVENTION, EMPLOYED\par
BY ALLIES' FLYING SERVICES?"\par
\par
\par
Three days later:\par
\par
"SHERBRAND R.F.C. RECEIVES POSTHUMOUS HONOURS FROM FRANCE AND BELGIUM.\par
CROIX D'HONNEUR AND ORDER OF LEOPOLD.  WHY NOT BRITISH D.S.O.?"\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXX*\par
\par
                          *A LOVER'S JOURNEY*\par
\par
\par
The crossing--in this Arctic April weather when all of Britain and\par
Belgium and North-West France lay under snowdrifts--had been calm and\par
smooth enough for the worst sea-stomachs on the steamer.  The tall young\par
woman in the Navy blue felt hat with the well-known V.A.D. ribbon, and\par
the long blue serge coat with the Red Cross shield-badge on the left\par
breast, seemed used to travelling alone in War-time. She had secured a\par
dry chair, set in the shelter of the after-deck-saloon, and a lifebelt\par
as stipulated by the authorities, and tucked herself in her\par
travelling-rug with her suit-case under her feet before the lights went\par
out.  Thus she had remained throughout the passage, with her dark eyes\par
looking seawards, as deaf to occasional bursts of uproarious song from a\par
draft of returning Blighties packed on the lower-deck, as to the siren's\par
raucous shrieks.\par
\par
Courteous fellow-passengers, chiefly British and Belgian officers\par
returning from leave, would have been ready enough to have chatted with\par
the young woman who was going to the Front.  Such attentions as they\par
offered her she accepted frankly.  One got her tea and sandwiches,\par
another offered chocolate, another a foot-warmer.  Yet another insisted\par
on lending her an unnecessary extra rug.  They pointed out the hovering\par
Fleet hydroplanes, and the diligently-scouting searchlights of the\par
destroyers guarding the sea-way, and the Hull-bound Dutch liner whose\par
neutrality was proclaimed in illuminated side-letters, blazing like a\par
sea-Alhambra upon the east horizon, and the Hospital ship that passed\par
close, coming from Boulogne laden with wounded, the huge Red Cross upon\par
her flank picked out with blazing green lights.\par
\par
One and all united in assuring the wearer of the V.A.D. uniform that\par
there was no danger.  Though when the red and green eyes on the ends of\par
the East and West jetties winked into sight over the coal-black shining\par
water, her fellow-passengers congratulated Patrine as heartily as though\par
some peril had been escaped.\par
\par
"Nothing more doing, Pinkums, old thing!" said an experienced youngster\par
of twenty to a susceptible senior whom Patrine's unprotected condition\par
had roused to a strong sense of responsibility.  "She's got enough\par
passes from British and French Headquarters to make a poker-hand. I saw\par
her showin' 'em to the authorities at Folkestone. Besides, have heart,\par
there's a Red Tab here to meet her.  We'd better hence it before we're\par
snubbed."\par
\par
And they saluted, and clattered down the crowded gangway, grabbing their\par
valises and buttoning up their British warms, and hurried away to get\par
into trench-kit, webbings, and waders, and swell the crowd in the\par
railway-station--waiting to go up to the Front and carry on with the\par
hourly, momentary game of touch-and-go with Death.\par
\par
While Patrine looked eagerly about her, listening to the hum of the vast\par
human beehive.  This was not the big rambling, old-fashioned French\par
seaport one had known so well before the War.  Under sky-blind\par
arc-lights and red, green, and white lamps, every form of activity\par
imaginable in connection with the running of that now huge and\par
complicated machine, the British Field Army, seemed even at this hour to\par
be in full swing.  The rumble of steam-cranes and the roar of dynamos,\par
the panting of pneumatic hold-dischargers, the clank of couplings, and\par
the shrieks of locomotives mingled with the tinny voices of gramophones\par
from the recreation-rooms at the great packed barracks and crowded\par
camps, and the sounds of song and laughter and applause from music-halls\par
and picture-palaces.\par
\par
"Yes, it goes on most of the time," said the Red Tab who had come to\par
meet Patrine, an officer upon the Staff of the Commandant of a\par
Headquarters not far from--a certain place where Miss Saxham wished to\par
go.  "The Army's got to be rationed and equipped and horsed and foraged,\par
and timbered and coaled and petroled and munitioned, as well as cobbled\par
and engineered and patched and tinkered and nursed--don't you follow me?\par
And these Base Ports are jolly useful.  Nobody goes to bed much, I\par
fancy.  Perhaps they'll make up the sleep they've lost by-and-by, after\par
the War."\par
\par
"What-ho, Nubbins!  Back from the Old Shop?  Sorry!--didn't happen to\par
see you weren't alone!"\par
\par
The station had vomited a flood of khaki, tumbling down the half-lit\par
quays to take later boats by storm.  A tall, lanky officer of Gunners\par
had hailed Red Tab effusively; then, seeing him to be engaged with a\par
lady, hurried on with apologies and a salute for Patrine.\par
\par
"Don't mind me!  Do call back your friend," she urged. "He seemed so\par
glad to see you."\par
\par
"Thanks much.  If you don't mind.  Whewip!  Whewip!"\par
\par
And the other, recalled by a shrill whistle, wheeled and came back upon\par
his stride, to grasp the offered hand. Whereupon, ensued the following\par
strictly private duologue:\par
\par
"How goes the Battery?"\par
\par
"First class.  And your crowd?"\par
\par
"Crawling along as per, usual.  Congrats on the Oudstyde affair!"\par
\par
"Thanks frightfully!  But the whole thing was a bit of a fluke--everyone\par
knows that.  _They_ had thrown down a gas-attack and the wind went\par
about-face.  So we stayed where we were and shelled them through their\par
chlorine. Then they got their Reserves up and came on in lumps--the old\par
Zulu formation--and Pyers and his Engineers got to work with the"--the\par
speaker's voice dropped to an undertone--"what Pyers calls the\par
'Piffbozzler.'"\par
\par
"The rose by any other name----" quoted Red Tab, and went on: "I'd have\par
given a tenner to have been there!--and as for old Clanronald--I wonder\par
if he got leave from--wherever he is--to see the stunt that day?"\par
\par
Said the Gunner:\par
\par
"If he did--and had such a thing as a stomach about him, he must have\par
simply--vomited!  Pyers says he felt like the Angel with the Flaming\par
Sword--when he didn't feel like an Indian jeweller with a\par
blowpipe--frizzling a column of white ants marching over the floor.\par
You've seen how the things come on and on----"\par
\par
"Yahgh!" remarked Red Tab expressively.\par
\par
"But--just for once--we didn't happen to be on the frizzled side.  The\par
C. in C. has laughed to the verge of hysterics over a leader in the\par
Berlin _Lokal Anzeiger_, with reference to the realised dream of the\par
'homicidal maniac' Clanronald.  'A deplorable example of the perversion\par
of _Die Wissenschaft_ at the murderous hands of English military\par
chemists,' they called it.  Pretty neat from Boches who've been pumping\par
burning paraffin into our trenches, and suffocating platoons of men with\par
asphyxiating gases, ever since May."\par
\par
"And particularly appropriate from people who bribed a crack Professor\par
of Literature to engage as librarian at Gwyll Castle--set the Library\par
Wing on fire and steal the portfolio with the plans of the 'homicidal\par
maniac' three weeks before the War--when Prinz Heinrich and old Moltke\par
were stopping in London.  They'd promised their agent twelve million\par
marks if he succeeded.  Wonder what he got from them when the plot\par
fizzled out?  Well, so-long!  Any message for Edith?'\par
\par
"Tell her you saw me topping, and remember me to your wife!"\par
\par
And they gripped hands and parted, and Red Tab hurried back to the tall\par
young woman waiting on the flagstones under a blue shaded arc-lamp,\par
saying:\par
\par
"Good of you not to mind.  But a shame to keep you waiting.  No--we go\par
out at this gate.  I've got a car waiting.  More cushy than a crowded\par
railway-carriage--unless you'd have preferred going by train?"\par
\par
The grey landaulette waiting in the side-street presented no more\par
unusual feature than unusually heavy armoured tyres, and a guard of\par
razor-edged steel bars protecting the front seat.\par
\par
"In case of barbed wire--strung across country roads," explained Red\par
Tab.  "One runs a chance of getting decapitated--travelling fast at\par
night--or in foggy weather--without a jigger of this sort.  Let me stick\par
this cushion at your back and tuck the rugs about you.  There's a\par
Thermos in the pocket with hot coffee--and sandwiches in a box.  Don't\par
restrain your appyloose if you feel at all hungry!  The grub was put in\par
specially for you.  No: you won't hear the guns yet, except at\par
intervals, and rather faintly.  Fact--I've heard 'em in the South of\par
England more distinctly than one does here!  But at St. O--,\par
twenty-eight miles from the Front--they're loud enough at times--though\par
there's nothing much doing.  Things have been as dull as ditch-water and\par
none of us'll be sorry when the Boches get a move on again.  No--thanks,\par
I'm not coming inside!  Responsible for your safety.  Advise you to tuck\par
up and go to by-by!"\par
\par
The car settled into its speed when the ups and downs of the old town\par
had been left behind, and the belated activities of the Base Port had\par
died into a distant hum.  It slackened pace when the blaze of its\par
headlights showed long black columns of laden motor-lorries upon the\par
wintry roads ahead of it--or horse-drawn transport waggons--or droves of\par
animals, the steam of whose breath and shaggy hides hung over them in a\par
cloud--or bodies of men in heavy marching order--French and British\par
soldiers wearing the new steel headpiece,--shaped after the fashion of\par
Mambrino's helmet, like a basin turned upside down.\par
\par
And sometimes there were the halts at barriers or patrol-posts near\par
towns or villages, where the light of swung lanterns reddened the\par
moustached faces of gendarmes of Chasseurs.  But usually when Patrine\par
cleared a space upon the misty window-glass, the snow-covered landscape\par
would be flying past under the fitful moonlight, with the elongated\par
shadow of the grey Staff car galloping beside it like a demon dog.\par
\par
Midnight was striking from an ancient church-tower when, passing the\par
guarded barriers of a town of old-world houses, and stopping in a street\par
running from a Place bathed in frosty moonlight, and dominated by a vast\par
cathedral, Red Tab, with icicles on his clipped moustache and fur\par
collar, got down and tapped upon the rimy glass.\par
\par
"Sorry to wake you up, Miss Saxham!" he said, opening the door as\par
Patrine sat up, straightened the dented brim of her hat and blinked\par
denial of her slumberousness, "but here's the end of your journey.  This\par
is the Ursuline Convent of St. O--, where we've arranged for you to\par
billet to-night. The Superioress is a frightfully hospitable old lady,\par
and my uncle--I mean Sir Roland--thought you'd be more cushy with the\par
Sisters than at a common hotel!"\par
\par
\par
"Sir Roland is always kind.  But you, Captain Smyth-Howell?"  She looked\par
out at her red-tabbed escort with compunction as he tugged at the chain\par
of a clanging bell, and beat his mittened hands together, stamping upon\par
the pavement to warm his frozen feet.\par
\par
"Me?  Oh, I'm pushing on to Divisional Headquarters--twenty-five miles\par
from this place and five miles north of the Belgian frontier.  You'll be\par
sent on to Pophereele in the morning, first thing.  The French Chaplain\par
of the Red Cross Hospital there is staying for the night with the Bishop\par
at the Palace here.  A tremendously agreeable old bird the Chaplain--and\par
a Monsignore of the Vatican.  I've met him--and he said he'd be\par
delighted to look after you.  Don't get down--it's frightfully\par
slippery!"\par
\par
But the tall, womanly figure was already standing beside him on the\par
snowy cobblestones, tilting a round white chin towards the sky, and\par
narrowing long eyes--"queer eyes" he mentally termed them--to see the\par
better through her veil.\par
\par
"What glorious stars!"\par
\par
He liked the soft warmth of her voice, as he answered:\par
\par
"Magnificent, aren't they?  Look at Draco blazing away, high over the\par
north transept of the Cathedral.  And that would be Aquila--I rather\par
fancy--lowish on the horizon, over that ruined tower.  That's a bit of\par
their famous Abbey----"\par
\par
"Great Scott!"\par
\par
"Did anything startle you?" he asked.  "You said----"\par
\par
"I know I said it, but I didn't mean to.  There, again--"  She pointed\par
as forked tongues of pale rainbow-tinted fire leaped up from the\par
northern horizon, throwing into momentary relief the Cathedral's stately\par
bulk and the huddled housetops.\par
\par
"Those are Boche fireworks!"\par
\par
"Fireworks?"\par
\par
"Star-shell, rockets, and so forth.  They regularly treat us to a\par
display before they begin to pound us again.  Where are we fighting?\par
Oh, pretty busy north--as far as Ypres and as far south as La Bassee.\par
French on our right--French and Belgians on the left of us.  More French\par
holding Verdun.  My hat! what gorgeous fighters!  Men of steel with\par
muscles of vulcanised rubber.  And we thought the Gaul an\par
absinthe-drinking degenerate.  I tell you we wanted this War to open our\par
eyes for us.  Perhaps they did too!  Here's one of the Sisters coming\par
now!"\par
\par
Hurrying felt slippers with rope soles shuffled over stone pavements.\par
The key grated and the bolts shot back.  A little Sister Portress in a\par
close guimpe and flowing black veil, with a blue-checked apron tied over\par
her habit, swung back the heavy door, holding her lantern high.\par
\par
Just Heaven, upon how cold a night Madame had arrived from England!\par
Madame must be perished.  But there was coffee, and soup _tres chaud_\par
not only for Madame but for M. l'Officier.  And also the chauffeur.\par
Madame _la Superieure_ would never permit that either should proceed\par
without nourishment.  If M. l'Officier and his attendant preferred not\par
to enter, the Sister would wait upon them in the car.\par
\par
And so Patrine, after taking leave of her red-tabbed escort, was led\par
away to the Mother Superior, a little, bright-eyed, kindly Religious,\par
full of solicitude for Mademoiselle, who, confessing to having emptied a\par
Thermos of hot coffee, and a box of sandwiches during the later stages\par
of the transit, was borne away from the guest's refectory up and down\par
several crooked flights of ancient stairs to a white-washed apartment,\par
containing a _prie-dieu_ and a big plaster Crucifix, a great walnut bed\par
with faded _Directoire_ curtains, a minute washstand,--a faint smell of\par
scorched wood, emanating from the perforated metal registers of a\par
_calorifere_, and a bad little coloured print of Lord Roberts, within a\par
stitched border of yellow _immortelles_ and faded laurel-leaves, that\par
had been green and fresh six months before....\par
\par
Patrine spent a white night in the town where the old brave heart of the\par
great soldier had given its last throb for England.  Not because those\par
thudding guns in the north and east kept her wakeful--or because she had\par
never stayed in a convent before.\par
\par
She was going to Sherbrand--her Flying Man--who had been supposed to be\par
dead and found to be living,--and who had written to say that he did not\par
want Patrine.  The letter lay against her heart, and her hands were\par
folded tightly over it, as she lay staring with shining eyes at the\par
drawn curtains flapping in the chill breeze stinging through the open\par
window that had been fastened with a nail when the English guest\par
arrived.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                             *CHAPTER LXXI*\par
\par
                           *LIVING AND DEAD*\par
\par
\par
"PATHETIC ECHO OF AIR-TRAGEDY.  SHERBRAND, R.F.C., NOT DEAD OR PRISONER.\par
RESCUED BY AMERICAN RED CROSS AMBULANCE.  IN HOSPITAL NEAR YPRES.  WILL\par
RECOVER, BUT BLIND FOR LIFE."\par
\par
\par
The clamorous headlines had followed close on a telephone from Sir\par
Roland.  Patrine had learned what it means to cry for joy--an\par
unforgettable experience.  She had discovered that one who kneels down\par
to thank God for a boon so marvellous, has no words left to offer Him,\par
nor even tears and sighs.\par
\par
She had written again and again to Sherbrand, saying only "_Let me come\par
to you!_"  Passionate, pitiful, tender letters, answered after weeks of\par
delay by one page in the stiff, neat handwriting of the American Red\par
Cross Nursing Sister who acted as amanuensis for the blind man.\par
\par
\par
"_April_, 1915.\par
\par
"You have said that you wish to visit me in my blindness. I thank you\par
for the expressed desire, but I cannot receive you here!  I have never\par
been the kind of man who bid for pity from women, and the ties that you\par
broke, voluntarily, six months ago, I do not wish to renew.  My mother\par
has been here to bring me some things"--the French and Belgian\par
decorations, guessed Patrine--"and has gone away again.  She understands\par
that it is best for me to remain here, because, although the War is over\par
as far as I am actively concerned, I can hear the guns and breathe the\par
breath of battle, and know when the 'planes pass overhead, and follow\par
them in thought.  There is little else a blind man can do, except make\par
toys or baskets!  Do not think me bitter or discontented--I am\par
neither--quite O.K. I wish people had been told I brought down the\par
Zepp., that's all!  With gratitude for your kind and friendly\par
remembrance,\par
\par
"Yours most sincerely,\par
       "A. S."\par
\par
\par
A formal letter, but between the cold, stiff lines Patrine had read\par
reproach, and love, and yearning.  An unkind letter--but could she judge\par
him harshly, her poor blind eagle, sitting in darkness never to be\par
lifted, listening to the guns, and the battle-song of the Birds of War,\par
drifting down out of "his sky"?\par
\par
There was Mass in the Convent chapel at seven next morning.  A military\par
chaplain offered the Divine Sacrifice, and the rush-bottomed chairs were\par
occupied by soldiers, French Chasseurs and Zouaves, Senegalese and\par
Negroes, English Guards and Irish Fusiliers, Highlanders and a German or\par
two,--all patients from the Hospital under the management of the\par
Ursuline Sisters--a big building next door to the Convent, that had been\par
a young ladies' boarding school in the days before the War.\par
\par
The chapel was a dusky place.  So dusky that though the red carnations\par
and white Eucharis lilies in the Altar vases struck vivid notes of\par
colour in the light of the Altar candles, the ruby spark of the\par
Sanctuary lamp and the bright flame of the Paschal candle were barely\par
visible in the brooding gloom.  You could only tell the place to be\par
crowded, by the deep-toned chorus of masculine voices joining fervently\par
in the _Confiteor_ and _Credo_.  Pale green flashes momentarily lit up\par
the crimson and purple and tawny tracery of the round east window, and\par
the distant thudding of the guns at the Front made an accompaniment to\par
the sacred rite.\par
\par
The French priest officiating was a lean, short, elderly personage with\par
brilliant eyes set in a mask of walnut-brown wrinkles and a resonant\par
voice that was illustrated by beautiful, illuminating gestures as he\par
preached.\par
\par
"Let none say in your hearing, unrebuked, that this War is an unrelieved\par
misfortune," he said to his hearers. "Recognize with me, my French\par
compatriots, the Divine Mercy as extended particularly to France in this\par
fiery ordeal! Her towns and villages have been destroyed,--her buildings\par
have been shattered, her sons in countless thousands slain, but her\par
national character has been purified--the soul of her people has been\par
raised from the mire.  If there is one here present among you--whatever\par
may be his nationality,--who is conscious of loving Virtue better and\par
loathing Vice more intensely, since the beginning of this War--then the\par
War has been a blessing--to him--and not a curse! Acts have been\par
performed--and are repeated hourly--acts of a sublime and touching\par
selflessness and an almost Divine tenderness,--not only by men and women\par
who are mild and gentle, but by the roughest and the most abandoned of\par
either sex.  The good seed was sown in time of peace--ah yes, my\par
children! but it might have perished.  And now Our Lord, who loves\par
flowers, has caused these pure and exquisite blossoms to spring for Him\par
from the field of War."\par
\par
After his tiny sermon, delivered in French, and repeated in English, he\par
hesitated a moment before turning to the Altar and said, with emotion in\par
his mobile face and quick utterance:\par
\par
"I have to ask a favour of you this morning.  It is that at the\par
Commemoration of the Departed you will unite with me in a mental act of\par
prayer.  Prayer for the soul of one to whom the gift of Faith, not being\par
sought, was not given. A soul that has passed forth in darkness into the\par
presence of Him who is the Light."\par
\par
He turned away and began the _Credo_.  As the deep chorus of male voices\par
followed, Patrine found herself agreeing with the preacher's discourse.\par
\par
"What was it," she asked herself, "that led me out from overheated,\par
crowded rooms, oppressive with the scent of flowers and perfumes of\par
triple extract--where the Tango and the Turkey Trot were being danced by\par
half-clad, painted women and effeminate young men--and set my feet upon\par
a mountain-slope with the free winds of heaven blowing upon me?  I must\par
answer--It was the War!"\par
\par
As the great waves of the _Credo_ surged and beat against the old brown\par
rafters she went on thinking:\par
\par
"What has made me quicken to the call of Humanity--awakened me to the\par
knowledge of my sisterhood with my fellow-women?  What has taught me how\par
to live without dissipation and do without useless luxuries?  Again--the\par
War!  And oh! what has taught me the meaning of Love in all its fulness,\par
and set within the shrine of my heart this great sacred sorrow, and\par
kindled in my soul the pure altar-flame of Faith?  The War, the terrible\par
War!"\par
\par
She prayed for Sherbrand at the Commemoration of the Living!  A somewhat\par
incoherent petition that her Flying Man might be helped to bear his\par
blindness, and find some happiness in her unchanged love.  And the\par
thought of the dead Agnostic haunted her.  Who was the man, and what had\par
brought about his ending?  Was he a patient in the Ursuline Hospital?\par
\par
A French, an English, or a German soldier?  By a subtle change in her\par
mental purview, recollections of von Herrnung began to occupy her mind.\par
\par
"I will not think of him!--I will not!" she said to herself desperately.\par
Then the obsession assumed an acute form. All that she most wished to\par
forget in her relations with the Kaiser's Flying Man was being revived\par
in her memory. Scene by scene, sentence by sentence, she was forced to\par
live over the hated Past again.\par
\par
She must have risen from her knees and left the chapel, so unbearable\par
became the torment, but that the sacring bell rang its triples, the deep\par
tones of the _Sanctus_ answered from the turret, and the Host was lifted\par
up.  Then her tense nerves relaxed.  The almost tangible presence of\par
evil withdrew itself.  She breathed more freely, and peace flowed in\par
balmy waves upon her stormy soul.  In prayer for herself and those who\par
were most dear to her, she lost the sense of the unseen hands plucking\par
at her garments and the soundless voice whispering at her ear.  And\par
presently at the _Ipsis Domine_, when supplication is made by priests\par
and people for the departed, she prayed for the soul of the Denier--that\par
the Divine Mercy might reach and enfold him, and lead him yet into the\par
Way of Peace.\par
\par
"_Christ is risen who created all things, and who hath had pity upon\par
mankind....  Purchased people, declare His virtues, alleluia!  Who hath\par
called you out of darkness into His admirable light._"\par
\par
To Patrine the Call had come.\par
\par
It was Easter Week and there were many communicants. The nuns and the\par
French and English Red Cross nurses helped the lame to reach the\par
Altar-rails and guided the blind.  When a tall, blond young English\par
Officer with bandaged eyes and an empty sleeve was led up to his\par
Master's Table, Patrine was grateful that the chapel was so dusk.\par
\par
She was to meet the Chaplain of the Pophereele Stationary Hospital after\par
Mass, the Mother Superioress had said. Thus, guided by an Ursuline\par
Sister, she passed from the chapel into a long, whitewashed cloister\par
looking on the garden, its open arches facing the doors of what had been\par
class-rooms, and now were wards.  Another Ursuline, the Sister\par
Superintendent of the Hospital, with a young, gentle face framed in her\par
close white guimpe and flowing black veil, sat writing in a big book at\par
a plain deal table.  Near her were some shelves with rows of bottles and\par
a chest of drawers with measuring-glasses upon it, and a pestle and\par
mortar and druggists' scales.  Above the table a black wooden Crucifix\par
hung against the whitewashed wall.\par
\par
"This is Soeur Catherine, who keeps the Hospital accounts and dispenses\par
the medicines, and posts the register in which we set down the names of\par
all the wounded received and discharged.  Take care, Mademoiselle!  That\par
paint is new and comes off!" cried the chaperoning Sister, snatching\par
aside the skirt of Patrine's long blue V.A.D. coat.\par
\par
She had brushed, in passing, against a wooden tablet that leaned against\par
the wall near the door through which she had come.  A big square of\par
black-painted deal surmounted by a gabled and eaved Cross of German\par
pattern, and bearing an inscription in white Gothic lettering:\par
\par
                           "HIER RUHT IM GOTT\par
                   EIN DEUTSCHER FLIEGENDE OFFIZIER."\par
\par
\par
"That is for the grave of the German officer who died yesterday.  One of\par
the Bavarian soldiers is painting it.  He has not finished--he has only\par
gone away for a moment to get some more _ceruse_ from Mother Madeleine."\par
\par
Sister Catherine offered the explanation.  She added, as the tall\par
English girl glanced at something that lay on the deal table beside the\par
register:\par
\par
"That is his flying-cap, poor man! and the belt that shows his _rang\par
militaire_.  They will be placed upon the pall when they carry him to\par
the cemetery.  But pardon!  One should have observed before that\par
Mademoiselle was suffering! What!  Mademoiselle is not ill, not even a\par
little fatigued?  Then what Mademoiselle needs is a _petit dejeuner_."\par
\par
And Patrine was whisked away to the guest's refectory to be refreshed\par
with _pistolets_ and coffee.  Monseigneur would follow a little later.\par
Madame la Superieure had arranged for Monseigneur to take _dejeuner_\par
with M. l'Aumonier. Later, Monseigneur hoped for the pleasure of meeting\par
the English Mademoiselle.\par
\par
Mademoiselle's tall rounded figure, ushered by the little active\par
Ursuline Sister, had barely passed through the glazed swing-doors\par
leading from the cloister to the Convent, when the short, spare, elderly\par
priest who had celebrated Mass entered from the chapel, followed by the\par
Convent Aumonier, who had served him at the altar.  Even as the nun rose\par
from her table, the vividly clear eyes of Monseigneur, set in the mask\par
of dry walnut-brown wrinkles, dropped on the painted head-board propped\par
against the wall.\par
\par
"That is for him?"\par
\par
The supple right hand of Monseigneur waved towards the chapel, then\par
extended itself to the Sister, who curtsied and kissed his amethyst\par
ring.\par
\par
"For him, Monseigneur," answered the Aumonier, to whom the question had\par
been addressed.\par
\par
"_Dieu veuille avoir son ame!_"\par
\par
The left sleeve of Monseigneur's decidedly rusty serge soutane bore the\par
well-known brassard.  Its scarlet and white peeped between the folds of\par
his heavy black mantle as he made the Sign of the Cross.\par
\par
"His name is missing from the inscription," he commented, producing a\par
battered silver snuff-box and helping himself to a generous pinch.\par
"Why, might one demand?"\par
\par
"The initials will be painted in presently, Monseigneur. There will be\par
no name--by desire of the deceased!"\par
\par
"He preferred anonymity?"  The amethyst ring of Monseigneur's prelacy\par
flashed violet as he dusted the brown powder from his upper-lip with a\par
blue checked handkerchief.  "The Pere Aumonier tells me," his\par
startlingly clear eyes were on the Sister, "that terrible as were his\par
injuries, he might have recovered--that his death occurred suddenly and\par
unexpectedly."\par
\par
"But yes, Monseigneur, he might have recovered!"  The fair face framed\par
in the narrow guimpe was shadowed and troubled.  "The _coup d'obus_ had\par
spared the brain, arteries, and vertebra.  His sight was uninjured--M.\par
le Commandant and his colleagues had achieved wonders in the partial\par
restoration of the visage.  Speech was difficult--but we could\par
understand him--unless he was sullen and would only speak German to us.\par
But at those times a Bavarian soldier interpreted--he who has painted\par
the headboard for the grave."\par
\par
"He--the German officer--was grateful to those who nursed him?" inquired\par
Monseigneur of the Aumonier.\par
\par
The stout little Chaplain visibly hesitated.  It was the Sister who\par
answered in her clear and gentle voice:\par
\par
"Alas! no, Monseigneur!  He was arrogant, even brutal. But then--he\par
suffered so terribly, in mind as in body--one could not be angry at\par
anything he said.  He could not resign himself to his disfigured\par
condition.  It was intolerable, he would cry, that he should now be an\par
object of horror to women--women who had worshipped him almost as a\par
god!"\par
\par
"Chut--chut!  Eh--well!  One presumes he meant a certain type of women,"\par
observed Monseigneur.\par
\par
"Possibly so, Monseigneur."  The simplicity of the fair face in the\par
narrow guimpe was touching.  "For when we assured him that we did not\par
regard him with horror he would say to us: 'That makes nothing!  I speak\par
of women. You are only nuns.'"\par
\par
"But nuns are women," objected Monseigneur.\par
\par
"Monseigneur, he said not.  When his condition seemed to him most\par
miserable he found relief in saying things--abusive--outrageous--about\par
nuns.  We didn't mind.  We pitied him--poor Number Twenty!  But the\par
French and English officers in the same ward resented this.  They\par
entreated us to remove him to a separate room.  This we did, and at his\par
request the Bavarian was placed in the same apartment--he has been an\par
officer's servant--and is active and useful, even though he has lost a\par
leg.  Thus things went better.  Poor Twenty seemed more contented.  He\par
even looked forward to leaving the Hospital!"\par
\par
"And then?  A change?--a relapse?" suggested Monseigneur.\par
\par
"A change.  He became more gloomy--more violent after a letter arrived\par
for him from England at the _Jour des morts_.  Since two days comes\par
another letter.  We heard him raving of perfidy, the folly of his\par
agents--the injustice of his Emperor--the revenge upon the Englishwoman\par
that he would never have now! ... Then all was quiet.  Towards morning\par
the Bavarian came out of the room and called an orderly.  The Herr\par
Hauptmann was sleeping, he said, in such a queer way....  From that\par
unnatural stupor he never awakened.  All his letters and papers were\par
torn up and scattered in fragments.  There was a little cardboard box on\par
the night-table and a pencil _billet_ for me.  I am to send a ring he\par
always wore to the address of a noble young lady at Berlin.  She was his\par
_fiancee_, I believe, Monseigneur.  He thanks me for the little I have\par
been able to do for him!--he begs the Sisters to pardon his rudeness....\par
He wishes no name upon his grave--but to be forgotten.... Poor broken\par
body--poor rebellious heart--poor stubborn, desperate soul!"\par
\par
"You think, then, that--he killed himself?" asked Monseigneur with\par
directness.\par
\par
"I dare not think!"  She was searching in her table drawer with tears\par
dropping on her hands.  "I can only pray that the autopsy of the surgeon\par
will not reveal that the death was not natural.  Look,\par
Monseigneur!--this is his ring.  A big black-and-white pearl.  And under\par
the pearl, which lifts up--is a little box for something....  A relic\par
perhaps--or a portrait, or a lock of a friend's hair."\par
\par
"It might serve as a reliquary--at need, my child," said Monseigneur,\par
examining the platinum setting.  He gave one swift glance at the\par
unsuspicious Aumonier and another at the innocent nun.  He peered again\par
narrowly at the empty hiding-place, to the shallow sides of which a few\par
atoms of glittering grey dust were adhering.  He lifted the ring to his\par
nose and sniffed, tapped the little box on his thumb-nail, and touched\par
his tongue to one of the glittering grey specks.  Then he hastily spat\par
in his handkerchief, and thunder-clouds sat on the furrowed forehead\par
over the great hooked beak.\par
\par
"Listen!"\par
\par
The nun started and grew paler still.  She hurried to the glazed doors\par
opening on the garden and threw them wide apart.  As the chill outer air\par
rushed in, sporting with the scant white locks of M. l'Aumonier,\par
fluttering the purple lappets at the throat of Monseigneur, and tugging\par
as with invisible hands at the Sister's thin black veil, approaching\par
footsteps crunched over the sloppy gravel of the cloister walk.\par
\par
\par
The small stout figure of the Sister-Keeper of the mortuary headed the\par
small, solemn procession.  She held up her habit out of the slush, and\par
carried as well as a mammoth iron doorkey, a small bunch of spring\par
flowers.\par
\par
A stretcher-squad of the French Red Cross followed the Sister of the\par
mortuary.  In life the man they bore must have been a magnificent\par
specimen of humanity.  In death the length of his rigid form appeared\par
phenomenal.  The black velvet pall, over which had been draped the\par
black-red-white German War Ensign, was far too short to cover the stiff\par
blanket-swathed feet.  That they projected beyond the stretcher-end with\par
an effect of arrogance and obstinacy, was the thought that occurred to\par
one of the three people gathered in a little group upon the threshold of\par
the cloister-doors.\par
\par
"Monseigneur....  My Father! ..."  Sister Catherine was speaking in\par
suppressed but eager accents.  "It is Number Twenty.  They are taking\par
him to the mortuary. The Sister-Keeper promised to carry flowers as a\par
sign that all was well.  You understand, do you not?  The surgeons have\par
decided--thanks be to God!--that the poor man did not poison himself!"\par
\par
She dropped to her knees and began to say a decade of her Rosary, the\par
wooden beads running between her fingers like brown water as she prayed.\par
The priests made the Sign of the Cross silently as the body was borne\par
past.  When the last feather of the Black Eagle had vanished, and the\par
crunching of footsteps on sloppy gravel had thinned away in distance,\par
the nun rose.\par
\par
"You feel happier now, my sister, do you not?" Monseigneur asked kindly.\par
\par
"Much happier, Monseigneur," she said, "for now I may pray for him!"\par
\par
Monseigneur, who had retained the ring, shut the hiding-place with a\par
decided click, snapped into its slot the end of the bar that held the\par
magpie pearl in place, and said as he restored the bauble to the nun:\par
\par
"Who knows but that some ray of Divine Grace may yet shine upon that\par
darkened soul!  Do as the owner begged of you, and pray for him by all\par
means!"\par
\par
"That I will!" she said fervently.  "And you also, will you not pray for\par
him? the poor, proud Pagan who believed no resurrection possible--unless\par
one were to exist again as a vapour or a tree.  Alas!  I fear I have\par
sinned much in yielding to the feeling he inspired in me!"\par
\par
She added, meeting the keen glance of Monseigneur's vivid eyes:\par
\par
"The feeling of repugnance.  Of horror, Monseigneur! Here comes the\par
Bavarian to finish the inscription.  Well, my good Kuehler, you have got\par
some more ceruse?"\par
\par
The glass-doors had been darkened by the shape of a one-legged man on\par
crutches, a black-haired, swarthy fellow dressed in the maroon flannel\par
uniform distinctive of the Hospital.  A little pot with a brush in it\par
dangled from one of his big fingers.  He glanced up under his heavy\par
brows, with a muttered word as he passed the Sister, and returned the\par
greeting of Monseigneur with a clumsy attempt at a salute.\par
\par
"You are better?  You are getting on?" said Monseigneur to him in\par
German.\par
\par
"Better, _mein Vater_, and getting on."\par
\par
"That is well!  And you have only a little bit to do, and then your work\par
is done?"\par
\par
"Done, _mein Vater!_" echoed the one-legged man.\par
\par
He went to the head-board where it was near the door leading to the\par
chapel, leaned his crutches against the wall, and began cautiously and\par
painfully to let himself down. Monseigneur and the Aumonier hurried to\par
his assistance, saw him safely squatted upon his folded sack, took leave\par
of the Sister, who knelt to receive the blessing of the hand that wore\par
the amethyst ring,--and vanished through the farther door at the urgent\par
summons of a bell.\par
\par
The Sister turned again to her big ledger.  A list of articles\par
appertaining to the deceased would have to be checked and verified.  Two\par
pairs of binoculars--surely the one bearing the name and address of an\par
officer in a British Guards regiment ought to be sent to the Allies'\par
Headquarters at St. O--.  Two purses, one full of English sovereigns, a\par
stout roll of French bank-notes in a pigskin case, and so forth.  When\par
next she looked round, the Bavarian was wiping his brushes.  The\par
finished inscription now stood:\par
\par
                           "HIER RUHT IM GOTT\par
                    EIN DEUTSCHER FLIEGENDE OFFIZIER\par
                               _T. v. H._\par
                             30 YAHRE ALT."\par
\par
\par
"You are sorry for him, are you not, my good Kuehler?" the nun asked\par
mildly as the Bavarian scrambled to his solitary foot, and stood\par
supporting himself against the wall.\par
\par
"Sorry, my Sister?"  He spoke in thick Teutonic French, and looked at\par
her under his lowering black brows as he reached his crutches out of the\par
corner and tucked them under his arms.  "Why should I be sorry?  He's\par
dead--and so an end of him.  _Total kaput_ for another officer!"  He\par
saluted the Sister and stumped out.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
                            *CHAPTER LXXII*\par
\par
                         *LOVE THAT HAS WINGS*\par
\par
\par
Under a blue sky--the forget-me-not blue of April--tiny blizzards--mere\par
dust of snow--alternated with slashes of sleet.  The road running east\par
from Pophereele was villainous; bad _pave_ in the centre, and on either\par
side morasses of mud from which rose at irregular intervals, scraggy\par
poplars hacked by shell-fire and barked by the impact of innumerable\par
iron-shod wheels.\par
\par
An almost continuous line of transports bumped over the abominable\par
_pave_.  Staff cars with British Brass Hats and red French _kepis_\par
gold-braided, motor-guns and caissons, motor-lorries, motor-ambulances,\par
motor-cyclists, pedestrians--chiefly Belgian peasants in tall peaked\par
caps and long blue blouses, caked to the knees in sticky mire. Odd\par
detachments of French Artillery, a squadron of Chasseurs in the new\par
uniform of sallow blue--a half-battalion of magnificent, singing\par
Canadians, loaded on the dark green motor-buses that used to run from\par
Holloway to Westminster Bridge.\par
\par
Where French police were posted at cross-roads and a working-party of\par
British Engineers were mending the highway--filling up shell-pits, and\par
the cunningly-concealed emplacements where a battery of French 75's had\par
been in action a few months before, and the shrapnel-riddled houses of a\par
small village yet harboured a few wizened Flemish peasants, was the\par
point whence you first caught sight of the towers of the ancient capital\par
of Western Flanders, rising above a bank of grey mist, sucked from the\par
thawing earth by the warmth of the April sun.\par
\par
An historic city of gabled houses, a city on a river long lost and\par
vaulted over--a city as famous through its industries of cloth-weaving,\par
and the exquisite manufacture of cob-webby lace of Valenciennes, as\par
precious to students of Art and Literature by reason of its stirring\par
history, and the wonders it enshrined.  A matchless city, the glory of\par
Flandre Occident, with its Cloth Hall of the marvellous Early Gothic\par
facades, its Renaissance _Nieuwerk_ and ancient _Stedehuus_, its\par
glorious cathedral on the north opposite the Halles, with the unfinished\par
tower by Marten Untenhove, and the triumphal arch in the West porch by\par
Urban Taillebert.\par
\par
Since October, 1914, when a British Brigade with two battalions of\par
another B.B., had successfully withstood the desperate attacks of the\par
flower of the Prussian Imperial Guard, the beautiful old city had\par
suffered bombardment, furious, purposeful, desultory, or intermittent,\par
from the enemy's 11.2-in. long range Krupps.  That First Battle--fought\par
upon a line extending from a few miles north-east of the city--had been\par
succeeded after the partial lull of winter, by a second, a stubborn and\par
sanguinary renewal of the struggle, rendered hideous by the use of the\par
Boche's trump-card, flaming oil-jets and asphyxiating gas.\par
\par
Now the pride of Flandre Occident stood as it stands to-day, like the\par
heart of a martyr calcined but unconsumed in the cold ashes of the pyre.\par
Its sad and stately dignity was marvellously beautiful, under the blue\par
April sky, with its lashes of wintry sleet.  Its gardens were dressed in\par
green spring livery, the grass was peeping between the cobble-stones,\par
the scorched and broken chestnut-trees that had shaded the promenades on\par
the site of its ancient ramparts were thrusting out their pinky-brown\par
finger-like buds. And above the shell-pitted waste of uncut brass now\par
representing the Plaine d'Amour,--where the reviews used to take place\par
and the Kermesses, and athletic Club competitions--where the aerodrome\par
is cut by the line of the canal that receives the waters of the\par
subterranean river--a lark was singing joyfully as it climbed its airy\par
spiral, and a blind man was standing by the twisted ruins of a British\par
aeroplane drinking in the music that rained from the sky.\par
\par
In the battered Rue d'Elverdinghe, behind a block of the ruined prison,\par
the car that had brought Sherbrand waited. A grey car with the Red Cross\par
and a miniature replica of Old Glory on the bonnet.  The Belgian\par
chauffeur smoked cigarettes and read the _Independence Belge_\par
industriously; the American V.A.D. orderly smoked also, surveying the\par
wreckage at the end of the wide thoroughfare, between whose gaunt and\par
roofless walls was revealed a vista of the Grand Place,--where the west\par
facade of the Cathedral reared, a calcined skeleton above the ruined\par
Halles,--and the Belfry whose massiveness defied the genii of\par
destruction for a few weeks to come.  Yet he kept his eye on his charge,\par
solicitously.  No creature is so utterly unaided by the senses, so\par
pathetically defenceless as a recently blind man.\par
\par
Drives were part of the treatment prescribed for Sherbrand by the\par
American surgeon of the Hospital at Pophereele. The chauffeur and the\par
attendant were instructed to humour him, and his humour craved solitude\par
and the sense of space. This excursion to the plain lying north-west of\par
the stricken city where Death and Ruin were Burgomaster and Bishop was\par
not the first by several.  The few remaining inhabitants--the pale women\par
who made lace in the shelter of broken doorways, the feeble old folks\par
from the almshouses, who peered from their cellar-refuges at the crunch\par
and grind of armoured wheels upon the bricks and timbers heaped upon the\par
littered thoroughfares--dully wondered at these visits of the blind\par
Englishman.\par
\par
They had seen many strange things of late, the red-eyed, meagre,\par
ague-bitten old people, since that day in early October when fifteen\par
thousand Kaisermen, chanting the German War Song, had defiled for six\par
mortal hours through the streets of their ancient town.\par
\par
"There are a great many of you gentlemen," some of the old folks had\par
ventured to say.\par
\par
"That may be so," they had been told, "but we have millions waiting to\par
follow.  We are sure to win; the French are cowards, and the English\par
stupid fools.  As for you--you are now all Belgo-Germans, our Kaiser has\par
said so! When we leave here we are going to Calais, Paris next, and then\par
London--it's nothing at all to get to London in our magnificent\par
Zeppelins!"\par
\par
Then suddenly the Germans had gone away--and with them trains of waggons\par
crammed with booty.  A week later, amidst the vivas of the people,\par
twenty-one thousand British had poured into the town.  They had rolled\par
down the streets like a tawny river singing lustily:\par
\par
    "Here we are--here we are--here we are again!\par
      Hallo!  Hallo!  Hallo!  HALLO!"\par
\par
\par
And the crowd had been quick to catch up the chorus, responding:\par
\par
    "Eeeweea--eeweea--eeweea--eggain!\par
      Allo!  Allo!  Allo!  ALLO!"\par
\par
\par
And the British Headquarters had established itself in its spider-web of\par
Intelligence at the house of the Burgomaster, and the very next day a\par
Boche aviator had tried to drop a bomb on it, and had been winged by a\par
clever shot from an anti-aircraft gun, and brought crashing down on the\par
Plaine d'Amour.  And there had been rejoicings on the part of the young\par
people who were thoughtless.  But the wise old folks had known quite\par
well that many more Taubes would come.\par
\par
What an autumn it had been, dear Lord! thought the trembling old people.\par
The first Sunday in August, with its decorations, processions, hymns,\par
and litanies, all in honour of Our Lady of Thuyn, had been turned into a\par
demonstration of penitents.  The Kermesse had been prohibited with the\par
other festivities.  No use baking honey-cakes and marzipan.  Nobody\par
would have bought them.  The Yprais were too busy listening to the\par
distant firing of terribly great guns. All the window-panes rattled and\par
shivered, and the earth vibrated without ceasing.  Each morning brought\par
dreadful news, contradicted every afternoon, and confirmed at night.\par
Towns bombarded, townsmen shot, hung, or burned, children and\par
women--even nuns--violated and murdered. Villages wiped out--these were\par
the stories that found their way into the deafest ears.  Crowds of\par
refugees evacuated from these towns and villages presently began to\par
throng in. Soon the streets were full from wall to wall.  Spies moved\par
everywhere, and no lights dared be shown at night-time. Bread grew\par
scarce, the dreadful sound of the guns drew nearer.  Wounded, Allies and\par
Germans also, were brought in, in thousands, by the ambulance-cars.  The\par
hospitals and hotels and convents were full--all the schools--and many\par
of the private houses.  Terrible rumours gained ground of a great battle\par
about to be fought in the neighbourhood of the town.\par
\par
Peering from garret-windows by day or night, one could see great banks\par
of black smoke towering on the north, east, and west horizons, pierced\par
by broad licking tongues of cherry-coloured flame.  Taubes and Allied\par
aircraft fought battles in the heavens.  Bombs were dropped upon public\par
buildings.  Death had begun to be common in the streets when the first\par
Krupp shells fell and exploded in the moat behind the Abbey Church of\par
St. Jacques.  Ten minutes later--upon the doomed city fell the direst\par
fury of the German hate.\par
\par
It had been as though hell had opened, as under that hail of iron and\par
fire the troops and transports of the Allies, and the long processions\par
of townspeople afoot and in carts and carriages had rolled out of the\par
town.  Even the dogs had left, following their owners.  Like the\par
cats--who clung to their familiar surroundings, and had to be removed by\par
force, if they were to be taken--the old folks resisted the sturdy hands\par
that tugged at them.  "Leave us! ..." they quavered.  "We are so old!\par
... We can never bear the journey! ... We should only die upon the roads\par
if we were to go!"\par
\par
Many did go, and many died, and of those who stayed behind them, Steel,\par
Iron, and Fire claimed a heavy toll. But in the Northern quarter, some\par
yet dwelt in cellar-basements, feeding on mouldy flour, and frozen\par
potatoes.  Sleeping on sacks of straw, covered with rugs or blankets,\par
warming their lean, shivery bodies at braziers, choking behind masks\par
taken from slain men through deadly gas-attacks,--creeping up between\par
bombardments for a breath of purer air.  Venturing forth to kneel upon\par
the littered pavements of roofless churches, and pray to Our Lord before\par
His vacant tabernacles and shattered Crucifixes--for an end to the\par
dreadful War.\par
\par
And no answer came, it seemed, for all their praying. They had grown\par
used to the dampness underground.  Their eyes were now accustomed to the\par
gloom, as their ears to the stunning crashes of the bombardments--and\par
the perpetual whirr and buzz and whine of the aircraft in the sky.  So\par
natural had become to them the abomination of desolation that they\par
actually resented the occasional visits of the Red Cross car from\par
Pophereele.\par
\par
"Behold him again," they grumbled, "the tall, blind Englishman.  What\par
does he seek here?  Hardly to view our ruins that he has no eyes to see!\par
And now in another big grey car arrive a French priest and a woman,\par
asking, wherever they meet a soul to ask, if the blind Englishman is\par
here?  The priest is a Monseigneur--Old Ottilie swears to the ring and\par
the purple collar.  The woman is English, it appears.  Perhaps she is\par
the blind man's wife?"\par
\par
The car moved on where the roadway was not broken by trenches, crawling\par
painfully over litter and wreck.  In the shadow of the ruined prison,\par
while yet the sun was high, they halted.  Their chauffeur nodded to his\par
Belgian compatriot, the Red Cross orderly, interrogated by Monseigneur,\par
pointed to the tall brown figure standing on the grass beside the\par
twisted wreckage of a British aeroplane.\par
\par
"I will wait here for you, Mademoiselle," said Monseigneur, getting out\par
and assisting his fellow-traveller.  She was very tall and of supple\par
figure, and wore a long blue coat with the Red Cross shield-badge, and a\par
felt hat banded with the V.A.D. ribbon, pulled down over luxuriant\par
masses of hair--hair that had been cloudy-black as storm-wrack and had\par
been bleached to the hue of wintry beech-leaves, and now had darkened to\par
the brown of peat-earth, deepening in colour every day.\par
\par
She gave Monseigneur her hand, thanking him, and suddenly he thought her\par
beautiful, although the tall young woman had not previously appealed to\par
the sense of beauty in Monseigneur.  Her long eyes under their widely\par
arching brows were stars, her mouth was smiling.  When she moved away\par
over the snow-patched grass, she seemed to tread on air....\par
\par
Throughout the drive Patrine had been torn with horrible misgivings.\par
"What shall I say or do," she had wondered. "How shall I bear it if the\par
look upon his face should tell me, when Alan first hears my voice--that\par
I was wrong to come?"  But the chilly fit had passed with the first\par
glimpse of Sherbrand.  The rich, warm flood rising in her veins had\par
swept her doubts away.\par
\par
Here on this shell-pitted expanse of turf you felt the War-pulse\par
beating.  French 75's were putting over a furious barrage from the\par
south.  North of the City of the Salient the British guns were slogging,\par
and through the chain-fire of the enemy's 77 mm.'s, his 11.2-in.\par
howitzers bellowed at short intervals, and sent in 600-pound shells.\par
\par
\par
The smoke of a train rose north-west in the direction of Thourout\par
Junction.  That the train was a German train, carrying troops and guns\par
and munitions for War purposes, did not at once occur to Patrine.  All\par
was well.  Not a doubt remained.  She was near her Flying Man again\par
after months of separation.  Here at last was food for her hungry eyes\par
and drink for her thirsting soul.\par
\par
"He has grown thin, poor dear!" she thought, seeing how the war-stained\par
khaki hung in folds on his tall figure.  The broad shoulders stooped.\par
The chest had sunken, and he leaned upon a heavy walking-stick.  The\par
beloved face was turned away, the line of the cheek was careworn.  She\par
choked upon a sob and stopped short, fighting her emotion down.\par
\par
The song of the soaring lark broke off.  The bird dived to earth and hid\par
itself amongst the frosty grasses as the snoring whirr of aircraft came\par
out of the distance high in the sky to the west.  Now the shape of a big\par
biplane gleamed pinky-white as a seagull, beating up against the thrust\par
of the snow-tanged easterly breeze.\par
\par
Nearer and nearer flew the 'plane.  Now one could see it distinctly.  A\par
French machine by its blue-white-red rings, and a Caudron by its great\par
square tail.  A silver-grey monoplane scurried in its wake, a Weiss by\par
the backward curve of its wing-tips.  The whirr of its tractor and the\par
blatter of its machine-gun wakened the echoes sleeping among the leprous\par
white ruins of the city.  The Caudron wheeled and circled beautifully,\par
and the trac-trac of its mitraille answered the machine-gun, and spent\par
bullets began to patter on the Plaine far below.\par
\par
Suddenly the Frenchman banked and began to climb. The Weiss, its\par
aluminium sheathing glittering in the sunshine, climbed too, so rapidly\par
that the enemy's purpose was foiled.  Then, at a great height they\par
circled round each other, and the crack and flare of explosive\par
revolver-bullets began to mingle with the blatter and trac-trac, and\par
little blobs of something that blazed and sputtered wickedly began to\par
drop with the bullets that tumbled out of the skies. It was the\par
prettiest sight.  It suggested the amorous dallying of two big\par
butterflies, the squabble of a pair of hawking swallows, and yet the\par
issues were Life and Death. Suddenly the Weiss took to flight.  A second\par
Caudron had showed upon the distance and the Kaiser's flier was not\par
taking any more on.  Waiting for his countryman to come abreast, the\par
Frenchman hovered like a kite-hawk.  And at the familiar buzz of the\par
horizontal screws a visible thrill went through Sherbrand.  He took off\par
the smoked glasses that he wore, and turned his blind eyes upwards\par
towards the sound, and on his haggard face was stamped the anguish of\par
his despair.\par
\par
\par
"My poor boy!" nearly broke from Patrine, and hot tears scalded her\par
eyelids.  He started, though she had uttered no word, and brought down\par
those unseeing eyes.  His nostrils expanded as he inhaled the air.  His\par
thick fair brows contracted.  The first Caudron, exchanging signals with\par
the second, had ceased hovering and floated onwards, but Sherbrand's\par
thoughts had been brought down out of his sky.\par
\par
"What is it? ... Why?" said the intent and frowning look.  He snuffed\par
the air again and pondered still, and suddenly Patrine comprehended.\par
Some waft of perfume from her hair or clothes had reached the sense made\par
keener by his blindness, evoked some once-loved image, roused some\par
memory of her.\par
\par
She crouched low, and looked up at the lean, lined visage yearningly.\par
Dear heart! how changed he was to-day from her young Mercury of the\par
Milles Plaisirs.  And yet this altered face of his, marred by the broad,\par
new-healed scar that traversed the left cheek and temple, and the cloudy\par
look of suffering in the prominent grey-blue eyes, was dearer than ever\par
to Patrine.\par
\par
How bravely the ribbon of the Croix de Guerre and the purple, green, and\par
silver of the Belgian Order showed against the war-stained khaki.  What\par
woman living would not glory in such a lover, welcome the sacred charge,\par
rejoice to be his guide and minister! ... "Oh, my blind eagle, to sit\par
mateless in the darkness shall not be your fate, God being good to me!"\par
Some words like these were on the lips of Patrine.\par
\par
But the words were unspoken.  He was turning those cloudy, troubled eyes\par
towards his unseen sky again as though trying to project the vision of\par
his soul through the depths of aerial distance.  Then he desisted as\par
though wearied by the effort.  His stern face softened to dreamy\par
tenderness.  His lips moved.  Very quietly, but with infinite\par
wistfulness, he uttered her own name:\par
\par
"Patrine!  Patrine!"\par
\par
He was thinking of her--he was dreaming of her--he was still her lover.\par
She knew a joyful shock, a checking of the pulses....  Then her blood\par
whirled on its crimson circle as though arteries and veins were brimmed\par
with wine.  Her bosom heaved, her eyes were misty jewels, and out of the\par
wonderful silence about them came to her the low, sweet soughing of her\par
long-lost Wind of Joy.\par
\par
She moved to Sherbrand, kissed him full upon the mouth, and called him:\par
"Alan!"  And a great cry broke from him--a cry of wonder, triumph, and\par
joy.  As his arms swept out to enfold her she knew that she had\par
conquered.  She had not been deceived in reading love between the formal\par
lines.\par
\par
"Life has nothing more to give!" was Patrine's thought as his arms held\par
her.  It seemed that Death would be a tiny price to pay for such a\par
wonderful moment as this.\par
\par
"My love, my love!  Did you really think we could live without each\par
other?" she stammered through his eager kisses.  "Didn't you know I\par
would have to come and carry you back home by the hair of your head?\par
Did you dare to dream that I or any of the people who love you could get\par
on without you?  Your mother, and Aunt Lynette--and Bawne and Uncle\par
Owen--and Sir Roland--who managed things for me to come to you!--and\par
Margot and her boy ... for there is a boy--a regular topper--born last\par
November--with eyes just like poor Franky's!  And you're to come back\par
and be kind to him and his mother--because you promised Franky you\par
would!  So that old ghost of your succession to the Viscounty is\par
laid--and I'm glad of it! Another stone heaved out of the way that leads\par
me back to you!"\par
\par
She went on, holding him as he held her embraced, pouring herself out in\par
a swift rush of eager utterance:\par
\par
"Come back and help us readjust values.  Everything's\par
changed--everything's altered--since the beginning of the War.  We women\par
have found out--even the idlest and the vainest of us--that the things\par
we used to live for really meant nothing!  What we have called Society\par
is a box of broken toys.  The plays we have laughed or cried at--the\par
books we have read--the music we have gone rabid over--the frocks we\par
have sported--the flirtations we have revelled in--the scandals we have\par
discussed--none of these mean anything, count for anything--weigh\par
anything!  Nothing is real but Life--and Love--and Death.  Not life like\par
the life we used to know--nor love like the love we talked of. A life of\par
work, and help, and prayer, and hope--and courage--and the kind of love\par
that has wings and doesn't crawl in the mud.  Nothing like the Death we\par
used to dodge and blink and dread so, but something nobler.  Something\par
that leads through the Gate of the Grave--to God!  Don't you see that\par
the War was sent to change us?--don't you see----"\par
\par
He cried out:\par
\par
"I shall never see again!"  An ugly spasm wrenched his jaw aside.  "They\par
think I take it pluckily.  But every night I dream it over once\par
more--and the sky is rushing back, and the ground is swirling up--and\par
the Bird is toppling, spinning downwards, in a trail of smoke and fire.\par
I can hear my observer screaming, poor, poor fellow!  How I escaped\par
burning I don't know.  Then comes the crash!--and the grey void of\par
Nothingness out of which, aeons later, I crawl into a blind man's\par
dreadful world.  A world that is all sounds and voices and sounds and\par
touches.  A world where I must live--and die--in the dark!"\par
\par
She said in her deep sweet voice, with her velvet cheek pressed against\par
Sherbrand's:\par
\par
"With me.  And suppose you saw me, and could not feel nor hear me?"\par
\par
She felt him shudder as he answered:\par
\par
"The thing would be Hell!"\par
\par
"Well, then, let me try and make the best of it!  For both of us, my\par
dear one!"  She pressed closer to his breast, magnetising him with her\par
touch, her breath, her presence, summoning all her forces of womanly\par
allurements to charm him from despair.  "Couldn't I reconcile my lover\par
to the dark?" she whispered.\par
\par
"Are you cold, dearest?" he asked.  For as the last words left her lips\par
a sharp vibration had passed through her. "You shivered as though you\par
were."\par
\par
"Perhaps? ... I hardly know," said Patrine, thrusting away the loathed\par
memory of the Upas.  "Perhaps the wind has shifted--or a goose walked\par
over my grave."\par
\par
She changed her tone and began to tell him how Margot had evicted her\par
Uncle Derek and his Lepidopthingambobs and handed over the caravanserai\par
in Hanover Square to the Red Cross people for a Hospital--and how all\par
the wards were to be covered with vulcanised rubber--not a corner to\par
catch a dust-speck anywhere.  And she went on to describe her journey in\par
search of Sherbrand, and her disappointment at finding him absent from\par
the Hospital at Pophereele--and the kindness shown her by the\par
Monseigneur who had escorted her from St. O--, and subsequently insisted\par
on accompanying her here.\par
\par
"For it's supposed to be risky," she ended, smiling.  "He says--to me it\par
seems like spitting in the face of a dead body!--that the Germans shell\par
the poor place nearly every day."\par
\par
"It's true.  They've pitched High Explosive in once already this\par
morning--and as I mean to marry you to-morrow," said Sherbrand, "we had\par
better be off out of it before they repeat the dose."  He added:\par
"There's an English Catholic priest at the Hospital--and I've my Special\par
Licence still tucked away in a pocket!"\par
\par
She exclaimed in delight:\par
\par
"Then you never meant to give me up?  Own it--you didn't!"\par
\par
"It was you who took your solid oath you wouldn't marry me."\par
\par
"Unless you were poor and ill--and wanted a woman to nurse you and look\par
after you"--her voice broke--"and work for you!  Oh, Boy!--no, not boy\par
any more!  My man of all the men that ever were or will be!  Don't\par
refuse me the right my love gives me--of working for you!" she urged.\par
\par
"Such true love.  Such fine love.  Pat, you're a glory of a woman.  And\par
you shall work--I'll give you lots of work," he promised her.  "But--my\par
sweet girl, I'm not poor."\par
\par
She asked him in her deep sweet voice:\par
\par
"Do you think you'd be poor to me--if you hadn't a copper halfpenny?"\par
And with his arm about her still, and her heart beating against his\par
hand, as they moved over the grass together, she began to describe their\par
home.  Quite a small, unpretending, but comfortable home.  The home of\par
two people who adored each other, and wanted nothing better than to go\par
on doing it up to the last day of their lives.\par
\par
\par
"We'll have children--stacks!" she assured him.  "Long-legged boys with\par
beaky, hatchet faces--boys who'll invent and build aeroplanes and fly\par
them too, you bet!"\par
\par
"And girls," put in Sherbrand, tightening his clasp about the supple\par
womanly body, "great big galumphing girls, like their mother!"\par
\par
"The sweets!" she sighed.  "I can see them now!"\par
\par
"Ah, that's what I shan't do ever," said Sherbrand. "Don't you think\par
they'll be bored with their blind father, sometimes, Pat?"\par
\par
"Just let them dare!  Let them--that's all!"  She winked away the tears\par
crowding to her eyelashes.  "Besides you mayn't be always blind--I'll\par
never give up praying! Didn't that American surgeon at the Hospital say\par
that cases of functional blindness from shock--like yours--supposing\par
there is no serious lesion in the brain--have been known to recover\par
sight suddenly and completely?  Don't shake your head!  Isn't there a\par
chance--a blessed possibility--to cling to, and fight for?  Ah! if you\par
were cured, don't you know I'd send you back to the Front next day?\par
Don't you, Alan? Yes!--yes! you do!"  The bright drops rushed in spate\par
over her underlids, and hopped over the front of her long blue coat, to\par
lose themselves among the frosted grasses as she went hotly on:\par
\par
"Don't you believe--you must believe--I'd lay down my life--just for the\par
glory of doing that!  Perhaps I usedn't to care much about\par
England--before the War.  But now I've found out what it means to be a\par
pup of the old bull-mother,--I'd meet Death jumping--rather than fail cf\par
doing my bit.  What's up?"\par
\par
Someone had whistled shrilly behind them, and she wheeled, to see\par
Monseigneur and a Red Cross orderly beckoning and signalling, standing\par
on a heap of rubbish on the outskirts of the Plaine.  Sherbrand, for\par
whom the call was meant, waved his stick and whistled in answer. The\par
orderly, at a gesture from Monseigneur, got nimbly down from the\par
rubbish-heap and started to cross the intervening stretch of grass.\par
\par
"Why is he coming?" began Patrine, vexedly.\par
\par
"To fetch the blind man, I suppose."\par
\par
"Ah-h!"  Her long eyes blazed resentment.  "If anyone but yourself had\par
called you that! ... Send him back!" she pleaded, jealously.  "From\par
henceforward nobody is to fetch you--or carry you either, except Me!"\par
\par
So Sherbrand laughed in his companioned darkness, waved again, and\par
shouted to the orderly to go back.  What he said was lost in the racket\par
accompanying the arrival of a German H.E. shell.\par
\par
For still at intervals during each day and sometimes at night-time the\par
sad dignity of the deserted City of the Salient was outraged by these\par
monstrous messengers of hate.  The thing came from the enemy's position\par
east of the city, and fell with a hideous droning note in the wooded\par
park by the Dixmude Gate.\par
\par
A shattering crash followed--as though the roof of the world were\par
tumbling in.  The green park of budding trees was rent and splintered,\par
cratered and riven as though a Dinosaur had died there of acute rabies,\par
biting and tearing and howking up the earth.\par
\par
Love is a wonderful wit-quickener in necessity.  It taught Patrine\par
Saxham, the woman of limitations, exactly what to do at the moment when\par
the great shell droned down to ground.  Irresistible as a mountain\par
torrent, she leaped straight for the blind man before her, hurling him\par
backwards by the sudden impact, over-balancing and bearing him down.\par
Pinning him with the sheer weight of her vigorous young body--covering\par
him as Nature teaches a tigress to cover her menaced cub, whilst their\par
ears were deafened with the appalling detonation, the solid earth heaved\par
and billowed under their prone, locked bodies, and the air surged and\par
winnowed about them as though beaten by the passage of huge invisible\par
wings.\par
\par
"Is this Death?" she asked herself.  "Then--for both!" was her\par
half-conscious prayer.  But Death passed by in a blizzard of scorching\par
gases, splinters of rending steel, gravel, and stones, splintered timber\par
and pulverised soil, leaving a huge cloud of reddish-yellow billowing\par
over the Plaine d'Amour.  A brown powder that stank of verbena, thickly\par
coated all visible objects.  Hair, skin, and clothes were tinted to\par
uniformity, and a smothering oppression burdened the lungs.  Yet as\par
Patrine lay gasping, nerveless, beaten, that fierce new-kindled instinct\par
of protection lived in her, potent, vital with possibilities as the\par
spark in the battery or the germ in the cell.\par
\par
The Great Test had found her not wanting nor unready. The dross of self\par
had been burned away in the flame of a passion high and pure.  The Crown\par
of a noble womanhood was hers in that great moment when her body had\par
made a rampart for the shielding of her love.\par
\par
Under the heave of her bosom Sherbrand's broad chest panted.  He\par
lived--and her heart went up in a rush of passionate thanks to Heaven.\par
She moved from him, quaking in every nerve and fibre, crouched beside\par
him, found her handkerchief, and wiped the pungent dust from his face.\par
It was pale, the mouth and eyes were closed, the nostrils fluttered with\par
quick panting.  His head had struck against the ground when her leap had\par
hurled him backwards. He had been stunned, she told herself.  He would\par
revive soon.\par
\par
"Patrine!" he choked out, opening his eyes.\par
\par
"Pat's here by you, my darling!"  She slipped her strong arm under his\par
neck and helped him to sit up:\par
\par
"You're not hurt?"  His lungs pumped hard, and his reddened eyes ran\par
water.  He blinked it away and caught her hands, crushing them in his\par
grip.  "You're sure you're not?"\par
\par
"Quite, quite sure!  And you're all right, aren't you?"\par
\par
"As right as rain, except for a bump on the head!"  He freed a hand and\par
rubbed it.  "When the shell came over--and the ground rose up and hit\par
me.  How did it happen?"\par
\par
"I--hardly know.  Oh, Alan!  God has been good to us! Hasn't He?"\par
\par
There was no immediate response.  Sherbrand's lean face was working.  He\par
rose to his knees and thus remained an instant, in silence that gave\par
thanks.  Then he got lightly on his feet, reached down and lifted\par
Patrine.  And thus they stood, the girl clinging to the young man's\par
broad shoulders as he held her, the tears from her own still smarting\par
eyes tracing white channels in the dust that masked her quivering face.\par
\par
"You and I! ... My hat!--" she gasped--"what a precious pair of\par
scallawags!  You lose nothing in not being able to see, my Flying\par
Man!--just now.  Oh! but the station!  And the park----"\par
\par
She stopped in sheer astonishment.  For the deadliest fury of the High\par
Explosive had wreaked itself on the bit of municipal woodland.  With the\par
electric train-station that had neighboured it, and the _abattoirs_ in\par
its vicinity, it had been clean wiped out.\par
\par
"Come," said Sherbrand, tightening his clasp as he felt her sway against\par
him.  He was supporting--he was guiding as they turned their faces\par
south.\par
\par
Here the Death that had passed by had left more traces of its passage.\par
The rent carcase of a gaunt cow that had grazed upon the Plaine d'Amour,\par
lay in a steaming crimson pool among the frosty grasses; and beyond,\par
some thirty paces from the Rue d'Elverdinghe, where the automobiles\par
waited near the ruins of the prison, Monseigneur in his flowing black\par
cloak knelt over a stained bundle of ragged blue clothing and shattered\par
humanity, and the Belgian and his fellow-chauffeur were bringing a\par
stretcher from the Red Cross car....\par
\par
\par
"The poor orderly has been wounded ... No! ... killed!" flashed through\par
Patrine's mind as Monseigneur glanced towards her, gesturing with a\par
supple hand in a swift expressive way.  "I must go over there--I may be\par
wanted," she mentally added, controlling her sick shudder and reached\par
back to take again the hand of her blind man.  But a sudden exclamation\par
from Sherbrand brought round her head, and the strange look stamped upon\par
the face she loved, arrested movement and checked utterance.\par
\par
"What is it?  What has happened?" she forced her stiffened tongue to ask\par
him.  "Oh, Alan! tell me!  You are not ill----"\par
\par
"Not ill!" came from the twisted mouth, wrung and convulsed with--was it\par
joy or anguish?  He shut his eyes, striving for calmness and coherent\par
speech and wrestling with a fierce emotion that made him sway and totter\par
like a drunken man.  "Give me your hand--both your dear hands! Don't\par
mind my shutting my eyes--it'll steady me to tell you! ... Just\par
now--when you let go of me--something happened--and I--_saw_!"\par
\par
He choked upon the last word.  She faced him, white and wild and\par
desperate, and cried in a voice quite strange to Sherbrand's ears:\par
\par
"You saw! ... My God!--do you want to drive me crazy?  Do you mean--you\par
can't mean----"\par
\par
"Does the truth sound so insane?"  His voice broke in a sob.  He opened\par
the shut, quivering lids through which the tears were streaming, and the\par
grey-blue eyes that looked at her were no longer the dead orbs of one\par
blind.  Life and light throbbed in their depths, they glowed with such a\par
radiance as the eyes of the First Lover may have shed on the face of the\par
new-made Eve. What was he saying in shaken tones of mingled awe and\par
rapture:\par
\par
"I saw what I am seeing now.  Trees--and green grass, and blue sky--and\par
your face!  Your dear face that stayed with me when the Big Dark blotted\par
out the rest....  More loving--more lovely than ever I have dreamed it.\par
Oh!  Pat, did ever any man get such a wedding-present?"  His tone\par
changed: "My sight for me--and death for that poor chap there!  Can it\par
be Carpenter--the American who's been so good to me! ... And the priest\par
helping to lift him--the old man with the noble face? ... Not\par
Monseigneur--our Chaplain at the Hospital!  He's beckoning!  Come! Let's\par
run!"\par
\par
\par
So these happy lovers with Death as travelling-companion drove away from\par
the City of the Salient.  There was a wedding next morning at the\par
Hospital of Pophereele. And twenty-four hours later, the big\par
black-capitalled broadsheets bellowed from Ludgate Hill up Fleet Street\par
and along the Strand to Charing Cross, and all through the West End:\par
\par
ROMANTIC SEQUEL TO FAMOUS AVIATOR'S STORY.  SHERBRAND OF THE R.F.C.,\par
BLINDED IN AIR-BATTLE, RECOVERS SIGHT THROUGH SHELL-SHOCK.  MARRIED\par
YESTERDAY. RETURNS WITH BRIDE.  CAPTAINCY AND D.S.O."\par
\par
\par
A closing picture of a young couple sitting very close together on a\par
rustic seat in the garden of a cottage on Seasheere Downs, where\par
hyacinths bloom, and clumps of pink-white peonies, and the Birds of War\par
whirr overhead in a June's sky of speedwell-blue.\par
\par
Patrine Sherbrand says to her husband, as the smoke of British\par
transports and heavily-laden supply-steamers slants against the east\par
horizon, and the knife-sharp bows of shepherding Destroyers cleave the\par
grey-green waters of the North Sea:\par
\par
"If without dishonour to your dear name it lay in my power to keep you\par
with me, do you think I'd have it so? Not I!  I'll have you carry on as\par
though I'd never even existed.  For me--the work that lies at hand.\par
When that's done--dreams of you.  If you were killed you'd live for\par
me--my man I gave for England!  Our England that they'll never beat--not\par
even if they win!"\par
\par
"Thanks, my sweet wife!  Then when I say--our honeymoon is over----?"\par
\par
"Ah, well! ... How soon? ..."\par
\par
He told her, looking in her eyes, that did not flinch beneath his:\par
\par
"In four days!  The Medical Board finds me quite fit--and there's a\par
Flying billet waiting.  Our Western Front...."\par
\par
She said, as her heart beat on his and their mouths met in a kiss:\par
\par
"Then--four more days of love with me, and fly, my Bird of War!"\par
\par
The Chief Scout had said to Sherbrand in those days of July, 1914: "The\par
Saxham breed's a stark breed--hard as granite, supple as incandescent\par
lava, with a strain of Berserk madness, and a dash of Oriental fatalism.\par
They can hate magnificently and forgive grandly, and love to the very\par
verge of Death."\par
\par
Sherbrand had found it so.  He thanked God that this heart that he had\par
won would never change nor fail him. He knew that he could call his own\par
the love that reaches living hands to Love beyond the grave.\par
\par
\par
\par
                                THE END\par
